(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"ettuthogai_puram","chapters":{"pt:1":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":1,"title":"2ஆம் பத்து, இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The second ten, Kumattur Kannanar's songs for Imayavaramban Neduncheralathan: the felling of the enemy's guardian kadamba at sea told as Murugan's killing of Sur, the drum made from that tree, enemy lands ruined against the guarded land that flowers, the queen left waiting while the king stays in camp, and a giver who will not fail even when the rains do; the preface adds his parents, the Himalayan bow and the binding of the Yavanas."},"pt:2":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":2,"title":"3ஆம் பத்து, பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The third ten, Palai Gauthamanar's songs for Palyanai Selkezhu Kuttuvan: the king's sacrifices and open kitchen, the faults that block dharma's wheel, the bards he feeds in drought, the six-duty Brahmins whose word he follows, a series of enemy lands seen at their height and ruined by his anger, his own land watered even in drought, and the great rice offering before battle; the preface adds his conquests and his last journey to the forest."},"pt:3":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":3,"title":"4ஆம் பத்து, களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The fourth ten, Kappiyaatru Kappiyanar's songs for Kalangaaykkanni Narmudi Cheral: a festival of Vishnu framing the king who set the shaken peoples right, a conqueror not angry in the enemy's land, an army that is its own fortress, the blood-field as the king's unfailing wealth, an unbent rule beside victory, a king who will not eat sweet things alone, and a dancer guided to the slayer of Nannan, who gives war elephants; the preface adds his birth, the Poozhi conquest and Nannan's beheading."},"pt:4":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":4,"title":"5ஆம் பத்து, கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The fifth ten, Paranar's songs for Kadal Pirakkottiya Senguttuvan: the bard's hard road to the king whose feet toiled at sea, scarred warriors and horses beyond counting, gifts like drought-breaking rain, the war at Mohur for a distant kinsman and the neem made into a drum, a king without equal who drove back the sea, performers who never take their leave, slaughter that is itself a gift, a name to outlast the river sands, a virali guided by the Mohur slaughter, and the sleepless king in camp; the preface adds the northern expedition for the Pattini stone and the Chola wars."},"pt:5":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":5,"title":"6ஆம் பத்து, ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The sixth ten, Kakkaipadiniyar Nachchellaiyar's songs for Adukotpattu Cheralathan: a king soft-seeming at home and like Death in battle, a hand that only gives, a war elephant that would charge his own city's gate, a giver who should forgo heaven and rest like a cloud after rain, the dance motif turned three ways (the queen's anger at the thunangai, the denial of festival skill, the victory dance on the field), virali guide poems, and the king's duty to keep the roads open; the preface ties his name to seized goats and his title Vaanavaramban to a gift."},"pt:6":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":6,"title":"7ஆம் பத்து, செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The seventh ten, Kapilar's songs for Selvakkadungo Vazhiathan: the poet arrives after Pari's death, drawn by a giver who does not regret his gifts; sieges and the terms of surrender, the king defined by four exceptions, gifts to brahmins and musicians that outshine other kings as the sun the stars, two guide songs to bards, warriors fasting before a fort while palace women mark days on the wall, ancestors who ruled like him, and the ancient debts paid; the preface adds his mother's line, his sacrifice and the gift of Okanthur."},"pt:7":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":7,"title":"8ஆம் பத்து, பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The eighth ten, Arisil Kizhar's songs for Perunjeral Irumporai: foes who stirred the hive and paid tribute, the doom-fire for those who resolve without thought, a king beyond comparison whom praise cannot make credible, a learned sacrificer who sends his old counsellor to tapas, an army too large to count, a virali sent to the hill of the destroyer of Tagadur, a couch made of a beaten king's tusks; the preface names Adhiyaman and the razing of Tagadur."},"pt:8":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":8,"title":"9ஆம் பத்து, இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"The ninth ten, Perunkunrur Kizhar's songs for Ilanjeral Irumporai: the king's camp in the rains and a plea to go home to the queen so the besieged may sleep, the inexhaustible wealth and army, a march sweet to watch and bitter to meet, foes who knew his strength and would not bow, gifts beyond Kapilar's villages, a fierce king gentler than the Vaani's water, a singer sent for jewels, the ancestors' deeds against Nannan and the kadamba, rain brought by a straight sceptre, and a king likened to water, sky and sea; the preface adds his Chola wars and his rites."},"pt:9":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":9,"title":"fragments","section":"பதிற்றுப்பத்து","theme":"Four Patitruppattu fragments outside the surviving tens: the enemies' only choice of tribute before the king's army of thunder, wind, fire and flood; the march that makes kings behind a perfect fort lose heart; three lines of a woman denying another's equality; and the bard who finds the king giving gifts in a war camp that ignores the rains."},"pn:1":{"book":"purananuru","num":1,"title":"புறநானூறு 1-20","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 1-20 open with the invocation to the god of the konrai and then praise and counsel the three crowned lines: counsel (செவியறிவுறூஉ) on giving, impartiality and guarding the realm like a child, vanji poems that boast of burned enemy fields, questions about gifts paid for by conquest, and Kudapulaviyanar's argument that the king who stores water gives life."},"pn:2":{"book":"purananuru","num":2,"title":"புறநானூறு 21-40","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 21-40 are court poems to three kings: the Pandya Nedunchezhiyan of Talaiyalanganam and his predecessor at Kanapper fort (21-26), the Chola Nalankilli (27-33) and the Chola Killivalavan (34-40), with one Chera (22). Victory poems that make the fort, the battle sacrifice and the burned enemy land into praise sit beside kaanchi poems that turn the same kings toward transience, the three aims and grace, and counsel poems that tie a king's victories to the plough, his umbrella to his subjects and his fame to easy access and sweet speech."},"pn:3":{"book":"purananuru","num":3,"title":"புறநானூறு 41-60","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 41-60 are poems to the three crowns: Kovur Kizhar's interventions with Chola kings (omens before a march, a siege, a war between kinsmen, children before the elephant, a bard under suspicion), guide and praise poems for Chera kings, and Pandya poems that rank righteous rule above the army, liken the king to the gods and to sun and moon, and close with a Chola-Pandya alliance and a parasol mistaken for the moon. The recurring argument is restraint: poets use ancestry, shame and gain to hold kings back from cruelty and division."},"pn:4":{"book":"purananuru","num":4,"title":"புறநானூறு 61-80","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 61 to 80 praise Chola and Pandya kings and test what kingship is worth: the double death of two kings in one battle, the honour death after Venni and a poet ranking the loser over the victor, guide poems sending hungry bards to Chola patrons, three royal vows that stake justice, friendship, fame and giving, a captive king's refusal to beg, a king on the lightness of the throne, and the boy-king Neduncheliyan's single victory over seven."},"pn:5":{"book":"purananuru","num":5,"title":"புறநானூறு 81-100","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 81-100 open with praise of the Chola Porvaikko Perunarkilli, including Nakkannaiyar's three poems of a woman's one-sided love for him, and a mother's boast that her son will be found on the battlefield; the rest is Avvaiyar's run on Athiyaman Neduman Anji: warnings to his foes, the nelli fruit of long life, her poems as a child's prattle, his used and broken spears, his wounds, his threat of tribute and his unabated anger."},"pn:6":{"book":"purananuru","num":6,"title":"புறநானூறு 101-120","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 101-120 move from Avvai's praise of Athiyaman and his son, the bard's trust in a patron and the warning not to slight a young king, to Kapilar on Pari: his giving, the siege of Parambu by the three crowned kings, and the laments of Pari's daughters and his poet after his death."},"pn:7":{"book":"purananuru","num":7,"title":"புறநானூறு 121-140","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 121 to 140 are praise of three chiefs as givers: Malaiyaman Kari of Mullur (121 to 126), Ay Andiran of Pothiyil (127 to 136, 138) and the lord of Nanjil (137, 139, 140). They test what good giving is: judging rank, giving sober, giving without hope of a next-world return, and giving beyond what was asked."},"pn:8":{"book":"purananuru","num":8,"title":"புறநானூறு 141-160","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Gift poems to the hill chieftains Pekan, Nalli, Ori, the lord of Konkanam, Erai and Kumanan: the guide poems, the cycle pleading with Pekan to return to his wife, the roll of seven dead givers, and the bard's starving household pressed on Kumanan."},"pn:9":{"book":"purananuru","num":9,"title":"புறநானூறு 161-180","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 161-180 are almost all about giving and asking: Perunchiththiranar's and Saththanar's pleas to Kumanan and his gift of his own head, a bard's elephant tied in rebuke to a mean patron's tree, the hill chief Pittankotran praised by five poets as giver and as an anvil to his foes, the hunger-healer Pannan, and soldiers and small chiefs whose scars and spears feed the bards, with one brahmin sacrificer praised as a patron."},"pn:10":{"book":"purananuru","num":10,"title":"புறநானூறு 181-200","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 181-200 are mostly maxims on what keeps the world, a land, a king and a life worth while: the selfless, learning above birth, measured levies, good men, giving, steady friends and equanimity before fate. They end with bards' poems on asking and giving that test those maxims on real patrons."},"pn:11":{"book":"purananuru","num":11,"title":"புறநானூறு 201-220","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 201 to 220 are of two kinds. 201 to 211 are bards' gift poems that test the terms of patronage: a poet offering a dead patron's daughters, poets leaving patrons who gave without regard or kept them waiting, and the bard's household as evidence. 212 to 220 follow Kopperuncholan and his unseen friend Pisirandhaiyar from praise and counsel through the king's resolve to die to the laments for him."},"pn:12":{"book":"purananuru","num":12,"title":"புறநானூறு 221-240","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 221-240 are all laments (kaiyarunilai) for dead patrons: Pothiyar's three for Kopperunchozhan, whose friend was told to follow later and who as a hero-stone still makes room for old friends; laments for Karikalan, Nalankilli and Killi Valavan that reproach Death as a fool who ate its own seed or a suppliant who had to beg; the potter asked for an urn as big as the earth; Avvai's laments for Anji, his pyre, his hero-stone and the toddy he shared; poems for Evvi, Pari, Ilaveliman, Nambi Nedunchezhiyan and Aay, ending in the bard's resolve to seek a new patron and the scattering of poets to other lands."},"pn:13":{"book":"purananuru","num":13,"title":"புறநானூறு 241-260","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 241-260 are poems of death and what follows it: laments for patrons (Ay, Saththan of Ollaiyur), an old man's lament for his youth, a king's lament for his wife, widows who choose the pyre or live on alli and greens, wives alone with a husband's body in the wilds, two poems on an ascetic who was once a seducer, the vetchi raiders and their toddy, and a karanthai hero whose name passes onto a stone."},"pn:14":{"book":"purananuru","num":14,"title":"புறநானூறு 261-280","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 261-280 are the hero-stone and battlefield poems: laments for cattle-defenders who became stones, the victory feast of the raiders, the nochchi of the fort's defenders, single warriors against armies, and the old mothers and wives who measure a death by its honour and its cost."},"pn:15":{"book":"purananuru","num":15,"title":"புறநானூறு 281-300","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems of the fighting men and their households: the rites that guard a wounded man, the deaths of warriors from old martial houses, the mothers who weep and rejoice over sons, and the toddy, flowers and land a king owes those who go first."},"pn:16":{"book":"purananuru","num":16,"title":"புறநானூறு 301-320","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 301-320 move from the battle line to the warrior's house: horses and young riders, vows that frighten camps, a brahmin whose few words end a siege, warriors fallen with their elephants, and then the old houses and the able men of small villages, whose mothers assign duties, whose wives pray for guests at hero-stones, and whose lords pawn swords and promise gold to the bards."},"pn:17":{"book":"purananuru","num":17,"title":"புறநானூறு 321-340","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 321-340 praise the small warrior villages of hard, dry land (vaakai: வல்லாண் முல்லை and மூதின் முல்லை), whose chiefs and wives give away even their seed grain and whose only god is the hero-stone, then turn to the magatpaal kaanchi poems, where a king's demand for a chief's daughter brings war on her town."},"pn:18":{"book":"purananuru","num":18,"title":"புறநானூறு 341-360","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 341 to 355 are magatpaal kaanchi: a chieftain refuses his daughter to kings, and the poet sees the town's groves, fords and walls about to be ruined by the war her beauty brings. Poems 356 to 360 are perunkaanchi: the burning ground outlives all rulers, so kings are told to give while they can."},"pn:19":{"book":"purananuru","num":19,"title":"புறநானூறு 361-380","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"Poems 361 to 380 move from death to the bard's reward. 361 to 367 are kaanchi counsel on impermanence: kings more than the sand have gone to the burning ground, so give, feast and act now (361 to 364, 366), or, in 365, the Earth herself weeps at being held by king after king. 368 to 373 are களவழி poems in which a drummer sings a king's battle as ploughing, sowing and threshing and asks for elephants as his share; 374 to 380 are kinai-drummers' praises of the givers Ay, Nalliyathan, Perunarkilli, Ilanchetchenni and the lord of Nanjil."},"pn:20":{"book":"purananuru","num":20,"title":"புறநானூறு 381-400","section":"புறநானூறு","theme":"The last twenty poems of the Purananuru are almost all bards' gate poems (கடைநிலை, இயன்மொழி, வாழ்த்தியல், பரிசில் விடை): a kinai or thadari drummer comes at dawn to a chief's gate, often driven by famine (Venus in the south), sings, and is answered by the same ritual of rags exchanged for fine cloth, toddy, meat and rice, after which he swears to sing no other patron. Their argument is that giving is the giver's glory and the bard's memory its return."}},"verses":{"pt:11":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":1,"t":["வரைமருள் புணரி வான்பிசிர் உடைய","வளிபாய்ந்(து) அட்ட துளங்குஇருங் கமஞ்சூல்","நளிஇரும் பரப்பின் மாக்கடல் முன்னி","அணங்(கு)உடை அவுணர் ஏமம் புணர்க்கும்","சூருடை முழுமுதல் தடிந்த பேரிசைக்","கடுஞ்சின விறல்வேள் களி(று)ஊர்ந் தாங்குச்","செவ்வாய் எஃகம் விலங்குநர் அறுப்ப","அருநிறம் திறந்த புண்உமிழ் குருதியின்","மணிநிற இருங்கழி நீர்நிறம் பெயர்ந்து","மனாலக் கலவை போல அரண்கொன்று","முரண்மிகு சிறப்பின் உயர்ந்த ஊக்கலை","பலர்மொசிந்(து) ஓம்பிய திரள்பூங் கடம்பின்","கடியுடை முழுமுதல் துமிய வேஎய்","வென்(று)எறி முழங்குபணை செய்த வெல்போர்","நாரரி நறவின் ஆர மார்பின்","போர்அடு தானைச் சேர லாத","மார்புமலி பைந்தார் ஓடையொடு விளங்கும்","வலன்உயர் மருப்பின் பழிதீர் யானைப்","பொலன்அணி எருத்தம் மேல்கொண்டு பொலிந்தநின்","பலர்புகழ் செல்வம் இனிதுகண் டிகுமே","கவிர்ததை சிலம்பின் துஞ்சும் கவரி","பரந்துஇலங்(கு) அருவியொடு நரந்தம் கனவும்","ஆரியர் துவன்றிய பேரிசை இமயம்","தென்னம் குமரியொ(டு) ஆயிடை","மன்மீக் கூறுநர் மறம்தபக் கடந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Cheralathan, whose army kills in war and who drinks strained toddy, that just as Murugan, reaching the great sea, cut down Sur at the root and rode his elephant, the king went to the sea, turned the dark backwater red with the blood of the foes his spears tore open, and felled at the root the guarded kadamba that many had tended, and so the poet delights to see his much-praised wealth as he rides his tusker with its gold-ornamented neck, the king who broke the valour of every ruler from the Himalaya of the Aryans to Kumari in the south.","n":"Line 5 (சூருடை முழுமுதல் தடிந்த) and line 13 (கடியுடை முழுமுதல் துமிய) are built on the same words, so the felling of the enemy's guardian kadamba is told as a repetition of the god's killing of Sur; the simile is the frame of the deed, not decoration. The red backwater like vermilion paste (line 10) is the only colour in the battle; the closing lines reach from the Himalaya to Kumari, the usual measure of a Tamil king's claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 11","lb":"PT11","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புணரி","புணரி",1,"pira","scene","waves"],["விறல்வேள்","வேள்",6,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["களி(று)ஊர்ந்","களிறு",6,"maa","simile",""],["இருங்கழி","கழி",9,"pira","scene","backwater"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",12,"maram","scene",""],["முழங்குபணை","பணை",14,"parai","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",15,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யானைப்","யானை",18,"maa","scene",""],["கவிர்ததை","கவிர்",21,"maram","scene",""],["கவரி","கவரி",21,"maa","scene","yak"],["நரந்தம்","நரந்தம்",22,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","சேர லாத",16],["hill","இமயம்",23],["river","குமரி",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:12":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":2,"t":["வயவர் வீழ வாளரின் மயக்கி","இடங்கவர் கடும்பின் அரசுதலை பனிப்பக்","கடம்புமுதல் தடிந்த கடுஞ்சின வேந்தே","தார்அணி எருத்தின் வாரல் வள்உகிர்","அரிமான் வழங்கும் சாரல் பிறமான்","தோடுகொள் இனநிரை நெஞ்(சு)அதிர்ந் தாங்கு","முரசுமுழங்கு நெடுநகர் அரசுதுயில் ஈயாது","மாதிரம் பனிக்கும் மறம்வீங்கு பல்புகழ்","கேட்டற்(கு) இனிதுநின் செல்வம் கேள்தொறும்","காண்டல் விருப்பொடு கமழும் குளவி","வாடாப் பைம்மயிர் இளைய வாடுநடை","அண்ணல் மழகளி(று) அரிஞிமி(று) ஓப்பும்","கன்றுபுணர் பிடிய குன்றுபல நீந்தி","வந்(து)அவண் நிறுத்த இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல்","தொல்பசி உழந்த பழங்கண் வீழ","எஃகுபோழ்ந்(து) அறுத்த வாள்நிணக் கொழுங்குறை","மைஊன் பெய்த வெண்நெல் வெண்சோறு","நனைஅமை கள்ளின் தேறலொடு மாந்தி","நீர்ப்படு பருந்தின் இருஞ்சிற(கு) அன்ன","நிலத்தின் சிதாஅர் களைந்த பின்றை","நூலாக் கலிங்கம் வால்அரைக் கொளீஇ","வணர்இரும் கதுப்பின் வாங்(கு)அமை மென்தோள்","வசைஇல் மகளிர் வயங்(கு)இழை அணிய","அமர்புமெய் ஆர்த்த சுற்றமொடு","நுகர்தற்(கு) இனிதுநின் பெரும்கலி மகிழ்வே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king who cut the kadamba at its root, making rival kings tremble, that as the herds of other beasts quake when the maned lion walks the slope, so kings in their drum-loud towns cannot sleep for his fame, which is sweet to hear; and that his feasting is sweet to share, for the poet's large hungry kin, brought over many hills where a young elephant fans off bees beside its mother, have eaten fat meat cut with the knife, white rice with mutton and clear toddy until their old hunger ended, put off rags like a wet kite's wing, wrapped on fine cloth, and seen their women adorned with jewels.","n":"The poem is built on two parallel predicates, கேட்டற்கு இனிது (line 9: sweet to hear) and நுகர்தற்கு இனிது (line 25: sweet to enjoy): the king's fame is a terror for kings and a feast for bards, and the same wealth is meant by both. தொல்பசி (line 15), an old, inherited hunger, makes the gift the end of a condition, not a meal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pasi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 12","lb":"PT12","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["அரிமான்","அரிமா",5,"maa","simile","lion"],["பிறமான்","மான்",5,"maa","simile","beasts"],["குளவி","குளவி",10,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["அரிஞிமி(று)","ஞிமிறு",12,"pira","scene",""],["மழகளி(று)","களிறு",12,"maa","scene",""],["பிடிய","பிடி",13,"maa","scene",""],["மைஊன்","ஊன்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",18,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",19,"pul","simile",""],["கலிங்கம்","கலிங்கம்",21,"pira","gift_food","cloth"],["வயங்(கு)இழை","இழை",23,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:13":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":3,"t":["தொறுத்தவயல் ஆரல்பிறழ்நவும்","ஏறுபொருதசெறு உழாதுவித்துநவும்","கரும்பின் பாத்திப் பூத்த நெய்தல்","இருங்கண் எருமையின் நிரைதடுக் குநவும்","கலிகெழு துணங்கை ஆடிய மருங்கின்","வளைதலை மூதா ஆம்பல் ஆர்நவும்","ஒலிதெங்கின் இமிழ்மருதின்","புனல்வாயில் பூம்பொய்கைப்","பாடல் சான்ற பயம்கெழு வைப்பின்","நாடுகவின் அழிய நாமம் தோற்றிக்","கூற்(று)அடூஉ நின்ற யாக்கை போல","நீசிவந்(து) இறுத்த நீர்அழி பாக்கம்","விரிபூங் கரும்பின் கழனி புல்எனத்","திரிகாய் விடத்தரொடு காருடை போகிக்","கவைத்தலைப் பேய்மகள் கழு(து)ஊர்ந்(து) இயங்க","ஊரிய நெருஞ்சி நீ(று)ஆடு பறந்தலைத்","தா(து)எரு மறுத்த கலிஅழி மன்றத்(து)","உள்ளம் அழிய ஊக்குநர் மிடல்தபுத்","துள்ளுநர் பனிக்கும் பாழா யினவே","காடே கடவுள் மேன புறவே","ஒள்ளிழை மகளிரொடு மள்ளர் மேன","ஆறே அவ்வனைத்(து) அன்றியும் ஞாலத்துக்","கூலம் பகர்நர் குடிபுறந் தராஅக்","குடிபுறம் தருநர் பாரம் ஓம்பி","அழல்சென்ற மருங்கின் வெள்ளி ஓடாது","மழைவேண்டு புலத்து மாரி நிற்ப","நோயொடு பசிஇகந்(து) ஒரீஇப்","பூத்தன்று பெருமநீ காத்த நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet contrasts the lands the king entered in anger, once rich watered country where bulls sowed unploughed fields, buffaloes waded among sugarcane and neythal, and old cows grazed on water-lilies by the dancing grounds, now a waste like a body Death has seized, where a ghoul-woman rides a demon over thorny scrub and the dust of empty commons, with his own guarded land, where forests are for the gods, pastures for warriors and their women, families are protected, Venus keeps its course, rain falls where it is wanted, and disease and hunger have left, so that the land flowers.","n":"The poem sets the same kind of country twice, destroyed and flourishing, and the hinge is the king's anger (நீ சிவந்து இறுத்த, line 12) against his guarding (நீ காத்த, line 28). Lines 25 and 26 take the sky as witness: Venus not straying and rain coming on demand are signs of good rule, not luck.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kedu","sengol","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 13","lb":"PT13","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["ஆரல்பிறழ்நவும்","ஆரல்",1,"pira","absent","eel"],["ஏறுபொருதசெறு","ஏறு",2,"maa","absent","bull"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","absent",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","absent",""],["எருமையின்","எருமை",4,"maa","absent",""],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",5,"yaazh","absent","dance"],["மூதா","ஆ",6,"maa","absent","cow"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",6,"maram","absent",""],["தெங்கின்","தெங்கு",7,"maram","absent",""],["மருதின்","மருது",7,"maram","absent",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",13,"maram","absent",""],["விடத்தரொடு","விடத்தர்",14,"maram","scene",""],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",15,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["நெருஞ்சி","நெருஞ்சி",16,"maram","scene",""],["கூலம்","கூலம்",23,"unavu","scene","grain"],["மாரி","மாரி",26,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:14":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":4,"t":["நிலம்நீர் வளிவிசும்(பு) என்ற நான்கின்","அளப்பரி யையே","நாள்கோள் திங்கள் ஞாயிறு கனைஅழல்","ஐந்(து)ஒருங்கு புணர்ந்த விளக்கத்(து) அனையை","போர்தலை மிகுத்த ஈர்ஐம் பதின்மரொடு","துப்புத்துறை போகிய துணி(வு)உடை யாண்மை","அக்குரன் அனைய கைவண் மையையே","அமர்கடந்து மலைந்த தும்பைப் பகைவர்","போர்பீ(டு) அழித்த செருப்புகல் முன்ப","கூற்றுவெகுண்டு வரினும் ஆற்றுமாற் றலையே","எழுமுடி கெழீஇய திருஞெமர் அகலத்து","நோன்புரித் தடக்கைச் சான்றோர் மெய்ம்மறை","வான்உறை மகளிர் நலன்இகல் கொள்ளும்","வயங்(கு)இழை கரந்த வண்டுபடு கதுப்பின்","ஒடுங்(கு)ஈர் ஓதிக் கொடுங்குழை கணவ","பலகளிற்றுத் தொழுதியொடு வெல்கொடி நுடங்கும்","படைஏர் உழவ பாடினி வேந்தே","இலங்குமணி மிடைந்த பொலங்கலத் திகிரிக்","கடல்அக வரைப்பின்இப் பொழில்முழு(து) ஆண்டநின்","முன்திணை முதல்வர் போல நின்றுநீ","கெடாஅ நல்லிசை நிலைஇத்","தவாஅ லியரோஇவ் வுலகமோ(டு) உடனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that he is as beyond measure as earth, water, wind and sky, as bright as the stars, planets, moon, sun and fire joined in one light, as open-handed as Akkuran who fought beside the hundred, unshakable even if Death came in anger, the shield of the worthy, husband of a queen whose beauty rivals the celestial women, ploughman whose plough is his army, king of the singing woman, and wishes that like the first kings of his line who ruled the whole sea-bounded earth he may stand with undying fame as long as this world.","n":"The opening counts the elements as four, not five, and gives the light its own list of five (stars, planets, moon, sun, fire); the king is matched to the sky's sum, not to one body in it. படைஏர் உழவ (line 17) turns the army into a plough, the war king pictured as a farmer. The poem says nothing of Akkuran beyond his fight among the hundred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","vendhan","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 14","lb":"PT14","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",14,"pira","scene",""],["பலகளிற்றுத்","களிறு",16,"maa","scene",""],["பாடினி","பாடினி",17,"seythi","scene","singer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:15":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":5,"t":["யாண்டுதலைப் பெயர வேண்டுபுலத்(து) இறுத்து","முனைஎரி பரப்பிய துன்னரும் சீற்றமொடு","மழைதவழ்பு தலைஇய மதில்மரம் முருக்கி","நிரை களிறுஒழுகிய நிரைய வெள்ளம்","பரந்(து)ஆடு கழங்(கு)அழி மன்மருங்(கு) அறுப்பக்","கொடிவிடு குரூஉப்புகை பிசிரக் கால்பொர","அழல்கவர் மருங்கின் உருஅறக் கெடுத்துத்","தொல்கவின் அழிந்த கண்அகன் வைப்பின்","வெண்பூ வேளையொடு பைஞ்சுரை கலித்துப்","பீர்இவர்பு பரந்த நீர்அறு நிறைமுதல்","சிவந்த காந்தள் முதல்சிதை மூதின்","புலவுவில் உழவின் புல்லாள் வழங்கும்","புல்லிலை வைப்பின் புலம்சிதை அரம்பின்","அறியா மையான் மறந்துதுப்(பு) எதிர்ந்தநின்","பகைவர் நாடும் கண்டுவந் திசினே","கடலவும் கல்லவும் யாற்றவும் பிறவும்","வளம்பல நிகழ்தரு நனந்தலை நன்நாட்டு","விழ(வு)அறு(பு) அறியா முழ(வு)இமிழ் மூதூர்க்","கொடுநிழல் பட்ட பொன்உடை நியமத்துச்","சீர்பெறு கலிமகிழ் இயம்பு முரசின்","வயவர் வேந்தே பரிசிலர் வெறுக்கை","தார்அணிந்(து) எழிலிய தொடிசிதை மருப்பின்","போர்வல் யானைச் சேர லாத","நீவா ழியர்இவ் வுலகத் தோர்க்(கு)என","உண்(டு)உரை மாறிய மழலை நாவின்","மென்சொல் கலப்பையர் திருந்துதொடை வாழ்த்த","வெய்துற(வு) அறியாது நந்திய வாழ்க்கைச்","செய்த மேவல் அமர்ந்த சுற்றமோ(டு)","ஒன்றுமொழிந்(து) அடங்கிய கொள்கை என்றும்","பதிபிழைப்(பு) அறியாது துய்த்தல் எய்தி","நிரையம் ஒரீஇய வேட்கைப் புரையோர்","மேயினர் உறையும் பலர்புகழ் பண்பின்","நீபுறந் தருதலின் நோய்இகந்(து) ஒரீஇய","யாணர்நன் நாடுங் கண்டுமதி மருண்டனென்","மண்உடை ஞாலத்து மன்னுயிர்க்(கு) எஞ்சா(து)","ஈத்துக்கை தண்டாக் கைகடும் துப்பின்","புரைவயின் புரைவயின் பெரிய நல்கி","ஏமம் ஆகிய சீர்கெழு விழவின்","நெடியோன் அன்ன நல்இசை","ஒடியா மைந்தநின் பண்புபல நயந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Cheralathan that he has seen the lands of the enemies who forgot his might and opposed him, where his army camped year after year, burned the outposts, broke the walls' trees and turned the old towns to waste, so that white-flowered velai, gourd and peer creepers spread over the dry tank-beds and old houses and only base men with stinking bows walk there; and that he has also seen with wonder the king's own good land of sea, hill and river, with its unfailing festivals, its drums, its blessing bards and the worthy who keep from hell and live free of distress under his care, and so he has come, loving his many qualities and his fame like Nediyon's.","n":"The poem is told as two sights, கண்டுவந் திசினே (line 15) and கண்டுமதி மருண்டனென் (line 34): the poet's eye witnesses both the ruin and the plenty, so the praise rests on what he saw, not on report. The guarded land is defined by its people (bards who bless the world, the worthy who shun hell) as much as by its riches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kedu","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 15","lb":"PT15","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கழங்(கு)அழி","கழங்கு",5,"pira","absent","game seeds"],["வேளையொடு","வேளை",9,"maram","scene",""],["பைஞ்சுரை","சுரை",9,"maram","scene",""],["பீர்இவர்பு","பீர்",10,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",11,"maram","absent",""],["புல்லாள்","புல்லாள்",12,"seythi","scene","robbers"],["முழ(வு)இமிழ்","முழவு",18,"parai","scene",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",20,"parai","scene",""],["யானைச்","யானை",23,"maa","scene",""],["கலப்பையர்","கலப்பையர்",26,"seythi","scene","bards"],["நெடியோன்","நெடியோன்",39,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:16":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":6,"t":["கோ(டு)உறழ்ந்(து) எடுத்த கொடுங்கண் இஞ்சி","நாடுகண் டன்ன கணைதுஞ்சு விலங்கல்","துஞ்சுமரக் குழாஅம் துவன்றிப் புனிற்றுமகள்","பூணா ஐயவி தூக்கிய மதில","நல்எழில் நெடும்புதவு முருக்கிக் கொல்லு(பு)","ஏனம் ஆகிய நுனைமுரி மருப்பின்","கடாஅம் வார்ந்து கடுஞ்சினம் பொத்தி","மரங்கொல் மழகளிறு முழங்கும் பாசறை","நீடினை ஆகலின் காண்குவந் திசினே","ஆறிய கற்பின் அடங்கிய சாயல்","ஊடினும் இனிய கூறும் இன்நகை","அமிர்துபொதி துவர்வாய் அமர்த்த நோக்கின்","சுடர்நுதல் அசைநடை உள்ளலும் உரியள்","பாயல் உய்யுமோ தோன்றல் தாவின்று","திருமணி பொருத திகழ்விடு பசும்பொன்","வயங்குகதிர் வயிரமோ(டு) உறழ்ந்துபூண் சுடர்வர","எழுமுடி கெழீஇய திருஞெமர் அகலத்துப்","புரையோர் உண்கண் துயிலின் பாயல்","பாலும் கொளாலும் வல்லோய்நின்","சாயன் மார்பு நனிஅலைத் தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Because the king has lingered long in his war camp, where young elephants with tusks blunted from breaking the tall gates of fortress walls hung with the ayyavi roar in musth, the poet has come to see him, and asks whether the bed of his queen, of settled chastity and gentle grace, who speaks sweetly even when she sulks and has every right to think of him, will escape its loneliness, since his soft chest, bright with gems, gold and diamonds and the garland of seven crowns, which could be the bed where her eyes sleep, now torments her.","n":"A puram poem that turns at line 10 to the queen's waiting: the same jewelled chest that wears the seven crowns' garland (line 17, as in 14 line 11) is her pillow and the cause of her pain. பூணா ஐயவி (line 4) separates the ayyavi of the siege from the white mustard a new mother wears, a pun on one word. Lines 14 and 18 to 19 are hard to construe, so the reading of the queen's lines is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","karpu","pirivu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 16","lb":"PT16","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",4,"pira","scene","siege device"],["மழகளிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","scene",""],["திருமணி","மணி",15,"pira","ornament",""],["வயிரமோ(டு)","வயிரம்",16,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:17":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":7,"t":["புரைவது நினைப்பின் புரைவதோ இன்றே","பொரிய தப்புநர் ஆயினும் பகைவர்","பணிந்துதிறை பகரக் கொள்ளுநை ஆதலின்","துளங்குபிசிர் உடைய மாக்கடல் நீக்கிக்","கடம்(பு)அறுத்(து) இயற்றிய வலம்படு வியன்பணை","ஆடுநர் பெயர்ந்துவந்(து) அரும்பலி தூஉய்க்","கடிப்புக் கண்உறூஉம் தொடித்தோள் இயவர்","அரணம் காணாது மாதிரம் துழைஇய","நனம்தலைப் பைஞ்ஞிலம் வருகஇந் நிழல்என","ஞாயிறு புகன்ற தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின்","அமிழ்துதிகழ் கருவிய கணமழை தலைஇக்","கடும்கால் கொட்கும் நன்பெரும் பரப்பின்","விசும்புதோய் வெண்குடை நுவலும்","பசும்பூண் மார்ப பாடினி வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king of the green ornament, king of the singing woman, that nothing can be found to match him, since he accepts the tribute of foes who bow even when they are at fault; his great victorious drum, made from the kadamba he cut down after driving back the sea, is beaten by armleted drummers after offerings are scattered, and his sky-touching white umbrella, under rain-clouds and wheeling winds, proclaims to the wide world that has found no other refuge: come to this shade.","n":"The drum is made of the enemy's felled guardian tree, so every beating of it repeats the victory of 11 and 12. The umbrella's call, வருக இந் நிழல் (line 9), makes the king's protection an open invitation rather than a conquest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arasu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 17","lb":"PT17","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["கடம்(பு)அறுத்(து)","கடம்பு",5,"maram","scene",""],["வியன்பணை","பணை",5,"parai","scene",""],["இயவர்","இயவர்",7,"seythi","scene","drummers"],["பசும்பூண்","பூண்",14,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:18":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":8,"t":["உண்மின் கள்ளே அடுமின் சோறே","எறிக திற்றி ஏற்றுமின் புழுக்கே","வருநர்க்கு வரையாது பொலம்கலம் தெளிர்ப்ப","இருள்வணர் ஒலிவரும் புரிஅவிழ் ஐம்பால்","ஏந்துகோட்(டு) அல்குல் முகிழ்நகை மடவரல்","கூந்தல் விறலியர் வழங்குக அடுப்பே","பெற்ற(து) உதவுமின் தப்(பு)இன்று பின்னும்","மன்உயிர் அழிய யாண்டுபல துளக்கி","மண்உடை ஞாலம் புர(வு)எதிர் கொண்ட","தண்இயல் எழிலி தலையாது மாறி","மாரி பொய்க்குவ(து) ஆயினும்","சேர லாதன் பொய்யலன் நசையே."],"p":"","r":"Drink the toddy, cook the rice, cut the meat, set the stew on the fire, give to all who come without limit while the gold vessels clink, let the dancing women with dark curling hair serve from the hearths, and share what you have received without fault, the poet tells the bards, for even if the cloud that has taken on the care of the earth fails for many years and the rain lies, Cheralathan will not betray their hope.","n":"The commands are to the bards, not the king: the gift is to be passed on at once (பெற்றது உதவுமின், line 7), so the king's giving becomes the bards' hospitality. The rain is not a model he equals but one he outdoes, since rain can fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","mazhai","parisil","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 18","lb":"PT18","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கள்ளே","கள்",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சோறே","சோறு",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["திற்றி","திற்றி",2,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["புழுக்கே","புழுக்கு",2,"unavu","gift_food","stew"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",6,"seythi","scene",""],["மாரி","மாரி",11,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","சேர லாதன்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:19":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":9,"t":["கொள்ளை வல்சிக் கவர்கால் கூளியர்","கல்உடை நெடுநெறி போழ்ந்துசுரன் அறுப்ப","ஒண்பொறிக் கழல்கால் மாறா வயவர்","தின்பிணி எஃகம் புலிஉறை கழிப்பச்","செங்கள விருப்பொடு கூலம் முற்றிய","உருவச் செந்தினை குருதியொடு தூஉய்","மண்ணுறு முரசம் கண்பெயர்த்(து) இயவர்","கடிப்(பு)உடை வலத்தர் தொடித்தோள் ஓச்ச","வம்புகளை(வு) அறியாச் சுற்றமோ(டு) உடம்புதெரிந்(து)","அவ்வினை மேவலை ஆகலின்","எல்லும் நனிஇருந்(து) எல்லிப் பெற்ற","அரிதுபெறு பாயல்சிறுமகி ழானும்","கனவினுள் உறையும் பெருஞ்சால்(பு) ஒடுங்கிய","நாணுமலி யாக்கை வாள்நுதல் அரிவைக்(கு)","யார்கொல் அளியை","இனம்தோ(டு) அகல ஊருடன் எழுந்து","நிலம்கண் வாட நாஞ்சில் கடிந்துநீ","வாழ்தல் ஈயா வளன்அறு பைதிரம்","அன்ன ஆயின பழனம் தோறும்","அழல்மலி தாமரை ஆம்பலொடு மலர்ந்து","நெல்லின் செறுவில் நெய்தல் பூப்ப","அரிநர் கொய்வாள் மடங்க அறைநர்","தீம்பிழி எந்திரம் பத்தல் வருந்த","இன்றோ அன்றோ தொன்றோர் காலை","நல்லமன் அளிய தாம்எனச் சொல்லிக்","காணுநர் கைபுடைத்(து) இரங்க","மாணா மாட்சிய மாண்டன பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that, with his plundering goblin-like scouts cutting through the stony road, his warriors drawing spears from tiger-skin sheaths, and his drummers beating the war drum after an offering of red millet mixed with blood, he so loves the work of war with his companions who never take off their armour that he grants no grace even to his modest, bright-browed wife, who lives with him only in dreams on the rare night he sleeps; and that the lands he ravaged, where he forbade the plough and the people fled, once had lotus and water-lily in every pond, neythal in the paddy, reapers' sickles bending and sugar presses groaning, so that onlookers clap their hands in pity, saying these were good only yesterday, and many fine lands have come to nothing.","n":"The colophon calls this a song of the gift (பரிசிற்றுறை), but the poem asks for nothing: it sets the king's love of war against two losses, his wife's and the ravaged farmers'. The onlookers' words (lines 24 and 25) are the poem's only voice of grief. The syntax of lines 9 to 15 (who is pitied, and by whom) is hard, and the reading of the wife's lines is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu","naadu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 19","lb":"PT19","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"பரிசிற்றுறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கூளியர்","கூளியர்",1,"seythi","scene","scouts"],["புலிஉறை","புலி",4,"maa","ornament",""],["செந்தினை","தினை",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",7,"parai","scene",""],["இயவர்","இயவர்",7,"seythi","scene","drummers"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",17,"seythi","absent","plough"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",20,"maram","absent",""],["ஆம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",20,"maram","absent",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",21,"unavu","absent",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",21,"maram","absent",""],["எந்திரம்","எந்திரம்",23,"seythi","absent","sugar press"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:20":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":10,"t":["நும்கோ யார்என வினவின் எம்கோ","இருமுந்நீர்த் துருத்தியுள்","முரணியோர்த் தலைச்சென்று","கடம்புமுதல் தடிந்த கடுஞ்சின முன்பின்","நெடுஞ்சேர லாதன் வாழ்கஅவன் கண்ணி","வாய்ப்(பு)அறி யலனே வெயில்துகள் அனைத்தும்","மாற்றோர் தேஎத்து மாறிய வினையே","கண்ணின் உவந்து நெஞ்(சு)அவிழ்பு அறியா","நண்ணார் தேஎத்தும் பொய்ப்(பு)அறி யலனே","கனவினும், ஒன்னார் தேய ஓங்கி நடந்து","படியோர்த் தேய்த்து வடிமணி இரட்டும்","கடாஅ யானைக் கணநிரை அலற","வியல்இரும் பரப்பின் மாநிலம் கடந்து","புலவர் ஏத்த ஓங்குபுகழ் நிறீஇ","விரிஉளை மாவும் களிறும் தேரும்","வயிரியர் கண்ணுளர்க்(கு) ஓம்பாது வீசிக்","கடிமிளைக் குண்டுகிடங்கின்","நெடுமதில் நிலைஞாயில்","அம்(பு)உடை யார்எயில் உள்அழித்(து) உண்ட","அடாஅ அடுபகை அட்டுமலர் மார்பன்","எமர்க்கும் பிறர்க்கும் யாவர் ஆயினும்","பரிசில் மாக்கள் வல்லார் ஆயினும்","கொடைக்கடன் அமர்ந்த கோடா நெஞ்சினன்","மன்உயிர் அழிய யாண்டுபல மாறித்","தண்இயல் எழிலி தலையா(து) ஆயினும்","வயிறுபசி கூர ஈயலன்","வயிறும்ஆ(சு) இலீயர்அவன் ஈன்ற தாயே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask who our king is, says the poet, he is Neduncheralathan, who went against his foes on an island in the sea and cut down the kadamba at its root: may his garland live; his deeds against enemies never failed by a mote of sunlit dust, he is false neither in enemy land nor even in dreams, he crushed the proud and stormed moated, walled forts, he scatters horses, elephants and chariots to drummers and players without stint, and with a heart that does not swerve in the duty of giving, to our people or others, skilled or unskilled, he will not let a belly go hungry even if the rains fail for many years: may the womb of the mother who bore him be free of blemish.","n":"The poem is shaped as an answer to a stranger's question (நும்கோ யார், line 1), the form a bard uses to send another to his patron. The blessing falls not on the king but on his mother's womb (line 27), so the praise ends by honouring where he came from. Truthfulness is claimed even in dreams (கனவினும், line 10).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vaaimai","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 20","lb":"PT20","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""],["மாவும்","மா",15,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["களிறும்","களிறு",15,"maa","gift_food",""],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",16,"seythi","scene","drummers"],["கண்ணுளர்க்(கு)","கண்ணுளர்",16,"seythi","scene","players"]],"en":[["king","நெடுஞ்சேர லாதன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:patikam:2":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":11,"t":["மன்னிய பெரும்புகழ் மறுஇல் வாய்மொழி","இன்இசை முரசின் உதியஞ் சேரற்கு","வெளியன் வேண்மாள் நல்லினி ஈன்றமகன்","அமைவரல் அருவி இமையம் வில்பொறித்(து)","இமிழ்கடல் வேலித் தமிழகம் விளங்கத்","தன்கோல் நிறீஇத் தகைசால் சிறப்பொடு","பேர்இசை மரபின் ஆரியர் வணக்கி","நயன்இல் வன்சொல் யவனர்ப் பிணித்து","நெய்தலைப் பெய்து கைபின் கொளீஇ","அருவிலை நன்கலம் வயிரமொடு கொண்டு","பெருவிறல் மூதூர்த் தந்துபிறர்க்(கு) உதவி","அமையார்த் தேய்த்த அணங்(கு)உடை நோன்தாள்","இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதனைக்","குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Imayavaramban Neduncheralathan, son of Udhiyan Cheral of the sweet-sounding drum by Nallini of the Veliyan Vel line, carved his bow on the Himalaya, set up his rule so that the sea-fenced Tamil land shone, made the Aryans bow, bound the harsh-spoken Yavanas, poured ghee on their heads and tied their hands behind them, took their costly vessels and diamonds to his old city and gave to others, and that Kumattur Kannanar sang this ten.","n":"The preface supplies what the songs leave out: the king's parents, the Himalayan bow and the humiliation of the Yavanas. The seized treasure is at once given away (பிறர்க்கு உதவி, line 11), the same pairing of conquest and gift as in 12 and 20.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","maram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-14","lb":"PTpatikam:1-14","poet":"குமட்டூர்க் கண்ணனார்","pat":"இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""],["நெய்தலைப்","நெய்",9,"unavu","scene","ghee"]],"en":[["king","உதியஞ் சேரற்கு",2],["hill","இமையம்",4],["king","இமையவரம்பன் நெடுஞ்சேரலாதனை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:1"},"pt:21":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":12,"t":["சொல்பெயர் நாட்டம் கேள்வி நெஞ்சம்என்(று)","ஐந்(து)உடன் போற்றி அவைதுணை ஆக","எவ்வம் சூழாது விளங்கிய கொள்கைக்","காலை அன்ன சீர்சால் வாய்மொழி","உருகெழு மரபின் கடவுள் பேணியர்","கொண்ட தீயின் சுடர்எழு தோறும்","விரும்புமெய் பரந்த பெரும்பெயர் ஆவுதி","வருநர் வரையார் வார வேண்டி","விருந்துகண் மாறா(து) உணீஇய பாசவர்","ஊனத்(து) அழித்த வால்நிணக் கொழும்குறை","குய்யிடு தோறும் ஆனா(து) ஆர்ப்பக்","கடல்ஒலி கொண்டு செழுநகர் நடுவண்","அடுமை எழுந்த அடுநெய் ஆவுதி","இரண்(டு)உடன் கமழும் நாற்றமொடு வானத்து","நிலைபெறு கடவுளும் விழைதகப் பேணி","ஆர்வளம் பழுனிய ஐயம்தீர் சிறப்பின்","மாரிஅம் கள்ளின் போர்வல் யானைப்","போர்ப்(பு)உறு முரசம் கறங்க ஆர்ப்புச்சிறந்து","நன்கலம் தரூஉம் மண்படு மார்ப","முல்லைக் கண்ணிப் பல்ஆன் கோவலர்","புல்உடை வியன்புலம் பல்ஆ பரப்பிக்","கல்உயர் கடத்(து)இடைக் கதிர்மணி பெறூஉம்","மிதிஅல் செருப்பின் பூழியர் கோவே","குவியல் கண்ணி மழவர் மெய்ம்மறை","பல்பயம் தழீஇய பயம்கெழு நெடுங்கோட்டு","நீர்அறல் மருங்கு வழிப்படாப் பாகுடிப்","பார்வல் கொக்கின் பரிவேட்(பு) அஞ்சாச்","சீர்உடைத் தேஎத்த முனைகெட விலங்கிய","நேர்உயர் நெடுவரை அயிரைப் பொருந","யாண்டுபிழைப் பறியாது பயமழை சுரந்து","நோயின் மாந்தர்க்(கு) ஊழி ஆக","மண்ணா வாயின் மணம்கமழ் கொண்டு","கார்மலர் கமழும் தாழ்இரும் கூந்தல்","ஒரீஇயின போல விரவுமலர் நின்று","திருமுகத்(து) அலமரும் பெருமதர் மழைக்கண்","அலங்கிய காந்தள் இலங்குநீர் அழுவத்து","வேய்உறழ் பணைத்தோள் இவளோ(டு)","ஆயிர வெள்ளம் வாழிய பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the Chera who, guided by the five disciplines of the learned and speaking truth as clear as daybreak, keeps two fires going at once, the oblation of the sacrifice for the gods and the roaring kitchen where fat meat is fried without stint for every guest, so that both smells rise and please even the gods in the sky; he hails him as lord of the Puzhiyar whose cowherds find gems on the rocky pasture, shield of the Mazhavar and lord of the hill Ayirai, and wishes him, with his wife of fragrant hair and wandering rain-cool eyes, countless ages of life while the rains never fail.","n":"Lines 7 and 13 call both fires ஆவுதி: the sacrificial ghee and the cooking fat of the guests' kitchen are the same kind of offering, and line 14 has them smell together (இரண்டு உடன் கமழும்). The poem ends not on the king's war but on his queen, a rare domestic close for a decade poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aaguthi","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 21","lb":"PT21","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பாசவர்","பாசவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["கொழும்குறை","குறை",10,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["அடுநெய்","நெய்",13,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",17,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யானைப்","யானை",17,"maa","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",18,"parai","scene",""],["முல்லைக்","முல்லை",20,"maram","ornament",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",20,"seythi","scene",""],["பல்ஆ","ஆ",21,"maa","scene","cow"],["கதிர்மணி","மணி",22,"pira","scene","gem"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",27,"pul","scene",""],["கார்மலர்","மலர்",33,"maram","ornament",""],["விரவுமலர்","மலர்",34,"maram","simile",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",36,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்","வேய்",37,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","பூழியர் கோவே",23],["hill","அயிரைப்",29]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:22":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":13,"t":["சினனே காமம் கழிகண் ணோட்டம்","அச்சம் பொய்ச்சொல் அன்புமிகு வுடைமை","தெறல்கடு மையொடு பிறவும்இவ் வுலகத்(து)","அறம்தெரி திகிரிக்கு வழியடை யாகும்","தீதுசேண் இகந்து நன்றுமிகப் புரிந்து","கடலும் கானமும் பலபயம் உதவப்","பிறர்பிறர் நலியாது வேற்றுப்பொருள் வெஃகாது","மைஇல் அறிவினர் செவ்விதின் நடந்துதம்","அமர்துணைப் பிரியாது பாத்(து)உண்டு மாக்கள்","மூத்த யாக்கையொடு பிணிஇன்று கழிய","ஊழி உய்த்த உரவோர் உம்பல்","பொன்செய் கணிச்சித் திண்பிணி உடைத்துச்","சிரறுசில ஊறிய நீர்வாய்ப் பத்தல்","கயிறுகுறு முகவை மூயின மொய்க்கும்","ஆகெழு கொங்கர் நா(டு)அகப் படுத்த","வேல்கெழு தானை வெருவரு தோன்றல்","உளைப்பொலிந்த மா","இழைப்பொலிந்த களிறு","வம்புபரந்த தேர்","அமர்க்(கு)எதிர்ந்த புகல்மறவ ரொடு","துஞ்சுமரம் துவன்றிய மலர்அகன் பறந்தலை","ஓங்குநிலை வாயிதூங்குபு தகைத்த","வில்லிசை மாட்டிய விழுச்சீர் ஐயவிக்","கடிமிளைக் குண்டுகிடங்கின்","நெடுமதில் நிரைப்பதணத்","தண்ணலம் பெருங்கோட்(டு) அகப்பா எறிந்த","பொன்புனை உழிஞை வெல்போர்க் குட்டுவ","போர்த்(து)எறிந்த பறையாற் புனல்செறுக் குநரும்","நீர்த்தரு பூசலின் அம்(பு)அழிக்கு நரும்","ஒலித்தலை விழவின் மலியும் யாணர்","நாடுகெழு தண்பனை சீறினை ஆதலின்","குடதிசை மாய்ந்து குணம்முதல் தோன்றிப்","பாய்இருள் அகற்றும் பயம்கெழு பண்பின்","ஞாயிறு கோடா நன்பகல் அமயத்துக்","கவலை வெள்நரி கூஉம்முறை பயிற்றிக்","கழல்கண் கூகைக் குழறுகுரல் பாணிக்","கருங்கண் பேய்மகள் வழங்கும்","பெரும்பாழ் ஆகுமன் அளிய தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Kuttuvan, descendant of rulers who kept away anger, desire, excessive indulgence, fear, lies, too much love and harsh punishment, the obstacles to the wheel of dharma, and so led an age when no one harmed or coveted, couples were not parted, food was shared and people died old and free of disease, that he who took the Kongar's land of cows and deep rock-cut wells and broke the guarded Agappa fort with its moat, walls and bow-engines has turned his anger on a festive watered country where men dam floods to the drum, so that, pitiably, it will become a great waste where at noon the jackal howls, the owl hoots and the demoness walks.","n":"The list of faults in lines 1-4 is of excesses on both sides: harshness (தெறல் கடுமை) sits beside excessive kindness (கழிகண்ணோட்டம்) and excess of love, so the wheel of dharma is blocked by too much mercy as well as too little. The poem then praises the lineage for this restraint and ends with the king's anger laying a land waste; the poet's அளிய (pitiable) is his only comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kutram","kedu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 22","lb":"PT22","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["முகவை","முகவை",14,"seythi","scene","bucket"],["ஆகெழு","ஆ",15,"maa","scene","cow"],["மா","மா",17,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறு","களிறு",18,"maa","scene",""],["உழிஞை","உழிஞை",27,"maram","ornament",""],["பறையாற்","பறை",28,"parai","scene",""],["வெள்நரி","நரி",35,"maa","scene",""],["கூகைக்","கூகை",36,"pul","scene","owl"],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",37,"deyvam","scene","demoness"]],"en":[["town","அகப்பா",26],["king","குட்டுவ",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:23":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":14,"t":["அலந்தலை உன்னத்(து) அங்கவடு பொருந்திச்","சிதடி கரையப் பெருவறம் கூர்ந்து","நிலம்பை(து) அற்ற புலம்கெடு காலையும்","வாங்குபு தகைத்த கலப்பையர் ஆங்கண்","மன்றம் போந்து மறுகுசிறை பாடும்","வயிரிய மாக்கள் கடும்பசி நீங்கப்","பொன்செய் புனைஇழை ஒலிப்பப் பெரி(து)உவந்து","நெஞ்சுமலி உவகையர் உண்டுமலிந்(து) ஆடச்","சிறுமகி ழானும் பெருங்கலம் வீசும்","போர்அடு தானைப் பொலந்தார்க் குட்டுவ","நின்நயந்து வருவேம் கண்டனம் புல்மிக்கு","வழங்குநர் அற்(று)என மருங்குகெடத் தூர்ந்து","பெருங்கவின் அழிந்த ஆற்ற ஏறுபுணர்ந்(து)","அண்ணல் மரைஆ அமர்ந்(து)இனி(து) உறையும்","விண்உயர் வைப்பின கா(டு)ஆ யினநின்","மைந்துமலி பெரும்புகழ் அறியார் மலைந்த","போர்எதிர் வேந்தர் தார்அழிந்(து) ஒராலின்","மரு(து)இமிழ்ந்(து) ஓங்கிய நளிஇரும் பரப்பின்","மணல்மலி பெருந்துறைத் ததைந்த காஞ்சியொடு","முருக்குத்தாழ்(பு) எழிலிய நெருப்புறழ் அடைகரை","நந்து நாரையொடு செவ்வரி உகளும்","கழனி வாயிற் பழனப் படப்பை","அழல்மருள் பூவின் தாமரை வளைமகள்","குறாஅது மலர்ந்த ஆம்பல்","அறாஅ யாணர்அவர் அகன்தலை நாடே"],"p":"","r":"The bards tell Kuttuvan of the golden garland, who even in a drought when cicadas cry on the dead unnam tree and the earth has lost its green ends the hunger of the street-singing musicians and throws great jewels even at small pleasures, that on their way to him they saw the lands of the kings who fought him and fled: once watered country of marutham trees, kaanchi-lined fords, fire-red murukku, conches, herons and lotus fields, they have become sky-high forest where grass blocks the untravelled roads and the wild cow lives happily with her bull.","n":"The drought of lines 1-3 at home is answered by the king's giving, while the enemy's land, which had no drought, is ruined by war; the two halves weigh natural against man-made ruin. The wild cow living happily with her bull is the poem's sign that the forest has truly returned: pleasure has passed from people to beasts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","kedu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 23","lb":"PT23","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["உன்னத்(து)","உன்னம்",1,"maram","absent",""],["சிதடி","சிதடி",2,"pira","scene","cicada"],["வயிரிய","வயிரியர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["பெருங்கலம்","கலம்",9,"pira","gift_food","jewel"],["மரைஆ","மரை ஆ",14,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["மரு(து)","மருது",18,"maram","absent",""],["காஞ்சியொடு","காஞ்சி",19,"maram","absent",""],["முருக்குத்","முருக்கு",20,"maram","absent",""],["நந்து","நந்து",21,"pira","absent","snail"],["நாரையொடு","நாரை",21,"pul","absent",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",23,"maram","absent",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",24,"maram","absent",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவ",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:24":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":15,"t":["நெடுவயின் ஒளிறு மின்னுப்பரந்(து) ஆங்குப்","புலிஉறை கழித்த புலவுவாய் எஃகம்","ஏவல் ஆடவர் வலன்உயர்த்(து) ஏந்தி","ஆர்அரண் கடந்த தார்அரும் தகைப்பின்","பீடுகொள் மாலைப் பெரும்படைத் தலைவ","ஓதல் வேட்டல் அவைபிறர்ச் செய்தல்","ஈதல் ஏற்றல்என்(று) ஆறுபுரிந்(து) ஒழுகும்","அறம்புரி அந்தணர் வழிமொழிந்(து) ஒழுகி","ஞாலம் நின்வழி ஒழுகப் பாடல்சான்று","நா(டு)உடன் விளங்கும் நாடா நல்லிசைத்","திருந்திய இயல்மொழித் திருந்(து)இழை கணவ","குலைஇழி(பு) அறியாச் சாபத்து வயவர்","அம்புகளை(வு) அறியாத் தூங்குதுளங்(கு) இருக்கை","இடாஅ ஏணி இயல்அறைக் குருசில்","நீர்நிலம் தீவளி விசும்போ(டு) ஐந்தும்","அளந்துகடை அறியினும் அளப்பரும் குரையைநின்","வளம்வீங்கு பெருக்கம் இனிதுகண் டிகுமே","உண்மருந்(து) இன்மரும் வரைகோள் அறியாது","குரைத்தொடி மழுகிய உலக்கை வயின்தோ(று)","அடைச்சேம்(பு) எழுந்த ஆ(டு)று மடாவின்","எஃ(கு)உறச் சிவந்த ஊனத்(து) யாவரும்","கண்டுமதி மருளும் வாடாச் சொன்றி","வயங்குகதிர் விரிந்து வான்அகம் சுடர்வர","வறிதுவடக்(கு) இறைஞ்சிய சீர்கால் வெள்ளி","பயங்கெழு பொழுதோ(டு) ஆநியம் நிற்பக்","கலிழும் கருவியொடு கையுற வணங்கி","மன்னுயிர் புரைஇய வலன்ஏர்(பு) இரங்கும்","கொண்டல் தண்தளிக் கமம்சூல் மாமழை","கார்எதிர் பருவம் மறப்பினும்","பேரா யாணர்த்தால் வாழ்கநின் வளனே"],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the leader of a great army whose warriors raise spears drawn from tiger-skin sheaths like spreading lightning, who conducts himself by the word of the Brahmins devoted to dharma who keep the six duties of learning, sacrificing, having others do both, giving and receiving, so that the world walks his way; husband of the queen of refined speech, he is harder to measure than the five elements, and the poet has seen his swelling wealth: an unfailing rice for all comers, pounded by pestles worn ringless, with meat cut by knives in the great pots, which all marvel at, so that even if the rain-cloud forgot its season when Venus stands to the north, his wealth would keep its yield.","n":"The king's dharma is deference: he walks by the Brahmins' word and the world walks by his (lines 8-9). The closing claim that his wealth outlasts a failed monsoon makes royal plenty independent of the rain that the ethics books tie to just rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","selvam","eegai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 24","lb":"PT24","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["புலிஉறை","புலி",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["உலக்கை","உலக்கை",19,"seythi","scene",""],["அடைச்சேம்(பு)","சேம்பு",20,"maram","scene","colocasia"],["ஊனத்(து)","ஊன்",21,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["சொன்றி","சொன்றி",22,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["மாமழை","மழை",28,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:25":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":16,"t":["மாஆ டியபுலன் நாஞ்சில் ஆடா;","கடாஅம் சென்னிய கடுங்கண் யானை","இனம்பரந்த புலம் வளம்பரப்(பு) அறியா","நின்படைஞர், சேர்ந்த மன்றம் கழுதை போகி","நீ, உடன்றோர் மன்எயில் தோட்டி வையா","கடுங்கால் ஒற்றலின் சுடர்சிறந்(து) உருத்துப்","பசும்பிசிர் ஒள்அழல் ஆடிய மருங்கின்","ஆண்தலை வழங்கும் கான்உணங்கு கடுநெறி","முனைஅகன் பெரும்பாழ் ஆக மன்னிய","உரும்உறழ்(பு) இரங்கும் முரசிற் பெருமலை","வரைஇழி அருவியின் ஒளிறுகொடி நுடங்கக்","கடும்பரிக் கதழ்சிற(கு) அகைப்பநீ","நெடுந்தேர் ஓட்டியபிறர் அகன்தலை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that in the wide lands of others where he drove his tall chariot, its banners rippling like a waterfall off a mountain and its drum roaring like thunder, the fields trodden by his horses are no longer ploughed, the ground where his musth elephants spread knows no harvest, donkeys wander the commons his soldiers camped in, fire fanned by the wind has danced over the land, and the dried forest road where the owl-headed bird roams has become a great waste.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 25","lb":"PT25","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மா","மா",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",1,"seythi","absent","plough"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["கழுதை","கழுதை",4,"maa","scene","donkey"],["ஆண்தலை","ஆண்டலை",8,"pul","scene","owl"],["முரசிற்","முரசு",10,"parai","scene",""],["அருவியின்","அருவி",11,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:26":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":17,"t":["தேஎர் பரந்தபுலம் ஏஎர் பரவா","களி(று)ஆ டியபுலம் நாஞ்சில் ஆடா","மத்(து)உர றியமனை இன்னியம் இமிழா","ஆங்குப், பண்டுநற்(கு) அறியுநர் செழுவளம் நினைப்பின்","நோகோ யானே நோதக வருமே","பெயல்மழை புரவின்(று) ஆகிவெய்(து) உற்று","வலம்இன்(று) அம்ம காலையது பண்(பு)எனக்","கண்பனி மலிர்நிறை தாங்கிக் கைபுடையூ","மெலிவுடை நெஞ்சினர் சிறுமை கூரப்","பீர்இவர் வேலிப் பாழ்மனை நெருஞ்சிக்","கா(டு)உறு கடுநெறி யாக மன்னிய","முரு(கு)உடன்று கறுத்த கலிஅழி மூதூர்","உரும்பில் கூற்றத்(து) அன்னநின்","திருந்துதொழில் வயவர் சீறிய நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that in the land the king's warriors, like a Death that nothing afflicts, have raged against, where chariots spread no plough goes, where elephants trod no plough moves, and houses that echoed with churning hear no music, he grieves when he thinks of the wealth its old inhabitants knew: they, holding back tears and beating their hands, say the rain has failed and the heat has come and that this is the nature of the times, while their fenced homes turn to ruins overgrown with peerkku and nerunji and the old town loses its festivity.","n":"The victims blame the season (காலையது பண்பு, line 7), not the king, while the poet knows the cause and says so in a praise poem; his நோகோ யானே is the most open grief for the conquered in the decade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","naadu","kaalam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 26","lb":"PT26","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களி(று)","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",2,"seythi","absent","plough"],["மத்(து)","மத்து",3,"seythi","absent","churn"],["இன்னியம்","இன்னியம்",3,"yaazh","absent",""],["பீர்","பீர்",10,"maram","scene",""],["நெருஞ்சிக்","நெருஞ்சி",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:27":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":18,"t":["சிதைந்தது மன்றநீ சிவந்தனை நோக்கலின்","தொடர்ந்த குவளைத் தூநெறி அடைச்சி","அலர்ந்த ஆம்பல் அகமடி வையர்","சுரியல்அம் சென்னிப் பூஞ்செய் கண்ணி","அரியல் ஆர்கையர் இனிதுகூ டியவர்","துறைநணி மருதம் ஏறித் தெறுமார்","எல்வளை மகளிர் தெள்விளி இசைப்பின்","பழனக் காவில் பசுமயில் ஆலும்","பொய்கை வாயில் புனல்பொரு புதவின்","நெய்தல் மரபின் நிரைகள் செறுவின்","வல்வாய் உருளி கதும்என மண்ட","அள்ளல் பட்டுத் துள்ளூபு துரப்ப","நல்எருதும் முயலும் அளறுபோகு விழுமத்துச்","சாகாட் டாளர் கம்பலை அல்லது","பூசல் அறியா நன்னாட்(டு)","யாணர் அறாஅக் காமரு கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that surely it is ruined, since he looked at it in anger: the desirable beauty of a good land of unfailing yield, where men wearing kuvalai and ambal and drinking toddy climb the marutham by the ford to dive, peacocks dance in the field groves when bangled women call, and carts sink in the mud of the neythal-lined fields so that the oxen strain, and no uproar was known but the shouting of the carters.","n":"Only the first line tells of ruin; the other fifteen describe the land as it was, so the king's anger is measured by what it destroyed. The only 'uproar' of the old land is carts stuck in rich mud, the figure the next poem uses for the king's own country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kedu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 27","lb":"PT27","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குவளைத்","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["அரியல்","அரியல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["பசுமயில்","மயில்",8,"pul","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",10,"maram","scene",""],["எருதும்","எருது",13,"maa","scene",""],["சாகாட் டாளர்","சாகாட்டாளர்",14,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:28":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":19,"t":["திருவுடைத்(து) அம்ம பெருவிறல் பகைவர்","பைங்கண் யானைப் புணர்நிரை துமிய","உரம்துரந்(து) எறிந்த கறைஅடிக் கழல்கால்","கடுமா மறவர் கதழ்தொடை மறப்ப","இளைஇனிது தந்து விளைவுமுட்(டு) உறாது","புலம்பா உறையுள் நீணதொழில் ஆற்றலின்","விடுநிலக் கரம்பை விடர்அளை நிறையக்","கோடை நீடக் குன்றம் புல்லென","அருவி அற்ற பெருவறல் காலையும்","நிவந்துகரை இழிதரு நனம்தலைப் பேரியாற்றுச்","சீர்உடை வியன்புலம் வாய்பரந்து மிகீஇயர்","உவலை சூடி உருத்துவரு மலிர்நிறைச்","செந்நீர்ப் பூசல் அல்லது","வெம்மை அரிதுநின் அகன்தலை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the king's wide land is rich indeed: since he guards it so well that his warriors, who broke the paired ranks of the enemy's elephants, forget their quick archery and the harvest never fails, even in a great drought when summer lingers, the hills turn pale and the falls run dry, the flood of the great river, crowned with straw, overflows its banks into the fields, and apart from the uproar of that red water no heat is known there.","n":"The war is present only as something forgotten (கதழ்தொடை மறப்ப): peace at home is the warriors' idleness. Line 6 (நீணதொழில்) looks corrupt, so the cause clause is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","sengol","mazhai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 28","lb":"PT28","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"நாடுவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["நாடு வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["கடுமா","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["குன்றம்","குன்றம்",8,"pira","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",9,"pira","absent",""],["உவலை","உவலை",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["river","பேரியாற்றுச்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:29":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":20,"t":["அவல்எறிந்த உலக்கை வாழைச் சேர்த்தி","வளைக்கை மகளிர் வள்ளை கொய்யும்","முடந்தை நெல்லின் விளைவயல் பரந்த","தடந்தாள் நாரை இரிய அயிரைக்","கொழுமீன் ஆர்கைய மரந்தொறும் குழாஅலின்","வெண்கை மகளிர் வெண்குரு(கு) ஓப்பும்","அழியா விழவின் இழியாத் திவவின்","வயிரிய மாக்கள் பண்அமைத்(து) எழீஇ","மன்ற நண்ணி மறுகுசிறை பாடும்","அகன்கண் வைப்பின் ஆடுமன் அளிய","விரவுவேறு கூலமொடு குருதி வேட்ட","மயிர்புதை மாக்கண் கடிய கழற","அமர்கோள் நேர்இகந்(து) ஆர்எயில் கடக்கும்","பெரும்பல் யானைக் குட்டுவன்","வரம்பில் தானை பரவா ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the broad settlements, pitiably, used to flourish before the boundless army of Kuttuvan of many elephants spread over them, he whose fierce drum, fed with blood and mixed grain, calls him past the rules of battle to take hard forts: there bangled women rested their pestles against the plantains to pluck vallai, herons fat on ayirai fish crowded the trees above the ripe paddy and were chased off by the women, and at unceasing festivals the bards tuned their lutes and sang at the street corners.","n":"The whole description is governed by the past-and-gone particle மன் (ஆடுமன், line 10) and by ஊங்கே, 'before', in the last line, so the poem is a portrait of a land as it was until the army came, not a picture of it now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","naadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 29","lb":"PT29","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வாழைச்","வாழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",4,"pul","scene",""],["அயிரைக்","அயிரை",4,"pira","scene","fish"],["வெண்குரு(கு)","குருகு",6,"pul","scene",""],["திவவின்","திவவு",7,"yaazh","scene",""],["வயிரிய","வயிரியர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["கூலமொடு","கூலம்",11,"unavu","gift_food","grain offering"],["மாக்கண்","மாக்கண்",12,"parai","scene","drum"],["யானைக்","யானை",14,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:30":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":21,"t":["இணர்ததை ஞாழல் கரைகெழு பெருந்துறை","மணிக்கலத் தன்ன மாஇதழ் நெய்தல்","பாசடைப் பனிக்கழி துழைஇப் புன்னை","வால்இணர்ப் படுசினக் குரு(கு)இறை கொள்ளும்","அல்குறு கானல் ஓங்குமணல் அடைகரை","தாழ்அடும்பு மலைந்த புணரிவளை ஞரல","இலங்குநீர் முத்தமொடு வார்துகிர் எடுக்கும்","தண்கடல் படப்பை மென்பா லனவும்","காந்தள்அங் கண்ணிக் கொலைவில் வேட்டுவர்","செங்கோட்(டு) ஆமான் ஊனொடு காட்ட","மதன்உடை வேழத்து வெண்கோடு கொண்டு","பொன்உடை நியமத்துப் பிழிநொடை கொடுக்கும்","குன்றுதலை மணந்த புன்புல வைப்பும்","காலம் அன்றியும் கரும்(பு)அறுத்(து) ஒழியா(து)","அரிகால் அவித்துப் பலபூ விழவின்","தேம்பாய் மருதம் முதல்படக் கொன்று","வெண்தலைச் செம்புனல் பரந்துவாய் மிகுக்கும்","பலசூழ் பதப்பர் பரிய வெள்ளத்துச்","சிறைகொள் பூசலின் புகன்ற ஆயம்","முழவிமிழ் மூதூர் விழவுக்காணூஉப் பெயரும்","செழும்பல் வைப்பி பழனப் பாலும்","ஏனல் உழவர் வரகுமீ(து) இட்ட","கான்மிகு குளவிய வன்புசேர் இருக்கை","மென்தினை நுவணை முறைமுறை பகுக்கும்","புன்புலம் தழீஇய புறஅணி வைப்பும்","பல்பூஞ் செம்மல் காடுபயம் மாறி","அரக்கத் தன்ன நுண்மணல் கோடுகொண்(டு)","ஒண்நுதல் மகளிர் கழலொடு மறுகும்","விண்உயர்ந்(து) ஓங்கிய கடற்றவும் பிறவும்","பணைகெழு வேந்தரும் வேளிரும்ஒன்று மொழிந்து","கடலவுங் காட்டவும் அரண்வலியார் நடுங்க","முரண்மிகு கடுங்குரல் விசும்(பு)அடை(பு) அதிரக்","கடுஞ்சினங் கடாஅய் முழங்கு மந்திரத்(து)","அருந்திறல் மரபின் கடவுள் பேணியர்","உயர்ந்தோன் ஏந்திய அரும்பெறல் பிண்டம்","கருங்கண் பேய்மகள் கைபுடையூஉ நடுங்க","நெய்த்தோர் தூஉய நிறைமகிழ் இரும்பலி","எறும்பும் மூசா இறும்பூது மரபின்","கருங்கண் காக்கையொடு பருந்(து)இருந் தார","ஓடாப் பூட்கை ஒண்பொறிக் கழல்கால்","பெரும்சமம் ததைந்த செருப்புகல் மறவர்","உருமுநிலன் அதிர்க்குங் குரலொடு கொளைபுணர்ந்து","பெருஞ்சோ(று) உகுத்தற் கெறியும்","கடுஞ்சின வேந்தேநின் தழங்குகுரல் முரசே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the fiercely angry king that his drum, roaring with a voice that shakes the earth like thunder, calls his never-retreating warriors to scatter the great rice, while the drum-rich kings and velir of every land, the sea shore of neythal, punnai, pearls and coral, the hills where hunters trade tusks and meat for liquor, the watered fields of sugarcane and festivals, the dry millet uplands and the sand-ridged forests, tremble though they hold forts in sea and forest; the rice ball raised by the priest for the god of the rite, a blood-soaked offering that the demoness shakes before and that even ants do not touch, is eaten by crows and kites.","n":"Thirty lines survey every kind of land the enemies hold, sea, hill, field, upland and forest, so that the trembling of their kings at the drum (line 31) is the trembling of the whole land. The great rice is an offering before it is a meal: raised by the priest, shaken at by the demoness, left even by ants and eaten by crows and kites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perunjoru","padai","maram","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 30","lb":"PT30","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"பெருஞ்சோற்றுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருஞ்சோற்று நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","simile",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["குரு(கு)","குருகு",4,"pul","scene",""],["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",6,"maram","scene",""],["புணரிவளை","வளை",6,"pira","scene","conch"],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",7,"pira","scene",""],["துகிர்","துகிர்",7,"pira","scene","coral"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",9,"maram","ornament",""],["வேட்டுவர்","வேட்டுவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",10,"maa","scene",""],["வேழத்து","வேழம்",11,"maa","scene",""],["பிழி","பிழி",12,"unavu","gift_food","liquor"],["கரும்(பு)","கரும்பு",14,"maram","scene",""],["மருதம்","மருதம்",16,"maram","scene",""],["முழவிமிழ்","முழவு",20,"parai","scene",""],["வரகு","வரகு",22,"maram","scene",""],["மென்தினை","தினை",24,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["அரக்கத்","அரக்கு",27,"pira","simile",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",34,"deyvam","scene",""],["பிண்டம்","பிண்டம்",35,"unavu","gift_food","rice ball"],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",36,"deyvam","scene","demoness"],["எறும்பும்","எறும்பு",38,"pira","absent",""],["காக்கையொடு","காக்கை",39,"pul","scene",""],["பருந்(து)","பருந்து",39,"pul","scene",""],["முரசே","முரசு",44,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:patikam:3":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":22,"t":["இமைய வரம்பன் தம்பி அமைவர","உம்பற் காட்டைத் தன்கோல் நிறீஇ","அகப்பா எறிந்து பகல்தீ வேட்டு","மதிஉறழ் மரபின் முதியரைத் தழீஇக்","கண்ணகன் வைப்பின் மண்வகுத்(து) ஈத்துக்","கருங்களிற்(று) யானைப் புணர்நிரை நீட்டி","இருகடல் நீணரும் ஒருபகல் ஆடி","அயிரை பரைஇ ஆற்றல்சால் முன்போ(டு)","ஒடுங்கா நல்இசை உயர்ந்த கேள்வி","நெடும்பார தாயனார் முந்(து)உறக் காடுபோந்த","பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவனைப்","பாலைக் கெளதமனார் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Palyanai Selkezhu Kuttuvan, younger brother of Imayavaramban, set his sceptre over Umbarkadu, struck down Agappa, performed sacrifice with the daytime fire, took to himself the elders of moon-clear tradition, divided and gave away land, led long rows of black elephants, bathed in the two seas in one day and worshipped Ayirai, and that, of unfailing fame and high learning, he went to the forest with Nedumbarathayanar going before him; Palai Gauthamanar sang the ten.","n":"Only the preface tells that the king ended by leaving for the forest with a Brahmin teacher; the songs show him at war and at sacrifice. Agappa and Ayirai tie the preface to 22 and 21.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","thuravu","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-12","lb":"PTpatikam:1-12","poet":"பாலைக் கெளதமனார்","pat":"பல்யானைச் செல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","இமைய வரம்பன்",1],["town","அகப்பா",3],["hill","அயிரை",8],["king","குட்டுவனைப்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:2"},"pt:31":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":23,"t":["குன்றுதலை மணந்து குழூஉக்கடல் உடுத்த","மண்கெழு ஞாலத்து மாந்தர் ஓர்ஆங்குக்","கைசுமந்(து) அலறும் பூசல் மாதிரத்து","நால்வேறு நனம்தலை யொருங்கெழுந்(து) ஒலிப்பத்","தெள்உயர் வடிமணி எறியுநர் கல்லென","உண்ணாப் பைஞ்ஞிலம் பனித்துறை மண்ணி","வண்(டு)ஊது பொலிதார்த் திருஞெமர் அகலத்துக்","கண்பொரு திகிரிக் கமழ்குரல் துழாஅய்","அலங்கற் செல்வன் சேவடி பரவி","நெஞ்சுமலி உவகையர் துஞ்சுபதிப் பெயர","மணிநிற மையிருள் அகல நிலாவிரிபு","கோடுகூடு மதியம் இயல்உற் றாங்குத்","துளங்குகுடி விழுத்திணை திருத்தி முரசுகொண்(டு)","ஆண்கடன் நிறுத்தநின் பூண்கிளர் வியன்மார்பு","கருவி வானம் தண்தளி தலைஇய","வடதெற்கு விலங்கி விலகுதலைத்(து) எழிலிய","பனிவார் விண்டு விறல்வரை அற்றே","கடவுள் அஞ்சி வானத்(து) இழைத்த","தூங்(கு)எயில் கதவம் காவல் கொண்ட","எழூஉநிவந்(து) அன்ன பரேர்எறுழ் முழவுத்தோள்","வெண்திரை முந்நீர் வளைஇய உலகத்து","வண்புகழ் நிறுத்த வகைசால் செல்வத்து","வண்டன் அனையைமன் நீயே வண்டுபட","ஒலிந்த கூந்தல் அறம்சால் கற்பின்","குழைக்கு விளக்கு ஆகிய ஒண்ணுதல் பொன்னின்","இழைக்கு விளக்காகிய அவ்வாங்கு உந்தி,","விசும்புவழங்கு மகளிர் உள்ளும் சிறந்த","செம்மீன் அனையள்நின் தொல்நகர்ச் செல்வி","நிலன்அதிர்(பு) இரங்கல ஆகி வலன்ஏர்பு","வியன்பணை முழங்கும் வேல்மூ(சு) அழுவத்(து)","அடங்கிய புடையல் பொலம்கழல் நோன்தாள்","ஒடுங்காத் தெவ்வர் ஊக்(கு)அறக் கடைஇப்","புறக்கொடை எறியார்நின் மறப்படை கொள்ளுநர்","நகைவர்க்(கு) அரணம் ஆகிப் பகைவர்க்குச்","சூர்நிகழ்ந் தற்றுநின் தானை","போர்மிகு குருசில்நீ மாண்டனை பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets the king among scenes of a sacred festival, where fasting crowds from the four quarters, to the clang of bells, bathe at the cold ghat and praise the red feet of the lord with the tulasi garland and the discus before going home glad, and tells him that like the full moon driving off the dark he set the shaken peoples right and took the drum, that his chest is like the cloud-soaked mountain and his shoulders like the bar of the sky fortress, that he is like the rich and famous Vandan, that his queen is like the red star among the women of the sky, and that his army, which never strikes a fleeing back, is a shield to friends and a terror to foes.","n":"The first ten lines are a festival of Vishnu with no word yet about the king; the poem then turns to him through the moon simile, so the worshippers' joy at the god's feet becomes the frame for the people's relief under the king. The queen is praised in the same breath as his body and army (lines 23 to 28), as the red star (செம்மீன்) that tradition names Arundhati, the model of chastity. Line 33 (புறக்கொடை எறியார்) states the rule of not striking a fleeing back as a trait of his soldiers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai","karpu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 31","lb":"PT31","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வடிமணி","மணி",5,"parai","scene","bell"],["துழாஅய்","துழாய்",8,"maram","ornament","tulasi"],["செல்வன்","செல்வன்",9,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["மதியம்","மதி",12,"pira","simile","moon"],["வானம்","வானம்",15,"pira","simile","cloud"],["செம்மீன்","செம்மீன்",28,"pira","simile","red star"],["வியன்பணை","பணை",30,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:32":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":24,"t":["மாண்டனை பலவே போர்மிகு குருசில்நீ","மாதிரம் விளக்கும் சால்பும் செம்மையும்","முத்(து)உடை மருப்பின் மழகளிறு பிளிற","மிக்(கு)எழு கடும்தார் துய்த்தலைச் சென்று","துப்புத்துவர் போகப் பெருங்கிளை உவப்ப","ஈத்(து)ஆன்(று) ஆனா விடன்உடை வளனும்","துளங்குகுடி திருத்திய வலம்படு வென்றியும்","எல்லாம் எண்ணின் இடுகழங்கு தபுந","கொன்ஒன்று மருண்டனென் அடுபோர்க் கொற்றவ","நெடுமிடல் சாயக் கொடுமிடல் துமியப்","பெருமலை யானையொடு புலம்கெட இறுத்துத்","தடந்தாள் நாரை படிந்(து)இரை கவரும்","முடந்தை நெல்லின் கழைஅமல் கழனிப்","பிழையா விளையுள் நா(டு)அகப் படுத்து","வையா மாலையர் வசையுநர்க்(கு) அறுத்த","பகைவர் தேஎத்(து) ஆயினும்","சினவாய் ஆகுதல் இறும்பூதால் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the king's virtues are too many to count, his fame and rectitude that light the quarters, his wealth that gives until it is spent so that his kin rejoice, and the victory that set the shaken peoples right, yet one thing amazes him: having broken the enemy's strength and camped with mountain-like elephants to ruin their land, and having taken their country of sure harvests where the heron feeds in the paddy among canes, the king is not angry even in the land of those foes who reviled him.","n":"The praise turns on restraint, not ferocity: the conquest is told in a few lines so that the closing wonder (இறும்பூதால் பெரிதே) can fall on the absence of anger where it could be used. The counting pebbles failing (இடுகழங்கு தபுந, line 8) is a counting game used as a measure of the king's virtues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vendhan","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 32","lb":"PT32","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மழகளிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","young elephant"],["இடுகழங்கு","கழங்கு",8,"pira","scene","counting pebbles"],["யானையொடு","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",12,"pul","scene","heron"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",13,"maram","scene","paddy"],["கழைஅமல்","கழை",13,"maram","scene","cane"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:33":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":25,"t":["இறும்பூதால் பரிதே கொடித்தேர் அண்ணல்","வடிமணி அனைத்த பனைமருள் நோன்தாள்","கடிமரத்தான் களி(று)அணைத்து","நெடுநீர துறைகலங்க","மூழ்த்(து)இறுத்த வியன்தானையொடு","புலம்கெட நரிதரும் வரம்பில் வெள்ளம்","வாள்மதில் ஆக வேல்மிளை உயர்த்து","வில்இசை உமிழ்ந்த வைம்முள் அம்பின்","செவ்வாய் எஃகம் வளைஇய அகழின்","கார்இடி உருமின் உரறும் முரசின்","கால்வழங்(கு) ஆர்எயில் கருதின்","போர்எதிர் வேந்தர் ஒரூஉப நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the lord of the bannered chariot that, when his army ties its belled, palm-legged elephants to the enemy's guardian tree, muddies the deep fords and camps all around like an endless flood that ruins the land, it is itself a fortress, with swords for its wall, raised spears for its thorn hedge, the red points of arrows for its moat and drums that roar like thunder, a fort that moves on foot, and kings who would face him in war shrink from him once they think of it.","n":"The poem builds a fortress out of the marching army, part by part, so the king who breaks walls needs none of his own. Tying war elephants to the enemy's guarded tree (கடிமரம்) is the insult that opens the war; the decade's preface tells how the king cut down Nannan's guardian vakai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","aran","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 33","lb":"PT33","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வடிமணி","மணி",2,"parai","scene","bell"],["பனைமருள்","பனை",2,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["கடிமரத்தான்","கடிமரம்",3,"maram","scene","guardian tree"],["உருமின்","உரும்",10,"pira","simile","thunder"],["முரசின்","முரசு",10,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:34":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":26,"t":["ஒரூஉப நின்னை ஒருபெரு வேந்தே","ஓடாப் பூட்கை ஒண்பொறிக் கழல்கால்","இருநிலம் தோயும் விரிநூல் அறுவையர்","செவ்உளைய மாஊர்ந்து","நெடும்கொடிய தேர்மிசையும்","ஓடை விளங்கும் உருகெழு புகர்நுதல்","பொன்அணி யானை முரண்சேர் எருத்தினும்","மன்நிலத்(து) அமைந்த ..................","மாறா மைந்தர் மாறுநிலை தேய","முரை(சு)உடைப் பெரும்சமம் ததைய ஆர்ப்(பு)எழ","அரைசுபடக் கடக்கும் ஆற்றல்","புரைசால் மைந்தநீ ஓம்பல் மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says kings shun the great king because, when warriors with anklets and robes trailing to the ground come on red-maned horses, on long-bannered chariots and on the necks of gold-decked elephants with blazing forehead plates, he wears down the stand of unyielding men, breaks the great drummed battle amid rising shouts and overcomes kings, and so the noble young man guards his people.","n":"Line 8 is a gap in the text, so what the warriors of lines 2 to 7 are set against is lost; the chain of three mounts (horse, chariot, elephant) gives the three arms of the enemy force before the gap. The last line (ஓம்பல் மாறே) gives guarding as the reason for the victories, and is the line the next poem begins from.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 34","lb":"PT34","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"தும்பையரவம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தும்பை அரவம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மாஊர்ந்து","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:35":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":27,"t":["புரைசால் மைந்தநீ ஓம்பல் மாறே","உரைசான் றனவால் பெருமைநின் வென்றி","இரும்களிற்று யானை இலங்குவால் மருப்பொடு","நெடும்தேர்த் திகிரிதாய வியன்களத்(து)","அள(கு)உடைச் சேவல் கிளைபுகா வாரத்","தலைதுமிந்(து) எஞ்சிய மெய்ஆடு பறந்தலை","அந்தி மாலை விசும்புகண் டன்ன","செஞ்சுடர் கொண்ட குருதி மன்றத்துப்","பேஎய் ஆடும் வெல்போர்","வீயா யாணர் நின்வயி னானே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says the king's victories are worth telling: on the wide field where great elephants lie with their shining tusks, chariot wheels have run over them and crested birds will not go in with their flocks, where headless bodies dance on the battleground and the square of blood glows red like the evening sky, demons dance, and that conquering war is his unfailing wealth.","n":"The closing line calls the blood-field itself the king's unfailing abundance (வீயா யாணர்), a word used of fertile land; the battlefield is praised as a field that never stops yielding. The dancing headless bodies (மெய்ஆடு, line 6) are the கவந்தம் of later war poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 35","lb":"PT35","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"வாகைத்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாகைத் துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["சேவல்","சேவல்",5,"pul","scene","crested bird"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",9,"pira","scene","demon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:36":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":28,"t":["வீயா யாணர் நின்வயி னானே","தாவா(து) ஆகு மலிபெறு வயவே","மல்லல் உள்ளமொடு வம்(பு)அமர்க் கடந்து","செருமிகு முன்பின் மறவரொடு தலைச்சென்று","பனைதடி புனத்தின் கைதடிபு பலவுடன்","யானை பட்ட வாள்மயங்கு கடும்தார்","மாவும் மாக்களும் படுபிணம் உணீஇயர்","பொறித்த போலும் புள்ளி எருத்தின்","புன்புற எருவைப் பெடைபுணர் சேவல்","குடுமி எழாலொடு கொண்டுகிழக்(கு) இழிய","நிலம்இழி நிவப்பின் நீள்நிரை பலசுமந்(து)","உருஎழு கூளியர் உண்டுமகிழ்ந்(து) ஆடக்","குருதிச் செம்புனல் ஒழுகச்","செருப்பல செய்குவை வாழ்கநின் வளனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king, whose wealth never fails, that with a glad heart he overcame fresh battles at the head of mighty warriors, and in the fierce vanguard where swords clashed many elephants fell with their trunks cut like palms in a cleared field, the grey-backed vulture with its mate and spotted neck and the hawk carried off the flesh of fallen horses and men, fearsome demons bearing long rows of the dead ate and danced in joy, and red blood ran, so he makes many wars; long live his wealth.","n":"The cleared palm field (பனைதடி புனம், line 5) turns the felled elephants into farm work, as the kalavazhi thurai, named after the threshing floor, tends to do. The male vulture paired with its mate (பெடைபுணர் சேவல்) keeps a love-poem image in a scene of carrion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 36","lb":"PT36","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"களவழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["களவழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பனைதடி","பனை",5,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","simile",""],["மாவும்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["எருவைப்","எருவை",9,"pul","scene","vulture"],["எழாலொடு","எழால்",10,"pul","scene","hawk"],["கூளியர்","கூளி",12,"pira","scene","demons"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:37":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":29,"t":["வாழ்கநின் வளனே நின்னுடை வாழ்க்கை","வாய்மொழி வாயர் நின்புகழ் ஏத்தப்","பகைவர் ஆரப் பழங்கண் அருளி","நகைவர் ஆர நன்கலம் சிதறி","ஆன்(று)அவிந்(து) அடங்கிய செயிர்தீர் செம்மல்","வான்தோய் நல்இசை உலகமொ(டு) உயிர்ப்பத்","துளங்குடி திருத்திய வலம்படு வென்றியும்","மாஇரும் புடையல் மாக்கழல் புனைந்து","மன்எயில் எறிந்து மறவர்த் தாணஇத்","தொல்நிலைச் சிறப்பின் நின்நிழல் வாழ்நர்க்குக்","கோ(டு)அற வைத்த கோடாக் கொள்கையும்","நன்றுபெரி(து) உடையையால் நீயே","வெந்திறல் வேந்தேஇவ் வுலகத் தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet blesses the king's wealth and life, as truthful men praise his fame: he gives grief in full to enemies and scatters fine jewels in full to friends, a restrained and calm chief without fault whose fame breathes with the world; he has the victory that set the shaken peoples right, wears the dark palm garland and great anklets, breaks kings' forts and upholds his warriors, and keeps an unbending rule set straight for those who live in his shade; this the fierce king has in great measure for the people of the world.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 balance in one pattern (பகைவர் ஆர / நகைவர் ஆர): grief for foes and gifts for friends are given in the same full measure. The rule is described with the bending metaphor twice over (கோடற வைத்த கோடாக் கொள்கை), so straightness is set beside the victory as a second possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","eegai","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 37","lb":"PT37","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["புடையல்","புடையல்",8,"maram","ornament","palm garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:38":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":30,"t":["உலகத் தோரே பலர்மன் செல்வர்","எல்லா ருள்ளும்நின் நல்இசை மிகுமே","வளம்தலை மயங்கிய பைதிரம் திருத்திய","களங்காய்க் கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","எயில்முகம் சிதையத் தோட்டி ஏவலின்","தோட்டி தந்த தொடிமருப்பு யானைச்","செவ்உளைக் கலிமா ஈணகை வான்கழல்","செயல்அமை கண்ணிச் சேரலர் வேந்தே","பரிசிலர் வெறுக்கை பாணர் நாளவை","வாள்நுதல் கணவ! மள்ளர் ஏறே!","மைஅற விளங்கிய வடுவாழ் மார்பின்","வசையில் செல்வ! வான வரம்ப!","இனியவை பெறினே தனிதனி நுகர்கேம்","தருகென விழையாத் தாஇல் நெஞ்சத்துப்","பகுத்(து)ஊண் தொகுத்த ஆண்மைப்","பிறர்க்(கு)என வாழ்திநீ ஆகல் மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says many in the world are rich but the king's fame exceeds them all, Kalangaaykkanni Narmudi Cheral who set right the country where wealth had been thrown into confusion, lord of elephants with ringed tusks and red-maned horses, king of the Cheras, the wealth of suppliants, the day-court of bards, husband of the bright-browed queen, bull among warriors, of scarred and spotless chest, Vaanavaramban, because, with a heart free of the flaw that wishes sweet things to be given so each may enjoy them alone, he has gathered the manliness of sharing food and lives for others.","n":"Sharing food is called manliness (பகுத்(து)ஊண் தொகுத்த ஆண்மை), placed among the war titles as one more of them. Lines 5 and 6 may play on தோட்டி, the goad that drives the elephant against the fort gate and perhaps also the name of the fort it won; the second sense cannot be settled from this text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 38","lb":"PT38","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களங்காய்","களங்காய்",4,"maram","ornament","karonda fruit"],["யானைச்","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""],["கலிமா","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["ஏறே","ஏறு",10,"maa","scene","bull"]],"en":[["king","களங்காய்க் கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்",4],["king","வான வரம்ப",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:39":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":31,"t":["பிறர்க்(கு)என வாழ்திநீ ஆகல் மாறே","எமக்கில்என் னார்நின் மறம்கூறு குழாத்தர்","துப்புத்துறை போகிய வெப்(பு)உடைத் தும்பைக்","கறுத்த தெவ்வர் கடிமுனை அலற","எடுத்(து)எறிந்(து) இரங்கும் ஏவல் வியன்பனை","உரும்என அதிர்பட்டு முழங்கிச் செருமிக்(கு)","அடங்கார் ஆர்அரண் வாடச் செல்லும்","காலன் அனைய கடும்சின முன்ப","வாலிதின், நூலின்இழையா நுண்மயிர் இழைய","பொறித்த போலும் புள்ளி எருத்தின்","புன்புறப் புறவின் கணநிரை அலற","அலந்தலை வேலத்(து) உலவை அம்சினைச்","சிலம்பி கோலிய அலங்கல் போர்வையின்","இலங்குமணி மிடைந்த பசும்பொன் படலத்(து)","அவிர்இழை தைஇ மின்உமிழ்(பு) இலங்கச்","சீர்மிகு முத்தம் தைஇய","நார்முடிச் சேரல்நின் போர்நிழல் புகன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that because the king lives for others, the band who tell his valour never say they lack anything: in the hot thumbai battle, while the war drum roars like thunder, the guarded front of the angry foes cries out and the forts of the unyielding wither as he goes, fierce as Death; and in the mantle of green gold set with gems, woven not of thread but fine as hair, bright as lightning and sewn with pearls, like the spider's web hung on the dry branches of the velam tree where the flock of grey-backed, spotted-necked doves cries, they delight in the shade of his war.","n":"The syntax of lines 9 to 16 is hard: the gold mesh that is like a spider's web on a thorn tree seems to be the king's war mantle, and the crying doves are the frightened enemy's land, but the connection of the parts is not certain. The shade of war (போர்நிழல்) that the band delights in is the next poem's opening.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 39","lb":"PT39","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["தும்பைக்","தும்பை",3,"maram","ornament","thumbai flower"],["வியன்பனை","பணை",5,"parai","scene","drum"],["உரும்என","உரும்",6,"pira","simile","thunder"],["புறவின்","புறா",11,"pul","scene","dove"],["வேலத்(து)","வேலம்",12,"maram","scene","acacia"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",13,"pira","simile","spider"],["முத்தம்","முத்து",16,"pira","ornament","pearl"]],"en":[["king","நார்முடிச் சேரல்நின்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:40":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":32,"t":["போர்நிழல் புகன்ற சுற்றமொ(டு) ஊர்முகத்(து)","இறாஅ லியரோ பெருமநின் தானை","இன்இசை இமிழ்முர(சு) இயம்பக் கடிப்பிகூஉப்","புண்டோள் ஆடவர் போர்முகத்(து) இறுப்பக்","காய்த்த கரந்தை மாக்கொடி விளைவயல்","வந்(து)இறை கொண்டன்று தானை அந்தில்","களைநர் யார்இனிப் பிறர்எனப் பேணி","மன்எயில் மறவர் ஒலிஅவிந்(து) அடங்க","ஒன்னார் தேயப் பூமலைந்(து) உரைஇ","வெண்தோடு நிரைஇய வேந்(து)உடை அரும்சமம்","கொன்றுபுறம் பெற்று மன்பதை நிரப்பி","வென்றி ஆடிய தொடித்தோள் மீகை","எழுமுடி கெழீஇய திருஞெமர் அகலத்துப்","பொன்அம் கண்ணிப் பொலம்தேர் நன்னன்","சுடர்வீ வாகைக் கடிமுதல் தடிந்த","தார்மிகு மைந்தின் நார்முடிச் சேரல்","புன்கால் உன்னம் சாயத் தெள்கண்","வறிதுகூட்(டு) அரியல் இரவலர்த் தடுப்பத்","தான்தர உண்ட நனைநறவு மகிழ்ந்து","நீர்இமிழ் சிலம்பின் நேரி யோனே","செல்லா யோதில் சில்வளை விறலி","மலர்ந்த வேங்கையின் வயங்கிழை அணிந்து","மெல்இயல் மகளிர் எழில்நலம் சிறப்பப்","பாணர் பைம்பூ மலைய இளையர்","இன்களி வழாஅ மென்சொல் அமர்ந்து","நெஞ்சுமலி உவகையர் வியன்களம் வாழ்த்தத்","தோட்டி நீவாது தொடிசேர்பு நின்று","பாகர் ஏவலின் ஒண்பொறி பிசிரக்","காடுதலைக் கொண்ட நாடுகாண் அவிர்சுடர்","அழல்விடுபு மாணஇய மைந்தின்","தொழில்புகல் யானை நல்குவன் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Praying that the king's army, which with its kin delights in the shade of war, may never turn back from a town's front, the poet tells how it camped in ripe fields where karanthai spread, silenced the warriors of the kings' forts, killed and won the hard battle of crowned kings, filled the people and danced the victory, under Narmudi Cheral who cut down at the guarded root the flame-flowered vakai of Nannan of the golden chariot, who rejoices in the toddy he gives while strained liquor holds back suppliants, lord of Neri hill; and he tells the small-bangled dancer that if she goes, adorned like the flowering vengai, with bards in fresh flowers and young men praising the wide field, he will give her many trained war elephants.","n":"The guide poem gives the dancer war elephants, not jewels or food: the spoils of the battle in the first half are what the patron hands out in the second. Line 17 (புன்கால் உன்னம் சாய) names the unnam tree, which droops as an omen of defeat for the king's foes. The felling of Nannan's vakai (line 15) is the deed the decade's preface makes its centre.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 40","lb":"PT40","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலியாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கரந்தை","கரந்தை",5,"maram","scene",""],["வாகைக்","வாகை",15,"maram","scene","guardian tree"],["உன்னம்","உன்னம்",17,"maram","scene","omen tree"],["அரியல்","அரியல்",18,"unavu","gift_food","strained liquor"],["நனைநறவு","நறவு",19,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வேங்கையின்","வேங்கை",22,"maram","simile",""],["பைம்பூ","பூ",24,"maram","ornament",""],["யானை","யானை",31,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",14],["king","நார்முடிச் சேரல்",16],["hill","நேரி",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:patikam:4":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":33,"t":["ஆராத் திருவிற் சேர லாதற்கு","வேளாவிக் கோமான்","பதுமன் தேவி ஈன்ற மகன்முனை","பனிப்பப் பிறந்து பல்புகழ் வளர்த்(து)","ஊழின் ஆகிய உயர்பெரும் சிறப்பின்","பூழி நாட்டைப் படைஎடுத்துத் தழீஇ","உருள் பூங் கடம்பின் பெருவாயில் நன்னனை","நிலைச்செருவி னால்தலை யறுத்(து)அவன்","பொன்படு வாகை முழுமுதல் தடிந்து","குருதிச் செம்புனல் குஞ்சரம் ஈர்ப்பச்","செருப்பல செய்து செங்களம் வேட்டுத்","துளங்குகுடி திருத்திய வலம்படு வென்றிக்","களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரலைக்","காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Kalangaaykkanni Narmudi Cheral, son of Cheralathan of unfailing fortune by the queen of Padhuman, lord of the Vel Aavi line, was born to make the battle front tremble, grew in fame, took the Poozhi country by war, cut off the head of Nannan of Peruvaayil in a pitched battle, felled the whole trunk of his gold-bearing vakai, made the red flood of blood drag elephants, fought many battles, performed the sacrifice of the red field and set the shaken peoples right; and that Kappiyaatru Kappiyanar sang this ten.","n":"The preface supplies what the songs only touch: his parentage, the Poozhi conquest and the killing of Nannan, which 40 mentions only through the felled vakai. The battlefield sacrifice (செங்களம் வேட்டு) and the shaken peoples set right (துளங்குகுடி திருத்திய) repeat the songs' own formulas (32, 37).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-14","lb":"PTpatikam:1-14","poet":"காப்பியாற்றுக் காப்பியனார்","pat":"களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",7,"maram","scene",""],["வாகை","வாகை",9,"maram","scene","guardian tree"],["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",10,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","சேர லாதற்கு",1],["king","வேளாவிக் கோமான்",2],["king","பதுமன்",3],["king","நன்னனை",7],["town","பெருவாயில்",7],["king","களங்காய்க்கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரலைக்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:3"},"pt:41":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":34,"t":["புணர்புரி நரம்பின் தீம்தொடை பழுனிய","வணர்அமை நல்யாழ் இளையர் பொறுப்பப்","பண்அமை முழவும் பதலையும் பிறவும்","கண்அறுத்(து) இயற்றிய தூம்பொடு சுருக்கிக்","காவில் தகைத்த துறைகூடு கலப்பையர்","கைவல் இளையர் கடவுள் பழிச்ச","மறப்புலிக் குழூஉக்குரல் செத்து வயக்களிறு","வரைசேர்(பு) எழுந்த சுடர்வீ வேங்கைப்","பூவுடைப் பெருஞ்சினை வாங்கிப் பிளந்துதன்","மாஇருஞ் சென்னி அணிபெற மிலைச்சிச்","சேஎர் உற்ற செல்படை மறவர்","தண்(டு)உடை வலத்தர் போர்எதிர்ந் தாங்கு","வழைஅமல் வியன்காடு சிலம்பப் பிளிறும்","மழைபெயல் மாறிய கழைதிரங்(கு) அத்தம்","ஒன்(று)இரண்(டு) அலபல கழிந்து திண்தேர்","வசைஇல் நெடுந்தகை காண்குவந் திசினே","தாவல் உய்யுமோ மற்றே தாவாது","வஞ்சினம் முடித்த ஒன்றுமொழி மறவர்","முர(சு)உடைப் பெருஞ்சமத்(து) அரசுபடக் கடந்து","வெவ்வர் ஓச்சம் பெருகத் தெவ்வர்","மிள(கு)எறி உலக்கையின் இருந்தலை இடித்து","வை(கு)ஆர்ப்(பு) எழுந்த மைபடு பரப்பின்","எடுத்தே(று) ஏய கடிப்(பு)உடை வியன்கண்","வலம்படு சீர்த்தி ஒருங்(கு)உடன் இயைந்து","கால்உளைக் கடும்பிசிர் உடைய வால்உளைக்","கடும்பரிப் புரவி ஊர்ந்தநின்","படுந்திரைப் பனிக்கடல் உழந்த தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how his band of young performers, carrying lute, drums and horns packed in their bundles and praising the god, crossed many dry tracks of a forest where rain has failed and the bamboo withers, where a mighty elephant, taking a sound for the roar of a tiger pack, tears down a branch of flame-flowered vengai and wears it on its head like warriors of the army meeting battle, staff in hand; he has come to see the faultless lord of the strong chariot, and he asks whether the king's feet, which rode the swift maned horse and toiled in the cold sea of crashing waves, can be free of weariness, the feet of a king whose one-word warriors fulfilled their vows, broke kings in drum-loud battle and pounded enemy heads as pestles pound pepper.","n":"The poem is in two halves: the hardship of the road the poet bore to see the king, and then the hardship the king's own feet bore at sea, so the bard's journey and the king's campaign are set against each other. The question தாவல் உய்யுமோ (line 17) is a turn of courtesy, concern for the king's feet, that becomes praise of the campaign. The elephant that mistakes something for a tiger and attacks the vengai rests on the old pairing of the vengai's flowers with the tiger's stripes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["parisil","maram","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 41","lb":"PT41","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காட்சி வாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காட்சி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",1,"yaazh","scene","lute string"],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவும்","முழவு",3,"parai","scene",""],["பதலையும்","பதலை",3,"parai","scene",""],["தூம்பொடு","தூம்பு",4,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",6,"deyvam","scene",""],["மறப்புலிக்","புலி",7,"maa","scene",""],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",8,"maram","ornament","vengai tree"],["வழைஅமல்","வழை",13,"maram","scene",""],["கழைதிரங்(கு)","கழை",14,"maram","absent","bamboo"],["மிள(கு)எறி","மிளகு",21,"unavu","scene","pepper"],["புரவி","புரவி",26,"maa","scene","horse"],["பனிக்கடல்","கடல்",27,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:42":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":35,"t":["இரும்பனம் புடையல் ஈகை வான்கழல்","மீன்தேர் கொட்பின் பனிக்கயம் மூழ்கிச்","சிரல்பெயர்ந் தன்ன நெடுவெள் ஊசி","நெடுவசி பரந்த வடுஆழ் மார்பின்","அம்புசேர் உடம்பினர்ச் சேர்ந்தோர் அல்லது","தும்பை சூடாது மலைந்த மாட்சி","அன்னோர் பெரும நல்நுதல் கணவ","அண்ணல் யானை அடுபோர்க் குட்டுவ","மைந்(து)உடை நல்அமர்க் கடந்து வலம்தாணஇ","இஞ்சிவீ விராய பைந்தார் சூட்டிச்","சாந்துபுறத்(து) எறித்த தசும்புதுளங்(கு) இருக்கைத்","தீம்சேறு விளைந்த மணிநிற மட்டம்","ஓம்பா ஈகையின் வண்மகிழ் சுரந்து","கோடியர் பெரும்கிளை வாழ ஆ(டு)இயல்","உளைஅவிர் கலிமாப் பொழிந்தவை எண்ணின்","மன்பதை மருள அரசுபடக் கடந்து","முந்துவினை எதிர்வரப் பெறுதல் காணியர்","ஒளிறுநிலை உயர்மருப்(பு) ஏந்திய களி(று)ஊர்ந்து","மான மைந்தரொடு மன்னர் ஏத்தநின்","தேரொடு சுற்றம் உல(கு)உடன் மூய","மாஇருந் தெள்கடல் மலிதிரைப் பெளவத்து","வெண்தலைக் குரூஉப்பிசிர் உடையத்","தண்பல வரூஉம் புணரியின் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Kuttuvan, husband of the fair-browed queen and lord of men who wear the dark palmyra garland and anklets of giving, whose chests are deeply scarred where the long white needle went in and out like a kingfisher diving into a cold pool for fish and rising again, and who wear the thumbai only beside men whose bodies have taken arrows; after each battle won he puts on a fresh garland strewn with inji flowers, pours sweet gem-coloured wine from sandal-smeared jars with unsparing joy so that the great kin of the performers thrive, and the prancing maned horses he has showered on them, if counted, are more than the cold waves of the dark clear sea that come crashing in white-crested spray.","n":"The scar is the warrior's credential: thumbai is worn only with those already wounded, so courage is counted on the body before the gift is counted in horses. The kingfisher simile is of the surgeon's needle sewing the wound, the bird's dive and rise matching the needle's passage through the flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","parisil","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 42","lb":"PT42","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["இரும்பனம்","பனை",1,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["மீன்தேர்","மீன்",2,"pira","simile","fish"],["சிரல்பெயர்ந்","சிரல்",3,"pul","simile","kingfisher"],["தும்பை","தும்பை",6,"maram","ornament",""],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""],["இஞ்சிவீ","இஞ்சி",10,"maram","ornament",""],["மட்டம்","மட்டு",12,"unavu","gift_food","wine"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",14,"seythi","scene","performers"],["கலிமாப்","மா",15,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["களி(று)ஊர்ந்து","களிறு",18,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவ",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:43":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":36,"t":["கவரி முச்சிக் கார்விரி கூந்தல்","ஊசல் மேவல் சேய்இழை மகளிர்","உரல்போல் பெருங்கால் இலங்குவாள் மருப்பின்","பெரும்கை மதமாப் புகுதரின் அவற்றுள்","விருந்தின் வீழ்பிடி எண்ணுமுறை பெறாஅக்","கடவுள் நிலைய கல்ஓங்கு நெடுவரை","வடதிசை எல்லை இமய மாகத்","தென்னங் குமரியொ(டு) ஆயிடை அரசர்","முர(சு)உடைப் பெரும்சமம் ததைய ஆர்ப்(பு)எழச்","சொல்பல நாட்டைத் தொல்கவின் அழித்த","போர்அடு தானைப் பொலந்தார்க் குட்டுவ","இரும்பணை திரங்கப் பெரும்பயல் ஒளிப்பக்","குன்றுவறம் கூரச் சுடர்சினம் திகழ","அருவிஅற்ற பெருவறல் காலையும்","அருஞ்செலல் பேராற்(று) இருங்கரை உடைத்துக்","கடிஏர் பூட்டுநர் கடுக்கை மலைய","வரைவில் அதிர்சிலை முழங்கிப் பெயல்சிறந்(து)","ஆர்கலி வானம் தளிசொரிந் தாஅங்(கு)","உறுவர் ஆர ஓம்பா(து) உண்டு","நகைவர் ஆர நன்கலம் சிதறி","ஆடுசிறை அறுத்த நரம்புசேர் இன்குரல்","பாடு விறலியர் பல்பிடி பெறுக","துய்வீ வாகை நுண்கொடி உழிஞை","வென்றி மேவல் உருகெழு சிறப்பின்","கொண்டி மள்ளர் கொல்களிறு பெறுக","மன்றம் படர்ந்து மறுகுசிறைப் புக்குக்","கண்டி நுண்கோல் கொண்டுகளம் வாழ்த்தும்","அகவலன் பெறுக மாவே என்றும்","இகல்வினை மேவலை ஆகலின் பகைவரும்","தாங்காது புகழ்ந்த தூங்குகொளை முழவின்","தொலையாக் கற்பநின் நிலைகண் டிகுமே","நிணம்சுடு புகையொடு கனல்சினந்(து) அவிராது","நிரம்(பு)அகல்(பு) அறியா ஏறா ஏணி","நிறைந்து நெடி(து)இராத் தசும்பின் வயிரியர்","உண்(டு)எனத் தவாஅக் கள்ளின்","வண்கை வேந்தேநின் கலிமகி ழானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Kuttuvan of the gold garland, whose slaying army broke in shouting battle the drum-rich kings between the Himalaya, the god-haunted high mountain of the north where women with chowrie tufts swing and the musth tuskers come with she-elephants beyond counting, and Kumari in the south, and spoiled the old beauty of lands of many tongues; as in a drought, when palmyras wither, hills go dry and falls stop, the roaring sky pours rain with thunder and breaks the banks of the great river while ploughmen wearing konrai yoke their ploughs, so he lets the ascetics eat their fill, scatters jewels to the merry, and bids that the singing viralis get many she-elephants, the plundering warriors get killing tuskers and the herald who praises the field get horses; since he loves war, even his foes praise him, and the poet has seen his state at feasts where the fat-smoke fire never dies and the toddy never fails the musicians.","n":"The rain is not the ordinary rain of the season but rain that breaks a drought, so the king's gift is measured against need, not against plenty. The roll of gifts is graded by receiver: she-elephants to the singing women, war tuskers to the fighters, horses to the herald, each gift fitted to its use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","mazhai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 43","lb":"PT43","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"இயன்மொழிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கவரி","கவரி",1,"maa","simile","yak-tail"],["மதமாப்","மா",4,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வீழ்பிடி","பிடி",5,"maa","scene",""],["இரும்பணை","பனை",12,"maram","absent","palmyra"],["அருவிஅற்ற","அருவி",14,"pira","absent",""],["ஏர்","ஏர்",16,"seythi","scene","plough"],["கடுக்கை","கடுக்கை",16,"maram","ornament","konrai"],["நரம்புசேர்","நரம்பு",21,"yaazh","scene",""],["பல்பிடி","பிடி",22,"maa","gift_food","she-elephant"],["வாகை","வாகை",23,"maram","ornament",""],["உழிஞை","உழிஞை",23,"maram","ornament",""],["கொல்களிறு","களிறு",25,"maa","gift_food",""],["மாவே","மா",28,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["முழவின்","முழவு",30,"parai","scene",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",35,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["hill","இமய",7],["king","குட்டுவ",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:44":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":37,"t":["நிலம்புடைப்(பு) அன்னஆர்ப் பொடுவிசும்பு துடையூ","வான்தோய் வெல்கொடி தேர்மிசை நுடங்கப்","பெரிய ஆயினும் அமர்கடந்து பெற்ற","அரிய என்னா(து) ஓம்பாது வீசிக்","கலம்செலச் சுரத்தல் அல்லது கனவினும்","களை(க)என அறியாக் கச(டு)இல் நெஞ்சத்(து)","ஆடுநடை அண்ணல்நின் பாடுமகள் காணியர்","காணி லியரோநின் புகழ்ந்த யாக்கை","முழுவலி துஞ்சு நோய்தபு நோன்தொடை","நுண்கொடி உழிஞை வெல்போர் அறுகை","சேணன் ஆயினும் கேள்என மொழிந்து","புலம்பெயர்ந்(து) ஒளித்த களையாப் பூசற்(கு)","அரண்கடா உறீஇ அணங்குநிகழ்ந் தன்ன","மோகூர் மன்னன் முரசம் கொண்டு","நெடுமொழி பணித்(து)அவன் வேம்புமுதல் தடிந்து","முரசுசெய முரச்சிக் களிறுபல பூட்டி","ஒழுகை உய்த்த கொழுஇல் பைந்துணி","வைத்தலை மறந்த துய்த்தலைக் கூகை","கவலை கவற்றும் குராலம் பறந்தலை","முர(சு)டைத் தாயத்(து) அரசுபல ஓட்டித்","துளங்குநீர் வியல்அகம் ஆண்(டு)இனிது கழிந்த","மன்னர் மறைத்த தாழி","வன்னி மன்றத்து விளங்கிய நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the lord of swaying gait whose victorious banner touches the sky on his chariot amid a roar like the earth being struck, who flings away unsparingly what he won in battle without calling it rare, and has never known even in dream to say 'hold back': for the sake of Arukai of the fine uzhinai, whom he called kin though far off and who had fled his land in an unended feud, he stormed the fort like one possessed, took the drum of the king of Mohur, humbled his boast, cut down his neem at the root, yoked many elephants to drag it for a drum, on the Kuraalam field where tufted owls cry and the crossroads grieve; he drove off many drum-rich kings, and his land is the one where the vanni common holds the urns that hid kings who once ruled the wide earth.","n":"The war is told as a friend's duty: the king fights Mohur because Arukai, distant though he is, was declared kin (சேணன் ஆயினும் கேள்என மொழிந்து). The felled guardian neem becomes the victor's own war drum, so the enemy's sacred tree is made to sound his victories. Line 8, காணிலியரோ, and the burial urns of the last lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","natpu","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 44","lb":"PT44","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["உழிஞை","உழிஞை",10,"maram","ornament",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",14,"parai","scene",""],["வேம்புமுதல்","வேம்பு",15,"maram","absent","neem"],["களிறுபல","களிறு",16,"maa","scene",""],["கூகை","கூகை",18,"pul","scene","owl"],["வன்னி","வன்னி",23,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","அறுகை",10],["town","மோகூர்",14],["battle","குராலம் பறந்தலை",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:45":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":38,"t":["பொலம்பூந் தும்பைப் பொறிகிளர் தூணிப்","புற்(று)அடங்(கு) அரவின் ஒடுங்கிய அம்பின்","நொசி(வு)உடை வில்லின் ஒசியா நெஞ்சின்","களி(று)எறிந்து முரிந்த கதுவாய் எஃகின்","விழுமியோர் துவன்றிய அகன்கண் ணாட்பின்","எழுமுடி மார்பின் எய்திய சேரல்","குண்டுகண் அகழிய மதில்பல கடந்து","பண்டும் பண்டும்தாம் உள்அழித்(து) உண்ட","நாடுகெழு தாயத்து நனம்தலை அருப்பத்துக்","கதவம் காக்கும் கணைஎழு அன்ன","நிலம்பெறு திணிதோள் உயர ஓச்சிப்","பிணம்பிறங்(கு) அழுவத்துத் துணங்கை ஆடிச்","சோறுவே(று) என்னா ஊன்துவை அடிசில்","ஓடாப் பீடர் உள்வழி இறுத்து","முள்இடு(பு) அறியா ஏணித் தெவ்வர்","சிலைவிசை அடக்கிய மூரி வெண்தோல்","அனைய பண்பின் தானை மன்னர்","இனியார் உளரோநின் முன்னும் இல்லை","மழைகொளக் குறையாது புனல்புக நிறையாது","விலங்குவளி கடவும் துளங்(கு)இரும் கமம்சூல்","வயங்குமணி இமைப்பின் வேல்இடுபு","முழங்குதிரைப் பனிக்கடல் மறுத்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether there are now, or were before, any kings like this Cheral, who wears the gold thumbai and the chest-garland won from seven crowns, whose arrows lie quiet in the spotted quiver like snakes in an anthill, whose bow bends and heart does not, whose spears are notched from striking elephants; who crossed the deep-moated walls of forts that others had sacked and enjoyed of old, raised his land-winning shoulders like the bars that guard a gate, danced the thunangai on the corpse-heaped field, fed meat-paste rice to men who never retreat, and threw his spear to drive back the roaring cold sea that rain clouds do not empty nor rivers fill.","n":"The sea that neither shrinks when clouds drink from it nor swells when rivers enter (lines 19, 20) is the image of what cannot be moved, and the king moves it; the sea-repelling feat that gives the decade's king his name is here the climax of a list of land battles. The question 'are there such now? none before you either' makes the praise historical: he is measured against all past kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","pugazh","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 45","lb":"PT45","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தும்பைப்","தும்பை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",2,"pira","scene","snake"],["களி(று)எறிந்து","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["ஊன்துவை","ஊன்",13,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["பனிக்கடல்","கடல்",22,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","சேரல்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:46":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":39,"t":["இழையர் குழையர் நறுந்தண் மாலையர்","சுடர்நிமிர் அவிர்தொடி செறித்த முன்கைத்","திறல்விடு திருமணி இலங்கு மார்பின்","வண்டுபடு கூந்தல் முடிபுனை மகளிர்","தொடைபடு பேர்யாழ் பாலை பண்ணிப்","பணியா மரபின் உழிஞை பாட","இனிதுபுறந் தந்(து)அவர்க்(கு) இன்மகிழ் சுரத்தலின்","சுரம்பல கடவும் கரைவாய்ப் பருதி","ஊர்பாட்(டு) எண்ணில் பைந்தலை துமியப்","பல்செருக் கடந்த கொல்களிற்(று) யானைக்","கோடுநரல் பெளவம் கலங்க வேல்இட்(டு)","உடைதிரைப் பரப்பில் படுகடல் ஓட்டிய","வெல்புகழ்க் குட்டுவன் கண்டோர்","செல்குவம் என்னார் பாடுபு பெயர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Because Kuttuvan of victorious fame, lord of killing elephants who crossed many battles cutting countless heads, whose chariot wheel runs over many wastes, and who threw his spear to drive back the conch-loud sea of breaking waves, protects them well and pours sweet joy on them, the women in jewels, ear-rings and cool garlands, with bright bangles and gem-lit breasts and bee-haunted hair, tune the great lute to the paalai mode and sing the uzhinai of his unbowing line; those who have seen him never say 'we will go', and move about only singing.","n":"The praise works by an absence: the performers never speak the word of leave-taking (செல்குவம் என்னார்), which in other gift poems is the moment the bard departs rewarded. The women sing a war theme, uzhinai, in the paalai mode of the lute, a music named for the parting land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 46","lb":"PT46","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மாலையர்","மாலை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",4,"pira","scene","bee"],["பேர்யாழ்","யாழ்",5,"yaazh","scene",""],["கொல்களிற்(று)","களிறு",10,"maa","scene",""],["கோடுநரல்","கோடு",11,"pira","scene","conch"],["படுகடல்","கடல்",12,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:47":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":40,"t":["அட்(டு)ஆ னானே குட்டுவன் அடுதொறும்","பெற்(று)ஆ னாரே பரிசிலர் களிறே","வரைமிசை இழிதரும் அருவியின் மாடத்து","வளிமுனை அவிர்வரும் கொடிநுடங்கு தெருவில்","சொரிசுரை கவரும் நெய்வழி(பு) உராலின்","பாண்டில் விளக்குப் பரூஅச்சுடர் அழல","நன்நுதல் விறலியர் ஆடும்","தொல்நகர் வரைப்பின்அவன் உரைஆ னாவே."],"p":"","r":"Kuttuvan never tires of slaying, and each time he slays the suppliants never tire of receiving elephants; and in his ancient city, where banners wave in the wind from the mansions like falls pouring down a mountain, where the big lamps on their stands burn with broad flames fed from ghee-dripping spouts, and bright-browed viralis dance, the talk of him never ends.","n":"The eight lines are built on one verb repeated three times, ஆனான், ஆனார், ஆனா: the king's killing, the suppliants' gain and the city's talk of him are made one unbroken chain, so that each slaughter is at once a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 47","lb":"PT47","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களிறே","களிறு",2,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["நெய்வழி(பு)","நெய்",5,"unavu","scene","ghee"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",7,"seythi","scene","dancing women"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:48":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":41,"t":["பைம்பொன் தாமரை பாணர்ச் சூட்டி","ஒண்நுதல் விறலியர்க்(கு) ஆரம் பூட்டிக்","கெடல்அரும் பல்புகழ் நிலைஇ நீர்புக்குக்","கடலொ(டு) உழந்த பனித்துறைப் பரதவ","ஆண்டுநீர்ப் பெற்ற தாரம் ஈண்(டு)இவர்","கொள்ளாப் பாடற்(கு) எளிதின் ஈயும்","கல்லா வாய்மையன் இவன்எனத் தத்தம்","கைவல் இளையர் நேர்கை நிரைப்ப","வணங்கிய சாயல் வணங்கா ஆண்மை","முனைசுடு கனைஎரி எரித்தலின் பெரிதும்","இகழ்கவின் அழிந்த மாலையொடு சாந்துபுலர்","பல்பொறி மார்பநின் பெயர்வா ழியரோ","நின்மலைப் பிறந்து நின்கடல் மண்டும்","மலிபுனல் நிகழ்தரும் தீநீர் விழவின்","பொழில்வதி வேனில் பேர்எழில் வாழ்க்கை","மேவரு சுற்றமோ(டு) உண்(டு)இனிது நுகரும்","தீம்புனல் ஆயம் ஆடும்","காஞ்சிஅம் பெருந்துறை மணலினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet blesses the name of the king who crowns the bards with gold lotuses and hangs necklaces on the bright-browed viralis, who set his imperishable fame by entering the water and toiling with the sea, lord of the paravar of the cold shore, of whom his skilled attendants say as they stand in rows that he gives here easily, even to songs that ask nothing, the treasure he took there at sea, a man of truth who never learned otherwise; gentle in bearing and unbending in manhood, his many-spotted chest has lost its garland and sandal to the fire that burns the enemy front; may his name live longer than the sands of the great Kaanchi ford where bands bathe in the sweet water born on his mountains and rushing to his sea, at the festival where kin enjoy a summer life in the groves.","n":"The king is addressed as lord of the fishermen (பனித்துறைப் பரதவ) because of his sea war, and the river that runs from his mountain to his sea makes the whole land, hill to shore, his. வணங்கிய சாயல் வணங்கா ஆண்மை (line 9) pairs a bowing gentleness with an unbowing manhood; the line recurs in 70. Whether காஞ்சி names a river or the kaanchi trees of the ford is not settled by the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 48","lb":"PT48","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"இயன்மொழிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","gift_food","golden lotus"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",2,"pira","gift_food","necklace"],["பரதவ","பரதவர்",4,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["தாரம்","தாரம்",5,"pira","gift_food","treasure"],["மாலையொடு","மாலை",11,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பொழில்வதி","பொழில்",15,"maram","scene","grove"],["மணலினும்","மணல்",18,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காஞ்சிஅம் பெருந்துறை",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:49":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":42,"t":["யாமும் சேறுக நீயிரும் வம்மின்","துயிலுங் கோதைத் துளங்(கு)இயல் விறலியர்","கொளைவல் வாழ்க்கைநும் கிளைஇனி(து) உணீஇயர்","களிறுபரந்(து) இயலக் கடுமா தாங்க","ஒளிறுகொடி நுடங்கத் தேர்திரிந்து கொட்ப","எஃகுதுரந்(து) எழுதரும் கைகவர் கடும்தார்","வெல்போர் வேந்தரும் வேளிரும் ஒன்றுமொழிந்து","மொய்வளம் செருக்கி மொசிந்துவரு மோகூர்","வலம்படு குழூஉநிலை அதிர மண்டி","நெய்த்தோர் தொட்ட செங்கை மறவர்","நிறம்படு குருதி நிலம்படர்ந்(து) ஓடி","மழைநாள் புனலின் அவல்பரந்(து) ஒழுகப்","படுபிணம் பிறங்கப் பாழ்பல செய்து","படுகண் முரசம் நடுவண் சிலைப்ப","வளன்அற நிகழ்ந்து வாழுநர் பலர்படக்","கருஞ்சினை விறல்வேம்(பு) அறுத்த","பெருஞ்சினைக் குட்டுவன் கண்டனம் வரற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker calls the viralis of swaying gait and drooping garlands to come along with him so that their kin who live by song may eat well, for he has seen Kuttuvan of great anger, who, when kings and Velir agreed together and came crowding to Mohur swollen with strength, with elephants spreading, horses held in, banners gleaming and chariots wheeling, charged so that the massed array shook, while red-handed warriors' blood ran over the ground like a rain-day flood filling the hollows, corpses heaped up, the drum roared in the middle, the place was ruined and many dwellers died, and he cut down the victorious neem of dark branches.","n":"The guide does not describe the gifts at all, only the slaughter at Mohur: the proof that the viralis will eat well is the scale of the king's victory. The blood-flood simile borrows the rain image of plenty for the field of the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","maram","parisil","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 49","lb":"PT49","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலி ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கோதைத்","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["களிறுபரந்(து)","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["கடுமா","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["முரசம்","முரசு",14,"parai","scene",""],["விறல்வேம்(பு)","வேம்பு",16,"maram","absent","neem"]],"en":[["town","மோகூர்",8],["king","குட்டுவன்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:50":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":43,"t":["மாமலை முழக்கின் மான்கணம் பனிப்பக்","கால்மயங்கு கதழ்உறை ஆலியொடு சிதறிக்","கரும்(பு)அமல் கழனிய நாடுவளம் பொழிய","வளம்கெழு சிறப்பின் உலகம் புரைஇச்","செங்குணக்(கு) ஒழுகும் கலுழி மலிர்நிறைக்","காவிரி அன்றியும் பூவிரி புனலொரு","மூன்றுடன் கூடிய கூடல் அனையை","கொல்களிற்(று), உரவுத்திரை பிறழ அவ்வில் பிசிரப்","புரைதோல் வரைப்பின் எஃகுமன்ண அவிர்தர","விரவுப்பணை முழங்(கு)ஒலி வொணஇய வேந்தர்க்(கு)","அரணம் ஆகிய வெருவரு புனல்தார்","கல்மிசை யவ்வும் கடலவும் பிறவும்","அருப்பம் அமைஇய அமர்கடந்(து) உருத்த","ஆள்மலி மருங்கின் நா(டு)அகப் படுத்து","நல்இசை நனந்தலை இரிய ஒன்னார்","உருப்(பு)அற நிரப்பினை ஆதலின் சாந்துபுலர்பு","வண்ணம் நீவி வகைவனப்(பு) உற்ற","வரிஞிமி(று) இமிரும் மார்புபிணி மகளிர்","விரிமென் கூந்தல் மெல்அணை வதிந்து","கொல்பிணி திருகிய மார்புகவர் முயக்கத்துப்","பொழுதுகொள் மரபின் மென்பிணி அவிழ","எவன்பல கழியுமோ பெரும பல்நாள்","பகைவெம் மையின் பாசறை மாணஇப்","பா(டு)அரி(து) இயைந்த சிறுதுயில் இயலாது","கோடு முழங்(கு) இமிழ்இசை எடுப்பும்","பீடுகெழு செல்வம் மாணஇய கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that he is like the confluence where the Kaveri, flowing due east in muddy flood and fed by rains that thunder like mountains, scatter hail and make the deer herds tremble, enrich the cane-fields and sustain the world, joins two more flowery streams; having crossed battles at forts on hills and by the sea, taken the enemy's land and filled his foes with dread, he asks how the many days pass for the great one whose eyes, in the camp where hatred of the enemy keeps him, cannot even take a short hard-won sleep and are woken by the roar of horns, when he might lie embraced on the soft pillow of women's loosened hair, their sandal and colour rubbed away and bees humming on their breasts.","n":"The poem praises endurance by naming what the king gives up: the embrace is drawn in akam detail and then set aside for the camp's broken sleep, so the king's love is the measure of his resolve. Lines 8 to 10 are partly corrupt (மன்ண, வொணஇய), and the sense of the army as a flood that is the kings' fortress is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","pirivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 50","lb":"PT50","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மான்கணம்","மான்",1,"maa","scene","deer"],["ஆலியொடு","ஆலி",2,"pira","scene","hail"],["கரும்(பு)அமல்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","scene",""],["கொல்களிற்(று)","களிறு",8,"maa","scene",""],["வரிஞிமி(று)","ஞிமிறு",18,"pira","scene","bee"],["கோடு","கோடு",25,"yaazh","scene","horn"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:patikam:5":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":44,"t":["வடவர் உட்கும் வான்தோய் வெல்கொடிக்","குடவர் கோமான் நெடுஞ்சேர லாதற்குச்","சோழன் மணக்கிள்ளி ஈன்ற மகன்","கடவுள் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள் வேண்டிக்","கான்நவில் கானம் கணையின் போகி","ஆரிய அண்ணலை வீட்டிப் பேரிசை","இன்பல் அருவிக் கங்கை மண்ணி","இனம்தெரி பல்ஆன் கன்றொடு கொண்டு","மாறா வல்வில் இடும்பின் புறத்(து)இறுத்(து)","உறுபுலி அன்ன வயவர் வீழச்","சிறுகுரல் நெய்தல் வியலூர் நூறி","அக்கரை நண்ணிக் கொடுகூர் எறிந்து","பழையன் காக்கும் கரும்சினை வேம்பின்","முழாரை முழுமுதல் துமியப் பண்ணி","வால்இழை கழித்த நறும்பல் பெண்டிர்","பல்இருங் கூந்தல் முரற்சியால்","குஞ்சர ஒழுகை பூட்டி வெந்திறல்","ஆராச் செருவின் சோழர்குடிக்(கு) உரியோர்","ஒன்பதின்மர் வீழ வாயில்புறத்(து) இறுத்து","நிலைச்செருவின் ஆற்றலை அறுத்துக்","கெடல் அரும் தானையொடு","கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவனைக் கரணமமைந்த","காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Senguttuvan, who drove back the sea, son of Nedum Cheralathan, lord of the westerners whose sky-touching banner the northerners fear, by the Chola Manakkilli, went through the forests by the arrow to take a stone for the goddess Pattini, defeated the Aryan lord, bathed it in the far-famed Ganga of sweet falls, brought back herds of cows with their calves, camped outside Idumbil, cut down Viyalur where warriors like tigers fell, reached the far bank and destroyed Kodukur, felled the whole trunk of the dark neem guarded by Pazhaiyan, yoked it to an elephant cart with ropes of the hair of his many wives, who had put off their jewels, killed nine of the Chola line in endless battle, camped at their gate and broke their standing strength; and that Paranar of flawless verse sang this ten.","n":"The preface tells what the songs leave out: the northern expedition for the Pattini stone and the Chola wars. It also names whose neem was felled (Pazhaiyan's) and the ropes that dragged it, the hair of his widowed wives, which the songs (44, 49) only touch on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","padai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-23","lb":"PTpatikam:1-23","poet":"காசறு செய்யுட் பரணர்","pat":"கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["பல்ஆன்","ஆன்",8,"maa","scene","cow"],["உறுபுலி","புலி",10,"maa","simile","tiger"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",11,"maram","scene",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",13,"maram","absent","neem"],["குஞ்சர","குஞ்சரம்",17,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","நெடுஞ்சேர லாதற்குச்",2],["king","சோழன் மணக்கிள்ளி",3],["king","ஆரிய அண்ணலை",6],["river","கங்கை",7],["town","இடும்பின்",9],["town","வியலூர்",11],["town","கொடுகூர்",12],["king","பழையன்",13],["king","செங்குட்டுவனைக்",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:4"},"pt:51":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":45,"t":["துளங்குநீர் வியலகம் கலங்கக் கால்பொர","விளங்(கு)இரும் புணரி உருமென முழங்கும்","கடல்சேர் கானல் குடபுலம் முன்னிக்","கூவல் துழந்த தடந்தாள் நாரை","குவிஇணர் ஞாழல் மாச்சினைச் சேக்கும்","வண்(டு)இறை கொண்ட தண்கடல் பரப்பின்","அடும்(பு)அமல் அடைகரை அலவன் ஆடிய","வடுஅடு நுண்அயிர் ஊதை உஞற்றும்","தூஇரும் போந்தைப் பொழில்அணிப் பொலிதந்(து)","இயலினள் ஒல்கினள் ஆடும் மடமகள்","வெறிஉறு நுடக்கம் போலத் தோன்றிப்","பெருமலை வயின்வயின் விலங்கும் அருமணி","அரவழங்கும் பெரும்தெய்வத்து","வளைஞரலும் பனிப்பெளவத்துக்","குணகுட கடலோ(டு) ஆயிடை மணந்த","பந்தர் அந்தரம் வேய்ந்து","வண்பிணி அவிழ்ந்த கண்போல் நெய்தல்","நனைஉறு நறவின் நா(டு)உடன் கமழச்","சுடர் நுதல் மடநோக்கின்","வாள்நகை இலங்(கு)எயிற்(று)","அமிழ்துபொதி துவர்வாய் அசைநடை விறலியர்","பாடல் சான்று நீடினை உறைதலின்","வெள்வேல் அண்ணல் மெல்லியன் போன்மென","உள்ளுவர் கொல்லோநின் உணரா தோரே","மழைதவழும் பெருங்குன்றத்துச்","செயிர்உடைய அர(வு)எறிந்து","கடும்சினத்த மிடல்தபுக்கும்","பெரும்சினப்புயல் ஏ(று)அனையை","தாங்குநர், தடக்கை யானைத் தொடிக்கோடு துமிக்கும்","எஃ(கு)உடை வலத்தர்நின் படைவழி வாழ்நர்","மறம்கெழு போந்தை வெண்தோடு புனைந்து","நிறம்பெயர் கண்ணிப் பருந்(து) ஊ(று)அளப்பத்","தூக்கணை கிழித்த மாக்கண் தண்ணுமை","கைவல் இளையர் கையலை அழுங்க","மாற்(று)அரும் சீற்றத்து மாஇரும் கூற்றம்","வலைவிரித் தன்ன நோக்கலை","கடியையால் நெடுந்தகை செருவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that because he lingers in his cool sea-coast land, where the palmyra grove sways like a possessed girl dancing and the viralis sing to him, those who do not know him may think the lord of the white spear is gentle; but he is like the thunderbolt that strikes the angry snake on the cloud-hung hill, and his look in battle, where his spearmen cut the tusks of elephants and the drums torn by arrows fall silent, is Death spreading its net.","n":"The poem turns on a mistaken impression (மெல்லியன் போன்ம், line 23): the long, lush seaside description of lines 1 to 22 is the evidence the unknowing would use, and the second half answers it with two similes of sudden killing, the thunder and Death's net. The softness is not denied; it is placed at home. Lines 12 to 16 (gems, snakes, conches, the canopy between the eastern and western seas) are compressed and their syntax is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","kootru","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 51","lb":"PT51","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித் துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புணரி","புணரி",2,"pira","simile","sea"],["கானல்","கானல்",3,"maram","scene","seaside grove"],["நாரை","நாரை",4,"pul","scene",""],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["வண்(டு)இறை","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["அடும்(பு)அமல்","அடும்பு",7,"maram","scene",""],["அலவன்","அலவன்",7,"pira","scene","crab"],["ஊதை","ஊதை",8,"pira","scene","wind"],["போந்தைப்","போந்தை",9,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["அருமணி","மணி",12,"pira","scene","gem"],["வளைஞரலும்","வளை",14,"pira","scene","conch"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",17,"maram","simile",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",18,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",21,"seythi","scene",""],["அர(வு)எறிந்து","அரவு",26,"pira","simile","snake"],["ஏ(று)அனையை","ஏறு",28,"pira","simile","thunder"],["யானைத்","யானை",29,"maa","scene",""],["போந்தை","போந்தை",31,"maram","ornament","palmyra leaf"],["பருந்(து)","பருந்து",32,"pul","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",33,"parai","scene",""],["கூற்றம்","கூற்று",35,"deyvam","simile","Death"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:52":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":46,"t":["கொடிநுடங்கு நிலைய கொல்களிறு மிடைந்து","வடிமணி நெடுந்தேர் வேறுபுலம் பரப்பி","அருங்கலந் தாணஇயர் நீர்மிசை நிவக்கும்","பெருங்கலி வங்கம் திசைதிரிந் தாங்கு","மைஅணிந்(து) எழுதரும் ஆயிரம் பஃறோல்","மெய்புதை அரணம் எண்ணா(து) எஃகுசுமந்து","முன்சமத்(து) எழுதரும் வன்கண் ஆடவர்","தொலையாத் தும்பை தெவ்வழி விளங்க","உயர்நிலை உலகம் எய்தினர் பலர்பட","நல்அமர்க் கடந்தநின் செல்உறழ் தடக்கை","இரப்போர்க்குக் கவிதல் அல்லதை இரைஇய","மலர்(பு)அறி யாஎனக் கேட்டிகும் இனியே","சுடரும் பாண்டில் திருநாறு விளக்கத்து","முழாஇமிழ் துணங்கைக்குத் தழூஉப்புணை ஆகச்","சிலைப்புவல் ஏற்றின் தலைக்கை தந்துநீ","நளிந்தனை வருதல் உடன்றனள் ஆகி","உயவும் கோதை ஊரல்அம் தித்தி","ஈர்இதழ் மழைக்கண் பேர்இயல் அரிவை","ஒள்இதழ் அவிழ்அகம் கடுக்கும் சீறடிப்","பல்சில கிண்கிணி சிறுபர(டு) அலைப்பக்","கொல்புனல் தளிரின் நடுங்குவனள் நின்றுநின்","எறியர் ஓக்கிய சிறுசெங் குவளை","ஈஎன இரப்பவும் ஒல்லாள் நீஎமக்(கு)","யாரை யோஎனப் பெயர்வோள் கைஅதை","கதும்என உருத்த நோக்கமோ(டு) அதுநீ","பாஅல் வல்லாய் ஆயினை பாஅல்","யாங்குவல் லுநையோ வாழ்கநின் கண்ணி","அகல்இரு விசும்பில் பகல்இடம் தாணஇயர்","தெறுகதிர் திகழ்தரும் உழுகெழு ஞாயிற்(று)","உருபுகிளர் வண்ணம் கொண்ட","வான்தோய் வெண்குடை வேந்தர்தம் எயிலே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says she has heard that the king's hand, which won the good war when his elephants and chariots spread into foreign land like a great ship turned from its course and his warriors reached the high world, bends only to give to suppliants and never opens to take; but now, when he came leading the thunangai dance by lamplight, giving his lead hand to others, his queen grew angry, trembling like a sprout in a flood, refused him the small red kuvalai he begged for, asked 'who are you to us?' and turned away with a hot look, so that he who is able at taking the parasol kings' forts is asked how he can be able here.","n":"The colophon's குரவைநிலை names the dance, and the poem sets a domestic oodal inside a praise of war and giving: the hand that never opens to receive (lines 11, 12) is the hand that begs a flower from the queen and is refused (line 23). The closing lines are hard; they seem to play the king's strength against forts against his helplessness before her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","eegai","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 52","lb":"PT52","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"குரவைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குரவை நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கொல்களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",4,"pira","simile","ship"],["முழாஇமிழ்","முழவு",14,"parai","scene",""],["துணங்கைக்குத்","துணங்கை",14,"yaazh","scene","dance"],["கோதை","கோதை",17,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தளிரின்","தளிர்",21,"maram","simile","sprout"],["குவளை","குவளை",22,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:53":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":47,"t":["வென்றுகலம் தாணஇயர் வேண்டுபுலத்(து) இறுத்தவர்","வாடா யாணர் நாடுதிறை கொடுப்ப","நல்கினை ஆகுமதி எம்என்(று) அருளிக்","கல்பிறங்கு வைப்பின் கட(று)அரை யாத்தநின்","தொல்புகழ் மூதூர்ச் செல்குவை யாயின்","செம்பொறிச் சிலம்பொ(டு) அணித்தழை தூங்கும்","எந்திரத் தகைப்பின் அம்(பு)உடை வாயில்","கோள்வல் முதலைய குண்டுகண் அகழி","வான்உற ஓங்கிய வளைந்துசெய் புரிசை","ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர் முனைகெட விலங்கி","நின்னின் தந்த மன்எயில் அல்லது","முன்னும் பின்னும்நின் முன்னோர் ஓம்பிய","எயில்முகப் படுத்தல் யாவது வளையினும்","பிறி(து)ஆறு செல்மதி சினம்கெழு குருசில்","எழூஉப்புறம் தாணஇப் பொன்பிணிப் பலகைக்","குழூஉநிலைப் புதவின் கதவுமெய் காணின்","தேம்பாய் கடாத்தொடு காழ்கை நீவி","வேங்கை வென்ற பொறிகிளர் புகர்நுதல்","ஏந்துகை சுருட்டித் தோட்டி நீவி","மேம்படு வெல்கொடி நுடங்கத்","தாங்கல் ஆகா ஆங்குநின் களிறே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that when the foes he camped against give tribute and he grants them grace and goes home to his ancient famous city, he should take another road and not lead his elephant past the forts his forebears guarded, with their trap-set gates, crocodile moats and sky-high walls, because, apart from the forts he won himself, his spotted-browed elephant, on seeing any gate of gold-bound planks, will brush off the goad, curl its trunk and charge with the banner waving, and none can hold it.","n":"The praise is a warning against the king's own city: the elephant has broken so many gates that it cannot tell a friendly gate from an enemy's, so the hyperbole flatters the army while seeming to fear it. வேங்கை (line 18) is read as the tiger whose stripes the brow outdoes; it could also be the vengai tree, whose spotted flowers are the usual image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","aran","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 53","lb":"PT53","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["முதலைய","முதலை",8,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["கடாத்தொடு","கடாம்",17,"maa","scene","must"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",18,"maa","scene","tiger"],["களிறே","களிறு",21,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:54":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":48,"t":["வள்ளியை என்றலின் காண்குவந் திசினே","உள்ளியது முடித்தி வாழ்கநின் கண்ணி","வீங்(கு)இறைத் தடைஅய அமைமருள் பணைத்தோள்","ஏந்(து)எழில் மழைக்கண் வனைந்துவரல் இளமுலைப்","பூந்துகில் அல்குல் தேம்பாய் கூந்தல்","மின்இழை விறலியர் நின்மறம் பாட","இரவலர் புன்கண் தீர நாள்தொறும்","உரைசால் நன்கலம் வரை(வு)இல வீசி","அனையை ஆகல் மாறே எனையதூஉம்","உயர்நிலை உலகத்துச் செல்லா(து) இவண்நின்(று)","இருநிலம் மருங்கின் நெடிதுமன் னியரோ","நிலந்தப விடூஉம் ஏணிப் புலம்படர்ந்து","படுகண் முரசம் நடுவண் சிலைப்பத்","தோமர வலத்தர் நாமம் செய்ம்மார்","ஏவல் வியம்கொண்(டு) இளையரொ(டு) எழுதரும்","ஒல்லார் யானை காணின்","நில்லாத் தானை இறைகிழ வோயே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says she came to see the king because people called him a giver, wishes that he achieve what he intends and that his garland live, and since every day he scatters choice vessels without limit to end the suppliants' distress while the bright-jewelled viralis sing his valour, she wishes that he not go to the high world but abide long here on the broad earth, lord of an army that, when the drum sounds and the lancers rise with the young men, will not stand still once it sees the foes' elephants.","n":"The blessing reverses the usual reward: the giver's merit would carry him to the high world, and the poet asks that he forgo it and stay where the suppliants are (lines 9 to 11). The colophon's காட்சிவாழ்த்து matches the first line: she came because of his name and blesses on seeing him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","marumai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 54","lb":"PT54","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காட்சிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காட்சி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அமைமருள்","அமை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["மழைக்கண்","மழை",4,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",6,"seythi","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",13,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",16,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:55":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":49,"t":["ஆன்றோள் கணவ சான்றோர் புரவல","நின்நயந்து வந்தனென் அடுபோர்க் கொற்றவ","இன்இசைப் புணரி இரங்கும் பெளவத்து","நன்கல வெறுக்கை துஞ்சும் பந்தர்க்","கமழும் தாழைக் கானல்அம் பெருந்துறைத்","தண்கடல் படப்பை நல்நாட்டுப் பொருந","செவ்வூன் தோன்றா வெண்துவை முதிரை","வால்ஊன் வல்சி மழவர் மெய்ம்மறை","குடவர் கோவே கொடித்தேர் அண்ணல்","வாரார் ஆயினும் இரவலர் வேணடித்","தேரின் தந்(து)அவர்க்(கு) ஆர்பதம் நல்கும்","நகைசால் வாய்மொழி இசைசால் தோன்றல்","வேண்டுவ அளவையுள் யாண்டுபல கழியப்","பெய்துபுறம் தந்து பொங்கல் ஆடி","விண்டுச் சேர்ந்த வெண்மழை போலச்","சென்றா லியரோ பெரும அல்கலும்","நனம்தலை வேந்தர் தார்அழிந்(து) அலற","நீடுவரை அடுக்கத்த நாடுகைக் கொண்டு","பொருதுசினம் தணிந்த செருப்புகல் ஆண்மைத்","தாங்குநர்த் தகைத்த வொள்வாள்","ஓங்கல் உள்ளத்துக் குருசில்நின் நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the king as husband of the noble queen, protector of the worthy, lord of the cool sea-garden land of fragrant thaazhai shores, shield of the Mazhavar, king of the Kudavar, who even when suppliants do not come sends chariots to bring them and gives them food, and wishes that his days, beyond the measure of years he desires, may go like the white clouds that pour rain, protect the land and rise to rest on the hill, he whose anger cooled only after he took the high-hilled lands of wailing kings.","n":"The king does not wait to be asked (வாரார் ஆயினும், line 10): he fetches the suppliants. The blessing takes the rain cloud not as the model of giving but of a life that rests after giving, the cloud going up to the hill when its work is done. Lines 7 and 8 (the white pulse mash without red meat, the white meat as provision) read as the food of the Mazhavar warriors, whose shield he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vendhan","mazhai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 55","lb":"PT55","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புணரி","புணரி",3,"pira","scene","sea"],["தாழைக்","தாழை",5,"maram","scene",""],["முதிரை","முதிரை",7,"unavu","gift_food","pulse"],["வால்ஊன்","ஊன்",8,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["வெண்மழை","மழை",15,"pira","simile","cloud"]],"en":[["king","குடவர் கோவே",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:56":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":50,"t":["விழவுவீற்(று) இருந்த வியலுள் ஆங்கண்","கோடியர் முழவின் முன்னர் ஆடல்","வல்லான் அல்லன் வாழ்கஅவன் கண்ணி","வலம்படு முரசம் துவைப்ப வாள்உயர்த்(து)","இலங்கும் பூணன் பொலங்கொடி உழிஞையன்","மடம்பெரு மையின் உடன்றுமேல் வந்த","வேந்துமெய்ம் மறந்த வாழ்ச்சி","வீந்(து)உகு போர்க்களத்(து) ஆடும் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the king is no skilled dancer before the drums of the dancers in the wide festival ground, long live his garland, but when the victory drum sounds, with sword raised, shining ornaments and a golden uzhinai garland, he is the king who dances on the battlefield where the kings who attacked him in their great folly lose their lives.","n":"A mock dispraise: the denial of skill at the festival sets up the dance that counts, the victory dance with the bright sword that the colophon's ஒள்வாள் அமலை names. The uzhinai garland (line 5) is the siege flower, so the field he dances on is one where he took forts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 56","lb":"PT56","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"ஒள்வாள் அமலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஒள்வாள் அமலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["முழவின்","முழவு",2,"parai","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""],["உழிஞையன்","உழிஞை",5,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:57":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":51,"t":["ஓடாப் பூட்கை மறவர் மிடல்தப","இரும்பனம் புடையலொடு வான்கழல் சிவப்பக்","குருதி பனிற்றும் புலவுக்களத் தோனே","துணங்கை ஆடிய வலம்படு கோமான்","மெல்லிய வகுந்தில் சீறடி ஒதுங்கிச்","செல்லா மோதில் சில்வளை விறலி","பாணர் கையது பணிதொடை நரம்பின்","விரல்கவர் பேரியாழ் பாலை பண்ணிக்","குரல்புணர் இன்னிசைத் தழிஞ்சி பாடி","இளம்துணைப் புதல்வர் நல்வளம் பயந்த","வளங்கெழு குடைச்சூல் அடங்கிய கொள்கை","ஆன்ற அறிவின் தோன்றிய நல்லிசை","ஒண்நுதல் மகளிர் துனித்த கண்ணினும்","இரவலர் புன்க ணஞ்சும்","புர(வு)எதிர் கொள்வனைக் கண்டனம் வரற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker urges the small-bangled virali, walking on soft paths on her small feet, to tune the paalai mode on the bards' great lute, sing the thazhinji, and come with her, for they shall go to see the victorious king who danced the thunangai on the reeking battlefield where blood reddens his anklet and palmyra garland, husband of the restrained and wise queen who bore young sons, who fears the distress of suppliants more than the sulking eyes of bright-browed women and welcomes those who come for his support.","n":"The opening formula செல்லா மோதில் (line 6) is read here as செல்லாமோ தில், 'shall we not go', the same words that open Purananuru 64. The king's anxiety is ranked: the suppliant's sad face is a greater fear to him than the oodal of the women of his house (lines 13, 14).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 57","lb":"PT57","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலி ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["இரும்பனம்","பனை",2,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",4,"yaazh","scene","dance"],["பேரியாழ்","யாழ்",8,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாலை","பாலை",8,"yaazh","scene","musical mode"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:58":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":52,"t":["ஆடுக விறலியர் பாடுக பரிசிலர்","வெண்தோட்(டு) அசைத்த ஒண்பூங் குவளையர்","வாள்முகம் பொறித்த மாண்வரி யாக்கையர்","செல்உறழ் மறவர்தம் கொல்படைத் தாணஇயர்","இன்(று)இனிது நுகர்ந்தனம் ஆயின் நாளை","மண்புனை இஞ்சி மதில்கடந்(து) அல்ல(து)","உண்குவம் அல்லேம் புகாஎனக் கூறிக்","கண்ணி கண்ணிய வயவர் பெருமகன்","பொய்படு(பு) அறியா வயங்குசெந் நாவின்","எயில்எறி வல்வில் ஏவிளங்கு தடக்கை","ஏந்(து)எழில் ஆகத்துச் சான்றோர் மெய்ம்மறை","வான வரம்பன் என்ப கானத்துக்","கறங்(கு)இசைச் சிதடி பொரிஅரைப் பொருந்திய","சிறிஇலை வேலம் பெரிய தோன்றும்","புன்புலம் வித்தும் வன்கை வினைஞர்","சீர்உடைப் பல்பக(டு) ஒலிப்பப் பூட்டி","நாஞ்சில் அடிய கொழுவழி மருங்கின்","அலங்குகதிர்த் திருமணி பெறூஉம்","அகன்கண் வைப்பின் நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"Let the viralis dance and the gift-seekers sing, says the poet: the great lord of the warriors, who wear kuvalai on white palm leaf and bear sword scars and swore that if they feast today they will not eat tomorrow until they cross the earthen fort wall, the truthful-tongued, fort-breaking archer, shield of the worthy, is called Vaanavaramban, lord of a wide land where on dry ground under the small-leaved velam the farmers yoke their oxen and the ploughshare turns up bright gems.","n":"The vow (lines 5 to 7) belongs to the warriors, not the king, and binds their next meal to the taking of the wall. The poem ends on a plough that turns up gems in poor dry land, so the warlike king is finally praised as lord of farmers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vanjinam","padai","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 58","lb":"PT58","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குவளையர்","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["சிதடி","சிதடி",13,"pira","scene","cicada"],["வேலம்","வேலம்",14,"maram","scene",""],["பல்பக(டு)","பகடு",16,"maa","scene","ox"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",15,"seythi","scene","farmers"],["திருமணி","மணி",18,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[["king","வான வரம்பன்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:59":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":53,"t":["பகல்நீ(டு) ஆகா(து) இரவுப்பொழுது பெருகி","மாசி நின்ற மாகூர் திங்கள்","பனிச்சுரம் படரும் பாண்மகன் உவப்பப்","புல்இருள் விடியப் புலம்புசேண் அகலப்","பாய்இருள் நீங்கப் பல்கதிர் பரப்பி","ஞாயிறு குணமுதல் தோன்றி யாஅங்(கு)","இரவன் மாக்கள் சிறுகுடி பெருக","உலகம் தங்கிய மேம்படு கற்பின்","வில்லோர் மெய்ம்மறை வீற்(று)இரும் கொற்றத்துச்","செல்வர் செல்வ சேர்ந்தோர்க் கரணம்","அறியா(து) எதிர்ந்து துப்பின் குறைஉற்றுப்","பணிந்துதிறை தருபநின் பகைவர் ஆயின்","சினம்செலத் தணியுமோ வாழ்கநின் கண்ணி","பல்வேறு வகைய நனம்தலை ஈண்டிய","மலையவும் கடலவும் பண்ணியம் பகுக்கும்","ஆறுமுட்(டு) உறாஅ(து) அறம்புரிந்(து) ஒழுகும்","நாடல் சான்ற துப்பின் பணைத்தோள்","பாடுசால் நன்கலம் தரூஉம்","நாடுபுறந் தருதல் நினக்குமார் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"As in the cold month of Maasi, when the night is long, the sun rises in the east and spreads its rays to end the darkness and gladden the bard crossing the cold wastes, so the king makes the small homes of suppliants grow; the poet asks whether his anger will cool if foes who opposed him without knowing his refuge bow and pay tribute, and tells him that it is his duty too to protect the land where goods of hill and sea are shared out and the roads are kept unblocked in the way of dharma.","n":"The last lines (16 to 19) turn praise into a statement of duty (கடனே): keeping the roads open so the goods of hill and sea can be exchanged is named as the king's charge, beside his giving. The simile is timed: the sun of the long Maasi night is the one most longed for, as the bard on the cold road longs for the patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","naadu","aram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 59","lb":"PT59","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஞாயிறு","ஞாயிறு",6,"pira","simile","sun"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:60":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":54,"t":["கொல்வினை மேவற்றுத் தானை; தானே","இகல்வினை மேவலன் தண்டாது வீசும்","செல்லா மோதில் பாண்மகள் காணியர்","மிஞிறுபுறம் மூசவும் தீம்சுவை திரியா(து)","அரம்போழ் கல்லா மரம்படு தீம்கனி","அம்சே(று) அமைந்த முண்டை விளைபழம்","ஆறுசெல் மாக்கட்(கு) ஓய்தகை தடுக்கும்","மறாஅ விளையுள் அறாஅ யாணர்த்","தொடைமடி களைந்த சிலைஉடை மறவர்","பொங்குபிசிர்ப் புணரி மங்குலொடு மயங்கி","வருங்கடல் ஊதையின் பனிக்கும்","துவ்வா நறவின் சாயினத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the bard woman whether they shall not go to see the king, whose army is set on killing and who is himself set on war yet gives without ceasing, where sweet fruit that bees swarm over and ripe fruit with honeyed pulp end the weariness of travellers, the harvest never fails, and warriors with unstrung bows live by the sea whose spray mingles with the mist and whose wind brings cold.","n":"The first line sets the king's killing and his giving side by side without joining them. The last line (துவ்வா நறவின் சாயினத் தானே) may name the place where he stays, perhaps the Chera port Naravu, or speak of toddy; the reading is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 60","lb":"PT60","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலி ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மிஞிறுபுறம்","மிஞிறு",4,"pira","scene","bee"],["தீம்கனி","கனி",5,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"],["விளைபழம்","பழம்",6,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"],["புணரி","புணரி",10,"pira","scene","sea"],["ஊதையின்","ஊதை",11,"pira","scene","wind"],["நறவின்","நறவு",12,"unavu","scene","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:patikam:6":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":55,"t":["குடக்கோ நெடுஞ்சேர லாதற்கு வேஎள்","ஆவிக் கோமான் தேவி ஈன்றமகன்","தண்டாரணியத்துக் கோள்பட்ட வருடையைத்","தொண்டியுள் தந்து கொடுப்பித்துப் பார்ப்பார்க்குக்","கபிலையொடு குடநாட்(டு) ஓரூர் ஈத்து","வான வரம்பன்எனப் பேர்இனிது விளக்கி","ஏனை மழவரைச் செருவின் சுருக்கி","மன்னரை ஓட்டிக்","குழவிகொள் வாரின் குடிபுறந் தந்து","நாடல் சான்ற நயன்உடை நெஞ்சின்","ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதனை","யாத்த செய்யுள் அடங்கிய கொள்கைக்","காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","பாடினார் பத்துப் பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Adukotpattu Cheralathan, son of Kudakko Nedunjeralathan by the queen born of the Vel Avi line, brought to Thondi the goats seized in Dandaranya and had them given, gave the Brahmins tawny cows and a village in Kudanadu, made the name Vaanavaramban shine, crushed the other Mazhavar in war, drove off kings, protected his people as one tends an infant, and had a just heart, and that Kakkaipadiniyar Nachchellaiyar sang this ten.","n":"The preface ties the king's name to a seizure of goats (வருடை, line 3; ஆடு கோள்), while the songs play on ஆடு as dance (52, 56, 57); the two explanations sit side by side in the same decade. The title Vaanavaramban, which 58 gives him in the songs, is here earned by a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","niraikol","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-14","lb":"PTpatikam:1-14","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["வருடையைத்","வருடை",3,"maa","scene","mountain goat"],["கபிலையொடு","கபிலை",5,"maa","gift_food","tawny cow"]],"en":[["king","குடக்கோ நெடுஞ்சேர லாதற்கு",1],["town","தொண்டியுள்",4],["king","ஆடுகோட் பாட்டுச் சேரலாதனை",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:5"},"pt:61":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":56,"t":["பலாஅம் பழுத்த பசும்புண் அரியல்","வாடை துரக்கும் நாடுகெழு பெருவிறல்","ஓவத் தன்ன வினைபுனை நல்லில்","பாவை அன்ன நல்லோள் கணவன்","பொன்னின் அன்ன பூவின் சிறிஇலைப்","புன்கால் உன்னத்துப் பகைவன் எங்கோ","புலர்ந்த சாத்தின் புலரா ஈகை","மலர்ந்த மார்பின் மாவண் பாரி","முழவுமண் புலர இரவலர் இனைய","வாராச் சேண்புலம் படர்ந்தோன் அளிக்(க)என","இரக்கு வாரேன் எஞ்சிக் கூறேன்","ஈத்த(து) இரங்கான் ஈத்தொறும் மகிழான்","ஈத்தொறும் மாவள் ளியன்என நுவலும்நின்","நல்இசை தரவந் திசினே ஒள்வாள்","உரவுக் களிற்றுப் புலாஅம் பாசறை","நிலவின்அன்ன வெள்வேல் பாடினி","முழவின் போக்கிய வெண்கை","விழவின் அன்னநின் கலிமகி ழானே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar tells the Chera, lord of a land where the north wind carries the smell of toddy from ripe jackfruit and husband of a woman like a painted doll in a house like a painting, that since Pari of the broad chest, whose giving never dried though his sandal paste did, has gone to the far land from which no one returns, so that the drums' clay has dried and suppliants grieve, he has not come begging nor will he say less than the truth: what drew him was the king's fame, which says he does not regret what he gave nor exult each time he gives, and so he has come to the camp, rank with the flesh of strong elephants, where the singing woman's clapping hands stand in for the drum, to share a joy like a festival.","n":"The poem opens the decade with Kapilar's own history: Pari is dead (line 10, வாராச் சேண்புலம் படர்ந்தோன்), and the new patron is measured by a formula of giving without regret or elation (lines 12-13) that sounds like Pari's. Calling the Chera the enemy of the unnam (line 6), the omen tree, makes him a king who does not wait on signs. Line 11 is the bard's own ethic: he will neither beg nor trim his words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 61","lb":"PT61","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காட்சிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காட்சி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பலாஅம்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["அரியல்","அரியல்",1,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["வாடை","வாடை",2,"pira","scene","north wind"],["உன்னத்துப்","உன்னம்",6,"maram","scene","unnam tree"],["சாத்தின்","சாந்து",7,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["முழவுமண்","முழவு",9,"parai","absent",""],["களிற்றுப்","களிறு",15,"maa","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",17,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:62":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":57,"t":["இழைஅணிந்(து) எழுதரும் பல்களிற்றுத் தொழுதியொடு","மழைஎன மருளும் மாஇரும் பஃறோல்","எஃகுபடை அறுத்த கொய்சுவல் புரவியொடு","மைந்(து)உடை ஆர்எயில் புடைபட வளைஇ","வந்துபுறத்(து) இறுக்கும் பசும்பிசிர் ஒள்அழல்","ஞாயிறு பல்கிய மாயமொடு சுடர்திகழ்","பொல்லா மயலொடு பாடிமிழ்(பு) இழிதரும்","மடங்கல் வண்ணம் கொண்ட கடுந்திறல்","துப்புத்துறை போகிய கொற்ற வேந்தே","புனல்பொரு கிடங்கின் வரைபோல் இஞ்சி","அணங்(கு)உடைத் தடக்கையர் தோட்டி செப்பிப்","பணிந்துதிறை தருபநின் பகைவர் ஆயின்","புல்உடை வியன்புலம் பல்ஆ பரப்பி","வளன்உடைச் செறுவின் விளைந்தவை உதிர்ந்த","களன்அறு குப்பை காஞ்சிச் சேர்த்தி","அரியல் ஆர்கை வன்கை வினைநர்","அருவி ஆம்பல் மலைந்த சென்னியர்","ஆடுசிறை வரிவண்(டு) ஓப்பும்","பாடல் சான்றஅவர் அகன்தலை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the victorious king, fierce as a lion, whose ornamented elephants, shields many as clouds and horses with cropped manes surround strong forts and whose fire falls on them as if suns had multiplied, that if his enemies behind moats and hill-like walls name their goads and bow and pay tribute, then their broad land will remain one fit for song, with cattle spread over grassy fields, heaps of threshed grain piled by the kaanji trees, and strong-handed toddy-drinking workers, crowned with stream lilies, waving off the striped bees.","n":"The fertile farm scene of lines 13-19 is not description of the king's own land but the reward held out to an enemy who submits; the poem stops before saying what happens otherwise, and the siege of lines 1-7 has already said it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","naadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 62","lb":"PT62","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பல்களிற்றுத்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["மழைஎன","மழை",2,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["பல்ஆ","ஆ",13,"maa","scene","cattle"],["காஞ்சிச்","காஞ்சி",15,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"],["அரியல்","அரியல்",16,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வினைநர்","வினைநர்",16,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["அருவி","அருவி",17,"pira","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",17,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["வரிவண்(டு)","வண்டு",18,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:63":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":58,"t":["பார்ப்பார்க்(கு) அல்லது பணி(பு)அறி யலையே","பணியா உள்ளமொ(டு) அணிவரக் கெழீஇ","நட்டோர்க்கு அல்லது கண்அஞ் சலையே","வணங்குசிலை பொருதநின் மணம்கமழ் அகலம்","மகளிர்க்(கு) அல்லது மலர்ப்(பு)அறி யலையே","நிலம்திறம் பெயரும் காலை ஆயினும்","கிளந்த சொல்நீ பொய்ப்(பு)அறி யலையே","சிறிஇலை உழிஞைத் தெரியல் சூடிக்","கொண்டி மிகைபடத் தண்தமிழ் செறித்துக்","குன்றுநிலை தளர்க்கும் உருமின் சீறி","ஒருமுற்(று) இருவர் ஓட்டிய ஒள்வாள்","செருமிகு தானை வெல்போ ரோயே","ஆடுபெற்(று) அழிந்த மள்ளர் மாறி","நீகண் டனையேம் என்றனர் நீயும்","நும்நுகம் கொண்டினும் வென்றோய் அதனால்","செல்வக் கோவே சேரலர் மருக","கால்திரை எடுத்த முழங்குகுரல் வேலி","நனம்தலை உலகஞ் செய்தநன்(று) உண்(டு)எனின்","அடைஅடுப்(பு) அறியா அருவி ஆம்பல்","ஆயிர வெள்ள ஊழி","வாழி யாத வாழிய பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the Chera that he bows only to brahmins, his eyes are shy only before friends, his fragrant chest scarred by the bow opens only to women, and his spoken word will not fail even if the earth changes its nature; wearing the small-leaved uzhinai garland, crushing the Tamil foes with plunder and raging like thunder that shakes hills, he drove off two kings in one siege, so that the defeated warriors said they had seen his like, and since he won even with their own yoke, the poet wishes the lord of the Cheras, if any good has been done in this sea-girt world, a life of countless ages.","n":"Four lines end alike (அல்லது ... அறியலையே): the poem defines the king by his four exceptions, so his pride, fear, love and truth each have one bounded outlet. The uzhinai garland of line 8 marks the deed as a siege.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","maram","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 63","lb":"PT63","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["உழிஞைத்","உழிஞை",8,"maram","ornament","uzhinai flower"],["உருமின்","உரும்",10,"pira","scene","thunder"],["அருவி","அருவி",19,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செல்வக் கோவே",16],["king","சேரலர்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:64":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":59,"t":["வலம்படு முரசின் வாய்வாள் கொற்றத்துப்","பொலம்பூண் வேந்தர் பலர்தில் அம்ம","அறம்கரைந்து வயங்கிய நாவின் பிறங்கிய","உரைசால் வேள்வி முடித்த கேள்வி","அந்தணர் அரும்கலம் ஏற்ப நீர்பட்(டு)","இரும்சே(று) ஆடிய மணல்மலி முற்றத்துக்","களிறுநிலை முணைஇய தார்அரும் தகைப்பின்","புறம்சிறை வயிரியர்க் காணின் வல்லே","எஃகுபடை அறுத்த கொய்சுவல் புரவி","அலங்கும் பாண்டில் இழைஅணிந்(து) ஈம்என","ஆனாக் கொள்கையை ஆதலின் அவ்வயின்","மாஇரு விசும்பில் பல்மீன் ஒளிகெட","ஞாயிறு தோன்றி யாங்கு மாற்றார்","உறுமுரண் சிதைத்தநின் நோன்தாள் வாழ்த்திக்","காண்கு வந்திசின் கழல்தொடி அண்ணல்","மைபடு மலர்க்கழி மலர்ந்த நெய்தல்","இதழ்வனப்(பு) உற்ற தோற்றமொ(டு) உயர்ந்த","மழையினும் பெரும்பயம் பொழிதி அதனால்","பசிஉடை ஒக்கலை ஒரீஇய","இசைமேம் தோன்றல்நின் பாசறை யானே"],"p":"","r":"Many kings have drums of victory and true swords, says the poet, but this one, after a renowned sacrifice whose brahmins with tongues that proclaim dharma took rare vessels, has a fixed rule that when he sees musicians at the guarded gate of his sandy courtyard, where elephants wallowed in the mud of poured water, he says at once 'give them horses with cropped manes, swaying chariots and jewels'; so, as the rising sun puts out the many stars, the poet has come to praise the strong foot that broke his foes, a lord who, with the look of neythal blooming in the dark backwater, pours more benefit than the high cloud and has ended the hunger of the hungry kin.","n":"The sun-and-stars simile answers lines 1-2: other kings also have drums and swords, so what makes this one the sun is the gift at the gate, not the war. The giving begins at a sacrifice, to brahmins, and passes at once to musicians.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","andhanar","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 64","lb":"PT64","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காட்சிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காட்சி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",5,"seythi","gift_food","brahmins"],["களிறுநிலை","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["வயிரியர்க்","வயிரியர்",8,"seythi","scene","musicians"],["புரவி","புரவி",9,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",16,"maram","simile",""],["மழையினும்","மழை",18,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:65":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":60,"t":["எறிபிணம் இடறிய செம்மறுக் குளம்பின்","பரிஉடை நல்மா விரிஉளை சூட்டி","மலைத்த தெவ்வர் மறம்தபக் கடந்த","காஞ்சி சான்ற வயவர் பெரும","வில்லோர் மெய்ம்மறை சேர்ந்தோர் செல்வ","பூண்அணிந்(து) எழிலிய வனைந்துவரல் இளமுலை","மாண்வரி அல்குல் மலர்ந்த நோக்கின்","வேய்புரை(பு) எழிலிய விளங்(கு)இறைப் பணைத்தோள்","காமர் கடவுளும் ஆளும் கற்பின்","சேண்நாறு நறுநுதல் சேஇழை கணவ","பாணர் புரவல பரிசிலர் வெறுக்கை","பூண்அணிந்து விளங்கிய புகழ்சால் மார்பநின்","நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை இனிதுகண் டிகுமே","தீம்தொடை நரம்பின் பாலை வல்லோன்","பையுள் உறுப்பின் பண்ணுப் பெயர்த்தாங்குச்","வேறுசெய் மாரியின் அளிக்கும்நின்","சாறுபடு திருவின் நனைமகி ழானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the king as chief of warriors worthy of kaanchi, whose plumed horses have hooves red from trampling corpses, wealth of those who shelter behind the bowmen's guard, husband of the red-jewelled lady whose breasts, waist, wide eyes, bamboo-like shoulders and fragrant brow are joined to a chastity that rules even the god of desire, protector of bards and treasure of gift-seekers, and says that he has happily seen the king's daily feasting assembly, where, as a master of the paalai mode on the sweet strings changes the mournful tune, the king's toddy-joy, rich in festival wealth, gives like a rain that changes things.","n":"The lute simile is exact about music: the master shifts the mournful (பையுள்) mode, so the king's gift is pictured as a change of key in the suppliant's life, not merely a quantity. Line 4 recurs at 90 line 39.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","karpu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 65","lb":"PT65","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"பரிசிற்றுறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசிற்றுறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["நல்மா","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["வேய்புரை(பு)","வேய்",8,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",14,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["பாலை","பாலை",14,"yaazh","simile","paalai mode"],["மாரியின்","மாரி",16,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:66":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":61,"t":["வாங்கிரு மருப்பின் தீம்தொடை பழுனிய","இடன்உடைப் பேரியாழ் பாலை பண்ணிப்","படர்ந்தனை செல்லும் முதுவாய் இரவல","இடிஇசை முரசமொ(டு) ஒன்றுமொழிந்(து) ஒன்னார்","வேல்உடைக் குழூஉச்சமம் ததைய நூறிக்","கொன்றுபுறம் பெற்ற பிணம்பயில் அழுவத்துத்","தொன்றுதிறை தந்த களிற்றொடு நெல்லின்","அம்பண அளவை விரிந்(து)உறை போகிய","ஆர்பதம் நல்கும் என்ப கறுத்தோர்","உறுமுரண் தாங்கிய தார்அரும் தகைப்பின்","நாள்மழைக் குழூஉச்சிமை கடுக்கும் தோன்றல்","தோல்மிசைத்(து) எழுதரும் விரிந்(து)இலங்(கு) எஃகின்","தார்புரிந் தன்ன வாள்உடை விழவின்","போர்படு மள்ளர் போந்தொடு தொடுத்த","கடவுள் வாகைத் துய்வீ ஏய்ப்பப்","பூத்த முல்லைப் புதல்சூழ் பறவை","கடத்திடைப் பிடவின் தொடைக்குலைச் சேக்கும்","வான்பளிங்கு விரைஇய செம்பரல் முரம்பின்","இலங்குகதிர்த் திருமணி பெறூஉம்","அகன்கண் வைப்பின் நாடுகிழ வோனே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells an old-tongued bard who goes playing the paalai mode on his great lute with its curved neck that the king, who vowed with his thundering drum and crushed the massed spears of his foes in a corpse-strewn field, gives, they say, plentiful food, elephants brought as old tribute and paddy measured in heaps, a leader like peaks clouded with morning rain who bore the enemy's onslaught, lord of a wide land where in the festival of swords warriors wear palm leaf strung with the god's tufted vaakai flower, birds, looking like those flowers, roost in the pidavu clusters by the flowering mullai of the forest, and bright gems are found in the red gravel mixed with crystal.","n":"The poem is a guide song in all but its colophon: it addresses the bard and promises food. Its landscape image folds war into land: the vaakai flower worn for victory is the vehicle for ordinary birds in a mullai thicket, so the king's country itself wears his victory flower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aatruppadai","parisil","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 66","lb":"PT66","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பேரியாழ்","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாலை","பாலை",2,"yaazh","scene","paalai mode"],["முரசமொ(டு)","முரசம்",4,"parai","scene",""],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",7,"maa","gift_food",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",7,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["நாள்மழைக்","மழை",11,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["போந்தொடு","போந்தை",14,"maram","ornament","palmyra leaf"],["வாகைத்","வாகை",15,"maram","simile",""],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",16,"maram","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",16,"pul","simile",""],["பிடவின்","பிடவு",17,"maram","scene",""],["திருமணி","மணி",19,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:67":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":62,"t":["கொடுமணம் பட்ட நெடுமொழி ஒக்கலொடு","பந்தர்ப் பெயரிய பேரிசை மூதூர்க்","கடன்அறி மரபின் கைவல் பாண","தெள்கடல் முத்தமொடு நன்கலம் பெறுகுவை","கொல்படை தெரிய வெல்கொடி நுடங்க","வயங்குகதிர் வயிரொடு வலம்புரி ஆர்ப்பப்","பல்களிற்(று) இனநிரை புலம்பெயர்ந்(து) இயல்வர","அமர்க்கண் அமைந்த அவிர்நிணப் பரப்பின்","குழூஉச்சிறை எருவை குருதி ஆரத்","தலைதுமிந்(து) எஞ்சிய வாள்மலி யூபமொ(டு)","உருவில் பேய்மகள் கவலை கவற்ற","நா(டு)உடன் நடுங்கப் பல்செருக் கொன்று","நா(று)இணர்க் கொன்றை வெண்போழ்க் கண்ணியர்","வாள்முகம் பொறித்த மாண்வரி யாக்கையர்","நெறிபடு மருப்பின் இரும்கண் மூரியொடு","வளைதலை மாத்த தாழ்கரும் பாசவர்","எஃ(கு)ஆ(டு) ஊனம் கடுப்பமெய் சிதைந்து","சாந்(து)எழில் மறைத்த சன்றோர் பெருமகன்","மலர்ந்த காந்தள் மாறா(து) ஊதிய","கடும்பறைத் தும்பி சூர்நசைத் தாஅய்ப்","பறைபண் அழியும் பாடுசால் நெடுவரைக்","கல்உயர் நேரிப் பொருநன்","செல்வக் கோமான் பாடினை செலினே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a skilled bard of the old city of Panthar, with his famed kin of Kodumanam, that he will receive sea pearls and fine jewels if he goes singing to the wealthy lord of high stony Neri, where the bee, sucking the open kaanthal without pause, loses its hum in dread of the hill's spirit; that lord of warriors who wear konrai garlands with white palm strips and bodies scarred by swords, hacked like the butchers' chopping block on which they cut great curved-horned bulls, has made the land tremble in many battles, with banners waving, horns and conches roaring, elephant herds moving, vultures drinking blood on fields spread with fat, headless trunks standing like sacrificial posts and the formless demon woman stirring dread.","n":"The promised gifts name their sources: Kodumanam and Panthar were known for beads and pearls, so the bard's home towns are themselves the treasure. The bee on Neri that cannot hum for dread is a quiet echo of the battlefield's terror, set in the king's own hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","parisil","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 67","lb":"PT67","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",4,"pira","gift_food","pearls"],["வயிரொடு","வயிர்",6,"parai","scene","horn"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",6,"parai","scene","conch"],["பல்களிற்(று)","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["எருவை","எருவை",9,"pul","scene","vulture"],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",11,"deyvam","scene","demoness"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",13,"maram","ornament",""],["மூரியொடு","மூரி",15,"maa","simile","bull"],["பாசவர்","பாசவர்",16,"seythi","simile","butchers"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",19,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",20,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[["town","கொடுமணம்",1],["town","பந்தர்",2],["hill","நேரி",22],["king","செல்வக் கோமான்",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:68":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":63,"t":["கால்கடிப்(பு) ஆகக் கடல்ஒலித் தாங்கு","வேறுபுலத்(து) இறுத்த கட்டூர் நாப்பண்","கடும்சிலை கடவும் தழங்குகுரல் முரசம்","அகல்இரு விசும்பின் ஆகத்(து) அதிர","வெவ்வரி நிலைஇய எயில்எறிந்(து) அல்ல(து)","உண்ணா(து) அடுக்கிய பொழுதுபல கழிய","நெஞ்சுபுகல் ஊக்கத்தர் மெய்தயங்(கு) உயக்கத்(து)","இன்னார் உறையுள் தாம்பெறின் அல்லது","வேந்(து)ஊர் யானை வெண்கோடு கொண்டு","கள்கொடி நுடங்கும் ஆவணம் புக்(கு)உடன்","அருங்கள் நொடைமை தீர்ந்தபின் மகிழ்சிறந்து","நாமம் அறியா ஏம வாழ்க்கை","வடபுலம் வாழ்நரின் பெரி(து)அமர்ந்(து) அல்கலும்","இன்நகை மேய பல்உறை பெறுபகொல்","பாயல் இன்மையின் பாசிழை ஞெகிழ","நெடுமண் இஞ்சி நீள்நகர் வரைப்பின்","ஓ(வு)உறழ் நெடும்சுவர் நாள்பல எழுதிச்","செவ்விரல் சிவந்த அவ்வரிக் குடைச்சூல்","அணங்(கு)எழில் அரிவையர்ப் பிணிக்கும்","மணம்கமழ் மார்பநின் தாள்நிழ லோரே"],"p":"","r":"In the camp pitched in a foreign land, where the drum beaten with fierce sticks roars through the sky like the sea with the wind for its drumstick, the king's men, who will not eat until they have stormed the fort, pass many days eager at heart and weary in body, and wonder whether, once they have taken the enemy's dwelling, they will carry off the white tusks of his riding elephants, pay with them in the market of fluttering toddy flags, and, more content than the nameless untroubled dwellers of the northern land, have many sweet delights each day; meanwhile, in the long-walled palace, sleepless women whose green jewels slip draw the passing days on the painted walls until their red fingers redden, women bound by the king's fragrant chest.","n":"The poem holds two waits side by side: the warriors fasting until the fort falls and the women marking days on the wall. The akam figure of the woman counting days (compare Kural 1261) is set inside a war poem, where it measures the length of the siege. Who the wall-marking women are, the king's or the warriors', is not said; line 19 binds them to the king's chest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 68","lb":"PT68","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசம்",3,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["வெண்கோடு","கோடு",9,"maa","scene","tusk"],["அருங்கள்","கள்",11,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:69":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":64,"t":["மலைஉறழ் யானை வான்தோய் வெல்கொடி","வரைமிசை அருவியின் வயின்வயின் நுடங்கக்","கடல்போல் தானைக் கடுங்குரல் முரசம்","கால்உறு கடலின் கடிய உரற","எறிந்துசிதைந்த வாள்","இலை தெரிந்த வேல்","பாய்ந்(து)ஆய்ந்த மா","ஆய்ந்துதெரிந்த புகல்மறவரொடு","படுபிணம் பிறங்க நூறிப் பகைவர்","கெடுகுடி பயிற்றிய கொற்ற வேந்தே","நின்போல், அசைவில் கொள்கையர் ஆகலின் அசையா(து)","ஆண்டோர் மன்றஇம் மண்கெழு ஞாலம்","நிலம்பயம் பொழியச் சுடர்சினம் தணியப்","பயங்கெழு வெள்ளி ஆநியம் நிற்ப","விசும்புமெய் அகலப் பெயல்புர(வு) எதிர","நால்வேறு நனம்தலை ஓராங்கு நந்த","இலங்குகதிர்த் திகிரி முந்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the victorious king, whose sky-touching banners on mountain-like elephants wave like waterfalls on peaks, whose drum roars like the wind-struck sea before an army like the sea, and who with broken swords, chosen spears, tested horses and eager warriors heaped corpses and made his enemies' families wander ruined, that those who rolled the bright wheel of rule before him surely ruled this earth because, like him, they were of unwavering purpose, and in their time the land poured its yield, the sun's heat softened, Venus stood in its good place, the sky widened and the rains answered the people's need, and the four quarters prospered together.","n":"The comparison runs backwards: the ancestors are said to be like the present king (நின்போல், line 11), so he is the standard and they the copies. The good signs of lines 13-17 (the earth's yield, the gentle sun, Venus in place, timely rain) are the marks of rule that the second decade gives to the present king's land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","sengol","mazhai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 69","lb":"PT69","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["அருவியின்","அருவி",2,"pira","simile",""],["கடல்போல்","கடல்",3,"pira","simile",""],["முரசம்","முரசம்",3,"parai","scene",""],["கடலின்","கடல்",4,"pira","simile",""],["மா","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:70":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":65,"t":["களிறுகடைஇய தாள்","மாஉடற்றிய வடிம்பு","சமம்ததைந்த வேல்","கல்அலைத்த தோள்","வில்அலைத்த நல்வலத்து","வண்(டு)இசை கடாவாத் தண்பனம் போந்தைக்","குவிமுகிழ் ஊசி வெண்தோடு கொண்டு","தீம்சுனை நீர்மலர் மலைந்து மதம்செருக்கி","உடைநிலை நல்அமர் கடந்து மறம்கெடுத்துக்","கடும்சின வேந்தர் செம்மல் தொலைத்த","வலம்படு வான்கழல் வயவர் பெரும","நகையினும் பொய்யா வாய்மைப் பகைவர்","புறம்சொல் கேளாப் புரைதீர் ஒண்மைப்","பெண்மை சான்று பெருமடம் நிலைஇக்","கற்(பு)இறை கொண்ட கமழும் சுடர்நுதல்","புரையோள் கணவ பூண்கிளர் மார்ப","தொலையாக் கொள்கை சுற்றம் சுற்ற","வேள்வியின் கடவுள் அருத்தினை கேள்வி","உயர்நிலை உலகத்(து) ஐயர்இன்(பு) உறுத்தினை","வணங்கிய சாயல் வணங்கா ஆண்மை","இளம்துணைப் புதல்வரின் முதியர்ப் பேணித்","தொல்கடன் இறுத்த வெல்போர் அண்ணல்","மாடோர் உறையும் உலகமும் கேட்ப","இழும்என இழிதரும் பறைக்குரல் அருவி","முழுமுதல் மிசைய கோடுதொறும் துவன்றும்","அயிரை நெடுவரை போலத்","தொலையா(து) ஆகநீ வாழும் நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the king whose feet drove elephants, whose arrows harmed horses, whose spear crowded the battle and whose shoulders and strong hand bent the bow, who wears the palmyra's white leaf that bees do not hum over and flowers of sweet springs, defeated kings and destroyed their pride, husband of a lady of chastity who is true even in jest and never hears slander, and says that with his unfailing kin around him he fed the gods with sacrifice, pleased the sages with learning, bent in gentleness but never in manhood, cared for elders more than for his young sons and paid the ancient debts, and wishes his days to be unending like high Ayirai, whose falls drop with a drum's voice heard even by the dwellers of heaven.","n":"Lines 17-22 list what the king owes and has paid: gods by sacrifice (வேள்வியின்), sages by learning (கேள்வி), and elders before sons; தொல்கடன் names it as an inherited debt, not a virtue. Line 20 (வணங்கிய சாயல் வணங்கா ஆண்மை) puts the decade's balance in one line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","andhanar","karpu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 70","lb":"PT70","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["களிறுகடைஇய","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["மாஉடற்றிய","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["வண்(டு)இசை","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["போந்தைக்","போந்தை",6,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["நீர்மலர்","மலர்",8,"maram","ornament",""],["பறைக்குரல்","பறை",24,"parai","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",24,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","அயிரை",26]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:patikam:7":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":66,"t":["மடியா உள்ளமொடு மாற்றோர்ப் பிணித்த","நெடுநுண் கேள்வி அந்துவற்(கு) ஒருதந்தை","ஈன்றமகள் பொறையன் பெருந்தேவி ஈன்றமகன்","நாடுபதி படுத்து நண்ணார் ஓட்டி","வெருவரு தானை கொடுசெருப் பலகடந்(து)","ஏத்தல் சான்ற இடன்உடை வேள்வி","ஆக்கிய பொழுதின் அறத்துறை போகி","மாய வண்ணனை மனன்உறப் பெற்றவற்(கு)","ஓத்திர நெல்லின் ஒகந்தூர் ஈத்துப்","புரோசு மயக்கி","மல்லல் உள்ளமொடு மாசற விளங்கிய","செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதனைக்","கபிலர் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Selvakkadungo Vazhiathan, son of Porayan's queen, who was the daughter of Anthuvan of unflagging heart and long, subtle learning who bound his foes, settled lands, drove off enemies, won many battles with a dreaded army, performed a praised and ample sacrifice, went the way of dharma, held Mayavannan in his heart, gave Okanthur with its paddy and outdid the priests, and shone spotless with a generous mind; and that Kapilar sang this ten.","n":"The preface names a gift the songs do not: a village, Okanthur, given after the sacrifice. Its devotion to Mayavannan sits beside the brahmin-facing duties of 63, 64 and 70.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai","pugazh","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-13","lb":"PTpatikam:1-13","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழிஆதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["வண்ணனை","மாயவண்ணன்",8,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",9,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"]],"en":[["king","அந்துவற்(கு)",2],["king","பொறையன்",3],["town","ஒகந்தூர்",9],["king","செல்வக்கடுங்கோ",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:6"},"pt:71":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":67,"t":["அறாஅ யாணர் அகன்கண் செறுவின்","அருவி ஆம்பல் நெய்தலொடு அரிந்து","செறுவினை மகளிர் மலிந்த வெக்கைப்","பரூஉப்பக(டு) உதிர்த்த மென்செந் நெல்லின்","அம்பண அளவை உறைகுவித் தாங்குக்","கடுந்தே(று) உறுகிளை மொசிந்தன துஞ்சும்","செழுங்கூடு கிளைத்த இளம்துணை மகாரின்","அலந்தனர் பெருமநின் உடற்றி யோரே","ஊர்எரி கவர உருத்(து)எழுந்(து) உரைஇப்","போர்சுடு கமழ்புகை மாதிரம் மறைப்ப","மதில்வாய்த், தோன்றல் ஈயாது தம்பழி ஊக்குநர்","குண்டுகண் அகழிய குறுந்தாள் ஞாயில்","ஆர்எயல் தோட்டி வெளவினை ஏறொடு","கன்(று)உடை ஆயம் தாணஇப் புகல்சிறந்து","புலவுவில் இளையர் அங்கை விடுப்ப","மத்துக்கயி(று) ஆடா வைகல்பொழுது நினையூஉ","ஆன்பயம் வாழ்நர் கழுவுள் தலைமடங்கப்","பதிபாழ் ஆகா வேறுபுலம் படர்ந்து","விருந்தின் வாழ்க்கையொடு பெருந்திரு அற்றென","அரும்சமத்(து) அருநிலை தாங்கிய புகர்நுதல்","பெரும்களிற்(று) யானையொ(டு) அரும்கலம் தராஅர்","மெய்பனி கூரா அணங்(கு)எனப் பராவலின்","பலிகொண்டு பெயரும் பாசம் போலத்","திறைகொண்டு பெயர்தி வாழ்கநின் ஊழி","உரவரும் மடவரும் அறிவுதெரிந்(து) எண்ணி","அறிந்தனை அருளாய் ஆயின்","யார்இவண் நெடுந்தகை வாழு மோரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Perunjeral Irumporai that those who provoked him suffered like small children who poked a rich hive where fierce bees sleep clustered like paddy heaped by the measure on the threshing floor: their towns burned, war smoke hid the quarters, their cattle with bulls and calves were driven off by his young archers, the herders' chief Kazhuvul bowed his head as churning ropes stood still at dawn, the people left for other lands to live as guests, and they brought him a great spot-browed elephant and rare jewels, praising him as a dread god, so that he takes tribute and leaves like a spirit that takes its offering and goes; had he not weighed the strong and the simple and shown grace, no one here would live.","n":"The opening simile is double: the bees cluster like heaped grain, and the enemies are the children who stirred the hive, so their ruin is told as the sting of provoked bees, not as the king's first move. The still churning ropes at dawn (line 16) measure the cattle raid by the silence of the herders' homes. The poem ends on grace, but a grace that comes after the burning and the tribute.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","thirai","arul","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 71","lb":"PT71","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தலொடு","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",3,"seythi","scene","field women"],["பரூஉப்பக(டு)","பகடு",4,"maa","scene","bull"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",4,"unavu","simile","paddy"],["கடுந்தே(று)","தேறு",6,"pira","simile","bees"],["செழுங்கூடு","கூடு",7,"pira","simile","hive"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",13,"maa","scene","bull"],["ஆயம்","ஆயம்",14,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["மத்துக்கயி(று)","மத்துக் கயிறு",16,"pira","absent","churning rope"],["ஆன்பயம் வாழ்நர்","ஆன்பயம் வாழ்நர்",17,"seythi","scene","herders"],["யானையொ(டு)","யானை",21,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[["king","கழுவுள்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:72":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":68,"t":["இகல்பெரு மையின் படைகோள் அஞ்சார்","சூழாது துணிதல் அல்லது வறி(து)உடன்","காவல் எதிரார் கறுத்தோர் நாடுநின்","முன்திணை முதல்வர்க்(கு) ஓம்பினர் உறைந்து","மன்பதை காப்ப அறிவுவலி உறுத்து","நன்(று)அறி உள்ளத்துச் சான்றோர் அன்னநின்","பண்புநன்(கு) அறியார் மடம்பெரு மையின்","துஞ்சல் உறூஉம் பகல்புகு மாலை","நிலம்பொறை ஒராஅநீர் ஞெமரவந்(து) ஈண்டி","உரவுத்திரை கடுகிய உருத்(து)எழு வெள்ளம்","வரையா மாதிரத்(து) இருள்சேர்பு பரந்து","ஞாயிறு பட்ட அகன்றுவரு கூட்டத்(து)","அஞ்சாறு புரையும் நின்தொழில் ஒழித்துப்","பொங்கு பிசிர்நுடக்கிய செஞ்சுடர் நிகழ்வின்","மடங்கல் தீயிஅ அனையை","சினம்கெழு குருசில்நின் உடற்றிசி னோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the angry lord that those who provoke him, from the greatness of their enmity, do not fear taking up arms, resolve without deliberating and will not accept watchfulness, and in their folly do not know his nature, which is like that of the wise who served the first lords of his line, guarded the people and joined strength to knowledge; to them he is like the doom-fire of the world's end with its red flame and leaping sparks, as when at the evening that brings sleep a raging flood with strong waves overruns the earth and darkness spreads over the boundless quarters.","n":"The poem sets two kinds of knowing against each other: the wise who know what is good (நன்றறி உள்ளத்துச் சான்றோர்) and the foes who do not know the king's nature from folly (மடம்பெருமையின்). The deluge and the doom-fire are both images of the world's end, so the king's anger is cosmic rather than personal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","therithal","vekuli","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 72","lb":"PT72","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["வெள்ளம்","வெள்ளம்",10,"pira","simile","flood"],["ஞாயிறு","ஞாயிறு",12,"pira","simile","sun"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:73":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":69,"t":["உரவோர் எண்ணினும் மடவோர் எண்ணினும்","பிறர்க்குநீ வாயின் அல்லது நினக்குப்","பிறர்உவமம் ஆகா ஒருபெரு வேந்தே","மருதம் சான்ற மலர்தலை விளைவயல்","செய்யுள் நாரை ஒய்யும் மகளிர்","இரவும் பகலும் பாசிழை களையார்","குறும்பல் யாணர்க் குரவை அயரும்","காவிரி மண்டிய சேய்விரி வனப்பிற்","புகாஅர்ச் செல்வ! பூழியர் மெய்ம்மறை!","கழைவிரிந்(து) எழுதரும் மழைதவழ் நெடுங்கோட்டுக்","கொல்லிப் பொருந! கொடித்தேர்ப் பொறைய!நின்","வளனும் ஆண்மையும் கைவண் மையும்","மாந்தர் அள(வு)இறந் தனஎனப் பல்நாள்","யான்சென்(று) உரைப்பவும் தேறார் பிறரும்","சான்றோர் உரைப்பத் தெளிகுவர் கொல்லென","ஆங்குமதி மருளக் காண்குவல்","யாங்(கு)உரைப் பேன்என வருந்துவல் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the one great king who is the measure of comparison for others while no other can be his, whether the strong or the simple reckon it, lord of Pukar on the Kaveri where women who drive herons from the marutham fields dance the kuravai day and night without taking off their jewels, shield of the Puzhiyar, lord of Kolli where bamboo grows and clouds creep, Poraiya of the bannered chariot, and says that though he has gone for many days to tell people that the king's wealth, manliness and giving exceed human measure, they do not believe him, and wondering whether they will believe it if the wise say so, he sees their minds bewildered and grieves at how to tell it.","n":"The praise turns on the failure of praise: the king is beyond comparison, so the poet's report sounds like exaggeration, and the poem ends in the poet's own distress (வருந்துவல் யானே). The Chera is hailed as lord of Pukar on the Kaveri, a Chola port, which the poem does not explain. Two lines are lost after line 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","perumai","vendhan","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 73","lb":"PT73","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மருதம்","மருதம்",4,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["நாரை","நாரை",5,"pul","scene",""],["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",5,"seythi","scene","field women"],["குரவை","குரவை",7,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["கழைவிரிந்(து)","கழை",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மழைதவழ்","மழை",10,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",8],["town","புகாஅர்ச்",9],["hill","கொல்லிப்",11],["king","பொறைய",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:74":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":70,"t":["கேள்வி கேட்டுப் படிவம் ஒடியாது","வேள்வி வேட்டனை உயர்ந்தோர் உவப்பச்","சாய்அறல் கடுக்கும் தாழ்இரும் கூந்தல்","வேறுபடு திருவின் நின்வழி வாழியர்","கொடுமணம் பட்ட வினைமாண் அருங்கலம்","பந்தர்ப் பயந்த பலர்புகழ் முத்தம்","வரைஅகம் நண்ணிக் குறும்பொறை நாடித்","தெரியுநர் கொண்ட சிர(று)உடைப் பைம்பொறிக்","கவைமரம் கடுக்கும் கவலைய மருப்பின்","புள்ளி இரலைத் தோல்ஊன் உதிர்த்துத்","தீதுகளைந்(து) எஞ்சிய திகழ்விடு பாண்டில்","பருதி போகிய புடைகிளை கட்டி","எஃ(கு)உடை இரும்பின் உள்அமைத்து வல்லோன்","சூடுநிலை உற்றுச் சுடர்விடு தோற்றம்","விசும்(பு)ஆடு மரபின் பருந்(து)ஊ(று) அளப்ப","நலம்பெறு திருமணி கூட்டு நல்தோள்","ஒடுங்(கு)ஈர் ஓதி ஒள்நுதல் கருவில்","எண்இயல் முற்றி ஈர்அறிவு புரிந்து","சால்பும் செம்மையும் உளப்படப் பிறவும்","காவற்(கு) அமைந்த அரசுதுறை போகிய","வீறுசால் புதல்வன் பெற்றனை இவணர்க்(கு)","அருங்கடன் இறுத்த செருப்புகல் முன்ப","அன்னவை மருண்டனென் அல்லேன் நின்வயின்","முழு(து)உணர்ந்(து) ஒழுக்கும் நரைமூ தாளனை","வண்மையும் மாண்பும் வளனும் எச்சமும்","தெய்வமும் யாவதும் தவம்உடை யோர்க்கென","வேறுபடு நனம்தலைப் பெயரக்","கூறினை பெருமநின் படிமை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that he heard the learned texts and performed sacrifices without breaking his vows, pleasing the high ones; that his queen, with long dark hair like rippled black sand, adorned with fine jewels made at Kodumanam, pearls from Panthar and a gem cut and set by a skilled smith, bore a distinguished son who completed his learning and is fit to rule, so that he has paid his debt to those of his line; yet the poet is not amazed by these, but by the king's vow that he told his grey-haired elder, who knew everything and guided him, that generosity, dignity, wealth, offspring, the divine and all else belong to those who have tapas, and sent him to the wide forest.","n":"The poem lists the goods a king is praised for (learning, sacrifice, a fine queen, a worthy son) only to set them aside (அன்னவை மருண்டனென் அல்லேன்): what amazes is the king's release of his old counsellor to austerity. The long middle passage on the making of a gem (lines 7 to 16) is the most obscure stretch of the decade.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kalvi","makkal","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 74","lb":"PT74","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கல்வி"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சாய்அறல்","அறல்",3,"pira","simile","dark sand"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",6,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["கவைமரம்","கவைமரம்",9,"maram","simile","forked branch"],["இரலைத்","இரலை",10,"maa","simile","stag"],["பருந்(து)ஊ(று)","பருந்து",15,"pul","scene","kite"],["திருமணி","மணி",16,"pira","ornament","gem"]],"en":[["town","கொடுமணம்",5],["town","பந்தர்ப்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:75":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":71,"t":["இரும்புலி கொன்று பெரும்களி(று) அடூஉம்","அரும்பொறி வயமான் அனையை பல்வேல்","பொலந்தார் யானை இயல்தேர்ப் பொறைய","வேந்தரும் வேளிரும் பிறரும் கீழ்ப்பணிந்து","நின்வழிப் படாஅர் ஆயின் நெல்மிக்(கு)","அறைஉறு கரும்பின் தீம்சேற்(று) யாணர்","வருநர் வரையா வளம்வீங்(கு) இருக்கை","வன்புலம் தழீஇ மென்பால் தோறும்","அரும்பறை வினைஞர் புல்இகல் படுத்துக்","கள்உடை நியமத்(து) ஒள்விலை கொடுக்கும்","வெள்வர(கு) உழுத கொள்உடைக் கரம்பைச்","செந்நெல் வல்சி அறியார் தத்தம்","பாடல் சான்ற வைப்பின்","நா(டு)உடன் ஆடல் யாவண(து) அவர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Poraiya of the gold-garlanded elephants and well-running chariots that he is like the strong striped beast that kills the great tiger and slays the big tusker, and that if kings, Velir and others do not bow and follow him, their seats rich in paddy and in the sweet juice of milled sugarcane, open to all who come, will be reduced to hard ground where labourers tend dry plots of white millet and horse gram and do not know red paddy rice, so where would they have their lands famed in song to sport in together.","n":"The threat is told through food: the rice and cane of the submissive lands against the millet and horse gram of the ruined ones. The middle lines (8 to 11) are hard to parse, and the contrast of fertile and barren land is the reading taken here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 75","lb":"PT75","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",1,"maa","simile","tiger"],["பெரும்களி(று)","களிறு",1,"maa","simile",""],["வயமான்","வயமா",2,"maa","simile","lion"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["நெல்மிக்(கு)","நெல்",5,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",6,"maram","scene",""],["அரும்பறை","பறை",9,"parai","scene",""],["கள்உடை","கள்",10,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["வெள்வர(கு)","வரகு",11,"maram","scene","millet"],["கொள்உடைக்","கொள்",11,"maram","scene","horse gram"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",12,"unavu","absent","red paddy"]],"en":[["king","பொறைய",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:76":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":72,"t":["களி(று)உடைப் பெரும்சமம் ததைய எஃ(கு)உயர்த்(து)","ஒளிறுவாள் மன்னர் துதைநிலை கொன்று","முரசுகடிப்(பு) அடைய அரும்துறை போகிப்","பெரும்கடல் நீந்திய மரம்வலி உறுக்கும்","பண்ணிய விலைஞர் போலப் புண்ஒரீஇப்","பெரும்கைத் தொழுதியின் வன்துயர் கழிப்பி","இரந்தோர் வாழ நல்கி இரப்போர்க்(கு)","ஈதல் தண்டா மாசித(று) இருக்கை","கண்டனென் செல்கு வந்தனென் கால்கொண்டு","கருவி வானம் தண்தளி சொரிந்தெனப்","பல்விதை உழவின் சில்ஏ ராளர்","பனித்துறைப் பகன்றைப் பாங்(கு)உடைத் தெரியல்","கழு(வு)உறு கலிங்கம் கடுப்பச் சூடி","இலங்குகதிர்த் திருமணி பெறூஉம்","அகன்கண் வைப்பின் நாடுகிழ வோயே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the lord of a land of wide fields that after the great battle with elephants, where he raised his spear, killed sword-flashing kings in their crowded ranks and crossed hard fords while the drum was struck, he healed his wounded men and ended the hard pain of his great host, as merchants who have crossed the sea refit their ship, and gave so that askers might live, never ceasing to give; the poet has come to see that faultless court, in a land where, when the clouds pour cool rain, the few ploughmen of many-seeded fields, wearing garlands of pakanrai from the cold ford white as washed cloth, find bright gems in their furrows.","n":"The merchant simile makes the care of the wounded army a refitting after a voyage: the king's war is an enterprise, and its wounds a cost to be made good. The ploughmen who turn up gems with the soil give the land's wealth as something that rises unasked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","padai","naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 76","lb":"PT76","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["களி(று)உடைப்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["முரசுகடிப்(பு)","முரசு",3,"parai","scene",""],["மரம்வலி","மரம்",4,"pira","simile","ship"],["விலைஞர்","விலைஞர்",5,"seythi","simile","merchants"],["வானம்","வானம்",10,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["உழவின்","உழவு",11,"seythi","scene","ploughing"],["பகன்றைப்","பகன்றை",12,"maram","ornament",""],["கலிங்கம்","கலிங்கம்",13,"pira","simile","cloth"],["திருமணி","மணி",14,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:77":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":73,"t":["எனைப்பெரும் படையனோ சினப்போர்ப் பொறையன்","எனறனிர் ஆயின் ஆறுசெல் வம்பலீர்","மன்பதை பெயர அரசுகளத்(து) ஒழியக்","கொன்றுதோள் ஓச்சிய வென்(று)ஆடு துணங்கை","மீபிணத்(து) உருண்ட தேயா ஆழியின்","பண்அமை தேரும் மாவும் மாக்களும்","எண்ணற்(கு) அருமையின் எண்ணின்றோ இலனே","கந்துகோள் ஈயாது காழ்பல முருக்கி","உகக்கும் பருந்தின் நிலத்துநிழல் சாடிச்","சேண்பரல் முரம்பின் ஈர்ம்படைக் கொங்கர்","ஆபரந் தன்ன செலவின்பல்","யானை காண்பல்அவன் தானை யானே."],"p":"","r":"To travellers who ask how large an army the angry Poraiyan has, the poet answers that after the people fled and kings fell on the field and his men danced the victory thunangai, his chariots whose wheels rolled over corpses, his horses and his men were beyond counting and he could not count them, and that he sees in his army many elephants that refuse the tethering post, snap their stakes and sweep with their shadow the kites wheeling over the ground, spreading like the cattle of the Kongar of wet spears over the gravel upland.","n":"The poem is an answer to a question, and its answer is that the army cannot be counted: the poet counts only the elephants, and those through a simile of spreading herds. The colophon calls it the clamour of a siege (உழிஞை அரவம்), but no fort is named in the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 77","lb":"PT77","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"உழிஞை அரவம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உழிஞை அரவம்"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",4,"yaazh","scene","victory dance"],["மாவும்","மா",6,"maa","scene","horse"],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",9,"pul","scene","kite"],["ஆபரந்","ஆ",11,"maa","simile","cattle"],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:78":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":74,"t":["வலம்படு முரசின் இலங்குவன விழூஉம்","அவ்வெள் அருவி உவ்வரை அதுவே","சில்வளை விறலி செல்குவை ஆயின்","வள்இதழ்த் தாமரை நெய்தலொ(டு) அரிந்து","மெல்இயல் மகளிர் ஒய்குவனர் இயலிக்","கிளிகடி மேவலர் புறவுதொறும் நுவலப்","பல்பயம் நிலைஇய கட(று)உடை வைப்பின்","வெல்போர் ஆடவர் மறம்புரிந்து காக்கும்","வில்பயில் இறும்பின் தகடூர் நூறி","பேஎ மன்ற பிறழநோக் கியவர்","ஓ(டு)உறு கடுமுரண் துமியச் சென்று","வெம்முனை தபுத்த காலைத் தம்நாட்(டு)","யாடுபரந் தன்ன மாவின்","ஆபரந் தன்ன யானையோன் குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a virali with a few bangles that if she goes on, the hill yonder, where a lovely white waterfall drops shining with a sound like the victorious drum, is the hill of the king who, in a land of many yields and forests where soft women cutting lotus with neythal call out in every grove to drive off parrots, razed Tagadur of bow-thick thickets guarded by valiant men, cut down the fierce enmity of those who glared at him and destroyed their hot front, and then had in his own land horses spread like goats and elephants spread like cattle.","n":"The guide poem points the dancer not to the king's town but to his hill, recognised by its waterfall that sounds like his war drum, so the landscape itself announces him. The herds of horses and elephants at the end are the spoils of Tagadur, which the virali can expect a share of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","maram","naadu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 78","lb":"PT78","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலி ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",1,"parai","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["சில்வளை","வளை",3,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",4,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தலொ(டு)","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",6,"pul","scene","parrot"],["யாடுபரந்","யாடு",13,"maa","simile","goat"],["மாவின்","மா",13,"maa","simile","horse"],["ஆபரந்","ஆ",14,"maa","simile","cattle"],["யானையோன்","யானை",14,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[["town","தகடூர்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:79":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":75,"t":["உயிர்போற் றலையே செருவத் தானே","கொடைபோற் றலையே இரவலர் நடுவண்","பெரியோர்ப் பேணிச் சிறியோரை அளித்தி","நின்வயின் பிரிந்த நல்இசை கனவினும்","பிறர்நசை அறியா வயங்குசெந் நாவின்","படியோர்த் தேய்த்த ஆண்மைத் தொடியோர்","தோளிடைக் குழைந்த கோதை மார்ப","அனைய அளப்(ப)அருங் குரையை அதனால்","நின்னொடு வாரார் தம்நிலத்(து) ஒழிந்து","கொல்களிற்(று) யானை எருத்தம் புல்லென","வில்குலை அறுத்துக் கோலின் வாரா","வெல்போர் வேந்தர் முரசுகண் போழ்ந்(து)அவர்","அர(சு)உவா அழைப்பக் கோ(டு)அறுத்(து) இயற்றிய","அணங்(கு)உடை மரபின் கட்டில்மேல் இருந்து","தும்பை சான்ற மெய்தயங்(கு) உயக்கத்து","நிறம்படு குருதி புறம்படின் அல்லது","மடைஎதிர் கொள்ளா அஞ்சுவரு மரபின்","கடவுள் அயிரையின் நிலைஇக்","கேடில ஆக பெரும்நின் புகழே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that he does not spare his life in battle and does spare nothing in giving among suppliants, honours the great and cherishes the small, that his fame has gone out from him whose shining tongue never knew desire for others' goods even in dream, whose manliness wore down his foes and whose chest bears a garland crushed by women's bangled arms, so that he is beyond measure; and therefore the victorious kings who would not come with him but stayed in their lands lost their elephants and bows, their drums were split and their royal elephant's tusks cut and made into a dread couch on which he sits, and he prays that the king's fame may stand unfailing like the hill of Ayirai, whose fearful god accepts no offering but the blood that spills from bodies shaken in battle.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are built on one verb, போற்றலையே: the king does not guard his life but guards his giving. The couch made from the tusks of a defeated king's royal elephant turns the enemy's emblem into the victor's seat. The closing simile ties the king's fame to a war god who takes only blood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","maram","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 79","lb":"PT79","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",7,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கொல்களிற்(று)","களிறு",10,"maa","scene",""],["முரசுகண்","முரசு",12,"parai","scene",""],["அர(சு)உவா","உவா",13,"maa","scene","royal elephant"],["தும்பை","தும்பை",15,"maram","scene",""],["குருதி","குருதி",16,"pira","scene","blood"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",18,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","அயிரையின்",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:80":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":76,"t":["வான்மருப்பின் களிற்றுயானை","மாமலையில் கணண்கொண்(டு)அவர்","எடுத்(து)எறிந்த விறல்முரசம்","கார்மழையின் கடிதுமுழங்கச்","சாந்துபுலர்ந்த வியல்மார்பிற்","தொடிசுடர்வரும் வலிமுன்கைப்","புண்உடை எறுழ்த்தோள் புடையல்அம் கழல்கால்","பிறக்(கு)அடி ஒதுங்காப் பூட்கை ஒள்வாள்","ஒடிவல் தெவ்வர் எதிர்நின்(று) உரைஇ","இடுக திறையே புர(வு)எதிர்ந் தோற்(கு)என","அம்(பு)உடை வலத்தர் உயர்ந்தோர் பரவ","அனையை ஆகல் மாறே பகைவர்","கால்கிளர்ந் தன்ன கதழ்பரிப் புரவிக்","கடும்பரி நெடுந்தேர் மீமிசை நுடங்குகொடி","புலவரைத் தோன்றல் யாவது சினப்போர்","நிலவரை நிறீஇய நல்லிசைத்","தொலையாக் கற்பநின் தெம்முனை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that since his warriors, with broad chests where the sandal has dried, bangled strong forearms, wounded mighty shoulders and anklets, who never step back and bear bright swords, stand before his foes and say 'pay tribute to him who has undertaken protection', while the drum of victory, taken from his enemies on the great mountain, roars like the monsoon cloud and those with bows in their right hands praise him, how could the banners of his enemies' tall chariots with horses swift as the rising wind ever show at his border, he of unfailing firmness whose good fame is fixed on the earth.","n":"The demand for tribute is put in the warriors' mouths as a protection offered (புரவு எதிர்ந்தோற்கு): tribute is the price of the king's guard. The enemy banner never even appears at the frontier, so the war is won before it is fought.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","thirai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 80","lb":"PT80","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"வஞ்சித்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சித் துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிற்றுயானை","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["விறல்முரசம்","முரசம்",3,"parai","scene",""],["கார்மழையின்","கார் மழை",4,"pira","simile","monsoon cloud"],["சாந்துபுலர்ந்த","சாந்து",5,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["தொடிசுடர்வரும்","தொடி",6,"pira","ornament","armlet"],["புடையல்அம்","புடையல்",7,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கால்கிளர்ந்","கால்",13,"pira","simile","wind"],["புரவிக்","புரவி",13,"maa","simile","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:patikam:8":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":77,"t":["பொய்இல் செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வுக்கு","வேளாவிக் கோமான் பதுமன் தேவிஈன்றமகன்","கொல்லிக் கூற்றத்து நீர்கூர் மீமிசைப்","பல்வேல் தானை அதிக மானோ(டு)","இருபெரு வேந்தரையும் உடன்நிலை வென்று","முரசும் குடையும் கலனும்கொண்(டு)","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் அடுகளம் வேட்டுத்","துகள்தீர் மகளிர் இரங்கத் துப்(பு)அறுத்துத்","தகடூர் எறிந்து நொச்சிதந்(து) எய்திய","அருந்திறல் ஒள்இசைப் பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறையை","மறுஇல் வாய்மொழி அரிசில்கிழார்","பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Perunjeral Irumporai, son of Kadungo of unfailing wealth by Padumandevi, daughter of Padumam, lord of the Velavi line, defeated together Adhiyaman of the many-speared army of the watery Kolli district and the two great kings, took their drums, umbrellas and jewels, performed the sacrifice of the battlefield, cut down the strength of the enemy so that their faultless women wept, razed Tagadur and won its defending fort, and that Arisil Kizhar of truthful speech sang this ten.","n":"The preface gives the one fact the songs only point to: that the razing of Tagadur, named in 78, was a war against Adhiyaman fought alongside two other kings. The weeping women of line 8 are the only sign of the defeated side in the whole decade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pugazh","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-12","lb":"PTpatikam:1-12","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசும்","முரசு",6,"parai","scene",""],["நொச்சிதந்(து)","நொச்சி",9,"maram","scene","nochchi"]],"en":[["king","கடுங்கோ",1],["king","பதுமன்",2],["hill","கொல்லிக்",3],["king","அதிக மானோ(டு)",4],["town","தகடூர்",9],["king","பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறையை",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:7"},"pt:81":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":78,"t":["உலகம் புரக்கும் உருகெழு சிறப்பின்","வண்ணக் கருவிய வளம்கெழு கமம்சூல்","அகல்இரு விசும்பின் அதிர்சினம் சிறந்து","கடும்சிலை கழறி விசும்(பு)அடையூ நிவந்து","காலை இசைக்கும் பொழுதொடு புலம்புகொளக்","களிறுபாய்ந்(து) இயலக் கடுமா தாங்க","ஒளிறுகொடி நுடங்கத் தேர்திரிந்து கொட்ப","அரசுபுறத்(து) இறுப்பினும் அதிர்விலர் திரிந்து","வாயில் கொள்ளா மைந்தினர் வயவர்","மாஇரும் கங்குலும் விழுத்தொடி சுடர்வரத்","தோள்பிணி மீகையர் புகல்சிறந்து நாளும்","முடிதல் வேட்கையர் நெடிய மொழியூஉக்","கெடாஅ நல்லிசைத் தம்குடி நிறுமார்","இடாஅ ஏணி வியல்அறைக் கொட்ப","நா(டு)அடிப் படுத்தலின் கொள்ளை மாற்றி","அழல்வினை அமைந்த நிழல்விடு கட்டி","கட்டளை வலிப்பநின் தானை உதவி","வேறுபுலத்(து) இறுத்த வெல்போர் அண்ணல்","முழவின் அமைந்த பெரும்பழம் இசைந்து","சா(று)அயர்ந் தன்ன கார்அணி யாணர்த்","தூம்(பு)அகம் பழுனிய தீம்பிழி மாந்திக்","காந்தள்அம் கண்ணிச் செழுங்குடிச் செல்வர்","கலிமகிழ் மேவலர் இரவலர்க்(கு) ஈயும்","சுரும்(பு)ஆர் சோலைப் பெரும்பெயல் கொல்லிப்","பெருவாய் மலரொடு பசும்பிடி மகிழ்ந்து","மின்உமிழ்ந் தன்ன சுடர்இழை ஆயத்துத்","தன்நிறங் கரந்த வண்டுபடு கதுப்பின்","ஒடுங்(கு)ஈர் ஓதி ஒள்நுதல் அணிகொளக்","கொடுங்குழைக் கமர்த்த நோக்கின் நயவரப்","பெருந்தகைக்(கு) அமர்ந்த மென்சொல் திருமுகத்து","மாண்இழை அரிவை காணிய ஒருநாள்","பூண்க மாளநின் புரவி நெடுந்தேர்","முனைகை விட்டு முன்னிலைச் செல்லாது","தூஎதிர்ந்து பெறாஅத் தாஇல் மள்ளரொடு","தொல்மருங்(கு) அறுத்தல் அஞ்சி அரண்கொண்டு","துஞ்சா வேந்தரும் துஞ்சுக","விருந்தும் ஆக நின்பெருந் தோட்கே."],"p":"","r":"When the rain cloud, fierce and heavy with water, thunders across the sky at morning and brings loneliness, the poet describes the king's camp in a foreign land, where elephants charge, horses are reined, chariots wheel, and warriors who keep their bracelets bright even in the dark night and make long vows to fix their clan's fame circle the wide rock floor, while in Kolli the rich hill folk with kaanthal garlands drink sweet toddy matured in bamboo and give to suppliants; then he asks the king to yoke his horses to his tall chariot for one day to see his jewelled wife with soft words and loving eyes, so that the enemy kings who keep to their forts sleepless, afraid their old line will be cut off, may sleep too, and her embrace be a feast for his great shoulders.","n":"The return home is asked for as a mercy to the enemy as well as a joy for the king: the last three lines make the besieged kings' sleep depend on the queen's embrace. The opening cloud is the rainy season of mullai, when the absent man is due home, so the bard uses the akam occasion to end a war poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasarai","pirivu","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 81","lb":"PT81","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"mixed","th":"mullai","cth":"","ctu":"முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["pira","thalaivan",null,"karpu","முல்லை"]],"pt":["முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",6,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கடுமா","மா",6,"maa","scene","horse"],["பெரும்பழம்","பழம்",19,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["தீம்பிழி","பிழி",21,"unavu","gift_food","strained toddy"],["காந்தள்அம்","காந்தள்",22,"maram","ornament",""],["சுரும்(பு)ஆர்","சுரும்பு",24,"pira","scene","bees"],["பசும்பிடி","பிடி",25,"maram","ornament","fragrant plant"],["புரவி","புரவி",32,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["hill","கொல்லிப்",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:82":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":79,"t":["பகைபெரு மையின் தெய்வம் செப்ப","ஆர்இறை அஞ்சா வெருவரு கட்டூர்ப்","பல்கொடி நுடங்கும் முன்பின் செறுநர்","செல்சமம் தொலைத்த வினைநவில் யானை","கடாஅம் வார்ந்து கடும்சினம் பொத்தி","வண்டுபடு சென்னிய பிடிபுணர்ந்(து) இயல","மறவர் மறல மாப்படை உறுப்பத்","தேர்கொடி நுடங்கத் தோல்புடை ஆர்ப்பக்","காடுகை காய்த்திய நீடுநாள் இருக்கை","இன்ன வைகல் பல்நாள் ஆகப்","பாடிக் காண்கு வந்திசின் பெரும","பாடுநர், கொளக்கொளக் குறையாச் செல்வத்துச் செற்றோர்","கொலக்கொலக் குறையாத் தானைச் சான்றோர்","வண்மையும் செம்மையும் சால்பும் மறனும்","புகன்றுபுகழ்ந்(து) அசையா நல்லிசை","நிலம்தரு திருவின் நெடியோய் நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he came to see and sing the king's long stay in the dreadful camp, which fears no hard toll, whose many flags wave, where the war elephant that destroyed the foes' battles walks with its bee-haunted mate, musth flowing, where warriors clash, cavalry strikes, chariot flags wave and shields resound, a camp that has scorched the forest, with many days such as this; and he praises the tall king who has wealth given by the land, which never lessens however much singers take, an army that never lessens however much foes kill, and generosity, rectitude, nobility and valour of an unshaken fame.","n":"The closing pair is built as one formula (கொளக்கொளக் குறையா / கொலக்கொலக் குறையா): the same endless supply serves the singers who take and the army the foes cut down, so giving and fighting are measured alike. The first line has the god speak because the enmity is great, a hint that the war began with an omen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasarai","padai","eegai","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 82","lb":"PT82","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பிடி","பிடி",6,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bees"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",7,"seythi","scene","warriors"],["மாப்படை","மா",7,"maa","scene","cavalry"],["தோல்","தோல்",8,"pira","scene","shield"],["பாடுநர்","பாடுநர்",12,"seythi","scene","singers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:83":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":80,"t":["கார்மழை முன்பின் கைபரிந்(து) எழுதரும்","வான்பறைக் குருகின் நெடுவரி பொற்பக்","கொல்களிறு மிடைந்த பஃறோல் தொழுதியொடு","நெடுந்தேர் நுடங்குகொடி அவிர்வரப் பொலிந்து","செலவுபெரி(து) இனிதுநின் காணு மோர்க்கே","இன்னா(தூ) அம்மஅது தானே பல்மா","நாடுகெட எருக்கி நன்கலம் தரூஉம்நின்","போர்அருங் கடும்சினம் எதிர்ந்து","மாறுகொள் வேந்தர் பாசறை யோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that his advance, with elephants massed among many shields and tall chariots whose waving flags shine, splendid as the long line of high-flying cranes that rises before the rain clouds, is very sweet to those who watch it, but bitter indeed to the kings in the rival camp who must face his fierce rage, which ruins lands of many horses and brings back fine jewels.","n":"One sight is given two audiences: the onlookers for whom the march is beautiful and the enemy camp for whom it is ruin, and the poem turns on the pair இனிது and இன்னாது.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","paasarai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 83","lb":"PT83","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"தும்பையரவம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தும்பை அரவம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",2,"pul","simile","crane"],["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பஃறோல்","தோல்",3,"pira","scene","shields"],["பல்மா","மா",6,"maa","scene","horses"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:84":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":81,"t":["எடுத்(து)ஏ(று) ஏய கடிப்புடை அதிரும்","போர்ப்(பு)உறு முரசம் கண்அதிர்ந் தாங்குக்","கார்மழை முழக்கினும் வெளில்பிணி நீவி","நுதல்அணந்(து) எழுதரும் தொழில்நவில் யானைப்","பார்வல் பாசறைத் தரூஉம் பல்வேல்","பூழியர் கோவே பொலம்தேர்ப் பொறைய","மன்பதை சவட்டும் கூற்ற முன்ப","கொடிநுடங்(கு) ஆர்எயில் எண்ணுவரம்(பு) அறியா","பல்மா பரந்தபுலம் ஒன்(று)என்(று) எண்ணாது","வலியை ஆதல்நற்(கு) அறிந்தனர் ஆயினும்","வார்முகில் முழக்கின் மழகளிறு மிகீஇத்தன்","கால்முளை மூங்கில் கவர்கிளை போல","உய்தல்யா வதுநின் உடற்றி யோரே","வணங்கல் அறியார் உடன்(ரு)எழுந்(து) உரைஇப்","போர்ப்(பு)உறு தண்ணுமை ஆர்ப்(பு)எழுந்து நுவல","நோய்த்தொழில் மலைந்த வேல்ஈண்(டு) அழுவத்து","முனைபுகல் புகல்வின் மாறா மைந்தரொ(டு)","உரும்எறி வரையின் களிறு நிலம் சேரக்","காஞ்சி சான்ற செருப்பல செய்துநின்","குவவுக்குரை இருக்கை இனிதுகண் டிகுமே","காலை மாரி பெய்துதொழில் ஆற்றி","விண்டு முன்னிய புயல்நெடும் காலைக்","கல்சேர்பு மாமழை தலைஇப்","பல்குரல் புள்ளின் ஒலிஎழுந் தாங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the lord of the Puzhiyar, Poraiyan of the golden chariot, mighty as Death who crushes mankind, whose trained war elephant, when the rain cloud roars like the struck war drum, slips its tether and rises with lifted brow; though his foes knew well that he was strong, they did not count his countless flag-topped forts and his land of horses as one, and like forked bamboo shoots trampled by a young elephant maddened by the thunder, those who provoked him could not escape: not knowing how to bow, they rose together with drums sounding, and in the spear-crowded field elephants fell like thunder-struck hills; having fought many battles worthy of kaanchi, he sits in a crowded camp the poet has gladly seen, loud as the many-voiced birds when the morning rain has done its work on the hills.","n":"The foes are not ignorant: line 10 says they knew his strength well, and line 14 that they did not know how to bow, so their ruin comes from pride rather than from a mistake. The poem opens and closes on rain, with the elephant roused by thunder and the camp loud as birds after rain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","kootru","paasarai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 84","lb":"PT84","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசு",2,"parai","simile","war drum"],["யானைப்","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பல்மா","மா",9,"maa","scene","horses"],["மழகளிறு","களிறு",11,"maa","simile","young elephant"],["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",12,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",15,"parai","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",18,"maa","simile","elephant"],["புள்ளின்","புள்",24,"pul","simile","birds"]],"en":[["king","பொறைய",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:85":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":82,"t":["நல்மரம் துவன்றிய நாடுபல தாணஇப்","பொன்அவிர் புனைசெயல் இலங்கும் பெரும்பூண்","ஒன்னாப் பூட்கைச் சென்னியர் பெருமான்","இட்ட வெள்வேல் முத்தைத் தம்மென","முன்திணை முதல்வர் போல நின்று","தீம்சுனை நிலைஇய திருமா மருங்கின்","கோடுபல விரிந்த நாடுகாண் நெடுவரைச்","சூடா நறவின் நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை","அர(சு)அவை பணிய அறம்புரிந்து வயங்கிய","மறம்புரி கொள்கை வயங்குசெந் நாவின்","உவலை கூராக் கவலைஇல் நெஞ்சின்","நனவில் பாடிய நல்லிசைக்","கபிலன் பெற்ற ஊரினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that what the king gives, he who trampled many lands thick with good trees and made the hostile lord of the Chenniyar, of gleaming gold ornament, yield white pearls, and who stands like the founders of his ancient line in the daily revel on the long land-viewing hill with its many peaks and sweet springs, is more than the villages won by Kapilan, the poet of good fame and shining red tongue, of valiant creed and a heart without care, who sang before a royal assembly that bowed to him.","n":"The measure of the king's giving is another poet's reward: Kapilar, who sang the king's grandfather in the seventh ten, is named so that this ten's gifts can be said to exceed his. சூடா நறவின் (line 8) and the half-line about the Chola's spear and pearls (line 4) are hard to construe; the subject of the final பலவே is left to be supplied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 85","lb":"PT85","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நல்மரம்","மரம்",1,"maram","scene","trees"],["முத்தைத்","முத்து",4,"pira","gift_food","pearls"],["தீம்சுனை","சுனை",6,"pira","scene","spring"],["நறவின்","நறவு",8,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["king","சென்னியர் பெருமான்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:86":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":83,"t":["உறல்உறு குருதிச் செருக்களம் புலவக்","கொன்(று)அமர்க் கடந்த வெம்திறல் தடக்கை","வென்வேல் பொறையன் என்றலின் வெருவர","வெப்(பு)உடை ஆடூஉச் செத்தனென் மன்யான்","நல்இசை நிலைஇய நனம்தலை உலகத்(து)","இல்லோர் புன்கண் தீர நல்கும்","நாடல் சான்ற நயன்உடை நெஞ்சின்","பாடுநர் புரவலன் ஆடுநடை அண்ணல்","கழைநிலை பெறாஅக் குட்டத் தாயினும்","புனல்பாய் மகளிர் ஆட ஒழிந்த","பொன்செய் பூங்குழை மீமிசைத் தோன்றும்","சாந்துவரு வானி நீரினும்","தீந்தண் சாயலன் மன்ற தானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that when he heard men call him Poraiyan of the victorious spear, whose fierce strong hand killed and won in war and made the blood-soaked field stink, he feared him as a man of heat; but this lord of swaying gait, patron of singers, with a kind heart worth seeking, who gives to end the misery of the poor in the wide world where good fame stays, is surely sweeter and cooler of gentleness than the water of the Vaani, which brings down sandal and in whose pools, too deep for a bamboo pole to touch bottom, the gold flower earrings lost by bathing women show on the surface.","n":"The poem is built as a correction: the report of slaughter (lines 1 to 3) made the poet afraid, and the meeting shows the opposite temper. The river image keeps both: the pools are too deep to fathom, yet the water is cool and clear enough to show the lost earrings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 86","lb":"PT86","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப் பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["கழைநிலை","கழை",9,"maram","scene","bamboo pole"],["புனல்பாய் மகளிர்","மகளிர்",10,"seythi","scene","bathing women"],["பூங்குழை","குழை",11,"pira","ornament","earring"],["சாந்துவரு","சாந்து",12,"maram","simile","sandalwood"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",3],["river","வானி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:87":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":84,"t":["சென்மோ பாடினி நன்கலம் பெறுகுவை","சந்தம் பூழிலொடு பொங்குநுரை சுமந்து","தெண்கடல் முன்னிய வெண்தலைச் செம்புனல்","ஒய்யும் நீர்வழிக் கரும்பினும்","பல்வேல் பொறையன் வல்லனால் அளியே"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a singing woman to go, for she will receive fine jewels: Poraiyan of many spears is more given to kindness than the sugarcane carried along the water's course of the red flood, white-crested with swelling foam, that bears sandal and akil down to the clear sea.","n":"The whole guide poem is five lines: the road, the patron and the gifts of the longer guide poems are compressed into one image of a river carrying fragrant wood to the sea, which already pictures wealth flowing outward. Whether the cane is sweet or yielding (bending with the flow) is not settled by the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","arul","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 87","lb":"PT87","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலி ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["பாடினி","பாடினி",1,"seythi","scene","singing woman"],["நன்கலம்","கலம்",1,"pira","gift_food","jewels"],["சந்தம்","சந்தம்",2,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["பூழிலொடு","பூழில்",2,"maram","scene","akil"],["கரும்பினும்","கரும்பு",4,"maram","simile","sugarcane"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:88":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":85,"t":["வையகம் மலர்ந்த தொழில்முறை ஒழியாது","கடவுள் பெயரிய கானமொடு கல்உயர்ந்து","தெண்கடல் வளைஇய மலர்தலை உலகத்துத்","தம்பெயர் போகிய ஒன்னார் தேயத்","துளங்(கு)இரும் குட்டம் தொலைய வேல்இட்(டு)","அணங்(கு)உடைக் கடம்பின் முழுமுதல் தடிந்து","பொருமுரண் எய்திய கழுவுள் புறம்பெற்று","நாம மன்னர் துணிய நூறிக்","கால்வல் புரவி அண்டர் ஓட்டிச்","சுடர்வீ வாகை நன்னன் தேய்த்துக்","குருதி விதிர்த்த குவவுச்சோற்றுக் குன்றோ(டு)","உருகெழு மரபின் அயிரை பரைஇ","வேந்தரும் வேளிரும் பின்வந்து பணியக்","கொற்றம் எய்திய பெரியோர் மருக","வியல்உளை அரிமான் மறம்கெழு குருசில்","விரவுப்பணை முழங்கு நிரைதோல் வரைப்பின்","உரவுக்களிற்று வெல்கொடி நுடங்கும் பாசறை","ஆர்எயில் அலைத்த கல்கால் கவணை","நார்அரி நறவின் கொங்கர் கோவே","உடலுநர்த் தபுத்த பொலம்தேர்க் குருசில்","வளைகடல் முழவின் தொண்டியோர் பொருந","நீநீடு வாழிய பெரும நின்வயின்","துவைத்த தும்பை நனவுற்று வினவும்","மாற்றரும் தெய்வத்துக் கூட்ட முன்னிய","புனல்மலி பேரியா(று) இழிதந் தாங்கு","வருநர் வரையாச் செழும்பல் தாரம்","கொளக்கொளக் குறையாது தலைத்தலைச் சிறப்ப","ஓவத் தன்ன உருகெழு நெடுநகர்ப்","பாவை அன்ன மகளிர் நாப்பண்","புகன்ற மாண்பொறிப் பொலிந்த சாந்தமொடு","தண்கமழ் கோதை சூடிப் பூண்சுமந்து","திருவில் குலைஇத் திருமணி புரையும்","உருகெழு கருவிய பெருமழை சேர்ந்து","வேங்கை விரிந்து விசும்(பு)உறு சேட்சிமை","அருவி அருவரை அன்ன மார்பின்","சேண்நாறு நல்இசைச் சேயிழை கணவ","மாகம் சுடர மாவிசும்(பு) உகக்கும்","ஞாயிறு போல விளங்குதி பல்நாள்","ஈங்குக் காண்கு வந்தனென் யானே","உறுகால் எடுத்த ஓங்குவரல் புணரி","நுண்மணல் அடைகரை உடைதரும்","தண்கடல் படப்பை நாடுகிழ வோயே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the descendant of the great ones who, never abandoning the order that made the world flourish, hurled a spear so that the dark sea of a hostile country was destroyed, cut down the whole trunk of the spirit-guarded kadamba, saw the back of Kazhuvul, crushed famed kings, drove off the Andar of swift horses, wore down Nannan of the bright-flowered vaakai and worshipped Ayirai with heaps of blood-sprinkled rice, so that kings and Velir followed and bowed; he calls him lion-maned chief, king of the Kongar, lord of the people of Tondi whose drum is the curving sea, whose camp has banners on strong elephants and slings that batter forts, whose gifts flow to all comers without restraint like a great river in flood and never lessen however taken, who in a mansion like a painting among women like dolls wears sandal and garlands on a chest like a hill with waterfalls where vengai blooms and clouds gather, husband of a jewelled lady of far-spread fame; may he live long and shine like the sun, lord of the cool sea-shore land where wind-raised waves break on fine sand, for the poet has come to see him.","n":"The deeds in lines 4 to 13 are the ancestors' (பெரியோர் மருக, line 14), not the present king's: the sea-spear and the kadamba of the second ten and Nannan's vaakai of the fourth are gathered into one inherited record. Lines 23 and 24 (துவைத்த தும்பை நனவுற்று வினவும்) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pugazh","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 88","lb":"PT88","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"செந்துறைப்பாடாண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செந்துறைப் பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",6,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["புரவி","புரவி",9,"maa","scene","horse"],["வாகை","வாகை",10,"maram","scene",""],["குவவுச்சோற்றுக்","சோறு",11,"unavu","gift_food","rice offering"],["அரிமான்","அரிமான்",15,"maa","scene","lion"],["பணை","பணை",16,"parai","scene","drum"],["களிற்று","களிறு",17,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நறவின்","நறவு",19,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["முழவின்","முழவு",21,"parai","scene","drum"],["பேரியா(று)","யாறு",25,"pira","simile","river"],["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",29,"seythi","scene","women"],["சாந்தமொடு","சாந்தம்",30,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கோதை","கோதை",31,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",34,"maram","simile",""],["புணரி","புணரி",40,"pira","scene","waves"]],"en":[["king","கழுவுள்",7],["king","நன்னன்",10],["hill","அயிரை",12],["town","தொண்டியோர்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:89":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":86,"t":["வானம் பொழுதொடு சுரப்பக் கானம்","தோ(டு)உறு மடமான் ஏறுபுணர்ந்(து) இயலப்","புள்ளும் மிஞிறும் மாச்சினை ஆர்ப்பப்","பழனும் கிழங்கும் மிசைஅற(வு) அறியாது","பல்ஆன் நல்நிரை புல்அருந்(து) உகளப்","பயம்கடை அறியா வளம்கெழு சிறப்பின்","பெரும்பல் யாணர்க் கூலம் கெழும","நன்பல் ஊழி நடுவுநின்(று) ஒழுகப்","பல்வேல் இரும்பொறை நின்கோல் செம்மையின்","நாளின் நாளின் நாடுதொழு(து) ஏத்த","உயர்நிலை உலகத் (து) உயர்ந்தோர் பரவ","அரசியல் பிழையாது செருமேம் தோன்றி","நோய்இலை ஆகியர் நீயே நின்மாட்(டு)","அடங்கிய நெஞ்சம் புகர்படு(பு) அறியாது","கனவினும் பிரியா உறையுளொடு தண்எனத்","தகரம் நீவிய துவராக் கூந்தல்","வதுவை மகளிர் நோக்கினர் பெயர்ந்து","வாழ்நாள் அறியும் வயங்குசுடர் நோக்கத்து","மீனொடு புரையும் கற்பின்","வாள்நுதல் அரிவையொடு காண்வரப் பொலிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that because Irumporai's sceptre is straight, the sky pours in its season, in the forest the young doe of the herd walks with its stag, birds and bees hum on the dark boughs, fruit and tubers never fail, fine herds of cattle graze and frisk, grain abounds with a yield that knows no end and justice holds for many ages, so that the land worships him day by day and those of the high world praise him; never erring in kingship and great in war, may he be free of illness together with his bright-browed wife, whose heart, bound to him, knows no fault, who never parts from him even in dreams, whose hair is smoothed with tagaram, and whose chastity is like the star that brides look at in the wedding rite.","n":"The plenty of lines 1 to 8 is the effect of a cause stated only in line 9 (நின்கோல் செம்மையின்), so the land's order is read back from the king's justice. The doe walking with its stag (line 2) is the forest's picture of the union the poem then blesses in the queen; in a puram poem it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","mazhai","karpu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 89","lb":"PT89","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காவல்முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காவல் முல்லை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["மடமான்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","doe"],["ஏறுபுணர்ந்(து)","ஏறு",2,"maa","scene","stag"],["புள்ளும்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","birds"],["மிஞிறும்","மிஞிறு",3,"pira","scene","bees"],["பழனும்","பழம்",4,"unavu","scene","fruit"],["கிழங்கும்","கிழங்கு",4,"unavu","scene","tubers"],["பல்ஆன்","ஆன்",5,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கூலம்","கூலம்",7,"unavu","scene","grain"],["தகரம்","தகரம்",16,"maram","ornament","fragrant paste"],["மீனொடு","மீன்",19,"pira","simile","star"]],"en":[["king","இரும்பொறை",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:90":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":87,"t":["மீன்வயின் நிற்ப வானம் வாய்ப்ப","அச்(சு)அற்(று) ஏமம் ஆகி இருள்தீர்ந்(து)","இன்பம் பெருகத் தோன்றித் தம்துணைத்","துறையின் எஞ்சாமை நிறையக் கற்றுக்","கழிந்தோர் உடற்றும் கடும்தூ அஞ்சா","ஒளிறுவாள் வயவேந்தர்","களிறொடு கலம்தந்து","தொன்றுமொழிந்து தொழில்கேட்ப","அகல்வையத்துப் பகல்ஆற்றி","மாயாப் பல்புகழ் வியல்விசும்(பு) ஊர்தர","வாள்வலி உறுத்துச் செம்மை பூஉண்(டு)","அறன்வாழ்த்த நற்(கு)ஆண்ட","விறல்மாந்தரன் விறல்மருக","ஈரம் உடைமையின் நீரோர் அனையை","அளப்பரு மையின் இருவிசும்(பு) அனையை","கொளக்குறை படாமையின் முந்நர்ண அனையை","பல்மீன் நாப்பண் திங்கள் போலப்","பூத்த சுற்றமொடு பொலிந்து தோன்றலை","உருகெழு மரபின் அயிரை பரவியும்","கடல்இகுப்ப வேல்இட்டும்","உடலுநர் மிடல்சாய்த்தும்","மலயவும் நிலத்தவும் அருப்பம் வெளவிப்","பெற்ற பெரும்பெயர் பலர்கை இரீஇய","கொற்றத் திருவின் உரவோர் உம்பல்","கட்டிப் புழுக்கின் கொங்கர் கோவே","மட்டப் புகாவின் குட்டுவர் ஏறே","எழாஅத் துணைத்தோள் பூழியர் மெய்ம்மறை","இரங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின் மரந்தையோர் பொருந","வெண்பூ வேளையொடு சுரைதலை மயக்கிய","விரவுமொழிக் கட்டூர் வயவர் வேந்தே","உரவுக்கடல் அன்ன தாங்(க)அரும் தானையொடு","மாண்வினைச் சாபம் மார்(பு)உற வாங்கி","ஞாண்பொர விளங்கிய வலிகெழு தடக்கை","வார்ந்துபுனைந் தன்ன வேந்துகுவவு மொய்ம்பின்","மீன்பூத் தன்ன விளங்குமணிப் பாண்டில்","ஆய்மயிர்க் கவரிப் பாய்மா மேல்கொண்டு","காழ்எஃகம் பிடித்(து)எறிந்து","விழுமத்தின் புகலும் பெயரா ஆண்மைக்","காஞ்சி சான்ற வயவர் பெரும","வீங்குபெரும் சிறப்பின் ஓங்குபுக ழோயே","கழனி உழவர் தண்ணுமை இசைப்பின்","பழன மஞ்ஞை மழைசெத்(து) ஆலும்","தண்புனல் ஆடுநர் ஆர்ப்பொடு மயங்கி","வெம்போர் மள்ளர் தெள்கிணை கறங்கக்","கூழ்உடை நல்இல் ஏறுமாறு சிலைப்பச்","செழும்பல இருந்த கொழும்பல் தண்பணைக்","காவிரிப் படப்பை நல்நா(டு) அன்ன","வளம்கெழு குடைச்சூல் அடங்கிய கொள்கை","ஆறிய கற்பின் தேறிய நல்லிசை","வண்(டு)ஆர் கூந்தல் ஒண்தொடி கணவ","நின்நாள், திங்கள் அனைய ஆக திங்கள்","யாண்(டு)ஓர் அனைய வாக யாண்டே","ஊழி அனைய ஆக ஊழி","வெள்ள வரம்பின ஆ(க)என உள்ளிக்","காண்கு வந்திசின் யானே செருமிக்(கு)","உரும்என முழங்கும் முரசில்","பெருநல் யானை இறைகிழ வோயே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the descendant of victorious Mantharan, who ruled so well that dharma praised him, while the stars kept their places, the sky gave rain, fear and darkness left, joy grew and kings with gleaming swords brought him elephants and jewels and heard his commands; he tells the king that he is like water for his kindness, like the sky for being beyond measure, like the sea for not lessening however taken, that he shines among his flowering kin like the moon among many stars, and heir of mighty ones who worshipped Ayirai, made the sea retreat with a spear, broke the strength of foes and seized the forts of hill and plain; he names him king of the Kongar, bull of the Kuttuvar, shield of the Puzhiyar, lord of the people of Maranthai and king of the warriors of a camp of mixed tongues, who with an army like the sea draws his great bow to his chest, rides a plumed horse with gem trappings bright as blossoming stars and hurls the lance, chief of warriors of unretreating courage, husband of a bright-bangled lady of calm chastity as rich as the good Kaveri land where field peacocks dance to the ploughmen's drum taking it for rain; and he says he came to see him wishing that his days be as months, months as years, years as ages and ages beyond count, lord of great elephants and of drums that roar like thunder.","n":"Each of the three likenesses in lines 14 to 16 names its own ground (ஈரம், அளப்பருமை, கொளக்குறைபடாமை), so the poem does the work of saying what water, sky and sea share with the king. The closing wish multiplies time step by step, days to months to years to ages to a countless number. The peacock that mistakes the field drum for thunder (line 42) is a picture of plenty, not a comparison with the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","kudi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu 90","lb":"PT90","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"காட்சிவாழ்த்து","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["காட்சி வாழ்த்து"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறொடு","களிறு",7,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["வேளையொடு","வேளை",29,"maram","scene",""],["சுரைதலை","சுரை",29,"maram","scene","gourd"],["கவரிப்","கவரி",36,"pira","ornament","yak-tail plume"],["பாய்மா","மா",36,"maa","scene","horse"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",41,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",41,"parai","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",42,"pul","scene","peacock"],["தெள்கிணை","கிணை",44,"parai","scene",""],["ஏறுமாறு","ஏறு",45,"maa","scene","bull"],["முரசில்","முரசு",56,"parai","simile","drum"],["யானை","யானை",57,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","மாந்தரன்",13],["hill","அயிரை",19],["town","மரந்தையோர்",28],["river","காவிரிப்",47]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:patikam:9":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":88,"t":["குட்டுவன் இரும்பொறைக்கு மையூர் கிழாஅன்","வேண்மாள் அந்துவஞ் செள்ளை ஈன்றமகன்","வெருவரு தானையொடு வெய்(து)உறச் செய்துசென்(று)","இருபெரு வேந்தரும் விச்சியும் வீழ","அருமிளைக் கல்லகத்(து) ஐந்(து)எயில் எறிந்து","பொத்தி ஆண்ட பெரும்சோ ழனையும்","வித்தை ஆண்டஇளம் பழையன் மாறனையும்","வைத்த வஞ்சினம் வாய்ப்ப வென்று","வாஞ்சி மூதூர்த் தந்துபிறர்க்(கு) உதவி","மந்திர மரபின் தெய்வம் பேணி","மெய்யூர் அமைச்சியன் மையூர் கிழானைப்","புரைஅறு கேள்விப் புரோசு மயக்கி","அருந்திறல் மரபின் பெருஞ்சதுக்(கு) அமர்ந்த","வெந்திறல் பூதரைத் தந்(து)இவண் நிறீஇ","ஆய்ந்த மரபில் சாந்தி வேட்டு","மன்உயிர் காத்த மறுஇல் செங்கோல்","இன்இசை முரசின் இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறையைப்","பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார் பாடினார் பத்துப்பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"The decade's preface says that Ilanjeral Irumporai, son of Kuttuvan Irumporai by Velmal Anthuvan Chellai, daughter of the lord of Maiyur, made war hot with a dreadful army so that the two great kings and Vichchi fell, razed five forts in the stony land of hard thickets, defeated Perunchozhan who ruled Poththi and Ilampazhaiyan Maran who ruled Viththai as his vow had said, brought the spoils to the old town of Vanji and helped others with them, tended the god in the way of the mantras, set the truthful minister, the lord of Maiyur, beside the purohita of faultless learning, brought the fierce spirits of the great crossroads and installed them here, performed the rite of peace and guarded living beings with a flawless sceptre; Perunkunrur Kizhar sang this ten.","n":"Half the preface is ritual, not war: mantra worship, the purohita, the installed spirits and the shanti rite follow the victories, as if the preface-writer counted these among a Chera king's deeds. Lines 11 and 12 (மெய்யூர் அமைச்சியன் ... புரோசு மயக்கி) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","vazhipaadu","sengol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu patikam, lines 1-18","lb":"PTpatikam:1-18","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",10,"deyvam","scene",""],["பூதரைத்","பூதர்",14,"deyvam","scene","spirits"],["முரசின்","முரசு",17,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன் இரும்பொறைக்கு",1],["town","மையூர்",1],["king","விச்சியும்",4],["king","பெரும்சோ ழனையும்",6],["town","பொத்தி",6],["king","இளம் பழையன் மாறனையும்",7],["town","வித்தை",7],["town","வாஞ்சி",9],["king","இளஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறையைப்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:8"},"pt:fragment:1":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":89,"t":["இருங்கண் யானையொ(டு) அரும்கலம் துறுத்துப்","பணிந்துவழி மொழிதல் அல்லது பகைவர்","வணங்கார் ஆதல் யாவதோ மற்றே","உரும்உடன்று சிலைத்தலின் விசும்(பு)அதிர்ந் தாங்குக்","கண்அதிர்பு முழங்கும் கடும்குரல் முரசமொடு","கால்கிளர்ந் தன்ன ஊர்திக் கால்முளை","எரிநிகழ்ந் தன்ன நிறைஅரும் சீற்றத்து","நளிஇரும் பரப்பின் மாக்கடல் முன்னி","நீர்துனைந் தன்ன செலவின்","நிலந்திரைப் பன்ன தானையோய் நினக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The poet asks how enemies could fail to bow, except by heaping rare jewels together with dark-eyed elephants and speaking in submission, before a king whose army spreads like the earth in waves, whose fierce drum roars as the sky shakes when thunder rages, whose vehicles go like the rising wind, whose rage is a fire none can hold, and whose march runs like water racing to the great dark sea.","n":"The poem gives the enemy only one course: tribute and submissive speech are not one choice among others but the only way to stay unbroken, and the four similes that follow (thunder, wind, fire, water) are the proof. The closing image of the army as land rising in waves turns the solid earth itself into a flood.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai","panivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu fragment, lines 1-10","lb":"PTfragment:1-10","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானையொ(டு)","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["உரும்உடன்று","உரும்",4,"pira","simile","thunder"],["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",5,"parai","scene",""],["கால்கிளர்ந்","கால்",6,"pira","simile","wind"],["எரிநிகழ்ந்","எரி",7,"pira","simile","fire"],["மாக்கடல்","கடல்",8,"pira","simile","sea"],["நீர்துனைந்","நீர்",9,"pira","simile","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:9"},"pt:fragment:2":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":90,"t":["இலங்கு தொடி மருப்பிற் கடாஅம் வார்ந்து","நிலம்புடையூ எழுதரும் வலம்படு குஞ்சரம்","எரிஅவிழ்ந் தன்ன விரிஉளை சூட்டிக்","கால்கிளர்ந் தன்ன கடும்செல(வு) இவுளி","கோன்முனைக் கொடியினம் விரவா வல்லோ(டு)","ஊன்வினை கடுக்குந் தோன்றல பெரி(து)எழுந்(து)","அருவியின் ஒலிக்கும் வரிபுனை நெடுந்தேர்","கண்வேட் டனவே முரசம் கண்உற்றுக்","கதித்(து)எழு மாதிரம் கல்என ஒலிப்பக்","கறங்(கு)இசை வயிரொடு வலம்புரி ஆர்ப்ப","நெடுமதில் நிரைஞாயில்","கடிமிளைக் குண்டுகிடங்கின்","மீப்புடை ஆர்அரண் காப்(பு)உடைத் தேஎம்","நெஞ்சுபுகல் அழிந்து நிலைதளர்(பு) ஒரீஇ","ஒல்லா மன்னர் நடுங்க","நல்ல மன்றஇவண் வீங்கிய செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the king that his march is truly a fine thing here: tuskers with bangled tusks streaming must and digging the earth as they rise, horses crowned with plumes like opening fire and swift as the wind, tall chariots roaring like a waterfall, drums whose faces have received their offering sounding so that the quarters ring, with horn and right-whorled conch blaring, until the kings who would not yield, though their land is guarded by tall walls, rows of battlements, a thorn forest and a deep moat, lose heart, slip from their stand and tremble.","n":"The enemy's defence is listed in full (wall, battlements, guard forest, moat), and then it fails inside the defenders: the poem places the defeat in their hearts (நெஞ்சுபுகல் அழிந்து) before any assault is described. Line 6's கடுக்கும் is a comparison verb, but what it compares is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","aran","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu fragment, lines 1-16","lb":"PTfragment:1-16","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["எரிஅவிழ்ந்","எரி",3,"pira","simile","fire"],["கால்கிளர்ந்","கால்",4,"pira","simile","wind"],["இவுளி","இவுளி",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",7,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",8,"parai","scene",""],["வயிரொடு","வயிர்",10,"parai","scene","horn"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",10,"pira","scene","conch"],["கடிமிளைக்","மிளை",12,"maram","scene","guard forest"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:9"},"pt:fragment:3":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":91,"t":["பேணுதரு சிறப்பின் பெண்இயல்(பு) ஆயினும்","என்னொடு புரையுநள் அல்லள்","தன்னொடு புரையுநர்த் தான்அறி குநளே."],"p":"","r":"A woman says of another that, though she has a woman's nature of cherished distinction, she is no equal of the speaker, and she knows well enough who her own equals are.","n":"Three lines without a frame: who speaks and of whom is not given. The measure is rank, not beauty; the speaker grants the other woman her excellence and still denies her equality, and the last line says the other woman knows it herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu fragment, lines 1-3","lb":"PTfragment:1-3","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:9"},"pt:fragment:4":{"book":"patitruppattu","num":92,"t":["வந்தனென் பெரும கண்டனென் செலற்கே","களிறு கலிமான் தேரொடு சுரந்து","நன்கலன் ஈயும் நகைசால் இருக்கை","மாரி என்னாய் பனிஎன மடியாய்","பகைவெம் மையின் அசையா ஊக்கலை","வேறுபுலத்(து) இறுத்த விறல்வெந் தானையொடு","மாறா மைந்தர் மாறு நிலைதேய","மைந்துமலி ஊக்கத்த கந்துகால் கீழ்ந்து","கடாஅ யானை முழங்கும்","இடாஅ ஏணிநின் பாசறை யானே"],"p":"","r":"The bard tells the lord that he has come and has seen him in order to go away rewarded: the king sits in a joyful court giving tuskers, spirited horses, chariots and fine jewels in plenty, does not stop for the rains nor slacken for the cold, keeps an eagerness that the heat of enmity never lets rest, and with his fierce victorious army camped in a foreign land wears down the stand of unyielding warriors, in his boundless camp where must elephants full of strength tear up their posts and roar.","n":"The gift is given in the war camp, not the capital: the court of laughter (நகைசால் இருக்கை) and the camp of roaring elephants are the same place. The rains and the cold, the seasons that normally halt a campaign, are named only to be ignored.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","ookkam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Patitruppattu fragment, lines 1-10","lb":"PTfragment:1-10","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["கலிமான்","மான்",2,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["மாரி","மாரி",4,"pira","scene","rain"],["பனிஎன","பனி",4,"pira","scene","cold"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pt:9"},"pn:1":{"book":"purananuru","num":1,"t":["கண்ணி கார்நறுங் கொன்றை; காமர்","வண்ண மார்பின் தாருங் கொன்றை;","ஊர்தி வால்வெள் ளேறே; சிறந்த","சீர்கெழு கொடியும் அவ்வேறு என்ப;","கறைமிடறு அணியலும் அணிந்தன்று; அக்கறை","மறைநவில் அந்தணர் நுவலவும் படுமே;","பெண்ணுரு ஒரு திறன் ஆகின்று; அவ்வுருத்","தன்னுள் அடக்கிக் கரக்கினும் கரக்கும்;","பிறை நுதல் வண்ணம் ஆகின்று; அப்பிறை","பதினெண் கணனும் ஏத்தவும் படுமே;","எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் ஏமம் ஆகிய,","நீரறவு அறியாக் கரகத்துத்,","தாழ்சடைப் பொலிந்த அருந்தவத் தோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"An invocation that names the god only by his marks: a wreath and garland of konrai, the white bull as mount and banner, the dark stain on the throat that Vedic brahmins speak of, a woman's form that is one part of him and that he may also hide within himself, the crescent on his brow praised by the eighteen hosts, and the ever-full water pot and hanging matted locks of the great ascetic who is a refuge to all living beings.","n":"Each mark is stated and then given a second clause that raises it (the stain is also spoken of by the brahmins; the crescent is also praised by the hosts), so the poem is built in pairs. The woman-form clause goes further than the usual half-woman image: the god can also conceal her within himself (கரக்கினும் கரக்கும்). The name Siva is never used.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 1","lb":"PN1","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"இறைவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["வெள் ளேறே","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:2":{"book":"purananuru","num":2,"t":["மண் திணிந்த நிலனும்,","நிலம் ஏந்திய விசும்பும்,","விசும்பு தைவரு வளியும்","வளித் தலைஇய தீயும்,","தீ முரணிய நீரும், என்றாங்கு","ஐம்பெரும் பூதத்து இயற்கை போலப்","போற்றார்ப் பொறுத்தலும், சூழ்ச்சியது அகலமும்","வலியும், தெறலும், அணியும், உடையோய்!","நின்கடற் பிறந்த ஞாயிறு பெயர்த்தும் நின்","வெண்தலைப் புணரிக் குடகடல் குளிக்கும்","யாணர் வைப்பின், நன்னாட்டுப் பொருந!","வான வரம்பனை! நீயோ, பெரும!","அலங்குளைப் புரவி ஐவரோடு சினைஇ,","நிலந்தலைக் கொண்ட பொலம்பூந் தும்பை","ஈரைம் பதின்மரும் பொருது, களத்து ஒழியப்","பெருஞ்சோற்று மிகுபதம் வரையாது கொடுத்தோய்!","பாஅல் புளிப்பினும், பகல் இருளினும்,","நாஅல் வேத நெறி திரியினும்","திரியாச் சுற்றமொடு முழுதுசேண் விளங்கி,","நடுக்கின்றி நிலியரோ அத்தை; அடுக்கத்துச்,","சிறுதலை நவ்விப் பெருங்கண் மாப்பிணை,","அந்தி அந்தணர் அருங்கடன் இறுக்கும்","முத்தீ விளக்கிற், றுஞ்சும்","பொற்கோட்டு இமயமும், பொதியமும், போன்றே!"],"p":"","r":"Murinjiyur Mudinagarayar praises the Chera Uthiyan Cheralathan, whose forbearance, breadth of counsel, strength, fierceness and grace match the nature of the five elements, in whose sea the sun rises and in whose western sea it sets, who gave food without limit while the hundred fought the five until they fell; he wishes him to stand unshaken with his unwavering kin, even if milk turns sour, day turns dark and the Vedas' way changes, like the Himalaya and Pothiyam where deer sleep by the light of the brahmins' evening fires.","n":"Lines 13 to 16 tie the patron's name (பெருஞ்சோறு, great food) to the war of the five and the hundred, the earliest Tamil reference to that war; the poem says only that he gave food without limit until the hundred fell, not to whom. The blessing works by impossibilities (milk souring, day darkening, the Veda's path changing), so the king's steadiness is set beyond the order of nature itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai","porai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 2","lb":"PN2","poet":"முரஞ்சியூர் முடிநாகராயர்","pat":"சேரமான் பெருஞ்சோற்று உதியன் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ; வாழ்த்தியலும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ","வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["தும்பை","தும்பை",14,"maram","ornament",""],["நவ்வி","நவ்வி",21,"maa","simile","fawn"],["மாப்பிணை","மான் பிணை",21,"maa","simile","doe"]],"en":[["king","வான வரம்பனை",12],["hill","இமயமும்",24],["hill","பொதியமும்",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:3":{"book":"purananuru","num":3,"t":["உவவுமதி உருவின் ஓங்கல் வெண்குடை","நிலவுக்கடல் வரைப்பின் மண்ணகம் நிழற்ற,","ஏம முரசம் இழுமென முழங்க,","நேமி யுய்த்த நேஎ நெஞ்சின்,","தவிரா ஈகைக், கவுரியர் மருக!","செயிர்தீர் கற்பின் சேயிழை கணவ!","பொன் னோடைப் புகர் அணிநுதல்","துன்னருந் திறல் கமழ்கடா அத்து","எயிரு படையாக, எயிற்கதவு இடாஅக்","கயிறுபிணிக் கொண்ட கவிழ்மணி மருங்கில்.","பெருங்கை யானை இரும்பிடர்த் தலையிருந்து","மருந்தில் கூற்றத்து அருந்தொழில் சாயாக்","கருங்கை ஒள்வாள் பெரும்பெயர் வழுதி!","நிலம் பெயரினும், நின்சொற் பெயரல்;","பொலங் கழற்காற்,புலர் சாந்தின்","விலங் ககன்ற வியன் மார்ப!","ஊர் இல்ல, உயவு அரிய,","நீர் இல்ல, நீள் இடைய,","பார்வல் இருக்கைக், கவிகண் நோக்கிற்,","செந்தொடை பிழையா வன்கண் ஆடவர்","அம்புவிட, வீழ்ந்தோர் வம்பப் பதுக்கைத்","திருந்துசிறை வளைவாய்ப் பருந்திருந்து உயவும்","உன்ன மரத்த துன்னருங் கவலை,","நின்நசை வேட்கையின் இரவலர் வருவர்! அது","முன்னம் முகத்தின் உணர்ந்து, அவர்","இன்மை தீர்த்தல் வன்மை யானே."],"p":"","r":"Irumpidarthalaiyar praises the Pandya Vazhuthi of the black hand and bright sword, scion of the Kauriyar, whose white umbrella like the full moon shades the sea-girt earth and whose word does not move even if the earth moves, and counsels him that suppliants come longing for him across waterless, townless roads where bowmen in ambush shoot travellers and kites sit grieving on their fresh stone-heaps, so that his true strength is to read their need from their faces and end their poverty.","n":"The counsel is compressed into the last two and a half lines, and its weight falls on line 25: the giver should know the need from the face before it is spoken. The road passage (lines 17 to 23) does work for the counsel: it is the price the suppliants have already paid to come. The kites on the fresh cairns are a scene, not an image of the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","vaaimai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 3","lb":"PN3","poet":"இரும்பிடர்த் தலையார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் கருங்கை ஒள்வாள் பெரும்பெயர் வழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ; வாழ்த்தியலும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ","வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசு",3,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",20,"seythi","scene","waylaying bowmen"],["பருந்திருந்து","பருந்து",22,"pul","scene",""],["உன்ன","உன்னம்",23,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கவுரியர்",5],["king","வழுதி",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:4":{"book":"purananuru","num":4,"t":["வாள்,வலந்தர, மறுப் பட்டன","செவ் வானத்து வனப்புப் போன்றன!","தாள், களங்கொளக், கழல் பறைந்தன","கொல் ஏற்றின் மருப்புப் போன்றன;","தோல்; துவைத்து அம்பின் துனைதோன்றுவ,","நிலைக்கு ஒராஅ இலக்கம் போன்றன;","மாவே, எறிபதத்தான் இடங் காட்டக்,","கறுழ் பொருத செவ் வாயான்,","எருத்து வவ்விய புலி போன்றன;","களிறே, கதவு எறியாச், சிவந்து, உராஅய்,","நுதி மழுங்கிய வெண் கோட்டான்,","உயிர் உண்ணும் கூற்றுப் போன்றன;","நீயே, அலங்கு உளைப் பரீஇ இவுளிப்","பொலந் தேர்மிசைப் பொலிவு தோன்றி,","மாக் கடல் நிவந் தெழுதரும்","செஞ் ஞாயிற்றுக் கவினை மாதோ!","அனையை ஆகன் மாறே,","தாயில் தூவாக் குழவி போல,","ஓவாது கூஉம், நின் உடற்றியோர் நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Paranar describes the Chola's war gear after battle, each in a simile (swords red like the evening sky, feet like the horns of a killing bull, shields pierced like a fixed target, horses red-mouthed like a tiger that has seized a neck, elephants with blunted tusks like Death that eats lives), sets the king on his golden chariot like the red sun rising from the sea, and ends that the lands of those who angered him cry without ceasing like a child its mother has not fed.","n":"The poem is a chain of seven comparisons, five of them in the same frame (thing, its battle mark, போன்றன), so the king is approached through his gear before he is named as the sun. Only the last simile turns from the victor to the victim: the enemy country as an unfed infant, which is the lament that gives the thurai its name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 4","lb":"PN4","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"சோழன் உருவப் பஃறேர் இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"கொற்ற வள்ளை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கொற்ற வள்ளை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஏற்றின்","ஏறு",4,"maa","simile","bull"],["மாவே","மா",7,"maa","simile","horse"],["புலி","புலி",9,"maa","simile",""],["களிறே","களிறு",10,"maa","simile",""],["இவுளி","இவுளி",13,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:5":{"book":"purananuru","num":5,"t":["எருமை அன்ன கருங்கல் இடை தோறு,","ஆனிற் பரக்கும் யானைய, முன்பின்,","கானக நாடனை!நீயோ, பெரும!","நீயோர் ஆகலின், நின் ஒன்று மொழிவல்;","அருளும் அன்பும் நீக்கி நீங்கா","நிரயங் கொள்பவரொடு ஒன்றாது, காவல்,","குழவி கொள் பவரின், ஓம்புமதி!","அளிதோ தானே; அது பெறல்அருங் குரைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Nariveruuthalaiyar tells the Chera, lord of a forest land where elephants spread like cattle among black rocks like buffaloes, that since he is such a man he will say one thing: do not side with those who cast off grace and love and take a hell that never leaves; guard the realm as those who rear a child, for that guarding is a thing of grace and hard to come by.","n":"The opening picture is double: rocks like buffaloes and elephants like cattle make the wild forest a pasture, which prepares the counsel that the king should be a herdsman and nurse. The comparisons in lines 2 and 7 use the comparative -இன் (ஆனிற், பவரின்) rather than அன்ன or போல. What அது in line 8 refers to (the guarding, the realm, or the reward of guarding) is not fixed by the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu","naragam","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 5","lb":"PN5","poet":"நரிவெரூஉத் தலையார்","pat":"சேரமான் கருவூரேறிய ஒள்வாட் கோப்பெருஞ் சேரல்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ: பொருண் மொழிக் காஞ்சியும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ","பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","simile","buffalo"],["யானைய","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["ஆனிற்","ஆன்",2,"maa","simile","cattle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:6":{"book":"purananuru","num":6,"t":["வாடாஅது பனிபடு நெடுவரை வடக்கும்,","தெனாஅது உருகெழு குமரியின் தெற்கும்,","குணாஅது கரைபொரு தொடுகடல் குணக்கும்,","குடாஅது தொன்றுமுதிர் பொளவத்தின் குடக்கும்,","கீழது முப்புணர் அடுக்கிய முறைமுதற் கட்டின்","நீர்நிலை நிவப்பின் கீழும், மேலது","ஆனிலை உலகத் தானும், ஆனாது,","உருவும் புகழும் ஆகி, விரிசீர்த்","தெரிகோல் ஞமன்ன் போல, ஒரு திறம்","பற்றல் இலியரோ! நின் திறம் சிறக்க!","செய்வினைக்கு எதிர்ந்த தெவ்வர் தேஎத்துக்,","கடற்படை குளிப்ப மண்டி, அடர்ப் புகர்ச்","சிறுகண் யானை செவ்விதின் ஏவிப்,","பாசவற் படப்பை ஆர்எயில் பலதந்து,","அவ்வெயில் கொண்ட செய்வுறு நன்கலம்","பரிசின் மாக்கட்கு வரிசையின் நல்கிப்,","பணியியர் அத்தை நின் குடையே; முனிவர்","முக்கண் செல்வர் நகர்வலஞ் செயற்கே!","இறைஞ்சுக, பெரும! நின் சென்னி; சிறந்த","நான்மறை முனிவர் ஏந்துகை எதிரே!","வாடுக, இறைவ நின் கண்ணி! ஒன்னார்","நாடுசுடு கமழ்புகை எறித்த லானே!","செலிஇயர் அத்தை, நின் வெகுளி; வால்இழை","மங்கையர் துனித்த வாள்முகத்து எதிரே!","ஆங்க, வென்றி எல்லாம் வென்றுஅகத்துஅடக்கிய","தண்டா ஈகைத் தகைமாண் குடுமி!","தண்கதிர் மதியம் போலவும், தெறுசுடர்","ஒண்கதிர் ஞாயிறு போலவும்,","மன்னிய, பெரும! நீ நிலமிசை யானே!"],"p":"","r":"Karikizhar wishes the Pandya Mudukudumi, whose form and fame reach from beyond the Himalaya to below Kumari, from sea to sea and from the waters below to the world above, that he may lean to no side like the beam of a balance, that his umbrella bow only in circling the three-eyed god's shrine, his head only before the raised hands of the Vedic sages, his garland wilt only in the smoke of burned enemy lands and his anger yield only to his women's sulking faces, and that he abide on earth like the cool moon and the burning sun.","n":"Four optative clauses (பணியியர், இறைஞ்சுக, வாடுக, செலிஇயர்) map where the king should give way and where he should not; the paired moon and sun at the close repeat the split between mildness and heat. The giving of captured jewels to bards in due order (வரிசையின்) is placed inside the praise of conquest, so the plunder is legitimated by its distribution.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","panivu","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 6","lb":"PN6","poet":"காரிகிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் பல்யாகசாலை முதுகுடுமிப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ; வாழ்த்தியலும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ","வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",21,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:7":{"book":"purananuru","num":7,"t":["களிறு கடைஇய தாள்,","கழல் உரீஇய திருந்துஅடிக்,","கணை பொருது கவிவண் கையால்,","கண் ஒளிர்வரூஉம் கவின் சாபத்து","மா மறுத்த மலர் மார்பின்,","தோல் பெயரிய எறுழ் முன்பின்,","எல்லையும் இரவும் எண்ணாய், பகைவர்","ஊர்சுடு விளக்கத்து அழுவிளிக் கம்பலைக்","கொள்ளை மேவலை; ஆகலின், நல்ல","இல்ல ஆகுபவால் இயல்தேர் வளவ!","தண்புனல் பரந்த பூசல் மண் மறுத்து","மீனின் செறுக்கும் யாணர்ப்","பயன்திகழ் வைப்பின், பிறர் அகன்றலை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Karunkuzhal Athanar praises Karikalan, whose feet drive elephants, whose hand is bent from the bow and whose chest has turned back horses, for taking no account of day or night and delighting in plunder amid the wailing in the light of burning enemy towns, so that the good things are no more in others' wide, rich lands where cool floods spread and are held back by fish.","n":"The praise has a sting: நல்ல இல்ல ஆகுபவால் (the good things come to be gone) is said to the king, and the last three lines spend their richest description on the lands he ruins. The exact sense of lines 11 and 12 (a flood held back not by earth but by fish) is a hyperbole of plenty whose syntax is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kedu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 7","lb":"PN7","poet":"கருங்குழல் ஆதனார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"கொற்ற வள்ளை: மழபுல வஞ்சியும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கொற்ற வள்ளை","மழபுல வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["மா","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"],["மீனின்","மீன்",12,"pira","scene","fish"]],"en":[["king","வளவ",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:8":{"book":"purananuru","num":8,"t":["வையம் காவலர் வழிமொழிந்து ஒழுகப்,","போகம் வேண்டிப், பொதுச்சொல் பொறாஅது ,","இடம் சிறிது என்னும் ஊக்கம் துரப்ப,","ஒடுங்கா உள்ளத்து, ஓம்பா ஈகைக்,","கடந்து அடு தானைச் சேரலாதனை","யாங்கனம் ஒத்தியோ? வீங்குசெலல் மண்டிலம்!","பொழுதுஎன வரைதி; புறக்கொடுத்து இறத்தி;","மாறி வருதி; மலைமறைந்து ஒளித்தி;","அகல்இரு விசும்பி னானும்","பகல்விளங் குதியால் பல்கதிர் விரித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar asks the sun how it could equal Cheralathan, whom the world's kings obey, who will not bear the earth being called common to others and finds it too small, of unshrinking heart and unsparing gifts: the sun keeps set hours, turns its back and goes, comes back changed, hides behind the hills, and shines only by day.","n":"The comparison is denied: each of the sun's motions (keeping hours, turning away, changing, hiding, shining only by day) becomes a lack set against the king's constancy. ஊக்கம் is named in line 3 as the drive that finds the earth too small (இடம் சிறிது), a praise that also admits his ambition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","eegai","ookkam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 8","lb":"PN8","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"சேரமான் கடுங்கோ வாழியாதன் : சேரமான் செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வாழியாதன் என்பவனும் இவனே","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி; பூவை நிலையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி","பூவை நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","சேரலாதனை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:9":{"book":"purananuru","num":9,"t":["‘ஆவும், ஆனியற் பார்ப்பன மாக்களும்,","பெண்டிரும், பிணியுடை யீரும் பேணித்","தென்புலம் வாழ்நர்க்கு அருங்கடன் இறுக்கும்","பொன்போற் புதல்வர்ப் பெறாஅ தீரும்,","எம்அம்பு கடிவிடுதும், நுன்அரண் சேர்மின்’ என","அறத்துஆறு நுவலும் பூட்கை, மறத்தின்","கொல்களிற்று மீமிசைக் கொடிவிசும்பு நிழற்றும்","எங்கோ, வாழிய குடுமி! தங் கோச்","செந்நீர்ப் பசும்பொன் வயிரியர்க்கு ஈத்த,","முந்நீர் விழவின், நெடியோன்","நன்னீர்ப் பஃறுளி மணலினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Nettimaiyar blesses the Pandya Kudumi, who before battle proclaims the way of dharma, telling cows, brahmin-natured men, women, the sick and those without sons to perform the rites for the ancestors to go to shelter before he lets fly his arrows, and wishes him years more than the sands of the Pahruli river of the ancient Pandya Nediyon, who gave fresh gold to dancers at the sea festival.","n":"The opening quotation is the king's own war proclamation, and its list defines who is outside war: animals of the household, priests, women, the sick, and men whose line would end with them. The blessing reaches back to Nediyon and the Pahruli, linking this Pandya to a legendary ancestor's generosity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 9","lb":"PN9","poet":"நெட்டிமையார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் பல்யாகசாலை முதுகுடுமிப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["ஆவும்","ஆ",1,"maa","scene","cow"],["களிற்று","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["வயிரியர்க்கு","வயிரியர்",9,"seythi","scene","dancers"]],"en":[["king","குடுமி",8],["king","நெடியோன்",10],["river","பஃறுளி",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:10":{"book":"purananuru","num":10,"t":["வழிபடு வோரை வல்லறி தீயே!","பிறர்பழி கூறுவோர் மொழிதே றலையே;","நீமெய் கண்ட தீமை காணின்,","ஒப்ப நாடி அத்தக ஒறுத்தி;","வந்து, அடி பொருந்தி, முந்தை நிற்பின்,","தண்டமும் தணிதி, நீ பண்டையிற் பெரிதே;","அமிழ்துஅட்டு ஆனாக் கமழ்குய் அடிசில்","வருநர்க்கு வரையா வசையில் வாழ்க்கை","மகளிர் மலைத்தல் அல்லது, மள்ளர்","மலைத்தல் போகிய, சிலைத்தார் மார்ப!","செய்து இரங்காவினைச், சேண்விளங் கும்புகழ்,","நெய்தருங் கானல் நெடியோய்!","எய்த வந்தனம்யாம்; ஏத்துகம் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Oonpothi Pasunkudaiyar praises the Chola of Neythalankanal, who knows well those who serve him, does not believe those who slander others, punishes a proven wrong after weighing it fairly and lessens the penalty when the offender comes and stands at his feet, whose house feeds all comers, and whose chest only women now fight with since warriors have given up fighting him; the poet has come to him to praise him at length.","n":"The chest that only women 'fight' (மலைத்தல், also to wear or embrace) while warriors have stopped fighting it is a pun that sets love-quarrel against war. Mercy here follows submission, not rank: the softened penalty is for the one who comes and holds the king's feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","oruththal","therithal","kannottam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 10","lb":"PN10","poet":"ஊன் பொதி பசுங் குடையார்","pat":"சோழன் நெய்தலங் கானல் இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[["அடிசில்","அடிசில்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["town","நெய்தருங் கானல்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:11":{"book":"purananuru","num":11,"t":["அரி மயிர்த் திரள் முன்கை","வால் இழை, மட மங்கையர்","வரி மணற் புனை பாவைக்குக்","குலவுச் சினைப் பூக் கொய்து","தண் பொருநைப் புனல் பாயும்","விண் பொருபுகழ், விறல்வஞ்சிப்","பாடல் சான்ற விறல்வேந் தனும்மே","வெப் புடைய அரண் கடந்து,","துப்புறுவர் புறம்பெற் றிசினே:","புறம் பொற்ற வய வேந்தன்","மறம் பாடிய பாடினி யும்மே,","ஏர் உடைய விழுக் கழஞ்சின்,","சீர் உடைய இழை பெற்றிசினே!","இழை பெற்ற பாடி னிக்குக்","குரல் புணர்சீர்க் கொளைவல்பாண் மகனும்மே.","என ஆங்கு,","ஒள்அழல் புரிந்த தாமரை","வெள்ளி நாரால் பூப்பெற் றிசினே."],"p":"","r":"Peymagal Ilaveyiniyar tells how, by the cool Porunai where girls with fine jewels pick flowers for their sand dolls and plunge into the water, the Chera king worthy of the vanji song crossed a fierce fortress and saw his enemies' backs; the singer who sang that valour received fine gold jewels, and her partner the bard, skilled in tune, received a gold lotus strung on silver thread.","n":"The poem never asks; it lists who received what (the king, the enemy's backs; the singer, jewels; the bard, the lotus), and the request is left for the next link, the poet herself, which is how பரிசில் கடாநிலை works here. விறல்வஞ்சிப் பாடல் is the vanji song of victory, not the Chera town Vanji.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 11","lb":"PN11","poet":"பேய்மகள் இளவெயினியார்","pat":"சேரமான் பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பூக்","பூ",4,"maram","scene",""],["பாடினி","பாடினி",11,"seythi","scene",""],["பாண்","பாண்",15,"seythi","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",17,"maram","gift_food","gold lotus"]],"en":[["river","பொருநை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:12":{"book":"purananuru","num":12,"t":["பாணர் தாமரை மலையவும், புலவர்","பூநுதல் யானையோடு புனைதேர் பண்ணவும்,","அறனோ மற்றஇது விறல்மாண் குடுமி!","இன்னா ஆகப் பிறர் மண் கொண்டு,","இனிய செய்தி நின் ஆர்வலர் முகத்தே?"],"p":"","r":"Nettimaiyar asks the Pandya Kudumi whether it is dharma that bards wear gold lotuses and poets are given chariots with elephants, when he takes others' land so that it is painful to them and does what is sweet before those who love him.","n":"The five lines can be read as playful praise (so generous it is almost wrong) or as a real question about gifts paid for by conquest; the poem gives no answer, and the pairing இன்னா ... இனிய holds the two sides of the same act together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 12","lb":"PN12","poet":"நெட்டிமையார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் பல்யாகசாலை முதுகுடுமிப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","gift_food","gold lotus"],["யானையோடு","யானை",2,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","குடுமி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:13":{"book":"purananuru","num":13,"t":["‘இவன் யார்?’ என்குவை ஆயின், இவனே,","புலிநிறக் கவசம் பூம்பொறி சிதைய,","எய்கணை கிழித்த பகட்டுஎழில் மார்பின்,","மறலி அன்ன களிற்றுமிசை யோனே;","களிறே, முந்நீர் வழங்கு நாவாய் போலவும்,","பன்மீன் நாப்பண் திங்கள் போலவும்,","சுறவு இனத்து அன்ன வாளோர் மொய்ப்ப,","மரீஇயோர் அறியாது, மைந்துபட் டன்றே;","நோயிலன் ஆகிப் பெயர்கதில் அம்ம!","பழன மஞ்ஞை உகுத்த பீலி","கழனி உழவர் சூட்டொடு தொகுக்கும்,","கொழுமீன், விளைந்த கள்ளின்,","விழுநீர் வேலி நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"Mudamosiyar answers someone who asks who the man is: the one on the Death-like elephant, his armour torn by arrows, while the elephant, like a ship on the sea and the moon among many stars, surrounded by swordsmen like a shoal of sharks, has gone wild beyond its keepers' control; may he return unharmed, the lord of a land of water-fenced fields where ploughmen gather dropped peacock feathers with their sheaves and fish and toddy abound.","n":"The poem turns from danger to plenty without transition: the last four lines describe the king's home country so richly that the wish for his return becomes a picture of what he must return to. Who asks 'who is he?' and where the elephant has run are not stated in the poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 13","lb":"PN13","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"சோழன் முடித்தலைக் கோப்பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["களிற்றுமிசை","களிறு",4,"maa","simile",""],["சுறவு","சுறவு",7,"pira","simile","shark"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",10,"pul","scene",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["சூட்டொடு","சூடு",11,"maram","scene","sheaves"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",12,"pira","scene","fish"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",12,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:14":{"book":"purananuru","num":14,"t":["கடுங்கண்ண கொல் களிற்றால்","காப் புடைய எழு முருக்கிப்,","பொன் இயல் புனை தோட்டியான்","முன்பு துரந்து, சமந் தாங்கவும்;","பார்உடைத்த குண்டு அகழி","நீர் அழுவம் நிவப்புக் குறித்து,","நிமிர் பரிய மா தாங்கவும்;","ஆவம் சேர்ந்த புறத்தை தேர்மிசைச்","சாப நோன்ஞாண் வடுக்கொள வழங்கவும்;","பரிசிலர்க்கு அருங்கலம் நல்கவும்; குரிசில்!","வலிய ஆகும் நின் தாள்தோய் தடக்கை,","புலவு நாற்றத்த பைந்தடி","பூ நாற்றத்த புகை கொளீஇ, ஊன்துவை","கறிசோறு உண்டு வருந்துதொழில் அல்லது","பிறிதுதொழில் அறியா ஆகலின், நன்றும்","மெல்லிய பெரும! தாமே, நல்லவர்க்கு","ஆரணங்கு ஆகிய மார்பின், பொருநர்க்கு","இருநிலத்து அன்ன நோன்மை","செருமிகு சேஎய் ! நின் பாடுநர் கையே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar tells the Chera that his knee-long hands are hard, since they drive the elephant to break the crossbars, rein the horses at the deep moat, are scarred by the bowstring and give rare jewels to suppliants, while the hands of the poets who sing him are very soft, since they know no work but eating meat smoked with fragrant smoke, mashed meat and rice with curry.","n":"The contrast is playful self-mockery: the king's hands are praised through the softness of the poets' own, which is the proof of his feeding. The hard hands do four labours, and the last of them is giving, so generosity is counted among the toils of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 14","lb":"PN14","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"சேரமான் செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வாழியாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["களிற்றால்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["மா","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["பைந்தடி","தடி",12,"unavu","gift_food","fresh meat"],["ஊன்துவை","ஊன் துவை",13,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கறிசோறு","கறி சோறு",14,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:15":{"book":"purananuru","num":15,"t":["கடுந்தேர் குழித்த ஞெள்ளல் ஆங்கண்,","வெள்வாய்க் கழுதைப் புல்லினப் பூட்டிப்,","பாழ்செய் தனை, அவர் நனந்தலை நல்லெயில்;","புள்ளினம் இமிழும் புகழ்சால் விளைவயல்,","வெள்ளுளைக் கலிமான் கவிகுளம்பு உகளத்","தேர்வழங் கினைநின் தெவ்வர் தேஎத்துத்;","துளங்கு இயலாற், பணை எருத்தின்,","பா வடியாற்,செறல் நோக்கின்,","ஒளிறு மருப்பின் களிறு அவர","காப் புடைய கயம் படியினை;","அன்ன சீற்றத்து அனையை; ஆகலின்,","விளங்கு பொன் எறிந்த நலங்கிளர் பலகையொடு","நிழல்படு நெடுவேல் ஏந்தி ஒன்னார்","ஒண்படைக் கடுந்தார் முன்புதலைக் கொண்மார்,","நசைதர வந்தோர் நசைபிறக்கு ஒழிய,","வசைபட வாழ்ந்தோர் பலர்கொல்? புரையில்","நற் பனுவல் நால் வேதத்து","அருஞ் சீர்த்திப் பெருங் கண்ணுறை","நெய்ம் மலி ஆவுதி பொங்கப், பன்மாண்","வீயாச் சிறப்பின் வேள்வி முற்றி,","யூபம் நட்ட வியன்களம் பலகொல்?","யாபல கொல்லோ? பெரும! வார் உற்று","விசிபிணிக் கொண்ட மண்கனை முழவின்","பாடினி பாடும் வஞ்சிக்கு","நாடல் சான்ற மைந்தினோய்! நினக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Nettimaiyar asks the Pandya Kudumi which are more: the enemies who came wanting to take the head of his vanguard and went away living in shame, or the wide fields where he planted sacrificial posts after completing Vedic sacrifices with ghee offerings, after he had ploughed their walled towns with white-mouthed donkeys, driven his chariots over their bird-loud fields and bathed his elephants in their guarded tanks.","n":"The closing question (யாபல கொல்லோ) leaves war and sacrifice uncounted and equal, so the Vedic rite and the razing of cities are praised in one breath. Ploughing a conquered town with donkeys is a rite of ruin, the opposite of the ploughing that founds a field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vedham","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 15","lb":"PN15","poet":"நெட்டிமையார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் பல்யாகசாலை முதுகுடுமிப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கழுதைப்","கழுதை",2,"maa","scene","donkey"],["புள்ளினம்","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["கலிமான்","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",23,"parai","scene",""],["பாடினி","பாடினி",24,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:16":{"book":"purananuru","num":16,"t":["வினை மாட்சிய விரை புரவியொடு,","மழை யுருவின தோல் பரப்பி,","முனை முருங்கத் தலைச்சென்று, அவர்","விளை வயல் கவர்பு ஊட்டி","மனை மரம் விறகு ஆகக்","கடி துறைநீர்க் களிறு படீஇ","எல்லுப்பட இட்ட சுடுதீ விளக்கம்","செல்சுடர் ஞாயிற்றுச் செக்கரின் தோன்றப்,","புலம்கெட இறுக்கும் வரம்பில் தானைத்,","துணை வேண்டாச் செரு வென்றிப்,","புலவு வாள் புலர் சாந்தின்","முருகன் சீற்றத்து, உருகெழு குருசில்!","மயங்கு வள்ளை, மலர் ஆம்பல்,","பனிப் பகன்றைக், சுனிப் பாகல்,","கரும்பு அல்லது காடு அறியாப்","பெருந் தண்பணை பாழ் ஆக,","ஏமநன் னாடு ஒள்எரி ஊட்டினை,","நாம நல்லமர் செய்ய,","ஒராங்கு மலைந்தன, பெரும! நின் களிறே."],"p":"","r":"Pandarangannanar tells the Chola Perunarkilli that his army, with swift horses and shields spread like clouds, broke the enemy front, fed on their ripe fields, burned their house-beams as firewood, bathed elephants at their guarded ghats and lit fires glowing like the setting sun, so that the great cool fields that knew no wood but sugarcane, with vallai, ambal, pakanrai and pagal, became waste, and his elephants fought as one.","n":"The fields are described at their richest (a land whose only 'forest' is sugarcane) in the same breath as they are made waste, so the beauty of the enemy's land is the measure of the king's might. The water plants are listed as what was there before the burning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","kedu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 16","lb":"PN16","poet":"பாண்டரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"சோழன் இராசசூயம் வேட்ட பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"மழபுல வஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மழபுல வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புரவியொடு","புரவி",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறு","களிறு",6,"maa","scene",""],["முருகன்","முருகன்",12,"deyvam","simile",""],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",13,"maram","absent",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",13,"maram","absent",""],["பகன்றைக்","பகன்றை",14,"maram","absent",""],["பாகல்","பாகல்",14,"maram","absent",""],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",15,"maram","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:17":{"book":"purananuru","num":17,"t":["தென் குமரி, வட பெருங்கல்,","குண குட கடலா வெல்லை,","குன்று, மலை, காடு, நாடு","ஒன்று பட்டு வழி மொழியக்,","கொடிது கடிந்து, கோல் திருத்திப்,","படுவது உண்டு, பகல் ஆற்றி,","இனிது உருண்ட சுடர் நேமி","முழுது ஆண்டோர் வழி காவல!","குலை இறைஞ்சிய கோள் தாழை","அகல் வயல், மலை வேலி,","நிலவு மணல் வியன் கானல்,","தெண் கழிமிசைச் சுடர்ப் பூவின்,","தண் தொண்டியோர் அடு பொருந!","மாப் பயம்பின் பொறை போற்றாது,","நீடு குழி அகப் பட்ட","பீடு உடைய எறுழ் முன்பின்","கோடு முற்றிய கொல் களிறு,","நிலை கலங்கக் குழி கொன்று,","கிளை புகலத் தலைக்கூடி யாங்கு","நீ பட்ட அரு முன்பின்,","பெருந் தளர்ச்சி, பலர் உவப்பப்,","பிறிது சென்று, மலர் தாயத்துப்","பலர் நாப்பண் மீக் கூறலின்,","‘உண் டாகிய உயர் மண்ணும்,","சென்று பட்ட விழுக் கலனும்,","பெறல் கூடும், இவன்நெஞ்சு உறப்பெறின்’எனவும்,","‘ஏந்து கொடி இறைப் புரிசை,","வீங்கு சிறை, வியல் அருப்பம்,","இழந்து வைகுதும்.இனிநாம்; இவன்","உடன்று நோக்கினன், பெரிது’ எனவும்,","வேற்று அரசு பணி தொடங்குநின்","ஆற்ற லொடு புகழ் ஏத்திக்,","காண்கு வந்திசின், பெரும! ஈண்டிய","மழையென மருளும் பல் தோல், மலையெனத்","தேன்இறை கொள்ளும் இரும்பல் யானை,","உடலுநர் உட்க வீங்கிக், கடலென","வான்நீர்க்கு ஊக்கும் தானை, ஆனாது","கடுஒடுங்கு எயிற்ற அரவுத்தலை பனிப்ப,","இடியென முழங்கு முரசின்,","வரையா ஈகைக் குடவர் கோவே!"],"p":"","r":"Kurunkozhiyur Kizhar praises the Chera Irumporai, heir of kings who ruled the whole land from Kumari to the northern mountain and from sea to sea with a straight sceptre, lord of cool Thondi of screwpine, fields, sandy groves and backwaters, who escaped his great distress as a tusker fallen into a covered pit breaks its bank and rejoins its rejoicing herd, so that enemy kings, fearing to lose their walls and moats, began to submit; the poet has come to see the king of the Kudavar, with shields like clouds, elephants like hills, an army like the sea and a drum like thunder.","n":"The pit simile (lines 14 to 21) implies a captivity or reverse from which the king broke free, but the poem names neither; the enemy kings' two quoted thoughts (we might regain our land if he is pleased; we shall lose our walls, he looked at us in anger) are a rare piece of the defeated's reasoning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","sengol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 17","lb":"PN17","poet":"குறுங்கோழியூர் கிழார்","pat":"சேரமான் யானைக்கண்சேய் மாந்தரஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை; இயன்மொழியும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை","இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",9,"maram","scene",""],["பூவின்","பூ",12,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",17,"maa","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",35,"maa","scene",""],["தேன்இறை","தேன்",35,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["அரவுத்தலை","அரவு",38,"pira","scene","snake"],["முரசின்","முரசு",39,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","பெருங்கல்",1],["town","தொண்டி",13],["king","குடவர் கோ",40]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:18":{"book":"purananuru","num":18,"t":["முழங்கு முந்நீர் முழுவதும் வளைஇப்","பரந்து பட்ட வியன் ஞாலம்","தாளின் தந்து, தம்புகழ் நிறீஇ:","ஒருதாம் ஆகிய உரவோர் உம்பல்!","ஒன்றுபத்து அடுக்கிய கோடிகடை இரீஇய","பெருமைத்து ஆக நின் ஆயுள் தானே!","நீர்த் தாழ்ந்த குறுங் காஞ்சிப்","பூக் கதூஉம் இன வாளை,","நுண் ஆரல், பரு வரால்,","குரூஉக் கெடிற்ற, குண்டு அகழி;","வான் உட்கும் வடிநீண் மதில்;","மல்லல் மூதூர் வய வேந்தே!","செல்லும் உலகத்துச் செல்வம் வேண்டினும்,","ஞாலம் காவலர் தோள்வலி முருக்கி,","ஒருநீ ஆகல் வேண்டினும், சிறந்த","நல்இசை நிறுத்தல் வேண்டினும், மற்றதன்","தகுதி கேள், இனி, மிகுதியாள!","நீர்இன்று அமையா யாக்கைக்கு எல்லாம்","உண்டி கொடுத்தோர் உயிர்கொடுத் தோரே;","உண்டி முதற்றே உணவின் பிண்டம்;","உணவெனப் படுவது நிலத்தோடு நீரே;","நீரும் நிலனும் புணரியோர் ஈண்டு","உடம்பும் உயிரும் படைத்திசி னோரே;","வித்திவான் நோக்கும் புன்புலம் கண்ணகன்","வைப்புற்று ஆயினும், நண்ணி ஆளும்","இறைவன் தாட்குஉத வாதே; அதனால்,","அடுபோர்ச் செழிய! இகழாது வல்லே","நிலன்நெளி மருங்கின் நீர்நிலை பெருகத்","தட்டோர் அம்ம, இவண்தட் டோரே;","தள்ளா தோர்இவண் தள்ளா தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Kudapulaviyanar blesses the Pandya Nedunchezhiyan, lord of an old city with a moat full of fish and a wall the sky fears, and tells him that whether he wants wealth in the next world, sole rule over the kings of this one, or lasting fame, he should hear what is fitting: bodies cannot live without water, food makes the body, food is land joined with water, so those who join land and water give body and life; land that sows and waits on the sky does not help its ruler however wide, so he should quickly build up tanks where the land dips, for those who bind water here have bound those goals, and those who do not have not.","n":"The argument is a chain (water, food, body, life) closed by a pun on தட்டோர்: those who dammed (water) are those who secured (the three goals), and those who did not, did not. All three royal ambitions of lines 13 to 16, including heaven, are made to depend on irrigation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","uzhavu","mazhai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 18","lb":"PN18","poet":"குடபுலவியனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி : பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி எனவும் பாடம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி","பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["காஞ்சிப்","காஞ்சி",7,"maram","scene",""],["பூக்","பூ",8,"maram","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",8,"pira","scene",""],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",9,"pira","scene",""],["வரால்","வரால்",9,"pira","scene",""],["கெடிற்ற","கெடிறு",10,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செழிய",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:19":{"book":"purananuru","num":19,"t":["இமிழ்கடல் வளைஇய ஈண்டுஅகல் கிடக்கைத்","தமிழ்தலை மயங்கிய தலையாலங் கானத்து,","மன்உயிர்ப் பன்மையும், கூற்றத்து ஒருமையும்.","நின்னொடு தூக்கிய வென்வேற் செழிய!","‘இரும்புலி வேட்டுவன் பொறிஅறிந்து மாட்டிய","பெருங்கல் அடாரும் போன்ம் என விரும்பி,","முயங்கினேன் அல்லனோ யானே! மயங்கிக்","குன்றத்து இறுத்த குரீஇஇனம் போல,","அம்புசென்று இறுத்த அறும்புண் யானைத்","தூம்புஉடைத் தடக்கை வாயொடு துமிந்து.","நாஞ்சில் ஒப்ப, நிலமிசைப் புரள,","எறிந்துகளம் படுத்த ஏந்துவாள் வலத்தர்","எந்தையோடு கிடந்தோர் எம்புன் தலைப்புதல்வர்;","‘இன்ன விறலும் உளகொல், நமக்கு?’என,","மூதில் பெண்டிர் கசிந்து அழ, நாணிக்","கூற்றுக்கண் ஓடிய வெருவரு பறந்தலை,","எழுவர் நல்வலங் கடந்தோய்! நின்","கழூஉ விளங்கு ஆரம் கவைஇய மார்பே?"],"p":"","r":"Kudapulaviyanar recalls, before the Pandya who defeated the seven at Thalaiyalanganam, where Tamils clashed and he alone was weighed against Death, that he embraced the king's chest thinking it like a stone deadfall set by a tiger-hunter, and describes the dreadful field where elephants with severed trunks rolled like ploughs and the women of old houses wept, 'our little sons lie with our fathers: is such valour ours?', so that Death fled ashamed; he asks if this is the chest wearing the bright garland.","n":"Line 3 weighs the king against Death (the many lives against Death's singleness), and line 16 has Death flee ashamed, so the battle is told as a contest the king won against Death itself. The mothers' cry, 'is there such valour for us?', joins grief and pride in one sentence, and the poem lets them stand together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 19","lb":"PN19","poet":"குடபுலவியனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",5,"maa","simile",""],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",5,"seythi","simile",""],["குரீஇஇனம்","குரீஇ",8,"pul","simile","sparrows"],["யானைத்","யானை",9,"maa","simile",""],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",11,"seythi","simile","plough"]],"en":[["battle","தலையாலங் கானத்து",2],["king","செழிய",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:20":{"book":"purananuru","num":20,"t":["இரு முந்நீர்க் குட்டமும்,","வியன் ஞாலத்து அகலமும்,","வளி வழங்கு திசையும்,","வறிது நிலைஇய காயமும், என்றாங்கு","அவை அளந்து அறியினும், அளத்தற்கு அரியை;","அறிவும், ஈரமும், பெருங்க ணோட்டமும்;","சோறு படுக்கும் தீயோடு","செஞ் ஞாயிற்றுத் தெறல் அல்லது","பிறிது தெறல் அறியார் நின் நிழல்வாழ் வோரே;","திருவில் அல்லது கொலைவில் அறியார்;","நாஞ்சில் அல்லது படையும் அறியார்;","திறனறி வயவரொடு தெவ்வர் தேய, அப்","பிறர்மண் உண்ணும் செம்மல்; நின் நாட்டு","வயவுறு மகளிர் வேட்டு உணின் அல்லது,","பகைவர் உண்ணா அருமண் ணினையே;","அம்பு துஞ்சும்கடி அரணால்,","அறம் துஞ்சும் செங்கோலையே;","புதுப்புள் வரினும், பழம்புள் போகினும்,","விதுப்புற அறியா ஏமக் காப்பினை;","அனையை ஆகல் மாறே,","மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் நின்அஞ் சும்மே."],"p":"","r":"Kurunkozhiyur Kizhar tells the Chera that though one might measure the sea's depth, the earth's breadth, the wind's ways and the empty sky, his knowledge, compassion and kindness are beyond measure; those in his shade know no burning but the cooking fire and the sun, no bow but the rainbow, no weapon but the plough; enemies never eat his land's soil, which only craving pregnant women eat; his fortress lets arrows sleep and his sceptre lets dharma rest; his guard is not shaken by omen birds coming or going; so all living beings fear for him.","n":"The poem works by exclusions (no burning but..., no bow but the rainbow, திருவில் against கொலைவில்), so the realm's peace is described through what its people do not know. The last line, மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் நின் அஞ்சும், can mean all beings fear you or fear for you; the praise of a peaceful realm favours the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arivu","kannottam","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 20","lb":"PN20","poet":"குறுங்கோழியூர்கிழார்","pat":"சேரமான் யானைக்கட்சேய் மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் இரும்பொறை; மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் எனவும் குறிப்பர்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சோறு","சோறு",7,"unavu","scene",""],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",11,"seythi","scene","plough"],["புதுப்புள்","புள்",18,"pul","scene","omen birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:1"},"pn:21":{"book":"purananuru","num":21,"t":["புலவரை இறந்த புகழ்சால் தோன்றல்!","நிலவரை இறந்த குண்டுகண் அகழி,","வான்தோய் வன்ன புரிசை, விசும்பின்","மீன்பூத் தன்ன உருவ ஞாயில்,","கதிர்நுழை கல்லா மரம்பயில் கடிமிளை,","அருங் குறும்பு உடுத்த கானப்பேர் எயில்,","கருங்கைக் கொல்லன் செந்தீ மாட்டிய","இரும்புஉண் நீரினும், மீட்டற்கு அரிதுஎன,","வேங்கை மார்பின் இரங்க வைகலும்","ஆடுகொளக் குழைந்த தும்பைப், புலவர்","பாடுதுறை முற்றிய கொற்ற வேந்தே!","இகழுநர் இசையொடு மாயப்,","புகழொடு விளங்கிப் பூக்க, நின் வேலே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the Pandya who took Kanapper fort, whose moat, sky-high wall, star-like battlements and sunless guard forest made it as hard to win back as water that hot iron has drunk, and blesses his spear to flourish in fame while those who scorn him perish with their name.","n":"The fort is described at length only to be the measure of the man who took it: every defence is listed and then cancelled by the single word கடந்த of the colophon and the poem's 'hard to recover'. வேங்கை மார்பின் (line 9) reads best as the name of the fort's lord, Vengaimarpan, made to grieve; taken as 'tiger-chested' it would describe the victor instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","maram","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 21","lb":"PN21","poet":"ஐயூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"கானப்பேரெயில் கடந்த உக்கிரப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மரம்பயில்","மரம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",7,"seythi","simile","smith"],["தும்பைப்","தும்பை",10,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["town","கானப்பேர் எயில்",6],["king","வேங்கை மார்பின்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:22":{"book":"purananuru","num":22,"t":["தூங்கு கையான் ஓங்கு நடைய,","உறழ் மணியான் உயர் மருப்பின,","பிறை நுதலான் செறல் நோக்கின,","பா வடியால் பணை எருத்தின,","தேன் சிதைந்த வரை போல,","மிஞிறு ஆர்க்கும் கமழ்கடா அத்து,","அயறு சோரூம் இருஞ் சென்னிய,","மைந்து மலிந்த மழ களிறு","கந்து சேர்பு நிலைஇ வழங்கப்:","பாஅல் நின்று கதிர் சோரும்","வான உறையும் மதி போலும்","மாலை வெண் குடை நீழலான்,","வாள் மருங்கு இலோர் காப்பு உறங்க,","அலங்கு செந்நெல் கதிர் வேய்ந்த","‘ஆய் கரும்பின் கொடிக் கூரை,","சாறு கொண்ட களம் போல,","வேறு வேறு பொலிவு தோன்றக்","குற் றானா உலக் கையால்;","கலிச் சும்மை வியல் ஆங்கண்","பொலம் தோட்டுப் பைந் தும்பை","மிசை அலங்கு உளைய பனைப்போழ் செரிஇச்,","சின மாந்தர் வெறிக் குரவை","ஓத நீரில் பெயர்பு பொங்க;","வாய் காவாது பரந்து பட்ட","வியன் பாசறைக் காப் பாள!","வேந்து தந்த பணி திறையாற்","சேர்ந் தவர் கடும்பு ஆர்த்தும்,","ஓங்கு கொல்லியோர், அடு பொருந!","வேழ நோக்கின் விறல்வெம் சேஎய்!","வாழிய, பெரும! நின் வரம்பில் படைப்பே!","நிற் பாடிய அலங்கு செந்நாப்","பிற்பிறர் இசை நுவ லாமை,","ஒம்பாது ஈயும் ஆற்றல் எங்கோ!","‘மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் இரும்பொறை ஓம்பிய நாடே","புத்தேள் உலகத்து அற்று’ எனக் கேட்டு, வந்து","இனிது காண்டிசின்: பெரும! முனிவிலை,","வேறுபுலத்து இறுக்கும் தானையோடு","சோறுயட நடத்தி; நீ துஞ்சாய் மாறே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet, having come to see the land of Mantharam Cheral Irumporai said to be like the world of the gods, praises his musth elephants at their posts, his moon-white umbrella under whose shade the unarmed sleep safe, his open war camp loud with the thumbai-crowned warriors' kuravai, and his giving to the kin of those who bring tribute, and says the king never wearies of leading his army into enemy land because he does not sleep.","n":"The camp is called வாய் காவாது பரந்து பட்ட, spread wide with its entrance unguarded: the king's safety is shown by a camp that needs no watch, and the sleepers 'without a sword at their side' sleep under his guard. The closing turn makes his sleeplessness the reason the kingdom is heaven-like.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","eegai","naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 22","lb":"PN22","poet":"குறுங்கோழியூர் கிழார்","pat":"சேரமான் யானைக்கட் சேஎய் மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தேன்","தேன்",5,"pira","simile","honeycomb"],["மிஞிறு","மிஞிறு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["களிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",14,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",15,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பை","தும்பை",20,"maram","ornament",""],["பனைப்போழ்","பனை",21,"maram","ornament","palmyra strip"],["குரவை","குரவை",22,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["சோறு","சோறு",38,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["hill","கொல்லி",28],["king","மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் இரும்பொறை",34]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:23":{"book":"purananuru","num":23,"t":["வெளிறில் நோன்காழ்ப் பணைநிலை முனைஇக்,","களிறுபடிந்து உண்டெனக், கலங்கிய துறையும்!","கார்நறுங் கடம்பின் பாசிலைத் தெரியல்,","சூர்நவை, முருகன் சுற்றத்து அன்ன, நின்","கூர்நல் அம்பின் கொடுவில் கூளியர்","கொள்வது கொண்டு, கொள்ளா மிச்சில்","கொள்பதம் ஒழிய வீசிய புலனும்;","வடிநவில் நவியம் பாய்தலின், ஊர்தொறும்","கடிமரம் துளங்கிய காவும்; நெடுநகர்","வினைபுனை நல்லில் வெவ்வெரி நைப்பக்,","கனைஎரி உரறிய மருங்கும்; நோக்கி,","நண்ணார் நாண, நாள்தொறும் தலைச்சென்று,","இன்னும் இன்னபல செய்குவன், யாவரும்","துன்னல் போகிய துணிவினோன், என,","ஞாலம் நெளிய ஈண்டிய வியன்படை","ஆலங் கானத்து அமர்கடந்து அட்ட","கால முன்ப! நின் கண்டனென் வருவல்;","அறுமருப்பு எழிற்கலை புலிப்பால் பட்டெனச்,","சிறுமறி தழீஇய தெறிநடை மடப்பிணை","பூளை நீடிய வெருவரு பறந்தலை","வேளை வெண்பூக் கறிக்கும்","ஆளில் அத்தம் ஆகிய காடே."],"p":"","r":"Kalladanar tells Nedunchezhiyan, the victor of Alankanam, that he has seen his work in the enemy country: fords churned by bathing elephants, fields stripped by his Murugan-like bowmen, guarded trees felled in every grove, fine houses burned, a land where even the foe says he will do more, and a forest turned to a waste where a doe with her fawn, her stag killed by a tiger, nibbles white velai flowers.","n":"The closing picture is not drawn as a comparison, but a doe left with her young after the stag's death, eating the flowers of a frightening field, is placed exactly where the poem's catalogue of ruin ends; it reads as the enemy's widowed households without saying so. The poet presents the devastation as what he has seen on his way to the king (கண்டனென் வருவல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 23","lb":"PN23","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங் கானத்து நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை; நல்லிசை வஞ்சியும் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை","நல்லிசை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிறுபடிந்து","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",3,"maram","ornament",""],["முருகன்","முருகன்",4,"deyvam","simile",""],["கடிமரம்","கடிமரம்",9,"maram","absent",""],["கலை","கலை",18,"maa","absent","stag"],["புலிப்பால்","புலி",18,"maa","scene",""],["மறி","மறி",19,"maa","scene","fawn"],["பிணை","பிணை",19,"maa","scene","doe"],["பூளை","பூளை",20,"maram","scene",""],["வேளை","வேளை",21,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[["battle","ஆலங் கானத்து",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:24":{"book":"purananuru","num":24,"t":["நெல் அரியும் இருந் தொழுவர்","செஞ் ஞாயிற்று வெயில் முனையின்,","தென் கடல்திரை மிசைப்பா யுந்து;","திண் திமில் வன் பரதவர்","வெப் புடைய மட் டுண்டு,","தண் குரவைச் சீர்தூங் குந்து;","தூவற் கலித்த தேம்பாய் புன்னை","மெல்லிணர்க் கன்ணி மிலைந்த மைந்தர்","எல்வளை மகளிர்த் தலைக்கை தரூஉந்து;","வண்டுபட மலர்ந்த தண்ணறுங் கானல்","முண்டகக் கோதை ஒண்டொடி மகளிர்","இரும் பனையின் குரும்பை நீரும்,","பூங் கரும்பின் தீஞ் சாறும்","ஓங்கு மணற் குலவுத் தாழைத்","தீ நீரோடு உடன் விராஅய்,","முந்நீர் உண்டு முந்நீர்ப் பாயும்;","தாங்கா உறையுள் நல்லூர் கெழீஇய","ஒம்பா ஈகை மாவேள் எவ்வி","புனலம் புதவின் மிழலையொடு கழனிக்","கயலார் நாரை போர்வில் சேக்கும்,","பொன்னணி யானைத் தொன்முதிர் வேளிர்,","குப்பை நெல்லின், முத்தூறு தந்த","கொற்ற நீள்குடைக், கொடித்தேர்ச் செழிய!","நின்று நிலைஇயர் நின் நாண்மீன்; நில்லாது","படாஅச் செலீஇயர், நின்பகைவர் மீனே;","நின்னொடு, தொன்றுமூத்த உயிரினும், உயிரொடு","நின்று மூத்த யாக்கை யன்ன, நின்","ஆடுகுடி மூத்த விழுத்திணைச் சிறந்த","வாளின் வாழ்நர் தாள்வலம் வாழ்த்த,","இரவன் மாக்கள் ஈகை நுவல,","ஒண்டொடி மகளிர் பொலங்கலத்து ஏந்திய","தண்கமழ் தேறல் மடுப்ப, மகிழ்சிறந்து,","ஆங்குஇனிது ஒழுகுமதி, பெரும! ‘ஆங்கது","வல்லுநர் வாழ்ந்தோர், என்ப, தொல்லிசை,","மலர்தலை உலகத்துத் தோன்றிப்","பலர்செலச் செல்லாது, நின்று விளிந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Mangudi Kizhar pictures Nedunchezhiyan's land, where reapers cool off in the sea, fishermen drink and dance, and women drink palm, cane and screw-pine waters, names Evvi's Mizhalai and the Velir's Muthooru among his gains, blesses his star and curses his enemies', bids him live in joy among his warriors, suppliants and women, and closes with the saying that the only ones who truly lived are those whose fame stays when they die.","n":"Sea-shore and field are run together on purpose: the reapers of the paddy plunge into the southern sea and the women drink 'three waters' and bathe in the 'three waters' (முந்நீர், the sea), so the Pandya land is shown as one where marutham and neythal meet. The joyful blessing is a setting for the closing maxim, which turns the poem toward kaanchi: enjoy, but be among those who 'did not go as the many go'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","nilaiyaamai","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 24","lb":"PN24","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழவர்:மாங்குடி மருதனார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங் கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["நெல்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",1,"seythi","scene","field labourers"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மட்","மட்டு",5,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["குரவைச்","குரவை",6,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","ornament",""],["முண்டகக்","முண்டகம்",11,"maram","ornament",""],["பனையின்","பனை",12,"maram","gift_food",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",13,"maram","gift_food",""],["தாழைத்","தாழை",14,"maram","gift_food",""],["கயலார்","கயல்",20,"pira","scene","carp"],["நாரை","நாரை",20,"pul","scene",""],["யானைத்","யானை",21,"maa","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",22,"unavu","scene",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",32,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","எவ்வி",18],["town","மிழலை",19],["town","முத்தூறு",22],["king","செழிய",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:25":{"book":"purananuru","num":25,"t":["மீன்திகழ் விசும்பின் பாய்இருள் அகல","ஈண்டு செலல் மரபின் தன் இயல் வழாஅது,","உரவுச்சினம் திருகிய உருகெழு ஞாயிறு,","நிலவுத்திகழ் மதியமொடு, நிலஞ்சேர்ந் தாஅங்கு,","உடலருந் துப்பின் ஒன்றுமொழி வேந்தரை","அணங்கரும் பறந்தலை உணங்கப் பண்ணிப்,","பிணியுறு முரசம் கொண்ட காலை,","நிலைதிரிபு எறியத், திண்மடை கலங்கிச்","சிதைதல் உய்ந்தன்றோ, நின்வேல்; செழிய!","முலைபொலி அகம் உருப்ப நூறி,","மெய்ம்மறந்து பட்ட வரையாப் பூசல்","ஒள் நுதல் மகளிர் கைம்மை கூர,","அவிர் அறல் கடுக்கும் அம் மென்","குவை யிரும் கூந்தல் கொய்தல் கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Kalladanar tells Chezhiyan that when two allied kings came against him like the sun and the moon come down to earth together, he wasted them on the battlefield and took their drums, and asks whether his spear, which had broken firm ranks, did not stop short of further ruin when he saw the widowed women beating their breasts and shearing their dark hair.","n":"The poem's turn is a question about the spear (சிதைதல் உய்ந்தன்றோ), and whether it means the spear itself escaped damage or was spared further slaughter at the sight of the widows is not certain; on the second reading the victory poem ends in the victor's restraint. The widows shearing their hair is the plain statement of what the same poet left as an image at the end of 23.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 25","lb":"PN25","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசு",7,"parai","scene",""],["அறல்","அறல்",13,"pira","simile","rippled sand"]],"en":[["king","செழிய",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:26":{"book":"purananuru","num":26,"t":["நளி கடல் இருங் குட்டத்து","வளி புடைத்த கலம் போலக்,","களிறு சென்று களன் அகற்றவும்,","களன் அகற்றிய வியல் ஆங்கண்","ஒளிறு இலைய எஃகு ஏந்தி,","அரைசு பட அமர் உழக்கி,","உரை செல முரசு வெளவி,","முடித் தலை அடுப் பாகப்,","புனல் குருதி உலைக் கொளீஇத்,","தொடித்தோள் துடுப்பின் துழந்த வல்சியின்,","அடுகளம் வேட்ட அடுபோர்ச் செழிய!","ஆன்ற கேள்வி, அடங்கிய கொள்கை,","நான்மறை முதல்வர் சுற்ற மாக,","மன்னர் ஏவல் செய்ய, மன்னிய","வேள்வி முற்றிய வாய்வாள் வேந்தே!","நோற்றோர் மன்ற நின் பகைவர், நின்னொடு","மாற்றார் என்னும் பெயர் பெற்று,","ஆற்றார் ஆயினும், ஆண்டுவாழ் வோரே."],"p":"","r":"Mangudi Kizhar praises Chezhiyan, whose elephants clear the field like wind-driven ships on the sea, who killed kings and took their drums and cooked a battlefield sacrifice with crowned heads for a hearth, blood for water and bangled arms for ladles, and who also completed the Vedic sacrifices among learned Brahmins with kings serving him, and says his enemies must have done penance, since by being called his foes they now live in the other world.","n":"The two sacrifices are set side by side with the same verb: அடுகளம் வேட்ட (he sacrificed the killing field) and வேள்வி முற்றிய (he completed the rite). The ending's compliment is grim: the enemies were lucky, because dying at his hand earned them heaven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","padai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 26","lb":"PN26","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழவர்; மாங்குடி மருதனார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile",""],["வல்சியின்","வல்சி",10,"unavu","scene","cooked food"]],"en":[["king","செழிய",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:27":{"book":"purananuru","num":27,"t":["சேற்று வளர் தாமரை பயந்த, ஒண் கேழ்,","நூற் றிதழ் அலரின் நிறை கண் டன்ன,","வேற்றுமை ‘இல்லா விழுத்திணைப் பிறந்து,","வீற்றிருந் தோரை எண்ணுங் காலை,","உரையும் பாட்டும் உடையோர் சிலரே;","மரைஇலை போல மாய்ந்திசினோர் பலரே:","‘புலவர் பாடும் புகழுடையோர் விசும்பின்","வலவன் ஏவா வான ஊர்தி","எய்துப என்ப, தம் செய்வினை முடித்து’ எனக்","கேட்பல்; எந்தை! சேட்சென்னி! நலங்கிள்ளி!","தேய்தல் உண்மையும், பெருகல் உண்மையும்,","மாய்தல் உண்மையும், பிறத்தல் உண்மையும்,","அறியா தோரையும், அறியக் காட்டித்,","திங்கட் புத்தேள் திரிதரும் உலகத்து,","வல்லார் ஆயினும், வல்லுநர் ஆயினும்,","வருந்தி வந்தோர் மருங்கு நோக்கி,","அருள வல்லை ஆகுமதி; அருளிலர்","கொடா அமை வல்லர் ஆகுக;","கெடாஅத் துப்பின்நின் பகைஎதிர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Among those born in flawless lines like the full hundred-petalled lotus and seated on thrones, the poet tells Nalankilli, few have fame in prose and song while many perish like lotus leaves; he has heard that those whom poets praise reach a sky-car no charioteer drives, so in this world where the moon shows everyone waning, waxing, dying and being born, Nalankilli should show grace to all who come in need, skilled or not, and leave ungiving to his enemies.","n":"One plant gives both images: the full lotus flower for the few of noble birth who are remembered and its leaf, which rots unnoticed, for the many who are not. The moon's phases are offered as the plain proof of mortality that even the unlearned can see. The heaven-for-the-praised belief is reported as hearsay (என்ப ... எனக் கேட்பல்), not asserted by the poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pugazh","arul","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 27","lb":"PN27","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["மரைஇலை","தாமரை இலை",6,"maram","simile","lotus leaf"],["திங்கட்","திங்கள்",14,"deyvam","scene","moon god"]],"en":[["king","நலங்கிள்ளி",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:28":{"book":"purananuru","num":28,"t":["‘சிறப்பில் சிதடும், உறுப்பில் பிண்டமும்,","கூனும், குறளும், ஊமும், செவிடும்","மாவும், மருளும், உளப்பட வாழ்நர்க்கு","எண்பேர் எச்சம் என்றிவை எல்லாம்","பேதைமை அல்லது ஊதியம் இல்,’ என","முன்னும் அறிந்தோர் கூறினர்; இன்னும்,","அதன் திறம் அத்தை யான் உரைக்க வந்தது:-","வட்ட வரிய செம்பொறிச் சேவல்","ஏனல் காப்போர் உணர்த்திய கூஉம்","கானத் தோர், நின் தெவ்வர்; நீயே’","புறஞ்சிறை மாக்கட்கு அறங்குறித்து, அகத்தோர்","புய்த்தெறி கரும்பின் விடுகழை தாமரைப்","பூம்போது சிதைய வீழ்ந்தெனக், கூத்தர்","ஆடுகளம் கடுக்கும் அகநாட் டையே;","அதனால், அறனும் பொருளும் இன்பமும் மூன்றும்","ஆற்றும், பெரும! நின்செல்வம்;","ஆற்றாமை நின் போற்றா மையே."],"p":"","r":"Citing the ancients' saying that the eight defective births bring their bearers nothing but folly, the poet tells Nalankilli that his enemies now live in jungles where the jungle-cock wakes the millet-watchers, while his own heartland, where thrown cane-stalks crush lotus buds, looks like a dancers' floor; so his wealth can achieve virtue, wealth and pleasure, and failing to do so would be only his own neglect.","n":"The argument seems to be that a man born whole and rich, unlike the eight kinds of defective birth, has no excuse: if the three aims are not achieved, the fault is his own neglect (போற்றாமை). The link between the opening list and the rest is compressed, and line 11 (புறஞ்சிறை மாக்கட்கு அறங்குறித்து) is hard to place, so this reading is offered with doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 28","lb":"PN28","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",8,"pul","scene","jungle cock"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",9,"maram","scene","millet"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",12,"maram","scene",""],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",12,"maram","scene",""],["கூத்தர்","கூத்தர்",13,"seythi","simile","dancers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:29":{"book":"purananuru","num":29,"t":["அழல் புரிந்த அடர் தாமரை","ஐது அடர்ந்ற நூற் பெய்து,","புனை விளைப் பொலிந்த பொலன் நறுந் தெரியல்","பாறு மயிர் இருந்தலை பொலியச் சூடிப்,","பாண் முற்றுக, நின் நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை!","பாண் முற்று ஒழிந்த பின்றை, மகளிர்","தோள் முற்றுக, நின் சாந்துபுலர் அகலம்! ஆங்க","முனிவில் முற்றத்து, இனிது முரசு இயம்பக்,","கொடியோர்த் தெறுதலும், செவ்வியோர்க்குஅளித்தலும்,","ஒடியா முறையின் மடிவிலை யாகி","‘நல்லதன் நலனும் தீயதன் தீமையும்","இல்லை’ என்போர்க்கு இனன் ஆகி லியர்!","நெல்விளை கழனிப் படுபுள் ஓப்புநர்","ஒழி மடல் விறகின் கழுமீன் சுட்டு,","வெங்கள் தொலைச்சியும் அமையார், தெங்கின்","இளநீர் உதிர்க்கும் வளமிகு நன்னாடு","பெற்றனர் உவக்கும் நின் படைகொள் மாக்கள்","பற்றா மாக்களின் பரிவு முந்து உறுத்துக்,","கூவை துற்ற நாற்கால் பந்தர்ச்,","சிறுமனை வாழ்க்கையின் ஒரீஇ, வருநர்க்கு","உதவி ஆற்றும் நண்பின் பண்புடை","ஊழிற்று ஆக, நின் செய்கை! விழவின்","கோடியர் நீர்மை போல முறை முறை","ஆடுநர் கழியும்இவ் உலகத்துக், கூடிய","நகைப் புறனாக, நின் சுற்றம்!","இசைப்புற னாக, நீ ஓம்பிய பொருளே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet blesses Nalankilli: may bards throng his court, then women embrace him; may he punish the wicked and protect the good without slackening and never side with those who say good and evil bear no fruit; may his soldiers who enjoy his rich land and his deeds be kind to those who come; and in a world where people pass in turn like the players at a festival, may his kin be his joy and his stored wealth turn into fame.","n":"The poem names and rejects a view: 'there is no good in good and no evil in evil' (lines 11, 12), a denial of moral consequence, so the anthology already knows such a doctrine to argue against it. Lines 17 to 22 are syntactically difficult, and whose kindness and whose small huts are meant is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pugazh","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 29","lb":"PN29","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","ornament","golden lotus"],["சாந்துபுலர்","சாந்து",7,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["முரசு","முரசு",8,"parai","scene",""],["படுபுள்","புள்",13,"pul","scene",""],["மடல்","மடல்",14,"maram","scene","palm frond"],["கழுமீன்","மீன்",14,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["வெங்கள்","கள்",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தெங்கின்","தெங்கு",15,"maram","scene",""],["இளநீர்","இளநீர்",16,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கூவை","கூவை",19,"maram","scene",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",23,"seythi","simile","players"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:30":{"book":"purananuru","num":30,"t":["செஞ்ஞா யிற்றுச் செலவும்","அஞ் ஞாயிற்றுப் பரிப்பும்,","பரிப்புச் சூழ்ந்த மண் டிலமும்,","வளி திரிதரு திசையும்,","வறிது நிலைஇய காயமும், என்றிவை","சென்றளந்து அறிந்தார் போல, என்றும்","இனைத்து என்போரும் உளரே அனைத்தும்","அறிவுஅறி வாகச் செறிவினை யாகிக்,","களிறுகவுள் அடுத்த எறிகல் போல","ஒளித்த துப்பினை ஆதலின், வெளிப்பட","யாங்ஙனம் பாடுவர், புலவர்? கூம்பொடு","மீப்பாய் களையாது, மிசைப் பரந் தோண்டாது,","புகாஅர்ப் புகுந்த பெருங்கலந் தகாஅர்","இடைப்புலப் பெருவழிச் சொரியும்","கடல்பல் தாரத்த நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"There are people who speak of the sun's path and motion, the circle it moves in, the quarters the wind blows and the empty sky as if they had gone and measured them, the poet tells Nalankilli, but his strength is hidden like the stone an elephant holds in its cheek, so how can poets sing it openly, lord of the land where great ships enter Puhar without striking mast or sail and pour out the sea's goods on the roads?","n":"The praise works by contrast: the heavens can be measured, but this king cannot. The image of the elephant's cheek-stone (kept hidden until it is thrown) makes the hidden strength a weapon held back, not a lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 30","lb":"PN30","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["களிறுகவுள்","களிறு",9,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["town","புகாஅர்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:31":{"book":"purananuru","num":31,"t":["சிறப்புடை மரபிற் பொருளும் இன்பமும்","அறத்து வழிப்படூஉம் தோற்றம் போல,","இரு குடை பின்பட ஓங்கி ஒரு குடை","உருகெழு மதியின் நிவந்துசேண் விளங்க,","நல்லிசை வேட்டம் வேண்டி, வெல்போர்ப்","பாசறை யல்லது நீயொல் லாயே;","நிதிமுகம் மழுங்க மண்டி, ஒன்னார்","கடிமதில் பாயும் நின் களிறு அடங் கலவே;","‘போர்’ எனில் புகலும் புனைகழல் மறவர்,","‘காடிடைக் கிடந்த நாடுநனி சேஎய;","செல்வேம் அல்லேம்’ என்னார்; ‘கல்லென்","விழவுடை ஆங்கண் வேற்றுப்புலத்து இறுத்துக்,","குண கடல் பின்ன தாகக், குட கடல்","வெண் தலைப் புணரி நின் மான்குளம்பு அலைப்ப,","வலமுறை வருதலும் உண்டு’ என்று அலமந்து","நெஞ்சு நடுங்கு அவலம் பாயத்,","துஞ்சாக் கண்ண, வட புலத்து அரசே."],"p":"","r":"As wealth and pleasure follow behind virtue, the poet tells Nalankilli, the other two kings' umbrellas follow behind his single umbrella shining like the moon; seeking fame he will stay only in a war camp, his elephants never rest from charging forts, his warriors never call any land too far, and so the northern kings lie awake in dread that he will march round from the eastern sea to the western.","n":"The opening simile takes the ordering of the three aims (virtue first, wealth and pleasure following) and uses it to rank the three crowned kings, so an ethical doctrine is made into the vehicle of a political claim. The march 'with the eastern sea behind' and the horses' hooves in the western surf is a circuit of conquest round the land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 31","lb":"PN31","poet":"கோவூர்கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை: மழபுல வஞ்சியும் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை","மழபுல வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","scene",""],["மான்குளம்பு","மா",14,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:32":{"book":"purananuru","num":32,"t":["கடும்பின் அடுகலம் நிறையாக, நெடுங் கொடிப்","பூவா வஞ்சியும் தருகுவன்; ஒன்றோ?","‘வண்ணம் நீவிய வணங்குஇறைப் பணைத்தோள்,","ஒண்ணுதல், விறலியர் பூவிலை பெறுக!’ என,","மாட மதுரையும் தருகுவன்; எல்லாம்","பாடுகம் வம்மினோ, பரிசில் மாக்கள்!","தொன்னிலக் கிழமை சுட்டின், நன்மதி","வேட்கோச் சிறாஅர் தேர்க்கால் வைத்த","பசுமண் குரூஉத்திரள் போல, அவன்","கொண்ட குடுமித்தும், இத் தண்பணை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the gift-seekers to come and sing Nalankilli with him, since he will give Vanji (a 'vanji that bears no flowers') to fill their cooking pots and towered Madurai so the viralis may get the price of flowers; and by ancient right this cool land is in his hand like the fresh clay the potter's boys set on the wheel.","n":"பூவா வஞ்சி is a pun: not the flowering vanji creeper but the Chera capital Vanji. The potter's clay image says the land is his to shape as he likes; the generosity promised is a conquest promised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 32","lb":"PN32","poet":"கோவூர்கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["வேட்கோச்","வேட்கோ",8,"seythi","simile","potter"]],"en":[["town","வஞ்சியும்",2],["town","மதுரையும்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:33":{"book":"purananuru","num":33,"t":["கான் உறை வாழ்க்கைக் கதநாய், வேட்டுவன்","மான்தசை சொரிந்த வட்டியும், ஆய்மகள்","தயிர்கொடு வந்த தசும்பும், நிறைய,","ஏரின் வாழ்நர் பேரில் அரிவையர்","குளக்கீழ் விளைந்த களக்கொள் வெண்ணெல்","முகந்தனர் கொடுப்ப, உகந்தனர் பெயரும்","தென்னம் பொருப்பன் புன்னாட்டுள்ளும்,","ஏழெயில் கதவம் எறிந்து, கைக்கொண்டு நின்;","பேழ்வாய் உழுவை பொறிக்கும் ஆற்றலை;","பாடுநர் வஞ்சி பாடப், படையோர்","தாதுஎரு மறுகின் பாசறை பொலியப்,","புலராப் பச்சிலை இடையிடுபு தொடுத்த","மலரா மாலைப் பந்துகண் டன்ன","ஊன்சோற் றமலை பான்கடும்பு அருத்தும்","செம்மற்று அம்மநின் வெம்முனை இருக்கை;","வல்லோன் தைஇய வரிவனப்பு உற்ற","அல்லிப் பாவை ஆடுவனப்பு ஏய்ப்பக்","காம இருவர் அல்லது, யாமத்துத்","தனிமகன் வழங்காப் பனிமலர்க் காவின்,","ஒதுக்குஇன் திணிமணல் புதுப்பூம் பள்ளி","வாயின் மாடந்தொறும் மைவிடை வீழ்ப்ப","நீஆங்குக் கொண்ட விழவினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Even in the Pandya's country, where the hunter's basket of venison and the herdgirl's pot of curds are filled with white paddy by the ploughmen's women, the poet tells Nalankilli, he broke the doors of seven forts and carved his tiger emblem, and his fierce war camp, where bards sing vanji and the bards' families are fed heaps of meat and rice like balls of unopened flowers, outdoes all the festivals he held in his flowering groves.","n":"The opening shows the barter of three lands (hill meat, pasture curds, field paddy) as a picture of a settled, prosperous country, which makes the conquest larger. Lines 16 to 22 on the festival groves are hard to construe, and the subject of the doll simile and of the rams slaughtered at every gate is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 33","lb":"PN33","poet":"கோவூர்கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கதநாய்","நாய்",1,"maa","scene",""],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்தசை","மான் தசை",2,"unavu","gift_food","venison"],["ஆய்மகள்","ஆய்மகள்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["தயிர்","தயிர்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஏரின் வாழ்நர்","உழவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["உழுவை","உழுவை",9,"maa","scene","tiger emblem"],["பச்சிலை","பச்சிலை",12,"maram","simile",""],["ஊன்சோற்","ஊன் சோறு",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மைவிடை","மை விடை",21,"maa","gift_food","ram"]],"en":[["king","தென்னம் பொருப்பன்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:34":{"book":"purananuru","num":34,"t":["‘ஆன்மலை அறுத்த அறனி லோர்க்கும்,","மாண்இழை மகளிர் கருச்சிதைத் தோர்க்கும்,","குரவர்த் தப்பிய கொடுமை யோர்க்கும்,","வழுவாய் மருங்கின் கழுவாயும் உள’ என,","‘நிலம்புடை பெயர்வ தாயினும், ஒருவன்","செய்தி கொன்றோர்க்கு உய்தி இல்’ என’","அறம் பாடின்றே ஆயிழை கணவ!","‘காலை அந்தியும், மாலை அந்தியும்,","புறவுக் கருவன்ன புன்புல வரகின்","பாற்பெய் புன்கம் தேனொடு மயக்கிக்,","குறுமுயற் கொழுஞ்சூடு கிழித்த ஒக்கலொடு,","இரத்தி நீடிய அகன்தலை மன்றத்துக்,","கரப்பில் உள்ளமொடு வேண்டுமொழி பயிற்றி,","அமலைக் கொழுஞ்சோறு ஆர்ந்த பாணர்க்கு","அகலாச் செல்வம் முழுவதும் செய்தோன்,","எங்கோன்,வளவன் வாழ்க!’என்று, நின்","பீடுகெழு நோன்தாள் பாடேன் ஆயின்,","படுபறி யலனே, பல்கதிர்ச் செல்வன்;","யானோ தஞ்சம்; பெரும! இவ் வுலகத்துச்","சான்றோர் செய்த நன்றுண் டாயின்,","இமையத்து ஈண்டி, இன்குரல் பயிற்றிக்,","கொண்டல் மாமழை பொழிந்த","நுண்பல் துளியினும் வாழிய, பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Dharma says there is expiation for those who cut a cow's udders, destroy women's unborn children or wrong their elders, but none, even if the earth is overturned, for those who kill a benefit; so, the poet tells Killivalavan, if he did not sing the king who made the bards rich after they ate millet with milk and honey and roast hare, the sun would not rise; and he blesses him to live more years than the drops the Himalayan rain-cloud pours.","n":"The poem quotes a rule of dharma in order to apply it to the poet himself: not to praise his benefactor would be the one sin with no atonement. The poet calls himself தஞ்சம் (a humble, dependent man), and the cosmic oath (the sun would not rise) raises his debt of gratitude to the order of nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai","parisil","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 34","lb":"PN34","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஆன்மலை","ஆன்",1,"maa","scene","cow"],["புறவுக்","புறவு",9,"pul","simile","dove"],["வரகின்","வரகு",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தேனொடு","தேன்",10,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["குறுமுயற்","முயல்",11,"maa","gift_food",""],["கொழுஞ்சோறு","சோறு",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பாணர்க்கு","பாணர்",14,"seythi","scene",""],["மாமழை","மழை",22,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["king","வளவன்",16],["hill","இமையத்து",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:35":{"book":"purananuru","num":35,"t":["நளிஇரு முந்நீர் ஏணி யாக,","வளிஇடை வழங்கா வானம் சூடிய","மண்திணி கிடக்கைத் தண்தமிழ்க் கிழவர்,","முரசு முழங்கு தானை மூவர் உள்ளும்,","அரசுஎனப் படுவது நினதே, பெரும!","அலங்குகதிர்க் கனலி நால்வயின் தோன்றினும்,","இலங்குகதிர் வெள்ளி தென்புலம் படரினும்,","அந்தண் காவிரி வந்து கவர்பு ஊட்டத்,","தோடு கொள் வேலின் தோற்றம் போல,","ஆடுகண் கரும்பின் வெண்பூ நுடங்கும்","நாடுஎனப் படுவது நினதே அத்தை; ஆங்க","நாடுகெழு செல்வத்துப் பீடுகெழு வேந்தே!","நினவ கூறுவல்: எனவ கேண்மதி!","அறம்புரிந் தன்ன செங்கோல் நாட்டத்து","முறைவெண்டு பொழுதின் பதன் எளியோர் ஈண்டு","உறைவேண்டு பொழுதில் பெயல்பெற் றோறே;","ஞாயிறு சுமந்த கோடுதிரள் கொண்டுமூ","மாக விசும்பின் நடுவுநின் றாங்குக்,","கண்பொர விளங்கும்நின் விண்பொரு வியன்குடை","வெயில்மறைக் கொண்டன்றோ? அன்றே; வருந்திய","குடிமறைப் பதுவே; கூர்வேல் வளவ!","வெளிற்றுப்பனந் துணியின் வீற்றுவீற்றுக் கிடப்பக்,","களிற்றுக் கணம் பொருத கண்ணகன் பறந்தலை,","வருபடை தாங்கிப், பெயர்புறத் தார்த்துப்,","பொருபடை தரூஉங் கொற்றமும் உழுபடை","ஊன்றுசால் மருங்கின் ஈன்றதன் பயனே;","மாரி பொய்ப்பினும், வாரி குன்றினும்,","இயற்கை அல்லன செயற்கையில் தோன்றினும்,","காவலர்ப் பழிக்கும், இக் கண்ணகன் ஞாலம்;","அதுநற்கு அறிந்தனை யாயின், நீயும்","நொதும லாளர் பொதுமொழி கொள்ளாது,","பகடுபுறந் தருநர் பாரம் ஓம்பிக்,","குடிபுறம் தருகுவை யாயின், நின்","அடிபுறம் தருகுவர், அடங்கா தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Killivalavan that among the three Tamil kings kingship is his and the Kaveri-fed land is his, then counsels him: in the land of the just sceptre where the humble can reach the king when they seek justice, rain comes when it is sought; his tall umbrella is not for shade but to shelter suffering subjects; his victories are the fruit of the plough's furrow; the world blames kings when rains fail or harvests drop, so he should ignore the talk of malcontents, bear the burden of those who rear the plough-oxen and protect his people, and even the unsubmitting will bow at his feet.","n":"Two causal claims carry the counsel: just rule brings rain, and victory in battle grows out of the furrow. The question about the umbrella (is it to keep off the sun? no) turns the most visible royal emblem into a duty of shelter. The poem also warns that kings are blamed even for drought, whether or not they caused it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","uzhavu","mazhai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 35","lb":"PN35","poet":"வெள்ளைக்குடி நாகனார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ"],"mp":[],"k":[["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",10,"maram","simile",""],["பனந்","பனை",22,"maram","simile","palmyra trunk"],["களிற்றுக்","களிறு",23,"maa","scene",""],["பகடுபுறந்","பகடு",32,"maa","scene","plough ox"],["உழுபடை","உழுபடை",25,"seythi","scene","plough"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:36":{"book":"purananuru","num":36,"t":["அடுநை யாயினும், விடுநை யாயினும்,","நீ அளந் தறிதி நின் புரைமை; வார்தோல்,","செயறியரிச் சிலம்பின், குறுந்தொடி மகளிர்","பொலஞ்செய் கழங்கின் தெற்றி யாடும்","தண்ணான் பொருநை வெண்மணல் சிதையக்,","கருங்கைக் கொல்லன் அரஞ்செய் அவ்வாய்","நெடுங்கை நவியம் பாய்தலின், நிலையழிந்து,","வீகமழ் நெடுஞ்சினை புலம்பக், காவுதொறும்","கடிமரம் தடியும் ஓசை தன்ஊர்","நெடுமதில் வரைப்பின் கடிமனை இயம்ப,","ஆங்குஇனி திருந்த வேந்தனொடு, ஈங்குநின்","சிலைத்தார் முரசும் கறங்க,","மலைத்தனை எண்பது நாணுத்தகவு உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Whether he kills or releases the enemy, the poet tells Killivalavan, he knows his own worth: while the sound of his smiths' axes felling the guarded trees on the Porunai's banks, where girls once played with gold stones on the sand, echoes into the walled palace where the other king sits at ease, and Valavan's drum roars here, to say he fought such a king is a matter of shame.","n":"The poem praises by rebuke: it says nothing against the siege's power, only that a king who will not come out is no fit opponent, so the honour of continuing the war is gone. The girls' play on the river sand, ruined by the felling, is a small picture of what the war destroys.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maram","pagai","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 36","lb":"PN36","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["சிலம்பின்","சிலம்பு",3,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",4,"pira","scene","game stones"],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",6,"seythi","scene","smith"],["கடிமரம்","கடிமரம்",9,"maram","absent",""],["முரசும்","முரசு",12,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["river","பொருநை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:37":{"book":"purananuru","num":37,"t":["நஞ்சுடை வால் எயிற்று, ஐந்தலை சுமந்த,","வேக வெந்திறல், நாகம் புக்கென,","விசும்புதீப் பிறப்பத் திருகிப், பசுங்கொடிப்","பெருமலை விடரகத்து உரும்எறிந் தாங்குப்,","புள்ளுறு புன்கண் தீர்த்த, வெள் வேல்","சினங்கெழு தானைச், செம்பியன் மருக!","கராஅம் கலித்த குண்டுகண் அகழி,","இடம்கருங் குட்டத்து உடந்தொக்கு ஓடி,","யாமம் கொள்பவர் சுடர்நிழல் கதூஉம்","கடுமுரண் முதலைய நெடுநீர் இலஞ்சிச்,","செம்புஉறழ் புரிசைச், செம்மல் மூதூர்,","வம்புஅணி யானை வேந்துஅகத் துண்மையின்,","‘நல்ல’ என்னாது, சிதைத்தல்","வல்லையால் நெடுந்தகை! செருவத் தானே!"],"p":"","r":"Descendant of the Chembiyan who relieved a bird's distress, with an army that strikes like the thunderbolt that hits the hill cleft a five-headed cobra has entered, the poet tells Killivalavan, he is able to wreck in battle the old town walled like copper, with its crocodile-filled moat where the beasts snap at the torch-shadows of the night watch, without sparing it as 'fine', because a king with decked elephants is inside.","n":"Lineage and conduct are set against each other: the king is first named by his ancestor's famous mercy to a bird, and then praised for showing no mercy to a fine old town, because a rival king shelters in it. The poem neither blames nor excuses the destruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aran","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 37","lb":"PN37","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"வாகை; உழிஞை எனவும் பாடம்","ctu":"அரச வாகை குற்றுழிஞை எனவும், முதல் வஞ்சி எனவும் பாடம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை","குற்றுழிஞை","முதல் வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நாகம்","நாகம்",2,"pira","simile","cobra"],["புள்ளுறு","புள்",5,"pul","scene","dove of the Sibi story"],["கராஅம்","கராம்",7,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["முதலைய","முதலை",10,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செம்பியன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:38":{"book":"purananuru","num":38,"t":["வரை புரையும் மழகளிற்றின் மிசை,","வான் துடைக்கும் வகைய போல","விரவு உருவின கொடி நுடங்கும்","வியன் தானை விறல் வேந்தே!","நீ, உடன்று நோக்கும்வாய் எரிதவழ,","நீ, நயந்து நோக்கும்வாய் பொன்பூப்பச்,","செஞ் ஞாயிற்று நிலவு வேண்டினும்,","வெண் திங்களுள் வெயில் வேண்டினும்,","வேண்டியது விளைக்கும் ஆற்றலை ஆகலின்,","நின்நிழல் பிறந்து, நின்நிழல் வளர்ந்த,","எம் அளவு எவனோ மற்றே? ‘இன்நிலைப்","பொலம்பூங் காவின் நன்னாட் டோரும்","செய்வினை மருங்கின் எய்தல் அல்லதை,","உடையோர் ஈதலும், இல்லோர் இரத்தலும்","கடவ தன்மையின், கையறவு உடைத்து’என,","ஆண்டுச் செய் நுகர்ச்சி ஈண்டும் கூடலின்,","நின்நாடு உள்ளுவர், பரிசிலர்:","ஒன்னார் தேஎத்தும், நின்னுடைத் தெனவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Killivalavan, whose angry glance spreads fire and whose loving glance makes gold bloom, and who could bring moonlight out of the sun or sunshine out of the moon, that those born and raised in his shade cannot measure him, and that gift-seekers even in enemy lands think of his country, because heaven, where people only enjoy what their deeds earned and where no one gives or asks, is lacking, while here the same enjoyment is had along with giving.","n":"The poem ranks the king's land above heaven on one ground: in heaven there is neither giving nor asking, so the gift-relation itself, which bards live by, is what earth has that heaven lacks. The comparison of the two worlds is put in the bards' mouths.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","naadu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 38","lb":"PN38","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மழகளிற்றின்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:39":{"book":"purananuru","num":39,"t":["புறவின் அல்லல் சொல்லிய, கறையடி","யானை வான்மருப் பெறிந்த வெண்கடைக்","கோல்நிறை துலாஅம் புக்கோன் மருக!","ஈதல்நின் புகழும் அன்றே; சார்தல்","ஒன்னார் உட்கும் துன்னரும் கடுந்திறல்","தூங்கெயில் எறிந்தநின் ஊங்கணோர் நினைப்பின்,","அடுதல்நின் புகழும் அன்றே; கெடுவின்று,","மறங்கெழு சோழர் உறந்தை அவையத்து,","அறம்நின்று நிலையிற் றாகலின், அதனால்","முறைமைநின் புகழும் அன்றே; மறம்மிக்கு,","எழுசமம் கடந்த எழுஉறழ் திணிதோள்,","கண்ணார் கண்ணிக், கலிமான், வளவ!","யாங்கனம் மொழிகோ யானே; ஓங்கிய","வரையளந் தறியாப் பொன்படு நெடுங்கோட்டு","இமையம் சூட்டியஏம விற்பொறி,","மாண்வினை நெடுந்தேர், வானவன் தொலைய","வாடா வஞ்சி வாட்டும்நின்","பீடுகெழு நோன்தாள் பாடுங் காலே?"],"p":"","r":"Descendant of the king who entered the ivory-beamed scale for a dove's sake, the poet tells Killivalavan, giving is not his own fame; with ancestors who destroyed the hanging fort, killing is not his fame; with justice standing firm in the Chola court at Urantai, justice is not his fame; so how can he praise him, except by singing the might that withered the unwithering Vanji when the Chera of the Himalaya-carved bow fell?","n":"Each inherited virtue is set aside as the family's, not his, which leaves one deed that is his alone: the defeat of the Chera. வாடா வஞ்சி வாட்டும் is a pun, the vanji (creeper, and the Chera capital) that does not wither, made to wither.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai","sengol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 39","lb":"PN39","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["புறவின்","புறவு",1,"pul","scene","dove"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["கலிமான்","மா",12,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["town","தூங்கெயில்",6],["town","உறந்தை",8],["hill","இமையம்",15],["king","வானவன்",16],["town","வஞ்சி",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:40":{"book":"purananuru","num":40,"t":["நீயே, பிறர் ஓம்புறு மறமன் னெயில்","ஓம்பாது கடந்தட்டு, அவர்","முடி புனைந்த பசும் பொன்னின்","அடி பொலியக் கழல் தைஇய","வல் லாளனை, வய வேந்தே!","யாமே, நின், இகழ் பாடுவோர் எருத்தடங்கப்,","புகழ் பாடுவோர் பொலிவு தோன்ற,","இன்றுகண் டாங்குக் காண்குவம், என்றும்","இன்சொல் எண்பதத்தை ஆகுமதி; பெரும!","ஒருபிடி படியுஞ் சீறிடம்","எழுகளிறு புரக்கும் நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Killivalavan, who took others' guarded forts and wears anklets made of the gold of their crowns, that his poets wish to see him always as today, so that his detractors bow their necks and his praisers shine, and asks him to be sweet of speech and easy to reach, lord of the land where the small spot one cow-elephant lies on feeds seven bull elephants.","n":"The counsel is two words long, இன்சொல் and எண்பதம் (ease of access), and it is given to a conqueror at the height of his strength, as the condition for his praise lasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","arasu","naadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 40","lb":"PN40","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஒருபிடி","பிடி",10,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["எழுகளிறு","களிறு",11,"maa","scene","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:2"},"pn:41":{"book":"purananuru","num":41,"t":["காலனும் காலம் பார்க்கும்; பாராது","வேல்ஈண்டு தானை விழுமியோர் தொலைய,","வேண்டிடத்து அடூஉம் வெல்போர் வேந்தே!","திசைஇரு நான்கும் உற்கம் உற்கவும்,","பெருமரத்து, இலையில் நெடுங்கோடு வற்றல் பற்றவும்,","வெங்கதிர்க் கனலி துற்றவும், பிறவும்,","அஞ்சுவரத் தகுந புள்ளுக்குரல் இயம்பவும்,","எயிறுநிலத்து வீழவும், எண்ணெய் ஆடவும்,","களிறுமேல் கொள்ளவும், காழகம் நீப்பவும்,","வெள்ளி நோன்படை கட்டிலொடு கவிழவும்,","கனவின் அரியன காணா, நனவின்","செருச்செய் முன்ப,! நின் வருதிறன் நோக்கி,","மையல் கொண்ட ஏமம்இல் இருக்கையர்,","புதல்வர் பூங்கண் முத்தி, மனையோட்கு","எவ்வம் சுரக்கும் பைதல் மாக்களொடு","பெருங்கலக் குற்றன்றால் தானே; காற்றோடு","எரிநிகழ்ந் தன்ன செலவின்","செருமிகு வளவ! நிற் சினைஇயோர் நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Death itself waits for its moment, but this Chola strikes whenever he wishes; in the lands of those who angered him every evil sign appears, in the sky, in the trees, in birdcalls and in dreams, and the people, dazed and defenceless, kiss their children and grieve with their wives in a great uproar.","n":"The praise works entirely through the victims: a native list of portents (meteors in all eight directions, a leafless branch catching fire, teeth falling out, an oil bath and riding an elephant in dreams) makes the coming defeat look already decided, and the king is greater than Death because he does not wait. Several of the dream signs in lines 8 to 10 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","acham","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 41","lb":"PN41","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"கொற்ற வள்ளை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கொற்ற வள்ளை"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பெருமரத்து","மரம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளுக்குரல்","புள்",7,"pul","scene",""],["களிறுமேல்","களிறு",9,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","வளவ",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:42":{"book":"purananuru","num":42,"t":["ஆனா ஈகை, அடு போர், அண்ணல்! நின்","யானையும் மலையின் தோன்றும்; பெரும! நின்","தானையும் கடலென முழங்கும்; கூர்நுனை","வேலும் மின்னின் விளங்கும் ; உலகத்து","அரைசுதலை பனிக்கும் ஆற்றலை யாதலின்,","புரைதீர்ந் தன்று; அது புதுவதோ அன்றே;","தண்புனற் பூசல் அல்லது, நொந்து,","‘களைக, வாழி, வளவ! ‘ என்று, நின்","முனைதரு பூசல் கனவினும் அறியாது,","புலிபுறங் காக்கும் குருளை போல,","மெலிவில் செங்கோல் நீபுறங் காப்பப்,","பெருவிறல் யாணர்த் தாகி, அரிநர்","கீழ்மடைக் கொண்ட வாளையும், உழவர்","படைமிளிர்ந் திட்ட யாமையும், அறைநர்","கரும்பிற் கொண்ட தேனும், பெருந்துறை","நீர்தரு மகளிர் குற்ற குவளையும்,","வன்புலக் கேளிர்க்கு வருவிருந் தயரும்","மென்புல வைப்பின் நன்னாட்டுப் பொருந!","மலையின் இழிந்து, மாக்கடல் நோக்கி,","நிலவரை இழிதரும் பல்யாறு போலப்,","புலவ ரெல்லாம் நின்நோக் கினரே;","நீயே, மருந்தில் கணிச்சி வருந்த வட்டித்துக்","கூற்றுவெகுண் டன்ன முன்பொடு,","மாற்றுஇரு வேந்தர் மண்நோக் கினையே."],"p":"","r":"The Chola's elephants loom like hills, his army roars like the sea and his spears flash like lightning, so every king trembles; yet under his unbending sceptre his own fertile country hears no cry of war, only the noise of floods, and feeds guests from the dry lands; poets flow to him as rivers to the sea, while he turns on the land of the two rival kings with the force of angry Death.","n":"The poem sets two kinds of பூசல் (clamour) against each other: inside the kingdom only the noise of rising water, never the cry of a raid; the peace within is the other face of the terror without. The catalogue of plenty (fish taken by reapers, tortoises turned up by the plough, honey from the cane, lilies plucked by water-carrying women) is offered as food for guests, so the land's wealth is measured by its hospitality. Line 18 recurs in poem 209, a formula for the watered country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","maram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 42","lb":"PN42","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["யானையும்","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["புலிபுறங்","புலி",10,"maa","simile","tiger"],["குருளை","குருளை",10,"maa","simile","cub"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["வாளையும்","வாளை",13,"pira","gift_food","vaalai fish"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["யாமையும்","யாமை",14,"pira","gift_food","tortoise"],["கரும்பிற்","கரும்பு",15,"maram","scene",""],["தேனும்","தேன்",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["குவளையும்","குவளை",16,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","வளவ",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:43":{"book":"purananuru","num":43,"t":["நிலமிசை வாழ்நர் அலமரல் தீரத்,","தெறுகதிர்க் கனலி வெம்மை தாங்கிக்,","கால்உண வாகச், சுடரொடு கொட்கும்","அவிர்சடை முனிவரும் மருளக், கொடுஞ்சிறைக்","கூருகிர்ப் பருந்தின் ஏறுகுறித், தொரீஇத்,","தன்னகம் புக்க குறுநடைப் புறவின்","தபுதி யஞ்சிச் சீரை புக்க","வரையா ஈகை உரவோன் மருக!","நேரார்க் கடந்த முரண்மிகு திருவின்","தேர்வண் கிள்ளி தம்பி! வார் கோல்,","கொடுமர மறவர் பெரும! கடுமான்","கைவண் தோன்றல்! ஐயம் உடையேன்:","‘ஆர்புனை தெரியல்நின் முன்னோர் எல்லாம்","பார்ப்பார் நோவன செய்யலர்: மற்றுஇது","நீர்த்தோ நினக்கு?’ என வெறுப்பக் கூறி,","நின்யான் பிழைத்தது நோவாய் என்னினும்,","நீபிழைத் தாய்போல் நனிநா ணினையே;","‘தம்மைப் பிழைத்தோர்ப் பொறுக்குஞ் செம்மல்!","இக்குடிப் பிறந்தோர்க் கெண்மை காணும்’ எனக்","காண்டகு மொய்ம்ப! காட்டினை; ஆகலின்,","யானே பிழைத்தனென் ! சிறக்கநின் ஆயுள்;","மிக்குவரும் இன்னீர்க் காவிரி","எக்கர் இட்ட மணலினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet addresses a Chola prince of the line of the king who entered the scales to save a dove from a hawk: he admits he spoke in anger, asking whether hurting brahmins befits a son of such ancestors, and that the prince, instead of resenting it, was ashamed as if he himself had erred, showing that his house bears with those who wrong it; so the poet declares the fault his own and wishes the prince more years than the sands of the Kaveri.","n":"The dove story is used twice: the poet invokes the ancestor's sacrifice for a weak bird to rebuke the prince, and the prince's forbearance turns the same family standard back on the poet, who ends with யானே பிழைத்தனென் (it is I who erred). The poem does not say what the quarrel was about; பார்ப்பார் நோவன in line 14 implies that the poet speaks as a brahmin who felt wronged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","naanam","kudi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 43","lb":"PN43","poet":"தாமப்பல் கண்ணனார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி தம்பி மாவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["முனிவரும்","முனிவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",5,"pul","scene",""],["புறவின்","புறவு",6,"pul","scene","dove"],["மணலினும்","மணல்",23,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","கிள்ளி தம்பி",10],["river","காவிரி",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:44":{"book":"purananuru","num":44,"t":["இரும்பிடித் தொழுதியொடு பெருங்கயம் படியா,","நெல்லுடைக் கவளமொடு நெய்ம்மிதி பெறாஅ,","திருந்தரை நோன்வெளில் வருந்த ஒற்றி,","நிலமிசைப் புரளும் கைய, வெய்துயிர்த்து,","அலமரல் யானை உருமென முழங்கவும்,","பாலில் குழவி அலறவும், மகளிர்","பூவில் வறுந்தலை முடிப்பவும், நீரில்","வினைபுனை நல்லில் இனைகூஉக் கேட்பவும்,","இன்னாது அம்ம, ஈங்கு இனிது இருத்தல்;","துன்னரும் துப்பின் வயமான் தோன்றல்!","அறவை யாயின்,’நினது’ எனத் திறத்தல்!","மறவை யாயின், போரொடு திறத்தல்;","அறவையும் மறவையும் அல்லை யாகத்,","திறவாது அடைத்த திண்ணிலைக் கதவின்","நீள்மதில் ஒருசிறை ஒடுங்குதல்","நாணுத்தக வுடைத்திது காணுங் காலே."],"p":"","r":"Inside the besieged fort the elephants, unbathed and unfed, rub against their posts and roar like thunder, babies cry for milk, women tie up hair without flowers and the houses cry out for water; it is bitter to sit here at ease: if you are righteous, open the gate saying it is theirs; if you are brave, open it with battle; being neither, to cower behind shut doors in a corner of the long wall is shameful.","n":"The poet gives the besieged king exactly two honourable exits, yielding as an act of aram or fighting as an act of maram, and names the third, waiting behind the wall, as the shameful one. The suffering of elephants, infants and women is the argument: counsel here works by making the cost of delay visible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","naanam","aram","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 44","lb":"PN44","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நெடுங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["இரும்பிடித்","பிடி",1,"maa","absent","she-elephant"],["நெல்லுடைக்","நெல்",2,"unavu","absent",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பூவில்","பூ",7,"maram","absent",""],["நீரில்","நீர்",7,"pira","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:45":{"book":"purananuru","num":45,"t":["இரும்பனை வெண்தோடு மலைந்தோன் அல்லன்;","கருஞ்சினை வேம்பின் தெரியலோன் அல்லன்!","நின்ன கண்ணியும் ஆர்மிடைந் தன்றே; நின்னொடு","பொருவோன் கண்ணியும் ஆர்மிடைந் தன்றே;","ஒருவீர் தோற்பினும், தோற்ப நும் குடியே;","இருவீர் வேறல் இயற்கையும் அன்றே; அதனால்,","குடிப்பொருள் அன்று, நும் செய்தி; கொடித்தேர்","நும்மோர் அன்ன வேந்தர்க்கு","மெய்ம்மலி உவகை செய்யும், இவ் இகலே!"],"p":"","r":"Neither of you wears the Chera's palmyra leaf or the Pandya's neem; both your garlands are of aar, so if either of you loses, it is your own house that loses, and both cannot win; this fight does not befit your clan, and it will only give deep delight to other kings like you with bannered chariots.","n":"The argument is made through garlands, the emblems of the three houses: because both kings wear the same aar, the war can have no winner, only a losing house, and the only gain goes to dynasties outside. The appeal is to lineage and to the rivals' delight, not to compassion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 45","lb":"PN45","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளியும், நெடுங்கிள்ளியும்","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்பனை","பனை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆர்மிடைந்","ஆர்",3,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:46":{"book":"purananuru","num":46,"t":["நீயே, புறவின் அல்லல் அன்றியும், பிறவும்","இடுக்கண் பலவும் விடுத்தோன் மருகனை;","இவரே, புலனுழுது உண்மார் புன்கண் அஞ்சித்,","தமதுபகுத்து உண்ணும் தண்ணிழல் வாழ்நர்!","களிறுகண்டு அழூஉம் அழாஅல் மறந்த","புன்றலைச் சிறாஅர் மன்று மருண்டு நோக்கி,","விருந்திற் புன்கண்நோ வுடையர்;","கெட்டனை யாயின், நீ வேட்டது செய்ம்மே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet reminds the Chola that he descends from the king who relieved the dove's distress and many others' troubles, and points to children of a house that shares its own food for fear of the hardship of those who live by learning; these small boys, who have stopped crying at the sight of the elephant, now stare bewildered at the assembly in a new distress; having heard this, the king may do as he wishes.","n":"Two lineages are set face to face, the king's, famous for saving a dove, and the children's, famous for sharing with the learned; the verdict is left to the king, so cruelty would be a betrayal of his own ancestry. The children's having stopped crying at the elephant and now staring at the court shows a fear too young to understand what is happening. The syntax of lines 3 and 4 and the reading of line 8 (see source_issues) are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kannottam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 46","lb":"PN46","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புறவின்","புறவு",1,"pul","scene","dove"],["களிறுகண்டு","களிறு",5,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:47":{"book":"purananuru","num":47,"t":["வள்ளியோர்ப் படர்ந்து, புள்ளின் போகி,","‘நெடிய’ என்னாது சுரம்பல கடந்து,","வடியா நாவின் வல்லாங்குப் பாடிப்,","பெற்றது மகழ்ந்தும், சுற்றம் அருத்தி,","ஓம்பாது உண்டு, கூம்பாது வீசி,","வரிசைக்கு வருந்தும்இப் பரிசில் வாழ்க்கை","பிறர்க்குத் தீதறிந் தன்றோ? இன்றே; திறம்பட","நண்ணார் நாண, அண்ணாந்து ஏகி,","ஆங்குஇனிது ஒழுகின் அல்லது, ஓங்கு புகழ்","மண்ணாள் செல்வம் எய்திய","நும்மோர் அன்ன செம்மலும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The bard's life of gifts, flying to patrons like birds, crossing wastes without counting them long, singing as well as an untrained tongue allows, rejoicing in what it gets, feeding its kin, spending without saving and giving without stint, troubled only about due honour, knows no harm to anyone; it walks with head high so that its rivals are shamed, and has a dignity like that of kings who rule the earth.","n":"The poem answers a charge it never names: by insisting that the wandering bard harms no one, it lets us infer that a man who crosses frontiers from court to court could be suspected of serving the enemy. It then claims for the bard a pride equal to the king's, so the defence ends as a statement of rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","maanam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 47","lb":"PN47","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"காரியாற்றுத் துஞ்சிய நெடுங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புள்ளின்","புள்",1,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:48":{"book":"purananuru","num":48,"t":["கோதை மார்பிற் கோதை யானும்,","கோதையைப் புணர்ந்தோர் கோதை யானும்,","மாக்கழி மலர்ந்த நெய்த லானும்,","கள்நா றும்மே, கானல்அம் தொண்டி;","அஃதுஎம் ஊரே; அவன்எம் இறைவன்;","அன்னோற் படர்தியாயின், நீயும்","எம்மும் உள்ளுமோ; முதுவாய் இரவல!","‘அமர்மேம் படூஉங் காலை, நின்","புகழ்மேம் படுநனைக் கண்டனம்’ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"Thondi of the seaside grove smells of honey from the garland on Kothai's chest, from the garlands of the women who embrace him and from the neytal flowers of the great backwater; that is our town and he is our lord; old-tongued suppliant, if you go to him, remember us too, and tell him: we saw the man whose fame rises whenever battle rises.","n":"The pun on கோதை, the king's name and a garland, runs through the first two lines, so the king, his garland and the garlands of the women who embrace him all perfume one town. The request to be remembered turns the guide poem into a message the suppliant carries back to the patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aatruppadai","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 48","lb":"PN48","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"சேரமான் கோக்கோதை மார்பன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"புலவராற்றுப் படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["புலவர் ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["நெய்த லானும்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["கள்நா","கள்",4,"unavu","scene","honey"]],"en":[["king","கோதை",1],["town","தொண்டி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:49":{"book":"purananuru","num":49,"t":["நாடன் என்கோ? ஊரன் என்கோ?","பாடிமிழ் பனிக்கடற் சேர்ப்பன் என்கோ?","யாங்கனம் மொழிகோ, ஓங்குவாள் கோதையை?","புனவர் தட்டை புடைப்பின், அயலது","இறங்குகதிர் அலமரு கழனியும்,","பிறங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பினும் , புள் ஒருங்கு எழுமே!"],"p":"","r":"Shall I call Kothai of the raised sword lord of the hills, lord of the towns, or lord of the cold shore where the sea roars? How can I name him, when the millet-watchers beat their rattles and the birds rise together from the nearby fields of bending ears and from the shining shore?","n":"நாடன், ஊரன் and சேர்ப்பன் are the akam titles of the lords of hill, field and shore, and the answer is a single picture: one rattle in the millet patch lifts the birds of the paddy and of the shore at once, so the three landscapes lie close enough for one sound to cross them. Unlike 48, the poem gives no direction to a suppliant, so the colophon's guide-poem thurai fits it only by its pairing with 48.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 49","lb":"PN49","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"சேரமான் கோக்கோதை மார்பன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"புலவராற்றுப் படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["புலவர் ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["புனவர்","புனவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["தட்டை","தட்டை",4,"pira","scene","bamboo rattle"],["இறங்குகதிர்","கதிர்",5,"maram","scene","ear of paddy"],["புள்","புள்",6,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கோதையை",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:50":{"book":"purananuru","num":50,"t":["மாசற விசித்த வார்புஉறு வள்பின்","மைபடு மருங்குல் பொலிய, மஞ்ஞை","ஒலிநெடும் பீலி ஒண்பொறி, மணித்தார்,","பொலங்குழை உழிஞையொடு, பொலியச் சூட்டிக்,","குருதி வேட்கை உருகெழு முரசம்","மண்ணி வாரா அளவை, எண்ணெய்","நுரைமுகந் தன்ன மென்பூஞ் சேக்கை","அறியாது ஏறிய என்னைத் தெறுவர,","இருபாற் படுக்குநின் வாள்வாய் ஒழித்ததை","அதூஉம் சாலும், நற் றமிழ்முழுது அறிதல்;","அதனொடும் அமையாது, அணுக வந்து, நின்","மதனுடை முழவுத்தோள் ஓச்சித், தண்ணென","வீசி யோயே; வியலிடம் கமழ,","இவன்இசை உடையோர்க்கு அல்லது, அவணது","உயர்நிலை உலகத்து உறையுள் இன்மை","விளங்கக் கேட்ட மாறுகொல்:","வலம்படு குருசில்! நீ ஈங்குஇது செயலே?"],"p":"","r":"Before the blood-thirsty war drum, strapped tight and decked with peacock plumes, a garland of gems and golden uzhinai, had come back from its ritual bath, the poet unknowingly lay down on its soft couch, white as foam of oil; instead of cutting him in two with his sword, which would itself have proved that he knew all good Tamil, the king came near and fanned him with his drum-like arm, as if he had heard that only the famous have a dwelling in the higher world.","n":"Two graces are graded: sparing the trespasser on the sacred drum would have been enough to show the king's learning in Tamil, but he went further and fanned the sleeper. The closing question explains the act by belief in fame as the passport to the higher world, so mercy is presented as the king's investment in his name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","arul","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 50","lb":"PN50","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"சேரமான் தகடூர் எறிந்த பெருஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",2,"pul","ornament","peacock"],["உழிஞையொடு","உழிஞை",4,"maram","ornament",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""],["எண்ணெய்","எண்ணெய்",6,"pira","simile",""],["முழவுத்தோள்","முழவு",12,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:51":{"book":"purananuru","num":51,"t":["நீர்மிகின், சிறையும் இல்லை; தீமிகின்,","மன்னுயிர் நிழற்றும் நிழலும் இல்லை;","வளிமிகின், வலியும் இல்லை; ஒளிமிக்கு","அவற்றோர் அன்ன சினப்போர் வழுதி,","‘தண் தமிழ் பொது’ எனப் பொறாஅன், போர் எதிர்ந்து,","கொண்டி வேண்டுவன் ஆயின், ‘கொள்க’ எனக்","கொடுத்த மன்னர் நடுக்கற் றனரே;","அளியரோ அளியர், அவன் அளிஇழந் தோரே;","நுண்பல் சிதலை அரிதுமுயன்று எடுத்த","செம்புற்று ஈயல் போல,","ஒருபகல் வாழ்க்கைக்கு உலமரு வோரே!"],"p":"","r":"As nothing dams a flood, no shade shelters from overgrown fire and no strength stands against a gale, so nothing stands against angry Vazhuthi, who will not bear the claim that the cool Tamil land is shared; the kings who gave him tribute when he asked are free from fear, but pitiable are those who lost his favour, fluttering for a one-day life like the winged termites rising from the red mound the tiny termites built with toil.","n":"'தண் தமிழ் பொது' is quoted as a claim the king will not bear, that the Tamil land is common to the three crowns, so the poem praises a bid for sole rule. The termite image is double: the colony's long labour builds the mound, and the winged ones that leave it live a single day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu","nilaiyaamai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 51","lb":"PN51","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்! ஐயூர் கிழார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"பாண்டியன் கூடகாரத்துத் துஞ்சிய மாறன் வழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["சிதலை","சிதல்",9,"pira","simile","termite"],["செம்புற்று","புற்று",10,"pira","simile",""],["ஈயல்","ஈயல்",10,"pira","simile","winged termite"]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:52":{"book":"purananuru","num":52,"t":["அணங்கு உடை நெடுங்கோட்டு அளையகம் முனைஇ,","முணங்கு நிமிர் வயமான் முழுவலி ஒருத்தல்,","ஊன்நசை உள்ளம் துரப்ப, இசை குறித்துத்","தான் வேண்டு மருங்கின் வேட்டுஎழுந் தாங்கு,","வடபுல மன்னர் வாட, அடல் குறித்து,","இன்னா வெம்போர் இயல்தேர் வழுதி!","இதுநீ கண்ணியது ஆயின், இரு நிலத்து","யார்கொல் அளியர் தாமே? ஊர்தொறும்","மீன்சுடு புகையின் புலவுநாறு நெடுங் கொடி","வயல்உழை மருதின் வாங்குசினை வலக்கும்,","பெருநல் யாணரின் ஒரீஇ, இனியே","கலி கெழு கடவுள் கந்தம் கைவிடப்","பலிகண் மாறிய பாழ்படு பொதியில்,","நரைமூ தாளர் நாயிடக் குழிந்த","வல்லின் நல்லகம் நிறையப், பல்பொறிக்","கான வாரணம் ஈனும்","காடாகி விளியும் நாடுடை யோரே!"],"p":"","r":"Like a strong male tiger that tires of its cave in the haunted peak, stretches with a groan and, driven by hunger, goes out to hunt where it pleases, Vazhuthi sets out to make the northern kings wither; if that is his aim, pitiable are those whose rich towns, where fish-smoke clings to creepers on the marutha trees, will turn to jungle, the common hall abandoned by its god and offerings, a forest fowl laying eggs in the dice holes the grey elders carved.","n":"Praise is delivered as pity for the enemy: most of the poem pictures the after-state, the common hall emptied of its god and of its elders' game, with a jungle fowl nesting in the dice holes, so the king's glory is measured by the ruin it will make.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","naadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 52","lb":"PN52","poet":"மருதன் இளநாகனார்; மருதிள நாகனார் என்பதும் பாடம்","pat":"பாண்டியன் கூடகாரத்துத் துஞ்சிய மாறன் வழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயமான்","வயமான்",2,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மீன்சுடு","மீன்",9,"pira","scene","fish"],["கொடி","கொடி",9,"maram","scene","creeper"],["மருதின்","மருது",10,"maram","scene",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",12,"deyvam","absent",""],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",16,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:53":{"book":"purananuru","num":53,"t":["முதிர்வார் இப்பி முத்த வார் மணல்,","கதிர்விடு மணியின் கண்பொரு மாடத்து,","இலங்கு வளை மகளிர் தெற்றி ஆடும்","விளங்கு சீர் விளங்கில் விழுமம் கொன்ற","களங்கொள் யானைக், கடுமான், பொறைய!","விரிப்பின் அகலும்; தொகுப்பின் எஞ்சும்;","மம்மர் நெஞ்சத்து எம்மனோர்க்கு ஒருதலை","கைம்முற் றலநின் புகழே, என்றும்;","ஒளியோர் பிறந்தஇம் மலர்தலை உலகத்து","வாழேம் என்றலும் அரிதே; ‘தாழாது","செறுத்த செய்யுள் செய்செந் நாவின்,","வெறுத்த கேள்வி, விளங்குபுகழ்க் கபிலன்","இன்றுளன் ஆயின், நன்றுமன், என்ற நின்","ஆடு கொள் வரிசைக்கு ஒப்பப்","பாடுவன் மன்னால், பகைவரைக் கடப்பே."],"p":"","r":"To Poraiyan of the war elephants and swift horses, who ended the distress of Vilankil, the pearl-strewn town where bangled women play on the terraces of gem-bright mansions: your fame widens if spelled out and is still unfinished if summed up, beyond the grasp of bewildered minds like mine; yet in a world where the great were born one cannot refuse to sing, and since you wished that Kapilan of the flawless verse were alive today, I will sing your conquests as your rank deserves.","n":"The poet measures himself against a famous predecessor whom the king himself named: the king's wish for Kapilar sets the standard, and the poet accepts it only by promising to sing to the measure of the king's rank. It is early evidence that a poet's name outlived him at court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kalvi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 53","lb":"PN53","poet":"பொருந்தில் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"சேரமான் மாந்தரஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["இப்பி","இப்பி",1,"pira","scene","pearl oyster"],["முத்த","முத்து",1,"pira","scene",""],["யானைக்","யானை",5,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கடுமான்","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["town","விளங்கில்",4],["king","பொறைய",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:54":{"book":"purananuru","num":54,"t":["எங்கோன் இருந்த கம்பலை முதூர்,","உடையோர் போல இடையின்று குறுகிச்,","செம்மல் நாளவை அண்ணாந்து புகுதல்","எம்அன வாழ்க்கை இரவலர்க்கு எளிதே;","இரவலர்க்கு எண்மை யல்லது; புரவு எதிர்ந்து","வானம் நாண, வரையாது, சென்றோர்க்கு","ஆனாது ஈயும் கவிகை வண்மைக்","கடுமான் கோதை துப்பெதிர்ந்து எழுந்த","நெடுமொழி மன்னர் நினைக்குங் காலைப்,","பாசிலைத் தொடுத்த உவலைக் கண்ணி,","மாசுண் உடுக்கை, மடிவாய், இடையன்","சிறுதலை ஆயமொடு குறுகல் செல்லாப்","புலிதுஞ்சு வியன்புலத்து அற்றே","வலிதுஞ்சு தடக்கை அவனுடை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"For suppliants like us it is easy to walk straight, as if we owned it, into the noisy old town where our lord sits and enter his court with heads high; but to the boastful kings who rose against Kothai, who gives without stint until the rain-cloud is ashamed, his land is like a wide tiger-haunted pasture that a herdsman with a leaf garland, grimy cloth and whistling lips will not approach with his small-headed flock.","n":"The open gate of the court and the closed frontier of the land are made one fact: the ease with which bards walk in is the other side of the dread that keeps rival kings out. The herdsman with his leaf garland and grimy cloth is an everyday figure used to measure royal danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","maram","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 54","lb":"PN54","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சலூர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"சேரமான் குட்டுவன் கோதை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["உவலைக்","உவலை",10,"maram","ornament",""],["இடையன்","இடையன்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["ஆயமொடு","ஆயம்",12,"maa","simile","flock"],["புலிதுஞ்சு","புலி",13,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[["king","கோதை",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:55":{"book":"purananuru","num":55,"t":["ஓங்கு மலைப் பெருவில் பாம்புஞாண் கொளீஇ,","ஒரு கணை கொண்டு மூவெயில் உடற்றிப்","பெரு விறல் அமரர்க்கு வெற்றி தந்த","கறை மிடற்று அண்ணல் காமர் சென்னிப்","பிறை நுதல் விளங்கும் ஒருகண் போல,","வேந்து மேம்பட்ட பூந்தார் மாற!","கடுஞ் சினத்த கொல் களிறும்;","கதழ் பரிய கலி மாவும்,","நெடுங் கொடிய நிமிர் தேரும்,","நெஞ் சுடைய புகல் மறவரும், என","நான்குடன் மாண்ட தாயினும், மாண்ட","அறநெறி முதற்றே, அரசின் கொற்றம்;","அதனால், நமரெனக் கோல்கோ டாது,","‘பிறர்’ எனக் குணங் கொல்லாது,,","ஞாயிற் றன்ன வெந்திறல் ஆண்மையும்,","திங்கள் அன்ன தண்பெருஞ் சாயலும்,","வானத்து அன்ன வண்மையும், மூன்றும்,","உடையை ஆகி, இல்லோர் கையற,","நீநீடு வாழிய நெடுந்தகை! தாழ்நீர்!","வெண் தலைப் புணரி அலைக்கும் செந்தில்","நெடுவேள் நிலைஇய காமர் வியந்துறைக்","கடுவளி தொகுப்ப ஈண்டிய","வடுஆழ் எக்கர் மணலினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Maran, foremost among kings as the single eye shines on the forehead of the blue-throated god who strung the mountain bow with a snake and burned the three forts with one arrow: though elephants, horses, chariots and warriors make an army, a king's victory has the righteous way at its root; so do not bend the sceptre for your own or kill merit because it is another's; be like the sun in valour, the moon in gentleness and the rain-cloud in giving, so that the needy want nothing, and live longer than the sands the wind heaps at Senthil.","n":"The four-limbed army is listed and then ranked below conduct, and conduct is defined as impartiality both ways: not bending the rod for 'ours', not destroying merit because it is 'theirs'. The three similes give the king one face for enemies, one for subjects and one for suppliants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sengol","aram","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 55","lb":"PN55","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ"],"mp":[],"k":[["அண்ணல்","அண்ணல்",4,"deyvam","simile",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",7,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மாவும்","மா",8,"maa","scene","horse"],["நெடுவேள்","வேள்",21,"deyvam","scene",""],["மணலினும்","மணல்",23,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["king","மாற",6],["town","செந்தில்",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:56":{"book":"purananuru","num":56,"t":["ஏற்று வலன் உயரிய எரிமருள் அவிர்சடை,","மாற்றருங் கணிச்சி, மணி மிடற் றோனும்,","கடல் வளர் புரிவளை புரையும் மேனி,","அடல் வெந் நாஞ்சில், பனைக்கொடி யோனும்;","மண் ணுறு திருமணி புரையும் மேனி,","விண்ணுயர் புல்கொடி, விறல்வெய் யோனும்,","மணி மயில் உயரிய மாறா வென்றிப்,","பிணிமுக ஊர்தி, ஒண்செய் யோனும்-என","ஞாலம் காக்கும் கால முன்பின்,","தோலா நல்இசை, நால்வர் உள்ளும்,","கூற்றுஒத் தீயே, மாற்றருஞ் சீற்றம்;","வலிஒத் தீயே, வாலி யோனைப்;","புகழ்ஒத் தீயே, இகழுநர் அடுநனை;","முருகுஒத் தீயே, முன்னியது முடித்தலின்;","ஆங்குஆங்கு அவரவர் ஒத்தலின், யாங்கும்","அரியவும் உளவோ, நினக்கே? அதனால்,","இரவலர்க்கு அருங்கலம் அருகாது ஈயா,","யவனர் நன்கலம் தந்த தண்கமழ் தேறல்","பொன்செய் புனை கலத்து ஏந்தி, நாளும்","ஒண் தொடி மகளிர் மடுப்ப, மகிழ்சிறந்து,","ஆங்கினிது ஒழுகுமதி! ஓங்குவாள் மாற!","அங்கண் விசும்பின் ஆரிருள் அகற்றும்","வெங்கதிர்ச் செல்வன் போலவும், குடதிசைத்","தண்கதிர் மதியம் போல்வும்,","நின்று நிலைஇயர், உலகமோடு உடனே!"],"p":"","r":"Of the four gods who guard the world, the bull-bannered one with the axe and the dark throat, the conch-white one with the plough and palmyra banner, the sapphire-dark one with the bird banner and the peacock-bannered rider of the elephant, the Pandya equals the first in wrath, the second in strength, the third in fame and the fourth in achieving what he sets out to do; so let him give rare treasures to suppliants, drink cool Yavana wine poured in gold cups by bangled women, live happily, and endure with the world like the sun and the moon.","n":"The likening to gods is systematic: four gods, each identified by banner, weapon and colour, each lending one quality. The counsel to enjoy life (இனிது ஒழுகுமதி) comes only after the command to give to suppliants, and the Yavana wine is a sign of foreign trade at a Pandya court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arasu","eegai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 56","lb":"PN56","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரனார்; (மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார் எனவும் பாடம்)","pat":"பாண்டியன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பூவை நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பூவை நிலை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["ஏற்று","ஏறு",1,"maa","ornament","bull"],["மிடற் றோனும்","மிடற்றோன்",2,"deyvam","simile",""],["புரிவளை","வளை",3,"pira","simile","conch"],["பனைக்கொடி யோனும்","பனைக்கொடியோன்",4,"deyvam","simile",""],["விறல்வெய் யோனும்","வெய்யோன்",6,"deyvam","simile",""],["மயில்","மயில்",7,"pul","ornament",""],["ஒண்செய் யோனும்","செய்யோன்",8,"deyvam","simile",""],["அருங்கலம்","கலம்",17,"pira","gift_food","jewel"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",18,"unavu","gift_food","wine"]],"en":[["king","மாற",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:57":{"book":"purananuru","num":57,"t":["வல்லார் ஆயினும், வல்லுநர் ஆயினும்,","புகழ்தல் உற்றோர்க்கு மாயோன் அன்ன,","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் புகழ்சால் மாற!","நின்னொன்று கூறுவது உடையோன்; என்னெனின்,","நீயே, பிறர்நாடு கொள்ளும்காலை, அவர் நாட்டு","இறங்கு கதிர் கழனிநின் இளையரும் கவர்க:","நனந்தலைப் பேரூர் எரியும் நைக்க;","மின்னு நிமிர்ந் தன்ன நின்ஒளிறு இலங்கு நெடுவேல்","ஒன்னார்ச் செகுப்பினும் செகுக்க; என்னதூஉம்","கடிமரம் தடிதல் ஓம்பு! நின்","நெடுதல் யானைக்குக் கந்தாற் றாவே."],"p":"","r":"Maran, whom all praisers, skilled or not, liken to Mayon: when you take another's land, let your young men plunder its fields of bending grain, let fire waste its wide towns, let your lightning-bright spear kill your foes if it will, but whatever you do, spare their guardian trees, for they will not serve as posts for your tall elephants.","n":"The counsel allows plunder, burning and killing and draws its one line at the enemy's guardian tree, the emblem a house protects; the reason given is a practical irony, that such a tree makes no tethering post, which lets the poet restrain the king without preaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","maram","pagai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 57","lb":"PN57","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணை வஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["இறங்கு கதிர்","கதிர்",6,"maram","scene","ear of grain"],["கடிமரம்","கடிமரம்",10,"maram","scene","guardian tree"],["யானைக்குக்","யானை",11,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","மாற",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:58":{"book":"purananuru","num":58,"t":["நீயே, தண்புனற் காவிரிக் கிழவனை; இவளே,","முழுமுதல் தொலைந்த கோளி ஆலத்துக்","கொழுநிழல் நெடுஞ்சினை வீழ்பொறுத் தாங்குத்,","தொல்லோர் மாய்ந்தெனத் துளங்கல் செல்லாது.","நல்லிசை முதுகுடி நடுக்குஅறத் தழீஇ,","இளையது ஆயினும் கிளைஅரா எறியும்,","அருநரை உருமின், பெருநரைப் பொறாஅச்","செருமாண் பஞ்சவர் ஏறே; நீயே,","அறந்துஞ்சு உறந்தைப் பொருநனை; இவனே,","நெல்லும் நீரும் எல்லார்க்கும் எளியவென,","வரைய சாந்தமும், திரைய முத்தமும்,","இமிழ்குரல் முரசம் மூன்றுடன் ஆளும்,","தமிழ்கெழு கூடல் தண்கோல் வேந்தே;","பால்நிற உருவின் பனைக்கொடி யோனும்,","நீல்நிற உருவின், நேமியோனும், என்று","இருபெருந் தெய்வமும் உடன் நின் றாஅங்கு,","உருகெழு தோற்றமொடு உட்குவர விளங்கி,","இந்நீர் ஆகலின், இனியவும் உளவோ?","இன்னும் கேண்மின் நும் இசைவா ழியவே;","ஒருவீர் ஒருவீர்க்கு ஆற்றுதிர்; இருவீரும்","உடனிலை திரியீர் ஆயின், இமிழ்திரைப்","பெளவம் உடுத்தஇப் பயங்கெழு மாநிலம்","கையகப் படுவது பொய்யா காதே;","அதனால், நல்ல போலவும், நயவ போலவும்,","தொல்லோர் சென்ற நெறியர் போலவும்,","காதல் நெஞ்சின் நும் இடை புகற்கு அலமரும்","ஏதில் மாக்கள் பொதுமொழி கொள்ளாது,","இன்றே போல்க நும் புணர்ச்சி; வென்று வென்று","அடுகளத்து உயர்க நும் வேலே; கொடுவரிக்","கோள்மாக் குயின்ற சேண்விளங்கு தொடுபொறி","நெடுநீர்க் கெண்டையொடு பொறித்த","குடுமிய ஆக, பிறர் குன்றுகெழு நாடே."],"p":"","r":"To the Chola, lord of the Kaveri and of Urandai, and the Pandya of Tamil Koodal, the young bull of the Panchavas who upheld his ancient house when the elders died as aerial roots hold up a banyan whose trunk has perished: standing together you shine like the white god of the palmyra banner and the blue god of the wheel; help each other, and if you never break your union the sea-girt earth will surely be yours; do not heed outsiders who, seeming good and kind and loyal to old ways, are eager to come between you; may your union stay as it is today, your spears rise in victory and other kings' hill-countries bear your tiger and carp on their peaks.","n":"The address is balanced exactly, நீயே and இவனே twice, a line of praise for each, before the two are joined in one image of two gods standing together. The danger named is not war but go-betweens, described by three 'seeming' (போலவும்) phrases; the closing wish to carve tiger and carp together on others' hills makes the alliance a programme of conquest. Lines 6 and 7 (the young thunder and the snake) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 58","lb":"PN58","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"சோழன் குராப்பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய பெருந் திருமா வளவனும் பாண்டியன் வெள்ளியம் பலத்துத் துஞ்சிய பெரு வழுதியும்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"உடனிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உடனிலை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஆலத்துக்","ஆலம்",2,"maram","simile","banyan"],["கிளைஅரா","அரா",6,"pira","simile","snake"],["ஏறே","ஏறு",8,"maa","simile","bull"],["நெல்லும்","நெல்",10,"unavu","scene",""],["சாந்தமும்","சாந்தம்",11,"maram","scene","sandal"],["முத்தமும்","முத்து",11,"pira","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",12,"parai","scene",""],["பனைக்கொடி யோனும்","பனைக்கொடியோன்",14,"deyvam","simile",""],["நேமியோனும்","நேமியோன்",15,"deyvam","simile",""],["கோள்மாக்","கோள்மா",30,"maa","ornament","tiger emblem"],["கெண்டையொடு","கெண்டை",31,"pira","ornament","carp emblem"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",1],["king","பஞ்சவர்",8],["town","உறந்தை",9],["town","கூடல்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:59":{"book":"purananuru","num":59,"t":["ஆரம் தாழ்ந்த அணிகிளிர் மார்பின்,","தாள்தோய் தடக்கைத், தகைமாண் வழுதி!","வல்லை மன்ற, நீநயந் தளித்தல்!","தேற்றாய், பெரும! பொய்யே; என்றும்","காய்சினம் தவிராது கடல்ஊர்பு எழுதரும்","ஞாயிறு அனையை, நின் பகைவர்க்குத்;","திங்கள் அனையை, எம்ம னோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Vazhuthi with the garland on your chest and arms that reach your knees, you are truly skilled at protecting with love and you know no falsehood; to your enemies you are like the sun that rises from the sea with unabating heat, to people like us you are like the moon.","n":"The poem compresses the double simile of 55 into two lines, and the turn is in எம்மனோர்க்கே: the poet places himself among those who receive the moon's coolness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 59","lb":"PN59","poet":"மதுரைக் கூலவாணிகன் சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் சித்திரமாடத்துத் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பூவைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பூவை நிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["king","வழுதி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:60":{"book":"purananuru","num":60,"t":["முந்நீர் நாப்பண் திமில்சுடர் போலச்,","செம்மீன் இமைக்கும் மாக விசும்பின்","உச்சி நின்ற உவவுமதி கண்டு,","கட்சி மஞ்ஞையின் சுரமுதல் சேர்ந்த,","சில் வளை விறலியும், யானும், வல்விரைந்து,","தொழுதனம் அல்லமோ, பலவே! கானல்","கழிஉப்பு முகந்து கல்நாடு மடுக்கும்","ஆரைச் சாகாட்டு ஆழ்ச்சி போக்கும்","உரனுடை நோன்பகட்டு அன்ன எங்கோன்,","வலன் இரங்கு முரசின் வாய்வாள் வளவன்,","வெயில்மறைக் கொண்ட உருகெழு சிறப்பின்","மாலை வெண்குடை ஒக்குமால் எனவே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet and the virali, who had reached the edge of the wasteland like peahens, saw the full moon at the zenith, among twinkling red stars like a fishing boat's lamp in mid-sea, and hurried to worship it many times, because it looked like the white parasol of their lord Valavan of the true sword, a king like the strong ox that drags a salt cart's sunken wheel out of the rut on the road from the backwaters to the hill country.","n":"The usual simile is reversed: the bards worship the moon because it resembles the king's parasol, not the other way round. The ox that hauls a salt cart out of a rut gives the king a working animal's image, strength shown when the load sinks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 60","lb":"PN60","poet":"உறையூர் மருத்துவன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"சோழன் குராப்பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய பெருந் திருமா வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"குடை மங்கலம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குடை மங்கலம்"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["திமில்சுடர்","திமில்",1,"pira","simile","fishing boat"],["மஞ்ஞையின்","மஞ்ஞை",4,"pul","simile","peahen"],["கழிஉப்பு","உப்பு",7,"pira","scene","salt"],["சாகாட்டு","சாகாடு",8,"pira","simile","cart"],["நோன்பகட்டு","பகடு",9,"maa","simile","ox"],["முரசின்","முரசு",10,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வளவன்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:3"},"pn:61":{"book":"purananuru","num":61,"t":["கொண்டைக் கூழைத் தண்டழைக் கடைசியர்","சிறுமாண் நெய்தல் ஆம்பலொடு கட்கும்,","மலங்கு மிளிர், செறுவின் தளம்புதடிந் திட்ட","பழன வாளைப் பரூஉக்கண் துணியல்","புதுநெல் வெண்சோற்றுக் கண்ணுறை ஆக,","விலாப் புடை மருங்கு விசிப்ப மாந்தி,","நீடுகதிர்க் கழனிச் சூடுதடு மாறும்","வன்கை வினைஞர் புன்தலைச் சிறாஅர்","தெங்குபடு வியன்பழம் முனையின், தந்தையர்","குறைக்கண் நெடுபோர் ஏறி, விசைத் தெழுந்து,","செழுங்கோட் பெண்ணைப் பழந்தொட முயலும்,","வைகல் யாணர், நன்னாட்டுப் பொருநன்,","எஃகுவிளங்கு தடக்கை இயல்தேர்ச் சென்னி,","சிலைத்தார் அகலம் மலைக்குநர் உளர்எனின்,","தாமறி குவர்தமக்கு உறுதி; யாம் அவன்","எழுஉறழ் திணிதோள் வழுவின்றி மலைந்தோர்","வாழக் கண்டன்றும் இலமே; தாழாது,","திருந்து அடி பொருந்த வல்லோர்","வருந்தக் காண்டல், அதனினும் இலமே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet paints the plenty of Chenni's farmland, where weeding women eat fresh rice with thick slices of fish and the labourers' children, tired of coconuts, climb the straw stacks to reach for palmyra fruit, and then says that whoever challenges this king learns his fate: no one who fought him has been seen to live, and no one who bowed at his feet has been seen to suffer.","n":"The last lines set two fates side by side: those who fight him do not live, and those who come to fit themselves to his feet (திருந்து அடி பொருந்த) do not suffer. The praise of might is also an offer of protection, and the farm scene of children too full to want coconuts is what that protection yields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 61","lb":"PN61","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சிலுர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"சோழன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நலங்கிள்ளி சேட்சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடைசியர்","கடைசியர்",1,"seythi","scene","farm women"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily (weeded)"],["ஆம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["மலங்கு","மலங்கு",3,"pira","scene","eel"],["வாளைப்","வாளை",4,"pira","gift_food","vaalai fish"],["புதுநெல்","புது நெல்",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வெண்சோற்றுக்","வெண் சோறு",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",8,"seythi","scene","field labourers"],["தெங்குபடு","தெங்கு",9,"maram","gift_food","coconut"],["பெண்ணைப்","பெண்ணை",11,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[["king","சென்னி",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:62":{"book":"purananuru","num":62,"t":["வருதார் தாங்கி, அமர்மிகல் யாவது?","பொருது ஆண்டொழிந்த மைந்தர் புண்தொட்டுக்,","குருதிச் செங்கைக் கூந்தல் தீட்டி,","நிறம்கிளர் உருவின் பேஎய்ப் பெண்டிர்","எடுத்துஎறி அனந்தற் பறைச்சீர் தூங்கப்,","பருந்து அருந்துற்ற தானையொடு செருமுனிந்து,","அறத்தின் மண்டிய மறப்போர் வேந்தர்","தாம்மாய்ந் தனரே; குடைதுளங் கினவே;","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் முரசு ஒழிந்தனவே;","பன்னூறு அடுக்கிய வேறுபடு பைஞ்ஞிலம்","இடம்கெட ஈண்டிய வியன்கண் பாசறைக்,","களங்கொளற்கு உரியோர் இன்றித், தெறுவர,","உடன்வீழ்ந் தன்றால், அமரே; பெண்டிரும்","பாசடகு மிசையார், பனிநீர் மூழ்கார்,","மார்பகம் பொருந்தி ஆங்கமைந் தன்றே;","வாடாப் பூவின், இமையா நாட்டத்து,","நாற்ற உணவினோரும் ஆற்ற","அரும்பெறல் உலகம் நிறைய","விருந்துபெற் றனரால்; பொலிக, நும் புகழே!"],"p":"","r":"Asking what victory there can be in such a war, the poet describes a field where both kings, who fought by the rule of right, have died together with their armies: demon women dance with bloodied hands to the drum, the umbrellas shake, the drums are silent, no one is left to claim the field, the queens refuse the widow's greens and cold bath and lie down on their husbands' breasts, and only the gods in their unfading world gain guests; the poet ends 'may your fame flourish'.","n":"The opening question (அமர்மிகல் யாவது?) and the closing blessing frame the poem as counsel against such war: the only party that gains is heaven, which gets its guests. The queens who neither eat the widow's greens nor take the cold bath but settle on their husbands' breasts are, on the plain sense, dying with them; the poem states it without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","padai","pugazh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 62","lb":"PN62","poet":"கழாத் தலையார்","pat":"சேரமான் குடக்கோ நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்; சோழன் வேற்பஃறடக் கைப் பெருவிறற் கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தொகை நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தொகை நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பேஎய்ப்","பேய்",4,"pira","scene","demon"],["பறைச்சீர்","பறை",5,"parai","scene",""],["பருந்து","பருந்து",6,"pul","scene","kite"],["முரசு","முரசு",9,"parai","absent",""],["பாசடகு","பாசடகு",14,"unavu","absent","green leaves (widow's food)"],["பூவின்","பூ",16,"maram","scene","flowers of the gods"],["உணவினோரும்","நாற்ற உணவினோர்",17,"deyvam","scene","gods"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:63":{"book":"purananuru","num":63,"t":["எனைப்பல் யானையும் அம்பொடு துளங்கி,","விளைக்கும் வினையின்றிப் படைஒழிந் தனவே;","விறற் புகழ் மாண்ட புரவி எல்லாம்","மறத் தகை மைந்தரொடு ஆண்டுப்பட் டனவே;","தேர்தர வந்த சான்றோர் எல்லாம்,","தோல் கண் மறைப்ப, ஒருங்கு மாய்ந்தனரே;","விசித்து வினை மாண்ட மயிர்க்கண் முரசம்,","பொறுக்குநர் இன்மையின், இருந்துவிளிந் தனவே;","சாந்தமை மார்பின் நெடுவேல் பாய்ந்தென,","வேந்தரும் பொருது, களத்து ஒழிந்தனர்; இனியே,","என்னா வதுகொல் தானே; கழனி","ஆம்பல் வள்ளித் தொடிக்கை மகளிர்","பாசவல் முக்கித், தண்புனல் பாயும்,","யாணர் அறாஅ வைப்பின்","காமர் கிடக்கை அவர் அகன்றலை நாடே?"],"p":"","r":"After a battle in which the elephants, horses, warriors and drums are all lost and both kings have fallen on the field, the poet asks what will now become of their wide, rich lands, where women with bangles of lily stalk eat fresh flattened rice and dive into cool water.","n":"Each line of the catalogue ends on a verb of ending (ஒழிந்தனவே, பட்டனவே, மாய்ந்தனரே, விளிந்தனவே), and the drums die 'for want of anyone to carry them'. The question at the end turns from the dead to the living land, which now has no king; the poem counts the cost to the country rather than the glory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","naadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 63","lb":"PN63","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"சோழன் வேற்பஃறடக்கைப் பெருவிறற் கிள்ளி; சேரமான் குடக்கோ நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தொகை நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தொகை நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானையும்","யானை",1,"maa","absent",""],["புரவி","புரவி",3,"maa","absent","horse"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",7,"parai","absent",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",12,"maram","ornament","lily-stalk bangle"],["பாசவல்","பசு அவல்",13,"unavu","gift_food","fresh flattened rice"],["புனல்","புனல்",13,"pira","scene","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:64":{"book":"purananuru","num":64,"t":["நல்யாழ்,ஆகுளி, பதலையொடு சுருக்கிச்,","செல்லா மோதில் சில்வளை விறலி!","களிற்றுக் கணம் பொருத கண்ணகன் பறந்தலை,","விசும்புஆடு எருவை புசுந்தடி தடுப்பப்,","பகைப்புலம் மரீஇய தகைப்பெருஞ் சிறப்பின்","குடுமிக் கோமாற் கண்டு,","நெடுநீர்ப் புற்கை நீத்தனம் வரற்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The speaker urges a dancing woman with few bangles to pack her lute and small drums and set out with him to see Kudumi, the king of great honour who stays on in the enemy's land on the battlefield where elephant herds fought and vultures wheel over the fresh flesh, so that they may leave behind their thin watery gruel.","n":"The gift is never named; it is implied by what the travellers will leave behind, their watery gruel. The king is to be found not in his capital but in the enemy's country, so the dancer is sent to a war camp.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","iravu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 64","lb":"PN64","poet":"நெடும்பல்லியத்தனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் பல்யாகசாலை முதுகுடுமிப் பெருவழுதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலியாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["ஆகுளி","ஆகுளி",1,"parai","scene","small drum"],["பதலையொடு","பதலை",1,"parai","scene","one-faced drum"],["களிற்றுக்","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["எருவை","எருவை",4,"pul","scene","vulture"],["புற்கை","புற்கை",7,"unavu","gift_food","thin gruel"]],"en":[["king","குடுமி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:65":{"book":"purananuru","num":65,"t":["மண்முழா மறப்பப், பண் யாழ் மறப்ப","இருங்கண் குழிசி கவிழ்ந்து இழுது பறப்பச்,","சுரும்பூஆர் தேறல் சுற்றம் மறப்ப,","உழவர் ஓதை மறப்ப, விழவும்","அகலுள் ஆங்கண் சீறூர் மறப்ப,","உவவுத் தலைவந்த பெருநாள் அமையத்து,","இருசுடர் தம்முள் நோக்கி, ஒரு சுடர்","புன்கண் மாலை மலைமறைந் தாங்குத்,","தன்போல் வேந்தன் முன்பு குறித்து எறிந்த","புறப்புண் நாணி, மறத்தகை மன்னன்","வாள் வடக்கு இருந்தனன்; ஈங்கு,","நாள்போல் கழியல, ஞாயிற்றுப் பகலே!"],"p":"","r":"The drums and lutes are forgotten, the great pots are overturned and the butter gone, kin forget the toddy, the ploughmen's call and the village festivals are forgotten, because, as at the full moon when sun and moon face each other and one sinks behind the hill in the sad evening, the warrior king, ashamed of a wound in his back from a king his equal, has sat facing north with his sword to die; here the days no longer pass as days.","n":"The full-moon image fits the event closely: two lights of equal rank face each other and one goes down, as two equal kings met and one withdrew. The shame named is of the wound's place (புறப்புண், a wound on the back), not of losing, and the five 'forgets' (மறப்ப) show a whole country stopping its music, food and work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vadakkiruththal","maanam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 65","lb":"PN65","poet":"கழாஅத் தலையார்","pat":"சேரமான் பெருஞ்சேரலாதன்; இவன் கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தானோடு பொருது புறப்புண்பட்டு, வடக்கிருந்தபோது பாடியது","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மண்முழா","முழா",1,"parai","absent",""],["யாழ்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","absent",""],["இழுது","இழுது",2,"unavu","absent","butter"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",3,"unavu","absent","toddy"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",4,"seythi","absent","ploughmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:66":{"book":"purananuru","num":66,"t":["நளியிரு முந்நீர் நாவாய் ஓட்டி,","வளிதொழில் ஆண்ட உரவோன் மருக!","களி இயல் யானைக் கரிகால் வளவ!","சென்று, அமர்க் கடந்த நின் ஆற்றல் தோன்ற","வென்றோய், நின்னினும் நல்லன் அன்றே","கலிகொள் யாணர் வெண்ணிப் பறந்தலை,","மிகப் புகழ் உலகம் எய்திப்,","புறப்புண் நாணி, வடக் கிருந்தோனே!"],"p":"","r":"Addressing Karikal Valavan, descendant of the one who drove ships over the sea by mastering the wind, the poet grants that he went and won and showed his strength, then asks whether the king who, ashamed of a wound on his back at the field of Venni, sat facing north and reached the world of great fame is not a better man than he.","n":"The praise is real for four lines and then turns: the victor is told to his face that the defeated king, by his death, is the better man. The poem does not name him; the colophon of 65 and the line புறப்புண் நாணி, shared with 65, identify him as Cheralathan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vadakkiruththal","maanam","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 66","lb":"PN66","poet":"வெண்ணிக் குயத்தியார்: வெண்ணிற் குயத்தியார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",1,"pira","scene","ship"],["யானைக்","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கரிகால் வளவ",3],["battle","வெண்ணிப் பறந்தலை",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:67":{"book":"purananuru","num":67,"t":["அன்னச் சேவல் ! அன்னச் சேவல் !","ஆடுகொள் வென்றி அடுபோர் அண்ணல்","நாடு தலை அளிக்கும் ஒண்முகம் போலக்,","கோடுகூடு மதியம் முகிழ்நிலா விளங்கும்","மையல் மாலை யாம் கையறுபு இனையக்,","குமரிஅம் பெருந்துறை அயிரை மாந்தி,","வடமலைப் பெயர்குவை ஆயின், இடையது","சோழ நன்னாட்டுப் படினே, கோழி","உயர் நிலை மாடத்துக், குறும்பறை அசைஇ,","வாயில் விடாது கோயில் புக்கு, எம்","பெருங் கோக் கிள்ளி கேட்க, இரும்பிசிர்","ஆந்தை அடியுறை எனினே, மாண்ட நின்","இன்புறு பேடை அணியத், தன்","அன்புறு நன்கலம் நல்குவன் நினக்கே-"],"p":"","r":"In an evening when the moon with joined horns shines like the bright face of the victorious lord who guards the land and the poet is helpless with longing, he asks a swan, if it flies north after eating fish at the ford of Kumari and passes through the Chola country, to alight on the high mansion at Kozhi, enter the palace without waiting at the gate, and say within the hearing of the great king Killi that it serves at the feet of Pisir Aandhai; then the king will give it his own beloved jewels for its mate to wear.","n":"The bird messenger and the helpless evening are akam devices, here turned to a poet's longing for a king. The only credential the swan is to carry is the poet's name, and the poet is sure it will open the palace without a wait at the gate; the poem shows the bond by what it expects the name to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vendhan","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 67","lb":"PN67","poet":"பிசிராந்தையார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அன்னச்","அன்னம்",1,"pul","scene","swan"],["அயிரை","அயிரை",6,"pira","gift_food","small fish"],["பேடை","பேடை",13,"pul","scene","the swan's mate"]],"en":[["hill","வடமலை",7],["town","கோழி",8],["king","கிள்ளி",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:68":{"book":"purananuru","num":68,"t":["உடும்பு உரித்து அன்ன என்பு எழு மருங்கின்","கடும்பின் கடும்பசி களையுநர்க் காணாது,","சில்செவித்து ஆகிய கேள்வி நொந்து நொந்து,","ஈங்குஎவன் செய்தியோ? பாண ! பூண்சுமந்து,","அம் பகட்டு எழிலிய செம் பொறி ஆகத்து","மென்மையின் மகளிர்க்கு வணங்கி,வன்மையின்","ஆடவர்ப் பிணிக்கும் பீடுகெழு நெடுந்தகை,","புனிறு தீர் குழவிக்கு இலிற்றுமுலை போலச்","சுரந்த காவிரி மரங்கொல் மலிநீர்","மன்பதை புரக்கும் நன்னாட்டுப் பொருநன்,","உட்பகை ஒருதிறம் பட்டெனப், புட்பகைக்கு","ஏவான் ஆகலின், சாவோம் யாம் என,","நீங்கா மறவர் வீங்குதோள் புடைப்பத்,","தணிபறை அறையும் அணிகொள் தேர்வழிக்","கடுங்கண் பருகுநர் நடுங்குகை உகத்த","நறுஞ்சேறு ஆடிய வறுந்தலை யானை","நெடுனகர் வரைப்பின் படுமுழா ஓர்க்கும்","உறந்தை யோனே குருசில்;","பிறன்கடை மறப்ப நல்குவன், செலினே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet asks a bard, whose kin have ribs that stand out like a skinned monitor lizard's and who is weary of patrons who barely listen, why he lingers here, and tells him of the lord at Uranthai, gentle to women and binding men by strength, whose Kaveri nourishes the people like a mother's milk to a newborn, where warriors with no war to go to beat their shoulders and the drum sounds, and elephants wallow in mud of spilled toddy; if the bard goes, the lord will give so much that he forgets other men's doors.","n":"Lines 11 and 12 are hard: the warriors seem to complain that, inner enmity having been settled, the king will not send them against foes, so they will die idle; the sense of புட்பகை is not certain. Either way the warriors' frustration is praise of a peaceful land. பிறன்கடை மறப்ப names the bard's real wish: an end to going from door to door.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","iravu","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 68","lb":"PN68","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["உடும்பு","உடும்பு",1,"maa","simile","monitor lizard"],["தணிபறை","பறை",14,"parai","scene",""],["கடுங்கண்","கடுங்கண்",15,"unavu","scene","strong toddy"],["யானை","யானை",16,"maa","scene",""],["படுமுழா","முழா",17,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",9],["town","உறந்தை",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:69":{"book":"purananuru","num":69,"t":["கையது, கடன் நிறை யாழே; மெய்யது,","புரவலர் இன்மையின் பசியே; அரையது","வேற்றிழை நுழைந்த வேர்நனை சிதாஅர்","ஓம்பி உடுத்த உயவற் பாண!","பூட்கை இல்லோன் யாக்கை போலப்","பெரும்புல் என்ற இரும் பேர் ஒக்கலை;","வையகம் முழுவதுடன் வளைஇப், பையென","என்னை வினவுதி ஆயின், மன்னர்","அடுகளிறு உயவும் கொடிகொள் பாசறைக்,","குருதிப் பரப்பின் கோட்டுமா தொலைச்சிப்.","புலாக் களம் செய்த கலாஅத் தானையன்","பிறங்கு நிலை மாடத்து உறந்தை யோனே!","பொருநர்க்கு ஓங்கிய வேலன், ஒரு நிலைப்","பகைப் புலம் படர்தலும் உரியன் ; தகைத் தார்","ஒள்ளெரி புரையும் உருகெழு புசும்பூண்","கிள்ளி வளவற் படர்குவை ஆயின்,","நெடுங் கடை நிற்றலும் இலையே; கடும் பகல்","தேர்வீசு இருக்கை ஆர நோக்கி,","நீ அவற் கண்ட பின்றைப், பூவின்","ஆடுவண்டு இமிராத் தாமரை","சூடாய் ஆதல் அதனினும் இலையே."],"p":"","r":"To a bard with a lute in hand, hunger in his body for want of patrons and a patched rag at his waist, whose large family is wan like the body of a man with no resolve, the poet says: go to Killi Valavan of Uranthai of tall mansions, whose army made the battle camp a field of flesh; you will not even wait at his gate, and once you have seen him in his court where he gives away chariots, you will not fail to wear a golden lotus that no bee hums over.","n":"The opening inventory (கையது, மெய்யது, அரையது: in the hand, in the body, at the waist) makes the bard's poverty a list of possessions, the last of them hunger. The lotus that no bee visits is a gold lotus, the bard's ornament from the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aatruppadai","iravu","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 69","lb":"PN69","poet":"ஆலந்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["யாழே","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["அடுகளிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கோட்டுமா","கோட்டு மா",10,"maa","scene","tusked elephant"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",20,"maram","ornament","golden lotus"],["ஆடுவண்டு","வண்டு",20,"pira","absent","bee"]],"en":[["town","உறந்தை",12],["king","கிள்ளி வளவ",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:70":{"book":"purananuru","num":70,"t":["தேஎம் தீந்தொடைச் சீறியாழ்ப் பாண!","கயத்து வாழ் யாமை காழ்கோத் தன்ன","நுண்கோல் தகைத்த தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை","இனிய காண்க; இவண் தணிக எனக் கூறி;","வினவல் ஆனா முதுவாய் இரவல!","தைத் திங்கள் தண்கயம் போலக்,","கொளக்கொளக் குறைபடாக் கூழுடை வியனகர்,","அடுதீ அல்லது சுடுதீ அறியாது;","இருமருந்து விளைக்கும் நன்னாட்டுப் பொருநன்,","கிள்ளி வளவன் நல்லிசை யுள்ளி,","நாற்ற நாட்டத்து அறுகாற் பறவை","சிறுவெள் ளாம்பல் ஞாங்கர்,ஊதும்","கைவள் ஈகைப் பண்ணன் சிறுகுடிப்","பாதிரி கமழும் ஓதி, ஒண்ணுதல்,","இன்னகை விறலியொடு மென்மெல இயலிச்","செல்வை ஆயின், செல்வை ஆகுவை;","விறகுஒய் மாக்கள் பொன்பெற் றன்னதோர்,","தலைப்பாடு அன்று, அவன் ஈகை;","நினைக்க வேண்டா; வாழ்க, அவன் தாளே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a bard who keeps asking whether his drum will at last be heard with pleasure and his hunger eased that if he goes slowly with his dancer, past the village of Pannan the open-handed where bees hum over the white lily, to Killi Valavan, whose city's food never runs low however much is taken, like a cool pond in the month of Thai, and which knows only the cooking fire and never the fire that burns towns, he will arrive; his giving is no windfall like woodcutters stumbling on gold, so the bard need not doubt it.","n":"அடுதீ அல்லது சுடுதீ அறியாது, only the fire that cooks and never the fire that burns, makes the king's land a land without war. The last comparison is a negated one: the gift is not like a lucky find, because a lucky find cannot be counted on; the poem praises the giver's reliability, not only his wealth. Pannan, another patron, is named in passing on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","naadu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 70","lb":"PN70","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்: (கோவூர் அழகியார் எனவும் பாடம்)","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்ப்","சீறியாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["யாமை","யாமை",2,"pira","simile","tortoise"],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",3,"parai","scene",""],["கூழுடை","கூழ்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பறவை","அறுகால் பறவை",11,"pira","scene","bee"],["ளாம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",12,"maram","scene",""],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",14,"maram","ornament",""],["விறகுஒய்","விறகு ஒய் மாக்கள்",17,"seythi","simile","woodcutters"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி வளவன்",10],["patron","பண்ணன்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:71":{"book":"purananuru","num":71,"t":["மடங்கலின் சினைஇ, மடங்கா உள்ளத்து,","அடங்காத் தானை வேந்தர் உடங்கு இயைந்து","என்னொடு பொருந்தும் என்ப ; அவரை","ஆரமர் அலறத் தாக்கித் தேரொடு","அவர்ப்புறம் காணேன் ஆயின் - சிறந்த","பேரமர் உண்கண் இவளினும் பிரிக:","அறன்நிலை திரிய அன்பின் அவையத்துத்,","திறன்இல் ஒருவனை நாட்டி, முறை திரிந்து","மெலிகோல் செய்தேன் ஆகுக; மலி புகழ்","வையை சூழ்ந்த வலங்கெழு வைப்பின்","பொய்யா யாணர் மையற் கோமான்","மாவனும், மன்எயில் ஆந்தையும், உரைசால்","அந்துவஞ் சாத்தனும், ஆதன் அழிசியும்,","வெஞ்சின இயக்கனும், உளப்படப் பிறரும்,","கண்போல் நண்பிற் கேளிரொடு கலந்த","இன்களி மகிழ்நகை இழுக்கிய யான் ஒன்றோ,","மன்பதை காக்கும் நீள்குடிச் சிறந்த","தென்புலம் காவலின் ஒரிஇப், பிறர்","வன்புலங் காவலின் மாறி யான் பிறக்கே!"],"p":"","r":"Boothappandiyan vows that if he does not strike the kings who, raging like lions, say they have joined to fight him, and see their backs as they flee with their chariots, he may be parted from his wife of wide painted eyes, may become a king who in his assembly put an incapable man in office out of fondness and wielded a weak sceptre against right, and may lose the joy of his friends, the lord of Maiyal, Maavan, Aanthai, Anthuvan Saathan, Aathan Azhisi, Iyakkan and the rest, and be born to guard others' harsh lands instead of the southern land of his line.","n":"The curses form a ranked set of the three things a king holds dear: his wife, his justice and his friends, and then his birthright. The friends are named one by one; the poem keeps a roll of a Pandya court's companions. The lion comparison uses the comparative case ending (மடங்கலின், 'than / like a lion') rather than அன்ன or போல.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","sengol","natpu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 71","lb":"PN71","poet":"ஒல்லையூர் தந்த பூதப்பாண்டியன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மடங்கலின்","மடங்கல்",1,"maa","simile","lion"]],"en":[["river","வையை",10],["town","மையற்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:72":{"book":"purananuru","num":72,"t":["நகுதத் கனரே, நாடு மீக் கூறுநர்;","இளையன் இவன் என உளையக் கூறிப்","படுமணி இரட்டும் பாவடிப் பணைத்தாள்","நெடுநல் யானையும், தேரும், மாவும்,","படைஅமை மறவரும், உடையும் யாம் என்று","உறுதுப்பு அஞ்சாது, உடல்சினம் செருக்கிச்","சிறுசொல் சொல்லிய சினங்கெழு வேந்தரை","அருஞ்சமஞ் சிதையத் தாக்கி, முரசமொடு","ஒருங்கு அகப் படேஎன் ஆயின்; பொருந்திய","என் நிழல் வாழ்நர் சென்னிழல் காணாது,","கொடியன்எம் இறை எனக் கண்ணீர் பரப்பிக்,","குடிபழி தூற்றும் கோலேன் ஆகுக!","ஓங்கிய சிறப்பின் உயர்ந்த கேள்வி","மாங்குடி மருதன் தலைவன் ஆக,","உலகமொடு நிலைஇய பலர்புகழ் சிறப்பின்","புலவர் பாடாது வரைக, என் நிலவரை;","புரப்போர் புன்கண் கூர,","இரப்போர்க்கு ஈயா இன்மை யான் உறவே."],"p":"","r":"Neduncheliyan vows that if he does not shatter and capture with their drums the angry kings who mocked him as a boy and, trusting their elephants, chariots, horses and warriors, spoke small words, then may his people, finding no cool shade under his umbrella, weep and blame him as a cruel king; may the poets led by Maangudi Maruthan cease to sing of his land; and may he know the poverty of having nothing to give suppliants while those he keeps suffer.","n":"What the king stakes is telling: not death or defeat but his people's tears and blame, the silence of poets, and the inability to give. A king's worst fate is to be a cruel sceptre, unsung and ungiving. The mockery 'he is young' (இளையன் இவன்) recurs as the enemies' boast in 78.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","kodungol","ilamai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 72","lb":"PN72","poet":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானையும்","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["மாவும்","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",8,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:73":{"book":"purananuru","num":73,"t":["மெல்ல வந்து, என் நல்லடி பொருந்தி,","ஈயென இரக்குவர் ஆயின், சீருடை","முரசுகெழு தாயத்து அரசோ தஞ்சம்;","இன்னுயிர்ஆயினும்கொடுக்குவென், இந்நிலத்து","ஆற்றல் உடையோர் ஆற்றல் போற்றாது, என்","உள்ளம் எள்ளிய மடவோன், தெள்ளிதின்","துஞ்சு புலி இடறிய சிதடன் போல,","உய்ந்தனன் பெயர்தலோ அரிதே; மைந்துடைக்","கழைதின் யானைக் கால் அகப் பட்ட","வன்றிணி நீண்முளை போலச், சென்று அவண்","வருந்தப் பொரேஎன் ஆயின், பொருந்திய","தீது இல் நெஞ்சத்துக் காதல் கொள்ளாப்","பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் மகளிர்","ஒல்லா முயக்கிடைக் குழைக, என் தாரே!"],"p":"","r":"Nalankilli says that if men come gently, touch his feet and ask, the kingdom he inherited with its drums is a small thing to give and he would give even his life; but the fool who ignored the strength of the strong and scorned him will hardly escape alive, like a blind man who has stumbled on a sleeping tiger; and if he does not crush them like a hard bamboo shoot caught under the foot of a cane-eating elephant, may his garland wither in the reluctant embrace of women who feel no love.","n":"The vow sets giving and fighting as two answers to the same kingdom: asked humbly, the king gives it away, even with his life; taken by scorn, he defends it. The curse he accepts is the embrace of women who do not love him, a shame of pleasure rather than of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","eegai","maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 73","lb":"PN73","poet":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி; 'நல்லுருத்திரன் பாட்டு' எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வஞ்சினக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",7,"maa","simile","tiger"],["கழைதின்","கழை",9,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["யானைக்","யானை",9,"maa","simile",""],["நீண்முளை","முளை",10,"maram","simile","bamboo shoot"],["தாரே","தார்",14,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:74":{"book":"purananuru","num":74,"t":["குழவி இறப்பினும், ஊன்தடி பிறப்பினும்,","ஆள் அன்று என்று வாளின் தப்பார்","தொடர்ப்படு ஞமலியின் இடர்ப்படுத்து இரீஇய","கேளல் கேளிர் வேளாண் சிறுபதம்,","மதுகை இன்றி, வயிற்றுத் தீத் தணியத்,","தாம் இரந்து உண்ணும் அளவை","ஈன்ம ரோ, இவ் உலகத் தானே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet-king asks whether a warrior line that will not let even a child who dies, or a lump of flesh born dead, go without the sword's cut, saying it did not die a man's death, would ever bear a son like him, who, held in trouble like a chained dog, without strength, begs to quench the fire in his belly a little water given in charity by men who are no kin.","n":"The poem leaves its subject implicit and puts it as a question: the speaker is the one who begs, and the question says such a lineage could not have borne him. The colophon here gives no circumstance; the chain and the begging for a drink are in the poem, but who holds him is not said. The sword-cut given to the dead child shows a code in which even death must be a warrior's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 74","lb":"PN74","poet":"சேரமான் கணைக்கா லிரும்பொறை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுமொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஞமலியின்","ஞமலி",3,"maa","simile","dog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:75":{"book":"purananuru","num":75,"t":["மூத்தோர் மூத்தோர்க் கூற்றம் உய்த்தெனப்,","பால்தர வந்த பழவிறல் தாயம்","எய்தினம் ஆயின், எய்தினம் சிறப்பு என","குடிபுரவு இரக்கும் கூரில் ஆண்மைச்","சிறியோன் பெறின்அது சிறந்தன்று மன்னே!","மண்டுஅமர்ப் பரிக்கும் மதனுடை நோன்தாள்","விழுமியோன் பெறுகுவன் ஆயின், தாழ்நீர்","அறுகய மருங்கின் சிறுகோல் வெண்கிடை","என்றூழ் வாடுவறல் போல, நன்றும்","நொய்தால் அம்ம தானே-மையற்று,","விசும்புஉற ஓங்கிய வெண்குடை","முரசுகெழு வேந்தர் அரசுகெழு திருவே,"],"p":"","r":"When the elders have gone to death and the ancient heritage of rule comes by right, a petty man of blunt manhood who thinks 'we have gained greatness' and begs dues from his people will think it a great thing, but if a worthy man whose strong legs bear the brunt of battle gets it, the fortune of kings with drums and sky-high white umbrella is as light as the dry stick of the white kidai plant withered by summer beside a dried-up pond.","n":"The petty heir's mark is that he 'begs' from his own people (குடிபுரவு இரக்கும்): taxing is named as asking. The worthy man's lightness about the throne is not renunciation; it comes from his strength in war, the நோன்தாள் that bears battle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 75","lb":"PN75","poet":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வெண்கிடை","கிடை",8,"maram","simile","kidai reed"],["முரசு","முரசு",12,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:76":{"book":"purananuru","num":76,"t":["ஒருவனை ஒருவன் அடுதலும், தொலைதலும்,","புதுவது அன்று; இவ் உலகத்து இயற்கை;","இன்றின் ஊங்கோ கேளலம்; திரளரை","மன்ற வேம்பின் மாச்சினை ஒண்தளிர்","நெடுங்கொடி உழிஞைப் பவரொடு மிடைந்து,","செறியத் தொடுத்த தேம்பாய் கண்ணி,","ஒலியல் மாலையொடு, பொலியச் சூடிப்,","பாடின் தெண்கிணை கறங்கக், காண்தக,","நாடுகெழு திருவிற் பசும்பூட் செழியன்","பீடும் செம்மலும் அறியார் கூடிப்,","பொருதும் என்று தன்தலை வந்த","புனைகழல் எழுவர் நல்வலம் அடங்க,","ஒருதான் ஆகிப் பொருது, களத்து அடலே!"],"p":"","r":"Killing one another and being killed is nothing new; it is the way of this world; but never till today had we heard of this: seven kings with ornate anklets, not knowing the might and greatness of Cheliyan, who wears a honey-dripping garland of neem shoots twined with uzhinai and goes to the sound of the clear kinai drum, came together saying 'we will fight', and he alone fought and destroyed their strength on the field.","n":"The opening concedes that killing is commonplace so that the novelty of one against seven stands out: the wonder the poem names is of the new (இன்றின் ஊங்கோ கேளலம்). The neem is the Pandya's emblem and the uzhinai the flower of siege.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 76","lb":"PN76","poet":"இடைக்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",4,"maram","ornament","neem"],["உழிஞைப்","உழிஞை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["மாலையொடு","மாலை",7,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தெண்கிணை","கிணை",8,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:77":{"book":"purananuru","num":77,"t":["கிண்கிணி களைந்த கால் ஓண் கழல்தொட்டுக்,","குடுமி களைந்த நுதல்வேம்பின் ஒண்தளிர்","நெடுங்கொடி உழிஞைப் பவரொடு மிலைந்து,","குறுந்தொடி கழித்தகைச் சாபம் பற்றி,","நெடுந்தேர்க் கொடிஞ்சி பொலிய நின்றோன்","யார்கொல்? வாழ்க, அவன் கண்ணி! தார்பூண்டு,","தாலி களைந்தன்றும் இலனே; பால்விட்டு","அயினியும் இன்று அயின்றனனே; வயின்வயின்","உடன்றுமேல் வந்த வம்ப மள்ளரை","வியந்தன்றும், இழிந்தன்றும், இலனே; அவரை","அழுந்தபற்றி, அகல்விசும்பு ஆர்ப்புஎழக்","கவிழ்ந்து நிலம் சேர அட்டதை","மகிழ்ந்தன்றும்,இகழ்ந்தன்றும்,அதனினும் இலனே."],"p":"","r":"Who is this who stands gracing the front of his tall chariot, the warrior's anklet on legs that have only just shed a child's tinkling anklets, neem shoots and uzhinai on a brow just shorn of its tuft, a bow in a hand just stripped of small bangles? Long live his garland! He has put on the garland but not yet taken off the child's amulet; weaned from milk, he ate rice only today; he neither marvelled at nor looked down on the fresh warriors who came at him from every side, and when he seized them and killed them so they fell to the ground he neither rejoiced nor scorned them.","n":"Each line pairs a child's thing just removed with a warrior's thing just put on, so the boy's age is told entirely through objects. The praise ends on his evenness of mind: no wonder at the enemy, no contempt, no gloating after the kill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilamai","maram","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 77","lb":"PN77","poet":"இடைக்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ் செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","சிறுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","ornament","neem"],["உழிஞைப்","உழிஞை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பால்விட்டு","பால்",7,"unavu","absent","milk"],["அயினியும்","அயினி",8,"unavu","gift_food","rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:78":{"book":"purananuru","num":78,"t":["வணங்கு தொடைப் பொலிந்த வலிகெழுநோன்தாள்,","அணங்குஅருங் கடுந்திறல் என்ஐ முணங்கு நிமிர்ந்து,","அளைச்செறி உழுவை இரைக்குவந் தன்ன","மலைப்பரும் அகலம் மதியார், சிலைத்தெழுந்து,","விழுமியம், பெரியம், யாமே; நம்மிற்","பொருநனும் இளையன்; கொண்டியும் பெரிது என,","எள்ளி வந்த வம்ப மள்ளர்","புல்லென் கண்ணர்; புறத்திற் பெயர,","ஈண்டுஅவர் அடுதலும் ஒல்லான்; ஆண்டுஅவர்","மாண்இழை மகளிர் நாணினர் கழியத்,","தந்தை தம்மூர் ஆங்கண்,","தெண்கிணை கறங்கச்சென்று, ஆண்டு அட்டனனே."],"p":"","r":"The new warriors who came scorning my lord, whose broad chest rises to battle like a tiger leaving its den for prey, saying 'we are great and many, the fighter is young and the plunder large', went back with dull eyes and turned their backs; not content to kill them here, he followed them to their fathers' town and, to the sound of the clear drum, killed them there, so that their jewelled women passed away in shame.","n":"The enemies' boast (பொருநனும் இளையன், 'the fighter is young too') answers to the taunt in 72; here it is reported from the other side and punished. Pursuing them to their fathers' town makes the defeat a shame for their families, not only for themselves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","ilamai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 78","lb":"PN78","poet":"இடைக்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["உழுவை","உழுவை",3,"maa","simile","tiger"],["தெண்கிணை","கிணை",12,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:79":{"book":"purananuru","num":79,"t":["மூதூர் வாயில் பனிக் கயம் மண்ணி,","மன்ற வேம்பின் ஒண்குழை மிலைந்து,","தெண்கிணை முன்னர்க் களிற்றின் இயலி,","வெம்போர்ச் செழியனும் வந்தனன்; எதிர்ந்த","வம்ப மள்ளரோ பலரே;","எஞ்சுவர் கொல்லோ, பகல்தவச் சிறிதே?"],"p":"","r":"Having bathed in the cool pond at the gate of the old town and put on bright neem leaves from the common tree, walking like an elephant before the clear kinai drum, Cheliyan of the fierce war has come; the new warriors facing him are many; will any of them be left, when the day is far too short?","n":"The question at the end is a boast put as worry: the day is too short for him to kill so many, not too short for them to beat him. The ritual bath and the neem from the common tree (மன்ற வேம்பு) show the king's going to war as a public rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 79","lb":"PN79","poet":"இடைக்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","ornament","neem"],["தெண்கிணை","கிணை",3,"parai","scene",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[["king","செழியனும்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:80":{"book":"purananuru","num":80,"t":["இன்கடுங் கள்ளின் ஆமூர் ஆங்கண்,","மைந்துடைமல்லன் மதவலி முருக்கி,","ஒருகால் மார்பொதுங் கின்றே; ஒருகால்","வருதார் தாங்கிப் பின்னெதுங் கின்றே;","நல்கினும் நல்கான் ஆயினும், வெல்போர்ப்","போர் அருந் தித்தன் காண்கதில் அம்ம-","பசித்துப் பணைமுயலும் யானை போல,","இருதலை ஒசிய எற்றிக்,","களம்புகும் மல்லன் கடந்துஅடு நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"At Amur of sweet strong toddy the wrestler crushed the might of a mighty wrestler, pressing one leg on his chest and with the other bracing against his rush; let Tittan, whether he looks kindly on him or not, see how the wrestler who enters the field conquers and kills, bending his foe at both ends like a hungry elephant trying to break a bamboo.","n":"The poem wants a particular witness: Tittan, 'whether he grants or not' (நல்கினும் நல்கான் ஆயினும்), so the hero's standing with Tittan is in question and the fight is offered as proof. The poem does not say what Tittan is to the hero; the colophon names the hero as Perunarkilli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 80","lb":"PN80","poet":"சாத்தந்தையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"எருமை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["எருமை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",1,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["பணைமுயலும்","பணை",7,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["town","ஆமூர்",1],["king","தித்தன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:4"},"pn:81":{"book":"purananuru","num":81,"t":["ஆர்ப்பு எழு கடலினும் பெரிது; அவன் களிறே","கார்ப்பெயல் உருமின் முழங்கல் ஆனாவே;","யார்கொல் அளியர் தாமே ஆர்; நார்ச்","செறியத் தொடுத்த கண்ணிக்","கவிகை மள்ளன் கைப்பட் டோரே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet says Killi's battle cry is louder than the roaring sea and his elephants trumpet without pause like the thunder of the monsoon, and asks who could be more pitiable than those who fall into the hands of this open-handed warrior with his tightly strung aathi garland.","n":"The poem never shows a fight: it measures the king entirely by sound (sea, thunder) and then turns, in a single question, to the victims, so praise is made out of the dread it implies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 81","lb":"PN81","poet":"சாத்தந்தையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["களிறே","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["உருமின்","உரும்",2,"pira","simile","thunder"],["ஆர்","ஆர்",3,"maram","ornament","aathi flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:82":{"book":"purananuru","num":82,"t":["சாறுதலைக் கொண்டெனப், பெண்ணீற்றுற்றெனப்","பட்ட மாரி ஞான்ற ஞாயிற்றுக்,","கட்டில் நிணக்கும் இழிசினன் கையது","போழ்தூண்டு ஊசியின் விரைந்தன்று மாதோ;","ஊர்கொள வந்த பொருநனொடு,","ஆர்புனை தெரியல் நெடுந்தகை போரே!"],"p":"","r":"Killi's fight with the champion who came to take his town was over quicker than the needle in the hand of a leather-worker stitching a cot when the festival has begun, his wife is in labour and the rain has come down as the sun sets.","n":"The simile stacks three pressures on one low-status craftsman (the festival trade, the birth, the failing light and rain), so the king's speed is measured by a working man's desperate haste, a rare reach of praise into ordinary life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 82","lb":"PN82","poet":"சாத்தந்தையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மாரி","மாரி",2,"pira","simile","rain"],["இழிசினன்","இழிசினன்",3,"seythi","simile","leather-worker"],["ஆர்புனை","ஆர்",6,"maram","ornament","aathi flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:83":{"book":"purananuru","num":83,"t":["அடிபுனை தொடுகழல், மையணல் காளைக்குஎன்","தொடிகழித் திடுதல்யான் யாய்அஞ் சுவலே;","அடுதோள் முயங்கல் அவைநா ணுவலே;","என்போற் பெருவிதுப் புறுக; என்றும்","ஒருபால் படாஅது ஆகி","இருபாற் பட்ட இம் மையல் ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"A woman in love with Killi says she fears her mother if her bangles slip for this young bull of a man with ankleted feet and dark beard, and is ashamed before the assembly to embrace his conquering shoulders; she wishes that this bewildered town, split two ways and never on one side, may tremble with longing as she does.","n":"Her own split (between fear of her mother and shame before others on one side, desire on the other) is set against a town split into two sides; 85 suggests the town is divided as the crowd at a contest, so her curse wishes her inner division onto the spectators. The exact sense of இருபாற் பட்ட is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 83","lb":"PN83","poet":"பெருங்கோழி நாய்கண் மகள் நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"mixed","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"கைக்கிளை","ctu":"பழிச்சுதல்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,null,"பழிச்சுதல்"]],"pt":["பழிச்சுதல்"],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["தொடுகழல்","கழல்",1,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["தொடிகழித்","தொடி",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:84":{"book":"purananuru","num":84,"t":["என்ஜ, புற்கை யுண்டும் பெருந்தோ ளன்னே;","யாமே, புறஞ்சிறை இருந்தும் பொன்னன் னம்மே","போரெதிர்ந்து என்னை போர்க்களம் புகினே,","கல்லென் பேரூர் விழவுடை ஆங்கண்,","ஏமுற்றுக் கழிந்த மள்ளர்க்கு","உமணர் வெரூஉம் துறையன் னன்னே!"],"p":"","r":"She says her lord has great shoulders though he eats coarse gruel, while she, kept outside his enclosure, has turned the colour of gold; when he goes out to fight, he is to the swaggering warriors of the noisy festive town like a ford the salt-carriers dread.","n":"Two concessions run in parallel (he eats gruel yet is broad-shouldered; I am shut out yet golden), and பொன் here is the gold of பசலை, the pallor of longing, not wealth. Line 1's என்ஜ is a corrupt spelling of என்னை, my lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","pasalai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 84","lb":"PN84","poet":"பெருங்கோழி நாய்கன் மகள் நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"mixed","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"கைக்கிளை","ctu":"பழிச்சுதல்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"பழிச்சுதல்"]],"pt":["பழிச்சுதல்"],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புற்கை","புற்கை",1,"unavu","gift_food","gruel"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",6,"seythi","simile","salt-carriers"],["துறையன்","துறை",6,"pira","simile","ford"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:85":{"book":"purananuru","num":85,"t":["என்னைக்கு ஊர் இஃது அன்மை யானும்,","என்னைக்கு நாடு இஃது அன்மை யானும்,","ஆடுஆடு என்ப, ஒருசா ரோரே;","ஆடன்று என்ப, ஒருசா ரோரே;","நல்ல,பல்லோர் இருநன் மொழியே;","அஞ்சிலம்பு ஒலிப்ப ஓடி, எம்இல்,","முழா அரைப் போந்தை பொருந்தி நின்று,","யான்கண் டனன் அவன் ஆடா குதலே."],"p":"","r":"Because this is not her lord's own town or country, one side of the crowd says he is winning and the other says he is not; she finds both words good, for she ran with her anklets ringing, stood against the palmyra by her house, and saw for herself that he won.","n":"The poem does not say what the contest is; ஆடு is read as victory. A stranger's contest splits the crowd, and the woman, who has no claim on him, settles the dispute only for herself by looking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 85","lb":"PN85","poet":"பெருங்கோழி நாய்கன் மகள் நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"சோழன் போர்வைக்கோப் பெருநற்கிள்ளி","ap":"mixed","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"கைக்கிளை","ctu":"பழிச்சுதல்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"பழிச்சுதல்"]],"pt":["பழிச்சுதல்"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["அஞ்சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",6,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["போந்தை","போந்தை",7,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:86":{"book":"purananuru","num":86,"t":["சிற்றில் நற்றூண் பற்றி, நின்மகன்","யாண்டு உளனோ? என வினவுதி; என் மகன்","யாண்டு உளன் ஆயினும் அறியேன்; ஓரும்","புலி சேர்ந்து போகிய கல்அளை போல,","ஈன்ற வயிறோ இதுவே;","தோன்றுவன் மாதோ, போர்க்களத் தானே!"],"p":"","r":"Asked, as she holds the fine post of her small house, where her son is, a mother says she does not know where he is, but the womb that bore him is like a rock den a tiger has left: he will show himself on the battlefield.","n":"The empty den carries the whole boast: the womb is a lair, the son a tiger, and his absence from home is the proof of his nature. The small house (சிற்றில்) sets her poverty against her pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","makkal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 86","lb":"PN86","poet":"காவற் பெண்டு காதற்பெண்டு எனவும் பாடம். பாடப்பட்டோன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"ஏறாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஏறாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",4,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:87":{"book":"purananuru","num":87,"t":["களம்புகல் ஓம்புமின், தெவ்விர்! போர் எதிர்ந்து,","எம்முளும் உளன்ஒரு பொருநன்; வைகல்","எண் தேர் செய்யும் தச்சன்","திங்கள் வலித்த கால்அன் னோனே."],"p":"","r":"Enemies, beware of entering the battlefield: among us is a warrior like the wheel that a carpenter, who makes eight chariots a day, spent a month making.","n":"The vehicle is about care, not size: a craftsman who turns out eight chariots a day gave a whole month to this one wheel, so the warrior is the most thoroughly made thing in the army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 87","lb":"PN87","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமானஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["தச்சன்","தச்சன்",3,"seythi","simile","carpenter"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:88":{"book":"purananuru","num":88,"t":["யாவிர் ஆயினும், கூழை தார்கொண்டு","யாம்பொருதும் என்றல் ஓம்புமின் ஓங்குதிறல்;","ஒளிறுஇலங்கு நெடுவேல் மழவர் பெருமகன்,","கதிர்விடு நுண்பூண் அம்பகட்டு மார்பின்","விழவு மேம்பட்ட நல் போர்","முழவுத்தோள் என்னையைக் காணா ஊங்கே."],"p":"","r":"Whoever you are, mighty ones, do not boast that you will fight us with your rear and van until you have seen my lord, the great chief of the Mazhavar with the gleaming long spear, his broad chest bright with fine ornaments and his drum-like shoulders, a fighter who stands above the festival.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 88","lb":"PN88","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமானஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நுண்பூண்","பூண்",4,"pira","ornament","ornament"]],"en":[["patron","மழவர் பெருமகன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:89":{"book":"purananuru","num":89,"t":["இழை யணிப் பொலிந்த ஏந்துகோட் டல்குல்,","மடவரல், உண்கண், வாள்நதல், விறலி!","பொருநரும் உளரோ, நும் அகன்றலை நாட்டு? என,","வினவல் ஆனாப் பொருபடை வேந்தே!","எறிகோல் அஞ்சா அரவின் அன்ன","சிறுவன் மள்ளரும் உளரே; அதாஅன்று","பொதுவில் தூங்கும் விசியுறு தண்ணுமை","வளி பொரு தெண்கண் கேட்பின்,","அது போர்! என்னும் என்னையும் உளனே!"],"p":"","r":"To a warlike king who keeps asking whether there are fighters in her broad land, the dancer answers that there are young warriors like snakes that do not fear the striking stick, and besides, her lord, who on hearing the wind strike the taut drum hanging in the common hall says 'that is war!'.","n":"The lord's eagerness is shown by a mistake: he takes the wind on a drum for a call to battle. The dancer's answer turns the king's taunt into a threat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 89","lb":"PN89","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["விறலி","விறலி",2,"seythi","scene","dancer"],["அரவின்","அரவு",5,"pira","simile","snake"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",7,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:90":{"book":"purananuru","num":90,"t":["உடைவளை கடுப்ப மலர்ந்த காந்தள்","அடைமல்கு குளவியொடு கமழும் சாரல்","மறப்புலி உடலின், மான்கணம் உளவோ?","மருளின விசும்பின் மாதிரத்து ஈண்டிய","இருளும் உண்டோ, ஞாயிறு சினவின்?","அச்சொடு தாக்கிப் பாருற்று இயங்கிய","பண்டச் சாகாட்டு ஆழ்ச்சி சொல்லிய,","விரிமணல் ஞெமரக், கல்பக, நடக்கும்","பெருமிதப் பகட்டுக்குத் துறையும் உண்டோ?","எழுமரம் கடுக்கும் தாள்தோய் தடக்கை","வழுவில் வன்கை, மழவர் பெரும!","இருநில மண் கொண்டு சிலைக்கும்","பொருநரும் உளரோ, நீ களம் புகினே?"],"p":"","r":"Can deer herds stand where a fierce tiger rages on the slope scented with kaandhal like broken bangles and wild jasmine? Can darkness stay when the sun grows angry? Is there a ford the proud bullock cannot cross as it drags a laden cart over sand and stone? So, lord of the Mazhavar with arms like a gate-bar, are there foes who can stand once you enter the field?","n":"Three rhetorical questions from three orders (beast, sky, labour) all expect 'no', and only the fourth names the king, so the answer is already given before he appears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 90","lb":"PN90","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["உடைவளை","வளை",1,"pira","simile","conch bangle"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",2,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["மறப்புலி","புலி",3,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மான்கணம்","மான்",3,"maa","simile","deer"],["பகட்டுக்கு","பகடு",9,"maa","simile","bullock"]],"en":[["patron","மழவர் பெரும",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:91":{"book":"purananuru","num":91,"t":["வலம்படு வாய்வாள் ஏந்தி, ஒன்னார்","களம்படக் கடந்த கழல்தொடி தடக்கை,","ஆர்கலி நறவின், அதியர் கோமான்","போர்அடு திருவின் பொலந்தார் அஞ்சி!","பால் புரை பிறைநுதல் பொலிந்த சென்னி","நீல மணிமிடற்று ஒருவன் போல","மன்னுக, பெரும! நீயே தொன்னிலைப்","பெருமலை விடரகத்து அருமிசை கொண்ட","சிறியிலை நெல்லித் தீங்கனி குறியாது,","ஆதல் நின்னகத்து அடக்கிச்,","சாதல் நீங்க, எமக்கு ஈத்தனையே."],"p":"","r":"Avvai blesses Anji, lord of the Athiyar, with his true sword, anklet and golden garland, that he may last like the blue-throated one whose head shines with the milk-white crescent, because he gave her the sweet fruit of the small-leaved nelli taken from a hard crag of an ancient mountain, without telling her what it would do, so that she might escape death.","n":"The gift is in what he withheld: by keeping the fruit's virtue to himself (ஆதல் நின்னகத்து அடக்கி) he made sure she would eat it, and so gave up long life for a poet. The blessing matches the gift: life for life, and from the god who survived the poison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","uyir","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 91","lb":"PN91","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நறவின்","நறவு",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["ஒருவன்","ஒருவன்",6,"deyvam","simile","Shiva"],["நெல்லித்","நெல்லி",9,"maram","gift_food","gooseberry"]],"en":[["patron","அஞ்சி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:92":{"book":"purananuru","num":92,"t":["யாழொடும் கொள்ளா; பொழுதொடும் புணரா;","பொருள்அறி வாரா; ஆயினும், தந்தையர்க்கு","அருள்வந் தனவால், புதல்வர்தம் மழலை;","என்வாய்ச் சொல்லும் அன்ன; ஒன்னார்","கடி மதில் அரண்பல கடந்து","நெடுமான் அஞ்சி! நீ அருளல் மாறே."],"p":"","r":"A child's prattle does not keep to the lute, does not suit the hour and carries no sense, yet it moves fathers to tenderness; Avvai says her own words are like that, since Neduman Anji, who crossed many guarded walls of his enemies, receives them with grace.","n":"Modesty and praise are one move: by calling her poem prattle she makes the patron a father, so his favour is love, not judgement of merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","makkal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 92","lb":"PN92","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன் மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[["யாழொடும்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene","lute"]],"en":[["patron","நெடுமான் அஞ்சி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:93":{"book":"purananuru","num":93,"t":["திண் பிணி முரசம் இழுமென முழங்கச்","சென்று அமர் கடத்தல் யாவது? வந்தோர்","தார்தாங் குதலும் ஆற்றார், வெடிபட்டு,","ஓடல் மரீஇய பீடுஇல் மன்னர்","நோய்ப்பால் விளிந்த யாக்கை தழீஇக்,","காதல் மறந்து, அவர் தீதுமருங் கறுமார்","அறம்புரி கொள்கை நான்மறை முதல்வர்","திறம்புரி பசும்புல் பரப்பினர் கிடப்பி,","‘மறம் கந்து ஆக நல்லமர் வீழ்ந்த","நீழ் கழல் மறவர் செல்வுழிச் செல்க! என","வாள்போழ்ந்து அடக்கலும் உய்ந்தனர் மாதோ;","வரிஞிமிறு ஆர்க்கும் வாய்புகு கடாஅத்து","அண்ணல் யானை அடுகளத் தொழிய,","அருஞ்சமம் ததைய நூறி, நீ","பெருந் தகை! விழுப்புண் பட்ட மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Avvai asks what is left of going to war with drums booming, since the kings who could not even meet the van and ran would have died of sickness and been laid by the four-Veda priests on darbha grass and cut with a sword, so as to go where heroes go; they escaped that rite because they fell on your field, great one, where you crushed the elephants and took a noble wound.","n":"The poem records a rite: a body that died of disease was laid on darbha grass and cut with a sword so that it could go where those who died in battle go. A death without a wound needed a wound. The turn on உய்ந்தனர் (they escaped) is ironic: being killed by Anji saved them from that disgrace. The syntax of lines 2 to 11 is compressed and the reading is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 93","lb":"PN93","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசு",1,"parai","scene","war drum"],["பசும்புல்","புல்",8,"maram","scene","darbha grass"],["ஞிமிறு","ஞிமிறு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:94":{"book":"purananuru","num":94,"t":["ஊர்க்குறு மாக்கள் வெண்கோடு கழாஅலின்,","நீர்த்துறை படியும் பெருங்களிறு போல","இனியை, பெரும ! எமக்கே ; மற்றதன்","துன்னருங் கடாஅம் போல","இன்னாய், பெரும ! நின் ஒன்னா தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"To us you are gentle, great one, like the big bull elephant that lies in the ford while the village boys wash its white tusks; to your enemies you are harsh, like its musth that no one can come near.","n":"One elephant gives both halves: the same animal is a children's plaything and a terror, so the king's two faces are one nature, not a mask.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","maram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 94","lb":"PN94","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","simile","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:95":{"book":"purananuru","num":95,"t":["இவ்வே, பீலி அணிந்து, மாலை சூட்டிக்","கண்திரள் நோன்காழ் திருத்தி, நெய் அணிந்து,","கடியுடை வியன்நக ரவ்வே : அவ்வே,","பகைவர்க் குத்திக், கோடுநுதி சிதைந்து,","கொல்துறைக் குற்றில மாதோ ; என்றும்","உண் டாயின் பதம் கொடுத்து,","இல் லாயின் உடன் உண்ணும்,","இல்லோர் ஒக்கல் தலைவன்,","அண்ணல்எம் கோமான், வைந் நுதி வேலே."],"p":"","r":"These spears, decked with peacock feathers and garlands, their thick shafts trimmed and oiled, sit in a guarded mansion; those, their points and edges broken from stabbing enemies, lie in the smith's small shed: they are the sharp spears of our lord, head of the kin of the poor, who gives food when he has it and shares the meal when he has none.","n":"The praise of the other armoury is the insult: spears kept beautiful have never been used. The broken spears and the shared hunger are one portrait: what Anji has is always in use, by his army or his dependants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 95","lb":"PN95","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாண் மங்கலம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாண் மங்கலம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பீலி","பீலி",1,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["மாலை","மாலை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:96":{"book":"purananuru","num":96,"t":["அலர்பூந் தும்பை அம்பகட்டு மார்பின்,","திரண்டுநீடு தடக்கை, என்னை இளையோற்கு","இரண்டு எழுந் தனவால், பகையே; ஒன்றே,","பூப்போல் உண்கண் பசந்து, தோள் நுணுகி,","நோக்கிய மகளிர்ப் பிணித்தன்று; ஒன்றே,","‘விழவு இன்று ஆயினும், படு பதம் பிழை யாது,","மைஊன் மொசித்த ஒக்கலொடு, துறை நீர்க்","கைமான் கொள்ளு மோ?’ என","உறையுள் முனியும், அவன் செல்லும் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"Two enmities have risen against my young lord with the thumbai on his broad chest and his long, thick arms: one has seized the women who looked at him, whose flower-like eyes grow pale and shoulders thin; the other is the town he goes to, which resents his lodging, asking whether, though there is no festival, his mutton-fed retinue will miss no meal and his elephants will take the water of the ford.","n":"The 'enmities' are a joke of praise: one is the love-sickness he causes, the other the dread of feeding his following, so beauty and power are both counted as trouble he brings. The hosts' complaint in lines 6 to 8 is compressed and its sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pasalai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 96","lb":"PN96","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் மகன் பொகுட்டெழினி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன் மொழி"],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தும்பை","தும்பை",1,"maram","ornament","thumbai flower"],["பூப்போல்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"],["மைஊன்","ஊன்",7,"unavu","gift_food","mutton"],["கைமான்","கைமான்",8,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:97":{"book":"purananuru","num":97,"t":["போர்க்கு உரைஇப் புகன்று கழித்த வாள்,","உடன்றவர் காப்புடை மதில் அழித்தலின்,","ஊனுற மூழ்கி, உருவிழந் தனவே;","வேலே, குறும்படைந்த அரண் கடந்தவர்","நறுங் கள்ளின் நாடு நைத்தலின்,","சுரை தழீஇய இருங் காழொடு","மடை கலங்கி நிலைதிரிந் தனவே;","களிறே, எழூஉத் தாங்கிய கதவம் மலைத்து, அவர்","குழூஉக் களிற்றுக் குறும்பு உடைத்தலின்,","பரூஉப் பிணிய தொடிகழிந் தனவே;","மாவே, பரந்தொருங்கு மலைந்த மறவர்","பொலம் பைந்தார் கெடப் பரிதலின்,","களன் உழந்து அசைஇய மறுக்குளம் பினவே;","அவன் தானும், நிலம் திரைக்கும் கடல் தானைப்","பொலந் தும்பைக் கழல் பாண்டில்","கணை பொருத துளைத்தோ லன்னே;","ஆயிடை, உடன்றோர் உய்தல் யாவது?’ தடந்தாள்,","பிணிக் கதிர், நெல்லின் செம்மல் மூதூர்","நுமக்குஉரித்து ஆகல் வேண்டின், சென்றவற்கு","இறுக்கல் வேண்டும் திறையே; மறிப்பின்,","ஒல்வான் அல்லன், வெல்போ ரான்’ எனச்","சொல்லவும் தேறீர் ஆயின், மெல்லியல்,","கழற் கனி வகுத்த துணைச் சில் ஓதிக்","குறுந்தொடி மகளிர் தோள்விடல்","இறும்பூது அன்று; அஃது அறிந்துஆ டுமினே."],"p":"","r":"Anji's swords lost their shape plunged in flesh as he broke his foes' guarded walls, his spears are loose in their sockets from crushing the toddy-rich lands of fort-holders, his elephants have shed their thick bands battering gates, his horses' hooves are bloodied from trampling warriors' gold garlands, and he himself is like a shield pierced by arrows; so, if you want your old town of rich paddy, pay him tribute, for he will not give way, and if you will not believe it, it will be no wonder if your soft-natured women lose their husbands' shoulders.","n":"Each weapon is praised by its damage, ending with the king as a pierced shield; the threat is made through the enemy's women, whose loss is the last item of the catalogue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 97","lb":"PN97","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன் மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",5,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["களிறே","களிறு",8,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["மாவே","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"],["தும்பைக்","தும்பை",15,"maram","ornament","thumbai flower"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",18,"maram","scene","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:98":{"book":"purananuru","num":98,"t":["முனைத் தெவ்வர் முரண் அவியப்","பொர்க் குறுகிய நுதி மருப்பின் நின்","இனக் களிறு செலக் கண்டவர்","மதிற் கதவம் எழுச் செல்லவும்,","பிணன் அழுங்கக் களன் உழக்கிச்","செலவு அசைஇய மறுக் குளம்பின் நின்","இன நன்மாச் செயக் கண்டவர்","கவை முள்ளின் புழை யடைப்பவும்,","மார்புறச் சேர்ந்து ஒல்காத்","தோல் செறிப்பில் நின்வேல் கண்டவர்","தோள் கழியொடு பிடி செறிப்பவும்,","வாள் வாய்த்த வடுப் பரந்த நின்","மற மைந்தர் மைந்து கண்டவர்","புண்படு குருதி அம்பு ஒடுக்கவும்","நீயே, ஐயவி புகைப்பவும் தாங்காது, ஒய்யென,","உறுமுறை மரபின் புறம் நின்று உய்க்கும்","சுற்றத்து அனையை; ஆகலின்,போற்றார்","இரங்க விளிவது கொல்லோ; வரம்பு அணைந்து","இறங்குகதிர் அலம்வரு கழனிப்,","பெரும்புனல் படப்பை, அவர் அகன்றலை நாடே;"],"p":"","r":"Seeing your elephants with battle-blunted tusks, your foes bar their fort gates; seeing your horses with bloodied hooves, they block the gaps with forked thorns; seeing your sheathed spear, they tighten their grip on their shields; seeing your scarred warriors, they bind up their arrow wounds; you are like the band that stands outside by old custom to carry off the wounded, undeterred even by the smoke of white mustard: so must their wide land, its fields where the bending ears touch the ridges and its great waters, perish while they grieve?","n":"Each sight of Anji's army is answered by a defensive act, and the fourth turns to the wounded; the simile then makes Anji the death that waits outside the sickroom, against which the mustard smoke of the healing rite does nothing. The poem ends in pity for the enemy's rich land, the matter of கொற்றவள்ளை. The identity of the band in lines 15 to 17 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 98","lb":"PN98","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வாகை; வஞ்சியும்","ctu":"அரச வாகை; கொற்றவள்ளையுமாம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை","கொற்ற வள்ளை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["நன்மாச்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["முள்ளின்","முள்",8,"maram","scene","thorn"],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",15,"maram","scene","white mustard"],["கதிர்","கதிர்",19,"maram","scene","ears of grain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:99":{"book":"purananuru","num":99,"t":["அமரர்ப் பேணியும், ஆவுதி அருத்தியும்,","அரும்பெறல் மரபின் கரும்பு இவண் தந்தும்,","நீர்அக இருக்கை ஆழி சூட்டிய","தொன்னிலை மரபின் நின் முன்னோர் போல,","ஈகைஅம் கழற்கால் இரும்பனம் புடையல்,","பூவார் காவின், புனிற்றுப் புலால் நெடுவேல்,","எழுபொறி நாட்டத்து எழாஅத் தாயம்","வழுவின்று எய்தியும் அமையாய், செருவேட்டு,","இமிழ்குரல் முரசின் எழுவரொடு முரணிச்","சென்று, அமர் கடந்து, நின் ஆற்றல் தோற்றிய","அன்றும், பாடுநர்க்கு அரியை; இன்றும்","பரணன் பாடினன் மற்கொல், மற்று நீ","முரண் மிகு கோவலூர் நூறி, நின்","அரண் அடு திகிரி ஏந்திய தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"Like your forebears of the ancient line, who honoured the gods, fed the sacrificial fire, brought here the sugarcane that is hard to obtain and ruled the sea-girt land with the wheel of command, you, with your giving anklets, palmyra garland, flowered groves and freshly bloodied spear, won without fault an undivided inheritance, yet were not content, but fought the seven kings with booming drums; even then you were beyond the singers, and now only Paranar could sing your shoulder that crushed proud Kovalur and bore the fort-breaking wheel.","n":"The poem names another poet: Avvai places her patron beyond ordinary singers and hands the Kovalur victory to Paranar, which shows poets marking a deed as fit for a particular voice. The sense of எழுபொறி நாட்டத்து எழாஅத் தாயம் (line 7) and of பரணன் பாடினன் மற்கொல் (line 12) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 99","lb":"PN99","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",2,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["இரும்பனம்","பனை",5,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["காவின்","கா",6,"maram","scene","grove"],["முரசின்","முரசு",9,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[["town","கோவலூர்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:100":{"book":"purananuru","num":100,"t":["கையது வேலே; காலன புனைகழல்;","மெய்யது வியரே; மிடற்றது பசும்புண்;","வட்கர் போகிய வளரிளம் போந்தை","உச்சிக் கொண்ட ஊசி வெண்தோட்டு,","வெட்சி மாமலர், வேங்கையொடு விரைஇச்,","சுரி இரும் பித்தை பொலியச் சூடி,","வரி வயம் பொருத வயக்களிறு போல,","இன்னும் மாறாது சினனே; அன்னோ!","உய்ந்தனர் அல்லர், இவண் உடற்றி யோரே;","செறுவர் நோக்கிய கண், தன்","சிறுவனை நோக்கியுஞ், சிவப்பு ஆனாவே."],"p":"","r":"Spear in hand, anklets on his feet, sweat on his body and a fresh wound on his throat, wearing in his curly hair the white leaf from the top of a young palmyra with vetchi and vengai flowers, he is like a mighty elephant that has fought a striped tiger; his rage has not yet cooled, those who fought him here did not escape, and the eyes that glared at his enemies stay red even as he looks at his own small son.","n":"The last image tests the anger against the tenderest sight and the anger holds: the poem praises the hero and lets the cost show in the same line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 100","lb":"PN100","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["vekuli","கொலை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["போந்தை","போந்தை",3,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",5,"maram","ornament","vetchi flower"],["வேங்கையொடு","வேங்கை",5,"maram","ornament","vengai flower"],["வயம்","வயம்",7,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","simile","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:5"},"pn:101":{"book":"purananuru","num":101,"t":["ஒருநாள் செல்லலம்; இருநாட் செல்லலம்;","பன்னாள் பயின்று, பலரொடு செல்லினும்","தலைநாள் பொன்ற விருப்பினன் மாதோ;","அணிபூண் அணிந்த யானை இயல்தேர்","அதியமான்; பரிசில் பெறூஉங் காலம்","நீட்டினும், நீட்டா தாயினும், யானைதன்’","கோட்டிடை வைத்த கவளம் போலக்","கையகத் தது அது; பொய்யா காதே;","அருந்தே மாந்த நெஞ்சம்!","வருந்த வேண்டா; வாழ்க, அவன் தாளே!"],"p":"","r":"Avvai tells her own heart that Athiyaman greets a bard on the hundredth visit, even with a crowd, as eagerly as on the first, so whether the gift comes late or soon it is as sure as the food-ball an elephant holds between its tusks, and the heart need not fret.","n":"The colophon files it as a demand for the gift (பரிசில் கடா நிலை), but the poem turns the demand inward: the one being urged to wait is the poet's own hungry heart. The food-ball is in the elephant's own keeping and already between its tusks, so the image says the gift is decided and only the moment is open. Line 9 (அருந்தே மாந்த) can be divided more than one way; the sense of a heart greedy for its meal is the likeliest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 101","lb":"PN101","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["யானைதன்","யானை",6,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கவளம்","கவளம்",7,"unavu","simile","food-ball"]],"en":[["patron","அதியமான்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:102":{"book":"purananuru","num":102,"t":["‘எருதே இளைய; நுகம் உண ராவே;","சகடம் பண்டம் பெரிதுபெய் தன்றே;","அவல் இழியினும், மிசை ஏறினும்,","அவணது அறியுநர் யார்?’ என, உமணர்","கீழ்மரத்து யாத்த சேமஅச்சு அன்ன,","இசை விளங்கு கவிகை நெடியோய்! திங்கள்","நாள்நிறை மதியத்து அனையை; இருள்","யாவண தோ, நின் நிழல்வாழ் வோர்க்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The prince is compared to the spare axle that salt-carters tie under a heavily loaded cart drawn by young, unbroken bulls, for the climbs and drops of the road, and to the full moon, so that no darkness can come to those who live in his shade.","n":"The first image is of a prince who is not yet needed: a spare axle does nothing until the young bulls stumble and the main axle breaks, so the praise is that of a son held in reserve during his father's reign. The poem names no one; the patron, Athiyaman's son Pokuttezhini, comes from the colophon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 102","lb":"PN102","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சியின் மகன் பொகுட்டெழினி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["எருதே","எருது",1,"maa","simile","bullock"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",4,"seythi","simile","salt-carters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:103":{"book":"purananuru","num":103,"t":["ஒருதலைப் பதலை தூங்க, ஒருதலைத்","தூம்புஅகச் சிறுமுழாத் தூங்கத் தூக்கிக்,","‘கவிழ்ந்த மண்டை மலர்க்குநர் யார்?’ எனச்","சுரன்முதல் இருந்த சில்வளை விறலி!","செல்வை யாயின், சேணோன் அல்லன்;","முனைசுட வெழுந்த மங்குல் மாப்புகை","மலைசூழ் மஞ்சின், மழ களிறு அணியும்","பகைப்புலத் தோனே, பல் வேல் அஞ்சி;","பொழுது இடைப் படாஅப் புலரா மண்டை","மெழுகுமெல் அடையிற் கொழுநிணம் பெருப்ப,","வறத்தற் காலை யாயினும்,","புரத்தல் வல்லன்; வாழ்க, அவன் தாளே!"],"p":"","r":"Avvai tells a viraliyar sitting at the start of the wasteland road, with a one-headed drum at one end of her pole and a small hollow drum at the other, asking who will turn up her upturned bowl, that Anji is not far: he is in enemy country among his young elephants and the smoke of burning villages, and even in a dry season he can keep her bowl full of soft fat-rich food.","n":"The patron is found not at home but at the front, so the bard is sent toward war smoke; the poem makes his generosity and his raids one fact, since the fat that fills her bowl comes from the enemy's land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 103","lb":"PN103","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலியாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பதலை","பதலை",1,"parai","scene","one-headed drum"],["சிறுமுழாத்","முழா",2,"parai","scene",""],["விறலி","விறலி",4,"seythi","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["கொழுநிணம்","நிணம்",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["அடையிற்","அடை",10,"unavu","gift_food","cake"]],"en":[["patron","அஞ்சி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:104":{"book":"purananuru","num":104,"t":["போற்றுமின், மறவீர் ! சாற்றுதும், நும்மை;","ஊர்க்குறு மாக்கள் ஆடக் கலங்கும்","தாள்படு சின்னீர் களிறு அட்டு வீழ்க்கும்","ஈர்ப்புடைக் கராஅத்து அன்ன என்ஐ","நுண்பல் கருமம் நினையாது,","‘இளையன்’ என்று இகழின், பெறல் அரிது, ஆடே."],"p":"","r":"Avvai warns the enemy warriors: if you look down on my lord as a youth, not weighing his many subtle deeds, you will not win, for he is like the crocodile that drags down and kills an elephant in a foot-deep pool where village boys play.","n":"The water is made as small as possible (foot-deep, a children's pool), so the crocodile's strength owes nothing to its ground: the image answers both the scorn of youth and the scorn of a small domain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","maram","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 104","lb":"PN104","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரசவாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரசவாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile",""],["கராஅத்து","கரா",4,"maa","simile","crocodile"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:105":{"book":"purananuru","num":105,"t":["சேயிழை பெறுகுவை, வாள் நுதல் விறலி!","தடவுவாய்க் கலித்த மாஇதழ்க் குவளை","வண்டுபடு புதுமலர்த் தண் சிதர் கலாவப்","பெய்யினும், பெய்யா தாயினும், அருவி","கொள்ளுழு வியன்புலத்து உழைகால் ஆட,","மால்புஉடை நெடுவரைக் கோடுதொறு இழிதரும்","நீரினும் இனிய சாயல்","பாரி வேள்பால் பாடினை செலினே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar tells a bright-browed viraliyar that she will win fine jewels if she goes singing to Pari, whose gentleness is sweeter than the water that, rain or no rain, comes down every peak of his ladder-hung hill, carrying the cool petals of bee-haunted lilies over the wide horse-gram fields.","n":"The water flows whether it rains or not, which already says what the poem will say of the giver; the comparison is of degree (sweeter than), so Pari's manner outdoes the one thing in his land that never fails. Line 5 (உழைகால் ஆட) is hard to construe; the run of the water over the fields is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 105","lb":"PN105","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலியாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கொள்ளுழு","கொள்",5,"unavu","scene","horse-gram"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:106":{"book":"purananuru","num":106,"t":["நல்லவும் தீயவும் அல்ல குவி இணர்ப்","புல்லிலை எருக்கம் ஆயினும், உடையவை","கடவுள் பேணேம் என்னா; ஆங்கு","மடவர் மெல்லியர் செல்லினும்,","கடவன், பாரி கை வண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"As the gods do not refuse even the erukkam, with its clustered blossoms and poor leaves, neither good nor bad, when people offer it, so Pari's open hand holds itself bound to give even when the foolish and the weak come to him.","n":"The comparison reverses the usual direction: the giver is set in the place of the god who receives offerings, and the asker in the place of the poor flower, so to be given to by Pari is to be accepted by him. கடவன் makes the gift a debt he owes, not a favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iraivan","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 106","lb":"PN106","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["எருக்கம்","எருக்கு",2,"maram","scene","erukkam flower"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",3,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:107":{"book":"purananuru","num":107,"t":["‘பாரி பாரி’ என்றுபல ஏத்தி,","ஒருவர்ப் புகழ்வர், செந்நாப் புலவர்:","பாரி ஒருவனும் அல்லன்;","மாரியும் உண்டு, ஈண்டு உலகுபுரப் பதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The fine-tongued poets praise one man, calling out 'Pari, Pari'; but Pari is not the only one: there is also the rain, which sustains this world.","n":"It looks like a rebuke of the poets for flattering one man, and the rebuke is the praise: the only rival that can be found for him is the rain. The 'one' (ஒருவர், ஒருவனும்) is repeated so that the denial of uniqueness ends by granting it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","mazhai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 107","lb":"PN107","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மாரியும்","மாரி",4,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:108":{"book":"purananuru","num":108,"t":["குறத்தி மாட்டிய வறற்கடைக் கொள்ளி","ஆரம் ஆதலின், அம் புகை அயலது","சாரல் வேங்கைப் பூஞ்சினைத் தவழும்","பறம்பு பாடினர் அதுவே! அறம்பூண்டு,","பாரியும், பரிசிலர் இரப்பின்,","‘வாரேன்’ என்னான், அவர் வரை யன்னே."],"p":"","r":"Such is the Parambu the poets sing of: the dry-end firebrand a hill woman set burning is sandalwood, so its fragrant smoke drifts across to the flowering branches of the vengai on the slope; and Pari, having taken up virtue, would not say 'I will not come' if suppliants asked for him, but would be theirs.","n":"In this land sandalwood is common firewood and its scent goes to the neighbouring tree unasked; the poem sets that scene beside the giver without saying they are alike, and leaves the reader to make the match. The last line goes past giving goods: asked for himself, he would go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 108","lb":"PN108","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["குறத்தி","குறத்தி",1,"seythi","scene","hill woman"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",2,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","பறம்பு",4],["patron","பாரியும்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:109":{"book":"purananuru","num":109,"t":["அளிதோ தானே, பாரியது பறம்பே!","நளி கொள் முரசின் மூவிரும் முற்றினும்,","உழவர் உழாதன நான்கு பயன் உடைத்தே","ஒன்றே, சிறியிலை வெதிரின் நெல்விளை யும்மே;","இரண்டே, தீஞ்சுளைப் பலவின் பழம்ஊழ்க் கும்மே;","மூன்றே, கொழுங்கொடி வள்ளிக் கிழங்கு வீழ்க்கும்மே;","நான்கே, அணிநிற ஒரி பாய்தலின், மீது அழிந்து,","திணி நெடுங் குன்றம் தேன்சொரி யும்மே.","வான் கண் அற்று, அதன் மலையே; வானத்து","மீன் கண் அற்று, அதன் சுனையே; ஆங்கு,","மரந்தொறும் பிணித்த களிற்றினிர் ஆயினும்,","புலந்தொறும் பரப்பிய தேரினிர் ஆயினும்,","தாளின் கொள்ளலிர்; வாளின் தாரலன்;","யான்அறி குவென், அது கொள்ளும் ஆறே;","சுகிர்புரி நரம்பின் சீறியாழ் பண்ணி,","விரையொலி கூந்தல் நும் விறலியர் பின் வர,","ஆடினிர் பாடினிர் செலினே,","நாடும் குன்றும் ஒருங்குஈ யும்மே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar tells the three crowned kings besieging Parambu that it is to be pitied: drums and all, they cannot starve it, for it has four yields no ploughman sows (bamboo rice, jackfruit, valli tubers and honey), its hill is like the sky and its springs like the stars; tie elephants to every tree, spread chariots over every field, they will not take it by effort and he will not yield it to the sword; but if they tune small lutes and come singing and dancing with their viralis, he will give land and hill together.","n":"The poem ends a siege argument with a guide-poem: the one road into the fort is the bard's road, so the kings are mocked by being offered the only means they cannot use without ceasing to be kings. The colophon's thurai speaks of refusing a daughter, which the poem itself never mentions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","eegai","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 109","lb":"PN109","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"நொச்சி","ctu":"மகண் மறுத்தல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகள் மறுத்தல்"],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene",""],["வெதிரின்","வெதிர்",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நெல்விளை","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene",""],["வள்ளிக்","வள்ளி",6,"maram","scene",""],["கிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",6,"unavu","scene",""],["தேன்சொரி","தேன்",8,"unavu","scene","honey"],["களிற்றினிர்","களிறு",11,"maa","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்","யாழ்",15,"yaazh","scene",""],["விறலியர்","விறலி",16,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பாரியது",1],["hill","பறம்பே",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:110":{"book":"purananuru","num":110,"t":["கடந்து அடு தானை மூவிரும் கூடி","உடன்றனிர் ஆயினும், பறம்பு கொள்ற்கு அரிதே;","முந்நூறு ஊர்த்தே தண்பறம்பு நல்நாடு;","முந்நூறு ஊரும் பரிசிலர் பெற்றனர்;","யாமும் பாரியும் உளமே;","குன்றும் உண்டு; நீர் பாடினிர் செலினே."],"p":"","r":"Even if you three kings join your conquering armies in anger, Parambu is hard to take; its cool good country has three hundred villages and the suppliants have already received all three hundred; there remain only Pari and I, and the hill: come singing and they are yours.","n":"The arithmetic is the point: nothing is left to conquer because everything has already been given, so the kings are told they arrive after the bards. Kapilar counts himself with Pari among what remains (யாமும் பாரியும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 110","lb":"PN110","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"நொச்சி","ctu":"மகள் மறுத்தல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகள் மறுத்தல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[],"en":[["hill","பறம்பு",2],["patron","பாரியும்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:111":{"book":"purananuru","num":111,"t":["அளிதோ தானே, பேரிருங் குன்றே!","வேலின் வேறல் வேந்தர்க்கோ அரிதே;","நீலத்து, இணை மலர் புரையும் உண்கண்","கிணை மகட்கு எளிதால், பாடினள் வரினே."],"p":"","r":"The great dark hill is to be pitied: to win it with the spear is hard even for kings, but it is easy for a kinai-drummer's daughter whose kohl-lined eyes are like a pair of blue lilies, if she comes singing.","n":"அளிதோ (pitiable) is said of the hill, which can be had so cheaply by the humblest singer and is so costly to kings; the pity and the boast are the same word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 111","lb":"PN111","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வேள் பாரி","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"நொச்சி","ctu":"மகள் மறுத்தல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகள் மறுத்தல்"],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நீலத்து","நீலம்",3,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["கிணை","கிணை",4,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:112":{"book":"purananuru","num":112,"t":["அற்றைத் திங்கள் அவ் வெண் நிலவில்,","எந்தையும் உடையேம்; எம் குன்றும் பிறர் கொளார்;","இற்றைத் திங்கள் இவ் வெண் நிலவில்,","வென்று எறி முரசின் வேந்தர் எம்","குன்றும் கொண்டார்; யாம் எந்தையும் இலமே!"],"p":"","r":"Pari's daughters say: that month, in that white moonlight, we had our father and no one had taken our hill; this month, in this white moonlight, kings with victorious drums have taken our hill, and we have no father.","n":"The two halves use the same words in the same order, so the only change is the loss itself; திங்கள் is both the month and the moon, and the unchanging moonlight measures what has changed. The father is named first in the first half and last in the second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 112","lb":"PN112","poet":"பாரி மகளிர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறு நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:113":{"book":"purananuru","num":113,"t":["மட்டு வாய் திறப்பவும், மை விடை வீழ்ப்பவும்,","அட்டு ஆன்று ஆனாக் கொழுந் துவை ஊன் சோறும்","பெட்டாங்கு ஈயும் பெருவளம் பழுனி,","நட்டனை மன்னோ, முன்னே; இனியே,","பாரி மாய்ந்தெனக், கலங்கிக் கையற்று,","நீர் வார் கண்ணேம் தொழுது நிற் பழிச்சிச்","சேறும் - வாழியோ பெரும்பெயர்ப் பறம்பே!","கோல் திரள் முன்கைக் குறுந் தொடி மகளிர்","நாறு இருங் கூந்தற் கிழவரைப் படர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar says to Parambu: once you were our friend, opening toddy jars, felling dark rams and giving rice with rich meat relish as much as anyone wished; now Pari is dead, and bewildered and helpless, with streaming eyes, we worship and praise you and go, seeking husbands for the girls with small bangles and fragrant dark hair.","n":"The hill, not the dead man, is addressed and called a friend (நட்டனை), so the farewell is to the place that fed the bards; the last two lines give the reason for leaving, the duty to the daughters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 113","lb":"PN113","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மட்டு","மட்டு",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["விடை","விடை",1,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["ஊன்","ஊன்",2,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["தொடி","தொடி",8,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[["hill","பறம்பே",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:114":{"book":"purananuru","num":114,"t":["ஈண்டு நின் றோர்க்கும் தோன்றும்; சிறு வரை","சென்று நின் றோர்க்கும் தோன்றும், மன்ற;","களிறு மென்று இட்ட கவளம் போல,","நறவுப் பிழிந் திட்ட கோதுடைச் சிதறல்","வார் அசும்பு ஒழுகு முன்றில்,","தேர் வீசு இருக்கை, நெடியோன் குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"It can be seen from here and from the small hill beyond, surely: the hill of the tall one, where the courtyard runs wet with the dregs of pressed toddy, strewn like the food-balls an elephant chews and drops, and where chariots were given away from where he sat.","n":"The hill is still visible from near and far, and that is the grief: the place stands while the giving is over. Only the colophon makes this a lament; the lines themselves are a picture of a feasting court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","eegai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 114","lb":"PN114","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile",""],["கவளம்","கவளம்",3,"unavu","simile","food-ball"],["நறவுப்","நறவு",4,"unavu","scene","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:115":{"book":"purananuru","num":115,"t":["ஒரு சார் அருவி ஆர்ப்ப, ஒரு சார்","பாணர் மண்டை நிறையப் பெய்ம்மார்,","வாக்க உக்க தேக் கள் தேறல்","கல்அலைத்து ஒழுகும் மன்னே! பல் வேல்,","அண்ணல் யானை, வேந்தர்க்கு","இன்னான் ஆகிய இனியோன் குன்றே!"],"p":"","r":"On one side the waterfall roared, and on the other the clear honey-toddy, poured to fill the bards' bowls and spilled, ran washing over the stones: so it was once on the hill of the gentle one who was harsh to kings with many spears and great elephants.","n":"மன்னே marks the whole scene as past; the toddy for bards is set as a second waterfall. The closing phrase, harsh to kings and sweet (இன்னான் ஆகிய இனியோன்), joins the two faces Pari showed to two kinds of visitor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 115","lb":"PN115","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","scene","bards"],["கள்","கள்",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:116":{"book":"purananuru","num":116,"t":["தீநீர்ப் பெருங் குண்டு சுனைப் பூத்த குவளைக்","கூம்பவிழ் முழுநெறி புரள்வரும் அல்குல்,","ஏந்தெழில் மழைக் கண், இன் நகை, மகளிர்","புன் மூசு கவலைய முள் முடை வேலிப்,","பஞ்சி முன்றில், சிற்றில் ஆங்கண்,","பீரை நாறிய சுரை இவர் மருங்கின்,","ஈத்திலைக் குப்பை ஏறி உமணர்","உப்பு ஓய் ஒழுகை எண்ணுப மாதோ;","நோகோ யானே; தேய்கமா காலை!","பயில் பூஞ் சோலை மயில் எழுந்து ஆலவும்,","பயில் இருஞ் சிலம்பிற் கலை பாய்ந்து உகளவும்,","கலையுங் கொள்ளா வாகப்,பலவும்","காலம் அன்றியும் மரம் பயம் பகரும்","யாணர் அறாஅ வியன்மலை அற்றே","அண்ணல் நெடுவரை ஏறித், தந்தை","பெரிய நறவின், கூர் வேற் பாரியது","அருமை அறியார் போர் எதிர்ந்து வந்த","வலம் படுதானை வேந்தர்","பொலம் படைக் கலிமா எண்ணு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar grieves that the girls with lily garlands and rain-dark eyes now climb a heap of eecham leaves by a little hut with a thorn fence to count the salt-carters' carts, when once, from the high hill of their father Pari of strong toddy and sharp spear, a hill of unfailing plenty where peacocks danced, stags leapt and trees bore fruit out of season, they counted the golden-saddled horses of kings who came to war not knowing his strength.","n":"The poem is built on one act, counting, done in two places: then the girls counted enemy horses from a fort that could not be taken, now they count passing salt carts from a hut. Line 14 (வியன்மலை அற்றே) may be 'like the broad hill' or 'the broad hill is gone'; our text also lacks a line the other edition has after line 9, so the middle of the poem is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 116","lb":"PN116","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குவளைக்","குவளை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["பஞ்சி","பஞ்சி",5,"maram","scene","cotton"],["பீரை","பீர்க்கு",6,"maram","scene",""],["சுரை","சுரை",6,"maram","scene","gourd"],["ஈத்திலைக்","ஈந்து",7,"maram","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",7,"seythi","scene","salt-carters"],["உப்பு","உப்பு",8,"pira","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",10,"pul","scene",""],["கலை","கலை",11,"maa","scene","stag"],["மரம்","மரம்",13,"maram","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",16,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["கலிமா","மா",19,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","பாரியது",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:117":{"book":"purananuru","num":117,"t":["மைம் மீன் புகையினும், தூமம் தோன்றினும்,","தென் திசை மருங்கின் வெள்ளி ஓடினும்,","வயல்அகம் நிறையப், புதற்பூ மலர,","மனைத்தலை மகவை ஈன்ற அமர்க்கண்","ஆமா நெடு நிறை நன்புல் ஆரக்,","கோஒல் செம்மையின் சான்றோர் பல்கிப்,","பெயல் பிழைப்பு அறியாப் புன்புலத் ததுவே;","பிள்ளை வெருகின் முள் லெயிறு புரையப்","பாசிலை முல்லை முகைக்கும்","ஆய் தொடி அரிவையர் தந்தை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Even when Saturn smokes, comets appear and Venus runs to the south, the land of the father of the girls with fine bangles was a dry upland where rain never failed: fields filled, bush flowers bloomed, wild cows that had calved by the houses grazed in long herds on good grass, the worthy multiplied under a straight sceptre, and mullai budded like a kitten-civet's sharp teeth.","n":"The three opening signs are omens of drought, and the poem says they had no power over this land because of its ruler's justice, so a lord of a hill is given the crowned king's rain-making sceptre. The lament is only in the past tense and in the phrase 'the father of the girls'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","mazhai","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 117","lb":"PN117","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[["புதற்பூ","புதல் பூ",3,"maram","scene",""],["ஆமா","ஆமா",5,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["நன்புல்","புல்",5,"maram","scene","grass"],["வெருகின்","வெருகு",8,"maa","simile","civet cat"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:118":{"book":"purananuru","num":118,"t":["அறையும் பொறையும் மணந்த தலைய,","எண் நாள் திங்கள் அனைய கொடுங் கரைத்","தெண் ணீர்ச் சிறுகுளம் கீள்வது மாதோ-","கூர் வேல் குவைஇய மொய்ம்பின்","தேர்வண் பாரி தண் பறம்பு நாடே!"],"p":"","r":"The land of cool Parambu, of Pari who gave chariots and whose strong shoulders wielded sharp spears, is now seeing its little clear-water tank, with a curved bank like the eighth-day moon and a head where rocks and ridges meet, breaking.","n":"With no one to keep it, the tank, the land's store of water, gives way; the poem lets the breach of one small tank stand for the end of the whole country. கீள்வது (is breaking, is splitting) is the one word that carries the lament, and its exact sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 118","lb":"PN118","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சிறுகுளம்","குளம்",3,"pira","absent","tank"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",5],["hill","பறம்பு",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:119":{"book":"purananuru","num":119,"t":["கார்ப் பெயல் தலைஇய காண்பு இன் காலைக்","களிற்று முக வரியின் தெறுழ்வீ பூப்பச்,","செம் புற்று ஈயலின் இன்அளைப் புளித்து!","மெந்தினை யாணர்த்து; நந்துங் கொல்லோ;","நிழலில் நீளிடைத் தனிமரம் போலப்,","பணைகெழு வேந்தரை இறந்தும்","இரவலர்க்கு ஈயும் வள்ளியோன் நாடே!"],"p":"","r":"In the pleasant season after the rains, when therul blossoms open like the spots on an elephant's face and the red anthill holes turn sour with winged termites, the land rich in soft millet: will it last? It is the land of the generous one who, like a lone tree on a long shadeless road, gave to suppliants beyond what drum-rich kings would give.","n":"நந்துங் கொல்லோ can be heard as 'will it still flourish?' or 'will it perish?', and the doubt is the lament. The lone tree is valued because nothing else on the road gives shade, so the simile measures Pari against the kings as the tree against the bare road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 119","lb":"PN119","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிற்று","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["தெறுழ்வீ","தெறுழ்",2,"maram","scene",""],["ஈயலின்","ஈயல்",3,"pira","scene","winged termites"],["மெந்தினை","தினை",4,"unavu","scene","millet"],["தனிமரம்","மரம்",5,"maram","simile","tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:120":{"book":"purananuru","num":120,"t":["வெப்புள் விளைந்த வேங்கைச் செஞ் சுவல்","கார்ப் பெயர் கலித்த பெரும் பாட்டு ஈரத்துப்,","பூழி மயங்கப் பல உழுது, வித்திப்","பல்லி ஆடிய பல்கிளைச் செவ்விக்","களை கால் கழாலின், தோடு ஒலிபு நந்தி,","மென் மயிற் புனிற்றுப் பெடை கடுப்ப நீடிக்,","கருந்தாள் போகி, ஒருங்கு பீள் விரிந்து,","கீழும் மேலும் எஞ்சாமைப் பல காய்த்து,","வாலிதின் விளைந்த புது வரகு அரியத்","தினை கொய்யக், கவ்வை கறுப்ப, அவரைக்","கொழுங்கொடி விளர்க் காய் கோட்பதம்ஆக,","நிலம் புதைப் பழுனிய மட்டின் தேறல்","புல் வேய்க் குரம்பைக் குடிதொறும் பகர்ந்து.","நறுநெய்க் கடலை விசைப்பச் சோறு அட்டுப்,","பெருந் தோள் தாலம் பூசல் மேவர,","வருந்தா யாணர்த்து; நந்துங் கொல்லோ:","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் மடந்தையர் தந்தை","ஆடு கழை நரலும் சேட் சிமைப், புலவர்","பாடி யானாப் பண்பிற் பகைவர்","ஓடுகழல் கம்பலை கண்ட","செருவெஞ் சேஎய் பெருவிறல் நாடே!"],"p":"","r":"On the red upland where the vengai grew, after the rains men ploughed again and again, sowed, harrowed and weeded until the millet rose tall like a peahen fresh from laying and bore grain top to bottom; then came the harvest of new varagu and millet, the bean pods ripe, buried toddy offered at every grass hut, and rice cooked with ghee and gram: will this effortless plenty last, the land of the father of the long-haired maidens, the war-hot lord of the high bamboo peaks whose quality poets never tire of singing?","n":"The long account of farm labour ends in the word வருந்தா (without toil), so the plenty is called effortless just after the poem has shown the work; the lament turns on whether the work will still bear fruit without the lord. Several farm words (பல்லி, கவ்வை, பெருந் தோள் தாலம்) are rare and their sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","uzhavu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 120","lb":"PN120","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைச்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["மயிற்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile","peahen"],["வரகு","வரகு",9,"unavu","scene",""],["தினை","தினை",10,"unavu","scene","millet"],["அவரைக்","அவரை",10,"maram","scene","bean"],["மட்டின்","மட்டு",12,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கடலை","கடலை",14,"unavu","gift_food","gram"],["சோறு","சோறு",14,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["கழை","கழை",18,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:6"},"pn:121":{"book":"purananuru","num":121,"t":["ஒரு திசை ஒருவனை உள்ளி, நாற்றிசைப்","பலரும் வருவர், பரிசில் மாக்கள்;","வரிசை அறிதலோ அரிதே; பெரிதும்","ஈதல் எளிதே, மாவண் தோன்றல்;","அது நற்கு அறிந்தனை யாயின்,","பொது நோக்கு ஒழிமதி, புலவர் மாட்டே!"],"p":"","r":"Bards come from all four directions with one man in mind, so giving much is easy but knowing each one's due rank is hard; if the great giver understands this, he should stop looking at all poets with one undiscriminating eye.","n":"Kapilar praises Kari by correcting him: the fault named is not meanness but indiscriminate giving, so generosity is assumed and judgment is what is asked. The poet's own interest is plain, since a poet of standing is the one who loses when all are treated alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 121","lb":"PN121","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண் மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["புலவர்","புலவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:122":{"book":"purananuru","num":122,"t":["கடல் கொளப் படாஅது, உடலுநர் ஊக்கார்,","கழல்புனை திருந்துஅடிக் காரி! நின் நாடே;","அழல் புறம் தரூஉம் அந்தணர் அதுவே;","வீயாத் திருவின் விறல் கெழு தானை","மூவருள் ஒருவன், ‘துப்பா கியர்’ என,","ஏத்தினர் தரூஉங் கூழே, நும்குடி","வாழ்த்தினர் வரூஉம் இரவலர் அதுவே;","வடமீன் புரையுங் கற்பின், மடமொழி,","அரிவை தோள் அளவு அல்லதை,","நினது என இலைநீ பெருமிதத் தையே."],"p":"","r":"Kari's land cannot be taken by the sea and enemies dare not attack it, yet it belongs to the fire-tending brahmins; the tribute the three crowned kings send him to win his help belongs to the bards who praise his clan; nothing is his own but the shoulders of his wife, chaste as the northern star, and still he holds himself proud.","n":"The poem lists Kari's possessions only to give each away, so the praise is an inventory of dispossession. The one thing kept is his wife, set apart by her chastity; the closing word பெருமிதம் makes pride rest on what he has given, not what he holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 122","lb":"PN122","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["இரவலர்","இரவலர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["வடமீன்","வட மீன்",8,"pira","simile","star"]],"en":[["patron","காரி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:123":{"book":"purananuru","num":123,"t":["நாட் கள் உண்டு, நாள்மகிழ் மகிழின்,","யார்க்கும் எளிதே, தேர் ஈதல்லே;","தொலையா நல்லிசை விளங்கு மலயன்","மகிழாது ஈத்த இழையணி நெடுந்தேர்","பயன்கிழு முள்ளூர் மீமிசைப்","பட்ட மாரி உறையினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Anyone who drinks the morning's toddy and grows merry finds it easy to give away chariots, but the jewelled chariots that Malayan of unfading fame gave while sober outnumber the raindrops that fall on the heights of fertile Mullur.","n":"The measure of the gift is not its size but the giver's state: a drunk man's chariot costs him nothing in judgment, a sober man's is chosen. The rain on Mullur, his own hill, makes the gifts as natural to the land as its weather.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 123","lb":"PN123","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கள்","கள்",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மாரி","மாரி",6,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","மலயன்",3],["hill","முள்ளூர்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:124":{"book":"purananuru","num":124,"t":["நாளன்று போடிப், புள்ளிடைத் தட்பப்,","பதனன்று புக்குத், திறனன்று மொழியினும்,","வறிது பெயர்குநர் அல்லர்; நெறி கொளப்","பாடு ஆன்று, இரங்கும் அருவிப்","பீடு கெழு மலையற் பாடி யோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who sing Malayan of the mountain of loud waterfalls never go home empty-handed, even if they set out on an unlucky day, are held back by bad omens of birds, arrive at the wrong hour and speak without skill.","n":"Every rule a suppliant was meant to keep (the right day, good omens, timing, skilful speech) is listed only to be cancelled by the patron's nature, so the poem praises him by making the bard's craft unnecessary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 124","lb":"PN124","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புள்ளிடைத்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","omen bird"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","மலையற்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:125":{"book":"purananuru","num":125,"t":["பருத்திப் பெண்டின் பனுவல் அன்ன,","நெருப்புச் சினந்தணிந்த நிணந்தயங்கு கொழுங்குறை,","பரூஉக் கண் மண்டை யொடு, ஊழ்மாறு பெயர","உண்கும், எந்தை! நிற் காண்குவந் திசினே,","நள் ளாதார் மிடல் சாய்ந்த","வல்லாள ! நின் மகிழிருக் கையே","உழுத நோன் பகடு அழிதின் றாங்கு","நல்லமிழ்து ஆக, நீ நயந்துண்ணும் நறவே;","குன்றத் தன்ன களிறு பெயரக்,","கடந்தட்டு வென்றோனும், நிற் கூறும்மே;","‘வெலீஇயோன் இவன்’ எனக்","‘கழலணிப் பொலிந்த சேவடி நிலங் கவர்பு","விரைந்து வந்து, சமந் தாங்கிய,","வல்வேல் மலையன் அல்லன் ஆயின்","நல்லமர் கடத்தல் எளிதுமன், நமக்கு’ எனத்,","தோற்றோன் தானும், நிற்கூ றும்மே,","‘தொலைஇயோன் அவன்’ என,","ஒருநீ ஆயினை; பெரும! பெரு மழைக்கு","இருக்கை சான்ற உயர் மலைத்","திருத்தகு சேஎய் ! நிற் பெற்றிசி னோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The bard will feast on fat roast meat and drink by turns in Malayan's hall, where the toddy he relishes like nectar is a reward earned as the ploughing ox earns its straw; in the battle between two kings, the victor says Malayan made him win and the loser says that without Malayan victory would have been easy, so the strong hero of the high hill is the one man both sides name.","n":"The praise is built from the testimony of both enemies: Malayan fought as an ally, and his worth is proven by the loser's complaint as much as by the winner's thanks. The ox simile turns the patron's drinking into wages for war-work, not indulgence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 125","lb":"PN125","poet":"வடமவண்ணக்கண் பெருஞ்சாத்தனார்","pat":"தேர்வண் மலையன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பருத்திப்","பருத்தி",1,"maram","simile",""],["கொழுங்குறை","கொழுங் குறை",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பகடு","பகடு",7,"maa","simile",""],["நறவே","நறவு",8,"unavu","gift_food",""],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene",""],["சேஎய்","சேய்",20,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","மலையன்",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:126":{"book":"purananuru","num":126,"t":["ஒன்னார் யானை ஓடைப் பொன் கொண்டு,","பாணர் சென்னி பொலியத் தைஇ,","வாடாத் தாமரை சூட்டிய விழுச்சீர்","ஓடாப் பூட்கை உரவோன் மருக!","வல்லேம் அல்லேம் ஆயினும், வல்லே","நின்வயிற் கிளக்குவம் ஆயின், கங்குல்","துயில்மடிந் தன்ன தூங்கிருள் இறும்பின்,","பறை இசை அருவி, முள்ளூர்ப் பொருநர்","தெறலரு மரபின் நின் கிளையொடும் பொலிய,","நிலமிசைப் பரந்த மக்கட்டு எல்லாம்","புலன் அழுக்கு அற்ற அந்த ணாளன்,","இரந்து சென் மாக்கட்கு இனி இடன் இன்றிப்,","பரந்து இசை நிறகப் பாடினன், அதற்கொண்டு","சினமிகு தானை வானவன் குடகடல்,","பொலந்தரு நாவாய் ஓட்டிய அவ் வழிப்,","பிறகலம் செல்கலாது அனையேம் அத்தை,","இன்மை துரப்ப, அசை தர வந்து, நின்","வண்மையின் தொடுத்தனம், யாமே; முள்ளெயிற்று","அரவுஎறி உருமின் முரசெழுந்து இயம்ப,","அண்ணல் யானையொடு வேந்து களத்து ஒழிய,","அருஞ் சமம் ததையத் தாக்கி, நன்றும்","நண்ணாத் தெவ்வர்த் தாங்கும்","பெண்ணையம் படப்பை நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Descendant of the hero who crowned bards with gold lotuses taken from enemy elephants' brow-plates, we are not skilled singers, yet poverty drove us to you: since Kapilar, the flawless brahmin, sang your fame so fully that no room is left for those who come asking, we are like other boats that cannot sail the sea lane the Chera's gold-laden ships have cut; lord of the land of palmyra groves who holds back hostile kings in battle.","n":"A later poet admits he follows a master: Kapilar's praise has filled the space, and the ship simile makes poetic praise a trade route already taken. The modesty is itself a bid, since the bard binds himself to Kari's generosity rather than to his own skill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","inmai","pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 126","lb":"PN126","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["அருவி","அருவி",8,"pira","scene",""],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",15,"pira","simile",""],["முரசெழுந்து","முரசு",19,"parai","scene",""],["உருமின்","உரும்",19,"pira","simile","thunder"],["பெண்ணையம்","பெண்ணை",23,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[["hill","முள்ளூர்ப்",8],["king","வானவன்",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:127":{"book":"purananuru","num":127,"t":["‘களங் கனி யன்ன கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறி யாழ்ப்","பாடு இன் பனுவல் பாணர் உய்த்தெனக்,","களிறில வாகிய புல்லரை நெடுவெளிற்,","கான மஞ்ஞை கணனுடு சேப்ப,","ஈகை அரிய இழையணி மகளிரொடு","சாயின்று’ என்ப, ஆஅய் கோயில்;","சுவைக்கு இனி தாகிய குய்யுடை அடிசில்","பிறர்க்கு ஈவு இன்றித் தம் வயிறு அருத்தி,","உரைசால் ஓங்குபுகழ் ஒரிஇய","முரைசு கெழு செல்வர் நகர்போ லாதே."],"p":"","r":"They say Ay's palace stands bare, since bards have led away its elephants and the forest peacocks flock to the empty tethering posts, with only its women left wearing the jewel that cannot be given; yet it is not like the cities of drum-owning kings who fill their own bellies with savoury food, give none away and lose their fame.","n":"Poverty here is the proof of wealth: the emptied hall is set against the full but famous-less cities. The one thing not given, the women's jewel, is the marriage token, so the line marks the limit of a king's giving without saying so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 127","lb":"PN127","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"வேள் ஆய் அரண்டின்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைஇநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களங்","களா",1,"maram","simile",""],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["களிறில","களிறு",3,"maa","absent",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",4,"pul","scene",""],["அடிசில்","அடிசில்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முரைசு","முரசு",10,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:128":{"book":"purananuru","num":128,"t":["மன்றப் பலவின் மாச்சினை மந்தி","இரவலர் நாற்றிய விசிகூடு முழவின்","பாடின் தெண்கண், கனி செத்து, அடிப்பின்,","அன்னச் சேவல் மாறு எழுந்து ஆலும்,","கழல் தொடி ஆஅய் மழை தவழ் பொதியில்;","ஆடு மகள் குறுகின் அல்லது,","பீடுகெழு மன்னர் குறுகலோ அரிதே."],"p":"","r":"On Ay's cloud-wrapped Pothiyil, a female monkey on a branch of the jackfruit tree in the common ground beats the taut face of a drum the bards hung there, taking it for fruit, and the swan rises in answer and dances; only a dancing woman may approach that hill, and proud kings can hardly come near it.","n":"The hill is open and closed at once: performers' drums hang in its common ground and a dancer may walk in, but kings cannot. The comic scene of the monkey and the swan shows a place so full of the bards' music that even the animals play it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 128","lb":"PN128","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்து; இயன்மொழியும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்து","இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene",""],["இரவலர்","இரவலர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",2,"parai","scene",""],["அன்னச்","அன்னம்",4,"pul","scene","swan"],["மழை","மழை",5,"pira","scene",""],["ஆடு மகள்","ஆடுமகள்",6,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",5],["hill","பொதியில்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:129":{"book":"purananuru","num":129,"t":["குறியிறைக் குரம்பைக் குறவர் மாக்கள்","வாங்குஅமைப் பழுனிய தேறல் மகிழ்ந்து,","வேங்கை முன்றில் குரவை அயரும்,","தீஞ்சுளைப் பலவின், மாமலைக் கிழவன்;","ஆஅய் அண்டிரன், அடுபோர் அண்ணல்;","இரவலர்க்கு ஈத்த யானையின், கரவின்று,","வானம் மீன்பல பூப்பின், ஆனாது","ஒருவழிக் கருவழி யின்றிப்","பெருவெள் ளென்னிற், பிழையாது மன்னே."],"p":"","r":"Ay Andiran, war-winning lord of the great mountain of sweet jackfruit, where hill folk in low-eaved huts grow merry on toddy matured in bamboo and dance the kuravai in courtyards under the vengai: the elephants he gave to suppliants would match the stars only if the sky bloomed with stars everywhere, leaving no dark patch.","n":"The hyperbole is hedged with a condition: the stars as they are fall short, so the sky must be imagined full. The kurinji scene of drinking and dancing is the patron's land, set out before his name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 129","lb":"PN129","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["வாங்குஅமைப்","அமை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""],["குரவை","குரவை",3,"yaazh","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",4,"maram","scene",""],["யானையின்","யானை",6,"maa","gift_food",""],["மீன்பல","மீன்",7,"pira","scene","star"]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய் அண்டிரன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:130":{"book":"purananuru","num":130,"t":["விளங்குமணிக் கொடும்பூண் ஆஅய்! நின்னாட்டு","இளம்பிடி ஒருசூல் பத்து ஈனும்மோ?","நின்னும் நின் மலையும் பாடி வருநர்க்கு,","இன்முகம் கரவாது, உவந்து நீ அளித்த","அண்ணல் யானை எண்ணின், கொங்கர்க்","குடகடல் ஓட்டிய ஞான்றைத்","தலைப்பெயர்த் திட்ட வேலினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Ay of the bright jewelled ornament, does a young she-elephant in your land bear ten calves at a time? The noble elephants you gave gladly, without a grudging face, to those who come singing you and your mountain outnumber the spears the Kongar threw down when you drove them to the western sea.","n":"The question is a joke that works as praise: so many elephants have been given that the herd must breed beyond nature. The comparison joins his giving to his war, counting gifts against an enemy's discarded spears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 130","lb":"PN130","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["இளம்பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:131":{"book":"purananuru","num":131,"t":["மழைக் கணஞ் சேக்கும் மாமலைக் கிழவன்,","வழைப் பூங் கண்ணி வாய்வாள் அண்டிரன்","குன்றம் பாடின கொல்லோ;","களிறு மிக உடைய இக் கவின் பெறு காடே?"],"p":"","r":"Did this lovely forest, so full of elephants, also sing the hill of Andiran, lord of the great cloud-resting mountain, who wears the vazhai flower and whose sword never fails?","n":"The wit assumes the reader knows that singing Ay's hill earns elephants; the forest's wild herds are read as a bard's payment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 131","lb":"PN131","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மழைக்","மழை",1,"pira","scene",""],["வழைப்","வழை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","அண்டிரன்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:132":{"book":"purananuru","num":132,"t":["முன்னுள்ளு வோனைப் பின்னுள்ளி னேனே!","ஆழ்க, என் உள்ளம்! போழ்க என் நாவே!","பாழ் ஊர்க் கிணற்றின் தூர்க, என் செவியே!","நரந்தை நறும்புல் மேய்ந்த கவரி","குவளைப் பைஞ்சுனை பருகி, அயல","தகரத் தண்ணிழல் பிணையொடு வதியும்","வடதிசை யதுவே வான்தோய் இமையம்,","தென்திசை ஆஅய் குடி இன்றாயின்,","பிறழ்வது மன்னோ இம் மலர்தலை உலகே."],"p":"","r":"I thought of Ay only after others had thought of him first: may my heart sink, my tongue split and my ears silt up like a well in a deserted town; the Himalaya stands in the north, where the yak that grazed on fragrant naranthai grass drinks at the kuvalai pool and rests with its mate in the cool shade of the tagaram, and if Ay's clan did not stand in the south, this wide world would tip over.","n":"The self-curse is the poet's apology for coming late to praise. The world-balance image sets a patron's family against the Himalaya as its counterweight, and the yak's ease in the north is drawn so fully that the south's single name has to outweigh a whole scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 132","lb":"PN132","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"],["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["கிணற்றின்","கிணறு",3,"pira","simile",""],["நரந்தை","நரந்தை",4,"maram","scene",""],["கவரி","கவரி",4,"maa","scene","yak"],["குவளைப்","குவளை",5,"maram","scene",""],["தகரத்","தகரம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["பிணையொடு","பிணை",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","இமையம்",7],["patron","ஆஅய்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:133":{"book":"purananuru","num":133,"t":["மெல்லியல் விறலி! நீ நல்லிசை செவியிற்","கேட்பின் அல்லது, காண்பறி யலையே;","காண்டல் சால வேண்டினை யாயின்- மாண்ட நின்","விரை வளர் கூந்தல் வரைவளி உளரக்,","கலவ மஞ்ஞையின் காண்வர இயலி,","மாரி யன்ன வண்மைத்","தேர்வேள் ஆயைக் காணிய சென்மே!"],"p":"","r":"Gentle dancer, you have only heard of his fame and never seen him; if you want to see him, go, your fragrant hair tossed by the mountain wind, moving lovely as a plumed peacock, to see Ay of the chariots, whose giving is like the rain.","n":"The whole guide is a contrast of hearing and seeing: fame reaches the ear, but the gift is had only by going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 133","lb":"PN133","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"விறலியாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["விறலியாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["விறலி","விறலி",1,"seythi","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞையின்","மஞ்ஞை",5,"pul","simile",""],["மாரி","மாரி",6,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆயை",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:134":{"book":"purananuru","num":134,"t":["‘இம்மைச் செய்தது மறுமைக்கு ஆம்’ எனும்","அறவிலை வணிகன் ஆ அய் அல்லன்;","பிறரும் சான்றோர் சென்ற நெறியென,","ஆங்குப் பட்டன்று அவன் கைவண் மையே."],"p":"","r":"Ay is not a trader who buys virtue, saying that what is done in this life will pay in the next; his generous hand simply took the road that the worthy before him had travelled.","n":"The poem names and rejects a motive for giving that was evidently current: giving as an investment in the next birth. What it puts in its place is not inner kindness but custom, the path the worthy have walked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","marumai","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 134","lb":"PN134","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[],"en":[["patron","ஆ அய்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:135":{"book":"purananuru","num":135,"t":["கொடுவரி வழங்கும் கோடுயர் நெடுவரை,","அருளிடர்ச் சிறுநெறி ஏறலின், வருந்தித்,","தடவரல் கொண்ட தகைமெல் ஒதுக்கின்,","வளைக்கை விறலியென் பின்னள் ஆகப்,","பொன்வார்ந் தன்ன புரிஅடங்கு நரம்பின்","வரிநவில் பனுவல் புலம்பெயர்ந்து இசைப்பப்,","படுமலை நின்ற பயங்கெழு சீறியாழ்","ஒல்கல் உள்ளமொடு ஒருபுடைத் தழீஇப்,","புகழ்சால் சிறப்பின்நின் நல்லிசை உள்ளி","வந்தெனன் எந்தை! யானே: யென்றும்","மன்றுபடு பரிசிலர்க் காணின், கன்றொடு","கறையடி யானை இரியல் போக்கும்","மலைகெழு நாடன்! மாவேள் ஆஅய்!","களிறும் அன்றே; மாவும் அன்றே;","ஒளிறுபடைப் புரவிய தேரும் அன்றே;","பாணர், படுநர்,பரிசிலர், ஆங்கவர்,","தமதெனத் தொடுக்குவர் ஆயின், எமதெனப்","பற்றல் தேற்றாப் பயங்கெழு தாயமொடு,","அன்ன வாக, நின் ஊழி; நின்னைக்","காண்டல் வேண்டிய அளவை; வேண்டார்","உறுமுரண் கடந்த ஆற்றல்","பொதுமீக் கூற்றத்து நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"I climbed the narrow, painful path of the high tiger-haunted mountain, my bangled dancer following wearily, my small lute with strings like drawn gold held to my side, and came thinking only of your fame, lord of the mountain land where the elephant flees with her calf when she sees bards in the common ground: I want no elephants, horses or chariots; let bards and singers take those as their own, and may your days go on with the wealth you never call yours; I came only to see you.","n":"The elephant that runs from bards is a joke about how freely Ay gives them away. By refusing the usual gifts the poet asks for the patron's presence, a bid that flatters by claiming a higher kind of want.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 135","lb":"PN135","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["விறலியென்","விறலி",4,"seythi","scene",""],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",5,"yaazh","simile",""],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",7,"yaazh","scene",""],["கன்றொடு","கன்று",11,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",14,"maa","gift_food",""],["மாவும்","மா",14,"maa","gift_food","horse"]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:136":{"book":"purananuru","num":136,"t":["யாழ்ப் பத்தர்ப் புறம் கடுப்ப","இழை வலந்த பஃறுன்னத்து","இடைப் புரைபற்றிப், பிணி விடாஅ","ஈர்க் குழாத்தொடு இறை கூர்ந்த","பேஎன் பகையென ஒன்று என்கோ?","உண்ணா மையின் ஊன் வாடித்,","தெண் ணீரின் கண் மல்கிக்,","கசிவுற்ற என் பல் கிளையொடு","பசி அலைக்கும் பகைஒன் றென்கோ?","அன்ன தன்மையும் அறிந்து ஈயார்,","‘நின்னது தா’ என, நிலை தளர,","மரம் பிறங்கிய நளிச் சிலம்பின்,","குரங் கன்ன புன்குறுங் கூளியர்","பரந் தலைக்கும் பகைஒன் றென்கோ?","‘ஆஅங்கு, எனைப் பகையும் அறியுநன் ஆய்,","எனக் கருதிப், பெயர் ஏத்தி,","வா யாரநின் இசை நம்பிச்,","சுடர் சுட்ட சுரத்து ஏறி,","இவண் வந்த பெரு நசையேம்;","‘எமக்கு ஈவோர் பிறர்க்கு ஈவோர்;","பிறர்க்கு ஈவோர் தமக்கு ஈப’ வென","அனைத் துரைத்தனன் யான்ஆக,","நினக்கு ஒத்தது நீ நாடி,","நல்கினை விடுமதி, பரிசில்! அல்கலும்,","தண்புனல் வாயில் துறையூர் முன்றுறை","நுண்பல மணலினும் ஏத்தி,","உண்குவம், பெரும ! நீ நல்கிய வளனே."],"p":"","r":"Shall I call one enemy the lice and nits that cling in the seams of my many-patched rag, ribbed like the back of a lute? Another the hunger that wears down my many kin, flesh wasted and eyes brimming? Another the petty monkey-like robbers of the wooded slopes who swarm on me crying 'give what you have' without knowing my state? Trusting that Ay knows every enemy, praising your name, I crossed the sun-burnt wasteland: those who give to us give to others, and those who give to others give to themselves; I have said all this, so judge what suits you, give and send me off, and daily at Thuraiyur's ford we will eat your gift, praising you more times than its fine sands.","n":"The catalogue of three enemies turns the bard's poverty into a war the patron is asked to win. The maxim in lines 20 and 21 is an argument, not a plea: a gift to the bard circulates, and in the end returns to the giver as fame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","pasi","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 136","lb":"PN136","poet":"துறையூர் ஓடை கிழார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","simile",""],["ஈர்க்","ஈர்",4,"pira","scene","nit"],["பேஎன்","பேன்",5,"pira","scene",""],["மரம்","மரம்",12,"maram","scene",""],["குரங்","குரங்கு",13,"maa","simile",""],["புனல்","புனல்",25,"pira","scene",""],["மணலினும்","மணல்",26,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆய்",15],["town","துறையூர்",25]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:137":{"book":"purananuru","num":137,"t":["இரங்கு முரசின், இனம் சால் யானை,","முந்நீர் ஏணி விறல்கெழு மூவரை","இன்னும் ஓர் யான் அவாஅறி யேனே;","நீயே, முன்யான் அரியு மோனே! துவன்றிய","கயத்திட்ட வித்து வறத்திற் சாவாது,","கழைக் கரும்பின், ஒலிக்குந்து,","கொண்டல் கொண்டநீர் கோடை காயினும்,","கண் ணன்ன மலர்பூக் குந்து,","கருங்கால் வேங்கை மலரின், நாளும்","பொன் னன்ன வீ சுமந்து,","மணி யன்ன நீர் கடற் படரும்;","செவ்வரைப் படப்பை நாஞ்சிற் பொருந!","சிறுவெள் ளருவிப் பெருங்கல் நாடனை!","நீவா ழியர் நின் தந்தை","தாய்வா ழியர் நிற் பயந்திசி னோரே!"],"p":"","r":"I will no longer long for the three kings of the sea-bounded land with their roaring drums and herds of elephants; you are the one I know: in your land seed sown in the crowded pond does not die in drought but thrives like cane, flowers like eyes bloom even when summer dries the rain-water, and water like sapphire flows to the sea carrying the gold-like petals of the dark-stemmed vengai; lord of Nanjil of the red hills, ruler of the land of great rocks and small white falls, long may you live, and long live the father and mother who bore you.","n":"The poet's renunciation of the three crowned kings is the measure of the small chief: a land that never fails in drought is a patron who never fails a bard. The blessing on the parents is a closing formula of praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 137","lb":"PN137","poet":"ஒருசிறைப் பெரியனார்","pat":"நாஞ்சில் வள்ளுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி; பரிசில் துறையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி","பரிசில் துறை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",1,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["வித்து","வித்து",5,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",6,"maram","simile",""],["மலர்பூக்","மலர்",8,"maram","simile",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",9,"maram","scene",""],["வீ","வீ",10,"maram","simile",""],["நீர்","நீர்",11,"pira","simile",""],["சிறுவெள் ளருவிப்","வெள் அருவி",13,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","நாஞ்சிற்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:138":{"book":"purananuru","num":138,"t":["ஆனினம் கலித்த அதர்பல கடந்து,","மானினம் கலித்த மலையின் ஒழிய,","மீளினம் கலித்த துறைபல நீந்தி,","உள்ளி வந்த, வள்ளுயிர்ச் சீறியாழ்,","சிதாஅர் உடுக்கை, முதாஅரிப் பாண!","நீயே, பேரெண் ணலையே; நின்இறை,","’மாறி வா’ என மொழியலன் மாதோ;","ஒலியிருங் கதுப்பின் ஆயிழை கணவன்","கிளி மரீஇய வியன் புனத்து","மரன் அணி பெருங்குரல் அனையன் ஆதலின்,","நின்னை வருதல் அறிந்தனர் யாரே!"],"p":"","r":"Bard in rags, with your small lute of strong-voiced strings, who crossed many paths full of cattle, left behind hills full of deer and swam many fords full of fish to come here: do not worry; your lord will never say 'come back later', for the husband of the lady with thick dark hair is like the great ear of grain in the wide field the parrots frequent, so who would know beforehand that you were coming?","n":"The image of the grain-ear the parrots feed on makes the patron a crop open to any bird that comes, so no appointment is needed. The poem names no patron; the colophon gives Ay.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 138","lb":"PN138","poet":"மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப் படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆனினம்","ஆ",1,"maa","scene","cattle"],["மானினம்","மான்",2,"maa","scene",""],["மீளினம்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",4,"yaazh","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",9,"pul","simile",""],["பெருங்குரல்","குரல்",10,"maram","simile","ear of grain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:139":{"book":"purananuru","num":139,"t":["சுவல் அழுந்தப் பல காய","சில் லோதிப் பல்இளை ஞருமே,","அடி வருந்த நெடிது ஏறிய","கொடி மருங்குல் விறலிய ருமே,","வாழ்தல் வேண்டிப்","பொய் கூறேன்; மெய் கூறுவல்;","ஓடாப் பூட்கை உரவோர் மருக!","உயர் சிமைய உழாஅ நாஞ்சில் பொருந!","மாயா உள்ளமொடு பரிசில் துன்னிக்,","கனிபதம் பார்க்கும் காலை யன்றே;","ஈதல் ஆனான், வேந்தே; வேந்தற்குச்","சாதல் அஞ்சாய், நீயே; ஆயிடை,","இருநிலம் மிளிர்ந்திசின் ஆஅங்கு, ஒருநாள்,","அருஞ் சமம் வருகுவ தாயின்,","வருந்தலு முண்டு, என் பைதலங் கடும்பே"],"p":"","r":"Young men with loads pressing their shoulders and dancers with slim waists whose feet ache from the long climb are with me, and to live I speak the truth, not lies: lord of Nanjil of the high peaks, the plough-hill no one ploughs, descendant of men who never turned back, this is no time to wait for the ripe moment; the king has not stopped giving, and you do not fear death for your king, so if one day a hard battle comes like the earth heaving up, my suffering troupe may come to grief.","n":"The bard's argument for speed is the patron's courage: a man who will die for his king may not be there tomorrow. The pun of the unploughed plough (உழாஅ நாஞ்சில்) praises the hill and its lord in one phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kaalam","maram","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 139","lb":"PN139","poet":"மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["இளை ஞருமே","இளைஞர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["விறலிய ருமே","விறலி",4,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","நாஞ்சில்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:140":{"book":"purananuru","num":140,"t":["தடவுநிலைப் பலவின் நாஞ்சில் பொருநன்","மடவன், மன்ற; செந்நாப் புலவீர்!","வளைக்கை விறலியர் படப்பைக் கொய்த","அடகின் கண்ணுறை ஆக யாம் சில","அரிசி வேண்டினெம் ஆகத், தான் பிற","வரிசை அறிதலின் தன்னும் தூக்கி.","இருங்கடறு வளைஇய குன்றத் தன்ன ஓர்","பெருங்களிறு நல்கியோனே; அன்னதோர்","தேற்றா ஈகையும் உளதுகொல்?","போற்றார் அம்ம, பெரியோர் தம் கடனே?"],"p":"","r":"The lord of Nanjil with its wide-spreading jackfruit trees is surely a simpleton, red-tongued poets: when we asked for a little rice to go with the greens our bangled dancers had picked in the garden, he, knowing other kinds of due measure, weighed his own standing too and gave us a great elephant like a hill ringed by forest; is there any giving as unknowing as this? Do the great not keep watch over their own duty?","n":"Praise disguised as blame: the patron is called foolish because he measured the gift by his own greatness rather than by the request. The closing question can be read as mock reproach (the great do not count the cost of their duty) or as a real one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","alavu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 140","lb":"PN140","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை"],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene",""],["விறலியர்","விறலி",3,"seythi","scene",""],["அடகின்","அடகு",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["அரிசி","அரிசி",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["hill","நாஞ்சில்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:7"},"pn:141":{"book":"purananuru","num":141,"t":["பாணன் சூடிய பசும்பொன் தாமரை","மாணிழை விறலி மாலையொடு விளங்கக்,","கடும்பரி நெடுந்தேர் பூட்டுவிட்டு அசைஇ,","ஊரீர் போலச் சுரத்திடை இருந்தனிர்!","யாரீ ரோ?’ என வனவல் ஆனாக்,","காரென் ஒக்கல், கடும் பசி, இரவல!","வென்வேல் அண்ணல் காணா ஊங்கே,","நின்னினும் புல்லியேம் மன்னே; இனியே,","இன்னேம் ஆயினேம் மன்னே ; என்றும்","உடாஅ, போரா ஆகுதல் அறிந்தும்","படா அம் மஞ்ஞைக்கு ஈத்த எம் கோ,","கடாஅ யானைக் கலிமான் பேகன்,","‘எத்துணை ஆயினும் ஈதல் நன்று’ என","மறுமை நோக்கின்றோ அன்றே,","பிறர், வறுமை நோக்கின்று, அவன் கைவண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"A bard resting on the road with his chariot unyoked, wearing a gold lotus and with his viṟali garlanded, answers a hungry bard who keeps asking who they are: before they saw Pekan they were poorer than he is, for Pekan, who gave a cloak to a peacock knowing it could neither wear nor wrap it, gives not with an eye to the next world but with an eye to other people's want.","n":"The cloak given to a peacock is offered as proof of motive: a gift to a creature that can neither use it nor repay it cannot be a bid for merit, so Pekan's famous folly becomes the evidence that he looks only at others' need (வறுமை நோக்கின்று), not at the next world (மறுமை). The guide also admits that he was once poorer than the man he guides (நின்னினும் புல்லியேம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aatruppadai","parisil","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 141","lb":"PN141","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்று படை; புலவராற்றுப் படையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை","புலவராற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பாணன்","பாணன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","ornament","gold lotus"],["விறலி","விறலி",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞைக்கு","மஞ்ஞை",11,"pul","scene","peacock"],["யானைக்","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""],["கலிமான்","கலி மான்",12,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","பேகன்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:142":{"book":"purananuru","num":142,"t":["அறுகுளத்து உகுத்தும், அகல்வயல் பொழிந்தும்,","உறுமிடத்து உதவாது உவர்நிலம் ஊட்டியும்,","வரையா மரபின் மாரி போலக்,","கடாஅ யானைக் கழற்கால் பேகன்","கொடைமடம் படுதல் அல்லது,","படைமடம் படான் பிறர் படைமயக் குறினே."],"p":"","r":"Like the rain that by its unbounded habit pours into the drained tank, over the broad field, and wastes itself on salt land where it helps no one, Pekan of the rutting elephant and warrior's anklet is foolish only in giving, never foolish in battle when other armies come at him.","n":"கொடைமடம், folly in giving, is a fault named as praise: the poet concedes that Pekan gives without discrimination, as rain falls on salt land, then pairs it with the folly he lacks (படைமடம்), so the one fault becomes the measure of his greatness in the other field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 142","lb":"PN142","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அறுகுளத்து","குளம்",1,"pira","simile",""],["அகல்வயல்","வயல்",1,"pira","simile",""],["மாரி","மாரி",3,"pira","simile","rain"],["யானைக்","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பேகன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:143":{"book":"purananuru","num":143,"t":["‘மலைவான் கொள்க!’ என, உயர்பலி தூஉய்,","‘மாரி ஆன்று, மழைமேக்கு உயர்க! ‘ எனக்","கடவுட் பேணிய குறவர் மாக்கள்,","பெயல்கண் மாறிய உவகையர், சாரல்","புனைத்தினை அயிலும் நாட! சினப் போர்க்","கைவள் ஈகைக் கடுமான் பேக!","யார்கொல் அளியள் தானே; நெருநல்,","சுரன் உழந்து வருந்திய ஒக்கல் பசித்தெனக்,","குணில்பாய் முரசின் இரங்கும் அருவி","நளிஇருஞ் சிலம்பின் சீறூர் ஆங்கண்.","வாயில் தோன்றி, வாழ்த்தி நின்று,","நின்னும்நின் மலையும் பாட, இன்னாது","இகுத்த கண்ணீர் நிறுத்தல் செல்லாள்.","முலையகம் நனைப்ப, விம்மிக்","குழல்இனை வதுபோல் அழுதனள், பெரிதே?"],"p":"","r":"Kapilar addresses Pekan, lord of a hill where the Kuravar, having made offerings to the god about the rain and rejoicing that it turned as they asked, eat their millet, and asks who that pitiable woman was who yesterday, in a small village by the waterfalls, when his hungry troupe stood at her door praising Pekan and his hill, could not stop her tears and wept aloud, wetting her breast, like a lamenting flute.","n":"The poet never says the woman is Pekan's wife: the question யார்கொல் அளியள் leaves him to supply the answer, and it is the song in his praise that makes her weep, so the praise poem turns into the reproach. The hill people whose prayer was answered stand at the opening against a woman whose waiting is not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 143","lb":"PN143","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"mixed","th":"perunthinai","cth":"பெருந்திணை","ctu":"குறுங்கலி; தாபதநிலையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu","குறுங்கலி"]],"pt":["குறுங்கலி","தாபதநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["புனைத்தினை","தினை",5,"unavu","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",9,"pira","scene",""],["குழல்இனை","குழல்",15,"yaazh","simile","flute"]],"en":[["patron","பேக",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:144":{"book":"purananuru","num":144,"t":["அருளா யாகலோ கொடிதே; இருள்வரச்,","சீறியாழ் செவ்வழி பண்ணி யாழ நின்","கார்எதிர் கானம் பாடினே மாக,","நீல்நறு நெய்தலிற் பொலிந்த உண்கண்","கலுழ்ந்து, வார் அரிப் பனி பூண்அகம் நனைப்ப,","இனைதல் ஆனா ளாக, ‘இளையோய்!","கிளையை மன், எம் கேள்வெய் யோற்கு?’என,","யாம்தன் தொழுதனம் வினவக், காந்தள்","முகைபுரை விரலின் கண்ணீர் துடையா,","‘யாம், அவன் கிளைஞரேம் அல்லேம்; கேள்,இனி;","எம்போல் ஒருத்தி நலன்நயந்து, என்றும்,","வரூஉம் என்ப; வயங்கு புகழ்ப் பேகன்","ஒல்லென ஒலிக்கும் தேரொடு,","முல்லை வேலி, நல்லூ ரானே!’"],"p":"","r":"Paranar tells Pekan that his want of pity is cruel: when the bards at nightfall played the sevvazhi mode and sang of his forest that meets the rains, a woman whose kohl-lined eyes outshone the neythal flower wept until her tears wet her ornaments, and when they bowed and asked whether she was kin to their patron she wiped her eyes with fingers like kaanthal buds and said she was not, only that he is said to drive there always in his loud chariot, desiring the beauty of a woman like her, to the good town fenced with mullai.","n":"Asked whether she is his kin, she denies being his kin and names the other woman only as one 'like me', so the wife's restraint carries the rebuke the poet does not make himself. The cycle 143 to 147 shows several poets taking up one patron's domestic breach in turn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 144","lb":"PN144","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"mixed","th":"perunthinai","cth":"பெருந்திணை","ctu":"குறுங்கலி","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu","குறுங்கலி"]],"pt":["குறுங்கலி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["செவ்வழி","செவ்வழி",2,"yaazh","scene","evening mode"],["நெய்தலிற்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","simile",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",8,"maram","simile",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",14,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பேகன்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:145":{"book":"purananuru","num":145,"t":["‘மடத்தகை மாமயில் பனிக்கும்’ என்று அருளிப்","படாஅம் ஈத்த கெடாஅ நல்லிசைக்,","கடாஅ யானைக் கலிமான் பேக!","பசித்தும் வாரோம்; பாரமும் இலமே ;","களங்கனி யன்ன கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ்","நயம்புரிந் துறையுநர் நடுங்கப் பண்ணி,","‘அறம்செய் தீமோ, அருள்வெய் யோய்!’ என,","இஃதியாம் இரந்த பரிசில்: அஃது இருளின்,","இனமணி நெடுந்தேர் ஏறி,","இன்னாது உறைவி அரும்படர் களைமே!"],"p":"","r":"Paranar reminds Pekan, famed for giving a cloak to the peacock in pity lest it shiver, that the bards come neither from hunger nor with a burden to lay down: the gift they beg, playing the small lute until listeners tremble and crying 'do this dharma, lover of grace', is that he mount his chariot tonight and end the grief of the woman who lives in sorrow.","n":"The peacock is turned against Pekan: if he pitied a bird that might be cold, he cannot leave a woman in sorrow. By declaring that they are not hungry, the bards empty the parisil form of its usual content and make it a plea for reconciliation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","karpu","pirivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 145","lb":"PN145","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"mixed","th":"perunthinai","cth":"பெருந்திணை","ctu":"குறுங்கலி","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu","குறுங்கலி"]],"pt":["குறுங்கலி"],"mp":[],"k":[["மாமயில்","மா மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["யானைக்","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["கலிமான்","கலி மான்",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["களங்கனி","களங்கனி",5,"maram","simile",""],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",5,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","பேக",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:146":{"book":"purananuru","num":146,"t":["அன்ன வாக; நின் அருங்கல வெறுக்கை","அவை பெறல் வேண்டேம்; அடுபோர்ப் பேக!","சீறியாழ் செவ்வழி பண்ணி, நின் வன்புல","நன்னாடு பாட, என்னை நயந்து","பரிசில் நல்குவை யாயின், குரிசில் ! நீ","நல்கா மையின் நைவரச் சாஅய்,","அருந்துயர் உழக்கும்நின் திருந்திழை அரிவை","கலிமயிற் கலாவம் கால்குவித் தன்ன,","ஒலிமென் கூந்தல் கமழ்புகை கொளீஇத்,","தண்கமழ் கோதை புனைய,","வண்பரி நெடுந்தேர் பூண்க, நின் மாவே!"],"p":"","r":"Arisil Kizhar tells Pekan to keep his rare jewels and wealth: if, after the bard has sung his good hard-soiled land in the sevvazhi mode, he wishes to give a gift, let it be that his wife, who wastes away in grief at his neglect, may perfume her soft hair, thick as a peacock's gathered tail, with fragrant smoke and wear a cool garland, and that his horses be yoked to his tall chariot.","n":"The gift is described entirely through the wife's toilet: the incense and the garland she has given up will come back when he does, so the poem asks for his return without stating it, and ends by commanding his horses rather than him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 146","lb":"PN146","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"mixed","th":"perunthinai","cth":"பெருந்திணை","ctu":"குறுங்கலி","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu","குறுங்கலி"]],"pt":["குறுங்கலி"],"mp":[],"k":[["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene",""],["செவ்வழி","செவ்வழி",3,"yaazh","scene","evening mode"],["கலிமயிற்","மயில்",8,"pul","simile","peacock"],["கோதை","கோதை",10,"maram","ornament",""],["மாவே","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","பேக",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:147":{"book":"purananuru","num":147,"t":["கல்முழை அருவிப் பன்மலை நீந்திச்,","சீறியாழ் செவ்வழி பண்ணி வந்ததைக்,","கார்வான் இன்னுறை தமியள் கேளா,","நெருநல் ஒருசிறைப் புலம்புகொண்டு உறையும்","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண், அம்மா அரிவை","நெய்யொடு துறந்த மையிருங் கூந்தல்","மண்ணூறு மணியின் மாசுஅற மண்ணிப்,","புதுமலர் கஞல, இன்று பெயரின்","அதுமன், எம் பரிசில் ஆவியர் கோவே!"],"p":"","r":"Perunkunrur Kizhar tells the lord of the Aviyar that he has crossed many hills of cave-fed falls playing the sevvazhi mode, and that the gift he asks is this: that the lovely woman with streaked, rain-dark eyes, who heard alone yesterday the sweet rumble of the dark clouds and lives in one corner in loneliness, should wash clean as an earth-dulled gem her dark hair left without oil and crowd it with fresh flowers, if he goes back today.","n":"The rain-cloud heard 'yesterday' is the season of promised return, so the gift is set against a deadline already passed; hair left without oil (நெய்யொடு துறந்த) is the sign of a wife who has given up adornment while she waits, and the poet asks for it to be reversed today.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["parisil","pirivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 147","lb":"PN147","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"வையாவிக் கோப்பெரும் பேகன்","ap":"mixed","th":"perunthinai","cth":"பெருந்திணை","ctu":"குறுங்கலி","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu","குறுங்கலி"]],"pt":["குறுங்கலி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவிப்","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["செவ்வழி","செவ்வழி",2,"yaazh","scene","evening mode"],["மணியின்","மணி",7,"pira","simile","gem"],["புதுமலர்","மலர்",8,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆவியர் கோவே",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:148":{"book":"purananuru","num":148,"t":["கறங்குமிசை அருவிய பிறங்குமலை நள்ளி! நின்","அசைவுஇல் நோந்தாள் நசைவளன் ஏத்தி,","நாடொறும் நன்கலம் கனிற்றொடு கொணர்ந்து,","கூடுவிளங்கு வியன்நகர்ப், பரிசில் முற்று அளிப்பப்;","பீடில் மன்னர்ப் புகழ்ச்சி வேண்டிச்","செய்யா கூறிக் கிளத்தல்","எய்யா தாகின்று, எம் சிறு செந்நாவே."],"p":"","r":"Nalli of the high hill loud with falls: having praised his tireless strength and the wealth people long for, the poet has been given daily, in his bright great house, fine jewels with elephants, gifts in full, so that his small red tongue no longer knows how to recite deeds never done in seeking praise from kings without merit.","n":"The poem praises by renouncing a genre: Nalli's gifts have made flattery unnecessary, so the poet's honesty is counted among the things Nalli gave him. It also admits that bards did invent deeds for meaner patrons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","pugazh","vaaimai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 148","lb":"PN148","poet":"வன்பரணர்","pat":"கண்டீரக் கோப் பெருநள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அருவிய","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["கனிற்றொடு","களிறு",3,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[["patron","நள்ளி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:149":{"book":"purananuru","num":149,"t":["நள்ளி ! வாழியோ; நள்ளி ! நள்ளென்","மாலை மருதம் பண்ணிக் காலைக்","கைவழி மருங்கிற் செவ்வழி பண்ணி,","‘வரவுஎமர் மறந்தனர்; அது நீ","புரவுக்கடன் பூண்ட வண்மை யானே"],"p":"","r":"Long live Nalli: because of the generosity with which he has taken on the duty of keeping them, the poet's people have forgotten the order of their modes, playing marutham in the hushed evening and sevvazhi in the morning.","n":"The praise is a joke against the bards: plenty has done what hunger never did and made them lose the clock of their own craft, since each mode had its hour. The poem asks for nothing, which is why the colophon's பரிசில் sits awkwardly on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 149","lb":"PN149","poet":"வன்பரணர்","pat":"கண்டீரக் கோப் பெருநள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["மருதம்","மருதம்",2,"yaazh","scene","morning mode"],["செவ்வழி","செவ்வழி",3,"yaazh","scene","evening mode"]],"en":[["patron","நள்ளி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:150":{"book":"purananuru","num":150,"t":["கூதிர்ப் பருந்தின் இருஞ் சிறகு அன்ன","பாறிய சிதாரேன், பலவுமுதல் பொருந்தித்,","தன்னும் உள்ளேன், பிறிதுபுலம் படர்ந்த என்","உயங்குபடர் வருத்தமும் உலைவும் நோக்கி,","மான்கணம் தொலைச்சிய குருதியங் கழற்கால்,","வான்கதிர்த் திருமணி விளங்கும் சென்னிச்,","செல்வத் தோன்றல், ஓர் வல்வில் வேட்டுவன்,","தொழுதனென் எழுவேற் கைகவித்து இரீஇ,","இழுதின் அன்ன வால்நிணக் கொழுங்குறை,","கான்அதர் மயங்கிய இளையர் வல்லே,","தாம்வந்து எய்தா அளவை, ஒய்யெனத்","தான்ஞெலி தீயின் விரைவனன் சுட்டு, ‘நின்","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கலொடு தின்ம்’ எனத் தருதலின்,","அமிழ்தின் மிசைந்து, காய்பசி நீங்கி,","நல்மரன் நளிய நறுந்தண் சாரல்,","கல்மிசை அருவி தண்ணெனப் பருகி,","விடுத்தல் தொடங்கினேன் ஆக, வல்லே,","“பெறுதற் கரிய வீறுசால் நன்கலம்","பிறிதொன்று இல்லை; காட்டு நாட்டோம்” என,","மார்பிற் பூண்ட வயங்குகாழ் ஆரம்","மடைசெறி முன்கை கடகமொடு ஈத்தனன்;","‘எந்நா டோ?’ என, நாடும் சொல்லான்!","‘யாரீ ரோ!’ எனப், பேரும் சொல்லான்;","பிறர்பிறர் கூற வழிக்கேட் டிசினே;","‘இரும்பு புனைந்து இயற்றாப் பெரும்பெயர்த் தோட்டி","அம்மலை காக்கும் அணிநெடுங் குன்றின்","பளிங்கு வகுத் தன்ன தீநீர்,","நளிமலை நாடன் நள்ளிஅவன்’ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"In a cloak torn like an autumn kite's dark wing, the poet lay at the foot of a jack tree, past caring for himself, when a strong-bowed hunter with a gem on his head and an anklet bloody from killing deer saw his weariness, made him sit as he rose to bow, roasted white fat on a fire he struck himself before his men caught up, and bade him eat with his kin; when the poet had eaten, drunk at the cool falls and made to leave, the hunter said a forest people have no other rare treasure and gave him the chain from his chest and the bracelet from his arm, told neither his country nor his name, and only from others did the poet learn that he was Nalli, lord of the hill guarded by Thotti.","n":"The poem turns on what Nalli withholds, his country and his name, so the gift comes without the fame it usually buys, and the poet repays him by naming him in the last line. He cooks with his own fire before his attendants arrive. Line 25 plays on தோட்டி, an elephant goad and the name of the hill: a goad 'not forged of iron'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 150","lb":"PN150","poet":"வன் பரணர்","pat":"கண்டீரக் கோப் பெருநள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",1,"pul","simile","kite"],["பலவுமுதல்","பலா",2,"maram","scene",""],["மான்கணம்","மான்",5,"maa","scene","deer"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["நிணக்","நிணம்",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["இளையர்","இளையர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",16,"pira","gift_food",""],["பளிங்கு","பளிங்கு",27,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","நள்ளி",28],["hill","தோட்டி",25]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:151":{"book":"purananuru","num":151,"t":["பண்டும் பண்டும் பாடுநர் உவப்ப,","விண்தோய் சிமைய விறல்வரைக் கவாஅன்","கிழவன் சேட்புலம் படரின், இழை அணிந்து,","புன்தலை மடப்பிடி பரிசிலாகப்,","பெண்டிரும் தம்பதம் கொடுக்கும் வண்புகழ்க்","கண்டீ ரக்கோன் ஆகலின், நன்றும்","முயங்கல் ஆன்றிசின், யானே: பொலந்தேர்","நன்னன் மருகன் அன்றியும், நீயும்","முயங்கற்கு ஒத்தனை மன்னே: வயங்கு மொழிப்","பாடுநர்க்கு அடைத்த கதவின், ஆடு மழை","அணங்குசால் அடுக்கம் பொழியும் நும்","மணங்கமழ் மால்வரை வரைந்தனர், எமரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the young Vicchi lord why he embraced Kandeerakkon and not him: Kandeerakkon is of a house so generous that when the lord of the sky-touching hill is away even its women give jewelled young she-elephants to delight singers as of old; the Vicchi lord, kin of Nannan of the golden chariot, would also have deserved an embrace, but because of a door shut against singers the poet's people have shunned his fragrant great hill where the clouds pour.","n":"An embrace was a public honour that a poet could give or withhold, and this poem justifies a public snub. Whose door was shut, the young lord's or his line's, is not certain from the text. The irrealis மன்னே ('you would have been fit') keeps the refusal courteous.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 151","lb":"PN151","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"இளவிச்சிக்கோ","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","gift_food","she-elephant"],["மழை","மழை",10,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","கண்டீ ரக்கோன்",6],["king","நன்னன்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:152":{"book":"purananuru","num":152,"t":["‘வேழம் வீழ்த்த விழுத் தொடைப் பகழி","பேழ்வாய் உழுவையைப் பெரும்பிறிது உறீஇப்,","புழல்தலை புகர்க்கலை உருட்டி, உரல்தலைக்","கேழற் பன்றி வீழ, அயலது","ஆழற் புற்றத்து உடும்பில் செற்றும்,","வல்வில் வேட்டம் வலம்படுத் திருந்தோன்,","புகழ்சால் சிறப்பின் அம்புமிகத் திளைக்கும்","கொலைவன் யார்கொலோ? கொலைவன் மற்று இவன்","விலைவன் போலான்: வெறுக்கைநன்கு உடையன்:","ஆரம் தாழ்ந்த அம்பகட்டு மார்பின்,","சாரல் அருவிப் பயமலைக் கிழவன்,","ஓரி கொல்லோ? அல்லன் கொல்லோ?","பாடுவல், விறலி! ஓர் வண்ணம்; நீரும்","மண்முழா அமைமின் ; பண்யாழ் நிறுமின் ;","கண்விடு தூம்பின் களிற்று உயிர் தொடுமின்:","எல்லரி தொடுமின் ; ஆகுளி தொடுமின்;","பதலை ஒருகண் பையென இயக்குமின்;","மதலை மாக்கோல் கைவலம் தமின்’ என்று,","இறைவன் ஆகலின், சொல்லுபு குறுகி,","மூவேழ் துறையும் ழுறையுளிக் கழிப்பிக்,","‘கோ’வெனப் பெயரிய காலை, ஆங்கு அது","தன்பெயர் ஆகலின் நாணி, மற்று, யாம்","நாட்டிடன் நாட்டிடன் வருதும் ; ஈங்கு ஓர்","வேட்டுவர் இல்லை, நின் ஒப் போர்’ என,","வேட்டது மொழியவும் விடாஅன், வேட்டத்தில்","தான் உயிர் செகுத்த மான் நிணப் புழுக்கோடு,","ஆன் உருக்கு அன்ன வேரியை நல்கித்,","தன்மலைப் பிறந்த தாவில் நன்பொன்","பன்மணிக் குவையொடும் விரைஇக், ‘கொண்ம்’ எனச்,","சுரத்துஇடை நல்கி யோனே : விடர்ச் சிமை","ஓங்குஇருங் கொல்லிப் பொருநன்,","ஓம்பா ஈகை விறல்வெய் யோனே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he met a hunter whose one arrow felled an elephant, passed through a gape-mouthed tiger, rolled a spotted stag, dropped a boar and lodged in an iguana in an anthill; guessing that this rich killer, plainly no man who sells, might be Ori, lord of the fruitful hill, he had his viṟali and troupe set up drum, lute, horn and pipes and played all twenty-one modes in order, and when they hailed him as 'king' the man was abashed because it was his own name and, not letting them go, gave them the meat of his kill, toddy like melted ghee and gold and gems from his hill, there in the wilderness: the lord of tall Kolli, eager for unguarded giving.","n":"The single arrow through five beasts is the poem's credential for the man before he is named, and the bards test their guess by performance: hailed as கோ, he is abashed because it is his own name. As in 150, the patron is met as a hunter in the forest, not at court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 152","lb":"PN152","poet":"வண்பரணர்","pat":"வல்வில் ஓரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உழுவையைப்","உழுவை",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["புகர்க்கலை","கலை",3,"maa","scene","stag"],["கேழற்","கேழல்",4,"maa","scene","boar"],["உடும்பில்","உடும்பு",5,"pira","scene","iguana"],["விறலி","விறலி",13,"seythi","scene",""],["மண்முழா","முழா",14,"parai","scene",""],["பண்யாழ்","யாழ்",14,"yaazh","scene",""],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",15,"yaazh","scene",""],["ஆகுளி","ஆகுளி",16,"parai","scene",""],["பதலை","பதலை",17,"parai","scene",""],["வேட்டுவர்","வேட்டுவர்",24,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",26,"maa","gift_food","deer"],["வேரியை","வேரி",27,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["patron","ஓரி",12],["hill","கொல்லி",31]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:153":{"book":"purananuru","num":153,"t":["மழையணி குன்றத்துக் கிழவன், நாளும்,","இழையணி யானை இரப்போர்க்கு ஈயும்,","சுடர்விடு பசும்பூண், சூர்ப்பு அமை முன்கை,","அடுபோர் ஆனா, ஆதன் ஓரி","மாரி வண்கொடை காணிய, நன்றும்","சென்றது மன், எம் கண்ணுளங் கடும்பே;","பனிநீர்ப் பூவா மணிமிடை குவளை","வால்நார்த் தொடுத்த கண்ணியும், கலனும்,","யானை இனத்தொடு பெற்றனர்; நீங்கிப்,","பசியார் ஆகல் மாறுகொல்; விசிபிணிக்","கூடுகொள் இன்னியம் கறங்க,","ஆடலும் ஒல்லார் தம் பாடலும் மறந்தே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet's troupe of performers went to see the rain-like giving of Athan Ori, lord of the cloud-clad hill who daily gives jewelled elephants to suppliants, and received garlands of gold lilies on white fibre that no cold water ever bloomed, ornaments and herds of elephants; now, perhaps because they are no longer hungry, they will neither dance nor sing to their tight-strung drums.","n":"The lilies 'that do not bloom in cold water' are gold work, so even the flowers are imperishable; and the closing joke, that the troupe stopped performing when it stopped being hungry, admits that the bards' art ran on need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pasi","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 153","lb":"PN153","poet":"வண்பரணர்","pat":"வல்வில் ஓரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","gift_food",""],["குவளை","குவளை",7,"maram","ornament","gold lily"]],"en":[["patron","ஆதன் ஓரி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:154":{"book":"purananuru","num":154,"t":["திரைபொரு முந்நீர்க் கரைநணிச் செலினும்,","அறியுநர்க் காணின், வேட்கை நீக்கும்","சின்னீர் வினவுவர், மாந்தர் : அதுபோல்,","அரசர் உழைய ராகவும், புரைதபு","வள்ளியோர்ப் படர்குவர், புலவர் ; அதனால்,","‘யானும்,‘பெற்றது ஊதியம்; பேறியாது?’ என்னேன்;","உற்றனென் ஆதலின் உள்ளிவந் தனனே;","‘ஈயென இரத்தலோ அரிதே! நீ அது","நல்கினும், நல்காய் ஆயினும் வெல்போர்","எறிபடைக்கு ஓடா ஆண்மை, அறுவைத்","தூவிரி கடுப்பத் துவன்றி மீமிசைத்","தண்பல இழிதரும் அருவி நின்","கொண்டுபெருங்கானம், பாடல் எனக்கு எளிதே."],"p":"","r":"Even beside the wave-beaten sea people ask someone they know for a little water to quench their thirst, and so poets, though they live among kings, go to the faultlessly generous; the poet therefore does not ask what he will get, but came because he thought of the patron: to say 'give' is hard, but whether he gives or not, singing his valour that does not run from thrown spears and his great forest of Konkanam, where cool falls come down like spread white cloth, is easy.","n":"Kings are the sea, vast and undrinkable; the generous small lord is the little sweet water got from someone known. The poet separates what parisil poems usually join: asking is hard for him, praising is easy, so the praise is offered as free.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","parisil","maanam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 154","lb":"PN154","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"கொண்கானங் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் துறை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்துறை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கல்வி"]],"k":[["முந்நீர்க்","முந்நீர்",1,"pira","simile","sea"],["அருவி","அருவி",12,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","கொண்டுபெருங்கானம்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:155":{"book":"purananuru","num":155,"t":["வணர் கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ் வாடுபுடைத் தழீஇ,","‘உணர்வோர் யார், என் இடும்பை தீர்க்க”எனக்,","கிளக்கும், பாண! கேள், இனி நயத்தின்;","பாழ்ஊர் நெருஞ்சிப் பசலை வான்பூ","ஏர்தரு சுடரின் எதிர்கொண்டு ஆஅங்கு,","இலம்படு புலவர் மண்டை விளங்கு புகழ்க்","கொண்பெருங்காலத்துக் கிழவன்","தண்தார் அகலம் நோக்கின், மலர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Bard who hug the small curved-stemmed lute to your wasted side and cry out for someone to understand and end your distress, listen: as the pale white flower of the nerunchi in a deserted village opens to face the rising sun, the bowls of needy poets open in bloom when they face the cool-garlanded chest of the lord of Konperunkanam of shining fame.","n":"The image is a weed of the deserted village that turns to the sun, so the poor are flowers of a ruined place and the patron is their sun; the flower's colour is பசலை, the word akam keeps for the pallor of lovesickness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 155","lb":"PN155","poet":"மோசி கீரனார்","pat":"கொண்கானங் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பாணாற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["நெருஞ்சிப்","நெருஞ்சி",4,"maram","simile",""],["தண்தார்","தார்",8,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:156":{"book":"purananuru","num":156,"t":["ஒன்றுநன் குடைய பிறர் குன்றம்; என்றும்","இரண்டுநன் குடைத்தே கொண்பெருங் கானம்;","நச்சிச் சென்ற இரவலர்ச் சுட்டித்","தொடுத்துணக் கிடப்பினும் கிடக்கும்; அஃதான்று","நிறையருந் தானை வேந்தரைத்","திறைகொண்டு பெயர்க்குஞ் செம்மலும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Other hills have one good thing, but Konperunkanam always has two: it will lie open for the suppliants who come to it in desire to gather and eat, and besides that it has the lord who sends kings with numberless armies home after taking their tribute.","n":"The poem counts a hill's goods like assets: the land itself feeds suppliants, and the lord feeds it with kings' tribute, so giving and conquest are two products of one place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","naadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 156","lb":"PN156","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"கொண்கானங் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[],"en":[["hill","கொண்பெருங் கானம்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:157":{"book":"purananuru","num":157,"t":["தமர்தன் தப்பின் அதுநோன் றல்லும்,","பிறர்கை யறவு தான்நா ணுதலும்,","படைப்பழி தாரா மைந்தினன் ஆகலும்,","வேந்துடை அவையத்து ஓங்குபு நடத்தலும்,","நும்மோர்க்குத் தகுவன அல்ல; எம்மோன்","சிலைசெல மலர்ந்த மார்பின், கொலைவேல்,","கோடல் கண்ணிக் குறவர் பெருமகன்:","ஆடு மழை தவிர்க்கும் பயங்கெழு மீமிசை,","எற்படு பொழுதின், இனம்தலை மயங்கிக்,","கட்சி காணாக் கடமான் நல்லேறு","மடமான் நாகுபிணை பயிரின், விடர்முழை","இரும்புலிப் புகர்ப்போத்து ஓர்க்கும்","பெருங்கல் நாடன்-எம் ஏறைக்குத் தகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Bearing with the faults of one's own kin, feeling shame at others' destitution, having a strength that never brings disgrace on the army, and walking tall in the assembly of kings are not fitting for your lords; they fit our Erai, chief of the Kuravar, with broad chest, killing spear and kodal chaplet, lord of the great hill on whose cloud-cleared heights, at dusk, a fine stag that has lost its herd and cannot find its lair calls to its young doe while the spotted tiger in the rock cave listens.","n":"The virtues are those the Kural later files under shame and forbearance, claimed here by a woman poet of the Kuravar for her own hill chief against other men's lords. Who நும்மோர் ('your people') are is not certain. The closing scene of the stag, the doe and the listening tiger is left without comment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","porai","maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 157","lb":"PN157","poet":"குறமகள் இளவெயினி","pat":"ஏறைக் கோன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["கோடல்","கோடல்",7,"maram","ornament",""],["கடமான்","கடமான்",10,"maa","scene","sambar stag"],["மடமான்","மான்",11,"maa","scene","doe"],["இரும்புலிப்","புலி",12,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","ஏறை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:158":{"book":"purananuru","num":158,"t":["முரசுகடிப்பு இகுப்பவும், வால்வளை துவைப்பவும்,","அரசுடன் பொருத அண்ணல் நெடுவரைக்,","கறங்குவெள் அருவி கல் அலைத்து ஒழுகும்","பறம்பின் கோமான் பாரியும்; பிறங்கு மிசைக்","கொல்லி ஆண்ட வல்வில் ஓரியும்;","காரி ஊர்ந்து பேரமர்க் கடந்த,","மாரி ஈகை, மறப்போர் மலையனும்;","ஊராது ஏந்திய குதிரைக், கூர்வேல்,","கூவிளங் கண்ணிக், கொடும்பூண், எழினியும்;","ஈர்ந்தண் சிலம்பின் இருள் தூங்கும் நளிமுழை,","அருந்திறல் கடவுள் காக்கும் உயர்சிமைப்,","பெருங்கல் நாடன் பேகனும்; திருந்து மொழி","மோசி பாடிய ஆயும்; ஆர்வமுற்று","உள்ளி வருநர் உலைவுநனி தீரத்,","தள்ளாது ஈயும் தகைசால் வண்மைக்,","கொள்ளார் ஓட்டிய, நள்ளையும்; என ஆங்கு","எழுவர் மாய்ந்த பின்றை, அழி வரப்","பாடி வருநரும் பிறருங் கூடி","இரந்தோர் அற்றம் தீர்க்கென, விரைந்து இவண்","உள்ளி வந்தனென், யானே; விசும்புஉறக்","கழைவளர் சிலம்பின் வழையொடு நீடி,","ஆசினிக் கவினிய பலவின் ஆர்வுற்று,","முட்புற முதுகனி பெற்ற கடுவன்","துய்த்தலை மந்தியைக் கையிடூஉப் பயிரும்,","அதிரா யாணர், முதிரத்துக் கிழவ!","இவண்விளங்கு சிறப்பின் இயல்தேர்க் குமண!","இசைமேந் தோன்றிய வண்மையொடு,","பகைமேம் படுக, நீ ஏந்திய வேலே!"],"p":"","r":"Now that the seven are dead who fought and gave, Pari of Parambu, Ori who ruled Kolli, Malaiyan who rode Kari to victory, Ezhini of the horse held but not ridden, Pekan of the great hill guarded by a god, Ay whom Mosi sang, and Nalli who drove off his foes and gave without refusing, the poet has hurried here thinking of Kumanan to end the need of suppliants, and blesses the lord of Muthiram, where a male monkey that has eaten an old thorny jackfruit calls his soft-headed mate with his hand, that his spear may prevail over his enemies along with his famed generosity.","n":"The roll of seven makes generosity a line of succession with a gap that Kumanan is invited to fill, so the praise is also a summons. Each giver gets one emblem, compressing whole poems into an epithet. 'The horse held but not ridden' (ஊராது ஏந்திய குதிரை) may be Ezhini's hill named Kuthirai; the text does not settle it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","pugazh","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 158","lb":"PN158","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்; பரிசில் கடாநிலையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்","பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["முரசு","முரசு",1,"parai","scene",""],["வால்வளை","வளை",1,"pira","scene","conch"],["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["கூவிளங்","கூவிளம்",9,"maram","ornament",""],["பலவின்","பலா",22,"maram","scene",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",23,"maa","scene",""],["மந்தியைக்","மந்தி",24,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பாரியும்",4],["hill","பறம்பின்",4],["patron","ஓரியும்",5],["hill","கொல்லி",5],["patron","மலையனும்",7],["patron","எழினியும்",9],["patron","பேகனும்",12],["patron","ஆயும்",13],["patron","நள்ளையும்",16],["hill","முதிரத்துக்",25],["patron","குமண",26]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:159":{"book":"purananuru","num":159,"t":["‘வாழும் நாளொடு யாண்டுபல உண்மையின்,","தீர்தல்செல் லாது, என் உயிர்’ எனப் பலபுலந்து,","கோல்கால் ஆகக் குறும்பல ஒதுங்கி,","நூல்விரித் தன்ன கதுப்பினள், கண் துயின்று,","முன்றிற் போகா முதுர்வினள் யாயும்;","பசந்த மேனியொடு படர்அட வருந்தி,","மருங்கில் கொண்ட பல்குறு மாக்கள்","பிசைந்துதின வாடிய முலையள், பெரிது அழிந்து,","குப்பைக் கீரைக் கொய்கண் அகைத்த","முற்றா இளந்தளிர் கொய்துகொண்டு, உப்பின்று,","நீர்உலை யாக ஏற்றி, மோரின்று,","அவிழ்பதம் மறந்து, பாசடகு மிசைந்து,","மாசொடு குறைந்த உடுக்கையள், அறம் பழியாத்,","துவ்வாள் ஆகிய என்வெய் யோளும்;","என்றாங்கு, இருவர் நெஞ்சமும் உவப்பக் கானவர்","கரிபுனம் மயக்கிய அகன்கண் கொல்லை.","ஐவனம் வித்தி, மையுறக் கவினி,","ஈனல் செல்லா ஏனற்கு இழுமெனக்","கருவி வானம் தலைஇ யாங்கும்,","ஈத்த நின்புகழ் ஏத்தித், தொக்க என்,","பசிதினத் திரங்கிய, ஒக்கலும் உவப்ப-","உயர்ந்து ஏந்து மருப்பின் கொல்களிறு பெறினும்,","தவிர்ந்துவிடு பரிசில் கொள்ளலென்; உவந்து, நீ","இன்புற விடுதி யாயின், சிறிது","குன்றியும் கொள்வல், கூர்வேற் குமண!","அதற்பட அருளல் வேண்டுவல்-விறற்புகழ்","வசையில் விழுத்திணைப் பிறந்த","இசைமேந் தோன்றல்! நிற் பாடிய யானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet's mother, so old she complains that her life will not leave her, walks in short steps on a stick, hair like spread thread, never past the yard; his wife, pale and worn, her breasts dried by the many small children who knead and suck them, boils without salt the unripe shoots of rubbish-heap greens, forgets rice, eats the greens without buttermilk and, in soiled scant cloth, goes without and does not blame virtue; so that both their hearts and his hungry kin may rejoice, as cloud pours on a hunters' burnt clearing whose millet would not ear, he will not take a gift given grudgingly though it were a tusked killer elephant, but given gladly he will take even a kunri seed's worth, and asks Kumanan to grant it so.","n":"After the longest picture of household hunger in the chapter, the demand is about manner, not size: an elephant given grudgingly is refused and a kunri seed given gladly accepted, so the bard puts the giver's gladness above his family's need. அறம் பழியா, his wife does not blame virtue though she starves, gives the household its own pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","parisil","maanam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 159","lb":"PN159","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["நூல்விரித்","நூல்",4,"pira","simile","thread"],["கீரைக்","கீரை",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["உப்பின்று","உப்பு",10,"pira","absent",""],["மோரின்று","மோர்",11,"unavu","absent",""],["அவிழ்பதம்","அவிழ்",12,"unavu","absent","cooked rice"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",15,"seythi","simile",""],["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",17,"maram","simile",""],["ஏனற்கு","ஏனல்",18,"maram","simile",""],["கொல்களிறு","களிறு",22,"maa","gift_food",""],["குன்றியும்","குன்றி",25,"maram","gift_food","kunri seed"]],"en":[["patron","குமண",25]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:160":{"book":"purananuru","num":160,"t":["‘உருகெழு ஞாயிற்று ஒண்கதிர் மிசைந்த","முளிபுல் கானம் குழைப்பக், கல்லென","அதிர்குரல் ஏறோடு துளிசொரிந் தாங்குப்","பசிதினத் திரங்கிய கசிவுடை யாக்கை","அவிழ்புகுவு அறியா தாகலின், வாடிய","நெறிகொள் வரிக்குடர் குனிப்பத் தண்ணெனக்,","குய்கொள் கொழுந்துவை நெய்யுடை அடிசில்,","மதி சேர் நாள்மீன் போல, நவின்ற","சிறுபொன் நன்கலஞ் சுற்ற இரீஇக்","“கோடின் றாக, பாடுநர் கடும்பு” என,","அரிதுபெறு பொலங்கலம் எளிதினின் வீசி,","நட்டோர் நட்ட நல்லிசைக் குமணன்,","மட்டார் மறுகின், முதிரத் தோனே:","செல்குவை யாயின், நல்குவை, பெரிது’ எனப்,","பல்புகழ் நுவலுநர் கூற, வல் விரைந்து,","உள்ளம் துரப்ப வந்தனென்; எள்ளுற்று,","இல் உணாத் துறத்தலின், இல் மறந்து உறையும்","புல்லுளைக் குடுமிப் புதல்வன் பன்மாண்","பாலில் வறுமுலைசுவைத்தனன்பெறாஅன்,","கூழும் சோறும் கடைஇ, ஊழின்","உள்ளில் வருங்கலம் திறந்து, அழக் கண்டு,","மறப்புலி உரைத்தும், மதியங் காட்டியும்,","நொந்தனள் ஆகி, ‘நுந்தையை உள்ளிப்,","பொடிந்தநின் செவ்வி காட்டு’ எனப் பலவும்","வினவல் ஆனா ளாகி, நனவின்","அல்லல் உழப்போள் மல்லல் சிறப்பச்,","செல்லாச் செல்வம் மிகுந்தனை, வல்லே","விடுதல் வேண்டுவல் அத்தை; படுதிரை","நீர்சூழ் நிலவரை உயர நின்","சீர்கெழு விழுப்புகழ் ஏத்துகம் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Told by Kumanan's praisers that this lord of Muthiram, who flings away hard-won gold vessels so that the bards' troupe may not fail, would revive him like rain on a sun-scorched forest with rich seasoned rice and ghee served in small gold dishes ringed like stars around the moon, the poet hurried, driven by his heart, from a house where his little tufted son sucks his mother's milkless breast and cries opening the empty pots, while she quiets him with the tiger and the moon and at last asks him to show how he sulks when he thinks of his father; and he asks to be sent off quickly with lasting wealth so that she who suffers in waking life may flourish, and will praise Kumanan's fame across the sea-girt earth.","n":"Lines 1 to 14 quote the hearsay that brought him, so the poem holds Kumanan to a reputation. The mother uses the tiger and the moon, the props of nursery talk, in place of food. Four lines are flagged as possible misprints and the review finds two lines missing, so details of the household scene are not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["inmai","pasi","parisil","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 160","lb":"PN160","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["முளிபுல்","புல்",2,"maram","simile",""],["ஏறோடு","ஏறு",3,"pira","simile","thunder"],["அடிசில்","அடிசில்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மறப்புலி","புலி",22,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","குமணன்",12],["hill","முதிரத்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:8"},"pn:161":{"book":"purananuru","num":161,"t":["நீண்டொலி அழுவம் குறைய முகந்துகொண்டு,","ஈண்டுசெலல் கொண்மூ வேண்டுவயின் குழீஇப்","பெருமலை யன்ன தோன்றுதல், சூன்முதிர்பு,","உரும்உரறு கருவியொடு, பெயல்கடன் இறுத்து,","வள்மலை மாறிய என்றூழ்க் காலை,","மன்பதை யெல்லாம் சென்றுணர், கங்கைக்","கரைபொரு மலிநீர் நிறைந்து தோன்றியாங்கு,","எமக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் செம்மலை யாகலின்,","‘அன்பில் ஆடவர் கொன்று, ஆறு கவரச்,","சென்று தலைவருந அல்ல, அன்பின்று,","வன்கலை தெவிட்டும், அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர்க்கு,","இற்றை நாளொடும் யாண்டுதலைப் பெயர்’ எனக்","கண் பொறி போகிய கசிவொடு, உரன்அழிந்து,","அருந்துயர் உழக்கும்என் பெருந்துன் புறுவி நின்","தாள்படு செல்வம் காண்டொறும் மருளப்,","பனைமருள் தடக்கை யொடு முத்துப்படு முற்றிய","உயர்மருப்பு ஏந்திய வரைமருள் நோன்பகடு,","ஒளிதிகழ் ஓடை பொலிய, மருங்கில்","படுமணி இரட்ட, ஏறிச் செம்மாந்து,","செலல்நசைஇ உற்றனென்-விறல்மிகு குருசில்!","இன்மை துரப்ப, இசைதர வந்து, நின்","வண்மையில் தொடுத்தஎன் நயந்தினை கேண்மதி!","வல்லினும், வல்லேன் ஆயினும், வல்லே,","என்அளந்து அறிந்தனை நோக்காது, சிறந்த","நின் அளந்து அறிமதி, பெரும! என்றும்","வேந்தர் நாணப் பெயர்வேன்; சாந்தருந்திப்","பல்பொறிக் கொண்ட ஏந்துஎழில் அகலம்","மாண்இழை மகளிர் புல்லுதொறும் புகல,","நாள்முரசு இரங்கும் இடனுடை வரைப்பின்நின்","தாள்நிழல் வாழ்நர் நண்கலம் மிகுப்ப,","வாள் அமர் உயர்ந்தநின் தானையும்,","சீர்மிகு செல்வமும் ஏந்துகம் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Perunchiththiranar tells Kumanan, who is a height for him and for others as the clouds that gather from the sea fill the Ganga in the hot season, that his wife, sure he has died on the robber-haunted waste a year ago, should be dazed by the wealth of Kumanan's feet, so he asks to ride home on a tall tusked elephant with face-plate and bells, making kings ashamed, and asks Kumanan to measure him by his own greatness rather than by the bard's skill.","n":"The wife's lament is quoted inside the petition (lines 9 to 12): the size of the gift is set by her belief that he is dead. What he asks for is a spectacle, an elephant to ride home on so that ungiving kings are shamed, and the closing turn (do not measure me, measure yourself) puts the burden of worth on the giver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","inmai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 161","lb":"PN161","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",2,"pira","simile","cloud"],["உரும்உரறு","உரும்",4,"pira","simile","thunder"],["வன்கலை","கலை",11,"maa","scene","stag"],["பனைமருள்","பனை",16,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["முத்துப்படு","முத்து",16,"pira","scene",""],["நோன்பகடு","பகடு",17,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["சாந்தருந்திப்","சாந்து",26,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["நாள்முரசு","முரசு",29,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["river","கங்கைக்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:162":{"book":"purananuru","num":162,"t":["இரவலர் புரவலை நீயும் அல்லை!","புரவலர் இரவலர்க்கு இல்லையும் அல்லர்;","இரவலர் உண்மையும் காண்,இனி; இரவலர்க்கு","ஈவோர் உண்மையும் காண், இனி; நின்ஊர்க்","கடுமரம் வருந்தத் தந்து யாம் பிணித்த","நெடுநல் யானை எம் பரிசில்;","கடுமான் தோன்றல்! செல்வல் யானே."],"p":"","r":"Perunchiththiranar tells Ilaveliman that he is not the only patron of suppliants and that patrons are not lacking to them: let him see that askers exist and givers exist; the tall elephant the poet has tied to the guard tree of Ilaveliman's own town, straining it, is the poet's gift, and he is leaving.","n":"Tying an elephant to a chief's guard tree is what a conqueror does to an enemy; the bard does it with an elephant got elsewhere, so the leave-taking is a rebuke. The colophon's other poems by the same poet suggest the elephant was Kumanan's gift, but this poem does not say whose it was.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","parisil","maanam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 162","lb":"PN162","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"இளவெளிமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கடுமரம்","கடிமரம்",5,"maram","scene","guard tree"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["கடுமான்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:163":{"book":"purananuru","num":163,"t":["நின் நயந்து உறைநர்க்கும், நீ நயந்து உறைநர்க்கும்,","பன்மாண் கற்பின்நின் கிளைமுத லோர்க்கும்,","கடும்பின் கடும்பசி தீர யாழநின்","நெடுங்குறி எதிர்ப்பை நல்கி யோர்க்கும்,","இன்னோர்க்கு என்னாது, என்னோடும் சூழாது,","வல்லாங்கு வாழ்தும் என்னாது, நீயும்","எல்லோர்க்கும் கொடுமதி-மனைகிழ வோயே!","பழந்தூங்கு முதிரத்துக் கிழவன்","திருந்துவேல் குமணன் நல்கிய வளனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells his wife to give the wealth that Kumanan of the sharp spear, lord of Muthiram where fruit hangs heavy, has granted to everyone, to those who love her and those she loves, to her kin of manifold virtue and to those who lent to ease the family's hunger, without asking whom to favour, without consulting him and without thinking of saving to live well.","n":"The gift begged for the starving household is turned out of the house at once: the bard's wife becomes a giver in her own right, and the poem ends by naming the patron, so the redistribution itself is the praise. நெடுங்குறி எதிர்ப்பை (line 4) seems to mean a loan given on a long promise of return; யாழ in line 3 is a particle, not the lute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ilvaazhkkai","parisil","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 163","lb":"PN163","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"புலவரின் மனைவி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பழந்தூங்கு","பழம்",8,"maram","scene","fruit"]],"en":[["hill","முதிரத்துக்",8],["patron","குமணன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:164":{"book":"purananuru","num":164,"t":["ஆடுநனி மறந்த கோடுஉயர் அடுப்பின்","ஆம்பி பூப்பத், தேம்புபசி உழவாப்,","பாஅல் இன்மையின் தோலொடு திரங்கி,","இல்லி தூர்த்த பொல்லா வறுமுலை","சுவைத்தொறும் அழூஉம்தன் மகத்துவம் நோக்கி,","நீரொடு நிறைந்த ஈர்இதழ் மழைக்கண்என்","மனையோள் எவ்வம் நோக்கி, நினைஇ,","நிற்படர்ந் திசினே-நற்போர்க் குமண!","என்நிலை அறிந்தனை யாயின், இந்நிலைத்","தொடுத்தும் கொள்ளாது அமையலென்-அடுக்கிய","பண்ணமை நரம்பின் பச்சை நல்யாழ்,","மண்ணமை முழவின், வயிரியர்","இன்மை தீர்க்குங் குடிப்பிறந் தோயே."],"p":"","r":"Perunthalai Saththanar tells Kumanan that, seeing his hearth so long unused that mushrooms bloom in it, his son crying at each suck of a breast shrivelled and dry from hunger, and his wife's rain-dark eyes brimming, he has come to him, and that now Kumanan knows his state he will not rest without pressing his claim on him, born of a clan that ends the want of drummers and lute players.","n":"The petition is made of the body: the cold hearth, the dry breast with its closed ducts, the wife's tears. The last lines ground the claim not on Kumanan's mood but on his lineage, which is bound to end the want of performers, so the bard asks as of right and says he will hold the patron to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","pasi","parisil","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 164","lb":"PN164","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["ஆம்பி","ஆம்பி",2,"pira","scene","mushroom"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",11,"yaazh","scene","lute string"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",11,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",12,"parai","scene",""],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",12,"seythi","scene","performers"]],"en":[["patron","குமண",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:165":{"book":"purananuru","num":165,"t":["மன்னா உலகத்து மன்னுதல் குறித்தோர்","தம்புகழ் நிறீஇத் தாமாய்ந் தனரே;","துன்னரும் சிறப்பின் உயர்ந்த செல்வர்,","இன்மையின் இரப்போர்க்கு ஈஇ யாமையின்,","தொன்மை மாக்களின் தொடர்பு அறியலரே;","தாள்தாழ் படுமணி இரட்டும், பூனுதல்,","ஆடியல் யானை பாடுநர்க்கு அருகாக்","கேடில் நல்லிசை வயமான் தோன்றலைப்","பாடி நின்றெனன் ஆகக்,‘கொன்னே","பாடுபெறு பரிசிலன் வாடினன் பெயர்தல் என்","நாடுஇழந் ததனினும் நனிஇன் னாது’ என,","வாள்தந் தனனே, தலை எனக்கு ஈயத்,","தன்னிற் சிறந்தது பிறிதுஒன்று இன்மையின்;","ஆடுமலி உவகையோடு வருவல்,","ஓடாப் பூட்கைநிற் கிழமையோன் கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Those who wished to last in an unlasting world set up their fame and died, and the very rich who give nothing to the needy are not remembered like the men of old; the poet sang a lord whose elephants never failed singers, and he, saying that a singer going home empty would grieve him more than the loss of his country, gave the poet his sword so that he might take his head, having nothing dearer than himself; with that triumph the poet now comes, having seen the addressee's kinsman of unretreating resolve.","n":"The argument runs from a maxim (only fame lasts) through its negative (the ungiving rich are forgotten) to the extreme case, a giver who has lost his land and offers his own head. The closing joy is addressed to the giver's kinsman, for whom that head has a price; the poem names neither man, and the story that the kinsman is the brother who drove Kumanan out comes from the colophon tradition, not the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","nilaiyaamai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 165","lb":"PN165","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"குமணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["பாடுநர்க்கு","பாடுநர்",7,"seythi","scene","singers"],["வயமான்","மா",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:166":{"book":"purananuru","num":166,"t":["நன் றாய்ந்த நீள் நிமிர்சடை","முது முதல்வன் வாய் போகாது,","ஒன்று புரிந்த ஈரி ரண்டின்,","ஆறுணர்ந்த ஒரு முதுநூல்","இகல் கண்டோர் மிகல் சாய்மார்,","மெய் அன்ன பொய் உணர்ந்து,","பொய் ஓராது மெய் கொளீஇ,","மூவேழ் துறைபும் முட்டின்று போகிய","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் உரவோர் மருக!","வினைக்கு வேண்டி நீ பூண்ட","புலப் புல்வாய்க் கலைப் பச்சை","சுவல் பூண்ஞான் மிசைப் பொலிய;","மறம் கடிந்த அருங் கற்பின்,","அறம் புகழ்ந்த வலை சூடிச்,","சிறு நுதல், பேர் அகல் அல்குல்,","சில சொல்லின் பல கூந்தல், நின்","நிலைக் கொத்தநின் துணைத் துணைவியர்","தமக்கு அமைந்த தொழில் கேட்பக்;","காடு என்றா நாடுஎன்று ஆங்கு","ஈரேழின் இடம் முட்டாது,","நீர் நாண நெய் வழங்கியும்,","எண் நாணப் பல வேட்டும்,","மண் நாணப் புகழ் பரப்பியும்,","அருங் கடிப் பெருங் காலை,","விருந்து உற்றநின் திருந்து ஏந்துநிலை,","என்றும், காண்கதில் அம்ம, யாமே! குடாஅது","பொன்படு நெடுவரைப் புயல்ஏறு சிலைப்பின்,","பூவிரி புதுநீர்க் காவிரி புரக்கும்","தண்புனற் படப்பை எம்மூர் ஆங்கண்,","உண்டும் தின்றும் ஊர்ந்தும் ஆடுகம்;","செல்வல் அத்தை யானே; செல்லாது,","மழைஅண் ணாப்ப நீடிய நெடுவரைக்","கழைவளர் இமயம்போல,","நிலீஇயர் அத்தை, நீ நிலமிசை யானே?"],"p":"","r":"Avur Mulankizhar praises Vinnanthayan, descendant of learned men who, keeping to the word of the ancient matted-haired First One, mastered the one old book of four parts and six limbs, broke the pride of disputants by telling false that looks true from truth, and completed the twenty-one sacrifices; wearing the black-buck skin for the rite, with his wives of chaste conduct doing their parts, he has poured ghee more than water, made sacrifices past counting and spread his fame wider than the earth; the poet wishes always to see him so, receiving guests, and says he will go home to his town on the Kaveri to eat and enjoy, while the brahmin should stand on earth like the bamboo-grown Himalaya.","n":"The victory in this vaakai is not in war: it lies in debate and in the count of sacrifices, and the poem counts in riddles (one aim, four, six, three times seven). The bard's closing wish to go home and eat makes the sacrificer a patron like any chief. Several phrases (அறம் புகழ்ந்த வலை சூடி, துணைத் துணைவியர்) are obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedham","virundhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 166","lb":"PN166","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங் கிழார்","pat":"சோணாட்டுப் பூஞ்சாற்றூர்ப் பார்ப்பான் கௌணியன் விண்ணந்தாயன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"பார்பபன வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பார்ப்பன வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கல்வி"]],"k":[["முதல்வன்","முதல்வன்",2,"deyvam","scene","the First One"],["கலைப்","கலை",11,"maa","ornament","black buck"],["நெய்","நெய்",21,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["புயல்ஏறு","ஏறு",27,"pira","scene","thunder"],["கழைவளர்","கழை",33,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",28],["hill","இமயம்",33]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:167":{"book":"purananuru","num":167,"t":["நீயே, அமர்காணின் அமர்கடந்து, அவர்","படை விலக்கி எதிர் நிற்றலின்,","வாஅள் வாய்த்த வடுவாழ் யாக்கை யொடு,","கேள்விக்கு இனியை, கட்கின் னாயே!","அவரே, நிற்காணின் புறங் கொடுத்தலின்,","ஊறுஅறியா மெய் யாக்கை யொடு.","கண்ணுக்கு இனியர்; செவிக்குஇன் னாரே!","அதனால்,நீயும் ஒன்று இனியை;அவரும்ஒன்றுஇனியர்;","ஒவ்வா யாவுள, மற்றே? வெல்போர்க்","கழல்புனை திருந்தடிக் கடுமான் கிள்ளி!","நின்னை வியக்குமிவ் வுலகம்; அஃது","என்னோ? பெரும! உரைத்திசின் எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Killi of the swift horse that, since he stands against the enemy's army and wins, his sword-scarred body is sweet to hear of and harsh to see, while his enemies, who turn their backs at the sight of him, have unhurt bodies sweet to see and harsh to hear of; each is sweet in one way, so what is there to marvel at in him that the world marvels so?","n":"The praise is a riddle in the senses: the king's wounds belong to the ear (fame), the enemies' whole skins to the eye, and the closing question pretends there is nothing to wonder at when the whole poem has just said what it is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 167","lb":"PN167","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சிலூர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"சோழன் கடுமான் கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கடுமான்","மா",10,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:168":{"book":"purananuru","num":168,"t":["அருவி ஆர்க்குங் கழைபயில் நனந்தலைக்","கறிவளர் அடுக்கத்து மலரந்த காந்தள்","கொழுங்கிழங்கு மிளிரக் கிண்டிக், கிளையொடு,","கடுங்கண் கேழல் உழுத பூழி,","நன்னாள் வருபதம் நோக்கிக், குறவர்","உழாஅது வித்திய பரூஉக்குரற் சிறுதினை","முந்துவிளை யாணர் நாள்புதிது உண்மார்,","மரையான் கறந்த நுரைகொள் தீம்பால்,","மான்தடி புழுக்கிய புலவுநாறு குழிசி","வான்கேழ் இரும்புடை கழாஅது, ஏற்றிச்,","சாந்த விறகின் உவித்த புன்கம்,","கூதளங் கவினிய குளவி முன்றில்,","செழுங்கோள் வாழை அகல்இலைப் பகுக்கும்","ஊராக் குதிரைக் கிழவ! கூர்வேல்,","நறைநார்த் தொடுத்த வேங்கையங் கண்ணி,","வடிநவில் அம்பின் வில்லோர் பெரும!","கைவள் ஈகைக் கடுமான் கொற்ற!","வையக வரைப்பில் தமிழகம் கேட்பப்,","பொய்யாச் செந்நா நெளிய ஏத்திப்","பாடுப என்ப பரிசிலர், நாளும்;","ஈயா மன்னர் நாண,","வீயாது பரந்தநின் வசையில் வான் புகழே!"],"p":"","r":"On the slopes of Kotran's hill, where boars dig up kanthal tubers and the kuravar sow millet in the turned earth without ploughing, then cook the first new grain with wild-cow milk and venison in an unwashed pot over sandalwood and share it on banana leaves in yards fragrant with wild jasmine, the bards, it is said, praise this lord of the unridden horse, chief of bowmen with vengai garlands and generous hands, every day across the Tamil land, so that his unfading fame shames kings who do not give.","n":"The long kurinji scene praises before the chief is named: a land that feeds without the plough and a meal shared on leaves are the image of his giving. ஊராக் குதிரை, the horse no one rides, reads as the name of his hill (Kuthirai), a riddle on his title.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 168","lb":"PN168","poet":"கருவூர்க் கந்தப்பிள்ளைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"பிட்டங் கொற்றன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்; இயன்மொழியும், அரச வாகையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்","இயன்மொழி","அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கழைபயில்","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கறிவளர்","கறி",2,"maram","scene","pepper"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","scene",""],["கொழுங்கிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",3,"maram","scene","tuber"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",4,"maa","scene","boar"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["சிறுதினை","தினை",6,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["மரையான்","மரையா",8,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",8,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["மான்தடி","மான் தடி",9,"unavu","gift_food","venison"],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",11,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["புன்கம்","புன்கம்",11,"unavu","gift_food","cooked grain"],["கூதளங்","கூதளம்",12,"maram","scene",""],["குளவி","குளவி",12,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["வாழை","வாழை",13,"maram","scene","banana"],["வேங்கையங்","வேங்கை",15,"maram","ornament",""],["கடுமான்","மா",17,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["hill","குதிரைக்",14],["patron","கொற்ற",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:169":{"book":"purananuru","num":169,"t":["நும்படை செல்லுங் காலை, அவர்படை","எறித்தெறி தானை முன்னரை எனாஅ,","அவர்படை வருஉங் காலை, நும்படைக்","கூழை தாங்கிய, அகல் யாற்றுக்","குன்று விலங்கு சிறையின் நின்றனை எனாஅ,","அரிதால், பெரும! நின் செவ்வி என்றும்;","பெரிதால் அத்தை, என் கடும்பினது இடும்பை;","இன்னே விடுமதி பரிசில்! வென்வேல்","இளம்பல் கோசர் விளங்குபடை கன்மார்,","இகலினர் எறிந்த அகல்இலை முருக்கின்","பெருமரக் கம்பம் போலப்,","பொருநர்க்கு உலையாநின் வலன் வாழியவே!"],"p":"","r":"Since, when your army advances, you are in its van, and when the enemy comes, you hold the rear of your army like a hill across a wide river, the poet tells the chief, a moment of your leisure is always hard to find, but my family's distress is great, so give the gift now; and may your strength, unshaken by foes like the great murukku post at which the young Kosar of victorious spears practise their throws, live long.","n":"The praise is also the excuse: because the chief is always at the front or the rear of battle, the bard cannot wait for the proper moment (செவ்வி) and asks at once. The Kosar's target post makes his strength something that takes every blow and does not fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","maram","inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 169","lb":"PN169","poet":"காவிரிபூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"பிட்டங்கொற்றன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",10,"maram","simile","murukku tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:170":{"book":"purananuru","num":170,"t":["மரைபிரித்து உண்ட நெல்லி வேலிப்,","பரலுடை முன்றில், அங்குடிச் சீறூர்,","எல்அடிப் படுத்த, கல்லாக் காட்சி","வில்லுழுது உண்மார் நாப்பண், ஒல்லென,","இழிபிறப் பாளன் கருங்கை சிவப்ப,","வலிதுரந்து சிலைக்கும் வன்கண் கடுந்துடி,","புலிதுஞ்சு நெடுவரைக் குடிஞையோடு இரட்டும்","மலைகெழு நாடன் கூர்வேல் பிட்டன்,","குறுகல் ஓம்புமின், தெவ்விர்; அவனே","சிறுகண் யானை வெண்கோடு பயந்த","ஒளிதிகழ் முத்தம் விறலியர்க்கு ஈந்து,","நார்பிழிக் கொண்ட வெங்கள் தேறல்","பண்அமை நல்யாழ்ப் பாண்கடும்பு அருத்தி,","நசைவர்க்கு மென்மை அல்லது, பகைவர்க்கு","இரும்புபயன் படுக்குங் கருங்கைக் கொல்லன்","விசைத்துஎறி கூடமொடு பொருஉம்","உலைக்கல் அன்ன, வல்லா ளன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The poet warns the enemy to keep clear of Pittan of the sharp spear, lord of a hill country of little villages with gooseberry fences stripped by wild cows and pebbled yards, where among bowmen who live by the bow the drum of the low-born drummer, struck till his dark hand reddens, echoes the owl in tiger-haunted heights; he gives the dancing women pearls from elephants' tusks and feeds the bards strong toddy, gentle to those who long for him, but to his foes he is like the anvil stone that takes the blows of the dark-handed smith's hammer.","n":"The simile turns on the anvil: what bears the hammer is what shapes the iron, so the enemy who strikes him breaks himself. The poem names the drummer by his low birth (இழிபிறப்பாளன்), a rare plain statement of rank in the hill village.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 170","lb":"PN170","poet":"உறையூர் மருத்துவன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"பிட்டங்கொற்றன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை; தானை மறமும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை","தானை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மரைபிரித்து","மரை",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",1,"maram","scene","gooseberry"],["வில்லுழுது","வில்லோர்",4,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["இழிபிறப்","இழிபிறப்பாளன்",5,"seythi","scene","drummer"],["கடுந்துடி","துடி",6,"parai","scene",""],["புலிதுஞ்சு","புலி",7,"maa","scene","tiger"],["குடிஞையோடு","குடிஞை",7,"pul","scene","owl"],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முத்தம்","முத்து",11,"pira","gift_food","pearl"],["விறலியர்க்கு","விறலி",11,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",12,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",13,"yaazh","scene",""],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",15,"seythi","simile","smith"],["உலைக்கல்","உலைக்கல்",17,"pira","simile","anvil"]],"en":[["patron","பிட்டன்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:171":{"book":"purananuru","num":171,"t":["இன்று செலினுந் தருமே; சிறுவரை","நின்று செலினுந் தருமே ; பின்னும்,","‘முன்னே தந்தனென்’ என்னாது, துன்னி","வைகலும் செலினும், பொய்யலன் ஆகி,","யாம்வேண்டி யாங்குஎம் வறுங்கலம் நிறைப்போன்;","தான்வேண்டி யாங்குத் தன்இறை உவப்ப","அருந்தொழில் முடியரோ, திருந்துவேல் கொற்றன்;","இனமலி கதச்சேக் களனொடு வேண்டினும்,","களமலி நெல்லின் குப்பை வேண்டினும்,","அருங்கலம் களிற்றொடு வேண்டினும், பெருந்தகை","பிறர்க்கும் அன்ன அறத்தகை யன்னே,","அன்னன் ஆகலின், எந்தை உள்ளடி","முள்ளும் நோவ உற்றாக தில்ல!","ஈவோர் அரியஇவ் உலகத்து,","வாழ்வோர் வாழ, அவன் தாள் வாழியவே!"],"p":"","r":"Whether we go today, after a short while, or day after day, Kotran gives, never saying he gave before, and fills our empty vessels as we wish; may he finish the hard work his overlord wants; whether one asks for herds of fierce bulls with their stalls, heaps of threshed paddy or jewels with elephants, he gives, and gives to others as well; so may not even a thorn hurt the sole of our lord's foot, and in this world where givers are rare may his feet live so that those who live may live.","n":"The giver is measured by repetition: going daily and being met as on the first day. The wish that his overlord's work be done ties the bards' gifts to the chief's service of his king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 171","lb":"PN171","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"பிட்டங்கொற்றன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["கதச்சேக்","சே",8,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",9,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",10,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["முள்ளும்","முள்",13,"maram","scene","thorn"]],"en":[["patron","கொற்றன்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:172":{"book":"purananuru","num":172,"t":["ஏற்றுக உலையே; ஆக்குக சோறே;","கள்ளும் குறைபடல் ஓம்புக; ஒள்ளிழைப்","பாடுவல் விறலியர் கோதையும் புனைக;","அன்னவை பலவும் செய்க ; என்னதூஉம்","பரியல் வெண்டா வருபதம் நாடி,","ஐவனங் காவல் பெய்தீ நந்தின்.","ஒளிதிகழ் திருந்துமணி நளியிருள் அகற்றும்","வன்புல நாடன், வயமான் பிட்டன்;","ஆரமர் கடக்கும் வேலும், அவனிறை","மாவள் ஈகைக் கோதையும்.","மாறுகொள் மன்னரும், வாழியர் நெடிதே!"],"p":"","r":"Set the pot on the fire, cook the rice, see the toddy does not run short, let the dancing women with bright jewels put on garlands, do all such things without worrying about the next meal, for Pittan of the strong horses lives, lord of the hard country where the fire set to guard the hill paddy dies down and bright gems drive off the thick dark; may his spear that wins hard battles, the great giving of his overlord Kothai, and even the hostile kings live long.","n":"The blessing takes in the enemy kings: while they live, Pittan has wars to win and spoils to give, so the feast in the first lines depends on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 172","lb":"PN172","poet":"வடமண்ணக்கன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"பிட்டங்கொற்றன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["சோறே","சோறு",1,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["கள்ளும்","கள்",2,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",3,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["கோதையும்","கோதை",3,"maram","ornament","garland"],["ஐவனங்","ஐவனம்",6,"unavu","scene","hill paddy"],["திருந்துமணி","மணி",7,"pira","scene","gem"],["வயமான்","மா",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","பிட்டன்",8],["king","கோதை",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:173":{"book":"purananuru","num":173,"t":["யான்வாழும் நாளும் பண்ணன் வாழிய!","பாணர் ! காண்க, இவன் கடும்பினது இடும்பை;","யாணர்ப் பழுமரம் புள்இமிழ்ந் தன்ன","ஊணொலி அரவந் தானும் கேட்கும்;","பொய்யா எழிலி பெய்விடம் நோக்கி","முட்டை கொண்டு வற்புலம் சேரும்","சிறுநுண் எறும்பின் சில்லொழுக்கு ஏய்ப்பச்,","சோறுடைக் கையர் வீறுவீறு இயங்கும்","இருங்கிளைச் சிறாஅர்க் காண்டும்; கண்டும்,","மற்றும் மற்றும் வினவுதும், தெற்றெனப்;","பசிப்பிணி மருத்துவன் இல்லம்","அணித்தோ? சேய்த்தோ? கூறுமின், எமக்கே,"],"p":"","r":"May Pannan live as many days as I live; bards, see the trouble of his household: the noise of eating sounds like birds clamouring in a fruiting tree, and children of his large kin go about with rice in their hands like the thin line of tiny ants that carry their eggs to high ground before sure rain; we see it, and yet we ask again and again: is the house of the physician of hunger near or far? Tell us.","n":"The speaker is a Chola king who wishes his own years on a small-village chief and plays a lost suppliant asking the way; the question is feigned, since the crowds with rice in hand already show the road. The hardship (இடும்பை) of the household is its press of guests.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","virundhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 173","lb":"PN173","poet":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","pat":"சிறுகுடி கிழான் பண்ணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","scene","bards"],["பழுமரம்","பழுமரம்",3,"maram","simile","fruiting tree"],["புள்இமிழ்ந்","புள்",3,"pul","simile",""],["எழிலி","எழிலி",5,"pira","simile","cloud"],["முட்டை","முட்டை",6,"pira","simile","eggs"],["எறும்பின்","எறும்பு",7,"pira","simile","ant"],["சோறுடைக்","சோறு",8,"unavu","gift_food","rice"]],"en":[["patron","பண்ணன்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:174":{"book":"purananuru","num":174,"t":["அணங்குடை அவுணர் கணம்கொண்டுஒளித்தெனச்,","சேண்விளங்கு சிறப்பின் ஞாயிறு காணாது,","இருள்கண் கெடுத்த பருதி ஞாலத்து","இடும்பைகொள் பருவரல் தீரக், கடுந்திறல்","அஞ்சன் உருவன் தந்து நிறுத்தாங்கு,","அர சிழந்து இருந்த அல்லற் காலை,","முரசுஎழுந்து இரங்கும் முற்றமொடு, கரைபொருது","இரங்குபுனல் நெரிதரு மிகுபெருங் காவிரி","மல்லல் நன்னாட்டு அல்லல் தீரப்,","பொய்யா நாவிற் கபிலன் பாடிய,","மையணி நெடுவரை ஆங்கண் ஒய்யெனச்","செருப்புகல் மறவர் செல்புறம் கண்ட","எள்ளறு சிறப்பின் முள்ளூர் மீமிசை,","அருவழி இருந்த பெருவிறல் வளவன்","மதிமருள் வெண்குடை காட்டி, அக்குடை","புதுமையின் நிறுத்த புகழ்மேம் படுந!","விடர்ப்புலி பொறித்த கோட்டைச், சுடர்ப் பூண்,","சுரும்பார் கண்ணிப், பெரும்பெயர் நும்முன்","ஈண்டுச்செய் நல்வினை யாண்டுச்சென்று உணீஇயர்,","உயர்ந்தோர் உலகத்துப் பெயர்ந்தனன் ஆகலின்,","ஆறுகொள் மருங்கின் மாதிரம் துழவும்","கவலை நெஞ்சத்து அவலந் தீர","நீதோன் றினையே-நிரைத்தார் அண்ணல்!","கல்கண் பொடியக், கானம் வெம்ப","மல்குநீர் வரைப்பில் கயம்பல உணங்கக்,","கோடை நீடிய பைதறு காலை,","இருநிலம் நெளிய ஈண்டி,","உரும்உரறு கருவிய மழைபொழிந் தாங்கே!"],"p":"","r":"As the god of dark hue brought back the sun when the asuras hid it and the world lay in darkness, so, when the rich Kaveri country had lost its king, the chief showed the moon-white umbrella of the great Valavan, who had stayed in the hard refuge on Mullur, the hill truthful-tongued Kapilan sang, and set it up anew; and now that his famed elder has gone to the world above to enjoy the good he did here, he has appeared to end the grief of hearts searching every road, like thunder-rain that pours after a long summer has cracked the stones, scorched the forest and dried the ponds.","n":"The poem cites an earlier poem: Mullur is the hill Kapilan sang, so the chief's house carries a fame already made in verse. Both similes (the sun restored, rain after drought) are about restoring what was lost, once for the Chola kingdom, once for the chief's own people.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh","mazhai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 174","lb":"PN174","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"மலையமான் சோழிய வேனாதி திருக்கண்ணன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"அரச வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["அரச வாகை"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["அவுணர்","அவுணர்",1,"deyvam","simile","asuras"],["அஞ்சன்","அஞ்சன் உருவன்",5,"deyvam","simile","the dark god"],["விடர்ப்புலி","புலி",17,"maa","ornament","tiger emblem"],["சுரும்பார்","சுரும்பு",18,"pira","scene","bee"],["கயம்பல","கயம்",25,"pira","simile","ponds"],["மழைபொழிந்","மழை",28,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",8],["hill","முள்ளூர்",13],["king","வளவன்",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:175":{"book":"purananuru","num":175,"t":["எந்தை; வாழி; ஆதனுங்க ! என்","நெஞ்சம் திறப்போர் நிற்காண் குவரே;","நின்யான் மறப்பின், மறக்குங் காலை,","என்உயிர் யாக்கையிற் பிரியும் பொழுதும்,","என்யான் மறப்பின், மறக்குவென் - வென்வேல்","விண்பொரு நெடுங்குடைக் கொடித்தேர் மோரியர்","திண்கதிர்த் திகிரி திரிதரக் குறைத்த","உலக இடைகழி அறைவாய் நிலைஇய","மலர்வாய் மண்டிலத் தன்ன, நாளும்","பலர்புரவு எதிர்ந்த அறத்துறை நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"My lord Athanungan, those who open my heart will find you there; if ever I forget you, it will be when I forget myself, when life leaves my body, you who every day take up the protection of many, like the wide sun's orb that stands at the rock pass the Moriyar, with their sky-touching umbrellas and bannered chariots, cut for the wheels of their chariots to roll through.","n":"Gratitude is made bodily (open my heart and you are there) and is bounded by life itself, not by any later birth. The Mauryan road cut through the rock serves as a figure for what stays fixed and open to all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","nanri","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 175","lb":"PN175","poet":"கள்ளில் ஆத்திரையனார்","pat":"ஆதனுங்கன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["patron","ஆதனுங்க",1],["king","மோரியர்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:176":{"book":"purananuru","num":176,"t":["ஓரைஆயத்து ஒண்தொடி மகளிர்","கேழல் உழுத இருஞ்சேறு கிளைப்பின்,","யாமை ஈன்ற புலவுநாறு முட்டையைத்","தேன்நாறு ஆம்பல் கிழங்கொடு பெறூஉம்,","இழுமென ஒலிக்கும் புனலம் புதவின்,","பெருமா விலங்கைத் தலைவன், சீறியாழ்","இல்லோர் சொன்மலை நல்லியக் கோடனை","உடையை வாழி, யெற் புணர்ந்த பாலே!","பாரி பறம்பிற் பனிச்சுனைத் தெண்ணீர்","ஓரூர் உண்மையின் இகழ்ந்தோர் போலக்,","காணாது கழிந்த வைகல், காணா","வழிநாட்கு இரங்கும், என் நெஞ்சம்-அவன்","கழிமென் சாயல் காண்தொறும் நினைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Blessed be the fate that joined me to Nalliyakkodan, lord of great Ilangai with its loud sluices, where girls at play scratching the mud a boar has ploughed find a turtle's rank-smelling eggs with honey-scented lily roots, a giver to those who lack even a small lute; my heart grieves for the days I let pass without seeing him, like people who scorned the cool clear springs of Pari's Parambu because they were in their own town, and thinks of him each time it sees his gentle nature.","n":"The regret is for a giver who was always near: the springs of Parambu were proverbial for never failing, and their very constancy is why those beside them did not value them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","eegai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 176","lb":"PN176","poet":"புறத்திணை நன்னாகனார்","pat":"ஓய்மான் நல்லியக் கோடான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",2,"maa","scene","boar"],["யாமை","யாமை",3,"pira","scene","turtle"],["முட்டையைத்","முட்டை",3,"pira","scene","eggs"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",4,"maram","scene","water lily"],["கிழங்கொடு","கிழங்கு",4,"maram","scene","lily root"],["சீறியாழ்","யாழ்",6,"yaazh","scene",""],["பனிச்சுனைத்","சுனை",9,"pira","simile","spring"]],"en":[["town","பெருமா விலங்கைத்",6],["patron","நல்லியக் கோடனை",7],["patron","பாரி",9],["hill","பறம்பிற்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:177":{"book":"purananuru","num":177,"t":["ஒளிறுவாள் மன்னர் ஒண்சுடர் நெடுநகர்,","வெளிறுகண் போகப் பன்னாள் திரங்கிப்,","பாடிப் பெற்ற பொன்னணி யானை,","தமர்எனின், யாவரும் புகுப; அமர்எனின்,","திங்களும் நுழையா எந்திரப் படுபுழைக்,","கண்மாறு நீட்ட நணிநணி இருந்த","குறும்பல் குறும்பின் ததும்ப வைகிப்,","புளிச்சுவை வேட்ட செங்கண் ஆடவர்","தீம்புளிக் களாவொடு துடரி முனையின்,","மட்டுஅறல் நல்யாற்று எக்கர் ஏறிக்,","கருங்கனி நாவல் இருந்துகொய்து உண்ணும்,","பெரும்பெயர் ஆதி, பிணங்கரில் குடநாட்டு,","எயினர் தந்த எய்ம்மான் எறிதசைப்","பைஞ்ஞிணம் பெருத்த பசுவெள் அமலை,","வருநர்க்கு வரையாது தருவனர் சொரிய,","இரும்பனங் குடையின் மிசையும்","பெரும்புலர் வைகறைச் சீர்சா லாதே."],"p":"","r":"The gold-decked elephant a bard wins by singing, after waiting many lean days at the bright towns of sword-bearing kings, is not worth as much as the meal at great dawn in the Kudanadu of Athi, whose men, tired of sour kalaa and thudari berries, climb the river's sandbank to pick black jambu fruit, and who, among clustered little forts that let in all who are friends and not even the moonlight if foes, has the white rice thick with the fat of porcupine meat brought by the Eyinar poured without stint for all comers, eaten from big palm-leaf cups.","n":"The comparison runs against the bard's own trade: the king's elephant, won after waiting and hunger, is worth less than a hill chief's rice given at once to anyone who comes. The forts that open to friends and shut even the moon to foes give his hospitality its edge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 177","lb":"PN177","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"மல்லி கிழான் காரியாதி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["களாவொடு","களா",9,"maram","gift_food",""],["துடரி","துடரி",9,"maram","gift_food",""],["நாவல்","நாவல்",11,"maram","gift_food","jambu"],["எயினர்","எயினர்",13,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["எய்ம்மான்","எய்ம்மான்",13,"maa","gift_food","porcupine"],["அமலை","அமலை",14,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["இரும்பனங்","பனை",16,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[["patron","ஆதி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:178":{"book":"purananuru","num":178,"t":["கந்துமுனிந்து உயிர்க்கும்யானையொடு,பணைமுனிந்து,","கால்இயற் புரவி ஆலும் ஆங்கண்,","மணல்மலி முற்றம் புக்க சான்றோர்","உண்ணார் ஆயினும், தன்னொடு சூளுற்று","உண்மென இரக்கும் பெரும்பெயர்ச் சாத்தன்","ஈண்டோர் இன்சா யலனே ; வேண்டார்","எறிபடை மயங்கிய வெருவரு ஞாட்பின்,","கள்ளுடைக் கலத்தர் உள்ளூர்க் கூறிய","நெடுமொழி மறந்த சிறுபே ராளர்","அஞ்சி நீங்கும் காலை,","ஏம மாகத் தான்முந் துறுமே."],"p":"","r":"Saththan of great name, in whose sanded yard elephants weary of the post sigh and horses tire of the stable, swears an oath with the worthy who enter and begs them to eat even when they will not; but he is not only gentle here: in the fearful battle where the weapons of foes are tangled, when the little men who made tall boasts in their own villages with cups of toddy forget them and run in fear, he goes forward and becomes their shelter.","n":"The poem sets the oath he swears to make guests eat beside the boasts the toddy drinkers forget: one man's word at home is kept in battle, the others' is not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","virundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 178","lb":"PN178","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் கீரஞ்சாத்தன் பாண்டிக் குதிரைச் சாக்கையன் எனவும் பாடம்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["யானையொடு","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புரவி","புரவி",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["மணல்மலி","மணல்",3,"pira","scene","sand"],["கள்ளுடைக்","கள்",8,"unavu","scene","toddy"]],"en":[["patron","சாத்தன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:179":{"book":"purananuru","num":179,"t":["ஞாலம் மீமிசை வள்ளியோர் மாய்ந் தென,","ஏலாது கவிழ்ந்தஎன் இரவல் மண்டை","மலர்ப்போர் யார்?’ என வினவலின் மலைந்தோர்","விசிபிணி முரசமொடு மண்பல தந்த","திருவீழ் நுண்பூண் பாண்டியன் மறவன்,","படை வேண்டுவழி வாள் உதவியும்,","வினை வேண்டுவழி அறிவு உதவியும்,","வேண்டுப வேண்டுப வேந்தன் தேஎத்து","அசைநுகம் படாஅ ஆண்தகை உள்ளத்துத்,","தோலா நல்லிசை, நாலை கிழவன்,","பருந்துபசி தீர்க்கும் நற்போர்த்","திருந்துவேல் நாகற் கூறினர், பலரே."],"p":"","r":"When the generous of the earth had died and my begging bowl lay upturned, unfilled, I asked who would fill it, and many named Nagan, lord of Nalai, the soldier of the Pandya who took many lands with tight-strung drums: where an army is wanted he gives his sword, where counsel is wanted his wisdom, a manly heart that never tires under the yoke of what his king wants, of unbeaten fame, whose good battles end the kites' hunger.","n":"The poem is shaped as a bard's search for a giver, with the praise of the soldier given as the answer others offered; the colophon files it under valour, but its frame is the upturned begging bowl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","maram","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 179","lb":"PN179","poet":"வடநெடுந்தத்தனார்; வடம நெடுந்தத்தனார் எனவும், வடம நெடுந்தச்சனார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"நாலை கிழவன் நாகன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",4,"parai","scene",""],["பருந்துபசி","பருந்து",11,"pul","scene","kite"]],"en":[["king","பாண்டியன்",5],["patron","நாகற்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:180":{"book":"purananuru","num":180,"t":["நிரப்பாது கொடுக்கும் செல்வமும் இலனே;","இல்லென மறுக்கும் சிறுமையும் இலனே;","இறையுறு விழுமம் தாங்கி, அமர்அகத்து","இரும்புசுவைக் கொண்ட விழுப்புண்நோய் தீர்ந்து,","மருந்துகொள் மரத்தின் வாள்வடு மயங்கி,","வடுவின்றி வடிந்த யாக்கையன், கொடையெதிர்ந்து,","ஈர்ந்தை யோனே, பாண்பசிப் பகைஞன்;","இன்மை தீர வேண்டின், எம்மொடு","நீயும் வம்மோ? முதுவாய் இரவல!","யாம்தன் இரக்கும் காலைத், தான்எம்","உண்ணா மருங்குல் காட்டித், தன்ஊர்க்","கருங்கைக் கொல்லனை இரக்கும்,","‘திருந்திலை நெடுவேல் வடித்திசின்’ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"He has no wealth to give without end, nor the meanness to refuse saying he has none; bearing his king's troubles, the wounds that tasted iron in battle have healed and his body is so crossed with sword scars, like a tree cut again and again for medicine, that it is shaped of scars; he lives at Eernthai, enemy of the bards' hunger: old-tongued suppliant, if you want your want ended, come with us, for when we ask him he points to our unfed bellies and asks the dark-handed smith of his town to forge him a long leaf-bladed spear.","n":"The patron is poor, and his gift is a raid: the bards' hunger is shown to the smith, not to a treasury. The scars and the spear make giving the direct fruit of fighting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","maram","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 180","lb":"PN180","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சிலூர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"ஈர்ந்தூர் கிழான் தோயன் மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண்முல்லை; பாணாற்றுப் படையும் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை","பாணாற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மரத்தின்","மரம்",5,"maram","simile","medicine tree"],["கொல்லனை","கொல்லன்",12,"seythi","scene","smith"]],"en":[["town","ஈர்ந்தை",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:9"},"pn:181":{"book":"purananuru","num":181,"t":["மன்ற விளவின் மனைவீழ் வெள்ளில்,","கருங்கண் எயிற்றி காதல் மகனொடு,","கான இரும்பிடிக் கன்றுதலைக் கொள்ளும்","பெருங்குறும்பு உடுத்த வன்புல இருக்கைப்,","புலாஅல் அம்பின், போர்அருங் கடிமிளை,","வலாஅ ரோனே, வாய்வாள் பண்ணன்;","உண்ணா வறுங்கடும்பு உய்தல் வேண்டின்,","இன்னே சென்மதி, நீயே; சென்று, அவன்","பகைப்புலம் படரா அளவை, நின்","பசிப்பகைப் பரிசில் காட்டினை கொளற்கே."],"p":"","r":"A poet sends a hungry bard straight to Pannan, the true-sworded lord of a harsh, fenced forest country, telling him to go at once and show his hunger before Pannan rides out to enemy land.","n":"The opening picture of the hunters' settlement, where the woman's son and a forest elephant's calf go after the same fallen wood-apple, is both a sign of a wild, unploughed land and of a lord whose people live without fear; the urgency of இன்னே and the timing clause (before he goes to the enemy's land) make the patron's generosity a thing that can be missed. Who takes the fruit in lines 1 to 3 is not certain from the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 181","lb":"PN181","poet":"சோணாட்டு முகையலூர்ச் சிறுகருந் தும்பி யார்","pat":"வல்லார் கிழான் பண்ணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண்முல்லை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["விளவின்","விளவு",1,"maram","scene",""],["வெள்ளில்","வெள்ளில்",1,"maram","scene","wood-apple fruit"],["எயிற்றி","எயிற்றி",2,"seythi","scene",""],["இரும்பிடிக்","பிடி",3,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["கன்றுதலைக்","கன்று",3,"maa","scene","elephant calf"]],"en":[["patron","பண்ணன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:182":{"book":"purananuru","num":182,"t":["உண்டால் அம்ம, இவ்வுலகம்; இந்திரர்;","அமிழ்தம் இயைவ தாயினும், இனிதுஎனத்","தமியர் உண்டலும் இலரே; முனிவிலர்!","துஞ்சலும் இலர்; பிறர் அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சிப்,","புகழ்எனின், உயிருங் கொடுக்குவர், பழியெனின்,","உலகுடன் பெறினும், கொள்ளலர், அயர்விலர்;","அன்ன மாட்சி அனைய ராகித்,","தமக்கென முயலா நோன்தாள்,","பிறர்க்கென முயலுநர் உண்மை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The world goes on only because there are people who would not eat even the gods' nectar alone, who are without anger or sloth, fear what others fear, give their lives for honour and refuse the whole world if it comes with blame, and strive not for themselves but for others.","n":"The poem is a single sentence that holds its subject back to the last line: the list of refusals comes first and only then does it say who these people are. The poet is a Pandya prince, so the claim that the world rests on the selfless comes from a ruler, not a supplicant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","eegai","pugazh","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 182","lb":"PN182","poet":"கடலுள் மாய்ந்த இளம்பெரு வழுதி","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["இந்திரர்","இந்திரன்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["அமிழ்தம்","அமிழ்தம்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:183":{"book":"purananuru","num":183,"t":["உற்றுழி உதவியும், உறுபொருள் கொடுத்தும்,","பிற்றைநிலை முனியாது, கற்றல் நன்றே!","பிறப்பு ஓர் அன்ன உடன்வயிற்று உள்ளும்,","சிறப்பின் பாலால், தாயும் மனம் திரியும்;","ஒருகுடிப் பிறந்த பல்லோ ருள்ளும்,","‘மூத்தோன் வருக’ என்னாது, அவருள்","அறிவுடை யோன் ஆறு அரசும் செல்லும்;","வேற்றுமை தெரிந்த நாற்பால் உள்ளும்,","கீழ்ப்பால் ஒருவன் கற்பின்,","மேற்பால் ஒருவனும் அவன்கண் படுமே,"],"p":"","r":"Learning is good even at the cost of serving and paying the teacher and not resenting the lower place: a mother's heart leans to the more accomplished of her own children, a king takes counsel from the wise brother rather than the eldest, and when a man of the lowest of the four orders learns, a man of the highest comes to him.","n":"Three hierarchies are tested in turn, the family, the royal house and the four orders, and in each learning wins over birth order or birth rank. The poet is a king, Nedunchezhiyan, so the poem concedes that even the throne's counsel follows learning. The hierarchies are named, not abolished.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 183","lb":"PN183","poet":"ஆரியப் படை கடந்த நெடுஞ்செழியன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:184":{"book":"purananuru","num":184,"t":["காய்நெல் அறுத்துக் கவளங் கொளினே,","மாநிறைவு இல்லதும், பன்நாட்கு ஆகும்;","நூறுசெறு ஆயினும், தமித்துப்புக்கு உணினே,","வாய்புகு வதனினும் கால்பெரிது கெடுக்கும்;","அறிவுடை வேந்தன் நெறியறிந்து கொளினே,","கோடி யாத்து, நாடுபெரிது நந்தும்;","மெல்லியன் கிழவன் ஆகி, வைகலும்","வரிசை அறியாக் கல்லென் சுற்றமொடு,","பரிவுதப எடுக்கும் பிண்டம் நச்சின்,","யானை புக்க புலம்போலத்,","தானும் உண்ணான், உலகமும் கெடுமே."],"p":"","r":"If a king takes his due as an elephant is fed ripe paddy cut into balls, even a small field lasts many days and the country thrives, but if a weak king with a noisy, unruly retinue grabs what he can, he is like an elephant let loose in a field that tramples more than it eats: he goes hungry and the land is ruined.","n":"The argument is made to the king's own interest, not the subject's: greed leaves the king himself unfed (தானும் உண்ணான்). The blame falls partly on the retinue that does not know order (வரிசை அறியா), so the weak king is the one who cannot restrain those around him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kodungol","arivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 184","lb":"PN184","poet":"பிசிராந்தையார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் அறிவுடை நம்பி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"செவியறிவுறூஉ","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செவியறிவுறூஉ"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["காய்நெல்","காய் நெல்",1,"unavu","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:185":{"book":"purananuru","num":185,"t":["கால்பார் கோத்து, ஞாலத்து இயக்கும்","காவற் சாகாடு உகைப்போன் மாணின்,","ஊறுஇன்றாகி ஆறுஇனிது படுமே;","உய்த்தல் தேற்றான் ஆயின், வைகலும்,","பகைக்கூழ் அள்ளற் பட்டு,","மிகப்பல் தீநோய் தலைத்தலைத் தருமே."],"p":"","r":"The realm is a cart of protection running on wheel and axle: if its driver is skilled it goes smoothly without harm, but if he cannot steer it sinks daily in the mire of enmity and brings one great evil after another.","n":"The poet is himself a king, Tondaiman Ilandiraiyan, so the image of the incompetent driver is a ruler's self-warning. The cart is protection (காவல்), not wealth or conquest: the realm exists to guard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 185","lb":"PN185","poet":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:186":{"book":"purananuru","num":186,"t":["நெல்லும் உயிர் அன்றே; நீரும் உயிர் அன்றே;","மன்னன் உயிர்த்தே மலர்தலை உலகம்;","அதனால், யான்உயிர் என்பது அறிகை","வேன்மிகு தானை வேந்தற்குக் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"Rice is not life and water is not life: the wide world lives by its king, so it is the duty of the king with a spear-strong army to know that he is its life.","n":"The last clause turns praise into duty: it is not the people who must know the king is their life, but the king who must know it (அறிகை ... கடனே). Read that way, the poem binds the king rather than flattering him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","uyir","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 186","lb":"PN186","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லும்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["நீரும்","நீர்",1,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:187":{"book":"purananuru","num":187,"t":["நாடா கொன்றோ ; காடா கொன்றோ;","அவலா கொன்றோ ; மிசையா கொன்றோ;","எவ்வழி நல்லவர் ஆடவர்,","அவ்வழி நல்லை ; வாழிய நிலனே!"],"p":"","r":"Be it settled country or forest, low ground or high, a land is good only where its men are good; bless you, land.","n":"Avvaiyar names four kinds of ground, the very terms by which land is classed, only to declare them irrelevant; the landscape that orders all Sangam poetry is set aside in four short lines. The goodness is the men's, and the land is praised for reflecting it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","panpu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 187","lb":"PN187","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:188":{"book":"purananuru","num":188,"t":["படைப்புப்பல படைத்துப் பலரோடு உண்ணும்","உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வர் ஆயினும், இடைப்படக்","குறுகுறு நடந்து, சிறுகை நீட்டி,","இட்டும், தொட்டும், கவ்வியும், துழந்தும்","நெய்யுடை அடிசில் மெய்பட விதிர்த்தும்,","மயக்குறு மக்களை இல்லோர்க்குப்","பயக்குறை இல்லைத் - தாம் வாழும் நாளே."],"p":"","r":"However rich a man is, with many dishes and many guests, if he has no little children who toddle up, stretch out small hands, put in, touch, bite and stir the food and scatter the ghee rice over themselves, his days of living bring him no profit.","n":"The four verbs of line 4 are a child's whole repertoire with food, and the poem lingers on the mess before it gives its verdict. The poet is the Pandya king Arivudai Nambi, a man of the feasting wealth he says is worthless without children.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 188","lb":"PN188","poet":"பாண்டியன் அறிவுடை நம்பி","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["அடிசில்","நெய் அடிசில்",5,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:189":{"book":"purananuru","num":189,"t":["தெண்கடல் வளாகம் பொதுமை ‘இன்றி","வெண்குடை நிழற்றிய ஒருமை யோர்க்கும்,","நடுநாள் யாமத்தும் பகலும் துஞ்சான்","கடுமாப் பார்க்கும் கல்லா ஒருவற்கும்,","உண்பது நாழி ; உடுப்பவை இரண்டே;","பிறவும் எல்லாம் ஓரொக் குமே;","அதனால், செல்வத்துப் பயனே ஈதல்;","துய்ப்பேம் எனினே, தப்புந பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Whether he is the sole king under the white umbrella of the sea-girt earth or an unlettered hunter who watches for game sleepless at midnight and by day, each eats one measure and wears two cloths, and all else is the same; so the use of wealth is giving, for whoever means to enjoy it all himself loses much.","n":"The argument is from the body's limit, not from virtue or merit: a stomach takes one naazhi whatever one owns, so surplus can only be given or lost. The final line can be read either as the hoarder failing in many duties or as many things slipping from his grasp.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 189","lb":"PN189","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["தெண்கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene",""],["கடுமாப்","கடுமா",4,"maa","scene","swift game"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:190":{"book":"purananuru","num":190,"t":["விளைபதச் சீறிடம் நோக்கி, வளைகதிர்","வல்சி கொண்டு, அளை மல்க வைக்கும்","எலிமுயன் றனைய ராகி, உள்ளதம்","வளன்வலி உறுக்கும் உளம் இலாளரோடு","இயைந்த கேண்மை இல்லா கியரோ!","கடுங்கண் கேழல் இடம்பட வீழ்ந்தென,","அன்று அவண் உண்ணா தாகி, வழிநாள்,","பெருமலை விடரகம் புலம்ப, வேட்டெழுந்து,","இருங்களிற்று ஒருத்தல் நல்வலம் படுக்கும்","புலிபசித் தன்ன மெலிவில் உள்ளத்து","உரனுடை யாளர் கேண்மையொடு","இயைந்த வைகல் உளவா கியரோ!"],"p":"","r":"Let me have no friendship with the small-hearted who, like the rat that eyes a little field at harvest and stuffs its hole with curved ears of grain, only build up what they already have; let my days be joined with the strong-hearted, unflagging as the hungry tiger that will not eat a boar fallen on its left side and next day rises from its cave to bring down a great bull elephant.","n":"The two similes are built as opposites of scale: the rat fills its hole from a small field, the tiger refuses an easy boar and hunts an elephant. The tiger's refusal of prey fallen on the left is an omen custom, and it makes the tiger's hunger a chosen one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","maanam","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 190","lb":"PN190","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுருத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வல்சி","வல்சி",2,"unavu","simile",""],["எலி","எலி",3,"maa","simile","rat"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",6,"maa","simile","boar"],["இருங்களிற்று","களிறு",9,"maa","simile","bull elephant"],["புலி","புலி",10,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:191":{"book":"purananuru","num":191,"t":["‘யாண்டுபல வாக , நரையில ஆகுதல்","யாங்கு ஆகியர்?’ என வினவுதிர் ஆயின்,","மாண்டஎன் மனைவியோடு, மக்களும் நிரம்பினர்;","யான்கண் டனையர்என் இளையரும்; வேந்தனும்","அல்லவை செய்யான், காக்க; அதன்தலை","ஆன்றுஅவிந்து அடங்கிய கொள்கைச்","சான்றோர் பலர் யான் வாழும் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"Asked why he has no grey hair after so many years, the poet answers that his wife is excellent, his children grown, his servants of one mind with him, the king does no wrong and protects, and above all his town has many worthy men, full in learning, calm and restrained.","n":"The answer moves outward in rings: wife, children, servants, king, and last the worthy men of the town, which the poet sets above all (அதன்தலை). Freedom from age is credited to the people one lives among, not to diet, discipline or renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","saandror","adakkam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 191","lb":"PN191","poet":"பிசிராந்தையர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:192":{"book":"purananuru","num":192,"t":["யாதும் ஊரே ; யாவரும் கேளிர் ;","தீதும் நன்றும் பிறர்தர வாரா ;","நோதலும் தணிதலும் அவற்றோ ரன்ன ;","சாதலும் புதுவது அன்றே ; வாழ்தல்","இனிதுஎன மகிழ்ந்தன்றும் இலமே; முனிவின்,","இன்னா தென்றலும் இலமே; ‘மின்னொடு","வானம் தண்துளி தலைஇ, ஆனாது","கல்பொருது இரங்கும் மல்லற் பேர்யாற்று","நீர்வழிப் படூஉம் புணைபோல, ஆருயிர்","முறைவழிப் படூஉம்’ என்பது திறவோர்","காட்சியின் தெளிந்தனம் ஆகலின், மாட்சியின்","பெரியோரை வியத்தலும் இலமே;","சிறியோரை இகழ்தல் அதனினும் இலமே."],"p":"","r":"Every town is ours and everyone our kin; good and ill do not come from others, nor do pain and its easing; death is nothing new, and we neither rejoice that life is sweet nor in disgust call it bitter, for we have seen through the wise that a life moves by its order like a raft carried down a great river roaring over rocks after the lightning and rain, so we do not marvel at the great and still less despise the small.","n":"The famous first line is the conclusion of an argument, not a slogan: because good and ill are not given by others, no place is foreign and no one a stranger. The raft image keeps the agent out of view; the poem says a life follows its course (முறைவழி) but does not name who set it. The equanimity is learnt from the wise (திறவோர் காட்சியின்), not claimed as one's own discovery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","oozh","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 192","lb":"PN192","poet":"கணியன் பூங்குன்றன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["மின்னொடு","மின்",6,"pira","simile",""],["வானம்","வானம்",7,"pira","simile",""],["பேர்யாற்று","பேர்யாறு",8,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:193":{"book":"purananuru","num":193,"t":["அதள்எறிந் தன்ன நெடுவெண் களரின்","ஒருவன் ஆட்டும் புல்வாய் போல,","ஓடி உய்தலும் கூடும்மன்;","ஒக்கல் வாழ்க்கை தட்கும்மா காலே."],"p":"","r":"Like an antelope chased by one man across a long white salt plain spread like a flayed hide, one might run and escape, but the life of a household binds the legs.","n":"The poet's name, the one-plough farmer, is taken from the poem's world, and the four lines leave unsaid what he would flee: the chase is from his troubles, and the household is both what makes him want to run and what keeps him from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","sutram","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 193","lb":"PN193","poet":"ஓரேருழவர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அதள்","அதள்",1,"pira","simile",""],["புல்வாய்","புல்வாய்",2,"maa","simile","antelope"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:194":{"book":"purananuru","num":194,"t":["ஓரில் நெய்தல் கறங்க, ஓர்இல்","ஈர்ந்தண் முழவின் பாணி ததும்பப்,","புணர்ந்தோர் பூவணி அணியப், பிரிந்தோர்","பைதல் உண்கண் பனிவார்பு உறைப்பப்,","படைத்தோன் மன்ற, அப் பண்பி லாளன்!","இன்னாது அம்ம, இவ் வுலகம்;","இனிய காண்க, இதன் இயல்புணர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"In one house the dirge drum sounds, in another the cool wedding drum throbs; the joined wear flowers while the parted weep from kohl-lined eyes: whoever made this world is surely without kindness. This world is harsh; let those who know its nature look for what is sweet.","n":"நெய்தல் here is the drum or tune of mourning, not the shore landscape or the flower. The poem blames the maker (படைத்தோன்) outright, which is rare, and then turns from complaint to counsel: knowing the world is harsh is the ground for seeking good (இனிய), not for leaving it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 194","lb":"PN194","poet":"பக்குடுக்கை நன்கணியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"parai","scene","dirge drum"],["முழவின்","முழவு",2,"parai","scene",""],["பூவணி","பூ",3,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:195":{"book":"purananuru","num":195,"t":["பல்சான் றீரே! பல்சான் றீரே!","கயல்முள் அன்ன நரைமுதிர் திரைகவுள்,","பயனில் மூப்பின், பல்சான் றீரே!","கணிச்சிக் கூர்ம்படைக் கடுந்திறல் ஒருவன்","பிணிக்கும் காலை, இரங்குவிர் மாதோ;","நல்லது செய்தல் ஆற்றீர் ஆயினும்,","அல்லது செய்தல் ஓம்புமின்; அதுதான்","எல்லாரும் உவப்பது ; அன்றியும்,","நல்லாற்றுப் படூஉம் நெறியுமார் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"You many worthies with hair grey as fish bones and wrinkled cheeks, in useless old age, you will grieve when the fierce one with the sharp axe binds you: if you cannot do good, at least keep from doing harm, for that pleases everyone and is also the road to the good way.","n":"The address பல்சான்றீரே, repeated three times, is ironic: the 'many worthies' have reached old age without doing good. Death is not named but drawn as one who carries a கணிச்சி, an axe. The advice sets a floor, not a summit: refraining from harm is offered as already a way to the good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kootru","nilaiyaamai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 195","lb":"PN195","poet":"நரிவெரூஉத் தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["கயல்","கயல்",2,"pira","simile","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:196":{"book":"purananuru","num":196,"t":["ஒல்லுவது ஒல்லும் என்றலும், யாவர்க்கும்","ஒல்லாது இல்லென மறுத்தலும், இரண்டும்,","ஆள்வினை மருங்கின் கேண்மைப் பாலே;","ஒல்லாது ஒல்லும் என்றலும், ஒல்லுவது","இல்லென மறுத்தலும், இரண்டும், வல்லே;","இரப்போர் வாட்டல் அன்றியும், புரப்போர்","புகழ்குறை படூஉம் வாயில் அத்தை","அனைத்தா கியர், இனி; இதுவே எனைத்தும்","சேய்த்துக் காணாது கண்டனம் ; அதனால்,","நோயிலர் ஆகநின் புதல்வர்; யானும்,","வெயிலென முனியேன், பனியென மடியேன்,","கல்குயின் றன்னஎன் நல்கூர் வளிமறை,","நாணலது இல்லாக் கற்பின் வாணுதல்","மெல்லியல் குறுமகள் உள்ளிச்","செல்வல் அத்தை ; சிறக்க, நின் நாளே!"],"p":"","r":"To say yes to what one can give and no to what one cannot are both proper ways of a giver's friendship, but to promise what one cannot and refuse what one can both wound the asker and lessen the giver's fame; I have seen it now, so may your sons be well, and I, not shrinking from sun or dew, will go home to my poor shelter thinking of my faithful, bright-browed wife.","n":"The rebuke is framed as a general rule of four cases before it becomes personal, and the poet's parting blessing on the king's sons keeps the rebuke from becoming a curse. The sense of கல்குயின்றன்ன, describing his shelter, is not secure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","vaaimai","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 196","lb":"PN196","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வெயிலென","வெயில்",11,"pira","scene",""],["பனியென","பனி",11,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:197":{"book":"purananuru","num":197,"t":["வளிநடந் தன்ன வாஅய்ச் செலல் இவுளியொடு","கொடிநுடங்கு மிசைய தேரினர் எனாஅக்,","கடல்கண் டன்ன ஒண்படைத் தானையொடு","மலைமாறு மலைக்குங் களிற்றினர் எனாஅ,","உரும்உடன் றன்ன உட்குவரு முரசமொடு","செருமேம் படூஉம் வென்றியர் எனாஅ,","மண்கெழு தானை, ஒண்பூண் வேந்தர்","வெண்குடைச் செல்வம் வியத்தலோ இலமே;","எம்மால் வியக்கப் படூஉ மோரே,","இடுமுள் படப்பை மறிமேய்ந்து ஒழிந்த","குறுநறு முஞ்ஞைக் கொழுங்கண் குற்றடகு,","புன்புல வரகின் சொன்றியொடு, பெறூஉம்,","சீறூர் மன்னர் ஆயினும், எம்வயின்","பாடறிந்து ஒழுகும் பண்பி னோரே;","மிகப்பேர் எவ்வம் உறினும், எனைத்தும்","உணர்ச்சி யில்லோர் உடைமை யுள்ளேம்;","நல்லறி வுடையோர் நல்குரவு","உள்ளுதும், பெரும! யாம், உவந்து நனி பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"We do not marvel at the white-umbrella wealth of kings with wind-swift horses, bannered chariots, sea-wide armies, hill-rivalling elephants and thunder-loud drums; we marvel at small-village chiefs who, though they eat only greens and varagu rice, know how to treat us; we think nothing of the riches of the unfeeling, and think of the poverty of the wise with great joy, lord.","n":"Spoken to a great Chola king, the poem is a refusal and a warning in the form of praise of others: the grand catalogue of lines 1 to 8 is built up only to be dismissed. The small chief's meal is described with care (greens the goats have left, millet from dry land), and the point is that he knows the poet's worth (பாடறிந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","selvam","inmai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 197","lb":"PN197","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சலூர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"சோழன் குராப்பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய பெருந்திருமாவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கல்வி"]],"k":[["இவுளி","இவுளி",1,"maa","simile",""],["கடல்","கடல்",3,"pira","simile",""],["களிற்றினர்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["உரும்","உரும்",5,"pira","simile","thunder"],["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",5,"parai","simile",""],["இடுமுள்","இடுமுள்",10,"maram","scene",""],["மறி","மறி",10,"maa","scene","goat kid"],["முஞ்ஞைக்","முஞ்ஞை",11,"maram","gift_food",""],["குற்றடகு","அடகு",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",12,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சொன்றியொடு","சொன்றி",12,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:198":{"book":"purananuru","num":198,"t":["“அருவி தாழ்ந்த பெருவரை போல","ஆரமொடு பொலிந்த மார்பின் தண்டாக்,","கடவுள் சான்ற, கற்பின் சேயிழை","மடவோள் பயந்த மணிமருள் அவ்வாய்க்","கிண்கிணிப் புதல்வர் பொலிக!” என்று ஏத்தித்,","திண்தேர் அண்ணல் நிற்பா ராட்டிக்,","காதல் பெறாமையின் கனவினும் அரற்றும்என்","காமர் நெஞ்சம் ஏமாந்து உவப்ப,","ஆல்அமர் கடவுள் அன்னநின் செல்வம்,","வேல்கெழு குருசில்! கண்டேன்; ஆதலின்,","விடுத்தனென்; வாழ்க, நின் கண்ணி! தொடுத்த","தண்டமிழ் வரைப்புஅகம் கொண்டி யாகப்,","பணிந்துக்கூட் டுண்ணும் மணிப்பருங் கடுந்திறல்","நின்னோ ரன்னநின் புதல்வர், என்றும்,","ஒன்னார் வாட அருங்கலம் தந்து, நும்","பொன்னுடை நெடுநகர் நிறைய வைத்தநின்","முன்னோர் போல்க; இவர் பெருங்கண் ணோட்டம்!","யாண்டும் நாளும் பெருகி, ஈண்டுதிரைப்","பெருங்கடல் நீரினும், அக்கடல் மணலினும்,","நீண்டுஉயர் வானத்து உறையினும், நன்றும்,","இவர்பெறும் புதல்வர்க் காண்தொறும், நீயும்,","புகன்ற செல்வமொடு புகழ்இனிது விளங்கி,","நீடு வாழிய! நெடுந்தகை; யானும்","கேளில் சேஎய் நாட்டின், எந் நாளும்,","துளிநசைப் புள்ளின்நின் அளிநசைக்கு இரங்கி, நின்","அடிநிழல் பழகிய வடியுறை;","கடுமான் மாற! மறவா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"Having blessed the king's sons, born of his chaste, god-like queen, and seen his wealth, like that of the god under the banyan, the poet takes leave wishing the sons to be like their ancestors who filled the palace with enemies' treasure, and the king to live on, his days more than the sea's water, its sand and the sky's rain, while the poet in his distant land, like the bird that longs for a raindrop, waits for his grace: 'Maran, do not forget me.'","n":"The poem is a leave-taking, not a thanks: the poet says his heart, which cries out even in dreams for lack of the king's favour, rejoiced in seeing his wealth (ஏமாந்து உவப்ப), and his last words ask not to be forgotten, so the gift is still awaited. The bird that lives on raindrops makes his waiting a matter of nature, not of choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","makkal","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 198","lb":"PN198","poet":"வடமவண்ணக்கண் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் இலவந்திகைப் பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய நன்மாறன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","simile",""],["ஆரமொடு","ஆரம்",2,"pira","ornament",""],["கிண்கிணிப்","கிண்கிணி",5,"pira","ornament",""],["ஆல்","ஆல்",9,"maram","simile",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",9,"deyvam","simile",""],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",11,"maram","ornament",""],["கடல்","கடல்",19,"pira","simile",""],["மணலினும்","மணல்",19,"pira","simile",""],["உறையினும்","உறை",20,"pira","simile","raindrop"],["புள்ளின்","புள்",25,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[["king","மாற",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:199":{"book":"purananuru","num":199,"t":["கடவுள் ஆலத்துத் தடவுச்சினைப் பல்பழம்","நெருநல் உண்டனம் என்னாது, பின்னும்","செலவுஆ னாவே, கலிகொள் புள்ளினம்;","அனையர் வாழியோ இரவலர்; அவரைப்","புரவுஎதிர் கொள்ளும் பெருஞ்செய் ஆடவர்","உடைமை ஆகும், அவர் உடைமை;","அவர் இன்மை ஆகும், அவர் இன்மையே."],"p":"","r":"A noisy flock of birds does not say 'we ate yesterday' but keeps returning to the many fruits of the wide-branched banyan where a god dwells; so are suppliants, and the wealth of the generous men who welcome them is the suppliants' wealth, their want the suppliants' want.","n":"The poem defends the suppliant's repeated asking by nature: birds do not count yesterday's meal. The last two lines join giver and asker in one fortune, so the patron's poverty would be the poet's poverty too; asking is made a bond, not a debt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 199","lb":"PN199","poet":"பெரும்பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடா நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["ஆலத்து","ஆலம்",1,"maram","simile",""],["பல்பழம்","பழம்",1,"unavu","simile",""],["புள்ளினம்","புள்",3,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:200":{"book":"purananuru","num":200,"t":["பனிவரை நிவந்த பாசிலைப் பலவின்","கனிகவர்ந் துண்ட கருவிரற் கடுவன்","செம்முக மந்தியொடு சிறந்துசேண் விளங்கி","மழைமிசை யறியா மால்வரை யடுக்கத்துக்","கழைமிசைத் துஞ்சுங் கல்லக வெற்ப","நிணந்தின்று செருக்கிய நெருப்புத்தலை நெடுவேற்","களங்கொண்டு கனலுங் கடுங்கண் யானை","விளங்குமணிக் கொடும்பூண் விச்சிக் கோவே","இவரே, பூத்தலை யறாஅப் புனைகொடி முல்லை","நாத்தழும் பிருப்பப் பாடா தாயினும்","கறங்குமணி நெடுந்தேர் கொள்கெனக் கொடுத்த","பரந்தோங்கு சிறப்பிற் பாரி மகளிர்","யானே, பரிசிலன் மன்னு மந்தண னீயே","வரிசையில் வணக்கும் வாண்மேம் படுநன்","நினக்கியான் கொடுப்பக் கொண்மதி சினப்போர்","அடங்கா மன்னரை யடக்கும்","மடங்கா விளையு ணாடுகிழ வோயே."],"p":"","r":"Kapilar addresses Vichikko, lord of high hills where monkeys feast on jackfruit and sleep on bamboo, a king with a fat-eating spear and fierce elephants: these are the daughters of Pari, who gave his tall belled chariot to a jasmine creeper though it never sang for him; I am a suppliant and a brahmin; take them as I give them, lord of a land of unfailing harvests.","n":"The bard offers the daughters as a gift he himself makes (கொடுப்பக் கொண்மதி), claiming the standing of brahmin and suppliant. Pari's gift to the jasmine is invoked to show what kind of father the girls had, generous even to a creeper that could not praise him. The monkey and his mate in the hill-description may be read as a hint at the marriage proposed, but nothing in the poem states it, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 200","lb":"PN200","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"விச்சிக்கோ","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரசிற்றுறை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசிற்றுறை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["கனி","கனி",2,"unavu","scene",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",2,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["மந்தியொடு","மந்தி",3,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["கழை","கழை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","scene","jasmine creeper"]],"en":[["king","விச்சிக் கோவே",8],["patron","பாரி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:10"},"pn:201":{"book":"purananuru","num":201,"t":["‘இவர் யார்?’ என்குவை ஆயின், இவரே,","ஊருடன் இரவலர்க்கு அருளித் ,தேருடன்","முல்லைக்கு ஈத்த செல்லா நல்லிசை,","படுமணி யானைப்,பறம்பின் கோமான்","நெடுமாப் பாரி மகளிர்; யானே","தந்தை தோழன்: இவர்என் மகளிர்;","அந்தணன், புலவன், கொண்டுவந் தனனே;","நீயே, வடபால் முனிவன் தடவினுள் தோன்றிச்,","செம்பு புனைந்து இயற்றிய சேண்நெடும் புரிசை,","உவரா ஈகைத், துவரை ஆண்டு,","நாற்பத்து ஒன்பது வழிமுறை வந்த","வேளிருள் வேளே! விறற்போர் அண்ணல்!","தாரணி யானைச் சேட்டிருங் கோவே!","ஆண்கடன் உடைமையின், பாண்கடன் ஆற்றிய","ஒலியற் கண்ணிப் புலிகடி மா அல்!","யான்தர, இவரைக் கொண்மதி! வான்கவித்து","இருங்கடல் உடுத்தஇவ் வையகத்து, அருந்திறல்","பொன்படு மால்வரைக் கிழவ! வென்வேல்","உடலுநர் உட்கும் தானைக்,","கெடல்அருங் குறைய நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Kapilar tells Irungovel that the girls with him are the daughters of Pari of Parambu, who gave his chariot to a jasmine creeper, that he himself, a brahmin poet and their father's friend, has brought them, and asks the lord of Thuvarai, chief of forty-nine generations of Velir, to take them as his gift.","n":"The poet makes the girls his own gift (யான்தர), so a request for a bridegroom is cast in the form of a bard's giving; the two lineages are set side by side, Pari's fame for an absurdly generous gift against Irungovel's descent from the sage's pit and forty-nine generations. Pari's death is never said: the orphaning is carried by யானே தந்தை தோழன், I am the father's friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","natpu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 201","lb":"PN201","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"இருங்கோவேள்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["இரவலர்க்கு","இரவலர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லைக்கு","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine creeper"],["யானைப்","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["அந்தணன்","அந்தணன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["புலவன்","புலவன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["புலிகடி","புலி",15,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[["hill","பறம்பின்",4],["patron","பாரி",5],["town","துவரை",10],["patron","சேட்டிருங் கோவே",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:202":{"book":"purananuru","num":202,"t":["வெட்சிக் கானத்து வேட்டுவர் ஆட்டக்,","கட்சி காணாக் கடமா நல்லேறு","கடறுமணி கிளரச், சிதறுபொன் மிளிரக்,","கடிய கதழும் நெடுவரைப் படப்பை","வென்றி நிலை இய விழுப்புகழ் ஒன்றி,","இருபால் பெயரிய உருகெழு மூதூர்க்,","கோடிபல அடுக்கிய பொருள் நுமக்கு உதவிய","நீடு நிலை அரையத்துக் கேடும் கேள், இனி;","நுந்தை தாயம் நிறைவுற எய்திய","ஒலியற் கண்ணிப் புலிகடி மாஅல்!","நும்போல் அறிவின் நுமருள் ஒருவன்","புகழ்ந்த செய்யுள் கழாஅத் தலையை","இகழ்ந்ததன் பயனே; இயல்தேர் அண்ணல்!","எவ்வி தொல்குடிப் படீஇயர், மற்று,‘இவர்","கைவண் பாரி மகளிர்’ என்றஎன்","தேற்றாப் புன்சொல் நோற்றிசின்; பெரும;","விடுத்தனென்; வெலீஇயர், நின் வேலே! அடுக்கத்து","அரும்பு அற மலர்ந்த கருங்கால் வேங்கை","மாத்தகட்டு ஒள்வீ தாய துறுகல்","இரும்புலி வரிப்புறம் கடுக்கும்","பெருங்கல் வைப்பின் நாடுகிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"After the chief has declined, Kapilar reminds him that his ancestors' town Araiyam, rich with wealth heaped in crores, fell because one of them scorned a poem by a wise man of his own kind, then asks pardon for his own foolish words offering Pari's daughters to Evvi's old clan, says he has let the matter go, and wishes the chief's spear victory.","n":"The refusal is never stated; it is read from the poet asking pardon for his own words and saying he has let the girls go. The story of Araiyam is a threat dressed as history: a house that once scorned a poet fell. Reading கழாஅத் தலையை as the name of the scorned poet is ours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","kudi","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 202","lb":"PN202","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"இருங்கோவேள்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["வெட்சிக்","வெட்சி",1,"maram","scene",""],["வேட்டுவர்","வேட்டுவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["கடமா","கடமா",2,"maa","scene","gaur"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",18,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",20,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[["town","அரையத்துக்",8],["patron","புலிகடி மாஅல்",10],["king","எவ்வி",14],["patron","பாரி",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:203":{"book":"purananuru","num":203,"t":["கழிந்தது பொழிந்ததென வான்கண் மாறினும்","தொல்லது விளைந்தென நிலம்வளம் கரப்பினும்,","எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் இல்லால், வாழ்க்கை;","இன்னும் தம்மென எம்ம்னோர் இரப்பின்,","முன்னும் கொண்டிர்என, நும்மனோர் மறுத்தல்","இன்னாது அம்ம; இயல்தேர் அண்ணல்!","இல்லது நிரப்பல் ஆற்றா தோரினும்,","உள்ளி வருநர் நசையிழப் போரே;","அனையையும் அல்லை, நீயே; ஒன்னார்","ஆர்எயில் அவர்கட்கு ஆகவும்,‘நுமது’ எனப்","பாண்கடன் இறுக்கும் வள்ளியோய்!","பூண்கடன், எந்தை! நீஇரவலர் புரவே."],"p":"","r":"Just as no living thing can live when the sky stops raining and the land hides its yield, it is cruel when a patron answers a bard's 'give again' with 'you took before'; worse than being unable to fill want is to kill the hope of those who come; you are not like that, generous lord who hands bards the enemy's fort as theirs before you take it, since protecting suppliants is your bounden duty.","n":"The poem argues from rain: a sky that stopped because it had already rained would kill all life, so 'you took before' is no reason to refuse. The bard's due is called a debt (பாண்கடன்) and protection of suppliants an ornament the king is bound to wear (பூண்கடன்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 203","lb":"PN203","poet":"ஊன்பொதி பசுங்குடையார்","pat":"சேரமான் பாமுளூரெறிந்த நெய்தலங்கானல் இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["வான்கண்","வான்",1,"pira","scene","rain sky"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:204":{"book":"purananuru","num":204,"t":["ஈஎன இரத்தல் இழிந்தன்று; அதன்எதிர்,","ஈயேன் என்றல் அதனினும் இழிந்தன்று;","கொள்எனக் கொடுத்தல் உயர்ந்தன்று, அதன்எதிர்,","கொள்ளேன் என்றல் அதனினும் உயர்ந்தன்று;","தெண்ணீர்ப் பரப்பின் இமிழ்திரைப் பெருங்கடல்","உண்ணார் ஆகுப, நீர்வேட் டோரே;","ஆவும் மாவும் சென்றுஉணக், கலங்கிச்,","சேறோடு பட்ட சிறுமைத்து ஆயினும்,","உண்ணீர் மருங்கின் அதர்பல ஆகும்;","புள்ளும் பொழுதும் பழித்தல் அல்லதை","உள்ளிச் சென்றோர் பழியலர்; அதனாற்","புலவேன் வாழியர், ஓரி; விசும்பின்","கருவி வானம் போல","வரையாது சுரக்கும் வள்ளியோய்! நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"To say 'give' is low and to refuse is lower still; to say 'take' and give is high and to refuse what is offered higher still; the thirsty do not drink from the clear wide sea but find many paths to a drinking pool, however muddied by cattle and beasts; those who come in hope blame the omens and the hour, never the giver, so I am not angry, Ori, giver who pours like the rain cloud without limit.","n":"The four-step ladder makes refusing a gift the highest act, which quietly ranks the proud bard above the giver. The poet also gives himself a rule of conduct: a disappointed suppliant blames birds and the hour, not the patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 204","lb":"PN204","poet":"கழைதின் யானையார்","pat":"வல் வில் ஓரி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["பெருங்கடல்","கடல்",5,"pira","simile","sea"],["ஆவும்","ஆ",7,"maa","simile","cow"],["மாவும்","மா",7,"maa","simile","beast"],["புள்ளும்","புள்",10,"pul","scene","omen bird"],["வானம்","வானம்",13,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[["patron","ஓரி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:205":{"book":"purananuru","num":205,"t":["முற்றிய திருவின் மூவர் ஆயினும்,","பெட்பின்றி ஈதல் யாம்வேண் டலமே;","விறற்சினம் தணிந்த விரைபரிப் புரவி","உறுவர் செல்சார்வு ஆகிச், செறுவர்","தாளுளம் தபுத்த வாள்மிகு தானை,","வெள்வீ வேலிக் கோடைப் பொருந!","சிறியவும் பெரியவும் புழைகெட விலங்கிய","மான்கணம் தொலைச்சிய கடுவிசைக் கதநாய்,","நோன்சிலை, வேட்டுவ! நோயிலை யாகுக!","ஆர்கலி யாணர்த் தரீஇய, கால் வீழ்த்துக்,","கடல்வயிற் குழீஇய அண்ணலங் கொண்மூ","நீரின்று பெயரா ஆங்குத், தேரொடு","ஒளிறுமறுப்பு ஏந்திய செம்மற்","களிறின்று பெயரல, பரிசிலர் கடும்பே."],"p":"","r":"Even from the three crowned kings of full fortune we do not want gifts given without regard; hunter lord of Kodai, with strong bow and fierce hounds that cut down herds of deer, be free of harm; as the great clouds that gather over the sea do not leave without water, the bards' kin do not leave you without chariots and bright-tusked elephants.","n":"The poem opens with a condition before any praise: honour first, gift second. The cloud image runs the usual figure backwards, since here the bards are the clouds that draw water from the patron's sea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maanam","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 205","lb":"PN205","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"கடிய நெடுவேட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["வெள்வீ","வெள்வீ",6,"maram","scene",""],["மான்கணம்","மான்",8,"maa","scene","deer"],["கதநாய்","நாய்",8,"maa","scene",""],["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",11,"pira","simile","cloud"],["களிறின்று","களிறு",14,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[["hill","கோடைப்",6],["patron","வேட்டுவ",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:206":{"book":"purananuru","num":206,"t":["வாயி லோயே! வாயிலோயே!","வள்ளியோர் செவிமுதல் வயங்குமொழி வித்தித், தாம்","உள்ளியது முடிக்கும் உரனுடை உள்ளத்து","வரிசைக்கு வருந்தும்இப் பரிசில் வாழ்க்கைப்","பரிசிலர்க்கு அடையா வாயி லோயே!","கடுமான் தோன்றல் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","தன்அறி யலன்கோல்? என்னறி யலன்கொல்?","அறிவும் பகழும் உடையோர் மாய்ந்தென,","வறுந்தலை உலகமும் அன்றே; அதனால்,","காவினெம் கலனே; சுருக்கினெம் கலப்பை;","மரங்கொல் தச்சன் மைவல் சிறாஅர்","மழுவுடைக் காட்டகத்து அற்றே;","எத்திசைச் செலினும், அத்திசைச் சோறே."],"p":"","r":"Gatekeeper who never shuts the gate on bards who sow bright words in givers' ears: does Anji not know himself, or not know me? The world is not left empty when men of learning and fame die, so we have shouldered our instruments and packed our bags; like the skilled sons of a woodcutting carpenter in a forest, wherever we go, there is rice.","n":"The address is to the gatekeeper, not the king, so the reproach reaches Anji at one remove. The phrase 'he does not know me' sets the bard's rank (வரிசை) against the patron's, and the carpenter's sons make the bard a craftsman who can earn anywhere, not a beggar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","parisil","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 206","lb":"PN206","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கல்வி"]],"k":[["கடுமான்","மா",6,"maa","scene","horse"],["தச்சன்","தச்சன்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["சிறாஅர்","சிறார்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["கலப்பை","கலப்பை",10,"yaazh","scene","instrument bag"],["சோறே","சோறு",13,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["patron","நெடுமான் அஞ்சி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:207":{"book":"purananuru","num":207,"t":["எழுஇனி, நெஞ்சம்! செல்கம்; யாரோ,","பருகு அன்ன வேட்கை இல்அழி,","அருகிற் கண்டும் அறியார் போல,","அகம்நக வாரா முகன்அழி பரிசில்","தாள்இலாளர் வேளார் அல்லர்?","வருகென வேண்டும் வரிசை யோர்க்கே","பெரிதே உலகம்; பேணுநர் பலரே;","மீளி முன்பின் ஆளி போல,","உள்ளம் உள்அவிந்து அடங்காது, வெள்ளென","நோவா தோன்வயின் திரங்கி,","வாயா வன்கனிக்கு உலமரு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"Rise, my heart, let us go: who would call them other than witless, those who see us close yet act as though they did not, and give a gift that does not gladden the heart, with a fallen face? The world is wide and providers many for those who want the honour of 'come'; only those whose spirit sinks, unlike the strong lion, pine over a hard fruit that will never ripen.","n":"The poem distinguishes a gift that makes the face move from one that makes the heart glad (அகம்நக ... முகன்அழி), and the bard refuses the first. The poet speaks to his own heart, as akam speakers do, but the matter is the bard's honour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","parisil","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 207","lb":"PN207","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"இளவெளிமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆளி","ஆளி",8,"maa","simile","lion"],["வன்கனிக்கு","கனி",11,"maram","simile","fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:208":{"book":"purananuru","num":208,"t":["‘குன்றும் மலையும் பலபின் ஒழிய,","வந்தனென், பரிசில் கொண்டனென் செலற்கு’ என","நின்ற என்நயந்து அருளி, ‘ஈது கொண்டு,","ஈங்கனம் செல்க, தான்’ என என்னை","யாங்குஅறிந் தனனோ, தாங்கரும் காவலன்?","காணாது ஈத்த இப்பொருட்கு யானோர்","வாணிகப் பரிசிலன் அல்லேன்; பேணித்","தினை அனைத்து ஆயினும், இனிதுஅவர்","துணை அளவு அறிந்து, நல்கினர் விடினே."],"p":"","r":"When I came past many hills and stood saying I had come to take a gift and go, the guardian sent it out without seeing me, saying 'take this and go'; how did he come to know me? I am not a trading bard for a thing given unseen; even a millet's worth is sweet when givers know the measure of the one they give to and give with care.","n":"The insult is not the size of the gift but that it was given unseen (காணாது ஈத்த): the meeting is part of the gift. The bard calls the alternative trade, so a gift without recognition is a sale.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","parisil","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 208","lb":"PN208","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["தினை","தினை",8,"unavu","gift_food","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:209":{"book":"purananuru","num":209,"t":["பொய்கை நாரை போர்வில் சேக்கும்","நெய்தல்அம் கழனி, நெல்அரி தொழுவர்","கூம்புவிடு மெய்பிணி அவிழ்ந்த ஆம்பல்","அகல் அடை அரியல் மாந்திக், தெண்கடல்","படுதிரை இன்சீர்ப் பாணி தூங்கும்","மென்புல வைப்பின் நன்னாட்டுப் பொருந!","பல்கனி நசைஇ, அல்கு விசும்பு உகத்து.","பெருமலை விடர்அகம் சிலம்ப முன்னிப்","பழனுடைப் பெருமரம் தீர்ந்தெனக், கையற்றுப்.","பெறாது பெயரும் புள்ளினம் போல, நின்","நசைதர வந்து, நின்இசை நுவல் பரிசிலென்","வறுவியேன் பெயர்கோ? வாள்மேம் படுந!","ஈயாய் ஆயினும், இரங்குவென் அல்லேன்;","நோயிலை ஆகுமதி; பெரும! நம்முள்","குறுநணி காண்குவ தாக - நாளும்,","நறும்பல் ஒலிவரும் கதுப்பின், தேமொழித்,","தெரியிழை மகளிர் பாணி பார்க்கும்","பெரு வரை அன்ன மார்பின்,","செருவெம் சேஎய்! நின் மகிழ்இருக்கையே!"],"p":"","r":"Lord of a gentle land of fields where the stork rests on the stack and reapers drink toddy from lily leaves and sway to the beat of the waves: like a flock that flew to the fruit tree in the mountain cleft and turns back with nothing when it is bare, shall I who came in hope singing your fame go away empty? Even if you do not give I will not grieve; be well, and may we meet close, at your court of joy where women watch your chest broad as a hill.","n":"The bird simile puts the fault on the bare tree, not the birds, so the patron is told he has gone barren; the refusal to grieve (இரங்குவென் அல்லேன்) and the closing blessing keep the reproach courteous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["parisil","eegai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 209","lb":"PN209","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"மூவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",1,"pul","scene","stork"],["நெய்தல்அம்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["அரியல்","அரியல்",4,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["பெருமரம்","மரம்",9,"maram","simile",""],["புள்ளினம்","புள்",10,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:210":{"book":"purananuru","num":210,"t":["மன்பதை காக்கும்நின் புரைமை நோக்காது,","அன்புகண் மாறிய அறனில் காட்சியொடு,","நும்ம னோரும்மற்று இனையர் ஆயின்,","எம்மனோர் இவண் பிறவலர் மாதோ;","செயிர்தீர் கொள்கை எம்வெங் காதலி","உயிர்சிறிது உடையள் ஆயின், எம்வயின்","உள்ளாது இருத்தலோ அரிதே; அதனால்,","அறனில் கூற்றம் திறனின்று துணியப்","பிறனா யினன்கொல்? இறீஇயர், என் உயிர்!’ என","நுவல்வுறு சிறுமையள் பலபுலந்து உறையும்","இடுக்கண் மனையோள் தீரிய, இந்நிலை","விடுத்தேன்; வாழியர், குருசில்! உதுக்காண்:","அவல நெஞ்சமொடு செல்வல்: நிற் கறுத்தோர்","அருங்கடி முனையரண் போலப்","பெருங்கை யற்றஎன் புலம்புமுந் துறத்தே."],"p":"","r":"If kings like you, ignoring your duty to protect the people, are loveless and unjust, our kind should not be born here; my faultless wife, saying that if I were even barely alive I would not fail to think of her and wondering whether unjust Death has made me another's, lives in grief, and to end her pain I give up this waiting; live long, lord: look, I go with a sad heart, my loneliness before me like the guarded fort of your enemies once you have broken it.","n":"The wife's words are quoted inside the bard's, so the reproach to the king is spoken by a woman at home who thinks her husband dead. The final simile is double-edged: the bard is as helpless as the king's beaten enemies, which ranks him with those the king defeats.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","ilvaazhkkai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 210","lb":"PN210","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"சேரமான் குடக்கோச் சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:211":{"book":"purananuru","num":211,"t":["அஞ்சுவரு மரபின் வெஞ்சினப் புயலேறு","அணங்குடை அரவின் அருந்தலை துமிய,","நின்றுகாண் பன்ன நீள்மலை மிளிரக்,","குன்றுதூவ எறியும் அரவம் போல,","முரசு எழுந்து இரங்கும் தானையோடு தலைச்சென்று,","அரைசுபடக் கடக்கும் உரைசால் தோன்றல்! நின்","உள்ளி வந்த ஓங்குநிலைப் பரிசிலென்,","‘வள்ளியை ஆதலின் வணங்குவன் இவன்’ எனக்,","கொள்ளா மாந்தர் கொடுமை கூற, நின்","உள்ளியது முடிந்தோய் மன்ற; முன்னாள்","கையுள் ளதுபோல் காட்டி, வழிநாள்","பொய்யொடு நின்ற புறநிலை வருத்தம்","நாணாய் ஆயினும், நாணக் கூறி, என்","நுணங்கு செந்நா அணங்க ஏத்திப்,","பாடப் பாடப் பாடுபுகழ் கொண்டநின்","ஆடுகொள் வியன்மார்பு தொழுதெனன் பழிச்சிச்","செல்வல் அத்தை, யானே வைகலும்,","வல்சி இன்மையின் வயின்வயின் மாறி,","இல்எலி மடிந்த தொல்சுவர் வரைப்பின்,","பாஅல் இன்மையின் பல்பாடு சுவைத்து,","முலைக்கோள் மறந்த புதல்வனொடு,","மனைத் தொலைந்திருந் தவென்வாள் நுதற் படர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Lord whose army crosses with drums that roar like thunder that splits a snake's head and shakes the hills: when men who would not give said 'he bows to you because you are generous', you achieved your aim; you showed the gift as if it were in my hand and then stood by a lie; though you feel no shame I have said what should shame you, praised you till my tongue ached, worshipped your chest that took fame from being sung, and I go to my bright-browed wife, in a house where even the rat has died for lack of grain, with a son who has forgotten the breast for lack of milk.","n":"The praise is sarcasm the poet names as such (நாணக் கூறி): the king 'achieved what he intended', which was to be praised without paying. The dead house rat is the poem's measure of want, a house too poor even for vermin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","inmai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 211","lb":"PN211","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"சேரமான் குடக்கோச் சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடாநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["புயலேறு","ஏறு",1,"pira","simile","thunder"],["அரவின்","அரவு",2,"pira","simile","snake"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",18,"unavu","absent","grain"],["இல்எலி","எலி",19,"maa","absent","house rat"],["பாஅல்","பால்",20,"unavu","absent","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:212":{"book":"purananuru","num":212,"t":["‘நுங்கோ யார்?’ வினவின், எங்கோக்","களமர்க்கு அரித்த விளையல் வெங்கள்","யாமைப் புழுக்கின் காமம் வீடஆரா,","ஆரற் கொழுஞ்சூடு அங்கவுள் அடாஅ,","வைகுதொழின் மடியும் மடியா விழவின்","யாணர் நல்நாட் டுள்ளும், பாணர்","பைதல் சுற்றத்துப் பசிப்பகை யாகிக்,","கோழி யோனே, கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","பொத்தில் நண்பின் பொத்தியொடு கெழீஇ,","வாயார் பெருநகை வைகலும் நமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask who your king is: in a rich land of unending festivals, where field hands drink strong toddy, eat boiled tortoise and roast eel, he is the lord of Kozhi, Kopperuncholan, enemy of the hunger of the bards' kin, who joined Potthi in flawless friendship and gives us great laughter every day.","n":"The speaker, Pisirandhaiyar of the Pandya country, calls the Chola 'our king' (எங்கோ), so the claim of the poem is friendship, not allegiance. The king is named last, after his land's food, and praised for laughter rather than for wealth or war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 212","lb":"PN212","poet":"பிசிராந்தையார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["களமர்க்கு","களமர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["கள்","கள்",2,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யாமைப்","யாமை",3,"unavu","gift_food","tortoise"],["ஆரற்","ஆரல்",4,"unavu","gift_food","eel"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",6,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["town","கோழி",8],["king","கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:213":{"book":"purananuru","num":213,"t":["மண்டுஅமர் அட்ட மதனுடை நோன்தாள்,","வெண்குடை விளக்கும், விறல்கெழு வேந்தே!","பொங்குநீர் உடுத்தஇம் மலர்தலை உலகத்து,","நின்தலை வந்த இருவரை நினைப்பின்,","தொன்றுறை துப்பின்நின் பகைஞரும் அல்லர்,","அமர்வெங் காட்சியொடு மாறுஎதிர்பு எழுந்தவர்:","நினையுங் காலை, நீயும் மற்றவர்க்கு","அனையை அல்லை; அடுமான் தோன்றல்!","பரந்துபடு நல்லிசை எய்தி, மற்று நீ","உயர்ந்தோர் உலகம் எய்திப்; பின்னும்","ஒழித்த தாயும் அவர்க்குஉரித்து அன்றே;","அதனால், அன்னது ஆதலும் அறிவோய்! நன்றும்","இன்னும் கேண்மதி, இசைவெய் யோயே!","நின்ற துப்பொடு நின்குறித்து எழுந்த","எண்ணில் காட்சி இளையோர் தோற்பின்,","நின்பெரும் செல்வம் யார்க்கும்எஞ் சுவையே?","அமர்வெஞ் செல்வ! நீ அவர்க்கு உலையின்,","இகழுநர் உவப்பப், பழியெஞ் சுவையே;","அதனால்,ஒழிகதில் அத்தை,நின் மறனே!வல்விரைந்து","எழுமதி; வாழ்க, நின் உள்ளம்! அழிந்தோர்க்கு","ஏமம் ஆகும்நின் தாள்நிழல் மயங்காது","செய்தல் வேண்டுமால். நன்றோ வானோர்","அரும்பெறல் உலகத்து ஆன்றவர்","விதும்புறு விருப்பொடு விருந்தெதிர் கொளற்கே."],"p":"","r":"Victorious king of the white umbrella: the two who have risen against you are not old enemies, and you are not their enemy either; when you have gained fame and gone to the higher world, what you leave belongs to them; if the young men lose, to whom will your wealth be left, and if you lose to them, only blame remains to please those who scorn you; so give up your fury, rise quickly, keep without confusion the foot-shade that shelters the fallen, for the worthy of the hard-won heaven wait eagerly to receive you as their guest.","n":"The poem never says 'your sons'; the two are only those who came against you (நின்தலை வந்த இருவரை), and the argument works by showing that every outcome is loss: if they die there is no heir, if he loses there is only disgrace. The closing turn to heaven's guests reframes withdrawal from war as the road to a hero's next world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","makkal","pazhi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 213","lb":"PN213","poet":"புல்லாற்றூர் எயிற்றியனார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"துணை வஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["துணைவஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["அடுமான்","மா",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:214":{"book":"purananuru","num":214,"t":["‘செய்குவம் கொல்லோ நல்வினை!’எனவே","ஐயம் அறாஅர், கசடுஈண்டு காட்சி","நீங்கா நெஞ்சத்துத் துணிவுஇல் லோரே;","யானை வேட்டுவன் யானையும் பெறுமே;","குறும்பூழ் வேட்டுவன் வறுங்கையும் வருமே;","அதனால், உயர்ந்த வேட்டத்து உயர்ந்திசி னோர்க்குச்","செய்வினை மருங்கின் எய்தல் உண்டெனின்,","செய்யா உலகத்து நுகர்ச்சியும் கூடும்;","செய்யா உலகத்து நுகர்ச்சி இல்லெனின்,","மாறிப் பிறப்பின் இன்மையும் கூடும்;","மாறிப் பிறவார் ஆயினும், இமையத்துக்","கோடுயர்ந் தன்ன தம்மிசை நட்டுத்,","தீதில் யாக்கையொடு மாய்தல் தவத் தலையே,"],"p":"","r":"Those whose minds hold doubt never get past asking 'shall we do good?'; the hunter of elephants gets an elephant while the hunter of quail may come home empty-handed; so if those who aim high get what their deeds earn, they may enjoy the world where no deeds are done; if there is no such joy, they may escape rebirth; and if not reborn, to plant one's fame as high as a Himalayan peak and die in a faultless body is the best.","n":"The argument is a chain of conditionals in which each fallback still pays: heaven, release from birth, or at least lasting fame and a whole body at death. It is spoken by the king himself; the colophon records his authorship, and the poem reads as a reason for the resolve the next poems mourn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","marumai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 214","lb":"PN214","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பொருண்மொழிக் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","simile",""],["குறும்பூழ்","குறும்பூழ்",5,"pul","simile","quail"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",4,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","இமையத்துக்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:215":{"book":"purananuru","num":215,"t":["கவைக் கதிர் வரகின் அவைப்புறு வாக்கல்","தாதொரு மறுகின் போதொடு பொதுளிய","வேளை வெண்பூ வெண்தயிர்க் கொளீஇ,","ஆய்மகள் அட்ட அம்புளி மிதவை","அவரை கொய்யுநர் ஆர மாந்தும்","தென்னம் பொருப்பன் நன்னாட்டு உள்ளும்","பிசிரோன் என்ப, என் உயிர்ஓம் புநனே;","செல்வக் காலை நிற்பினும்,","அல்லற் காலை நில்லலன் மன்னே."],"p":"","r":"In the fine land of the southern hill-lord, where bean-pickers eat their fill of the sour gruel the herd-woman cooks from forked-ear varagu with white velai flowers and white curd, lives Pisiron, they say, the guardian of my life; he may stay away in times of wealth, but in times of trouble he will not stay away.","n":"The king, about to die, praises a poet he has never met as the keeper of his life, and the proof offered is a prediction. The plain mullai food of the friend's land answers the toddy and eel of 212, so each friend praises the other through what is eaten at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idukkan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 215","lb":"PN215","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[["வரகின்","வரகு",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வேளை","வேளை",3,"maram","gift_food",""],["வெண்தயிர்க்","தயிர்",3,"unavu","gift_food","curd"],["ஆய்மகள்","ஆய்மகள்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["அவரை","அவரை",5,"maram","scene","bean"],["கொய்யுநர்","கொய்யுநர்",5,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தென்னம் பொருப்பன்",6],["patron","பிசிரோன்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:216":{"book":"purananuru","num":216,"t":["“கேட்டல் மாத்திரை அல்லது, யாவதும்","காண்டல் இல்லாது யாண்டுபல கழிய,","வழுவின்று பழகிய கிழமையர் ஆயினும்,","அரிதே, தோன்றல்! அதற்பட ஒழுகல் என்று","ஐயம் கொள்ளன்மின், ஆரறி வாளிர்!","இகழ்விலன்; இனியன்; யாத்த நண்பினன்;","புகழ்கெட வரூஉம் பொய்வேண் டலனே;","புன்பெயர் கிளக்கும் காலை, ‘என் பெயர்","பேதைச் சோழன்’ என்னும், சிறந்த","காதற் கிழமையும் உடையவன்; அதன் தலை,","இன்னதோர் காலை நில்லலன்;","இன்னே வருகுவன்; ஒழிக்க, அவற்கு இடமே!"],"p":"","r":"Do not doubt, wise men, saying that though friends by hearing alone, never having met in many years, it is hard for him to act on it now; he is without scorn, sweet, bound in friendship, wants no lie that would spoil his fame, and when he names himself he says 'my name is the foolish Chola', so close is his claim; he will not stay away at such a time; he will come now; keep his place.","n":"Friendship without meeting is the poem's claim and its wager: the king orders a seat kept for a man he has never seen. The friend's taking the king's own name (பேதைச் சோழன்) is offered as the sign of the bond; that he speaks of the king as 'foolish' is affection, and our reading of who says the name follows the line as it stands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kizhamai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 216","lb":"PN216","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["king","சோழன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:217":{"book":"purananuru","num":217,"t":["நினைக்கும் காலை மருட்கை உடைத்தே,","எனைப்பெரும் சிறப்பினோடு ஈங்கிது துணிதல்;","அதனினும் மருட்கை உடைத்தே, பிறன் நாட்டுத்","தோற்றம் சான்ற சான்றோன் போற்றி,","இசைமரபு ஆக, நட்புக் கந்தாக,","இனையதோர் காலை ஈங்கு வருதல்;","‘வருவன்’ என்ற கோனது பெருமையும்,","அது பழுது இன்றி வந்தவன் அறிவும்,","வியத்தொறும் வியத்தொறும் வியப்பிறந் தன்றே;","அதனால், தன்கோல் இயங்காத்தேயத்து உறையும்","சான்றோன் நெஞ்சுறப் பெற்ற தொன்றிசை","அன்னோனை இழந்தஇவ் வுலகம்","என்னா வதுகொல்? அளியது தானே!"],"p":"","r":"It is a wonder that a king of such great glory should resolve on this here; a greater wonder that a worthy man of renown from another land should come here at such a time, fame his tradition and friendship his pillar; the greatness of the king who said 'he will come' and the wisdom of the man who came without fail grow past wonder the more one wonders; so what will become of this world that has lost the one who won the heart of a worthy man living where his sceptre did not reach? It is to be pitied.","n":"Three wonders are stacked: the king's resolve, the friend's arrival, and the fulfilled prediction; the lament comes only in the last two lines. The poem treats the friend's coming as the proof of the king's worth: he won a heart beyond the reach of his rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saandror","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 217","lb":"PN217","poet":"பொத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:218":{"book":"purananuru","num":218,"t":["பொன்னும், துகிரும், முத்தும், மன்னிய","மாமலை பயந்த காமரு மணியும்,","இடைபடச் சேய ஆயினும், தொடை புணர்ந்து,","அருவிலை நன்கலம் அமைக்கும் காலை,","ஒருவழித் தோன்றியாங்கு-என்றும் சான்றோர்","சான்றோர் பாலர் ஆப;","சாலார் சாலார் பாலர் ஆகுபவே."],"p":"","r":"Gold, coral, pearl and the lovely gem of the great hill, though they come from far apart, come together in one place when a precious jewel is strung; so the worthy always fall to the side of the worthy, and the unworthy to the unworthy.","n":"Read in its place after 215 to 217, the jewel stands for the Chola and the Pandya-country poet joined at the end; the poem itself names no one, and its second half (the unworthy join the unworthy) gives the maxim an edge against those who stayed away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","natpu","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 218","lb":"PN218","poet":"கண்ணகனார் நத்தத்தனார் எனவும் பாடம்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[["பொன்னும்","பொன்",1,"pira","simile","gold"],["துகிரும்","துகிர்",1,"pira","simile","coral"],["முத்தும்","முத்து",1,"pira","simile","pearl"],["மணியும்","மணி",2,"pira","simile","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:219":{"book":"purananuru","num":219,"t":["உள்ளாற்றுக் கவலைப் புள்ளி நீழல்,","முழூஉ வள்ளுரம் உணக்கும் மள்ள!","புலவுதி மாதோ நீயே;","பலரால் அத்தை, நின் குறிஇருந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Warrior who dries out your whole strong body in the dappled shade on the fork of the river island, you are vexed with me; many indeed are those who sat with you in your resolve.","n":"The four lines carry the whole scene by implication: the king sits on the river island wasting away, others have already joined him, and the poet, arriving late, reads the king's displeasure as being with him. குறிஇருந்தோர் is read as those who sat by his purpose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vadakkiruththal","natpu","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 219","lb":"PN219","poet":"பெருஞ்கருவூர்ப்சதுக்கத்துப் பூதநாதனார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:220":{"book":"purananuru","num":220,"t":["பெருங்சோறு பயந்து, பல்யாண்டு புரந்த","பெருங்களிறு இழந்த பைதற் பாகன்","அதுசேர்ந்து அல்கிய அழுங்கல் ஆலை,","வெளில்பாழ் ஆகக் கண்டு கலுழ்ந்தாங்குக்,","கலங்கினேன் அல்லனோ, யானே-பொலந்தார்த்","தேர்வண் கிள்ளி போகிய","பேரிசை மூதூர் மன்றங் கண்டே?"],"p":"","r":"Like the grieving mahout who lost the great tusker that brought him plenty of food and kept him for many years, and weeps to see the empty post in the shed where it stood, was I not shaken when I saw the assembly hall of the famous old town that Killi, giver of chariots with golden garland, has left?","n":"The mahout was fed by the elephant (பெருஞ்சோறு பயந்து), so the simile casts the king as the provider and the poet as his dependant; the empty post (வெளில்) and the empty assembly (மன்றம்) are the poem's two images of absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai","paazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 220","lb":"PN220","poet":"பொத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பாகன்","பாகன்",2,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:11"},"pn:221":{"book":"purananuru","num":221,"t":["பாடுநர்க்கு ஈத்த பல்புக ழன்னே;","ஆடுநர்க்கு ஈத்த பேரன் பினனே;","அறவோர் புகழ்ந்த ஆய்கோ லன்னே;","திறவோர் புகழ்ந்த தின்நண் பினனே;","மகளிர் சாயல்; மைந்தர்க்கு மைந்து;","துகளறு கேள்வி உயர்ந்தோர் புக்கில்;","அனையன் என்னாது, அத்தக் கோனை,","நினையாக் கூற்றம் இன்னுயிர் உய்த்த்ன்று;","பைதல் ஒக்கல் தழீஇ, அதனை","வைகம் வம்மோ; வாய்மொழிப் புலவீர்!","‘நனந்தலை உலகம் அரந்தை தூங்கக்,","கெடுவில் நல்லிசை சூடி,","நடுகல் ஆயினன் புரவலன்’ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the dead king was: fame given to singers, love given to dancers, a just sceptre praised by the virtuous, firm friendship praised by the able, gentleness to women and strength to men, a home for the learned; Death, which does not weigh such worth, took his life, so he calls the truthful poets to come, hold the grieving kin, and revile Death, saying the patron has become a hero-stone wearing unfading fame while the wide world hangs in sorrow.","n":"The catalogue of six balanced half-lines is undone by one word, நினையாக் கூற்றம்: Death is blamed not for cruelty but for not thinking, for failing to weigh the very qualities the poem has just counted. The patron's end is a stone that wears fame like a garland (சூடி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","kootru","nadukal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 221","lb":"PN221","poet":"பொத்தியார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["vekuli","கொலை"]],"k":[],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:222":{"book":"purananuru","num":222,"t":["‘அழல் அவிர் வயங்கிழைப் பொலிந்த மேனி,","நிழலினும் போகா, நின் வெய்யோள் பயந்த","புகழ்சால் புதல்வன் பிறந்தபின் வா’ என","என்இவண் ஒழித்த அன்பி லாள!","எண்ணாது இருக்குவை அல்லை;","என்னிடம் யாது? மற்று இசைவெய் யோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the dead king, the poet recalls that he was told to come only after the famous son had been born to the king's wife, she who wears flame-bright jewels and never leaves him even as his shadow, and so was left behind here; he calls the king loveless and asks what place he now has, since the king, a lover of fame, is not one to sit without thinking of him.","n":"The poem does not name what the king was doing or what 'come' meant; read with 223 and the colophon's patron, the summons is to join him in his chosen death, so 'loveless' is a friend's reproach for being kept from dying with him, and the son's birth is the duty that held the poet back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vadakkiruththal","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 222","lb":"PN222","poet":"பொத்தியார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:223":{"book":"purananuru","num":223,"t":["பலர்க்கு நிழ லாகி, உலகம் மீக்கூறித்,","தலைப்போ கன்மையிற் சிறுவழி மடங்கி,","நிலைபெறு நடுகல் ஆகியக் கண்ணும்,","இடங் கொடுத்து அளிப்ப, மன்ற-உடம்போடு","இன்னுயிர் விரும்பும் கிழமைத்","தொன்னட் புடையார் தம்உழைச் செலினே!"],"p":"","r":"Having been shade to many and praised high by the world, the king, unable to go on, folded into a narrow place; yet even now that he has become an enduring hero-stone, he surely gives room and welcome to the old friends of long intimacy, whose bond is as close as life's love for the body, if they go to him.","n":"The hospitality the king showed alive (இடங் கொடுத்து அளிப்ப) is credited to the stone, so the friends who follow him in death are pictured as guests arriving at his gate; line 2 (தலைப்போ கன்மையிற் சிறுவழி மடங்கி) is compressed and may describe his last confinement rather than the stone's narrow site.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kizhamai","nadukal","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 223","lb":"PN223","poet":"பொத்தியார்","pat":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:224":{"book":"purananuru","num":224,"t":["அருப்பம் பேணாது அமர்கடந் ததூஉம்;","துணைபுணர் ஆயமொடு தசும்புடன் தொலைச்சி,","இரும்பாண் ஒக்கல் கடும்பு புரந்ததூஉம்;","அறம்அறக் கணட நெறிமாண் அவையத்து,","முறைநற்கு அறியுநர் முன்னுறப் புகழ்ந்த","பவியற் கொள்கைத் துகளறு மகளிரொடு,","பருதி உருவின் பல்படைப் புரிசை,","எருவை நுகர்ச்சி, யூப நெடுந்தூண்,","வேத வேள்வித் தொழில்முடித் ததூஉம்;","அறிந்தோன் மன்ற அறிவுடையாளன்;","இறந்தோன் தானே; அளித்துஇவ் வுலகம்","அருவி மாறி, அஞ்சுவரக் கருகிப்,","பெருவறம் கூர்ந்த வேனிற் காலைப்,","பசித்த ஆயத்துப் பயன்நிரை தருமார்,","பூவாட் கோவலர் பூவுடன் உதிரக்","கொய்துகட்டு அழித்த வேங்கையின்,","மெல்லியல் மகளிரும் இழைகளைந் தனரே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recounts the dead king's deeds: he overran battle without regard for forts, drank jars of toddy with his companions and kept the bards' large families, and in an assembly that knew right order completed the Vedic sacrifice with its sun-shaped layered altar and tall post, with his wives of spotless conduct; such a wise man is dead, and the world is to be pitied, for his gentle women have stripped off their ornaments like a vengai tree that cowherds have lopped bare, flowers and all, to feed their hungry herd in a summer of failed falls and great drought.","n":"The three -ததூஉம் clauses (fighting, feeding, sacrificing) build a sentence that never reaches a main verb of praise; it breaks into இறந்தோன் தானே. The closing simile is cruelly exact: the vengai is stripped not by malice but by need, as the women strip their jewels because the custom of widowhood demands it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","vendhan","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 224","lb":"PN224","poet":"கருங்குழல் ஆதனார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தசும்புடன்","தசும்பு",2,"unavu","gift_food","toddy jar"],["அருவி","அருவி",12,"pira","absent","waterfall"],["ஆயத்துப்","ஆயம்",14,"maa","simile","herd of cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",15,"seythi","simile","cowherds"],["பூவுடன்","பூ",15,"maram","simile","flower"],["வேங்கையின்","வேங்கை",16,"maram","simile","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:225":{"book":"purananuru","num":225,"t":["தலையோர் நுங்கின் தீங்சோறு மிசைய,","இடையோர் பழத்தின் பைங்கனி மாந்தக்,","கடையோர், விடுவாய்ப் பிசிரொடு சுடுகிழங்கு நுகர,","நிலமார் வையத்து வலமுறை வளைஇ,","வேந்துபீ டழித்த ஏந்துவேல் தானையொடு,","‘ஆற்றல்’ என்பதன் தோற்றம் கேள், இனிக்","கள்ளி போகிய களரியம் பறந்தலை,","முள்ளுடை வியன்காட் டதுவே-‘நன்றும்","சேட்சென்னி நலங்கிள்ளி கேட்குவன் கொல்?’","இன்னிசைப் பறையொடு வென்றி நுவலத்,","தூக்கணம் குரீஇத் தூங்குகூடு ஏய்ப்ப","ஒருசிறைக் கொளீஇய திரிவாய் வலம்புரி,","ஞாலங் காவலர் கடைத்தலைக்,","காலைத் தோன்றினும் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the king's army so long that its van ate sweet palmyra pulp while its middle ate ripe fruit and its rear roasted tubers, an army that circled the earth and broke kings' pride, and says that this strength is now to be seen in the thorny waste where kalli grows, the cremation ground; he grieves that the twisted valampuri conch, once proclaiming victory with the drums and now hung to one side like a weaver bird's hanging nest, may sound at dawn at the gates of other kings.","n":"The army is measured by what its three parts eat at the same moment, so its length is a span of the land's foods. Lines 13 and 14 are hard: the grief may be that the dead king's conch, now hung aside, will be blown at other kings' gates, his emblem passing to new masters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","padai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 225","lb":"PN225","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",1,"unavu","gift_food","palmyra pulp"],["பழத்தின்","பழம்",2,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"],["சுடுகிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",3,"unavu","gift_food","tuber"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",7,"maram","scene","spurge"],["பறையொடு","பறை",10,"parai","scene","drum"],["குரீஇத்","தூக்கணங் குரீஇ",11,"pul","simile","weaver bird"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",12,"pira","scene","right-whorled conch"]],"en":[["king","சேட்சென்னி நலங்கிள்ளி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:226":{"book":"purananuru","num":226,"t":["செற்றன்று ஆயினும், செயிர்த்தன்று ஆயினும்,","உற்றன்று ஆயினும், உய்வின்று மாதோ;","பாடுநர் போலக் கைதொழுது ஏத்தி,","இரந்தன்று ஆகல் வேண்டும்-பொலந்தார்","மண்டமர் கடக்கும் தானைத்","திண்தேர் வளவற் கொண்ட கூற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Whether it raged, burned with anger or fought, Death could not have escaped Valavan; to take the king of the golden garland, the strong chariot and the army that crosses fierce battle, it must have begged him, joining its hands and praising him as the singers do.","n":"The praise is double: no force could take him, and no request was ever refused by him, so Death succeeded only by joining the bards. The king's generosity becomes the cause of his death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","eegai","maram","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 226","lb":"PN226","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"சோழன் குளுமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","வளவற்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:227":{"book":"purananuru","num":227,"t":["நனிபே தையே, நயனில் கூற்றம்!","விரகுஇன் மையின் வித்துஅட்டு, உண்டனை","இன்னுங் காண்குவை, நன்வாய் ஆகுதல்;","ஒளிறுவாள் மறவரும், களிறும், மாவும்,","குருதியும் குரூஉப்புனற் பொருகளத்து ஒழிய,","நாளும் ஆனான் கடந்துஅட்டு, என்றும் நின்","வாடுபசி அருந்திய பழிதீர் ஆற்றல்","நின்னோர் அன்ன பொன்னியற் பெரும்பூண்","வளவன் என்னும் வண்டுமூசு கண்ணி","இனையோற் கொண்டனை ஆயின்,","இனியார் மற்றுநின் பசிதீர்ப் போரே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet calls Death a fool without sense, which for want of skill has cooked and eaten its own seed: Valavan, whose bee-swarmed garland and golden ornaments matched Death's own strength, fed Death's hunger every day by conquering and killing warriors, elephants and horses on fields flooded with blood; having taken such a man, who will now relieve its hunger?","n":"The mockery is a form of grief: by calling Death stupid, the poet keeps the king's killing power at the centre, and the threat in line 3 (you will yet see whether I speak true) is that Death will now go hungry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","maram","pedhaimai","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 227","lb":"PN227","poet":"ஆடுதுறை மாசாத்தனார்","pat":"சோழன் குளுமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிறும்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["மாவும்","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["வண்டுமூசு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",9,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["king","வளவன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:228":{"book":"purananuru","num":228,"t":["கலஞ்செய் கோவே! கலங்செய் கோவே!","இருள்தினிந் தன்ன குரூஉத்திறள் பருஉப்புகை","அகல்இரு விசும்பின் ஊன்றுஞ் சூளை,","நனந்தலை மூதூர்க் கலஞ்செய் கோவே!","அளியை நீயே; யாங்கு ஆகுவை கொல்?","நிலவரை சூட்டிய நீள்நெடுந் தானைப்","புலவர் புகழ்ந்த பொய்யா நல்இசை,","விரிகதிர் ஞாயிறு விசும்பு இவர்ந் தன்ன","சேண்விளங்கு சிறப்பின், செம்பியர் மருகன்","கொடிநுடங்கு யானை நெடுமா வளவன்","தேவர் உலகம் எய்தினன்; ஆதலின்,","அன்னோர் கவிக்கும் கண்ணகன் தாழி","வனைதல் வேட்டனை அயின், எனையதூஉம்","இருநிலம் திகிரியாப், பெருமலை","மண்ணா, வனைதல் ஒல்லுமோ, நினக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet calls on the potter of the old wide town, whose kiln plants in the sky a smoke as thick as darkness, and pities him: Valavan, descendant of the Chembiyans, whose army crowned the earth and whose fame shone far like the sun climbing the sky, has reached the world of the gods, and if the potter means to make an urn wide enough to cover him, he would need the great earth for his wheel and a great mountain for his clay.","n":"Grief is spoken as a practical problem set to a craftsman: the poet never weeps, he only shows that no urn could be large enough, and the triple call to the potter (lines 1 and 4) is the lament's refrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 228","lb":"PN228","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"சோழன் குளுமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"ஆனந்தப் பையுள்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆனந்தப் பையுள்"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோவே","கலஞ்செய் கோ",1,"seythi","scene","potter"],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","scene","elephant"],["தாழி","தாழி",12,"pira","scene","burial urn"]],"en":[["king","வளவன்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:229":{"book":"purananuru","num":229,"t":["ஆடு இயல் அழல் குட்டத்து","ஆர் இருள் அரை இரவில்","முடப் பனையத்து வேர் முதலாக்","கடைக் குளத்துக் கயம் காயப்,","பங்குனி உயர் அழுவத்துத்,","தலை நாள்மீன் நிலை திரிய,","நிலை நாள்மீன் அதன்எதிர் ஏர்தரத்,","தொல் நாள்மீன் துறை படியப்,","பாசிச் செல்லாது, ஊசித் துன்னாது","அளக்கர்த் திணை விளக்காகக்,","கனைஎரி பரப்பக், கால்எதிர்பு பொங்கி,","ஒருமீன் விழுந்தன்றால், விசும்பி னானே:","அதுகண்டு, யாமும்,பிறரும் பல்வேறு இரவலர்,","‘பறைஇசை அருவி நல்நாட்டுப் பொருநன்","நோயிலன் ஆயின் நன்றுமன் தில்’லென","அழிந்த நெஞ்சம் மடியுளம் பரப்ப,","அஞ்சினம்: எழுநாள் வந்தன்று, இன்றே;","மைந்துடை யானை கை வைத்து உறங்கவும்,","திண்பிணி முரசும் கண்கிழிந்து உருளவும்,","காவல் வெண்குடை கால்பரிந்து உலறவும்,","கால்இயல் கலிமாக் கதிஇன்றி வைகவும்,","மேலோர் உலகம் எய்தினன்; ஆகலின்,","ஒண்தொடி மகளிர்க்கு உறுதிணை ஆகித்,","தன்துணை ஆயம் மறந்தனன் கொல்லோ-","பகைவர்ப் பிணிக்கும் ஆற்றல், நசைவர்க்கு","அளந்து கொடை அறியா ஈகை,","மணிவரை அன்ன மாஅ யோனே?"],"p":"","r":"The poet records that on a Panguni midnight, when certain stars stood in their places, a star fell from the sky blazing like a lamp for the sea-girt land; seeing it, he and many other suppliants feared for the lord of the good land of drum-loud falls, and on the seventh day, with his elephant asleep with its trunk laid down, his drum rolling with its face torn, his white umbrella broken and his horse standing still, the king went to the upper world; the poet asks whether, now a support to bright-bangled women there, he has forgotten his own companions, he who bound his foes and gave without measure, dark like a sapphire mountain.","n":"The opening twelve lines fix the night by asterisms (lines 1 to 8), which this reading cannot identify with confidence; what is clear is that the omen comes first and the death seven days later, and that the royal emblems (elephant, drum, umbrella, horse) fail before he dies. The closing question imagines him in heaven, a support to women there, as if he had simply moved patrons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","vendhan","eegai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 229","lb":"PN229","poet":"கூடலூர் கிழார்","pat":"கோச்சேரமான் யானைக்கட்சே எய் மாந்தரஞ்சேரல் இரும்பொறை","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",14,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["யானை","யானை",18,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முரசும்","முரசு",19,"parai","absent","war drum"],["கலிமாக்","கலிமா",21,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:230":{"book":"purananuru","num":230,"t":["கன்று அமர் ஆயம் கானத்து அல்கவும்,","வெங்கால் வம்பலர் வேண்டுபுலத்து உறையவும்,","களம்மலி குப்பை காப்பில வைகவும்,","விலங்குபகைகடிந்த கலங்காச் செங்கோல்,","வையகம் புகழ்ந்த வயங்குவினை ஒள்வாள்,","பொய்யா எழினி பொருதுகளம் சேர-","ஈன்றோர் நீத்த குழவி போலத்,","தன்அமர் சுற்றம் தலைத்தலை இனையக்,","கடும்பசி கலக்கிய இடும்பைகூர் நெஞ்சமொடு","நோய் உழந்து வைகிய உலகிலும், மிக நனி","நீ இழந் தனையே, அறனில் கூற்றம்!","வாழ்தலின் வரூஉம் வயல்வளன் அறியான்,","வீழ்குடி உழவன் வித்து உண்டாஅங்கு","ஒருவன் ஆர் உயிர் உண்ணாய் ஆயின்,","நேரார் பல்லுயிர் பருகி,","ஆர்குவை மன்னோ, அவன் அமர்அடு களத்தே."],"p":"","r":"While Ezhini lived, herds stayed in the forest, travellers camped where they liked and grain heaps lay unguarded; now that the unfailing Ezhini of the unwavering sceptre and famed sword has fallen in battle, his kin grieve everywhere like a baby left by its mother and the world suffers in hunger, yet the poet tells unjust Death that it has lost more than they: like a farmer of a failing house who eats his seed grain not knowing the harvest it would bring, Death has eaten one man's life when, had it spared him, it could have drunk many enemies' lives on his battlefield.","n":"The first three lines describe peace by things left unguarded, so the king's protection is shown as an absence of fear. The argument against Death is an economic one: the loss of the protector is counted first as the people's loss and then, as a bitter joke, as Death's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sengol","kaiyarunilai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 230","lb":"PN230","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"அதியமான் தகடூர் பொருது வீழ்ந்த எழினி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையுறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["கன்று","கன்று",1,"maa","scene","calf"],["ஆயம்","ஆயம்",1,"maa","scene","herd"],["குப்பை","குப்பை",3,"unavu","scene","grain heap"],["உழவன்","உழவன்",13,"seythi","simile","farmer"],["வித்து","வித்து",13,"unavu","simile","seed grain"]],"en":[["king","எழினி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:231":{"book":"purananuru","num":231,"t":["எரிபுனக் குறவன் குறையல் அன்ன","கரிபுற விறகின் ஈம ஒள்அழல்,","குருகினும் குறுகுக; குறுகாது சென்று,","விசும்பஉற நீளினும் நீள்க: பசுங்கதிர்","திங்கள் அன்ன வெண்குடை","ஒண்ஞாயிறு அன்னோன் புகழ் மாயலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Let the bright flame of the pyre, of charred wood like the half-burnt logs of a hill farmer's burnt field, shrink low, or let it not shrink but rise till it touches the sky: the fame of the man who was like the bright sun, with a white umbrella like the cool-rayed moon, will not perish.","n":"Avvai concedes everything about the pyre (let it be small, let it be huge) to deny it the one thing that matters; the sun and moon of the last lines are what fire cannot reach. குருகினும் in line 3 is unclear (smaller than a heron, or than a smith's bellows); the sense of shrinking is not in doubt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kaiyarunilai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 231","lb":"PN231","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","simile","hill farmer"],["விறகின்","விறகு",2,"maram","scene","firewood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:232":{"book":"purananuru","num":232,"t":["இல்லா கியரோ, காலை மாலை!","அல்லா கியர், யான் வாழும் நாளே!","நடுகல் பீலி சூட்டி, நார்அரி","சிறுகலத்து உகுப்பவும் கொள்வன் கொல்லொ-","கோடு உயர் பிறங்குமலை கெழீஇய","நாடு உடன் கொடுப்புவும் கொள்ளா தோனே?"],"p":"","r":"Let there be no more mornings and evenings, and no more days of my life: will he who would not accept even a country of high-peaked mountains given to him now accept the toddy, strained through fibre, poured from a small cup on his hero-stone crowned with a peacock feather?","n":"The question turns on accepting (கொள்வன் கொல்): the man famous for refusing even a whole country as a gift is now offered a small cup, so the hero-stone rite is felt as an insult to his greatness and the poet's grief takes the form of disbelief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","kaiyarunilai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 232","lb":"PN232","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்; தும்பை","ctu":"கையறுநிலை; பாண்பாட்டும் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை","பாண்பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பீலி","பீலி",3,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["நார்அரி","நார் அரி",3,"unavu","gift_food","strained toddy"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:233":{"book":"purananuru","num":233,"t":["பொய்யா கியரோ! பொய்யா கியரோ!","பாவடி யானை பரிசிலர்க்கு அருகாச்","சீர்கெழு நோன்றாள் அகுதைகண் தோன்றிய","பொன்புனை திகிரியின் பொய்யா கியரோ!","‘இரும்பாண் ஒக்கல் தலைவன், பெரும்பூண்,","போர்அடு தானை, எவ்வி மார்பின்","எஃகுஉறு விழுப்புண் பல’ என","வைகறு விடியல், இயம்பிய குரலே."],"p":"","r":"Let it be false, let it be false, as false as the golden wheel that was said to appear with Akuthai, who never lacked elephants to give to suppliants: the voice that at dawn announced that Evvi, leader of the bards' great families, of great ornaments and a battle-killing army, had many deep spear wounds in his chest.","n":"The poem is all denial: the death itself is never stated, only the report of the wounds, which the poet wishes into a famous falsehood. What the story of Akuthai's golden wheel was cannot be recovered from the poem; it serves here as a proverbially false report.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","eegai","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 233","lb":"PN233","poet":"வெள்ளெருக்கிலையார்","pat":"வேள் எவ்வி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[["patron","அகுதை",3],["patron","எவ்வி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:234":{"book":"purananuru","num":234,"t":["நோகோ யானே? தேய்கமா காலை!","பிடி அடி அன்ன சிறுவழி மெழுகித்,","தன்அமர் காதலி புன்மேல் வைத்த","இன்சிறு பிண்டம் யாங்குஉண் டனன்கொல்-","உலகுபுகத் திறந்த வாயில்","பலரோடு உண்டல் மரீஇ யோனே?"],"p":"","r":"Shall I grieve? Let the mornings wane: how did he eat the small sweet ball of rice that his beloved wife set on grass, on a small spot she smeared clean no bigger than a she-elephant's footprint, he who was used to eating with many at a gate opened for the whole world to enter?","n":"Everything in the rite is small (சிறுவழி, இன்சிறு பிண்டம்) against the wide gate of his life, and the grief is that the host who never ate alone is now fed alone, by one hand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 234","lb":"PN234","poet":"வெள்ளெருக்கிலையார்","pat":"வேள் எவ்வி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["புன்மேல்","புல்",3,"maram","scene","grass"],["பிண்டம்","பிண்டம்",4,"unavu","gift_food","rice ball offering"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:235":{"book":"purananuru","num":235,"t":["சிறியகட் பெறினே, எமக்கீயும்; மன்னே!","பெரிய கட் பெறினே,","யாம் பாடத், தான்மகிழ்ந்து உண்ணும்; மன்னே!","சிறுசோற் றானும் நனிபல கலத்தன்; மன்னே!","பெருஞ்சோற்றானும் நனிபல கலத்தன்; மன்னே!","என்பொடு தடிபடு வழியெல்லாம் எமக்கீயும்; மன்னே!","அம்பொடு வேல்நுழை வழியெல்லாம் தான்நிற்கும் மன்னே!","நரந்தம் நாறும் தன் கையால்,","புலவு நாறும் என்தலை தைவரும்! மன்னே","அருந்தலை இரும்பாணர் அகன்மண்டைத் துளையுரீஇ,","இரப்போர் கையுளும் போகி,","புரப்போர் புன்கண் பாவை சோர,","அஞ்சொல் நுண்தேர்ச்சிப் புலவர் நாவில்","சென்றுவீழ்ந் தன்று, அவன்","அருநிறத்து இயங்கிய வேலே!","ஆசாகு எந்தை யாண்டுஉளன் கொல்லோ?","இனிப், பாடுநரும் இல்லை; படுநர்க்குஒன்று ஈகுநரும் இல்லை;","பனித்துறைப் பகன்றை நறைக் கொள் மாமலர்","சூடாது வைகியாங்குப், பிறர்க்கு ஒன்று","ஈயாது வீயும் உயிர்தவப் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Avvai remembers how Anji gave her any little toddy he got and drank the larger share happily while she sang, shared his rice in many dishes whether it was little or much, gave her every bone with meat on it and stood himself wherever arrow and spear went in, and stroked her flesh-smelling head with his narantham-scented hand; the spear that pierced his chest went through the bowls of the bards and the hands of suppliants, dimmed the eyes of those he kept, and fell on the tongues of poets; now there is no one to sing and no one to give to singers, and many lives die giving nothing, like the fragrant pakanrai flowers of the cold ford that wither unworn.","n":"The repeated மன்னே ('it was so, once') turns each memory into a loss as it is spoken. The spear is followed through everyone who lived off Anji, so one wound becomes the wound of a whole economy of giving; the last lines turn from him to the rest, who die like flowers no one wore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kaiyarunilai","anbu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 235","lb":"PN235","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சிறியகட்","கள்",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["சிறுசோற்","சோறு",4,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["தடிபடு","தடி",6,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["நரந்தம்","நரந்தம்",8,"maram","ornament","narantham scent"],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",18,"maram","simile","pakanrai flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:236":{"book":"purananuru","num":236,"t":["கலைஉணக் கிழிந்த, முழவுமருள் பெரும்பழம்","சிலைகெழு குறவர்க்கு அல்குமிசைவு ஆகும்","மலை கெழு நாட! மா வண் பாரி","கலந்த கேண்மைக்கு ஒவ்வாய், நீ; எற்","புலந்தனை யாகுவை- புரந்த யாண்டே","பெருந்தகு சிறப்பின் நட்பிற்கு ஒல்லாது","ஒருங்குவரல் விடாஅது ‘ஒழிக’ எனக்கூறி,","இனையை ஆதலின் நினக்கு மற்றுயான்","மேயினேன் அன்மை யானே; ஆயினும்,","இம்மை போலக் காட்டி, உம்மை","இடையில் காட்சி நின்னோடு","உடன்உறைவு ஆக்குக, உயர்ந்த பாலே!"],"p":"","r":"Addressing Pari, lord of the hill land where the great jackfruit, like a drum, torn open by the stag's feeding becomes the hunters' food, Kapilar says that he was not true to their close friendship: he must have been angry with him, for in the years he kept him, against the honour of their friendship, he would not let him come with him and said 'stay behind', and so Kapilar is not with him; still, may high fate, which showed them this life together, grant him in the next life to live with Pari without parting.","n":"The opening image of plenty (the stag's leftover fruit feeding the hunters) is Pari's hill as it was; the reproach that follows is the same as Pothiyar's in 222, that the patron forbade his friend to follow him, and Kapilar answers it not by dying but by asking fate for the next birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","marumai","kaiyarunilai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 236","lb":"PN236","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கலைஉணக்","கலை",1,"maa","scene","stag"],["முழவுமருள்","முழவு",1,"parai","simile","drum"],["பெரும்பழம்","பழம்",1,"unavu","gift_food","jackfruit"],["குறவர்க்கு","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill hunters"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:237":{"book":"purananuru","num":237,"t":["‘நீடுவாழ்க!’ என்று, யான் நெடுங்கடை குறுகிப்,","பாடி நின்ற பசிநாட் கண்ணே,","‘கோடைக் காலத்துக் கொழுநிழல் ஆகிப்,","பொய்த்தல் அறியா உரவோன் செவிமுதல்","வித்திய பனுவல் விளைந்தன்று நன்று’ என","நச்சி இருந்த நசைபழுது ஆக,","அட்ட குழிசி அழற்பயந் தாஅங்கு,","‘அளியர் தாமே ஆர்க’ என்னா","அறன்இல் கூற்றம் திறனின்று துணிய,","ஊழின் உருப்ப எருக்கிய மகளிர்","வாழைப் பூவின் வளைமுறி சிதற,","முதுவாய் ஒக்கல் பரிசிலர் இரங்கக்","கள்ளி போகிய களரியம் பறந்தலை,","வெள்வேல் விடலை சென்றுமாய்ந் தனனே;","ஆங்கு அது நோயின்று ஆக, ஓங்குவரைப்","புலிபார்த்து ஒற்றிய களிற்றுஇரை பிழைப்பின்,","எலிபார்த்து ஒற்றாது ஆகும்; மலி திரைக்","கடல்மண்டு புனலின் இழுமெனச் சென்று,","நனியுடைப் பரிசில் தருகம்,","எழுமதி, நெஞ்சே ! துணிபுமுந் துறுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"On his day of hunger the poet came to the patron's tall gate singing 'long may you live', trusting that the verse he had sown in the ear of that unfailing man, rich shade in a hot season, had ripened; that hope came to nothing, as if the cooking pot had yielded only fire, for unjust Death, never saying 'let these poor people eat', struck without reason, and amid widows scattering their broken bangles like plantain-flower petals and grieving old kin and gift-seekers, the young white-speared hero went to the kalli-grown cremation ground and perished; yet let that be no sorrow: a tiger that misses the elephant it stalked will not stalk a rat, so he bids his heart rise with resolve and rush like a river into the sea to bring great gifts.","n":"The poem moves from kaiyarunilai to a bard's resolve: grief is set aside by the tiger proverb, which makes the poet's pride (only a great patron will do) the reason to stop mourning. The cremation line (13) is the same as 225 line 7.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","kaiyarunilai","kootru","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 237","lb":"PN237","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"இளவெளிமான்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குழிசி","குழிசி",7,"pira","simile","cooking pot"],["வாழைப்","வாழை",11,"maram","simile","plantain"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",13,"maram","scene","spurge"],["புலிபார்த்து","புலி",16,"maa","simile","tiger"],["களிற்றுஇரை","களிறு",16,"maa","simile","elephant"],["எலிபார்த்து","எலி",17,"maa","simile","rat"],["புனலின்","புனல்",18,"pira","simile","river flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:238":{"book":"purananuru","num":238,"t":["கவிசெந் தாழிக் குவிபுறத்து இருந்த","செவிசெஞ் சேவலும் பொகுவலும் வெருவா,","வாய் வன் காக்கையும் கூகையும் கூடிப்","பேஎய் ஆயமொடு பெட்டாங்கு வழங்கும்","காடுமுன் னினனே, கட்கா முறுநன்;","தொடிகழி மகளிரின் தொல்கவின் வாடிப்,","பாடுநர் கடும்பும் பையென் றனவே;","தோடுகொள் முரசும் கிழிந்தன, கண்ணே;","ஆள்இல், வரைபோல் யானையும் மருப்புஇழந் தனவே;","வெந்திறல் கூற்றம் பெரும்பேது உறுப்ப,","எந்தை ஆகுல அதற்படல் அறியேன்;","அந்தோ! அளியேன் வந்தனென்; மன்ற","என்ஆ குவர்கொல், என் துன்னி யோரே?","மாரி இரவின், மரங்கவிழ் பொழுதின்,","ஆரஞர் உற்ற நெஞ்சமொடு , ஓராங்குக்","கண்இல் ஊமன் கடற்பட் டாங்கு,","வரையளந்து அறியாத் திரையரு நீத்தத்து,","அவல மறுசுழி மறுகலின்,","தவலே நன்றுமன் ; தகுதியும் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"He who loved toddy has gone ahead to the cremation forest, where on the round back of an overturned red urn the red-eared cock and the pokuval, unafraid, and the hard-beaked crow and the owl gather and roam at will with troops of ghosts; the bards' families have wilted, their old beauty gone like women who have taken off their bangles, the strapped drums have their faces torn, the riderless mountain-like elephants have lost their tusks; since fierce Death has struck, the poet, who came not knowing his lord had fallen, asks what will become of those who depend on him, and, his heart in anguish, whirling like a blind mute fallen into the sea on a rainy night when trees come down, says that to perish would be best and fitting.","n":"The poet arrives too late, and the poem's horror is placed in the birds who are no longer afraid; the blind mute in the sea is a man who can neither see a way nor call for help. Line 11 (ஆகுல அதற்படல்) is corrupt or obscure, and the reading of it as his not knowing of the death is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","kootru","idumbai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 238","lb":"PN238","poet":"பெருஞ்சித்திரனார்","pat":"இளவெளிமான்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["தாழிக்","தாழி",1,"pira","scene","burial urn"],["சேவலும்","சேவல்",2,"pul","scene","cock"],["பொகுவலும்","பொகுவல்",2,"pul","scene","a bird of the burning ground"],["காக்கையும்","காக்கை",3,"pul","scene","crow"],["கூகையும்","கூகை",3,"pul","scene","owl"],["முரசும்","முரசு",8,"parai","absent","war drum"],["யானையும்","யானை",9,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:239":{"book":"purananuru","num":239,"t":["தொடி யுடைய தோள் மணந்தணன் ;","கடி காவிற் பூச் சூடினன் ;","தண் கமழுஞ் சாந்து நீவினன் ;","செற் றோரை வழி தபுத்தனன் ;","நட் டோரை உயர்பு கூறினன் ;","வலியரென, வழி மொழியலன் ;","மெலியரென, மீக் கூறலன்;","பிறரைத் தான் இரப் பறியலன் ;","இரந் தோர்க்கு மறுப் பறியலன் ;","வேந்துடை அவையத்து ஓங்குபுகழ் தோற்றினன்;","வருபடை எதிர் தாங்கினன் ;","பெயர் படை புறங் கண்டனன் ;","கடும் பரிய மாக் கடவினன் ;","நெடுந் தெருவில் தேர் வழங்கினன் ;","ஓங்கு இயற் களிறு ஊர்ந்தனன்;","தீஞ் செறி தசும்பு தொலைச்சினன்;","பாண் உவப்பப் பசி தீர்த்தனன்;","மயக்குடைய மொழி விடுத்தனன்; ஆங்குச்","செய்ப எல்லாம் செய்தனன் ஆகலின்-","இடுக ஒன்றோ ! சுடுக ஒன்றோ !","படுவழிப் படுக, இப் புகழ்வெய்யோன் தலையே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet lists all the dead man did: he embraced bangled women, wore flowers from guarded groves, smeared cool fragrant sandal, destroyed his foes' line, raised his friends, did not fawn on the strong or lord it over the weak, never begged and never refused a beggar, won fame in kings' assemblies, held off advancing armies and saw retreating ones turn their backs, rode swift horses, drove chariots down long streets, rode tall elephants, drained jars of sweet toddy, ended the bards' hunger to their joy, and gave up confusing speech; since he did everything that should be done, let them bury him or burn him, wherever this lover of fame's head may fall.","n":"Pleasures (women, flowers, sandal, toddy) stand in the same list as justice, courage and giving, with no ranking between them: a complete life is a full life, and because it was complete the body's fate does not matter, a defiant answer to the funeral rite itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","saakkaadu","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 239","lb":"PN239","poet":"பேரெயின் முறுவலார்","pat":"நம்பி நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பூச்","பூ",2,"maram","ornament","flower"],["சாந்து","சாந்து",3,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["மாக்","மா",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறு","களிறு",15,"maa","scene","elephant"],["தசும்பு","தசும்பு",16,"unavu","gift_food","toddy jar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:240":{"book":"purananuru","num":240,"t":["ஆடு நடைப் புரவியும், களிறும், தேரும்,","வாடா யாணர் நாடும் ஊரும்,","பாடுநர்க்கு அருகா ஆஅய் அண்டிரன்","கோடுஏந்து அல்குல், குறுந்தொடி மகளிரொடு","காலன் என்னும் கண்ணிலி உய்ப்ப,","மேலோர் உலகம் எய்தினன் எனாஅப்,","பொத்த அறையுள் போழ்வாய்க் கூகை,","‘சுட்டுக் குவி’ எனச் செத்தோர்ப் பயிரும்","கள்ளியம் பறந்தலை ஒருசிறை அல்கி,","ஒள்ளெரி நைப்ப உடம்பு மாய்ந்தது;","புல்லென் கண்ணர் புரவலர்க் காணாது,","கல்லென் சுற்றமொடு கையழிந்து, புலவர்","வாடிய பசியர் ஆகிப், பிறர்","நாடுபடு செலவினர் ஆயினர், இனியே."],"p":"","r":"Aay Andiran, who never stinted singers of dancing-gaited horses, elephants, chariots, and lands and towns of unfailing yield, has been carried off with his women of small bangles by the eyeless one called Kaalan to the upper world; resting on one side of the kalli-grown waste where the split-beaked owl in its hollow calls to the dead 'burn and heap', his body was consumed by the bright fire, and now the poets, eyes dimmed, not seeing their patron, helpless with their clamouring kin and wasted with hunger, have become travellers to other men's lands.","n":"Line 4 sends his women with him to the upper world, so the poem records wives dying with the chief. The last image is the social consequence of a patron's death: his poets become migrants, the opposite of the fixed bounty listed in lines 1 to 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","eegai","parisil","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 240","lb":"PN240","poet":"குட்டுவன் கீரனார்","pat":"ஆய்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புரவியும்","புரவி",1,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["களிறும்","களிறு",1,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["கூகை","கூகை",7,"pul","scene","owl"],["கள்ளியம்","கள்ளி",9,"maram","scene","spurge"]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய் அண்டிரன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:12"},"pn:241":{"book":"purananuru","num":241,"t":["‘திண்தேர் இரவலர்க்கு, ஈத்த, தண்தார்","அண்டிரன் வரூஉம்’ என்ன, ஒண்தொடி","வச்சிரத் தடக்கை நெடியோன் கோயிலுள்,","போர்ப்புறு முரசும் கறங்க,","ஆர்ப்புஎழுந் தன்றால், விசும்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Andiran, the cool-garlanded giver of sturdy chariots to suppliants, is told as arriving among the gods: at the cry that he is coming, the leather-covered drum sounds in the palace of the tall one with the thunderbolt in his great hand, and a cheer goes up in the sky.","n":"Death is never named. The lament is turned inside out into a welcome: the only thing said of the king is that he gave chariots, and that alone is what the heavenly hall announces, so his giving is presented as his passage. The god is named only by his weapon (வச்சிரத் தடக்கை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","eegai","marumai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 241","lb":"PN241","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தண்தார்","தார்",1,"maram","ornament",""],["முரசும்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","அண்டிரன்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:242":{"book":"purananuru","num":242,"t":["இளையோர் சூடார் ; வளையோர் கொய்யார் ;","நல்யாழ் மருப்பின் மெல்ல வாங்கிப்,","பாணன் சூடான் ; பாடினி அணியாள் ;","ஆண்மை தோன்ற ஆடவர்க் கடந்த","வல்வேற் சாத்தன் மாய்ந்த பின்றை","முல்லையும் பூத்தியோ, ஒல்லையூர் நாட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"With Saththan of the strong spear dead, who overcame warriors so that his manhood showed, no young man wears the mullai, no bangled woman plucks it, the bard does not draw it down with the curved arm of his lute to wear it, and the bard-woman does not adorn herself with it; the poet asks the flower whether it still blooms in the land of Ollaiyur.","n":"The four refusals name his whole household of dependents: the young fighters, the women, the bard and the bard-woman. The question to the flower is a reproach: the land goes on producing what no one now has the heart to use, so the flower that should be ornament becomes the measure of loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 242","lb":"PN242","poet":"குடவாயிற் தீரத்தனார்","pat":"ஒல்லையூர் கிழான் மகன் பெருஞ்சாத்தன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முல்லையும்","முல்லை",6,"maram","scene",""],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","சாத்தன்",5],["town","ஒல்லையூர்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:243":{"book":"purananuru","num":243,"t":["இனிநினைந்து இரக்கம் ஆகின்று ; திணிமணல்","செய்வுறு பாவைக்கு கொய்பூத் தைஇத்,","தண்கயம் ஆடும் மகளிரொடு கைபிணைந்து,","தழுவுவழித் தழீஇத், தூங்குவழித் தூங்கி,","மறைஎனல் அறியா மாயமில் ஆயமொடு","உயர்சினை மருதத் துறையுறத் தாழ்ந்து,","நீர்நணிப் படிகோடு ஏறிச், சீர்மிகக்,","கரையவர் மருளத், திரையகம் பிதிர,","நெடுநீர்க் குட்டத்துத் துடுமெனப் பாய்ந்து,","குளித்துமணற் கொண்ட கல்லா இளமை","அளிதோ தானே! யாண்டுண்டு கொல்லோ-","“தொடித்தலை விழுத்தண்டு ஊன்றி, நடுக்குற்று,","இருமிடை மிடைந்த சிலசொல்","பெருமூ தாளரோம்” ஆகிய எமக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"An old man remembers the untaught youth in which he offered flowers to a doll of packed sand, joined hands with the girls bathing in the cool pond, swayed and embraced with a guileless crowd, climbed the low marutham branch at the ford and leapt into the deep water with a splash while those on the bank gasped, and dived to bring up sand; he pities it, and asks where it is now for him, propped trembling on a ringed staff, a very old man of few words broken by coughing.","n":"The colophon names the poet after the ringed staff of line 12, so the poem supplied its own author's name. The youth is praised as கல்லா, untaught, and its worth lies exactly in that: no secrecy, no calculation. The colophon files a lament for one's own youth under கையறுநிலை, which elsewhere is grief for a dead patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 243","lb":"PN243","poet":"தொடித்தலை விழுத்தண்டினார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["மருதத்","மருதம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["கொய்பூத்","பூ",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தண்கயம்","கயம்",3,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:244":{"book":"purananuru","num":244,"t":["பாணர் சென்னியும் வண்டுசென்று ஊதா;","விறலியர் முன்கையும் தொடியிற் பொலியா;","இரவல் மாக்களும் .. .. .. .. .. .. .. ."],"p":"","r":"A fragment: the bees no longer hum over the bards' heads, the bard-women's forearms no longer shine with bangles, and the suppliants too... (the rest is lost).","n":"The bees are absent because the bards wear no flowers, and the bangles because no gold is given: both are signs of a patron's death read through what his dependents no longer have. Whose death it was is lost with the rest of the poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 244","lb":"PN244","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்,","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["வண்டுசென்று","வண்டு",1,"pira","absent",""],["தொடியிற்","தொடி",2,"pira","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:245":{"book":"purananuru","num":245,"t":["யங்குப் பெரிது ஆயினும், நோய்அளவு எனைத்தே,","உயிர்செகுக் கல்லா மதுகைத்து அன்மையின்?","கள்ளி போகிய களரியம் பறந்தலை","வெள்ளிடைப் பொத்திய விளைவிறகு ஈமத்து,","ஒள்ளழற் பள்ளிப் பாயல் சேர்த்தி,","ஞாங்கர் மாய்ந்தனள், மடந்தை ;","இன்னும் வாழ்வல் ; என்இதன் பண்பே!"],"p":"","r":"The king asks what measure grief can have, however great, if it lacks the strength to take life: his young wife has been laid on a bed of bright flame on a pyre of seasoned wood in the bare, kalli-grown open ground and has died there, and he still lives; what kind of thing is this?","n":"The grief is turned against itself: it is called great and then shamed for being too weak to kill. The colophon calls it கையறுநிலை, but by the Tholkappiyam's list it is the husband's தபுதார நிலை; the poet is the king himself. Line 1 opens on யங்குப், which reads as யாங்குப் with its first letter damaged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 245","lb":"PN245","poet":"சேரமான் கோட்டம்பலத்துத் துஞ்சிய மாக்கோதை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",3,"maram","scene",""],["விளைவிறகு","விறகு",4,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:246":{"book":"purananuru","num":246,"t":["பல்சான் றீரே ; பல்சான் றீரே","செல்கெனச் சொல்லாது, ஒழிகென விலக்கும்,","பொல்லாச் சூழ்ச்சிப் பல்சான் றீரே;","துணிவரிக் கொடுங்காய் வாள்போழ்ந் தட்ட","காழ்போல் நல்விளர் நறுநெய் தீண்டாது,","அடைஇடைக் கிடந்த கைபிழி பிண்டம்","வெள்என் சாந்தொடு புளிப்பெய்து அட்ட","வேளை வெந்தை, வல்சி ஆகப்,","பரற்பெய் பள்ளிப் பாயின்று வதியும்","உயவற் பெண்டிரேம் அல்லேம் மாதோ;","பெருங்காட்டுப் பண்ணிய கருங்கோட்டு ஈமம்","நுமக்குஅரிது ஆகுக தில்ல; எமக்குஎம்","பெருந்தோள் கணவன் மாய்ந்தென அரும்புஅற","வள்இதழ் அவிழ்ந்த தாமரை","நள்இரும் பொய்கையும் தீயும் ஓரற்றே!"],"p":"","r":"Perunkoppendu, queen of Bhoothapandiyan, rebukes the many elders who will not say 'go' but hold her back with 'stay': she is not one of the wretched widows who, untouched by fragrant ghee, eat leaf-wrapped hand-squeezed rice cooked with white sesame paste and tamarind and boiled velai greens, and sleep on gravel with no mat; let the great pyre of dark logs be hard for them, but for her, since her broad-shouldered husband is dead, a lotus pond full of open flowers and the fire are one.","n":"Her list of what she will not become is the most exact account of a widow's observances in the anthology: no ghee, leaf-wrapped rice, sesame paste and tamarind, boiled greens, a gravel bed. She calls the elders' counsel பொல்லாச் சூழ்ச்சி, so the poem sets a wife's will against the wisdom of the assembly. By the Tholkappiyam's list the situation is பாலை நிலை (the wife who enters the fire with her husband); the colophon files it as ஆனந்தப் பையுள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaimmai","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 246","lb":"PN246","poet":"பூதப் பாண்டியன் தேவி பெருங்கோப்பெண்டு","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"ஆனந்தப் பையுள்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆனந்தப் பையுள்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கொடுங்காய்","கொடுங்காய்",4,"unavu","absent","cucumber"],["நறுநெய்","நெய்",5,"unavu","absent",""],["பிண்டம்","பிண்டம்",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புளிப்பெய்து","புளி",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வேளை","வேளை",8,"maram","gift_food","velai greens"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",14,"maram","simile",""],["பொய்கையும்","பொய்கை",15,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:247":{"book":"purananuru","num":247,"t":["யானை தந்த முளிமர விறகின்","கானவர் பொத்திய ஞெலிதீ விளக்கத்து;","மடமான் பெருநிரை வைகுதுயில் எடுப்பி,","மந்தி சீக்கும் அணங்குடை முன்றிலில்,","நீர்வார் கூந்தல் இரும்புறம் தாழப்,","பேரஞர்க் கண்ணள், பெருங்காடு நோக்கித்,","தெருமரும் அம்ம தானே- தன் கொழுநன்","முழுவுகண் துயிலாக் கடியுடை வியனகர்ச்","சிறுநனி தமியள் ஆயினும்,","இன்னுயிர் நடுங்குந்தன் இளமைபுறங் கொடுத்தே!"],"p":"","r":"In the burning ground, lit by the drill-fire that foresters kindled with dead wood the elephant brought, where a herd of gentle deer is roused from its sleep and female monkeys scamper in a courtyard where spirits dwell, the queen with wet hair falling down her dark back and eyes of great sorrow looks at the great burning ground and wavers: she who was seldom alone in her husband's wide, guarded mansion, where the drum's eye never slept, now turns her back on her youth, her sweet life trembling.","n":"The burning ground is drawn with forest life (the elephant's wood, the foresters' fire, startled deer, monkeys), so the wildness of the place is set against the guarded, drum-loud mansion she has left. Whether she is about to enter the fire or to live on as a widow the poem does not quite say; line 9 (சிறுநனி தமியள் ஆயினும்) and the sense of சீக்கும் in line 4 are hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","saakkaadu","ilamai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 247","lb":"PN247","poet":"மதுரைப் பேராலவாயர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"ஆனந்தப் பையுள்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆனந்தப் பையுள்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["முளிமர","முளிமரம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மடமான்","மான்",3,"maa","scene","deer"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",4,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:248":{"book":"purananuru","num":248,"t":["அளிய தாமே சிறுவெள் ளாம்பல்!","இளையம் ஆகத் தழையா யினவே; இனியே,","பெருவளக் கொழுநன் மாய்ந்தெனப், பொழுது மறுத்து,","இன்னா வைகல் உண்ணும்","அல்லிப் படுஉம் புல் ஆயினவே."],"p":"","r":"A widow pities the small white water-lilies: when she was young they were her garment of leaves, and now that her husband of great wealth is dead they have become the poor alli food she eats on painful days, refusing the proper hour.","n":"One plant spans her whole life: its leaves dressed the girl, its seeds feed the widow. The pity (அளிய) is spoken to the lilies, but it is her own change she is measuring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 248","lb":"PN248","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"தாபதநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தாபத நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ளாம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["தழையா","தழை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["அல்லிப்","அல்லி",5,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:249":{"book":"purananuru","num":249,"t":["கதிர்மூக்கு ஆரல் கீழ்ச் சேற்று ஒளிப்பக்,","கணைக்கோட்டு வாளை மீநீர்ப் பிறழ,","எரிப்பூம் பழனம் நெரித்துஉடன் வலைஞர்","அரிக்குரல் தடாரியின் யாமை மிளிரச்,","பனைநுகும்பு அன்ன சினைமுதிர் வராலொடு,","உறழ்வேல் அன்ன ஒண்கயல் முகக்கும்,","அகல்நாட்டு அண்ணல் புகாவே, நெருநைப்","பகல்இடம் கண்ணிப் பலரொடும் கூடி,","ஒருவழிப் பட்டன்று ; மன்னே! இன்றே","அடங்கிய கற்பின் ; ஆய்நுதல் மடந்தை,","உயர்நிலை உலகம் அவன்புக .. .. வரி","நீறாடு சுளகின் சீறிடம் நீக்கி,","அழுதல் ஆனாக் கண்ணள்,","மெழுகு, ஆப்பிகண் கலுழ்நீ ரானே."],"p":"","r":"Where the aaral fish hides in the mud, the vaalai leaps at the surface and turtles turn up at the rasping taḍāri drum as fishermen trample the flame-flowered fields together and scoop up roe-swollen varaal and bright kayal, the lord of that wide land once ate his food in company with many; now, as he has gone to the high world, his young wife of restrained chastity and fine brow clears a small space with an ash-smeared winnowing basket and smears it with cow dung and with the tears of her eyes that will not stop weeping.","n":"The abundance of the first seven lines and the shared meal of line 8 are set against the small cleared patch where the widow now eats alone: the poem turns on நெருநை (yesterday) and இன்றே (today). Line 11 is broken in our text, so what she does as he enters the high world is lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","karpu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 249","lb":"PN249","poet":"தும்பி சொகினனார்;தும்பிசேர் கீரனார் என்பதும் ஆம்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"தாபதநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தாபத நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",1,"pira","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","scene",""],["வலைஞர்","வலைஞர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["தடாரியின்","தடாரி",4,"parai","scene",""],["யாமை","யாமை",4,"pira","scene",""],["வராலொடு","வரால்",5,"pira","gift_food",""],["ஒண்கயல்","கயல்",6,"pira","gift_food",""],["பனைநுகும்பு","பனை",5,"maram","simile",""],["ஆப்பி","ஆப்பி",14,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:250":{"book":"purananuru","num":250,"t":["குய்குரல் மலிந்த கொழுந்துவை அடிசில்","இரவலர்த் தடுத்த வாயிற், புரவலர்","கண்ணீர்த் தடுத்த தண்ணறும் பந்தர்க்,","கூந்தல் கொய்து, குறுந்தொடு நீக்கி,","அல்லி உணவின் மனைவியொடு, இனியே","புல்என் றனையால்-வளங்கெழு திருநகர்!","வான் சோறு கொண்டு தீம்பால் வேண்டும்","முனித்தலைப் புதல்வர் தந்தை","தனித்தலைப் பெருங்காடு முன்னிய பின்னே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the prosperous mansion that it has become desolate: its gate once held back suppliants with rich, sizzling rice and relish and its cool fragrant pandal stopped the tears of patrons, and now, since the father of tuft-headed sons who hold white rice and ask for sweet milk has gone to the lonely burning ground, it holds a wife who has cut her hair, put off her small bangles and eats alli food.","n":"The house is addressed, not the widow, and food runs through every stage: the feast that stopped suppliants at the gate, the widow's alli, and the small sons still asking for milk. The children, who do not yet know what has happened, carry the poem's last weight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 250","lb":"PN250","poet":"தாயங் கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"தாபதநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தாபத நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அடிசில்","அடிசில்",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பந்தர்க்","பந்தர்",3,"pira","scene",""],["அல்லி","அல்லி",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சோறு","சோறு",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தீம்பால்","பால்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:251":{"book":"purananuru","num":251,"t":["ஓவத் தன்ன இடனுடை வரைப்பிற்,","பாவை அன்ன குறுந்தொடி மகளிர்","இழைநிலை நெகிழ்ந்த மள்ளற் கண்டிக்கும்-","கழைக்கண் நெடுவரை அருவியாடிக்,","கான யானை தந்த விறகின்","கடுந்தெறல் செந்தீ வேட்டுப்,","புறம்தாழ் புரிசடை புலர்த்து வோனே!"],"p":"","r":"The man who, in a spacious house like a painting, made doll-like girls with small bangles grow thin until their ornaments slipped, is seen now bathing in the waterfall of a tall hill of bamboo, tending a fierce red fire with wood the forest elephant brought, and drying the matted locks that hang down his back.","n":"The poem is built on the before and after of one man, and the only detail of his former life is the women who pined for him. The same elephant-gathered firewood that lights the pyre in 247 here feeds the ascetic's sacrificial fire. Line 3's கண்டிக்கும் is unusual (the old first-person plural கண்டிகும், 'we have seen', is expected).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 251","lb":"PN251","poet":"மாற்பித்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"தாபத வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தாபத வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["கழைக்கண்","கழை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["அருவியாடிக்","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""],["விறகின்","விறகு",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:252":{"book":"purananuru","num":252,"t":["கறங்குவெள் அருவி ஏற்றலின், நிறம் பெயர்ந்து,","தில்லை அன்ன புல்லென் சடையோடு,","அள்இலைத் தாளி கொய்யு மோனே","இல்வழங்கு மடமயில் பிணிக்கும்","சொல்வலை வேட்டுவன் ஆயினன், முன்னே.’"],"p":"","r":"The ascetic who now plucks soft-leaved thaali, his matted hair dulled and changed in colour like the thillai tree from standing under the roaring white waterfall, was once a hunter who snared, with a net of words, the gentle peacocks that move about the house.","n":"The hunter is the past and the ascetic the present, so the poem reverses the moralist's suspicion that an ascetic may be a hunter in hiding. His weapon was speech (சொல்வலை), and the women are not named but drawn as peacocks of the house. Line 5 ends with a stray closing quotation mark.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 252","lb":"PN252","poet":"மாற்பித்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"தாபத வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தாபத வாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["தில்லை","தில்லை",2,"maram","simile",""],["தாளி","தாளி",3,"maram","gift_food",""],["மடமயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:253":{"book":"purananuru","num":253,"t":["என்திறத்து அவலம் கொள்ளல், இனியே;","வல்வார் கண்ணி இளையர் திளைப்ப,","‘நாகாஅல்’ என வந்த மாறே, எழாநெல்","பைங்கழை பொதிகளைந்து அன்ன விளர்ப்பின்,","வளைஇல், வறுங்கை ஓச்சிக்,","கிளையுள்’ஒய்வலோ? கூறுநின் உரையே!"],"p":"","r":"A wife speaks to her husband who has fallen in the wasteland: let him not grieve for her any more; since he came as the young men with strong-strapped garlands rejoiced, how is she to go among her kin raising an empty hand without bangles, pale as a young bamboo stripped of its sheath; let him tell her his word.","n":"The bare arm is the public sign of widowhood, and her question is how to carry it among her people. Line 3 (நாகாஅல் என வந்த மாறே) is hard and the sense of her first line turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 253","lb":"PN253","poet":"குளம்பாதாயனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுபாலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுபாலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பைங்கழை","கழை",4,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:254":{"book":"purananuru","num":254,"t":["இளையரும் முதியரும் வேறுபுலம் படர,","எதிர்ப்ப எழாஅய், மார்பமண் புல்ல,","இடைச்சுரத்து இறுத்த, மள்ள ! விளர்த்த","வளையில் வறுங்கை ஓச்சிக் கிளையுள்,","‘இன்னன் ஆயினன், இளையோன்’ என்று,","நின்னுரை செல்லும் ஆயின்,’ மற்று","முன்ஊர்ப் பழுனிய கோளி ஆலத்துப்,","புள்ளார் யாணர்த் தற்றே’ என் மகன்","வளனும் செம்மலும் எமக்கு’ என , நாளும்","ஆனாது புகழும் அன்னை","யாங்குஆ குவள்கொல் ? அளியள் தானே!"],"p":"","r":"The speaker addresses the warrior who fell in the middle of the wasteland, not rising to meet the attack, his chest embracing the earth, while the young and old went off to other lands: if, raising her pale empty bangle-less hand among the kin, she carries the word that the young man has come to this, what will become of his mother, who daily, without tiring, praised him, saying that her son's wealth and greatness were to them like the bird-filled plenty of the fruiting banyan at the edge of the town.","n":"Lines 3 to 4, on the pale empty hand raised among the kin, repeat 253 lines 4 to 6 almost word for word, so the bare arm raised among kin was a set image of this thurai. The grief is displaced onto the mother's praise, quoted in her own words: the banyan full of birds is a son who feeds many. The comparison's marker is the அற்று of தற்றே, an அன்ன-type form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 254","lb":"PN254","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுபாலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுபாலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆலத்துப்","ஆலம்",7,"maram","simile",""],["புள்ளார்","புள்",8,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:255":{"book":"purananuru","num":255,"t":["ஐயோ! எனின் யான் புலி அஞ் சுவலே;","அணைத்தனன்’ கொளினே, அகன்மார்புஎடுக்கல்லேன்!","என்போல் பெருவிதிர்ப்பு உறுக, நின்னை","இன்னாது உற்ற அறனில் கூற்றே!","திரைவளை முன்கை பற்றி-","வரைநிழல் சேர்கம்- நடந்திசின் சிறிதே!"],"p":"","r":"A wife over her husband's body in the wilds says that if she cries out she fears the tiger and if she tries to embrace him she cannot lift his broad chest; she wishes on lawless Death, which did him this harm, a great trembling like her own, and asks him to take hold of her wrist with its wave-ridged bangles and walk a little, so they may reach the shade of the hill.","n":"Each of the first two lines closes a way of grieving (crying aloud, embracing); the last asks the dead man to do the impossible, take her hand and walk. Death is called அறனில், without justice, a charge laid against Death itself rather than fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 255","lb":"PN255","poet":"வன்பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுபாலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுபாலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:256":{"book":"purananuru","num":256,"t":["கலம்செய் கோவே : கலம்செய் கோவே!","அச்சுடைச் சாகாட்டு ஆரம் பொருந்திய","சிறுவெண் பல்லி போலத் தன்னொடு","சுரம்பல வந்த எமக்கும் அருளி,","வியன்மலர் அகன்பொழில் ஈமத் தாழி","அகலிது ஆக வனைமோ","நனந்தலை மூதூர்க் கலம்செய் கோவே!"],"p":"","r":"A widow asks the potter of the wide old town to take pity on her too, who came with her husband through many wastelands like the small white lizard that clings to the spoke of an axled cart, and to make the burial urn in the wide grove of broad flowers wide enough for both.","n":"The lizard on the spoke goes wherever the wheel turns without moving itself, which is how she describes her travels with him; the request is practical (make the urn wider), and the burial urn rather than the pyre is the rite named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 256","lb":"PN256","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"முதுபாலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["முதுபாலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பல்லி","பல்லி",3,"pira","simile","lizard"],["கலம்செய்","கலம் செய் கோ",1,"seythi","scene",""],["பொழில்","பொழில்",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:257":{"book":"purananuru","num":257,"t":["செருப்புஇடைச் சிறுபரல் அன்னன்; கணைக்கால்,","அவ்வயிற்று அகன்ற மார்பின், பைங்கண்,","குச்சின் நிரைத்த குரூஉமயிர் மோவாய்ச்,","செவிஇறந்து தாழ்தரும் கவுளன், வில்லொடு,","யார்கொலோ, அளியன் தானே? தேரின்","ஊர்பெரிது இகந்தன்றும் இலனே; அரண்எனக்","காடுகைக் கொண்டன்றும், இலனே ; காலைப்,","புல்லார் இனநிரை செல்புறம் நோக்கிக்,","கையின் சுட்டிப் பைஎன எண்ணிச்,","சிலையின் மாற்றி யோனே ; அவைதாம்","மிகப்பல ஆயினும், என்னாம்-எனைத்தும்","வெண்கோள் தோன்றாக் குழிசியொடு,","நாள்உறை மத்தொலி கேளா தோனே?"],"p":"","r":"Who is he, poor fellow, this man like a pebble in a sandal, with thick calves, a fine belly, a broad chest, fresh eyes, a beard of coarse hair set like rows of sticks, and cheek hair hanging below his ears, carrying a bow? He did not ride far from town in a chariot or take the forest as his fort, yet in the morning, watching the backs of the enemy's departing herd, he pointed with his hand, counted them slowly and turned them with his bow; and many though they are, what are they to him, who never hears the daily sound of the churning stick in a pot that shows no white?","n":"The poem begins as if puzzled by a stranger (யார்கொலோ, அளியன்) and ends by showing why he is pitied: he takes herds by the score, yet his own pot never shows the white of curd, because he keeps none. The seizure is made without chariot or fort, by a man counting cattle on his fingers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","niraikol","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 257","lb":"PN257","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vetchi","cth":"வெட்சி","ctu":"உண்டாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உண்டாட்டு"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["இனநிரை","நிரை",8,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["குழிசியொடு","குழிசி",12,"pira","absent",""],["மத்தொலி","மத்து",13,"pira","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:258":{"book":"purananuru","num":258,"t":["முட்கால் காரை முதுபழன் ஏய்ப்பத்","தெறிப்ப விளைந்த தீங்கந் தாரம்","நிறுத்த ஆயம் தலைச்சென்று உண்டு,","பச்சூன் தின்று, பைந்நிணப் பெருத்த","எச்சில் ஈர்ங்கை விற்புறம் திமிரிப்,","புலம்புக் கனனே, புல்அணற் காளை,","ஒருமுறை உண்ணா அளவைப், பெருநிரை","ஊர்ப்புறம் நிறையத் தருகுவன் ; யார்க்கும்","தொடுதல் ஓம்புமதி முதுகட் சாடி;","ஆதரக் கழுமிய துகளன்,","காய்தலும் உண்டு, அக் கள்வெய் யோனே."],"p":"","r":"The sweet toddy, matured until it bursts like the old fruit of the thorny kaarai, was drunk first by the young man with the thin beard at the head of the gathered company; he ate raw meat, wiped his wet hand, greasy with fat, on the back of his bow and went into the empty land, and before one round can be drunk he will bring a great herd to fill the outskirts of the town; so the speaker tells the woman to keep the old-mouthed jar from everyone's touch, for that lover of toddy, dusty with eagerness, can also be fierce.","n":"The toddy is not his pleasure but his advance pay: the jar is to be kept for him because he will bring back the herd before the others finish drinking. The comparison's marker is ஏய்ப்ப, a போல-type word. Line 10 (ஆதரக் கழுமிய துகளன்) is hard; the sense of eagerness is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kal","niraikol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 258","lb":"PN258","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vetchi","cth":"வெட்சி","ctu":"உண்டாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உண்டாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["காரை","காரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["தீங்கந் தாரம்","கந்தாரம்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பச்சூன்","ஊன்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பைந்நிணப்","நிணம்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",7,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["சாடி","சாடி",9,"pira","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:259":{"book":"purananuru","num":259,"t":["ஏறுஉடைப் பெருநிரை பெயர்தரப் பெயராது,","இலைபுதை பெருங்காட்டுத் தலைகரந்து இருந்த","வல்வில் மறவர் ஒடுக்கம் காணாய்,","செல்லல், செல்லல் ; சிறக்க நின், உள்ளம்;","முதுகுமெய்ப் புலைத்தி போலத்","தாவுபு தெறிக்கும் ஆன்மேல் ;","புடையிலங்கு ஒள்வாள் புனைகழ லோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The speaker warns the warrior with the bright sword at his side and the wrought anklet not to go, not to go, without seeing the ambush of strong-bowed fighters who stayed hidden, heads covered, in the leafy great forest when the herd with its bulls was driven back: may his heart do well, though it is set on the cattle that leap and spring like an old possessed pulaitti.","n":"The poem is a warning, not a praise: the recovered herd may be bait, and the hidden bowmen are the danger. What the simile compares is not certain: the leaping cattle, or the hero's eager rush toward them; முதுகுமெய் (possessed) is itself a rare compound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","niraikol","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 259","lb":"PN259","poet":"கோடை பாடிய பெரும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"செருமலைதல் (பிள்ளைப் பெயர்ச்சியுமாம்)","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செருமலைதல்","பிள்ளைப் பெயர்ச்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஏறுஉடைப்","ஏறு",1,"maa","scene","bull"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",1,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",5,"seythi","simile",""],["ஆன்மேல்","ஆன்",6,"maa","simile","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:260":{"book":"purananuru","num":260,"t":["வளரத் தொடினும், வெளவுபு திரிந்து,","விளரி உறுதரும் தீந்தொடை நினையாத்","தளரும் நெஞ்சம் தலைஇ; மனையோள்","உளரும் கூந்தல் நோக்கி, களர","கள்ளி நீழற் கடவுள் வாழ்த்திப்","பசிபடு மருங்குலை, கசிபு, கைதொழாஅக்,","‘காணலென் கொல் ?’ என வினவினை வரூஉம்","பாண ! கேண்மதி, யாணரது நிலையே;","புரவுத்தொடுத்து உண்குவை ஆயினும், இரவுஎழுந்து,","எவ்வம் கொள்வை ஆயினும், இரண்டும்,","கையுள போலும் கடிதுஅண் மையவே;","முன்ஊர்ப் பூசலின் தோன்றித் தன்னூர்","நெடுநிரை தழீஇய மீளி யாளர்","விடுகணை நீத்தம் துடிபுணை யாக,","வென்றி தந்து, கொன்றுகோள் விடுத்து,","வையகம் புலம்ப வளைஇய பாம்பின்","வைஎயிற்று உய்ந்த மதியின், மறவர்","கையகத்து உய்ந்த கன்றுடைப் பல்லான்","நிரையொடு வந்த உரைய னாகி,","உரிகளை அரவ மானத், தானே","அரிதுசெல் உலகில் சென்றனன் ; உடம்பே,","கானச் சிற்றியாற்று அருங்கரைக் கால்உற்றுக்,","கம்பமொடு துளங்கிய இலக்கம் போல,","அம்பொடு துளங்க ஆண்டுஒழிந் தன்றே;","உயர்இசை வெறுப்பத் தோன்றிய பெயரே,","மடஞ்சால் மஞ்ஞை அணிமயிர் சூட்டி,","இடம்பிறர் கொள்ளாச் சிறுவழிப்,","படஞ்செய் பந்தர்க் கல்மிசை யதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a bard whose tuning slips into the mourning mode, whose heart fails, who saw his wife's loosened hair as an omen, worshipped the god in the kalli shade of the salt waste and came hungry and weeping asking 'shall I not see him?', to hear how the prosperous one stands: whether he would have eaten under his protection or wakes at night in distress, both now lie close at hand; the hero appeared at the alarm of the near village, gathered his town's long herd, crossed the flood of arrows with the drum for a raft, won, killed, made the raiders let go, and as the moon escapes the sharp fang of the snake that seized it, the many cows with their calves escaped the raiders' hands and came back with his fame; then, like a snake shedding its skin, he went to the world hard to reach, his body stayed there shaking with arrows like a target post shaking with its pole on the steep bank of a forest stream, and his name, risen until high fame could hold no more, is on the stone in a narrow path no one else takes, under a cloth canopy, crowned with the fine feathers of the gentle peacock.","n":"The hero is split three ways at his death: the self goes like a snake from its skin, the body stays behind shaking with arrows like a target post, and the name moves onto the stone. The bard's omens at the start (the slipping tune, the loosened hair) are confirmed rather than answered, and the poem gives the bard the hero-stone in place of a patron. The marker of the snake simile is மான, a போல-type word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","nadukal","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 260","lb":"PN260","poet":"வடமோதங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை (பாடாண் திணையுமாம்)","ctu":"கையறுநிலை செருவிடை வீழ்தல்; கையறு நிலையுமாம்; பாண்பாட்டுமாம்; பாடாண் பாட்டுமாம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செருவிடை வீழ்தல்","கையறுநிலை","பாண்பாட்டு","பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["விளரி","விளரி",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",5,"maram","scene",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",5,"deyvam","scene",""],["துடிபுணை","துடி",14,"parai","simile",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",16,"pira","simile",""],["பல்லான்","ஆன்",18,"maa","scene","cows"],["அரவ","அரவு",20,"pira","simile",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",26,"pul","ornament",""]],"en":[["herostone","கல்மிசை",28]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:13"},"pn:261":{"book":"purananuru","num":261,"t":["அந்தோ! எந்தை அடையாப் பேரில்;","வண்டுபடு நறவின் தண்டா மண்டையொடு","வரையாப் பெருஞ்சோற்று முரிவாய் முற்றம்,","வெற்றுயாற்று அம்பியின் எற்று ? அற்று ஆகக்","கண்டனென், மன்ற ; சோர்க, என் கண்ணே;","வையங் காவலர் வளம்கெழு திருநகர்,","மையல் யானை அயாவுயிர்த் தன்ன","நெய்யுலை சொரிந்த மையூன் ஓசை","புதுக்கண் மாக்கள் செதுக்கண் ஆரப்","பயந்தனை, மன்னால், முன்னே! இனியே","பல்ஆ தழீஇய கல்லா வல்வில்","உழைக்குரற் கூகை அழைப்ப ஆட்டி,","நாகுமுலை அன்ன நறும்பூங் கரந்தை","விரகுஅறி யாளர் மரபிற் சூட்ட,","நிரைஇவண் தந்து, நடுகல் ஆகிய","வென்வேல் விடலை இன்மையின் புலம்பிக்,","கொய்ம்மழித் தலையொடு கைம்மையுறக் கலங்கிய","கழிகலம் மகடூஉப் போல","புல்என் றனையால், பல்அணி இழந்தே."],"p":"","r":"A poet sees the ever-open house of his dead lord, once loud with toddy bowls, endless rice and meat frying in ghee for newcomers, now as useless as a ferry on a dried river and as bare as a widow with shorn head, because the young spearman who brought back the cattle and was crowned with karanthai has become a hero-stone.","n":"The address shifts in mid-poem from the poet's own eyes (சோர்க, என் கண்ணே) to the house itself (பயந்தனை... புல்என் றனையால்), so the house is mourned as a person who has lost her husband. Lines 11 and 12 (the unlearned bowmen, the owl's call) are compressed and their syntax is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","nadukal","kaimmai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 261","lb":"PN261","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"கையறு நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["நறவின்","நறவு",2,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["பெருஞ்சோற்று","சோறு",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["அம்பியின்","அம்பி",4,"pira","simile","ferry boat"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""],["மையூன்","ஊன்",8,"unavu","gift_food","mutton"],["பல்ஆ","ஆ",11,"maa","scene","cows"],["கூகை","கூகை",12,"pul","scene","owl"],["நாகுமுலை","நாகு",13,"maa","simile","young cow"],["கரந்தை","கரந்தை",13,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:262":{"book":"purananuru","num":262,"t":["நறவும் தொடுமின் ; விடையும் வீழ்மின்;","பாசுவல் இட்ட புன்காற் பந்தர்ப்","புனல்தரும் இளமணல் நிறையப் பெய்ம்மின்-","ஒன்னார் முன்னிலை முருக்கிப், பின்நின்று;","நிரையோடு வரூஉம் என்னைக்கு","உழையோர் தன்னினும் பெருஞ்சா யலரே."],"p":"","r":"Draw the toddy, slaughter the ram, fill the leaf-roofed shed with fresh river sand: my lord is coming with the herd, having broken the enemy's front and guarded the rear, and those beside him are spent more than he.","n":"The leader is both in front (முருக்கி) and behind (பின்நின்று): he breaks the enemy and then guards the retreat with the herd. Line 6 can be read as his companions being more worn than he, or gentler than he; either way the feast is ordered for them all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kal","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 262","lb":"PN262","poet":"மதுரைப் பேராலவாயர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vetchi","cth":"வெட்சி","ctu":"உண்டாட்டு (தலை தோற்றமுமாம்)","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உண்டாட்டு","தலைத்தோற்றம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நறவும்","நறவு",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["விடையும்","விடை",1,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["பாசுவல்","உவல்",2,"maram","scene","green leaves"],["புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","river water"],["நிரையோடு","நிரை",5,"maa","scene","cattle herd"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:263":{"book":"purananuru","num":263,"t":["பெருங்களிற்று அடியின் தோன்றும் ஒருகண்","இரும்பறை இரவல ! சேறி ஆயின்,","தொழாதனை கழிதல் ஓம்புமதி; வழாது,","வண்டுமேம் படூஉம், இவ் வறநிலை யாறே-","பல்லாத் திரள்நிரை பெயர்தரப் பெயர்தந்து,","கல்லா இளையர் நீங்க நீங்கான்,","வில்லுமிழ் கடுங்கணை மூழ்கக்,","கொல்புனல் சிறையின் விலங்கியோன் கல்லே."],"p":"","r":"A bard with a one-faced drum like an elephant's footprint is told not to pass without worshipping the stone on this road: it stands for the man who turned back the stolen herd, stood fast when the untrained youths fled, and, sunk in arrows, held the enemy like a dam against a killing flood.","n":"The poem is an instruction to a passing performer: the dead man's stone has become a place of worship on the road, which is how a lament turns into a cult. Line 4 (வண்டுமேம் படூஉம், இவ் வறநிலை யாறே) is hard: it may mean the road through dry land where bees swarm at the garlanded stone, or a path of virtue.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","maram","saakkaadu","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 263","lb":"PN263","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிற்று","களிறு",1,"maa","simile","bull elephant"],["இரும்பறை","பறை",2,"parai","scene",""],["வண்டுமேம்","வண்டு",4,"pira","scene","bee"],["பல்லாத்","ஆ",5,"maa","scene","cows"],["கொல்புனல்","புனல்",8,"pira","simile","flood"]],"en":[["herostone","கல்லே",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:264":{"book":"purananuru","num":264,"t":["பரலுடை மருங்கிற் பதுக்கை சேர்த்தி,","மரல்வகுந்து தொடுத்த செம்பூங் கண்ணியொடு,","அணிமயிற் பீலி சூட்டிப், பெயர்பொறித்து","இனிநட் டனரே! கல்லும் ; கன்றொடு","கறவை தந்து பகைவர் ஓட்டிய","நெடுந்தகை கழிந்தமை அறியாது","இன்றும் வருங்கொல், பாணரது கடும்பே?"],"p":"","r":"On the gravelly ground they have heaped the cairn, crowned it with a red flower garland bound with split maral fibre and with peacock feathers, carved his name and set up the stone; will the bards' troupe still come today, not knowing that the noble man who drove off the enemy and brought back the cows with their calves is gone?","n":"The grief is measured from the bards' side: the rite is complete (cairn, garland, feather, name, stone) but the news has not travelled, so the performers who lived on his giving are still on the road to him. The order of the rite in lines 1 to 4 is itself a record of how a hero-stone was made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","kaiyarunilai","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 264","lb":"PN264","poet":"உறையூர் இளம்பொன் வாணிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பரலுடை","பரல்",1,"pira","scene","gravel"],["மரல்வகுந்து","மரல்",2,"maram","ornament","bowstring hemp"],["செம்பூங்","பூ",2,"maram","ornament",""],["மயிற்","மயில்",3,"pul","ornament","peacock"],["கன்றொடு","கன்று",4,"maa","scene","calf"],["கறவை","கறவை",5,"maa","scene","milch cow"],["பாணரது","பாணர்",7,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கை",1],["herostone","கல்லும்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:265":{"book":"purananuru","num":265,"t":["ஊர்நனி இறந்த பார்முதிர் பறந்தலை,","ஓங்குநிலை வேங்கை ஒள்ளிணர் நறுவீப்","போந்தை அம் தோட்டின் புனைந்தனர் தொடுத்துப்,","பல்ஆன் கோவலர் படலை சூட்டக்,","கல்ஆ யினையே-கடுமான் தோன்றல்!","வான்ஏறு புரையும்நின் தாள்நிழல் வாழ்க்கைப்","பரிசிலர் செல்வம் அன்றியும் ! விரிதார்க்","கடும்பகட்டு யானை வேந்தர்","ஒடுங்க வென்றியும், நின்னொடு செலவே."],"p":"","r":"On the old gravelly battlefield far beyond the town, the cowherds have crowned you with a garland of venkai blossom tied with palmyra leaf, and you have become a stone, prince of swift horses; and with you have gone not only the wealth of the bards who lived in the shade of your feet but the victory that made elephant-riding kings shrink.","n":"The cowherds, not kings or priests, perform the crowning, with flowers of the hill (venkai) and leaf of the palmyra: the stone belongs to the people he protected. The last lines make the loss double, patronage and conquest at once, so the dead man's giving and his fighting are one reputation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","kaiyarunilai","parisil","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 265","lb":"PN265","poet":"சோணாட்டு முகையலூர்ச் சிறுகருந்தும்பியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"karanthai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["போந்தை","போந்தை",3,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["பல்ஆன்","ஆன்",4,"maa","scene","cows"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",4,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["கடுமான்","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"],["வான்ஏறு","ஏறு",6,"pira","simile","thunder"],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","கல்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:266":{"book":"purananuru","num":266,"t":["பயங்கெழு மாமழை பெய்யாது மாறிக்,","கயங்களி முளியும் கோடை ஆயினும்,","புழற்கால் ஆம்பல் அகலடை நீழல்","கதிர்கோட்டு நந்தின் கரிமுக ஏற்றை","நாகுஇள வளையொடு பகல்மணம் புகூஉம்","நீர்திகழ் கழனி நாடுகெழு பெருவிறல்!","வான்தோய் நீள்குடை, வயமான் சென்னி!","சான்றோர் இருந்த அவையத்து உற்றோன்,","ஆசாகு என்னும் பூசல்போல,","வல்லே களைமதி அத்தை- உள்ளிய","விருந்துகண்டு ஒளிக்கும் திருந்தா வாழ்க்கைப்,","பொறிப்புணர் உடம்பில் தோன்றிஎன்","அறிவுகெட நின்ற நல்கூர் மையே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Chenni, lord of a land whose watered fields stay green even when the rains fail, to remove at once, as a plea made in the assembly of the wise is answered, the poverty that has risen in his body, ruining his understanding and making him hide from the very guests he longed for.","n":"The snail pair mating in the shade of a lily leaf at midday, in a drought, shows a land so well watered that even the weakest creatures prosper; the poet's own house is the one place in it where the guest is avoided. The comparison makes relief a matter of justice, as in a court, rather than of favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","parisil","virundhu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 266","lb":"PN266","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் உருவப்பஃறேர் இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் கடாநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் கடா நிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["மாமழை","மழை",1,"pira","absent","rain"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","scene","water lily"],["நந்தின்","நந்து",4,"pira","scene","snail"],["வளையொடு","வளை",5,"pira","scene","female snail"],["வயமான்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["king","சென்னி",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:269":{"book":"purananuru","num":267,"t":["குயில்வாய் அன்ன கூர்முகை அதிரல்","பயிலாது அல்கிய பல்காழ் மாலை,","மையிரும் பித்தை பொலியச் சூட்டிப்,","புத்தகல் கொண்ட புலிக்கண் வெப்பர்","ஒன்றுஇரு முறையிருந்து உண்ட பின்றை,","உவலைக் கண்ணித் துடியன் வந்தெனப்","பிழிமகிழ் வல்சி வேண்ட, மற்றிது","கொள்ளாய் என்ப, கள்ளின் வாழ்த்திக்","கரந்தை நீடிய அறிந்துமாறு செருவின்","பல்லான் இனநிரை தழீஇய வில்லோர்க்,","கொடுஞ்சிறைக் குரூஉப்பருந்து ஆர்ப்பத்,","தடிந்துமாறு பெயர்த்தது, இக் கருங்கை வாளே."],"p":"","r":"After the warrior, his hair wreathed with loose athiral buds sharp as a koel's beak, has drunk the warm liquor once or twice from a new bowl, the drummer comes asking for pressed toddy, and he is given instead, it is said, this dark-handled sword, which cut down the bowmen who had seized the herd and drove them back while the kites cried over them.","n":"Who says 'take this' in lines 7 and 8 (என்ப, 'they say') and what 'this' is are not settled by the syntax; read with the closing words இக் கருங்கை வாளே, the feast's answer to a request for drink is the sword's deed. Avvaiyar's poem keeps the feast and the slaughter in one sentence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kal","eegai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 269","lb":"PN269","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vetchi","cth":"வெட்சி","ctu":"உண்டாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உண்டாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குயில்வாய்","குயில்",1,"pul","simile","koel"],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",1,"maram","ornament","wild jasmine"],["துடியன்","துடியன்",6,"seythi","scene","drummer"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",8,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கரந்தை","கரந்தை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["பல்லான்","ஆன்",10,"maa","scene","cows"],["குரூஉப்பருந்து","பருந்து",11,"pul","scene","kite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:270":{"book":"purananuru","num":268,"t":["பன்மீன் இமைக்கும் மாக விசும்பின்","இரங்கு முரசின், இனம்சால் யானை,","நிலந்தவ உருட்டிய நேமி யோரும்","சமங்கண் கூடித் தாம்வேட் பவ்வே-","நறுவிரை துறந்த நாறா நரைத்தலைச்","சிறுவர் தாயே! பேரிற் பெண்டே!","நோகோ யானே ; நோக்குமதி நீயே;","மறப்படை நுவலும் அரிக்குரல் தண்ணுமை","இன்னிசை கேட்ட துன்னரும் மறவர்","வென்றிதரு வேட்கையர், மன்றம் கொண்மார்,","பேரமர் உழந்த வெருவரு பறந்தலை.","விழுநவி பாய்ந்த மரத்தின்,","வாண்மிசைக் கிடந்த ஆண்மையோன் திறத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Addressing the grey-haired mother of a great house, the poet says that even wheel-rolling emperors with drums and elephants meet in battle what they sought, and grieves while bidding her look at her son, who, after warriors stirred by the war drum fought a fearful battle, lies on the swords like a tree felled by an axe.","n":"The poet's நோகோ யானே (I grieve) is set against நோக்குமதி நீயே (but you, look): the mother is asked to see valour where the poet sees loss. Lines 1 to 4 on the emperors are elliptical and their link to the son is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaiyarunilai","maram","saakkaadu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 270","lb":"PN270","poet":"கழாத்தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",8,"parai","scene",""],["மரத்தின்","மரம்",12,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:271":{"book":"purananuru","num":269,"t":["நீரறவு அறியா நிலமுதற் கலந்த","கருங்குரல் நொச்சிக் கண்ணார் குரூஉத்தழை,","மெல்இழை மகளிர் ஐதகல் அல்குல்,","தொடலை ஆகவும் கண்டனம் ; இனியே,","வெருவரு குருதியொடு மயங்கி, உருவுகரந்து,","ஒறுவாய்ப் பட்ட தெரியல் ஊன்செத்துப்,","பருந்துகொண்டு உகப்பயாம் கண்டனம்","மறம்புகல் மைந்தன் மலைந்த மாறே!"],"p":"","r":"We once saw the bright nochchi leaves, grown where the ground never lacks water, worn as a girdle on the waists of jewelled women; now we have seen the same leaves, soaked in blood and shredded out of shape, carried off by a kite that took them for flesh, because the young warrior eager for battle wore them.","n":"The warrior's death is never stated; it is told only through what happened to the leaf he wore, from women's ornament to carrion. The kite's mistake (ஊன்செத்து) makes the garland stand for his flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 271","lb":"PN271","poet":"வெறி பாடிய காமக்கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"நொட்சி","ctu":"செருவிடை வீழ்தல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செருவிடை வீழ்தல்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நொச்சிக்","நொச்சி",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தெரியல்","தெரியல்",6,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பருந்துகொண்டு","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene","kite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:272":{"book":"purananuru","num":270,"t":["மணிதுணர்ந் தன்ன மாக்குரல் நொச்சி!","போதுவிரி பன்மர னுள்ளும் சிறந்த","காதல் நன்மரம் நீ; நிழற் றிசினே!","கடியுடை வியன்நகர்க் காண்வரப் பொலிந்த","தொடியுடை மகளிர் அல்குலும் கிடத்தி;","காப்புடைப் புரிசை புக்குமாறு அழித்தலின்,","ஊர்ப்புறம் கொடாஅ நெடுந்தகை","பீடுகெழு சென்னிக் கிழமையும் நினதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises the nochchi, its clusters like strung sapphires, as the best loved of flowering trees: it gives shade, lies on the waists of bangled women in the guarded mansion, and, because the enemy breached the guarded wall, it also wins the honour of crowning the noble man who would not yield the town's outskirts.","n":"The tree is addressed as a person with rights (கிழமையும் நினதே): its last and highest claim is to the head of the defender at the moment the wall falls. The colophon's falling in battle is implied by the breach, not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aran","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 272","lb":"PN272","poet":"மோசிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"நொட்சி","ctu":"செருவிடை வீழ்தல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["செருவிடை வீழ்தல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",1,"maram","ornament",""],["மணிதுணர்ந்","மணி",1,"pira","simile","sapphire"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:273":{"book":"purananuru","num":271,"t":["மாவா ராதே ; மாவா ராதே ;","எல்லார் மாவும் வந்தன ; எம்இல்,","புல்லுளைக் குடுமிப் புதல்வற் றந்த","செல்வன் ஊரும் மாவா ராதே-","இருபேர் யாற்ற ஒருபெருங் கூடல்","விலங்கிடு பெருமரம் போல,","உலந்தன்று கொல் ; அவன் மலைந்த மாவே?"],"p":"","r":"A wife cries that her husband's horse has not come while everyone else's horses have returned, and asks whether it has perished like a great tree that lies across the meeting of two great rivers.","n":"She asks only about the horse; the rider's fate is left unspoken, and naming him as the one who gave her a son puts the household's future into the question. The tree across the confluence fits a horse (and rider) that blocked two converging armies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 273","lb":"PN273","poet":"எருமை வெளியனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"குதிரை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குதிரை மறம்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாவா","மா",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["யாற்ற","யாறு",5,"pira","simile","river"],["பெருமரம்","மரம்",6,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:274":{"book":"purananuru","num":272,"t":["நீலக் கச்சைப் பூவார் ஆடைப்,","பீலிக் கண்ணிப் பெருந்தகை மறவன்","மேல்வரும் களிற்றொடு வேல்துரந்து ; இனியே,","தன்னும் துரக்குவன் போலும்-ஒன்னலர்","எஃகுடை வலத்தர் மாவொடு பரத்தரக்,","கையின் வாங்கித் தழீஇ,","மொய்ம்பின் ஊக்கி, மெய்க்கொண் டனனே;"],"p":"","r":"The great warrior in blue waistband, flowered cloth and peacock-feather crown hurled his spear at the oncoming elephant; now, as the foes with spears came on with their horses, he seems ready to hurl himself too, and seizing with his hand, embracing, lifting with his shoulder's strength, he took it upon his body.","n":"With his spear gone, the warrior turns himself into the weapon (தன்னும் துரக்குவன்). What he seizes in lines 6 and 7 (an enemy's spear, or an enemy) is not stated, and the last verb can mean taking it bodily or taking it into his body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 274","lb":"PN274","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"எருமை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["எருமை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பூவார்","பூ",1,"maram","ornament","flower"],["பீலிக்","பீலி",2,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["மாவொடு","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:275":{"book":"purananuru","num":273,"t":["கோட்டம் கண்ணியும், கொடுந்திரை ஆடையும்,","வேட்டது சொல்லி வேந்தனைத் தொடுத்தலும்,","ஒத்தன்று மாதோ, இவற்கே : செற்றிய","திணிநிலை அலறக் கூவை போழ்ந்து , தன்","வடிமாண் எஃகம் கடிமுகத்து ஏந்தி,","“ஓம்புமின், ஓம்புமின், இவண்!’ ஓம்பாது","தொடர்கொள் யானையின் குடர்கால் தட்பக்,","கன்றுஅமர் கறவை மான ;","முன்சமத்து எதிர்ந்ததன் தோழற்கு வருமே."],"p":"","r":"His bent garland, his cloth of curling folds and his naming what he wanted as he went at the king all suit him: splitting the koovai so the ranks cried out, raising his spear at the front, crying 'hold here, hold here!' without guarding himself, his legs tangled in the guts of a chained elephant, he rushes like a cow to its calf toward the comrade who faced the first clash.","n":"The simile of the cow running to its calf is a tender one inside a scene of entrails: the warrior's rush to his comrade is love, not only valour. Lines 3 and 4 (செற்றிய திணிநிலை, கூவை போழ்ந்து) are hard and the sense of the opening garland and cloth is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","natpu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 275","lb":"PN275","poet":"ஒரூஉத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"எருமை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["எருமை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கூவை","கூவை",4,"maram","scene","arrowroot"],["யானையின்","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["கறவை","கறவை",8,"maa","simile","milch cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:276":{"book":"purananuru","num":274,"t":["நல்லுரை துறந்த நறைவெண் கூந்தல்,","இருங்காழ் அன்ன திரங்குகண் வறுமுலைச்","செம்முது பெண்டின் காதலஞ் சிறாஅன்,","மடப்பால் ஆய்மகள் வள்உகிர்த் தெறித்த","குடப்பால் சில்லுறை போலப்,","படைக்குநோய் எல்லாம் தான்ஆ யினனே."],"p":"","r":"The beloved son of a red-skinned old woman, her white hair untended and her breasts dry and shrunk like dark seeds, became the ruin of a whole army, as a few drops of curd-starter flicked by a cowherd girl's nail turn a pot of milk.","n":"The simile turns on size: a few drops change a whole pot. Taken from the dairy of mullai, it makes the hero's deed as quiet and certain as a daily chore, and sets the dried breasts of the mother against the milk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 276","lb":"PN276","poet":"மாதுரைப் பூதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானை நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["இருங்காழ்","காழ்",2,"maram","simile","seed"],["ஆய்மகள்","ஆய்மகள்",4,"seythi","simile","cowherd girl"],["குடப்பால்","பால்",5,"unavu","simile","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:277":{"book":"purananuru","num":275,"t":["மீன்உண் கொக்கின் தூவிஅன்ன","வால்நரைக் கூந்தல் முதியோள் சிறுவன்","களிறுஎறிந்து பட்டனன்’ என்னும் உவகை","ஈன்ற ஞான்றினும் பெரிதே ; கண்ணீர்","நோன்கழை துயல்வரும் வெதிரத்து","வான்பெயத் தூங்கிய சிதரினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"When the old woman with hair white as the down of a fish-eating heron heard that her son had killed an elephant and fallen, her joy was greater than on the day she bore him, and her tears outnumbered the drops that hang on the swaying bamboo after rain.","n":"The thurai's name, joy and weeping together, is the poem's structure: one sentence for each. The bamboo after rain is an image of plenty, so even her tears are counted as abundance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","maram","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 277","lb":"PN277","poet":"பூங்கணுத்திரையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","simile","heron"],["களிறுஎறிந்து","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["நோன்கழை","கழை",5,"maram","simile","bamboo cane"],["வெதிரத்து","வெதிரம்",5,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["வான்பெயத்","வான்",6,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:278":{"book":"purananuru","num":276,"t":["“நரம்புஎழுந்து உலறிய நிரம்பா மென்தோள்","முளரி மருங்கின், முதியோள் சிறுவன்","படைஅழிந்து மாறினன்” என்று பலர் கூற,","“மண்டுஅமர்க்கு உடைந்தனன் ஆயின், உண்டஎன்","முலைஅறுத் திடுவென், யான்’ எனச் சினைஇக்,","கொண்ட வாளொடு படுபிணம் பெயராச்,","செங்களம் துழவுவோள், சிதைந்துவே றாகிய","படுமகன் கிடக்கை காணூஉ,","ஈன்ற ஞான்றினும் பெரிதுஉவந் தனளே!"],"p":"","r":"Told by many that her son had broken and fled, an old woman with withered, veined arms swore in rage to cut off the breasts he had sucked, took a sword and searched the red field turning over the dead, and when she found her son lying hacked to pieces, she rejoiced more than on the day she bore him.","n":"Her proof is in the wounds: a son cut to pieces (சிதைந்து வேறாகிய) could not have run, so the report of the many is refuted by what she sees. Her vow against her own breasts makes motherhood itself answerable for the son's courage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","maram","maanam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 278","lb":"PN278","poet":"காக்கைபாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"],["vekuli","கொலை"]],"k":[["முளரி","முளரி",2,"maram","scene","brushwood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:279":{"book":"purananuru","num":277,"t":["கெடுக சிந்தை ; கடிதுஇவள் துணிவே;","மூதின் மகளிர் ஆதல் தகுமே;","மேல்நாள் உற்ற செருவிற்கு இவள்தன்னை,","யானை எறிந்து, களத்துஒழிந் தன்னே;","நெருநல் உற்ற செருவிற்கு இவள்கொழுநன்,","பெருநிரை விலக்கி, ஆண்டுப்பட் டனனே;","இன்றும் செருப்பறை கேட்டு, விருப்புற்று மயங்கி,","வேல்கைக் கொடுத்து, வெளிதுவிரித்து உடீஇப்,","பாறுமயிர்க் குடுமி எண்ணெய் நீவி,","ஒருமகன் அல்லது இல்லோள்,","‘செருமுக நோக்கிச் செல்க’ என’ விடுமே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet, aghast, says this woman's resolve is fierce and worthy of an old warrior house: her elder kinsman died killing an elephant the day before yesterday, her husband died yesterday holding back the great herd, and today, hearing the war drum, she puts a spear in the hand of her only son, wraps him in white cloth, oils his tufted hair and sends him to face the battle.","n":"கெடுக சிந்தை, the poet's opening curse on her (or his own) mind, is awe close to horror. The days are counted (மேல்நாள், நெருநல், இன்றும்) so the poem becomes a three-day ledger of loss; the only son (ஒருமகன் அல்லது இல்லோள்) is the last entry. Whether தன்னை here is her father or elder brother is not fixed by the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","makkal","kudi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 279","lb":"PN279","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",6,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["செருப்பறை","பறை",7,"parai","scene","war drum"],["எண்ணெய்","எண்ணெய்",9,"pira","scene","oil"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:280":{"book":"purananuru","num":278,"t":["என்னை மார்பிற் புண்ணும் வெய்ய","நடுநாள் வந்து தும்பியும் துவைக்கும்;","நெடுநகர் வரைப்பின் விளக்கும் நில்லா;","துஞ்சாக் கண்ணே துயிலும் வேட்கும்;","அஞ்சுவரு குராஅல் குரலும் தூற்றும்;","நெல்நீர் எறிந்து விரிச்சி ஓர்க்கும்","செம்முது பெண்டின் சொல்லும் நிரம்பா;","துடிய! பாண ! பாடுவல் விறலி!","என்ஆ குவிர்கொல் ? அளியிர்; நுமக்கும்","இவண் உறை வாழ்க்கையோ, அரிதே ! யானும்","மண்ணுறு மழித்தலைத் , தெண்ணீர் வாரத்,","தொன்றுதாம் உடுத்த அம்பகைத் தெரியல்","சிறுவெள் ஆம்பல் அல்லி உண்ணும்","கழிகல மகளிர் போல,","வழிநினைந்து இருத்தல், அதனினும் அரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"A wife beside her husband's wounded body lists the bad signs, the burning wounds, the bee buzzing at midnight, the lamps that go out, the owl's cry, the old woman's omen that does not come right, pities the drummer, bard and virali whose life here is over too, and says that for her, to sit like widows with shaven heads eating white water-lily seeds and think of the road he took will be harder still.","n":"The first seven lines are a run of omens, each a short clause ending in a verb, so the poem enacts a night of watching. She foresees widowhood in the simile and judges it harder than his death. Line 12 (தொன்றுதாம் உடுத்த அம்பகைத் தெரியல்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","kaiyarunilai","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 280","lb":"PN280","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"pothuviyal","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"ஆந்தப் பையுள்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆனந்தப் பையுள்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["தும்பியும்","தும்பி",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["குராஅல்","குராஅல்",5,"pul","scene","owl"],["நெல்நீர்","நெல்",6,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["துடிய","துடியன்",8,"seythi","scene","drummer"],["பாண","பாணன்",8,"seythi","scene","bard"],["விறலி","விறலி",8,"seythi","scene","dancer"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",13,"maram","simile","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:14"},"pn:281":{"book":"purananuru","num":279,"t":["தீங்கனி இரவமொடு வேம்புமனைச் செரீஇ,","வாங்குமருப்பு யாழொடு பல்இயம் கறங்கக்,","கைபயப் பெயர்த்து மைஇழுது இழுகி;","ஐயவி சிதறி, ஆம்பல் ஊதி,","இசைமணி எறிந்து, காஞ்சி பாடி,","நெடுநகர் வரைப்பின் கடிநறை புகைஇக்,","காக்கம் வம்மோ-காதலந் தோழீ!","வேந்துறு விழுமம் தாங்கிய","பூம்பொறிக் கழற்கால் நெடுந்தகை புண்ணே!"],"p":"","r":"A woman calls her dear friend to come and guard the wound of the great man who took on the king's trouble: hang neem with iravam in the house, sound the lute and many instruments, smear the dark paste, scatter mustard, blow the ampal pipe, ring the bell, sing kaanchi and burn incense through the long house.","n":"The whole poem is a list of household rites and only the last line says whose wound it is, so the wound is guarded as much by the women's noise and smoke as by medicine; ampal here is the pipe that is blown, not the water-lily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anangu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 281","lb":"PN281","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பேய்க் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பேய்க்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["இரவமொடு","இரவம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["வேம்பு","வேம்பு",1,"maram","scene","neem"],["யாழொடு","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["பல்இயம்","இயம்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",4,"maram","scene","white mustard"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",4,"yaazh","scene","pipe"],["இசைமணி","மணி",5,"pira","scene","bell"],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",5,"yaazh","scene","kaanchi song"],["கடிநறை","நறை",6,"pira","scene","incense"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:282":{"book":"purananuru","num":280,"t":["எஃகுஉளம் கழிய இருநில மருங்கின்","அருங்கடன் இறுத்த பெருஞ்செ யாளனை,","யாண்டுளனோ?’வென, வினவுதி ஆயின்,",". . . . . . . . . . . .","வருபடை தாங்கிக் கிளர்தார் அகலம்","அருங்கடன் இறுமார் வயவர் எறிய,","உடம்பும் தோன்றா உயிர்கெட் டன்றே,","மலையுநர் மடங்கி மாறுஎதிர் கழியத்","அலகை போகிச் சிதைந்து வேறு ஆகிய","பலகை அல்லது, களத்துஒழி யதே;","சேண்விளங்கு நல்லிசை நிறீஇ,","நாநவில் புலவர் வாய் உளானே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask where the great doer is who paid his hard debt to the land with a spear through his chest, the answer is that holding the oncoming army he was cut down until neither body nor life could be seen, and nothing of him is left on the field but his split shield, yet he lives on, fame set far, in the mouths of poets.","n":"The poem answers the question 'where is he?' twice: nowhere on the field, where even the body is gone, and in the poets' speech, so the poem itself is offered as the place where the hero now is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 282","lb":"PN282","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வயவர்","வயவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["புலவர்","புலவர்",12,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:283":{"book":"purananuru","num":281,"t":["ஒண்செங் குரலித் தண்கயம் கலங்கி,","வாளை நீர்நாய் நாள்இரை பெறூஉப்","பெறாஅ உறையரா வராஅலின் மயங்கி","மாறுகொள் முதலையொடு ஊழ்மாறு பெயரும்","அழும்பிலன் அடங்கான் தகையும் என்றும்,","வலம்புரி கோசர் அவைக்களத் தானும்,","மன்றுள் என்பது கெட .. .. .. னே பாங்கற்கு","ஆர்சூழ் குவட்டின் வேல்நிறத்து இங்க,","உயிர்புறப் படாஅ அளவைத் தெறுவரத்,","தெற்றிப் பாவை திணிமணல் அயரும்,","மென்தோள் மகளிர் நன்று புரப்ப,",".. .. .. .. .. ண்ட பாசிலைக்","கமழ்பூந் தும்பை நுதல் அசைத் தோனே."],"p":"","r":"Through a broken text: after a scene of a cool pond where the vaalai fish and the otter take their daily prey and turn back from the crocodile, the poem names the unsubmitting Azhumbil man and the assembly of the Kosar, the women of soft shoulders playing with sand dolls, and a hero who wore the fragrant thumbai on his forehead.","n":"Lines 7 and 12 are gaps and line 3 is read differently in another edition, so who the Azhumbil man is to the hero (enemy or lord) cannot be fixed; the Kosar, a people, are not one of the entity types and are noted here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maram","padai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 283","lb":"PN283","poet":"அடை நெடுங் கல்வியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"பாண்பாட்டு (பாடாண் பாட்டும் ஆம்)","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாண்பாட்டு","பாடாண் பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",2,"maa","scene","otter"],["உறையரா","அரா",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["வராஅலின்","வரால்",3,"pira","scene","murrel fish"],["முதலையொடு","முதலை",4,"pira","scene",""],["தும்பை","தும்பை",13,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["king","அழும்பிலன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:284":{"book":"purananuru","num":282,"t":["‘வருகதில் வல்லே ; வருகதில் வல்’ என","வேந்துவிடு விழுத்தூது ஆங்காங்கு இசைப்ப,","நூலரி மாலை சூடிக் , காலின்,","தமியன் வந்த மூதி லாளன்","அருஞ்சமம் தாங்கி, முன்னின்று எறிந்த","ஒருகை இரும்பிணத்து எயிறு மிறையாகத்","திரிந்த வாய்வாள் திருத்தாத்,","தனக்குஇரிந் தானைப் பெயர்புறம் நகுமே."],"p":"","r":"When the king's messengers cry everywhere 'let him come quickly', the man of an old fighting house comes alone on foot, garlanded, holds the hard battle, and while he straightens his bent sword against the tusk of the elephant he killed in front, he laughs at the back of the man who fled from him.","n":"The elephant's tusk becomes a tool for mending the sword, so the kill is already work done; the laugh is the poem's last word and it is aimed at a back, the one part of a fighter that should not be seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","moothil","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 284","lb":"PN284","poet":"ஓரம் போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"பண்பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாண்பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மாலை","மாலை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["ஒருகை","ஒருகை",6,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:285":{"book":"purananuru","num":283,"t":["பாசறை யீரே ! பாசறை யீரே !","துடியன் கையது வேலே ; அடிபுணர்","வாங்குஇரு மருப்பின் தீந்தொடைச் சீறியாழ்ப்","பாணன் கையது தோலே ; காண்வரக்","கடுந்தெற்று மூடையின் .. .. ..","வாடிய மாலை மலைந்த சென்னியன்;","வேந்துதொழில் அயரும் அருந்தலைச் சுற்றமொடு","நெடுநகர் வந்தென, விடுகணை மொசித்த","மூரி வேண்டோள் .. .. .. .. ..","சேறுபடு குருதிச் செம்மலுக் கோஒ!","மாறுசெறு நெடுவேல் மார்புஉளம் போக;","நிணம்பொதி கழலொடு நிலம் சேர்ந் தனனெ;","அதுகண்டு, பரந்தோர் எல்லாம்-புகழத் தலைபணிந்து","இறைஞ்சி யோனே, குருசில் ! - பிணங்குகதிர்","அலமரும் கழனித் தண்ணடை ஒழிய;","இலம்பாடு ஒக்கல் தலைவற்குஓர்","கரம்பைச் சீறூர் நல்கினன் எனவே."],"p":"","r":"Through a damaged text: in the camp the drummer holds the spear and the bard the shield; a warrior with a faded garland, his chest pierced by a long spear, fell to the ground with his fat-smeared anklet, all who saw it praised him, and his lord bowed his head, because to this leader of a poor kin he had given only a small village of barren land and not the cool fields where the grain-ears sway.","n":"On this reading the lord's bow is shame, not grief: the man's death exposes how little he was paid, so the poem judges the giver; with lines 5 and 9 broken, whether it was the lord who gave the barren village is the likeliest but not a certain reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","naanam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 285","lb":"PN285","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"சால்பு முல்லை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["சால்பு முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["துடியன்","துடியன்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணன்","பாணன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மாலை","மாலை",6,"maram","ornament",""],["பிணங்குகதிர்","கதிர்",14,"maram","absent","grain-ears"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:286":{"book":"purananuru","num":284,"t":["வெள்ளை வெள்யாட்டுச் செச்சை போலத்","தன்னோர் அன்ன இளையர் இருப்பப்,","பலர்மீது நீட்டிய மண்டைஎன் சிறுவனைக்","கால்வழி கட்டிலிற் கிடப்பித்,","தூவெள் அறுவை போர்ப்பித் திலதே!"],"p":"","r":"Avvaiyar's speaker sets her son, who held out his bowl above many, against the other youths who remain like rams of white goats, and says it has not laid him on a bier with bound feet and covered him in pure white cloth.","n":"The closing negative can be read as a plain statement (he died on the field and was not even carried home and shrouded) or as an exclamation (has it not laid him on the bier!); either way the son who was first to the king's bowl is set apart from the youths who survive.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","makkal","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 286","lb":"PN286","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"வேத்தியல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வேத்தியல்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெள்யாட்டுச்","வெள்யாடு",1,"maa","simile","white goat"],["செச்சை","செச்சை",1,"maa","simile","ram"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:287":{"book":"purananuru","num":285,"t":["துடி எறியும் புலைய!","எறிகோல் கொள்ளும் இழிசின!","கால மாரியின் அம்பு தைப்பினும்,","வயல் கெண்டையின் வேல் பிறழினும்,","பொலம்புனை ஓடை அண்ணல் யானை","இலங்குவாள் மருப்பின் நுதிமடுத்து ஊன்றினும்,","ஓடல் செல்லாப் பீடுடை யாளர்","நெடுநீர்ப் பொய்கைப் பிறழிய வாளை","நெல்லுடை நெடுநகர்க் கூட்டுமுதல் புரளும்,","தண்ணடை பெறுதல் யாவது? படினே;","மாசில் மகளிர் மன்றல் நன்றும்,","உயர்நிலை உலகத்து, நுகர்ப; அதனால்","வம்ப வேந்தன் தானை","இம்பர் நின்றும் காண்டிரோ, வரவே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the drum-beating pulaiyan, the poet says that warriors of pride who do not run though arrows hit like the seasonal rain, spears flash like kendai fish in the paddy and an elephant drives its tusk into them, care nothing for winning cool fields where vaalai fish roll at the granary's foot, since if they die they enjoy the weddings of flawless women in the high world; so watch the upstart king's army come from here.","n":"The poem names the two rewards of the hero side by side and ranks them: land if he lives is waved aside with யாவது, and the reward that counts is in the high world, which is a Sangam poem itself making heaven, not only fame, the warrior's wage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 287","lb":"PN287","poet":"சாத்தந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"நீண்மொழி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["நீண்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புலைய","புலையன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["துடி","துடி",1,"parai","scene",""],["மாரியின்","மாரி",3,"pira","simile","rain"],["கெண்டையின்","கெண்டை",4,"pira","simile","kendai fish"],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""],["பொய்கைப்","பொய்கை",8,"pira","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",8,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["நெல்லுடை","நெல்",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:288":{"book":"purananuru","num":286,"t":["மண்கொள வரிந்த வைந்நுதி மறுப்பின்","அண்ணல் நல்ஏறு இரண்டு உடன் மடுத்து,","வென்றதன் பச்சை சீவாது போர்த்த","திண்பிணி முரசம் இடைப்புலத்து இரங்க,","ஆர்அமர் மயங்கிய ஞாட்பின், தெறுவர,","நெடுவேல் பாய்ந்த நாணுடை நெஞ்சத்து,","அருகுகை .. .. .. .. .. .. மன்ற","குருதியொடு துயல்வரும் மார்பின்","முயக்கிடை ஈயாது மொய்த்தன, பருந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The drum covered with the unshaved hide of the winner of two bulls set to fight sounds in the middle ground; in the confused battle a long spear pierced the warrior's heart that knew shame, and on his blood-swaying chest the kites crowded so thick they left no room for an embrace.","n":"The drum's hide is itself the trophy of a fight between bulls, so the poem opens on one victor's skin and ends on another's body; who came to embrace him was in the lost line 7, and the kites have taken that place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 288","lb":"PN288","poet":"கழாத்தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நல்ஏறு","ஏறு",2,"maa","scene","bull"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",4,"parai","scene",""],["பருந்தே","பருந்து",9,"pul","scene","kite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:289":{"book":"purananuru","num":287,"t":["ஈரச் செவ்வி உதவின ஆயினும்,","பல்எருத் துள்ளும் நல் எருது நோக்கி,","வீறுவீறு ஆயும் உழவன் போலப்,","பீடுபெறு தொல்குடிப் பாடுபல தங்கிய","மூதி லாளர் உள்ளும், காதலின்","தனக்கு முகந்து ஏந்திய பசும்பொன் மண்டை,","‘இவற்கு ஈக !’ என்னும்; அதுவும்அன் றிசினே;","கேட்டியோ வாழி-பாண! பாசறைப்,","‘பூக்கோள் இன்று’ என்று அறையும்","மடிவாய்த் தண்ணுமை இழிசினன் குரலே?"],"p":"","r":"As a ploughman, even when the wet season has favoured him, looks among his many bulls for the best one, the king looks among the men of old fighting houses and says of the gold bowl raised for himself, 'give it to this one'; and more, hear, bard, the drummer in the camp crying 'today is the taking of flowers'.","n":"The honour and the death are announced together: the bowl the king gives is the sign of who will go first, and the drum that follows calls the flower-taking for battle, so the chosen man is being picked as the ploughman picks the bull for the hardest furrow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","moothil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 289","lb":"PN289","poet":"கழாத்தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["எருது","எருது",2,"maa","simile","bull"],["உழவன்","உழவன்",3,"seythi","simile",""],["பாண","பாணன்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",10,"parai","scene",""],["இழிசினன்","இழிசினன்",10,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:290":{"book":"purananuru","num":288,"t":["இவற்குஈந்து உண்மதி, கள்ளே; சினப்போர்","இனக்களிற்று யானை-இயல்தேர்க் குருசில்!","நுந்தை தந்தைக்கு இவன்தந்தை தந்தை,","எடுத்துஎறி ஞாட்பின் இமையான், தச்சன்","அடுத்துஎறி குறட்டின், நின்று மாய்ந் தனனே:","மறப்புகழ் நிறைந்த மைந்தினோன் இவனும்,","உறைப்புழி ஓலை போல","மறைக்குவன்- பெரும ! நிற் குறித்துவரு வேலே."],"p":"","r":"Give this man toddy before you drink, lord of war elephants: his father's father stood unblinking and died, like the hub the carpenter fits the spokes into, beside your father's father in battle, and this man too, full of martial fame, will cover you from the spear aimed at you like thatch in the rain.","n":"The claim to the first cup rests on a debt between two lines, not on this man's own deeds, and the two similes are both household things (the wheel hub, the roof thatch), so the warrior is made part of the king's house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","maram","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 290","lb":"PN290","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"குடிநிலையுரைத்தல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குடிநிலையுரைத்தல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கள்ளே","கள்",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["இனக்களிற்று","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["தச்சன்","தச்சன்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["ஓலை","ஓலை",7,"maram","simile","thatch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:291":{"book":"purananuru","num":289,"t":["சிறாஅஅர் ! துடியர்! பாடுவல் மகாஅஅர்;","தூவெள் அறுவை மாயோற் குறுகி","இரும்புள் பூசல் ஓம்புமின்; யானும்,","விளரிக் கொட்பின், வெண்ணரி கடிகுவென்;","என்போற் பெருவிதுப்பு உறுக, வேந்தே-","கொன்னும் சாதல் வெய்யோற்குத் தன்தலை!","மணிமருள் மாலை சூட்டி, அவன் தலை","ஒருகாழ் மாலை தான்மலைந் தனனே!"],"p":"","r":"The bard tells the children and drummers to keep the birds' clamour off the dark man lying under pure white cloth while she drives off the white jackal with the turning of a lament tune, and wishes on the king the same great trembling she feels, for he once put his own gem-like garland on the man who longed to die and wore the dead man's single-strand garland himself.","n":"The exchange of garlands is the poem's charge against the king: by wearing the warrior's garland he took the warrior's place in a bond, so the mourner asks that he share the grief too; கொன்னும் makes the death look needless.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","kaiyarunilai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 291","lb":"PN291","poet":"நெடுங்கழுத்துப் பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"வேத்தியல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வேத்தியல்"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["துடியர்","துடியர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["இரும்புள்","புள்",3,"pul","scene",""],["விளரிக்","விளரி",4,"yaazh","scene","lament tune"],["வெண்ணரி","நரி",4,"maa","scene","jackal"],["மாலை","மாலை",7,"maram","ornament",""],["மாலை","மாலை",8,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:292":{"book":"purananuru","num":290,"t":["வேந்தற்கு ஏந்திய தீந்தண் நறவம்","யாம்தனக்கு உறுமறை வளாவ, விலக்கி,","வாய்வாள் பற்றி நின்றனென்’ என்று,","சினவல் ஓம்புமின் சிறுபுல் லாளர்!","ஈண்டே போல வேண்டுவன் ஆயின்,","‘என்முறை வருக’ என்னான், கம்மென","எழுதரு பெரும்படை விலக்கி,","ஆண்டு நிற்கும் ஆண்தகை யன்னே."],"p":"","r":"Do not be angry, petty men, that when the sweet cool toddy raised for the king was being mixed he stopped it and stood gripping his true sword: if he wants something, as he did here, he will not wait for his turn, and the manly one will stand there and stop the great army as it rises.","n":"The speaker turns a breach of the feast's order into a promise: the same refusal to wait for his turn that offends at the toddy is what will hold the front, so his rudeness is offered as evidence of his courage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kal","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 292","lb":"PN292","poet":"விரிச்சியூர் நன்னாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"வஞ்சி","ctu":"பெருஞ்சோற்று நிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருஞ்சோற்று நிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நறவம்","நறவு",1,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:293":{"book":"purananuru","num":291,"t":["நிறப்புடைக்கு ஒல்கா யானை மேலோன்","குறும்பர்க்கு எறியும் ஏவல் தண்ணுமை","நாண்உடை மாக்கட்கு இரங்கும் ஆயின்,","எம்மினும் பேர்எழில் இழந்து, வினை எனப்","பிறர்மனை புகுவள் கொல்லோ?","அளியள் தானே, பூவிலைப் பெண்டே!"],"p":"","r":"If the summoning drum that the rider of the unflinching elephant beats against the hill-fort men sounds for men who know shame, the speaker asks, will the flower-selling woman, who has lost more of her beauty than we have, go into other people's houses as her trade: she is to be pitied.","n":"The drum that sends men to take flowers for battle sends the flower-seller into houses that may soon be in mourning; who the 'we' are (the wives at home) and why she has lost more beauty than they is not said, so the pity may be for her poverty or for her trade in the flowers of the dead.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","inmai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 293","lb":"PN293","poet":"நொச்சி நியமங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பூக்கோட் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பூக்கோட்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",2,"parai","scene",""],["பூவிலைப்","பூவிலைப் பெண்டு",6,"seythi","scene","flower-seller"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:294":{"book":"purananuru","num":292,"t":["வெண்குடை மதியம் மேல்நிலாத் திகழ்தரக்;","கண்கூடு இறுத்த கடல்மருள் பாசறைக்,","குமரிப்படை தழீஇய கூற்றுவினை ஆடவர்","தமர்பிறர் அறியா அமர்மயங்கு அழுவத்து,","இறையும் பெயரும் தோற்றி,”நுமருள்","நாள்முறை தபுத்தீர் வம்மின், ஈங்கு” எனப்","போர்மலைந்து ஒருசிறை நிற்ப, யாவரும்","அரவுஉமிழ் மணியின் குறுகார்;","நிரைதார் மார்பின்நின் கேள்வனைப் பிறரே."],"p":"","r":"In the sea-like camp under the moon of the white umbrella, in a battle so confused that kin cannot be told from foe, your husband called out his lord's name and his own, 'come here, you who would cut short our men's days', and stood fighting apart, and no one came near him, as no one comes near the gem a serpent has spat out.","n":"Naming his lord and himself is the point: in a battle where nobody knows who is who, he makes himself known and so becomes untouchable, like the serpent's gem that is treasure and danger at once; குமரிப்படை in line 3 is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 294","lb":"PN294","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானைமறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடல்மருள்","கடல்",2,"pira","simile",""],["அரவுஉமிழ்","அரவு",8,"pira","simile","serpent"],["நிரைதார்","தார்",9,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:295":{"book":"purananuru","num":293,"t":["கடல்கிளர்ந் தன்ன கட்டூர் நாப்பண்,","வெந்துவாய் மடித்து வேல்தலைப் பெயரித்,","தோடுஉகைத்து ‘எழுதரூஉ, துரந்துஎறி ஞாட்பின்,","வருபடை போழ்ந்து வாய்ப்பட விலங்கி,","இடைப்படை அழுவத்துச் சிதைந்து வேறாகிய,","சிறப்புடை யாளன் மாண்புகண் டருவி,","வாடுமலை ஊறிச் சுரந்தன","ஓடாப் பூட்கை விடலை தாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"In the camp heaving like the sea, the young man of unretreating vow bit his lip, drove forward, split the oncoming army and lay broken apart in the middle of the press; seeing the glory of her excellent son, his mother's withered breasts welled and flowed.","n":"The mother sees the body, not a report, and the response is in the body too: breasts long dry give milk again, as at his birth, so his death returns her to his infancy; the thurai's name holds joy and weeping together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 295","lb":"PN295","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கடல்கிளர்ந்","கடல்",1,"pira","simile",""],["டருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","simile","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:296":{"book":"purananuru","num":294,"t":["வேம்புசினை ஒடிப்பவும், காஞ்சி பாடவும்,","நெய்யுடைக் கையர் ஐயவி புகைப்பவும்,","எல்லா மனையும் கல்லென் றவ்வே","வெந்துஉடன்று எறிவான் கொல்லோ","நெடிதுவந் தன்றால் நெடுந்தகை தேரே?"],"p":"","r":"Neem branches are broken, kaanchi is sung, mustard is smoked by ghee-smeared hands and every house is loud with it: is it that he fights on, burning with rage, that the great man's chariot is so long in coming?","n":"The question in the last two lines is a hope disguised as a guess: the rites of 281 are already under way in every house, which suggests wounds, but the speaker reads the delay as rage still fighting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 296","lb":"PN296","poet":"வெள்ளை மாளர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"எறான் முல்லை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஏறாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["வேம்பு","வேம்பு",1,"maram","scene","neem"],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",1,"yaazh","scene","kaanchi song"],["நெய்யுடைக்","நெய்",2,"unavu","scene",""],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",2,"maram","scene","white mustard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:297":{"book":"purananuru","num":295,"t":["பெருநீர் மேவல் தண்ணடை எருமை","இருமருப்பு உறழும் நெடுமாண் நெற்றின்","பைம்பயறு உதிர்த்த கோதின் கோல்அணைக்,","கன்றுடை மரையாத் துஞ்சும் சீறூர்க்","கோள்இவண் வேண்டேம், புரவே; நார்அரி","நனைமுதிர் சாடிநறவின் வாழ்த்தித்,","துறைநனி கெழீஇக் கம்புள் ஈனும்","தண்ணடை பெறுதலும் உரித்தே, வைந்நுதி","நெடுவேல் பாய்ந்த மார்பின்,","மடல்வன் போந்தையின், நிற்கு மோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"We do not want to seize this small village where the wild cow with her calf sleeps on a bed of threshed gram stalks whose pods are as long as a buffalo's horns; we want a grant, and blessing it with toddy from the jar it is right that cool fields by the ford where the waterhen lays her eggs be given to those who stand like the stiff-fronded palmyra, their chests pierced by the long spear.","n":"The poem refuses plunder of a poor village and asks instead for a lawful grant, so it sets a limit on what the victors may take; the palmyra image gives the wounded men a standing posture even with spears in them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 297","lb":"PN297","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வெட்சி","ctu":"இண்டாட்டு","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["உண்டாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","simile",""],["பைம்பயறு","பயறு",3,"maram","scene",""],["மரையாத்","மரையா",4,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["நறவின்","நறவு",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கம்புள்","கம்புள்",7,"pul","scene","waterhen"],["போந்தையின்","போந்தை",10,"maram","simile","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:298":{"book":"purananuru","num":296,"t":["எமக்கே கலங்கல் தருமே தானே","தேறல் உண்ணும் மன்னே : நன்றும்","இன்னான் மன்ற வேந்தே; இனியே-","நேரார் ஆரெயில் முற்றி,","வாய் மடித்து உரறி,’ நீ முந்து? என் னானே."],"p":"","r":"The warrior complains of his king: he used to give us the dregs and drink the clear toddy himself, which was once his way; he is truly unkind, for now, besieging the enemy's strong fort, biting his lip and roaring, he does not say to me 'you go first'.","n":"The grievance is not the poor drink but the denial of the vanguard: a warrior measures the king's favour by who is sent first to die, and மன் may mark the drinking order as a past custom now broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maram","vendhan","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 298","lb":"PN298","poet":"ஆவியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"கரந்தை","ctu":"நெடுமொழி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["நெடுமொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:299":{"book":"purananuru","num":297,"t":["பருத்தி வேலிச் சீறூர் மன்னன்","உழுத்துஅதர் உண்ட ஓய்நடைப் புரவி,","கடல்மண்டு தோணியின், படைமுகம் போழ-","நெய்ம்மிதி அருந்திய, கொய்சுவல் எருத்தின்,","தண்ணடை மன்னர், தாருடைப் புரவி,","அணங்குஉடை முருகன் கோட்டத்துக்","கலம்தொடா மகளிரின், இகழ்ந்துநின் றவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The horse of the chief of a small village fenced with cotton, slow-gaited from eating gram stalks, cleaved the battle-front like a boat driving into the sea, while the garlanded, ghee-fed, mane-clipped horses of the kings of rich fields held back, like women who may not touch the vessels in fearsome Murugan's shrine.","n":"Every detail is set in pairs, cotton fence against cool fields, gram stalks against ghee, so the poem argues that feeding and finery do not make a warhorse; the second simile turns the rich horses' hanging back into a ritual bar rather than open cowardice, which makes the scorn sharper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","selvam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 299","lb":"PN299","poet":"பொன் முடியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"நொச்சி","ctu":"குதிரை மறம்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குதிரை மறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பருத்தி","பருத்தி",1,"maram","scene","cotton"],["உழுத்துஅதர்","உழுந்து",2,"unavu","gift_food","black gram"],["புரவி","புரவி",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["தோணியின்","தோணி",3,"pira","simile","boat"],["நெய்ம்மிதி","நெய்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தாருடைப்","தார்",5,"maram","ornament",""],["முருகன்","முருகன்",6,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:300":{"book":"purananuru","num":298,"t":["‘தோல்தா; தோல்தா’ என்றி ; தோலொடு","துறுகல் மறையினும் உய்குவை போலாய்;","நெருநல் எல்லைநீ எறிந்தோன் தம்பி,","அகல்பெய் குன்றியின் சுழலும் கண்ணன்,","பேரூர் அட்ட கள்ளிற்கு","ஓர் இல் கோயின் தேருமால் நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"You cry 'give me a shield, give me a shield', but even with a shield behind a boulder you will not escape: the younger brother of the man you killed yesterday evening, his eyes whirling like kunri seeds dropped in a lamp, is searching for you as men search the great town for the one house where toddy was brewed.","n":"The revenge is a kinsman's duty, so the killing of yesterday makes today's enemy; lines 5 and 6 also compare the search to a hunt for toddy, but the phrase ஓர் இல் கோயின் is unclear and is left out of the comparisons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 300","lb":"PN300","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானைமறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானைமறம்"],"mp":[["vekuli","கொலை"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["துறுகல்","கல்",2,"pira","scene","boulder"],["குன்றியின்","குன்றி",4,"maram","simile","kunri seed"],["கள்ளிற்கு","கள்",5,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:15"},"pn:301":{"book":"purananuru","num":299,"t":["பல் சான்றீரே ! பல் சான்றீரே!","குமரி மகளிர் கூந்தல் புரைய,","அமரின் இட்ட அருமுள் வேலிக்","கல்லென் பாசறைப் பல்சான் றீரே!","முரசுமுழங்கு தானைநும் அரசும் ஓம்புமின்;","ஒளிறு ஏந்து ,மருப்பின்நும் களிறும் போற்றுமின்!","எனைநாள் தங்கும்நும் போரே, அனைநாள்","எறியர் எறிதல் யாவணது? எறிந்தோர்","எதிர்சென்று எறிதலும் செல்லான்; அதனால்","அறிந்தோர் யார், அவன் கண்ணிய பொருளே!","‘பலம்’ என்று இகழ்தல் ஓம்புமின்! உதுக்காண்","நிலன்அளப் பன்ன நில்லாக் குறுநெறி,","வண்பரிப் புரவிப் பண்புபா ராட்டி,","எல்லிடைப் படர்தந் தோனே ; கல்லென","வேந்தூர் யானைக்கு அல்லது,","ஏந்துவன் போலான், தன் இலங்கிலை வேலே!"],"p":"","r":"The speaker warns the many warriors in the thorn-fenced camp to guard their king and their elephants, for the hero opposite does not even go out to meet those who strike at him, no one knows what he intends, and having ridden off at night on his fine horse he looks like a man who will lift his spear only against the elephant the enemy king rides.","n":"The threat lies in restraint: the hero's refusal to answer ordinary blows (எறிந்தோர் எதிர்சென்று எறிதலும் செல்லான்) is what makes him dangerous, because it shows he is saving his spear for the king's own elephant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 301","lb":"PN301","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"தானை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["தானைமறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசுமுழங்கு","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",6,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புரவிப்","புரவி",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["யானைக்கு","யானை",15,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:302":{"book":"purananuru","num":300,"t":["வெடிவேய் கொள்வது போல ஓடித்","தாவுபு உகளும், மாவே; பூவே,","விளங்கிழை மகளிர் கூந்தற் கொண்ட;","நரந்தப் பல்காழ்க் கோதை சுற்றிய","ஐதுஅமை பாணி வணர்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ்க்","கைவார் நரம்பின் பாணர்க்கு ஒக்கிய,","நிரம்பா இயல்பின் கரம்பைச் சீறூர்;","நோக்கினர்ச் செகுக்கும் காளை ஊக்கி,","வேலின் அட்ட களிறு பெயர்த்து எண்ணின்,","விண்ணிவர் விசும்பின் மீனும்,","தண்பெயல் உறையும், உறையாற் றாவே."],"p":"","r":"The horses leap and bound like a bamboo bursting; the village's flowers went to the women's hair and its narantham garlands to the bards; the poor small village on stony ground is never full, yet if one counted the elephants the young warrior killed with his spear they would outnumber the stars of the sky and the drops of the cool rain.","n":"The poem sets the village's want (நிரம்பா இயல்பின் கரம்பைச் சீறூர்) against its giving: what little it has, flowers and garlands, has already gone to women and bards, and its only abundance is the count of its warrior's kills.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 302","lb":"PN302","poet":"வெறிபாடிய காமக் கண்ணியார் (காமக் கணியார் எனவும் பாடம்)","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"குதிரை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குதிரைமறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",1,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["மாவே","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["நரந்தப்","நரந்தம்",4,"maram","ornament",""],["சீறியாழ்க்","சீறியாழ்",5,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணர்க்கு","பாணர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:303":{"book":"purananuru","num":301,"t":["நிலம்பிறக் கிடுவது போலக் குளம்பு குடையூஉ","உள்ளம் அழிக்கும் கொட்பின் மான்மேல்","எள்ளுநர்ச் செகுக்கும் காளை கூர்த்த","வெந்திறல் எஃகம் நெஞ்சுவடு விளைப்ப","ஆட்டிக் காணிய வருமே; நெருநை,","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் வேந்தர் முன்னர்க்,","கரைபொரு முந்நீர்த் திமிலின் போழ்ந்து, அவர்","கயந்தலை மடப்பிடி புலம்ப,","இலங்கு மருப்பு யானை எறிந்த எற்கே."],"p":"","r":"A warrior speaks: the young rider who destroys his mockers is coming on a whirling horse that digs the ground with its hooves as if thrusting the earth back, to try his hot spear and scar the chest of me, who only yesterday, before famous kings, cut through their ranks like a boat through the sea and killed their tusked elephant so that its young cow mourned.","n":"The colophon names the horse's valour, but the poem is spoken by the target: the older warrior describes the young rider's charge as a test he welcomes, and answers it with his own record of yesterday.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 303","lb":"PN303","poet":"எருமை வெளியனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"குதிரை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குதிரைமறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மான்மேல்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["திமிலின்","திமில்",7,"seythi","simile","fishing boat"],["முந்நீர்த்","முந்நீர்",7,"pira","simile","sea"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",8,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:304":{"book":"purananuru","num":302,"t":["கொடுங்குழை மகளிர் கோதை சூட்டி,","நடுங்குபனிக் களைஇயர் நாரரி பருகி,","வளிதொழில் ஒழிக்கும் வண்பரிப் புரவி","பண்ணற்கு விரைதி, நீயே;’நெருநை","எம்முன் தப்பியோன் தம்பியொடு, ஓராங்கு","நாளைச் செய்குவென் அமர்’ எனக் கூறிப்,","புன்வயிறு அருத்தலும் செல்லான், வன்மான்","கடவும் என்ப, பெரிதே; அது கேட்டு,","வலம்படு முரசின் வெல்போர் வேந்தன்","இலங்கு இரும் பாசறை நடுங்கின்று;","‘இரண்டா காது அவன் கூறியது’ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero garlands the women, drinks strained toddy against the shivering cold, and hurries to harness his horse that outruns the wind; saying 'tomorrow I fight the younger brother of the man who escaped me yesterday', he will not even feed his lean belly but drives the strong horse hard, and when the victorious king's camp hears it, it trembles, because what he says does not come out two ways.","n":"The enemy camp fears a sentence, not an army: its trembling rests on the hero's record that his word is never 'two' (இரண்டா காது), so the vow does the work of an assault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vanjinam","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 304","lb":"PN304","poet":"அரிசில்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"குதிரை மறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["குதிரைமறம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["நாரரி","நாரரி",2,"unavu","gift_food","strained toddy"],["புரவி","புரவி",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["வன்மான்","மான்",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["முரசின்","முரசு",9,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:305":{"book":"purananuru","num":303,"t":["வயலைக் கொடியின் வாடிய மருங்கின்,","உயவல் ஊர்திப் , பயலைப் பார்ப்பான்","எல்லி வந்து நில்லாது புக்குச்,","சொல்லிய சொல்லோ சிலவே; அதற்கே","ஏணியும் சீப்பும் மாற்றி,","மாண்வினை யானையும் மணிகளைந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"A lean, pale brahmin, his waist shrunk like a withered vayalai creeper, came at nightfall and went straight in; he spoke only a few words, and for them the siege ladders and the gate-bars were put away and the bells were taken off the well-trained war elephants.","n":"The poem measures cause against effect: a frail man and a few words on one side, the whole apparatus of siege dismantled on the other; it never says what he said, which keeps the force on the brevity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 305","lb":"PN305","poet":"மதுரை வேளாசான்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"பார்ப்பன வாகை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பார்ப்பனவாகை"],"mp":[["marutkai","சிறுமை"]],"k":[["வயலைக்","வயலை",1,"maram","simile","vayalai creeper"],["யானையும்","யானை",6,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:306":{"book":"purananuru","num":304,"t":["களிறுபொரக் கலங்கு, கழன்முள் வேலி,","அரிதுஉண் கூவல், அங்குடிச் சீறூர்","ஒலிமென் கூந்தல் ஒண்ணுதல் அரிவை","நடுகல் கைதொழுது பரவும், ஒடியாது;","விருந்து எதிர் பெறுகதில் யானே; என்ஐயும்","ஒ .. .. .. .. .. .. வேந்தனொடு,","நாடுதரு விழுப்பகை எய்துக எனவே."],"p":"","r":"In a small village fenced with thorns that shake when elephants fight, where the well is hard to drink from, a woman with soft thick hair and bright brow worships the hero-stone with joined hands every day, praying that she may always have guests to receive and that her lord, [gap] with the king, may win a great war that brings land.","n":"Her first request to the dead hero of the stone is guests, and only then her husband's war; hospitality and battle are asked of the same stone, and the war she wants is one that brings land (நாடுதரு), not one that merely brings fame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 306","lb":"PN306","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன் முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின்முல்லை"],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறுபொரக்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கழன்முள்","கழல் முள்",1,"maram","scene","kazhal thorn"],["கூவல்","கூவல்",2,"pira","scene","well"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:307":{"book":"purananuru","num":305,"t":["ஆசாகு எந்தை யாண்டுளன் கொல்லோ?","குன்றத்து அன்ன களிற்றொடு பட்டோன்;","வம்பலன் போலத் தோன்றும்; உதுக்காண்;","வேனல் வரி அணில் வாலத்து அன்ன;","கான ஊகின் கழன்றுகு முதுவீ","அரியல் வான்குழல் சுரியல் தங்க,","நீரும் புல்லும் ஈயாது உமணர்","யாரும்இல் ஒருசிறை முடத்தொடு துறந்த","வாழா வான்பகடு ஏய்ப்பத், தெறுவர்","பேருயிர் கொள்ளும் மாதோ; அதுகண்டு,","வெஞ்சின யானை வேந்தனும், ‘ இக்களத்து","எஞ்சலின் சிறந்தது பிறிதொன்று இல்’ எனப்,","பண் கொளற்கு அருமை நோக்கி,","நெஞ்சற வீழ்ந்த புரைமை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"Where is my lord, our support? He fell with an elephant like a hill and lies like a stranger; see, the old ookam blossoms, like a summer squirrel's tail, settle on his long curled hair, and like a lame old bull the salt-carters abandoned without water or grass he still takes the lives of foes, so that the enemy king, seeing it, said there was nothing finer than to stay on this field and fell, his heart broken.","n":"The image of the abandoned bull is pitiless: the support of the house (ஆசாகு எந்தை) lies like a beast left to die by a road, and yet the same lines give him the power to kill, so the lament and the boast share one figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 307","lb":"PN307","poet":"பெயர் புலனாகவில்லை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"களிற்றுடனிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["களிற்றுடனிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["அணில்","அணில்",4,"maa","simile","squirrel"],["ஊகின்","ஊகம்",5,"maram","scene","ookam grass"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",7,"seythi","simile","salt-carters"],["பகடு","பகடு",9,"maa","simile","bull"],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:308":{"book":"purananuru","num":306,"t":["பொன்வார்ந் தன்ன புரியடங்கு நரம்பின்,","மின்நேர் பச்சை, மிஞிற்றுக்குரற் சீறியாழ்","நன்மை நிறைந்த நயவரு பாண!","சீறூர் மன்னன் சிறியிலை எஃகம்","வேந்துஊர் யானை ஏந்துமுகத் ததுவே;","வேந்துஉடன்று எறிந்த வேலே, என்னை","சார்ந்தார் அகலம் உளம்கழிந் தன்றே;","உளங்கழி சுடர்ப்படை ஏந்தி, நம் பெருவிறல்","ஓச்சினன் துரந்த காலை, மற்றவன்","புன்தலை மடப்பிடி நாணக்,","குஞ்சரம் எல்லாம் புறக்கொடுத் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"A woman of the warrior house tells the bard with the gold-stringed little lute that the small-village chief's little-leafed spear now stands in the face of the king's mounted elephant, while the spear the angry king threw passed through the chests of those around her lord; and when our hero raised the bright weapon and drove forward, all the bull elephants turned their backs, to the shame of their young cows.","n":"A small village's chief (சீறூர் மன்னன்) against a crowned king's elephants is the poem's proportion; the shame is transferred to the cow elephants, who watch their males flee.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 308","lb":"PN308","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின்முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["மிஞிற்றுக்குரற்","மிஞிறு",2,"pira","simile","bee"],["பாண","பாணன்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",10,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",11,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:309":{"book":"purananuru","num":307,"t":["இரும்புமுகம் சிதைய நூறி, ஒன்னார்","இருஞ்சமம் கடத்தல் ஏனோர்க்கும் எளிதே;","நல்லரா உறையும் புற்றம் போலவும்,","கொல்ஏறு திரிதரு மன்றம் போலவும்,","மாற்றருந் துப்பின் மாற்றோர், ‘பாசறை","உளன்’ என வெரூஉம் ஓர்ஒளி","வலன்உயர் நெடுவேல் என்னைகண் ணதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Smashing iron-faced shields and crossing a great battle of foes is easy for anyone; what belongs to my lord is the single shining tall victorious spear at whose mere report 'he is in the camp' matchless enemies take fright, as people fear an anthill where a cobra lives or a common where a killing bull roams.","n":"The poem demotes the deed the thurai names (cutting through the ranks) as common to all, and claims for the lord only the fear his presence causes; both similes are of a place made dangerous by what lives in it, not of the creature itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 309","lb":"PN309","poet":"மதுரை இளங்கண்ணிக் கௌசிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"நூழிலாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["நூழிலாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நல்லரா","அரா",3,"pira","simile","cobra"],["புற்றம்","புற்று",3,"pira","simile","anthill"],["கொல்ஏறு","ஏறு",4,"maa","simile","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:310":{"book":"purananuru","num":308,"t":["பால்கொண்டு மடுப்பவும் உண்ணான் ஆகலின்,","செறாஅது ஓச்சிய சிறுகோல் அஞ்சியொடு,","உயவொடு வருந்தும் மன்னே! இனியே","புகர்நிறங் கொண்ட களிறட்டு ஆனான்,","முன்நாள் வீழ்ந்த உரவோர் மகனே,","உன்னிலன் என்னும், புண்ஒன்று அம்பு-","மான்உளை அன்ன குடுமித்","தோல்மிசைக் கிடந்த புல்அண லோனே."],"p":"","r":"Once, when milk was brought to him he would not drink, and he fretted at the little stick she raised without anger; now the son of the strong man who fell the other day, having killed a spotted elephant, says he does not even feel the arrow in his wound, as he lies on the shield, his tuft like a horse's mane, his beard just coming.","n":"The before and after are both about refusing: the child who would not drink milk and feared a raised stick is now the youth who will not notice an arrow; the speaker's tenderness never says whether he lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","makkal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 310","lb":"PN310","poet":"பொன்முடியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"நூழிலாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["நூழிலாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பால்கொண்டு","பால்",1,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["களிறட்டு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மான்உளை","மான்",7,"maa","simile","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:311":{"book":"purananuru","num":309,"t":["களர்ப்படு கூவல் தோண்டி, நாளும்,","புலைத்தி கழீஇய தூவெள் அறுவை;","தாதுஎரு மறுகின் மாசுண இருந்து,","பலர்குறை செய்த மலர்த்தார் அண்ணற்கு","ஒருவரும் இல்லை மாதோ , செருவத்துச்;","சிறப்புடைச் செங்கண் புகைய, வோர்","தோல்கொண்டு மறைக்கும் சால்புடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"Avvai speaks of a lord in a white cloth washed daily by the washerwoman in water from a well dug in saline ground, who sat in the pollen-strewn street and served the needs of many: in battle there is no one like him, the noble one who, when the honoured warriors' red eyes smoke with rage, covers them with his single shield.","n":"The first half is all peace and humility: a plain washed cloth, sitting in the dust of the street, doing others' errands; the same man in battle is the one who hides others behind his shield, so his service does not change with the setting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","oppuravu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 311","lb":"PN311","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"thumbai","cth":"தும்பை","ctu":"பாண் பாட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பாண்பாட்டு"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கூவல்","கூவல்",1,"pira","scene","well"],["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",2,"seythi","scene","washerwoman"],["தாதுஎரு","தாது",3,"maram","scene","pollen"],["மலர்த்தார்","மலர்த் தார்",4,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:312":{"book":"purananuru","num":310,"t":["ஈன்று புறந்தருதல் என்தலைக் கடனே;","சான்றோன் ஆக்குதல் தந்தைக்குக் கடனே;","வேல்வடித்துக் கொடுத்தல் கொல்லற்குக் கடனே;","நன்னடை நல்கல் வேந்தற்குக் கடனே;","ஒளிறுவாள் அருஞ்சமம் முருக்கிக்,","களிறுஎறிந்து பெயர்தல் காளைக்குக் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"To bear him and bring him up is my duty, to make him a worthy man is his father's, to forge and give him a spear is the smith's, to give him good conduct is the king's, and to cut through the hard battle with his shining sword, kill elephants and come back is the young man's duty.","n":"Five duties are owed to the son by four givers, and the last, his own, is not to win but to return (பெயர்தல்) after killing elephants; the king's share is conduct (நன்னடை), not wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","maram","saandror","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 312","lb":"PN312","poet":"பொன்முடியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின்முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின்முல்லை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கொல்லற்கு","கொல்லன்",3,"seythi","scene","smith"],["களிறுஎறிந்து","களிறு",6,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:313":{"book":"purananuru","num":311,"t":["அத்தம் நண்ணிய நாடுகெழு பெருவிறல்","கைப்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இலனே; நச்சிக்","காணிய சென்ற இரவன் மாக்கள்","களிறொடு நெடுந்தேர் வேண்டினும், கடவ;","உப்பொய் சாகாட்டு உமணர் காட்ட","கழிமுரி குன்றத்து அற்றே,","எள் அமைவு இன்று, அவன் உள்ளிய பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The great hero of a land beside the wilderness has nothing in hand, yet if the suppliants who go longing to see him ask for elephants and tall chariots he is bound to give them; what he has in mind is as sure as the hill of salt the salt-carters with laden carts point to, without a sesame seed's doubt.","n":"The word for his obligation is கடவ, 'bound to': giving is owed, not chosen, and the poem asserts it while saying in the same breath that he has nothing in his hand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","parisil","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 313","lb":"PN313","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லான் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களிறொடு","களிறு",4,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["உப்பொய்","உப்பு",5,"pira","scene","salt"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","scene","salt-carters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:314":{"book":"purananuru","num":312,"t":["மனைக்கு விளக்காகிய வாள்நுதல் கணவன்,","முனைக்கு வரம்பாகிய வென்வேல் நெடுந்தகை,","நடுகல் பிறங்கிய உவல்இடு பறந்தலைப்,","புன்காழ் நெல்லி வன்புலச் சீறூர்க்","குடியும் மன்னுந் தானே; கொடியெடுத்து","நிறையழிந்து எழுதரு தானைக்குச்","சிறையும் தானே- தன் இறைவிழு முறினே."],"p":"","r":"The husband of the bright-browed woman, a lamp to his house, the tall noble with the conquering spear who is the boundary of the battle front, is alone the stay of the people of the small village of thin-stemmed nelli on hard ground beside the leaf-strewn field of standing hero-stones, and if his own lord falls, he alone is the dam for the army that breaks rank and flees with its banners.","n":"The three metaphors share one function at three scales: lamp to a house, boundary to a front, dam to a broken army; the village of hero-stones beside him shows what that function has cost others before him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kudi","ilvaazhkkai","nadukal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 314","lb":"PN314","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லான் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["உவல்இடு","உவல்",3,"maram","scene","dry leaves"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",4,"maram","scene","nelli tree"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:315":{"book":"purananuru","num":313,"t":["உடையன் ஆயின் உண்ணவும் வல்லன்;","கடவர் மீதும் இரப்போர்க்கு ஈயும்;","மடவர் மகிழ்துணை; நெடுமான் அஞ்சி;","இல்லிறைச் செரீஇய ஞெலிகோல் போலத்,","தோன்றாது இருக்கவும் வல்லன்; மற்றதன்","கான்றுபடு கனைஎரி போலத்,","தோன்றவும் வல்லன்- தான் தோன்றுங் காலே."],"p":"","r":"Avvai says of Neduman Anji: when he has, he can eat; he gives to suppliants even beyond those he is bound to; he is the joy and companion of simple folk; like the fire-drill stick tucked into the eaves of a house he can stay unseen, and like the roaring fire it gives off he can blaze out when he chooses to appear.","n":"The two similes are one object in two states: the fire is already in the stick, so his restraint is not weakness but stored force, and the word வல்லன் (he can) is repeated for both eating, hiding and blazing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","adakkam","kaalam","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 315","lb":"PN315","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லான் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஞெலிகோல்","ஞெலிகோல்",4,"pira","simile","fire-drill"],["கனைஎரி","எரி",6,"pira","simile","fire"]],"en":[["patron","நெடுமான் அஞ்சி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:316":{"book":"purananuru","num":314,"t":["கள்ளின் வாழ்த்திக், கள்ளின் வாழ்த்திக்,","காட்டொடு மிடைந்த சீயா முன்றில்,","நாட்செருக்கு அனந்தர்த் துஞ்சு வோனே!","அவன் எம் இறைவன்; யாம்அவன் பாணர்;","நெருநை வந்த விருந்திற்கு மற்றுத் தன்","இரும்புடைப் பழவாள் வைத்தனன்; இன்றுஇக்","கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ் பணையம்; இதுகொண்டு","ஈவது இலாளன் என்னாது, நீயும்;","வள்ளி மருங்குல் வயங்குஇழை அணியக்,","கள்ளுடைக் கலத்தேம் யாம்மகிழ் தூங்கச்,","சென்று வாய் சிவந்துமேல் வருக-","சிறுகண் யானை வேந்து விழுமுறவே."],"p":"","r":"The bard says: he who sleeps late in the yard overgrown with scrub, drowsy from the toddy he praises day after day, is our lord and we are his bards; yesterday he pawned his old iron-bound sword for guests who came, today this black-necked little lute is the pledge; do not think he has nothing to give, but take it, so that your slim-waisted woman may wear bright jewels and we may sway with our toddy pots, and may he go and come back red-mouthed when the king with small-eyed elephants meets trouble.","n":"The pledge passes downward: the lord pawns his sword for guests, and the bard then offers his own lute as surety to the toddy-seller, so the household's credit rests on the lord's coming war, which will redeem both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","kal","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 316","lb":"PN316","poet":"மதுரைக் கள்ளிற் கடையத்தன் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",7,"yaazh","scene",""],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",9,"maram","scene","creeper"],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:317":{"book":"purananuru","num":315,"t":["வென்வேல் .. .. .. .. .. .. நது","முன்றில் கிடந்த பெருங்களி யாளற்கு","அதளுண் டாயினும், பாய்உண்டு ஆயினும்,","யாதுண்டு ஆயினும், கொடுமின் வல்லே;","வேட்கை மீளப .. .. .. .. .. ..",".. .. .. .. கும், எமக்கும், பிறர்க்கும்,","யார்க்கும், ஈய்ந்து, துயில்ஏற் பினனே."],"p":"","r":"[Line 1 gapped] Whatever there is, a hide, a mat, anything, give it quickly to the great reveller lying in the front yard; [lines 5 and 6 gapped] having given to us, to others, to everyone, he took to sleep.","n":"What survives is a reversal: the man who gave to everyone has nothing left to lie on, and the speaker asks others to give him a hide or a mat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 317","lb":"PN317","poet":"மவேம்ப்ற்றூர்க் குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[],"k":[["அதளுண்","அதள்",3,"pira","scene","hide"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:318":{"book":"purananuru","num":316,"t":["கொய்யடகு வாடத், தருவிறகு உணங்க,","மயில்அம் சாயல் மாஅ யோளொடு","பசித்தன்று அம்ம, பெருந்தகை ஊரே-","மனைஉறை குரீஇக் கறையணற் சேவல்,","பாணர் நரம்பின் சுகிரொடு, வயமான்","குரல்செய் பீலியின் இழைத்த குடம்பைப்,","பெருஞ்செய் நெல்லின் அரிசி ஆர்ந்து, தன்","புன்புறப் பெடையொடு வதியும்","யாணர்த்து ஆகும்-வேந்துவிழு முறினே."],"p":"","r":"The noble's town has gone hungry together with his dark woman of peacock grace, the plucked greens wilting and the gathered firewood drying unused; yet if the king meets trouble it becomes a place of plenty, where the house sparrow with its dark throat eats rice from the big fields and lives with its dull-backed hen in a nest woven of bards' lute-string fibres and plumes.","n":"The village's plenty depends on its lord's distress turning into the warrior's opportunity (வேந்து விழுமுறின்): peace starves this house and war feeds it, so the sparrow's nest of bards' strings and plumes is a picture of the spoils to come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","inmai","maram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 318","lb":"PN318","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["கொய்யடகு","அடகு",1,"unavu","absent","greens"],["விறகு","விறகு",1,"pira","absent","firewood"],["மயில்அம்","மயில்",2,"pul","scene","peacock"],["குரீஇக்","குரீஇ",4,"pul","scene","sparrow"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",5,"yaazh","scene","lute string"],["பீலியின்","பீலி",6,"pul","scene","plume"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",7,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",8,"pul","scene","hen sparrow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:319":{"book":"purananuru","num":317,"t":["பூவற் படுவிற் கூவல் தோண்டிய","செங்கண் சின்னீர் பெய்த சீறில்","முன்றில் இருந்த முதுவாய்ச் சாடி,","யாம் கஃடு உண்டென, வறிது மாசின்று;","படலை முன்றிற் சிறுதினை உணங்கல்","புறவும் இதலும் அறவும் உண்கெனப்","பெய்தற்கு எல்லின்று பொழுதே; அதனால்,","முயல்சுட்ட வாயினும் தருகுவேம்; புகுதந்து","ஈங்குஇருந் தீமோ முதுவாய்ப் பாண!","கொடுங்கோட்டு ஆமான் நடுங்குதலைக் குழவி","புன்றலைச் சிறாஅர் கன்றெனப் பூட்டும்","சீறூர் மன்னன் நெருநை ஞாங்கர்,","வேந்துவிடு தொழிலொடு சென்றனன்; வந்து, நின்","பாடினி மாலை யணிய,","வாடாத் தாமரை சூட்டுவன் நினக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The wife of the small-village lord tells the old wise bard: the old jar in the yard of our small house, where a little reddish water from a well dug in red earth is poured, is clean and empty because we drank the toddy; it is too late to spread the millet out for the doves and quail; so though it is only roasted hare we will give it, come in and stay; our lord, whose boys yoke the trembling calf of the wild cow as if it were a calf of the house, went yesterday on the king's errand, and when he returns he will crown you with a lotus that never fades so that your paadini may wear a garland.","n":"The wife offers what is gone (the toddy drunk, the millet unspread) before what she has (roasted hare), and pays the rest on credit against her husband's return: the unfading lotus is the gold the king's errand will bring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inmai","parisil","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 319","lb":"PN319","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கூவல்","கூவல்",1,"pira","scene","well"],["சிறுதினை","தினை",5,"unavu","scene","millet"],["புறவும்","புறா",6,"pul","scene","dove"],["இதலும்","இதல்",6,"pul","scene","quail"],["முயல்சுட்ட","முயல்",8,"maa","gift_food","hare"],["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",10,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",15,"maram","ornament","gold lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:320":{"book":"purananuru","num":318,"t":["முன்றில் முஞ்ஞையொடு முசுண்டை பம்பிப்,","பந்தர் வேண்டாப் பலர்தூங்கு நீழல்,","கைம்மான் வேட்டுவன் கனைதுயில் மடிந்தெனப்,","பார்வை மடப்பிணை தழீஇப், பிறிதோர்","தீர்தொழில் தனிக்கலை திளைத்துவிளை யாட,","இன்புறு புணர்நிலை கண்ட மனையோள்","கணவன் எழுதலும் அஞ்சிக், கலையே","பிணைவயின் தீர்தலும் அஞ்சி, யாவதும்,","இவ்வழங் காமையின், கல்லென ஒலித்து,","மான்அதட் பெய்த உணங்குதினை வல்சி","கானக் கோழியொடு இதல்கவர்ந்து உண்டென,","ஆர நெருப்பின், ஆரல் நாறத்","தடிவுஆர்ந் திட்ட முழுவள் ளூரம்","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கலொடு ஒருங்குஇனிது அருந்தித்,","தங்கினை சென்மோ, பாண! தங்காது,","வேந்துதரு விழுக்கூழ் பரிசிலர்க்கு என்றும்","அருகாது ஈயும் வண்மை","உரைசால் நெடுந்தகை ஓம்பும் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"In a yard where munjai and musundai creepers make a shade no pandal is needed for, a hunter sleeps; a lone stag frolics with the tame decoy doe, and his wife, seeing their happy union, stays still, afraid her husband might wake and afraid the stag might leave the doe, while jungle fowl and quail noisily eat the millet drying on a deerskin; so, bard, eat the roasted meat smelling of sandal-fire with your whole kin, stay and then go, for this town is guarded by the famed noble who always gives the suppliants the fine food the king gives him.","n":"A love scene sits inside a puram poem: the wife holds still between two fears, for her husband's sleep and for the deer's union, and lets the birds eat the household's millet rather than break either; the poem then turns the same open-handedness on the bard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 320","lb":"PN320","poet":"வீரை வெளியனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண்முல்லை"],"mp":[],"k":[["முஞ்ஞையொடு","முஞ்ஞை",1,"maram","scene","munjai creeper"],["முசுண்டை","முசுண்டை",1,"maram","scene","musundai creeper"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",3,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",4,"maa","scene","doe"],["தனிக்கலை","கலை",5,"maa","scene","stag"],["மான்அதட்","மான் அதள்",10,"pira","scene","deerskin"],["தினை","தினை",10,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கோழியொடு","கோழி",11,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["இதல்கவர்ந்து","இதல்",11,"pul","scene","quail"],["ஆர","ஆரம்",12,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["வள்ளூரம்","வள் ஊரம்",13,"unavu","gift_food","meat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:16"},"pn:321":{"book":"purananuru","num":319,"t":["பொறிப்புறப் பூழின் போர்வல் சேவல்","மேந்தோல் களைந்த தீங்கொள் வெள்ளெள்","சுளகிடை உணங்கல் செவ்வி கொண்டு உடன்","வேனிற் கோங்கின் பூம்பொகுட் டன்ன","குடந்தைஅம் செவிய கோட்டெலி யாட்டக்,","கலிஆர் வரகின் பிறங்குபீள் ஒளிக்கும்,","வன்புல வைப்பி னதுவே-சென்று","தின்பழம் பசீஇ.. .. .. ..ன்னோ, பாண!","வாள்வடு விளங்கிய சென்னிச்","செருவெங் குருசில் ஓம்பும் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The bard is told that the town guarded by the battle-loving lord whose head bears sword scars lies in hard country, where a fighting quail snatches husked sesame drying on a winnowing tray and, chased by a rat with ears like a summer kongu bud, hides in the thick millet stalks.","n":"The long opening shows a poor dry land where even a quail must steal grain and hide, so the town's strength is all in its lord: the poem turns from a small comic chase to the scarred head in the last two lines. Line 8 is broken, so what the bard is told to do on arrival (eat fruit and rest?) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 321","lb":"PN321","poet":"உறையூர் மருத்துவன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பூழின்","பூழ்",1,"pul","scene","quail"],["வெள்ளெள்","எள்",2,"unavu","scene","sesame"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",4,"maram","simile",""],["கோட்டெலி","எலி",5,"maa","scene","rat"],["வரகின்","வரகு",6,"unavu","scene","millet"],["தின்பழம்","பழம்",8,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:322":{"book":"purananuru","num":320,"t":["உழுதூர் காளை ஊழ்கோடு அன்ன","கவைமுள் கள்ளிப் பொரிஅரைப் பொருந்திப்,","புதுவரகு அரிகால் கருப்பை பார்க்கும்","புன்தலைச் சிறாஅர் வில்லெடுத்து ஆர்ப்பின்,","பெருங்கண் குறுமுயல் கருங்கலன் உடைய","மன்றிற் பாயும் வன்புலத் ததுவே;","கரும்பின் எந்திரம் சிலைப்பின், அயலது,","இருஞ்சுவல் வாளை பிறழும் ஆங்கண்,","தண்பணை யாளும் வேந்தர்க்குக்","கண்படை ஈயா வேலோன் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The spearman who denies sleep to the kings of the cool wet fields lives in hard country, where dull-headed boys leaning on cactus with thorns like an old plough-bull's worn horns watch for rats in the millet stubble and their shouts send a hare leaping across the common, breaking the black pots, while near by, when the sugarcane press groans, the vaalai fish leap.","n":"Two economies sit side by side: boys hunting rats in a dry millet land, and the sugar mills and fish of the kings' watered fields next door. The hero belongs to the poor land, and his threat runs from it to the rich one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 322","lb":"PN322","poet":"ஆவூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["காளை","காளை",1,"maa","simile","bull"],["கள்ளிப்","கள்ளி",2,"maram","scene","cactus"],["புதுவரகு","வரகு",3,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கருப்பை","கருப்பை",3,"maa","scene","rat"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",5,"maa","scene","hare"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",7,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["வாளை","வாளை",8,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:323":{"book":"purananuru","num":321,"t":["புலிப்பாற் பட்ட ஆமான் குழவிக்குச்","சினங்கழி மூதாக் கன்றுமடுத்து ஊட்டும்","கா .. .. .. .. .. .. .. .. .. க்கு","உள்ளியது சுரக்கும் ஓம்பா ஈகை,","வெள்வேல் ஆவம்ஆயின், ஒள் வாள்","கறையடி யானைக்கு அல்லது","உறைகழிப் பறியா,வேலோன் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"An old cow, her anger gone, suckles the calf of a wild cow that a tiger has taken, and (after a broken line) the poem praises the spearman's unguarded giving that flows to whatever is wished for, and his sword, which leaves its sheath only for elephants.","n":"The image of a cow nursing an orphan of another kind is set before ஓம்பா ஈகை, giving that keeps nothing back; line 3, which would have joined them, is lost, so whether it was an open simile cannot be known.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 323","lb":"PN323","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புலிப்பாற்","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["மூதாக்","மூதா",2,"maa","scene","old cow"],["யானைக்கு","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:324":{"book":"purananuru","num":322,"t":["வெருக்கு விடையன்ன வெருள்நோக்குக் கயந்தலைப்","புள்ளூன் தின்ற புலவுநாறு கயவாய்,","வெள்வாய் வேட்டுவர் வீழ்துணை மகாஅர்","சிறியிலை உடையின் சுரையுடை வால்முள்","ஊக நுண்கோற் செறித்த அம்பின்,","வலாஅர் வல்வில் குலாவரக் கோலிப்,","பருத்தி வேலிக் கருப்பை பார்க்கும்","புன்புலம் தழீஇய அங்குடிச் சீறூர்க்,","குமிழ்உண் வெள்ளைப் பகுவாய் பெயர்த்த","வெண்வாழ் தாய வண்காற் பந்தர்,","இடையன் பொத்திய சிறுதீ விளக்கத்துப்,","பாணரொடு இருந்த நாணுடை நெடுந்தகை,","வலம்படு தானை வேந்தற்கு","உலந்துழி உலக்கும் நெஞ்சறி துணையே."],"p":"","r":"In a small village on poor land, where hunters' boys with frightened eyes like a wild tomcat's and mouths smelling of bird meat bend their bows with thorn-tipped reed arrows to watch for rats at the cotton hedge, the modest noble man who sits with the bards by a herdsman's small fire is the heart-knowing companion of the victorious king, who will perish when the king perishes.","n":"The hero is first shown at ease, modest among bards in a poor village; his value is stated only at the end and as a pledge, not a deed. Lines 9 and 10 (what spreads over the pandal) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 324","lb":"PN324","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வெருக்கு","வெருகு",1,"maa","simile","wild cat"],["புள்ளூன்","ஊன்",2,"unavu","scene","bird meat"],["உடையின்","உடை",4,"maram","scene","thorn tree"],["ஊக","ஊகம்",5,"maram","scene","reed"],["பருத்தி","பருத்தி",7,"maram","scene","cotton"],["கருப்பை","கருப்பை",7,"maa","scene","rat"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",11,"seythi","scene","herdsman"],["பாணரொடு","பாணர்",12,"seythi","scene","bards"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:325":{"book":"purananuru","num":323,"t":["களிறுநீ றாடிய விடுநில மருங்கின்,","வம்பப் பெரும்பெயல் வரைந்துசொரிந்து இறந்தெனக்,","குழிகொள் சின்னீர் குராஅல் உண்டலின்,","செறுகிளைத் திட்ட கலுழ்கண் ஊறல்","முறையன் உண்ணும் நிறையா வாழ்க்கை,","முளவுமாத் தொலைச்சிய முழுச்சொல்-ஆடவர்","உடும்பிழுது அறுத்த ஒடுங்காழ்ப் படலைச்","சீறில் முன்றில் கூறுசெய் திடுமார்,","கொள்ளி வைத்த கொழுநிண நாற்றம்","மறுகுடன் கமழும் மதுகை மன்றத்து,","அலந்தலை இரத்தி அலங்குபடு நீழல்,","கயந்தலைச் சிறாஅர் கணைவிளை யாடும்","அருமிளை இருக்கை யதுவே-வென்வேல்","வேந்து தலைவரினும் தாங்கும்,","தாங்கா ஈகை, நெடுந்தகை ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"In a dry settlement where cows take turns drinking the muddy seep of a dug pit after a stray rain has passed, where the porcupine hunters' share of fat burning on the firebrand scents the street and boys play at arrows under the jujube's swaying shade, stands the thorn-fenced town of the lord of unbounded giving, who will hold back even a king who comes at the head of a victorious army.","n":"The pun in the last lines turns on one verb: தாங்கும், he holds back the king, and தாங்கா ஈகை, giving that cannot be held back. The rain is வம்ப, untimely, and leaves only a muddy pit, so the land is shown as poor in water before it is praised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 325","lb":"PN325","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"வல்லாண் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வல்லாண் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["குராஅல்","குரால்",3,"maa","scene","tawny cow"],["முளவுமாத்","முளவுமா",6,"maa","scene","porcupine"],["உடும்பிழுது","உடும்பு",7,"unavu","gift_food","iguana fat"],["கொழுநிண","நிணம்",9,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["இரத்தி","இரத்தி",11,"maram","scene","jujube"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:326":{"book":"purananuru","num":324,"t":["ஊர்முது வேலிப் பார்நடை வெருகின்","இருட்பகை வெரீஇய நாகுஇளம் பேடை","உயிர்நடுக் குற்றுப் புலாவிட் டரற்றச்,","சிறையும் செற்றையும் புடையுநள் எழுந்த","பருத்திப் பெண்டின் சிறுதீ விளக்கத்துக்,","கலிர்ப்பூ நெற்றிச் சேவலின் தணியும்","அருமிளை இருக்கை யதுவே -மனைவியும்,","வேட்டச் சிறா அர் சேட்புலம் படராது,","படமடைக் கொண்ட குறுந்தாள் உடும்பின்","விழுக்குநிணம் பெய்த தயிர்க்கண் மிதவை","யாணர் நல்லவை பாணரொடு, ஒராங்கு","வருவிருந்து அயரும் விருப்பினள்; கிழவனும்","அருஞ்சமம் ததையத் தாக்கிப், பெருஞ்சமத்து","அண்ணல் யானை அணிந்த","பொன்செய் ஓடைப் பெரும்பரி சிலனே."],"p":"","r":"In a thorn-fenced village where a young hen, terrified by the wild cat that prowls the old hedge, cries until the cotton-spinning woman rises with her small lamp and it calms beside the crested cock, the wife longs to feast arriving guests and bards on curd-rice with iguana fat, and her husband, who shattered the enemy in hard battle, owns the great gift of the gold forehead plate from the leading elephant.","n":"The frightened hen that quiets beside the cock may be read as a quiet figure of the village safe beside its warrior, though the poem draws no comparison. The wife's wish that the hunting boys not go far is a wish to have meat ready for guests, so even the hunt serves hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","maram","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 326","lb":"PN326","poet":"தங்கால் பொற்கொல்லன் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["வெருகின்","வெருகு",1,"maa","scene","wild cat"],["பேடை","பேடை",2,"pul","scene","hen"],["பருத்திப்","பருத்தி",5,"maram","scene","cotton"],["சேவலின்","சேவல்",6,"pul","scene","cock"],["பாணரொடு","பாணர்",11,"seythi","scene","bards"],["உடும்பின்","உடும்பு",9,"maa","gift_food","iguana"],["விழுக்குநிணம்","நிணம்",10,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["மிதவை","மிதவை",10,"unavu","gift_food","curd rice"],["யானை","யானை",14,"maa","scene",""],["ஓடைப்","ஓடை",15,"pira","ornament","forehead plate"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:327":{"book":"purananuru","num":325,"t":["எருது கால் உறாஅது, இளைஞர் கொன்ற","சில்விளை வரகின் புல்லென் குப்பை,","தொடுத்த கடவர்க்குக் கொடுத்த மிச்சில்","பசித்த பாணர் உண்டு, கடை தப்பலின்,","ஒக்கல் ஒற்கம் சொலியத் தன்னூர்ச்","சிறுபுல் லாளர் முகத்தவை கூறி,","வரகுடன் இரக்கும் நெடுந்தகை","அரசுவரின் தாங்கும் வல்லா ளன்னே."],"p":"","r":"After the creditors took their share of a meagre millet crop threshed by hand without oxen and the hungry bards ate the rest, the noble man, to end his people's want, goes to the small farmers of his own town and begs millet face to face; and this is the strong man who holds back any king who comes.","n":"The poem sets the begging and the valour in one sentence, so the man who stoops to ask small farmers for grain is the same man who will stand against a king; begging is not shame when it is for others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 327","lb":"PN327","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["எருது","எருது",1,"maa","absent","ox"],["வரகின்","வரகு",2,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",4,"seythi","scene","bards"],["வரகுடன்","வரகு",7,"unavu","gift_food","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:328":{"book":"purananuru","num":326,"t":[".. .. டைமுதல் புறவு சேர்ந்திருந்த","புன்புலச் சீறூர், நெல்விளை யாதே;","வரகும் தினையும் உள்ளவை யெல்லாம்","இரவன் மாக்களுக்கு ஈயத் தொலைந்தன;",".. .. .. .. .. .. டமைந் தனனே;","அன்னன் ஆயினும், பாண ! நன்றும்","வள்ளத் திடும்பால் உள்ளுறை தொட.. ..","களவுப் புளியன்ன விளை.. .. .. ..",".. .. .. வாடூன் கொழுங்குறை","கொய்குரல் அரிசியொடு நெய்பெய்து அட்டுத்,","துடுப்பொடு சிவணிய களிக்கொள் வெண்சோறு","உண்டு, இனி திருந்த பின். .. .. ..",".. .. .. தருகுவன் மாதோ-","தாளி முதல் நீடிய சிறுநறு முஞ்ஞை","முயல்வந்து கறிக்கும் முன்றில்,","சீறூர் மன்னனைப் பாடினை செலினே."],"p":"","r":"In a small village of poor land by the woods where no paddy grows and all its millet and thinai have gone in gifts to suppliants, the bard is told that the little king, though so reduced, will still cook dried meat and fresh rice in ghee for him and give him gifts if he goes singing to the yard where hares nibble the fragrant muññai.","n":"Lines 1, 5, 7 to 9, 12 and 13 are broken; the sense of the whole (an empty granary, yet a feast promised) survives, but the details of the meal and of line 5 do not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","inmai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 328","lb":"PN328","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நெல்விளை","நெல்",2,"unavu","absent","paddy"],["வரகும்","வரகு",3,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["தினையும்","தினை",3,"unavu","gift_food","thinai millet"],["புளியன்ன","புளி",8,"maram","simile","tamarind"],["வாடூன்","ஊன்",9,"unavu","gift_food","dried meat"],["அரிசியொடு","அரிசி",10,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",11,"unavu","gift_food","boiled rice"],["தாளி","தாளி",14,"maram","scene",""],["முஞ்ஞை","முஞ்ஞை",14,"maram","scene","premna"],["முயல்வந்து","முயல்",15,"maa","scene","hare"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:329":{"book":"purananuru","num":327,"t":["இல்லடு கள்ளின் சில்குடிச் சீறூர்ப்","புடைநடு கல்லின் நாட்பலி யூட்டி,","நன்னீர் ஆட்டி, நெய்ந்நறைக் கொளீஇய,","மங்குல் மாப்புகை மறுகுடன் கமழும்,","அருமுனை இருக்கைத்து ஆயினும், வரிமிடற்று","அரவுஉறை புற்றத்து அற்றே, நாளும்","புரவலர் புன்கண் நோக்காது, இரவலர்க்கு","அருகாது ஈயும் வண்மை,","உரைசால், நெடுந்தகை ஓம்பும் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"Though the small village of a few houses that brew their own toddy sits at a dangerous frontier, where the hero-stone at its edge gets daily offerings, is bathed and lit with ghee and its dark smoke spreads like mist down the street, and though it is as hard to approach as an anthill where a striped cobra lives, the famed noble man who guards it gives to suppliants every day without stint.","n":"The anthill simile faces two ways: to enemies the village is a snake's hole, to suppliants it is open every day. The phrase in line 7 about the patron's distress is not certain: it may mean that he gives without minding what giving costs him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nadukal","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 329","lb":"PN329","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",1,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["நெய்ந்நறைக்","நெய்",3,"unavu","scene","ghee"],["அரவுஉறை","அரவு",6,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[["herostone","நடு கல்லின்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:330":{"book":"purananuru","num":328,"t":["வேந்துடைத் தானை முனைகெட நெரிதர","ஏந்துவாள் வலத்தன் ஒருவன் ஆகித்,","தன்இறந்து வாராமை விலக்கலின், பெருங் கடற்கு","ஆழி அனையன் மாதோ; என்றும்","பாடிச் சென்றோர்க்கு அன்றியும், வாரிப்","புரவிற்கு ஆற்றாச் சீறூர்த்","தொன்மை சுட்டிய வண்மை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"When a king's army pressed in and broke the front, he stood alone with raised sword and stopped it from passing him, as the shore stops the great sea; and always, beyond what he gave those who came singing, his generosity keeps up the old name of a small village too poor to pay its levy.","n":"ஆழி here is the shore or boundary that the sea cannot pass, so the army becomes the sea; the image measures one man against a whole host.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 330","lb":"PN330","poet":"மதுரை கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கடற்கு","கடல்",3,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:331":{"book":"purananuru","num":329,"t":["கல்லறுத்து இயற்றிய வல்லுவர்க் கூவல்","வில்லேர் வாழ்க்கைச், சீறூர் மதவலி","நனிநல் கூர்ந்தனன் ஆயினும், பனிமிகப்,","புல்லென் மாலைச் சிறுதீ ஞெலியும்","கல்லா இடையன் போலக், குறிப்பின்","இல்லது படைக்கவும் வல்லன் ; உள்ளது","தவச்சிறிது ஆயினும் மிகப்பலர் என்னாள்,","நீள்நெடும் பந்தர் ஊண்முறை ஊட்டும்","இற்பொலி மகடூஉப் போலச், சிற்சில்","வரிசையின் அளக்கவும் வல்லன்; உரிதினின்","காவல் மன்னர் கடைமுகத்து உகுக்கும்","போகுபலி வெண்சோறு போலத்","தூவவும் வல்லன், அவன் தூவுங் காலே."],"p":"","r":"The strength of a small village that lives by the bow, with a well cut through rock, is a chief who, though very poor, can make what is not there, like an unlearned herdsman kindling a small fire on a cold evening; can measure out in order to a few, like a good housewife who serves a long pandal of guests from very little without saying they are too many; and, when he scatters gifts, can scatter them like the white rice of offering cast at the gates of guarding kings.","n":"The three similes are a scale of giving: making something from nothing, sharing scarcity fairly, and pouring out freely. All three vehicles are humble or domestic, a herdsman and a housewife set beside the king's offering rice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 331","lb":"PN331","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகூத்தனார் (உறையூர் முது கூற்றனார் எனவும் பாடம்)","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கூவல்","கூவல்",1,"pira","scene","well"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",5,"seythi","simile","herdsman"],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",12,"unavu","simile","offering rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:332":{"book":"purananuru","num":330,"t":["பிறர்வேல் போலா தாகி, இவ்வூர்","மறவன் வேலோ பெருந்தகை உடைத்தே;","இரும்புறம் நீறும் ஆடிக், கலந்துஇடைக்","குரம்பைக் கூரைக் கிடக்கினும் கிடக்கும்;","மங்கல மகளிரொடு மாலை சூட்டி,","இன்குரல் இரும்பை யாழொடு ததும்பத்,","தெண்ணீர்ப் படுவினும் தெருவினும் திரிந்து,","மண்முழுது அழுங்கச் செல்லினும் செல்லும்; ஆங்கு,","இருங்கடல் தானை வேந்தர்","பெருங்களிற்று முகத்தினும் செலவு ஆனாதே."],"p":"","r":"Unlike other men's spears, this warrior's spear has its own greatness: sometimes it lies in the thatch of the hut with dust on its dark back; sometimes, garlanded among auspicious women, it is carried round the pond and the streets to the sound of drums and lutes till the land trembles; and it never stops short of the faces of the great elephants of kings whose armies are like the sea.","n":"The spear is given three states, idle, honoured and in battle, and the repeated கிடக்கினும் கிடக்கும் and செல்லினும் செல்லும் make its indifference to the first two a mark of greatness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 332","lb":"PN332","poet":"விரியூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மாலை","மாலை",5,"maram","ornament","garland"],["யாழொடு","யாழ்",6,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["இருங்கடல்","கடல்",9,"pira","scene","sea"],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",10,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:333":{"book":"purananuru","num":331,"t":["நீருள் பட்ட மாரிப் பேருறை","மொக்குள் அன்ன பொகுட்டுவிழிக் கண்ண,","கரும்பிடர்த் தலைய, பெருஞ்செவிக் குறுமுயல்","உள்ளூர்க் குறும்புதல் துள்ளுவன உகளும்","தொள்ளை மன்றத்து ஆங்கண் படரின்,","‘உண்க’என உணரா உயவிற்று ஆயினும்,","தங்கனீர் சென்மோ, புலவீர்! நன்றும்;","சென்றதற் கொண்டு, மனையோள் விரும்பி,","வரகும் தினையும் உள்ளவை எல்லாம்","இரவல் மாக்கள் உணக்கொளத் தீர்ந்தெனக்,","குறித்துமாறு எதிர்ப்பை பெறாஅ மையின்,","குரல்உணங்கு விதைத்தினை உரல்வாய்ப் பெய்து,","சிறிது புறப்பட்டன்றோ விலளே; தன்னூர்","வேட்டக் குடிதொறுங் கூட்டு .. .. ..",".. .. .. .. உடும்பு செய்","பாணி நெடுந்தேர் வல்லரோடு ஊரா,","வம்பணி யானை வேந்துதலை வரினும்,","உண்பது மன்னும் அதுவே;","பரிசில் மன்னும், குருசில்கொண் டதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Poets are urged to stay at a poor village where hares with eyes like raindrop bubbles leap in the bushes of the holed common, even though no one there knows how to say 'eat', for the housewife, her millet and thinai all given to suppliants and no loan coming back, has put the dried seed-millet into the mortar; and her lord, who would face a king with a new-decked elephant, eats only that and gives as gifts what he takes in battle.","n":"Cooking the seed grain is the extreme of hospitality: it spends next year's crop on today's guest. Lines 14 and 15 are broken, and the closing lines (what the lord eats, what he takes as his gift) can be read more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","inmai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 333","lb":"PN333","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பேருறை","உறை",1,"pira","simile","raindrop"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",3,"maa","scene","hare"],["வரகும்","வரகு",9,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["தினையும்","தினை",9,"unavu","gift_food","thinai millet"],["விதைத்தினை","தினை",12,"unavu","gift_food","seed millet"],["யானை","யானை",17,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:334":{"book":"purananuru","num":332,"t":["காகரு பழனக் கண்பின் அன்ன","தூமயிர்க் குறுந்தாள் நெடுஞ்செவிக் குறுமுயல்,","புன்றலைச் சிறாஅர் மன்றத்து ஆர்ப்பின்,","படப்புஒடுங் கும்மே.. .. .. .. பின்பு .. .. ..",".. .. .. .. .. .. னூரே மனையோள்","பாணர் ஆர்த்தவும், பரிசிலர் ஓம்பவும்,’","ஊணொலி அரவமொடு கைதூ வாளே;","உயர்மருப்பு யானைப் புகர்முகத்து அணிந்த","பொலம் .. .. .. .. .. .. .. ப்","பரிசில் பரிசிலர்க்கு ஈய,","உரவேற் காளையும் கைதூ வானே."],"p":"","r":"Where hares with fur like the sedge flower hide in the thickets when boys shout on the common, the housewife never rests her hands from the clatter of feeding bards and keeping suppliants, and the young bull-like spearman never rests his either, giving away the gold ornaments taken from the faces of high-tusked elephants.","n":"The poem rhymes the wife and the husband: கைதூ வாளே, she does not rest her hands, and கைதூ வானே, he does not rest his, which makes hospitality and battle-won giving one shared duty of the house. Lines 4, 5 and 9 are broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","eegai","parisil","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 334","lb":"PN334","poet":"மதுரைத் தமிழக் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கண்பின்","கண்பு",1,"maram","simile","sedge"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",2,"maa","scene","hare"],["யானைப்","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:335":{"book":"purananuru","num":333,"t":["அடலருந் துப்பின் .. .. .. ..",".. .. .. .. குருந்தே முல்லை யென்று","இந்நான் கல்லது பூவும் இல்லை;","கருங்கால் வரகே, இருங்கதிர்த் தினையே,","சிறுகொடிக் கொள்ளே, பொறிகிளர் அவரையொடு","இந்நான் கல்லது உணாவும் இல்லை;","துடியன், பாணன், பறையன், கடம்பன், என்று","இந்நான் கல்லது குடியும் இல்லை;","ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர் முன்னின்று விலங்கி,","ஒளிறுஏந்து மருப்பின் களிறுஎறிந்து வீழ்ந்தெனக்,","கல்லே பரவின் அல்லது,","நெல்உகுத்துப் பரவும் கடவுளும் இலவே."],"p":"","r":"In this land there are only four flowers (kuravam, ..., kuruntham, mullai), only four foods (millet, thinai, horsegram and beans), only four clans (the thudi drummer, the bard, the parai drummer and the kadamban), and no god worshipped with scattered paddy except the stone praised for the warrior who stood against the enemy, struck down a bright-tusked elephant and fell.","n":"The fourfold lists read like a census of a whole way of life, and the last item overturns the pattern: not four gods but one, and that one a man. The first line and a half are broken, so one flower is lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nadukal","vazhipaadu","maram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 335","lb":"PN335","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மூதின் முல்லை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மூதின் முல்லை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குருந்தே","குருந்து",2,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["வரகே","வரகு",4,"unavu","scene","millet"],["தினையே","தினை",4,"unavu","scene","thinai millet"],["கொள்ளே","கொள்",5,"unavu","scene","horsegram"],["அவரையொடு","அவரை",5,"unavu","scene","beans"],["துடியன்","துடியன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["பாணன்","பாணன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["பறையன்","பறையன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["கடம்பன்","கடம்பன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["களிறுஎறிந்து","களிறு",10,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நெல்உகுத்துப்","நெல்",12,"unavu","absent","paddy"],["கடவுளும்","கடவுள்",12,"deyvam","absent","god"]],"en":[["herostone","கல்லே",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:336":{"book":"purananuru","num":334,"t":["வேட்ட வேந்தனும் வெஞ்சினத் தினனே;","கடவன கழிப்புஇவள் தந்தையும் செய்யான்;","ஒளிறுமுகத்து ஏந்திய வீங்குதொடி மருப்பின்","களிறும் கடிமரம் சேரா; சேர்ந்த","ஒளிறுவேல் மறவரும் வாய்மூழ்த் தனரே;","இயவரும் அறியாப் பல்லியம் கறங்க,","அன்னோ, பெரும்பே துற்றன்று, இவ் வருங்கடி மூதூர்;","அறன்இலன் மன்ற தானே-விறன்மலை","வேங்கை வெற்பின் விரிந்த கோங்கின்","முகைவனப்பு ஏந்திய முற்றா இளமுலைத்","தகைவளர்த்து எடுத்த நகையொடு,","பகைவளர்த்து இருந்த இப் பண்புஇல் தாயே."],"p":"","r":"The king who wants the girl is hot with anger and her father will not do what is due; the elephants have not yet been tied to the guarded tree, the spearmen who reached it are silent, and with drums sounding that even the musicians do not know, this ancient guarded town is in great turmoil; truly without virtue is the mother who, raising the girl's young breasts with the beauty of kongu buds on the vengai hill, raised enmity along with her.","n":"The blame falls on the mother, not on the king or the father: her care in rearing the girl's beauty is said to have reared a war. Lines 4 and 5 (the guarded tree, the warriors' வாய்மூழ்த்தனரே) are hard, and the state of the siege is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","pagai","azhagu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 336","lb":"PN336","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["களிறும்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கடிமரம்","கடிமரம்",4,"maram","scene","guarded tree"],["இயவரும்","இயவர்",6,"seythi","scene","musicians"],["பல்லியம்","இயம்",6,"yaazh","scene","instruments"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",9,"maram","scene",""],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:337":{"book":"purananuru","num":335,"t":["ஆர்கலி யினனே, சோணாட்டு அண்ணல்;","கவிகை மண்ணாள் செல்வ ராயினும்,","வாள்வலத்து ஒழியப் பாடிச் சென்றாஅர்.","வரலதோறு அகம் மலர . .. .. .. ..","ஈதல் ஆனா இலங்குதொடித் தடக்கைப்","பாரி பறம்பின் பனிச்சுனை போலக்,","காண்டற்கு அரியளாகி, மாண்ட","பெண்மை நிறைந்த பொலிவொடு, மண்ணிய","துகில்விரி கடுப்ப நுடங்கித், தண்ணென","அகிலார் நறும்புகை ஐதுசென்று அடங்கிய","கபில நெடுநகர்க் கமழும் நாற்றமொடு,","மனைச்செறிந் தனளே, வாணுதல்; இனியே.","அற்றன் றாகலின், தெற்றெனப் போற்றிக்,","காய்நெல் கவளம் தீற்றிக், காவுதொறும்","கடுங்கண் யானை காப்பனர் அன்றி,","வருத லானார் வேந்தர்; தன்னையர்","பொருசமம் கடந்த உருகெழு நெடுவேல்","குருதி பற்றிய வெருவரு தலையர்","மற்றுஇவர் மறனும் இற்றால்; தெற்றென","யாரா குவர்கொல் தாமே - நேரிழை","உருத்த பல்சுணங்கு அணிந்த","மருப்புஇள வனமுலை ஞெமுக்கு வோரே?"],"p":"","r":"Though the Chola lord is loud with power and suitors who rule the earth have come singing, she, like the cold spring on Pari's Parambu, has become hard to see, kept indoors in the tall fragrant house; kings keep coming, feeding paddy to their fierce elephants in every grove, while her brothers' heads are clotted with blood from their long spears, and the poet asks who will ever be the ones to press her young breasts.","n":"Kapilar, Pari's own poet, uses his dead patron's hill spring as the measure of something precious and out of reach. The closing question is not a wish but a threat: given her brothers' spears, it is not clear anyone will live to marry her. Lines 3, 4 and 13 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","pagai","azhagu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 337","lb":"PN337","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பனிச்சுனை","சுனை",6,"pira","simile","spring"],["அகிலார்","அகில்",10,"maram","scene","eaglewood"],["காய்நெல்","நெல்",14,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["காவுதொறும்","கா",14,"maram","scene","grove"],["யானை","யானை",15,"maa","scene",""],["மருப்புஇள","மருப்பு",22,"maa","simile","tusk"]],"en":[["king","சோணாட்டு அண்ணல்",1],["patron","பாரி",6],["hill","பறம்பின்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:338":{"book":"purananuru","num":336,"t":["ஏர் பரந்த வயல், நீர் பரந்த செறுவின்,","நெல் மலிந்த மனைப், பொன் மலிந்த மறுகின்,","படுவண்டு ஆர்க்கும் பன்மலர்க் காவின்,","நெடுவேள் ஆதன் போந்தை அன்ன,","பெருஞ்சீர் அருங்கொண் டியளே ; கருஞ்சினை","வேம்பும் ஆரும் போந்தையும் மூன்றும்","மலைந்த சென்னியர், அணிந்த வில்லர்,","கொற்ற வேந்தர் தரினும், தன்தக","வணங்கார்க்கு ஈகுவன் அல்லன்- வண் தோட்டுப்","பிணங்கு கதிர்க் கழனி நாப்பண், ஏமுற்று","உணங்குகலன் ஆழியின் தோன்றும்","ஓர்எயில் மன்னன் ஒருமட மகளே!"],"p":"","r":"Like Neduvel Aathan's Ponthai, rich in ploughed fields, paddy-filled houses, gold-filled streets and bee-loud groves, she is of great worth and hard to win: even if the victorious kings who wear neem, aar and palmyra bring gifts, the king of a single fort, which stands among the paddy fields like a stranded ship in the sea, will not give his one young daughter to any who do not bow as befits him.","n":"The three garlands are the emblems of the Pandya, Chola and Chera, so the refusal is made to all the crowned kings at once by the lord of one fort. போந்தை is both the town of line 4 and the palmyra of line 6.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","maanam","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 338","lb":"PN338","poet":"குன்றூர் கிழார் மகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["படுவண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["பன்மலர்க்","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","flowers"],["வேம்பும்","வேம்பு",6,"maram","ornament","neem"],["ஆரும்","ஆர்",6,"maram","ornament","aar tree"],["போந்தையும்","போந்தை",6,"maram","ornament","palmyra"]],"en":[["king","நெடுவேள் ஆதன்",4],["town","போந்தை",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:339":{"book":"purananuru","num":337,"t":["வியன்புலம் படர்ந்த பல்ஆ நெடுஏறு","மடலை மாண்நிழல் அசைவிடக், கோவலர்","வீததை முல்லைப் பூப்பறிக் குந்து;","குறுங்கோல் எறிந்த நெடுஞ்செவிக் குறுமுயல்","நெடுநீர்ப் பரப்பின் வாளையொடு உகளுந்து;","தொடலை அல்குல் தொடித்தோள் மகளிர்","கடல் ஆடிக் கயம் பாய்ந்து,","கழி நெய்தற் பூக் குறூஉந்து;","பைந்தழை துயல்வருஞ் செறுவிறற்",".. .. .. .. .. . . ..லத்தி","வளர வேண்டும், அவளே, என்றும்-","ஆரமர் உழப்பதும் அமரிய ளாகி,","முறஞ்செவி யானை வேந்தர்","மறங்கெழு நெஞ்சங் கொண்டொளித் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"Where a long-horned bull rests in the shade, cowherds pluck crowded mullai flowers, a hare struck by a stick leaps among vaalai fish in the flood, and bangled women bathe in the sea, dive in ponds and pick neythal flowers from the backwater, she has grown, and the poet says she has taken and hidden in herself the fierce hearts of kings with winnow-eared elephants who are eager for hard battle.","n":"The opening gathers three landscapes, pasture, wet field and shore, into one country, so the girl is the flower of a land that has everything; the comic hare among the fish marks the floods. Line 10 is lost, and the sense of line 12 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","azhagu","pagai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 339","lb":"PN339","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["பல்ஆ","ஆ",1,"maa","scene","cows"],["நெடுஏறு","ஏறு",1,"maa","scene","bull"],["மடலை","மடலை",2,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",2,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",4,"maa","scene","hare"],["வாளையொடு","வாளை",5,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["கடல்","கடல்",7,"pira","scene","sea"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",8,"maram","scene","water lily"],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:340":{"book":"purananuru","num":338,"t":["அணித்தழை நுடங்க ஓடி, மணிப்பொறிக்","குரலம் குன்றி கொள்ளும் இளையோள்,","மாமகள் .. .. .. .. .. ..",".. .. .. ..லென வினவுதி, கேள், நீ","எடுப்பவெ .. .. .. .. .. .. ..",".. .. .. .. .. மைந்தர் தந்தை","இரும்பனை அன்ன பெருங்கை யானை","கரந்தையஞ் செறுவின் பெயர்க்கும்","பெருந்தகை மன்னர்க்கு வரைந்திருந் தனனே."],"p":"","r":"To one who asks about the young girl who runs, her leaf skirt swaying, to gather spotted kunri seeds, the speaker answers (through broken lines) that her father has set her apart for great kings whose elephants with trunks like dark palmyras trample the karanthai fields.","n":"The child still playing with seeds is already the subject of kings' wars; the gaps in lines 3 to 6 hide who asks and whose father is meant, and வரைந்திருந்தனனே may mean he has promised her or has limited her to such kings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 340","lb":"PN340","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["அணித்தழை","தழை",1,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["குன்றி","குன்றி",2,"maram","scene","crab's-eye seed"],["இரும்பனை","பனை",7,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""],["கரந்தையஞ்","கரந்தை",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:17"},"pn:341":{"book":"purananuru","num":339,"t":["வேந்துகுறை யுறவுங் கொடாஅன், ஏந்துகோட்டு","அம்பூந் தொடலை அணித்தழை அல்குல்,","செம்பொறிச் சிலம்பின் இளையோள் தந்தை,","எழுவிட்டு அமைத்த திண்நிலைக் கதவின்","அனரமண் இஞ்சி நாட்கொடி நுடங்கும்",".. .. .. . .. .. ... .. .. .. ..","புலிக்கணத் தன்ன கடுங்கண் சுற்றமொடு,","மாற்றம் மாறான், மறலிய சினத்தன்,","‘பூக்கோள்’ என ஏஎய்க், கயம்புக் கனனே;","விளங்குஇழைப் பொலிந்த வேளா மெல்லியல்,","சுணங்கணி வனமுலை, அவளொடு நாளை","மணம்புகு வைகல் ஆகுதல் ஒன்றோ-","ஆரமர் உழக்கிய மறம்கிளர் முன்பின்,","நீள்இலை எஃகம் மறுத்த உடம்பொடு","வாரா உலகம் புகுதல் ஒன்று- எனப்","படைதொட் டனனே, குருசில்; ஆயிடைக்","களிறுபொரக் கலங்கிய தண்கயம் போலப்,","பெருங்கவின் இழப்பது கொல்லோ,","மென்புனல் வைப்பின்இத் தண்பணை ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"A chieftain refuses his young daughter even to a king who pleads, calls his fierce kin to the flower-taking of war and wades into the pond, torn between wedding her tomorrow and entering the world of no return with a spear-scarred body; the poet fears the cool field-town will lose its beauty like a pool churned by fighting elephants.","n":"The hero (குருசில்) is given the choice as a pair of 'one or the other' (ஒன்றோ ... ஒன்று): the wedding day and the warrior's death are set as equal outcomes, and the poet's own worry is for the town, not for either man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","maram","padai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 341","lb":"PN341","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["தொடலை","தொடலை",2,"maram","ornament","flower string"],["அணித்தழை","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["புலிக்கணத்","புலி",7,"maa","simile","tiger"],["கயம்புக்","கயம்",9,"pira","scene",""],["களிறுபொரக்","களிறு",17,"maa","simile",""],["தண்கயம்","கயம்",17,"pira","simile",""],["மென்புனல்","புனல்",19,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:342":{"book":"purananuru","num":340,"t":["‘கானக் காக்கைக் கலிச்சிறகு ஏய்க்கும்","மயிலைக் கண்ணிப், பெருந்தோட் குறுமகள்,","ஏனோர் மகள்கொல் இவள்?’ என விதுப்புற்று,","என்னொடு வினவும் வென்வேல் நெடுந்தகை;","திருநயத் தக்க பண்பின் இவள் நலனே","பொருநர்க்கு அல்லது, பிறர்க்கு ஆகாதே;","பைங்கால் கொக்கின் பகுவாய்ப் பிள்ளை","மென்சேற்று அடைகரை மேய்ந்துஉண் டதற்பின்,","ஆரல் ஈன்ற ஐயவி முட்டை,","கூர்நல் இறவின் பிள்ளையொடு பெறூஉம்,","தன்பணைக் கிழவன்இவள் தந்தையும்; வேந்தரும்","பெறாஅ மையின் பேரமர் செய்தலின்,","கழிபிணம் பிறங்கு போர்பு அழிகளிறு எருதா,","வாள்தக வைகலும் உழக்கும்","மாட்சி யவர் இவள் தன்னை மாரே."],"p":"","r":"Asked by an eager spear-bearing lord whose daughter the broad-shouldered girl with the dark flower garland is, the speaker answers that her beauty is for warriors alone: her father owns rich fields where heron chicks feed on fish eggs and prawn, and because kings could not win her, her brothers thresh the battlefield daily with swords, using heaped corpses as sheaves and broken elephants as oxen.","n":"The heron chick that eats its fill and still finds eggs and prawn is a picture of the father's plenty; it is set against the brothers' harvest of corpses, so the poem's two 'fields' are the paddy land and the battlefield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","maram","kudi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 342","lb":"PN342","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["காக்கைக்","காக்கை",1,"pul","simile",""],["மயிலைக்","மயிலை",2,"maram","ornament","mayilai flower"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",7,"pul","scene",""],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",9,"pira","scene","eel fish"],["இறவின்","இறா",10,"pira","scene","prawn"],["அழிகளிறு","களிறு",13,"maa","simile",""],["எருதா","எருது",13,"maa","simile","ox"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:343":{"book":"purananuru","num":341,"t":["‘மீன் நொடுத்து நெல் குவைஇ","மிசை யம்பியின் மனைமறுக் குந்து!","மனைக் கவைஇய கறிமூ டையால்.","கலிச் சும்மைய கரைகலக் குறுந்து","கலந் தந்த பொற் பரிசம்","கழித் தொணியான் கரைசேர்க் குந்து;","மலைத் தாரமும் கடல் தாரமும்","தலைப் பெய்து, வருநர்க்கு ஈயும்","புனலங் கள்ளின் பொலந்தார்க் குட்டுவன்","முழங்கு கடல் முழவின் முசிறி யன்ன,","நலஞ்சால் விழுப்பொருள் பணிந்து கொடுப்பினும்,","புரையர் அல்லோர் வரையலள், இவள்’ எனத்","தந்தையும் கொடாஅன் ஆயின் - வந்தோர்,","வாய்ப்ப இறுத்த ஏணி ஆயிடை","வருந்தின்று கொல்லோ தானே-பருந்துஉயிர்த்து","இடைமதில் சேக்கும் புரிசைப்","படைமயங்கு ஆரிடை நெடுநல் ஊரே?"],"p":"","r":"Musiri, where fish is bartered for paddy, pepper sacks crowd the houses and the gold brought by ships comes ashore by canoe, belongs to Kuttuvan who gives the goods of hill and sea to comers; even if wealth like Musiri is humbly offered, the father says she will not be married to those not his equal, and the poet asks whether the walled town, with the attackers' ladders against it and kites resting on the inner wall, will not suffer.","n":"The bride-price is measured by a real port's trade, gold coming in and pepper going out; the father's refusal is framed as rank (புரையர்), not price, so no wealth can settle it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam","kudi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 343","lb":"PN343","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["மீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene",""],["நெல்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கறிமூ","கறி",3,"maram","scene","pepper"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",9,"unavu","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",10,"parai","simile",""],["பருந்துஉயிர்த்து","பருந்து",15,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",9],["town","முசிறி",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:344":{"book":"purananuru","num":342,"t":["செந்நெல் உண்ட பைந்தோட்டு மஞ்ஞை,","செறிவளை மகளிர், பறந்தெழுந்து,","துறைநணி மருதத்து இறுக்கும் ஊரொடு,","நிறைசால் விழுப்பொருள் தருதல் ஒன்றோ;","புகைபடு கூர்எரி பரப்பிப் பகைசெய்து,","பண்பில் ஆண்மை தருதல் ஒன்றோ;","இரண்டினுள் ஒன்றா காமையோ அரிதே,","காஞ்சிப் பனிமுறி ஆரங் கண்ணி. . .-","கணிமே வந்தவள் அல்குல்அவ் வரியே."],"p":"","r":"The suitor will do one of two things, give a town of fields, where peacocks that ate red paddy fly up with the bangled women and settle on the marutham tree by the ford, together with great wealth, or spread smoky fire and enmity with graceless manliness; one of them is sure to come, because of the girl with the kaanchi-shoot garland and the lines on her waist.","n":"The peacocks and the women rise together and settle in the marutham tree; whether the poet means them as one picture of the town's ease or likens the women to the birds is not fixed by the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam","pagai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 344","lb":"PN344","poet":"அடைநெடுங் கல்வியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","scene",""],["மருதத்து","மருதம்",3,"maram","scene",""],["காஞ்சிப்","காஞ்சி",8,"maram","ornament","kaanchi tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:345":{"book":"purananuru","num":343,"t":["களிறு அணைப்பக் கலங்கின, காஅ;","தேர்ஓடத் துகள் கெழுமின, தெருவு;","மா மறுகலின் மயக்குற்றன, வழி;","கலங் கழாஅலின், துறை கலக்குற்றன;","தெறல் மறவர் இறை கூர்தலின்,","பொறை மலிந்து நிலன் நெளிய,","வந்தோர் பலரே, வம்ப வேந்தர்,","பிடிஉயிர்ப் பன்ன கைகவர் இரும்பின்","ஓவுறழ் இரும்புறம் காவல் கண்ணிக்,","கருங்கண் கொண்ட நெருங்கல் வெம்முலை,","மையல் நோக்கின், தையலை நயந்தோர்","அளியர் தாமே; இவள் தன்னை மாரே","செல்வம் வேண்டார், செருப்புகல் வேண்டி,","‘நிரல்அல் லோர்க்குத் தரலோ இல்’ எனக்;","கழிப்பிணிப் பலகையர், கதுவாய் வாளர்,","குழாஅங் கொண்ட குருதிஅம் புலவொடு","கழாஅத் தலையர் கருங்கடை நெடுவேல்","இன்ன மறவர்த் தாயினும், அன்னோ !","என்னா வதுகொல் தானே-","பன்னல் வேலிஇப் பணைநல் லூரே!"],"p":"","r":"The groves are shaken by tethered elephants, the streets thick with chariot dust, the roads jammed with horses, the ford muddied by the washing of weapons, the ground bowed by the weight of fierce warriors: many new kings have come for the girl, and her lovers are to be pitied, for her brothers want war, not wealth, saying there is no giving to those out of line, and with shields, notched swords and blood-matted heads they are such men that the poet cries, what will become of this good town fenced with cotton?","n":"The siege is told entirely through what it does to the town (grove, street, road, ford, ground) before the girl is named; lines 8 and 9 (the iron like a she-elephant's breath, the back like a painting) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","padai","maram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 345","lb":"PN345","poet":"அடைநெடுங் கல்வியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["காஅ","கா",1,"maram","scene","grove"],["மா","மா",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["பிடிஉயிர்ப்","பிடி",8,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["பன்னல்","பன்னல்",20,"maram","scene","cotton"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:346":{"book":"purananuru","num":344,"t":["பிற .. .. .. ள பால் என மடுத்தலின்,","ஈன்ற தாயோ வேண்டாள் அல்லள்;","கல்வியென் என்னும், வல்லாண் சிறாஅன்;","ஒள்வேல் நல்லன், அதுவாய் ஆகுதல்-","அழிந்தோர் அழிய, ஒழிந்தோர் ஒக்கல்","பேணுநர்ப் பெறாஅது விளியும்","புன்தலைப் பெரும்பாழ் செயும் இவள் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"The mother does not refuse her and the young man, strong and good with the bright spear, insists; if that comes true, the fallen will fall, the kin who remain will die with none to care for them, and the girl's beauty will make a great wasteland.","n":"The last line makes the girl's beauty (நலன்) the grammatical agent of the ruin, the same move as 349's anangu.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu","kedu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 346","lb":"PN346","poet":"அண்டர் மகன் குறுவழுதி","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பால்","பால்",1,"unavu","scene","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:347":{"book":"purananuru","num":345,"t":["உண்போன் தான்நறுங் கள்ளின் இடச்சில","நாஇடைப் பஃறேர் கோலச் சிவந்த","ஒளிறுஒள் வாடக் குழைந்தபைந் தும்பை,","எறிந்துஇலை முறிந்த கதுவாய் வேலின்.","மணநாறு மார்பின், மறப்போர் அகுதை","குண்டுநீர் வரைப்பின், கூடல் அன்ன","குவைஇருங் கூந்தல் வருமுலை செப்ப,",". . . . . . . . . . . . . .","என்னா வதுகொல் தானே? . .. . .","விளங்குறு பராரைய வாயினும், வேந்தர்","வினைநவில் யானை பிணிப்ப,","வேர்துளங் கினநம் ஊருள் மரனே."],"p":"","r":"Akuthai of fierce war, who drinks fragrant toddy, wears battle-withered thumbai and carries a spear notched in fight, holds a Koodal ringed by deep water, and the girl with heavy hair is likened to it; though the trees of our town are thick-trunked, they have shaken at the root since the kings tethered their trained elephants to them.","n":"Line 8 is lost, so what the simile of Akuthai's Koodal is joined to cannot be read with certainty; the closing image of trees shaken at the root by tethered elephants recurs in 348.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","padai","azhagu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 347","lb":"PN347","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["தும்பை","தும்பை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["மரனே","மரம்",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","அகுதை",5],["town","கூடல்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:348":{"book":"purananuru","num":346,"t":["வெண்ணெல் அரிஞர் தண்ணுமை வெரீஇக்,","கண்மடற் கொண்ட தீந்தேன் இரியக்,","கள்ளரிக்கும் குயம், சிறுசின்","மீன் சீவும் பாண் சேரி,","வாய்மொழித் தழும்பன் ஊணூர் அன்ன,","குவளை உண்கண் இவளைத், தாயே","ஈனா ளாயினள் ஆயின், ஆனாது","நிழல்தொறும் நெடுந்தேர் நிற்ப, வயின்தொறும்,","செந்நுதல் யானை பிணிப்ப,","வருந்தல மன் - எம் பெருந்துறை மரனே."],"p":"","r":"The girl with kuvalai eyes is like Unur of truthful Thazhumban, where the drum of the rice reapers scares bees from the honey, toddy is strained in pots and the panar quarter scales fish; had her mother not borne her, the trees of our great ford would not suffer from long chariots standing in every shade and red-browed elephants tied at every place.","n":"The counterfactual (had her mother not borne her) blames no one; the harm is told only through the trees, which is how the speaker can grieve without accusing the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu","kedu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 348","lb":"PN348","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",1,"parai","scene",""],["தீந்தேன்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honey"],["கள்ளரிக்கும்","கள்",3,"unavu","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",4,"pira","scene",""],["பாண்","பாணர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",6,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["மரனே","மரம்",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","தழும்பன்",5],["town","ஊணூர்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:349":{"book":"purananuru","num":347,"t":["நுதிவேல் கொண்டு நுதல்வியர் தொடையாக்,","கடிய கூறும், வேந்தே; தந்தையும்,","நெடிய அல்லது, பணிந்துமொழி யலனே;","இஃதுஇவர் படிவம்; ஆயின், வைஎயிற்று,","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண், அம்மா அரிவை,","மரம்படு சிறுதீப் போல,","அணங்கா யினள், தான் பிறந்த ஊர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The king wipes the sweat from his brow with his spear-point and speaks harsh words, and the father too speaks only proud words and never bows; that is their vow, so the girl with fine teeth and cool streaked eyes has become, like a small fire born in wood, a danger to the town she was born in.","n":"The fire that is born in wood and burns the wood names exactly the girl's relation to her town; the poet calls her அணங்கு, a harmful power, not because of anything she does but because of two men's vows (படிவம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","anangu","kedu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 349","lb":"PN349","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மரம்படு","மரம்",6,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:350":{"book":"purananuru","num":348,"t":["தூர்ந்த கிடங்கின், சோர்ந்த ஞாயில்,","சிதைந்த இஞ்சிக், கதுவாய் மூதூர்","யாங்கா வதுகொல் தானே, தாங்காது?","படுமழை உருமின் இறங்கு முரசின்","கடுமான் வேந்தர் காலை வந்து, எம்","நெடுநிலை வாயில் கொட்குவர் மாதோ;","பொருதாது அமருவர் அல்லர்; போர் உழந்து","அடுமுரண் முன்பின் தன்னையர் ஏந்திய","வடிவேல் எஃகின் சிவந்த உண்கண்,","தொடியுறழ் முன்கை, இளையோள்","அணிநல் லாகத்து அரும்பிய சுணங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The old town with its moat filled in, its battlements sagging and its wall broken cannot hold out; kings with drums like falling thunder and fast horses come at dawn and circle our tall gate and will not leave without a fight, all for the freckles budding on the breast of the young girl whose eyes are red like her brothers' spears.","n":"The poem ends on the smallest thing in it, the freckles on a young girl's breast, as the cause of a siege; her eyes are said to be red like her brothers' spears, which ties her beauty to the men who will fight for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","varaivu","padai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 350","lb":"PN350","poet":"மதுரை ஓலைக்கடைக் கண்ணம் புகுந்தார் ஆயத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["உருமின்","உரும்",4,"pira","scene","thunder"],["முரசின்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""],["கடுமான்","மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:351":{"book":"purananuru","num":349,"t":["படுமணி மருங்கின் பணைத் தாள் யானையும்,","கொடிநுடங்கு மிசைய தேரும், மாவும்,","படைஅமை மறவரொடு, துவன்றிக் கல்லெனக்,","கடல்கண் டன்ன கண்அகன் தானை","வென்றுஎறி முரசின் வேந்தர், என்றும்,","வண்கை எயினன் வாகை அன்ன","இவள்நலம் தாராது அமைகுவர் அல்லர்;","என்ஆ வதுகொல் தானே- தெண்ணீர்ப்","பொய்கை மேய்ந்த செவ்வரி நாரை","தேம்கொள் மருதின் பூஞ்சினை முனையின்,","காமரு காஞ்சித் துஞ்சும்","ஏமம்சால் சிறப்பின், இப் பணைநல் லூரே?"],"p":"","r":"Kings whose army is as wide as the sea, with belled elephants, flag-topped chariots, horses and warriors, will never rest without taking the girl whose beauty is like the vaakai of generous Eyinan; what will become of this secure field-town where the red-striped heron, tired of the honeyed marutham's branch, sleeps in the lovely kaanchi?","n":"Whether வாகை here is Eyinan's victory or a place called Vaakai is not settled by the line. The heron that leaves the marutham for the kaanchi tree to sleep is a picture of the town's safety (ஏமம்), set against the army that threatens it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","padai","azhagu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 351","lb":"PN351","poet":"மதுரைப் படைமங்க மன்னியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["யானையும்","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["மாவும்","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["முரசின்","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""],["வாகை","வாகை",6,"maram","simile","vaakai, victory"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",9,"pira","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",9,"pul","scene",""],["மருதின்","மருது",10,"maram","scene",""],["காஞ்சித்","காஞ்சி",11,"maram","scene","kaanchi tree"]],"en":[["patron","எயினன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:352":{"book":"purananuru","num":350,"t":["தேஎங் கொண்ட வெண்மண் டையான்,","வீ . . . . . கறக்குந்து;","அவல் வகுத்த பசுங் குடையான்,","புதன் முல்லைப் பூப்பறிக் குந்து;","ஆம்பல் வள்ளித் தொடிக்கை மகளிர்","குன்றுஏறிப் புனல் பாயின்","புறவாயால் புனல்வரை யுந்து;",". . . . . நொடை நறவின்","மாவண் தித்தன் வெண்ணெல் வேலி","உறந்தை அன்ன உரைசால் நன்கலம்","கொடுப்பவும் கொளாஅ னெ. . . .",". . .ர்தந்த நாகிள வேங்கையின்,","கதிர்த்துஒளி திகழும் நுண்பல் சுணங்கின்","மாக்கண் மலர்ந்த முலையள்; தன்னையும்","சிறுகோல் உளையும் புரவி . . .",". . . . . . . . . . . . . .யமரே."],"p":"","r":"Through broken lines the poem sets milking, mullai picking with leaf cups and women with aambal-stalk bangles leaping into water beside a comparison with Uranthai of the great giver Thithan, ringed with white paddy; fine jewels like that town are offered and refused, and the girl's freckles shine like young vengai flowers.","n":"So many lines are lost that who refuses the jewels and what the brothers do cannot be read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 352","lb":"PN352","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",4,"maram","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","ornament",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",8,"unavu","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",9,"unavu","scene",""],["வேங்கையின்","வேங்கை",12,"maram","simile",""],["புரவி","புரவி",15,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தித்தன்",9],["town","உறந்தை",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:353":{"book":"purananuru","num":351,"t":["ஆசில் கம்மியன் மாசறப் புனைந்த","பொலஞ்செய் பல்காசு அணிந்த அல்குல்,","ஈகைக் கண்ணி இலங்கத் தைஇத்,","தருமமொடு இயல்வோள் சாயல் நோக்கித்,","தவிர்த்த தேரை, விளர்த்த கண்ணை,","வினவல் ஆனா வெல்போர் அண்ணல்!","‘யார்மகள்? என்போய்; கூறக் கேள், இனிக்;","குன்றுகண் டன்ன நிலைப்பல் போர்பு","நாள்கடா அழித்த நனந்தலைக் குப்பை","வல்வில் இளையர்க்கு அல்குபதம் மாற்றாத்","தொல்குடி மன்னன் மகளே; முன்நாள்","கூறி வந்த மாமுது வேந்தர்க்கு",". . . . . . . . . . . . . .",". . . உழக்குக் குருதி ஓட்டிக்,","கதுவாய் போகிய நுதிவாய் எஃகமொடு,","பஞ்சியும் களையாப் புண்ணர்.","அஞ்சுதகவு உடையர், இவள் தன்னை மாரே."],"p":"","r":"To the victorious lord who stopped his chariot, eyes pale, gazing at the girl in a gold-coin girdle and eekai garland, and keeps asking whose daughter she is, the speaker answers: she is the daughter of an old-line chieftain who never fails to feed the bowmen from grain heaps like hills, and her brothers, who answered the old kings who asked before with blood and still wear unchanged wound dressings, are men to fear.","n":"The answer moves from the father's open-handed feeding of fighters to the brothers' wounds, so the girl's lineage is described as both generous and dangerous; line 13 is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kudi","maram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 353","lb":"PN353","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஈகைக்","ஈகை",3,"maram","ornament","eekai flower"],["குப்பை","குப்பை",9,"unavu","gift_food","grain heap"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:354":{"book":"purananuru","num":352,"t":["அரைசுதலை வரினும் அடங்கல் ஆனா","நிரைகாழ் எஃகம் நீரின் மூழ்கப்","புரையோர் சேர்ந்தெனத், தந்தையும் பெயர்க்கும்;","வயல்அமர் கழனி வாயிற் பொய்கைக்,","கயலார் நாரை உகைத்த வாளை","புனலாடு மகளிர் வளமனை ஒய்யும்","ஊர்கவின் இழப்பவும் வருவது கொல்லோ-","சுணங்கு அணிந்து எழிலிய அணந்துஏந்து இளமுலை;","வீங்குஇறைப் பணைத்தோள், மடந்தை","மான்பிணை யன்ன மகிழ்மட நோக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"Spears that would not be stilled even if a king came against them are plunged in water, and the father turns back even worthy suitors; will the town, where the vaalai fish chased by the heron leaps from the field pond into the houses of bathing women, lose its beauty over the young girl's gaze, gentle as a doe's?","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (spears plunged in water; the father 'turning back' even the worthy) admit more than one construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kedu","azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 354","lb":"PN354","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மகட்பாற் காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["பொய்கைக்","பொய்கை",4,"pira","scene",""],["கயலார்","கயல்",5,"pira","scene","carp"],["நாரை","நாரை",5,"pul","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",5,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["மான்பிணை","மான்",10,"maa","simile","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:355":{"book":"purananuru","num":353,"t":["மதிலும் ஞாயில் இன்றே; கிடங்கும்,","நீஇர் இன்மையின், கன்றுமேய்ந்து உகளும்;","ஊரது நிலைமையும் இதுவே;",". . . . . . . . . . . . ."],"p":"","r":"The fort has no battlements, its moat is so dry that calves graze and frisk in it; that is the state of the town; the rest is lost.","n":"The calves playing in the dry moat make the town's helplessness almost comic; the colophon gives only the thinai and no thurai, poet or patron.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["aran","kedu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 355","lb":"PN355","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"nochchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கன்றுமேய்ந்து","கன்று",2,"maa","scene",""],["நீஇர்","நீர்",2,"pira","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:356":{"book":"purananuru","num":354,"t":["களரி பரந்து, கள்ளி போகிப்,","பகலும் கூஉம் கூகையொடு, பிறழ்பல்,","ஈம விளக்கின், பேஎய் மகளிரொடு","அஞ்சுவந் தன்று, இம் மஞ்சுபடு முதுகாடு;","நெஞ்சமர் காதலர் அழுத கண்ணீர்","என்புபடு சுடலை வெண்ணீறு அவிப்ப,","எல்லார் புறனும் தான்கண்டு, உலகத்து","மன்பதைக் கெல்லாம் தானாய்த்,","தன்புறம் காண்போர்க் காண்புஅறி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"The old misty burning ground, with its salt waste, spreading cactus, owls hooting by day, pyre lamps and ghoul-women with crooked teeth, is fearful; the tears of loving kin quench the white ash of its bone-strewn pyres, and having seen the backs of everyone and become the end for all mankind, it has never seen the back of anyone who would see its back.","n":"'Seeing someone's back' is to outlast them (they turn and go); the ground has seen everyone's, and no one has seen its own, so the cremation ground, not any king, is the true survivor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 356","lb":"PN356","poet":"தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களரி","களர்",1,"pira","scene","salt waste"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",1,"maram","scene",""],["கூகையொடு","கூகை",2,"pul","scene","owl"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",3,"pira","scene","ghoul"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:357":{"book":"purananuru","num":355,"t":["குன்று மணந்த மலைபிணித் தியாத்தமண்,","பொதுமை சுட்டிய மூவர் உலகமும்,","பொதுமை இன்றி ஆண்டிசி னோர்க்கும்,","மாண்ட வன்றே, ஆண்டுகள், துணையே","வைத்த தன்றே வெறுக்கை;",". . . . . . . . . . ணை","புணைகை விட்டோர்க்கு அரிதே, துணைஅழத்","தொக்குஉயிர் வெளவுங் காலை,","இக்கரை நின்று இவர்ந்து உக்கரை கொளலே."],"p":"","r":"Even those who ruled without sharing the hill-bound earth that is common to three kings have seen their years end, and wealth was not what stayed as their support; when death takes life as kin weep, it is hard for those who let go of the raft to climb from this bank to the far one.","n":"Line 6 is mostly lost, so what the raft (புணை) is, merit or giving, is inferred from the contrast with wealth in line 5.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","selvam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 357","lb":"PN357","poet":"பிரமனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:358":{"book":"purananuru","num":356,"t":["பருதி சூழ்ந்தஇப் பயங்கெழு மாநிலம்","ஒருபகல் எழுவர் எய்தி யற்றே;","வையமும் தவமும் தூக்கின், தவத்துக்கு","ஐயவி யனைத்தும் ஆற்றாது ஆகலின்,","கைவிட் டனரே காதலர்; அதனால்","விட்டோரை விடாஅள், திருவே;","விடாஅ தோர்இவள் விடப்பட் டோரே."],"p":"","r":"This rich earth circled by the sun has had seven owners in a single day; weighed against austerity the world is not worth a mustard seed of it, so those who loved it let it go; therefore Fortune does not leave those who leave her, and those who will not leave are left by her.","n":"The last two lines turn on one verb (விடு) four times, so that leaving and being left are the same act seen from Fortune's side; the poem praises renunciation in a book of kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 358","lb":"PN358","poet":"வான்மீகியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"மனையறம், துறவறம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மனையறம்","துறவறம்"],"mp":[],"k":[["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",4,"maram","scene","mustard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:359":{"book":"purananuru","num":357,"t":["பாறுபடப் பறைந்த பன்மாறு மருங்கின்,","வேறுபடு குரல வெவ்வாய்க் கூகையொடு","பிணந்தின் குறுநரி நிணம்திகழ் பல்ல,","பேஎய் மகளிர் பிணம்தழூஉப் பற்றி,","விளர்ஊன் தின்ற வெம்புலால் மெய்யர்","களரி மருங்கில் கால்பெயர்த் தாடி,","ஈம விளக்கின் வெருவரப் பேரும்","காடுமுன் னினரே, நாடுகொண் டோரும்!","நினக்கும் வருதல் வைகல் அற்றே;","வசையும் நிற்கும், இசையும் நிற்கும்;","அதனால் வசைநீக்கி இசைவேண்டியும்,","நசை வேண்டாது நன்று மொழிந்தும்,","நிலவுக் கோட்டுப் பலகளிற் றோடு,","பொலம் படைய மா மயங்கிட,","இழைகிளர் நெடுந்தேர் இரவலர்க்கு அருகாது","‘கொள்’ என விடுவை யாயின், வெள்ளென","ஆண்டுநீ பெயர்ந்த பின்னும்,","ஈண்டுநீடு விளங்கும், நீ எய்திய புகழே."],"p":"","r":"The burning ground of hooting owls, corpse-eating jackals with fat-shining teeth and ghoul-women who clasp the dead, eat pale flesh and dance on the salt waste by the pyre lamps, has received even conquerors of kingdoms, and your day too will come; blame stays and fame stays, so put away blame, seek fame, speak good without wanting, and give elephants, gold-trapped horses and tall chariots freely to suppliants, and your fame will shine here long after you go.","n":"'Blame stays and fame stays' (line 10) makes the counsel a choice between two lasting names, not between a name and oblivion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 359","lb":"PN359","poet":"கரவட்டனார்","pat":"அந்துவன் கீரன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கூகையொடு","கூகை",2,"pul","scene","owl"],["குறுநரி","நரி",3,"maa","scene","jackal"],["விளர்ஊன்","ஊன்",5,"unavu","scene",""],["பலகளிற்","களிறு",13,"maa","gift_food",""],["மா","மா",14,"maa","gift_food","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:360":{"book":"purananuru","num":358,"t":["பெரிது ஆராச் சிறு சினத்தர்,","சில சொல்லால் பல கேள்வியர்,","நுண் ணுணர்வினாற் பெருங் கொடையர்","கலுழ் நனையால் தண் தேறலர்,","கனி குய்யாற் கொழுந் துவையர்,","தாழ் உவந்து தழூஉ மொழியர்,","பயன் உறுப்பப் பலர்க்கு ஆற்றி","ஏம மாக இந்நிலம் ஆண்டோர்","சிலரே; பெரும! கேள் இனி ; நாளும்,","பலரே தகை அஃது அறியா தோரே!”","அன்னோர் செல்வமும் மன்னி நில்லாது;","இன்னும் அற்று, அதன் பண்பே; அதனால்","நிச்சமும் ஒழுக்கம் முட்டிலை; பரிசில்","நச்சுவர் கையின் நிரப்பல் ஓம்புமதி; அச்சுவரப்","பாறுஇறை கொண்ட பறந்தலை, மாகத","கள்ளி போகிய களரி மருங்கின்,","வெள்ளில் நிறுத்த பின்றைக் , கள்ளொடு","புல்லகத்து இட்ட சில்லவிழ் வல்சி,","புலையன் ஏவப் புன்மேல் அமர்ந்துண்டு,","அழல்வாய்ப் புக்க பின்னும்,","பலர்வாய்த்து இராஅர், பகுத்துஉண் டோரே"],"p":"","r":"Few have ruled this land as a shelter for others, men slow to anger, of few words and much learning, of fine understanding and great giving, who served cool liquor and rich food and helped many; even their wealth does not abide, so keep your conduct faultless and fill the hands of gift-seekers, for after the pulaiyan sets out the rice offering on grass in the vulture-haunted cremation ground and the body enters the fire, those who shared their food will not remain.","n":"The last line (பலர்வாய்த்து இராஅர்) can be read as 'they do not stay' or as a claim about being in many mouths; the sense of the close, whether sharers are remembered or simply die like all, is not settled. Line 10 carries a stray closing quotation mark.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","eegai","parisil","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 360","lb":"PN360","poet":"சங்க வருணர் என்னும் நாகரியர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["தேறலர்","தேறல்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["துவையர்","துவை",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பாறுஇறை","பாறு",15,"pul","scene","vulture"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",16,"maram","scene",""],["கள்ளொடு","கள்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வல்சி","வல்சி",18,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புலையன்","புலையன்",19,"seythi","scene",""],["புன்மேல்","புல்",19,"maram","scene","grass"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:18"},"pn:361":{"book":"purananuru","num":359,"t":["கார் எதிர் உருமின் உரறிக், கல்லென,","ஆருயிர்க்கு அலமரும் ஆராக் கூற்றம்!","நின்வரவு அஞ்சலன் மாதோ; நன்பல","கேள்வி முற்றிய வேள்வி அந்தணர்க்கு,","அருங்கலம் நீரொடு சிதறிப், பெருந்தகைத்","தாயின்நன்று பலர்க்கு ஈத்துத்,","தெருணடை மாகளிறொடு தன்","அருள் பாடுநர்க்கு நன்கு அருளியும்,","உருள்நடைப் பஃறேர் ஒன்னார்க் கொன்றுதன்","தாள் சேருநர்க்கு இனிது ஈத்தும்,","புரி மாலையர் பாடி னிக்குப்","பொலந் தாமரைப் பூம் பாணரொடு","கலந் தளைஇய நீள் இருக் கையால்","பொறையொடு மலிந்த கற்பின், மான்நோக்கின்,","வில்என விலங்கிய புருவத்து, வல்லென,","நல்கின் நாஅஞ்சும் முள்எயிற்று, மகளிர்","அல்குல் தாங்கா அசைஇ, மெல்லென","கலங்கலந் தேறல் பொலங்கலத்து ஏந்தி,","அமிழ்தென மடுப்ப மாந்தி, இகழ்விலன்,","நில்லா உலகத்து நிலையாமைநீ","சொல்லா வேண்டா தோன்றல், முந்துஅறிந்த","முழுதுஉணர் கேள்வியன் ஆகலின் விரகினானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells Death, who roars like thunder before the rains and is never sated with lives, that this king does not fear its coming: he has poured gifts with water for learned sacrificing brahmins, given like a mother to many, given elephants to those who sing him and chariots to those who come to his feet, drunk the toddy his women held to his lips like nectar, and needs no one to tell him that the world does not last, for he learned it long ago.","n":"The poem is addressed to Death, not to the king, and turns the kaanchi lesson round: the counsel that the world is unstable is declared unnecessary, because a life of giving and enjoying has already answered it. The colophon has no thinai, thurai or poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","eegai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 361","lb":"PN361","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["உருமின்","உரும்",1,"pira","simile","thunder"],["அந்தணர்க்கு","அந்தணர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மாகளிறொடு","மா களிறு",7,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["பாடி னிக்குப்","பாடினி",11,"seythi","scene",""],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",12,"maram","ornament",""],["பாணரொடு","பாணர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்நோக்கின்","மான் நோக்கு",14,"maa","simile","deer"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",18,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:362":{"book":"purananuru","num":360,"t":["ஞாயிற்றுஅன்ன ஆய்மணி மிடைந்த","மதியுறழ் ஆரம் மார்பில் புரளப்,","பலிபெறு முரசம் பாசறைச் சிலைப்பப்,","பொழிலகம் பரந்த பெருஞ்செய் ஆடவர்","செருப்புகன்று எடுக்கும் விசய வெண்கொடி","அணங்குஉருத் தன்ன கணங்கொள் தானை,","கூற்றத் தன்ன மாற்றரு முன்பன்,","ஆக்குரல் காண்பின் அந்த ணாளர்","நான்மறை குறித்தன்று அருளாகா மையின்","அறம்குறித் தன்று; பொருளா குதலின்","மருள் தீர்ந்து, மயக்கு ஒரீஇக்,","கைபெய்த நீர் கடற் பரப்ப,","ஆம் இருந்த அடை நல்கிச்,","சோறு கொடுத்து, மிகப் பெரிதும்","வீறுசான் நன்கலம் வீசி நன்றும்,","சிறுவெள் என்பின் நெடுவெண் களரின்,","வாய்வன் காக்கை கூகையொடு கூடிப்","பகலும் கூவும் அகலுள் ஆங்கண்,","காடுகண் மறைத்த கல்லென் சுற்றமொடு","இல்என்று இல்வயின் பெயர ; மெல்ல","இடஞ்சிறிது ஒதுங்கல் அஞ்சி,","உடம்பொடும் சென்மார், உயர்ந்தோர் நாட்டே,"],"p":"","r":"A king with a gem necklace like the sun and moon on his chest, a drum fed with offerings in his camp and an army dreadful as a demon, mighty as Death, having cleared his confusion, pours gift-water wide as the sea, gives land, rice and splendid jewels, and then, while his kin go home from the burning ground of small white bones, where crows and owls call even by day, saying 'he is no more', goes on with his body to the land of the high ones.","n":"Lines 8 to 10 contrast what a brahmin's four Vedas aim at with aram and with substance (பொருள்); their syntax is hard and the reading here is tentative. The closing image, going to the high world with the body, makes the gifts the king's last act rather than his reward, and the burning ground is drawn with the same small-white-bone detail the kaanchi poems share.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 362","lb":"PN362","poet":"சிறுவெண்டேரையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஞாயிற்றுஅன்ன","ஞாயிறு",1,"pira","simile","sun"],["மதியுறழ்","மதி",2,"pira","simile","moon"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",3,"parai","scene",""],["அந்த ணாளர்","அந்தணாளர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["சோறு","சோறு",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["காக்கை","காக்கை",17,"pul","scene",""],["கூகையொடு","கூகை",17,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:363":{"book":"purananuru","num":361,"t":["இருங்கடல் உடுத்தஇப் பெருங்கண் மாநிலம்","உடைஇலை நடுவணது இடைபிறர்க்கு இன்றித்,","தாமே ஆண்ட ஏமம் காவலர்","இடுதிரை மணலினும் பலரே; சுடுபிணக்","காடுபதி யாகப் போகித், தத்தம்","நாடு பிறர்கொளச் சென்றுமாய்ந் தனரே;","அதனால் நீயும் கேண்மதி அத்தை ! வீயாது","உடம்பொடு நின்ற உயிரும் இல்லை;","மடங்கல் உண்மை மாயமோ அன்றே;","கள்ளி ஏய்ந்த முள்ளியம் புறங்காட்டு.","வெள்ளில் போகிய வியலுள் ஆங்கண்,","உப்பிலாஅ அவிப் புழுக்கல்","கைக் கொண்டு, பிறக்கு நோக்காது,","இழி பிறப்பினோன் ஈயப் பெற்று,","நிலங்கல னாக, இலங்குபலி மிசையும்","இன்னா வைகல் வாரா முன்னே,","செய்ந்நீ முன்னிய வினையே,","முந்நீர் வரைப்பகம் முழுதுடன் துறந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The kings who ruled the sea-girt earth alone, sharing it with none, are more than the sand the waves heap up, and all went to the burning ground and died while others took their lands; so listen: no life stays with the body for ever and death is no illusion; before the bitter day when, in the burning ground of cactus, you eat with the earth for your plate the saltless boiled rice that a man of low birth hands over without looking back, do the work you intended, renouncing the whole sea-bounded world.","n":"The humiliation is precise: the funeral offering is boiled without salt, handed over by an outcaste who does not turn to look, and eaten off the ground. The king who held the world 'with no share to others' ends taking food from the lowest. What the deed to be done is (giving, or renunciation) is left open by செய்ந்நீ முன்னிய வினையே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu","arasu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 363","lb":"PN363","poet":"ஐயாதிச் சிறுவெண்டேரையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மணலினும்","மணல்",4,"pira","simile",""],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",10,"maram","scene",""],["முள்ளியம்","முள்ளி",10,"maram","scene",""],["புழுக்கல்","புழுக்கல்",12,"unavu","gift_food",""],["இழி பிறப்பினோன்","இழிபிறப்பினோன்",14,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:364":{"book":"purananuru","num":362,"t":["வாடா மாலை பாடினி அணியப்,","பாணன் சென்னிக் கேணி பூவா","எரிமருள் தாமரைப் பெருமலர் தயங்க,","மைவிடை இரும்போத்துச் செந்தீச் சேர்த்திக்,","காயங் கனிந்த கண்ணகன் கொழுங்குறை","நறவுண் செவ்வாய் நாத்திறம் பெயர்ப்ப","உண்டும், தின்றும், இரப்போர்க்கு ஈந்தும்,","மகிழ்கம் வம்மோ, மறப்போ ரோயே!","அரிய வாகலும் உரிய பெரும!","நிலம்பக வீழ்ந்த அலங்கல் பல்வேர்","முதுமரப் பொத்தின் கதுமென இயம்பும்","கூகைக் கோழி ஆனாத்","தாழிய பெருங்கா டெய்திய ஞான்றே."],"p":"","r":"Let the bard-woman wear the unfading gold garland and the bard the gold lotus that blooms in no pool; let us roast the black ram on the red fire, turn the tongue over the spiced fat chunks in a toddy-reddened mouth, eat, devour and give to those who ask, and rejoice, warrior; for it will be hard, great one, when we have reached the great burning ground of urns, where the owl hoots suddenly from the hollow of an old tree with many roots split into the earth.","n":"Kaanchi here means feasting, not renouncing: the same knowledge of death that makes 363 urge the king to give up the world makes this poet urge him to eat, drink and give now. The poet's name, கூகைக் கோரியார், is taken from the owl of line 12.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","nilaiyaamai","eegai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 364","lb":"PN364","poet":"கூகைக் கோரியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["மாலை","மாலை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["மைவிடை","மை விடை",4,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["நறவுண்","நறவு",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முதுமரப்","முது மரம்",11,"maram","scene",""],["கூகைக் கோழி","கூகைக் கோழி",12,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:365":{"book":"purananuru","num":363,"t":["மயங்குஇருங் கருவிய விசும்புமுக னாக,","இயங்கிய இருசுடர் கண் எனப், பெயரிய","வளியிடை வழங்கா வழக்கரு நீத்தம்,","வயிரக் குறட்டின் வயங்குமணி யாரத்து","பொன்னந் திகிரி முன்சமத்து உருட்டிப்,","பொருநர்க் காணாச் செருமிகு முன்பின்","முன்னோர் செல்லவும், செல்லாது, இன்னும்","விலைநலப் பெண்டிரிற் பலர்மீக் கூற,","உள்ளேன் வாழியர், யான்’ எனப் பன்மாண்","நிலமகள் அழுத காஞ்சியும்","உண்டென உரைப்பரால், உணர்ந்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wise say there is also a kaanchi in which the Earth, with the clouded sky for her face and the two moving lights for her eyes, wept: 'The ancestors who rolled the golden wheel of diamond hub and gem spokes before every battle and found no equal have gone, yet I do not go, and many still claim to be over me like a woman whose beauty is bought; I do not wish to live.'","n":"The kaanchi is told from the side of the land, not the king: kings pass while the Earth stays, and her lament is not for their deaths but for being possessed by one after another, which she likens to a courtesan's lot. The poem presents itself as a report of a known kaanchi (உண்டென உரைப்பரால்), a poem about a genre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 365","lb":"PN365","poet":"மார்க்கண்டேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பெண்டிரிற்","பெண்டிர்",8,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:366":{"book":"purananuru","num":364,"t":["விழுக்கடிப்பு அறைந்த முழுக்குரல் முரசம்","ஒழுக்குடை மருங்கின் ஒருமொழித் தாக,","அரவுஎறி உருமின் உரறுபு சிலைப்ப,","ஒருதா மாகிய பெருமை யோரும்,","தம்புகழ் நிறீஇச் சென்றுமாய்ந் தனரே;","அதனால், அறிவோன் மகனே! மறவோர் செம்மால்!",". . . . . . . . . . உரைப்பக் கேண்மதி;","நின் ஊற்றம் பிறர் அறியாது,","பிறர் கூறிய மொழி தெரியா,","ஞாயிற்று எல்லை ஆள்வினைக்கு உதவி,","இரவின் எல்லை வருவது நாடி,","உரை . . . . . . . . . . .","உழவொழி பெரும்பகடு அழிதின் றாங்குச்,","செங்கண் மகளிரொடு சிறுதுளி அளைஇ,","அங்கள் தேறல் ஆங்கலத்து உகுப்ப,","கெடல் அருந் திருவ . . . . . . .","மடை வேண்டுநர்க்கு இடை அருகாது,","அவிழ் வேண்டுநர்க்கு இடை அருளி","விடை வீழ்த்துச் சூடு கிழிப்ப,","நீர்நீலை பெருத்த வார்மணல் அடைகரைக்,","காவு தோறும் . . . . . . . .","மடங்கல் உண்மை மாயமோ அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Even the great ones who ruled alone, whose drum roared like snake-striking thunder with one command through the land, set up their fame and died; so, son of the wise one, lord of warriors, hear me: [the text is broken here] use the day for effort and the night for weighing what is to come, drink sweet toddy in fine vessels with red-eyed women like a great ox released from the plough, give food and rice without stint, fell bulls and share the roast in every grove on the sandy bank, for death is real and no illusion.","n":"Four lines are broken (7, 12, 16, 21), so the order of counsel is partly lost. What remains balances work by day (ஞாயிற்று எல்லை ஆள்வினைக்கு உதவி) with feasting and giving, and ends on the same line as 363. The colophon's patron தருமபுத்திரன் answers the poem's address அறிவோன் மகனே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aalvinai","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 366","lb":"PN366","poet":"கோதமனார்","pat":"தருமபுத்திரன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பொதுவியல்","ctu":"பெருங்காஞ்சி","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பெருங்காஞ்சி"],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசம்",1,"parai","scene",""],["அரவுஎறி","அரவு",3,"pira","simile","snake"],["உருமின்","உரும்",3,"pira","simile","thunder"],["பெரும்பகடு","பகடு",13,"maa","simile","ox"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["விடை","விடை",19,"maa","gift_food","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:367":{"book":"purananuru","num":365,"t":["நாகத் தன்ன பாகார் மண்டிலம்","தமவே யாயினும் தம்மொடு செல்லா;","வெற்றோர் ஆயினும் நோற்றோர்க்கு ஒழியும்;","ஏற்ற பார்ப்பார்க்கு ஈர்ங்கை நிறையப்","பூவும் பொன்னும் புனல்படச் சொரிந்து,","பாசிழை மகளிர் பொலங்கலத்து ஏந்திய","நாரறி தேறல் மாந்தி, மகிழ் சிறந்து,","இரவலர்க்கு அருங்கலம் அருகாது வீசி,","வாழ்தல் வேண்டும், இவண் வரைந்த வைகல்;","வாழச் செய்த நல்வினை அல்லது,","ஆழுங் காலைப் புணைபிறிது இல்லை;","ஒன்று புரிந்து அடங்கிய இருபிறப் பாளர்","முத்தீப் புரையக் காண்தக இருந்த","கொற்ற வெண்குடக் கொடித்தேர் வேந்திர்;","யான் அறி அளவையோ இவ்வே; வானத்து","வயங்கித் தோன்றும் மீனினும், இம்மெனப்","பரந்து இயங்கும் மாமழை உறையினும்,","உயர்ந்து மேந்தோன்றிப் பொலிக, நும் நாளே!"],"p":"","r":"Avvaiyar tells three crowned kings seated together, fair as the three sacred fires of the twice-born: the lands you hold do not go with you but stay for others; so pour flowers and gold with water into brahmins' hands, drink the toddy women bring in gold cups, scatter jewels to suppliants and live the days allotted, since only the good done while living is a raft when you sink; and may your days outnumber the stars and the raindrops.","n":"Blessing and warning are one poem: the kaanchi doctrine that possessions stay behind is the reason given for the court's pleasures and gifts, and the blessing that closes it counts days, not conquests. The kings are not named; the colophon gives no patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","eegai","nilaiyaamai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 367","lb":"PN367","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[],"k":[["பார்ப்பார்க்கு","பார்ப்பார்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["பூவும்","பூ",5,"maram","gift_food",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["இருபிறப் பாளர்","இருபிறப்பாளர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["மீனினும்","மீன்",16,"pira","simile","star"],["மாமழை","மா மழை",17,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:368":{"book":"purananuru","num":366,"t":["களிறு முகந்து பெயர்குவம் எனினே.","ஒளிறுமழை தவிர்க்கும் குன்றம் போலக்,","கைம்மா எல்லாம் கணையிடத் தொலைந்தன;","கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் முகக்குவம் எனினே;","கடும்பரி நன்மான் வாங்குவயின் ஒல்கி","நெடும்பீடு அழிந்து, நிலம்சேர்ந் தனவே;","கொய்சுவல் புரவி முகக்குவம் எனினே,","மெய்நிறைந்த வடுவொடு பெரும்பிறி தாகி,","வளிவழக் கறுத்த வங்கம் போலக்","குருதியம் பெரும்புனல் கூர்ந்தனவே; ஆங்க","முகவை இன்மையின் உகவை இன்றி,","இரப்போர் இரங்கும் இன்னா வியன்களத்து,","ஆள்அழிப் படுத்த வாளேர் உழவ !","கடாஅ யானைக் கால்வழி யன்னவென்","தெடாரித் தெண்கண் தெளிர்ப்ப வொற்றிப்,","பாடி வந்த தெல்லாம், கோடியர்","முழவுமருள் திருமணி மிடைந்தநின்","அரவுறழ் ஆரம் முகக்குவம் எனவே."],"p":"","r":"A drummer tells the Chera that he came to scoop up elephants, but all fell to arrows like hills that stop the clouds; chariots, but they lie broken on the ground; horses, but they lie dead and wounded in the flood of blood like ships becalmed; so on this cruel field where beggars grieve with nothing to take, ploughman whose plough is the sword, all his singing was to ask for the gem necklace on the king's chest.","n":"The poem praises by complaint: the king's battle was so destructive that there is no war booty left to give, so the bard asks for the necklace he wears. The request formula முகக்குவம் எனின் (if we would scoop) frames the whole poem, as 372 and 371 use முகவை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalavazhi","parisil","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 368","lb":"PN368","poet":"கழாத் தலையார்","pat":"சேரமான் குடக்கோ நெடுஞ் சேரலாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்களவழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","absent","elephant"],["கைம்மா","கைம்மா",3,"maa","absent","elephant"],["நன்மான்","நல் மான்",5,"maa","scene","horse"],["புரவி","புரவி",7,"maa","absent","horse"],["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",9,"pira","simile","ship"],["யானைக்","யானை",14,"maa","simile",""],["தெடாரித்","தடாரி",15,"parai","scene",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",16,"seythi","simile",""],["முழவுமருள்","முழவு",17,"parai","simile",""],["அரவுறழ்","அரவு",18,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:369":{"book":"purananuru","num":367,"t":["இருப்புமுகம் செறிந்த ஏந்தொழில் மருப்பின்,","கருங்கை யானை கொண்மூவாக,","நீண்மொழி மறவர் எறிவனர் உயர்த்த","வாள்மின் நாக, வயங்குடிப்பு அமைந்த","குருதிப் பலிய முரசுமுழக் காக,","அரசராப் பனிக்கும் அணங்குறு பொழுதின்,","வெவ் விசைப் புரவி வீசுவளி யாக,","விசைப்புறு வல்வில் வீங்குநாண் உகைத்த","கணைத்துளி பொழிந்த கண்ணகன் கிடக்கை,","ஈரச் செறுவயின் தேர்ஏ ராக,","விடியல் புக்கு, நெடிய நீட்டி, நின்","செருப்படை மிளிர்ந்த திருத்துறு பைஞ்சால்.","பிடித்தெறி வெள்வேல் கணையமொடு வித்தி,","விழுத்தலை சாய்த்த வெருவரு பைங்கூழ்ப்,","பேய்மகள் பற்றிய பிணம்பிறங்கு பல்போர்பு,","கணநரி யோடு கழுதுகளம் படுப்பப்,","பூதங் காப்பப் பொலிகளந் தழீஇப்,","பாடுநர்க்கு இருந்த பீடுடை யாள!","தேய்வை வெண்காழ் புரையும் விசிபிணி","வேய்வை காணா விருந்தின் போர்வை","அரிக்குரல் தடாரி உருப்ப ஒற்றிப்,","பாடி வந்திசின் பெரும; பாடான்று","எழிலி தோயும் இமிழிசை யருவிப்,","பொன்னுடை நெடுங்கோட்டு, இமையத் தன்ன","ஓடைநுதல, ஒல்குதல் அறியாத்,","துடியடிக் குழவிய பிடியிடை மிடைந்த","வேழ முகவை நல்குமதி;","தாழா ஈகைத், தகை வெய் யோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The bard sings the Chera's battle as a season of rain and tillage: elephants for clouds, swords for lightning, the drum for thunder, horses for wind, arrows for rain; chariots plough the wet field from dawn, spears sow it, fallen heads are the green crop, corpses the stacked sheaves that the demon-woman holds, jackals and ghouls thresh and spirits guard the heap; and he asks the lord of this field for a gift of elephants, cows like the gold-peaked Himalaya and their calves.","n":"This is the fullest form of the ploughing figure that 368 compresses into one epithet and 373's colophon names ஏர்க்கள உருவகம். The bard's request is placed exactly where a harvest's share would fall, after the threshing, so the allegory carries a claim: the drummer is owed a portion of this crop as a labourer is of grain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalavazhi","padai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 369","lb":"PN369","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"சேரமான் கடலோட்டிய வெல்கெழு குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்களவழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["முரசுமுழக் காக","முரசு",5,"parai","simile",""],["புரவி","புரவி",7,"maa","simile","horse"],["பைங்கூழ்ப்","பைங்கூழ்",14,"maram","simile",""],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",15,"deyvam","scene",""],["கணநரி","கண நரி",16,"maa","scene",""],["பூதங்","பூதம்",17,"deyvam","scene",""],["தடாரி","தடாரி",21,"parai","scene",""],["பிடியிடை","பிடி",26,"maa","gift_food","elephant cow"],["வேழ","வேழம்",27,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[["hill","இமையத்",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:370":{"book":"purananuru","num":368,"t":[". . . . . . . . . . . . . . . வி,","நாரும் போழும் செய்துண்டு, ஓராங்குப்","பசிதினத் திரங்கிய இரும்பே ரொக்கற்கு","ஆர்பதம் கண்ணென மாதிரம் துழைஇ,","வேர்உழந்து உலறி, மருங்கு செத்து ஒழியவந்து,","அத்தக் குடிஞைத் துடிமருள் தீங்குரல்","உழுஞ்சில்அம் கவட்டிடை இருந்த பருந்தின்","பெடைபயிர் குரலொடு இசைக்கும் ஆங்கண்","கழைகாய்ந்து உலறிய வறங்கூர் நீள்இடை,","வரிமரல் திரங்கிய கானம் பிற்படப்,","பழுமரம் உள்ளிய பறவை போல,","ஒண்படை மாரி வீழ்கனி பெய்தெனத்,","துவைத்தெழு குருதி நிலமிசைப் பரப்ப,","விளைந்த செழுங்குரல் அரிந்து, கால் குவித்துப்","படுபிணப் பல்போர்பு அழிய வாங்கி","எருதுகளி றாக, வாள்மடல் ஓச்சி","அதரி திரித்த ஆளுகு கடாவின்,","அகன்கண் தடாரி தெளிர்ப்ப ஒற்றி,","‘வெந்திறல் வியன்களம் பொலிக!’ என்று ஏத்தி","இருப்புமுகம் செறித்த ஏந்து எழில் மருப்பின்","வரைமருள் முகவைக்கு வந்தனென்; பெரும;","வடிநவில் எஃகம் பாய்ந்தெனக், கிடந்த","தொடியுடைத் தடக்கை ஓச்சி, வெருவார்","இனத்துஅடி விராய வரிக்குடர் அடைச்சி","அழுகுரற் பேய்மகள் அயரக், கழுகொடு","செஞ்செவி எருவை திரிதரும்;","அஞ்சுவரு கிடக்கைய களங்கிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"With his large family starved to eating fibre and bark, the drummer crossed the parched long road where the owl's call answers the kite's mate in the uzhinjil fork and the bamboo has dried, and came like a bird to a fruiting tree to the field where weapons rained down fruit, blood flooded the ground, corpses were reaped, heaped and threshed with elephants for oxen; praising the field, he asks for elephants with iron-capped tusks, while the demon-woman dances decked in entrails and vultures circle.","n":"The bard's hunger and the field's plenty are set side by side: the starving family of the opening is fed, in the figure, by the harvest of corpses. The first line is lost, and the review notes the opening lines read differently in another edition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","kalavazhi","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 370","lb":"PN370","poet":"ஊன்பொதி பசுங்குடையார்","pat":"சோழன் செரப்பாழி இறிந்த இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்களவழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவழி"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["நாரும்","நார்",2,"maram","gift_food",""],["போழும்","போழ்",2,"maram","gift_food",""],["குடிஞைத்","குடிஞை",6,"pul","scene","owl"],["துடிமருள்","துடி",6,"parai","simile",""],["உழுஞ்சில்அம்","உழுஞ்சில்",7,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene",""],["கழைகாய்ந்து","கழை",9,"maram","absent",""],["வரிமரல்","மரல்",10,"maram","absent",""],["பழுமரம்","பழு மரம்",11,"maram","simile",""],["பறவை","பறவை",11,"pul","simile",""],["எருதுகளி றாக","எருது",16,"maa","simile","ox"],["தடாரி","தடாரி",18,"parai","scene",""],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",25,"deyvam","scene",""],["கழுகொடு","கழுகு",25,"pul","scene",""],["எருவை","எருவை",26,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:371":{"book":"purananuru","num":369,"t":["அகன் தலை வையத்துப் புரவலர்க் காணாது,","மரந்தலைச் சேர்ந்து, பட்டினி வைகி,","போதவிழ் அலரி நாரின் தொடுத்துத்,","தயங்கு இரும் பித்தை பொலியச் சூடிப்","பறையொடு தகைத்த கலப்பையென், முரவுவாய்","ஆடுறு குழிசி பாடின்று தூக்கி,","மன்ற வேம்பின் ஒண்பூ உரைப்பக்,","குறைசெயல் வேண்டா நசைஇய இருக்கையேன்,","அரிசி இன்மையின் ஆரிடை நீந்திக்,","கூர்வாய் இருப்படை நீரின் மிளிர்ப்ப,","வருகணை வாளி . . . . . அன்பின்று தலைஇ,","இரைமுரசு ஆர்க்கும் உரைசால் பாசறை,","வில்லேர் உழவின் நின் நல்லிசை யுள்ளிக்,","குறைத்தலைப் படுபிணன் எதிரப், போர்பு அழித்து","யானை எருத்தின் வாள்மட லோச்சி","அதரி திரித்த ஆள் உகு கடாவின்,","மதியத் தன்ன என் விசியுறு தடாரி","அகன்கண் அதிர, ஆகுளி தொடாலின்,","பணைமருள் நெடுந்தாள், பல்பிணர்த் தடக்கைப்,","புகர்முக முகவைக்கு வந்திசின் - பெரும!","களிற்றுக்கோட்ட டன்ன வாலெயிறு அழுத்தி,","விழுக்கொடு விரை இய வெள்நிணச் சுவையினள்,","குடர்த்தலை மாலை சூடி, ‘உணத்தின","ஆனாப் பெருவளம் செய்தோன் வானத்து","வயங்குபன் மீனினும் வாழியர், பல’ என,","உருகெழு பேய்மகள் அயரக்,","குருதித்துக ளாடிய களம்கிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Finding no patron in the wide world, sleeping hungry under trees, his hair dressed with alari flowers tied on fibre, carrying his drum-bag and a useless broken pot with no rice in it, the drummer crossed the hard way to the camp, remembering the fame of the king's bow-plough that heaps headless corpses and threshes them with the elephant for ox; beating his moon-like drum he has come for an elephant, while the demon-woman, tasting fat and garlanded with entrails, blesses the giver of such plenty to live longer than the stars.","n":"The demon-woman's blessing (வாழியர் ... பல) is the bard's own blessing displaced: the creature fed by the battle thanks the king, and the drummer, fed by nothing yet, asks to be next. According to the review's line table our text begins at the poem's third line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kalavazhi","parisil","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 371","lb":"PN371","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"பாண்டியன் தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்களவழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவழி"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மரந்தலைச்","மரம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["அலரி","அலரி",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பறையொடு","பறை",5,"parai","scene",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",7,"maram","scene",""],["அரிசி","அரிசி",9,"unavu","absent",""],["யானை","யானை",15,"maa","simile",""],["தடாரி","தடாரி",17,"parai","scene",""],["ஆகுளி","ஆகுளி",18,"parai","scene",""],["புகர்முக","புகர்முகம்",20,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",26,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:372":{"book":"purananuru","num":370,"t":["விசிபிணித் தடாரி விம்மென ஒற்றி,","ஏத்தி வந்த தெல்லாம் முழுத்த","இலங்குவாள் அவிரொளி வலம்பட மின்னிக்","கணைத்துளி பொழிந்த கண்கூடு பாசறைப்,","பொருந்தாத் தெவ்வர் அரிந்ததலை அடுப்பின்,","கூவிள விறகின் ஆக்குவரி நுடங்கல்,","ஆனா மண்டை வன்னியந் துடுப்பின்,","ஈனா வேண்மாள் இடந்துழந்து அட்ட","மாமறி பிண்டம் வாலுவன் ஏந்த,","‘வதுவை விழவின் புதுவோர்க்கு எல்லாம்","வெவ்வாய்ப் பெய்த பூதநீர் சால்க” எனப்","புலவுக்களம் பொலிய வேட்டோய்! நின்","நிலவுத்திகழ் ஆரம் முகக்குவம் எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The drummer says all his praise was to ask for the king's moon-bright necklace: the king performed a sacrifice in the battlefield, where swords flashed and arrows rained, with enemy heads for the hearth, vilva wood for fuel and a vanni ladle, the demon who never bore a child stirring the pot, the cook holding up the lump of flesh, and the blessing 'may the ghoul-water poured in hot mouths fill all the new guests of this wedding feast'.","n":"The sacrifice is a full parody of a Vedic rite and of a wedding feast at once, with every ritual item replaced by a battle one. Mangudi Kizhar uses the same figure of a feast cooked on crowned heads in 26 for the same king; here it is the preface to a request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalavazhi","parisil","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 372","lb":"PN372","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன் நெடுஞ்செழியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்கள வேள்வி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவேள்வி"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["தடாரி","தடாரி",1,"parai","scene",""],["கூவிள","கூவிளம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["வன்னியந்","வன்னி",7,"maram","scene",""],["வேண்மாள்","வேண்மாள்",8,"deyvam","scene",""],["வாலுவன்","வாலுவன்",9,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:373":{"book":"purananuru","num":371,"t":["உருமிசை முழக்கென முரசும் இசைப்பச்,","செருநவில் வேழம் கொண்மூ ஆகத்,","தேர்மா அழிதுளி தலைஇ , நாம் உறக்","கணைக்காற் றொடுத்த கண்ணகன் பாசறை,","இழிதரு குருதியொடு ஏந்திய ஒள்வாள்","பிழிவது போலப் பிட்டைஊறு உவப்ப,","மைந்தர் ஆடிய மயங்குபெருந் தானைக்,","கொங்கு புறம் பெற்ற கொற்ற வேந்தே!",". . . . . . தண்ட மாப்பொறி","மடக்கண் மயில் இயன் மறலி யாங்கு","நெடுங்சுவர் நல்லில் புலம்பக் கடைகழிந்து,","மென்தோள் மகளிர் மன்றம் பேணார்,","புண்ணுவ. . . . . . . . . . . . .",". . . . .அணியப் புரவி வாழ்கெனச்,","சொல்நிழல் இன்மையின் நன்னிழல் சேர,","நுண்பூண் மார்பின் புன்றலைச் சிறாஅர்","அம்பழி பொழுதில் தமர்முகம் காணா,",". . . . . . . . . ற்றொக்கான","வேந்துபுறங் கொடுத்த வீய்ந்துகு பறந்தலை,","மாட மயங்கெரி மண்டிக், கோடிறுபு,","உரும் எறி மலையின், இருநிலம் சேரச்,","சென்றோன் மன்ற சொ . . . .",". . . . . ண்ணநிகர் கண்டுகண் அலைப்ப,","வஞ்சி முற்றம் வயக்கள னாக,","அஞ்சா மறவர் ஆட்போர்பு அழித்துக்","கொண்டனை பெரும! குடபுலத்து அதரி;","பொலிக அத்தை நின் பணைதனற . . . ளம்!","விளங்குதிணை, வேந்தர் களந்தொறுஞ் சென்ற,","“புகர்முக முகவை பொலிக!” என்றி ஏத்திக்,","கொண்டனர்’ என்ப பெரியோர் : யானும்","அங்கண் மாக்கிணை அதிர ஒற்ற,",". . . . . லெனாயினுங் காதலின் ஏத்தி","நின்னோர் அன்னோர் பிறரிவண் இன்மையின்,","மன்னெயில் முகவைக்கு வந்திசின், பெரும!","பகைவர் புகழ்ந்த அண்மை, நகைவர்க்குத்","தாவின்று உதவும் பண்பின், பேயொடு","கணநரி திரிதரும் ஆங்கண், நிணன் அருந்து","செஞ்செவி எருவை குழீஇ,","அஞ்சுவரு கிடக்கைய களங்கிழ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the victorious Chola who defeated the Kongu people: drums roared like thunder, war elephants were clouds, chariots and horses rain; swords dripped blood as if wrung; women left their high-walled houses; enemy kings turned their backs; mansions fell in flames like hills struck by thunder; he made the courtyard of Vanji a threshing floor and threshed the fearless warriors there; others came to every king's field for elephants, and he too, beating his drum for love, has come to this lord of the fearful field.","n":"Eight lines are broken (9, 13, 14, 18, 22, 23, 27, 32), so the sequence of siege, sack and request is only partly recoverable. The colophon's second thurai, ஏர்க்கள உருவகம், names the ploughing figure that 368 to 372 share.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maram","kalavazhi","parisil","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 373","lb":"PN373","poet":"கோவூர்கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குராப்பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"மறக்களவழி; ஏர்க்கள உருவகமும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["மறக்களவழி","ஏர்க்கள உருவகம்"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முரசும்","முரசு",1,"parai","simile",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["மயில்","மயில்",10,"pul","simile",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",31,"parai","scene",""],["பேயொடு","பேய்",36,"deyvam","scene",""],["கணநரி","கண நரி",37,"maa","scene",""],["எருவை","எருவை",38,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["town","வஞ்சி",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:374":{"book":"purananuru","num":372,"t":["கானல் மேய்ந்து வியன்புலத் தல்கும்","புல்வாய் இரலை நெற்றி யன்ன,","பொலம் இலங்கு சென்னிய பாறுமயிர் அவியத்","தண்பனி உறைக்கும் புலரா ஞாங்கர்,","மன்றப் பலவின் மால்வரைப் பொருந்தி, என்","தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை தெளிர்ப்ப ஒற்றி,","இருங்கலை ஓர்ப்ப இசைஇக், காண்வரக்,","கருங்கோற் குறிஞ்சி அடுக்கம் பாடப்,","புலிப்பற் றாலிப் புன்றலைச் சிறா அர்","மான்கண் மகளிர், கான்தேர் அகன்று உவா","சிலைப்பாற் பட்ட முளவுமான் கொழுங்குறை,","விடர்முகை அடுக்கத்துச் சினைமுதிர் சாந்தம்,","புகர்முக வேழத்து முருப்பொடு, மூன்றும்,","இருங்கேழ் வயப்புலி வரி அதள் குவைஇ,","விரிந்து இறை நல்கும் நாடன், எங்கோன்,","கழல்தொடி ஆஅய் அண்டிரன் போல,","வண்மையும் உடையையோ? ஞாயிறு!","கொன்விளங் குதியால் விசும்பி னானே!"],"p":"","r":"At dawn in the cold dew, his thin hair damp like the gold-lit forehead of a blackbuck, leaning on the hill by the jackfruit of the common ground, the drummer sang the kurinji melody so that the stags listened, in the land where tiger-toothed boys and deer-eyed women heap porcupine meat, ripe sandal and elephant tusks on tiger skins as tribute; and he asks the sun whether it too has the generosity of Ay Andiran, or whether it shines in the sky for nothing.","n":"The standard praise likens a giver to the sun or rain; this poem turns the measure round and finds the sun wanting. The colophon's பூவைநிலை usually names comparison of a king to a god; here the comparison is put as a challenge to the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 374","lb":"PN374","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேர் முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பூவைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பூவைநிலை"],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["இரலை","இரலை",2,"maa","simile","blackbuck"],["பனி","பனி",4,"pira","scene","dew"],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",6,"parai","scene",""],["இருங்கலை","கலை",7,"maa","scene","stag"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",8,"yaazh","scene","kurinji melody"],["புலிப்பற்","புலிப் பல்",9,"maa","ornament","tiger tooth"],["முளவுமான்","முளவு மான்",11,"maa","gift_food","porcupine"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",12,"maram","gift_food","sandal"],["வேழத்து","வேழம்",13,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["வயப்புலி","வயப் புலி",14,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய் அண்டிரன்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:375":{"book":"purananuru","num":373,"t":["அலங்குகதிர் சுமத்த கலங்கற் சூழி","நிலைதளர்வு தொலைந்த ஒல்குநிலைப் பல்காற்","பொதியில் ஒருசிறை பள்ளி யாக","முழாவரைப் போந்தை அரவாய் மாமடல்","நாரும் போழும் கிணையோடு சுருக்கி,","ஏரின் வாழ்நர் குடிமுறை புகாஅ,","‘ஊழ் இரந்து உண்ணும் உயவல் வாழ்வைப்","புரவுஎதிர்ந்து கொள்ளும் சான்றோர் யார்?’ எனப்","புரசம் தூங்கும் அறாஅ யாணர்,","வரையணி படப்பை, நன்னாட்டுப் பொருந!","பொய்யா ஈகைக் கழல்தொடி ஆஅய்!","யாவரும் இன்மையின் கிணைப்பத், தாவது,","பெருமழை கடல்பரந் தாஅங்கு, யானும்","ஒருநின் உள்ளி வந்தனென்; அதனால்","புலவர் புக்கில் ஆகி, நிலவரை","நிலீ இயர் அத்தை, நீயே! ஒன்றே","நின்னின்று வறுவிது ஆகிய உலகத்து,","நிலவன் மாரோ, புரவலர்! துன்னிப்,","பெரிய ஓதினும் சிறிய உணராப்","பீடின்று பெருகிய திருவின்,","பாடில், மன்னரைப் பாடன்மார் எமரே!"],"p":"","r":"Lodging in a corner of a ruined common hall with leaning pillars, his drum bundled with palmyra fibre and strips, never entering the ploughmen's households and asking who will take on his wretched life of begging by turns, the drummer came to Ay of unfailing giving thinking of him alone, as great rain spreads over the sea; so may Ay live on earth as the poets' home, and in a world empty of him let there be no patrons, lest our people sing kings of meritless wealth who understand nothing.","n":"The close is a curse on the alternative more than a blessing: if Ay goes, the poet would rather there were no patrons than have his people praise kings who, told great things, grasp not even small ones. Lines 17 and 18 can be read otherwise, and the reading of நிலவன்மாரோ புரவலர் is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","inmai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 375","lb":"PN375","poet":"உறையூர் ஏணிச்சேரி முடமோசியார்","pat":"ஆய் அண்டிரன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["போந்தை","போந்தை",4,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["கிணையோடு","கிணை",5,"parai","scene",""],["ஏரின் வாழ்நர்","ஏரின் வாழ்நர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["புரசம்","புரசம்",9,"maram","scene",""],["பெருமழை","பெரு மழை",13,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:376":{"book":"purananuru","num":374,"t":["விசும்பு நீத்தம் இறந்த ஞாயிற்றுப்","பசுங்கதிர் மழுகிய சிவந்துவாங்கு அந்தி","சிறுநனி பிறந்த பின்றைச், செறிபிணிச்","சிதாஅர் வள்பின்என் தடாரி தழீஇப்,","பாணர் ஆரும் அளவை, யான்தன்","யாணர் நல்மனைக் கூட்டு முதல் நின்றனென்!","இமைத்தோர் விழித்த மாத்திரை, ஞெரேரெனக்,","குணக்கு எழு திங்கள் கனைஇருள் அகற்றப்,","பண்டுஅறி வாரா உருவோடு, என் அரைத்","தொன்றுபடு துளையொடு பருஇழை போகி,","நைந்துகரை பறைந்தஎன் உடையும் நோக்கி,","‘விருந்தினன் அளியன், இவன்’ எனப், பெருந்தகை","நின்ற முரற்கை நீக்கி, நன்றும்","அரவுவெகுண் டன்ன தேறலொடு. சூடுதருபு,","நிரயத் தன்னஎன் வறன்களைந் தன்றே,","இரவி னானே, ஈத்தோன் எந்தை;","அற்றை ஞான்றினோடு இன்றின் ஊங்கும்,","இரப்பச் சிந்தியேன், நிரப்படு புணையின்;","உளத்தின் அளக்கும் மிளிர்ந்த தகையேன்;","நிறைக்குளப் புதவின் மகிழ்ந்தனெ னாகி,","ஒருநாள், இரவலர் வரையா வள்ளியோர் கடைத்தலை,","ஞாங்கர் நெடுமொழி பயிற்றித்,","தோன்றல் செல்லாது, என் சிறுகிணைக் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"Just after sunset the drummer, holding his drum with its torn straps, stood at the granary of the patron's rich house; as the moon rose and drove off the dark, the great one saw his strange gaunt shape and his cloth worn to holes, called him a pitiable guest, stopped his song, and that same night gave him toddy fierce as an angry snake and roast meat and cleared his hell-like want; since that day he has not thought of begging, and his little drum has never sounded at the gates of other givers.","n":"The gift is praised by its consequence: one night's giving ended a career of asking. The patron interrupts the song before it is sung (நின்ற முரற்கை நீக்கி), so the giving is not a payment for praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","iravu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 376","lb":"PN376","poet":"புறத்திணை நன்னாகனார்","pat":"ஓய்மான் நல்லியாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["தடாரி","தடாரி",4,"parai","scene",""],["தேறலொடு","தேறல்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சூடுதருபு","சூடு",14,"unavu","gift_food","roast meat"],["கிணைக்","கிணை",23,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:377":{"book":"purananuru","num":375,"t":["பனி பழுநிய பல் யாமத்துப்","பாறு தலை மயிர் நனைய,","இனிது துஞ்சும் திருநகர் வரைப்பின்,","இனையல் அகற்ற என் கிணைதொடாக் குறுகி,","‘அவி உணவினோர் புறங் காப்ப,","அற, நெஞ்சத்தோன் வாழ, நாள்’ என்று,","அதற் கொண்டு வரல் ஏத்திக்","கரவு “இல்லாக் கவிவண் கையான்,","வாழ்க!” எனப் பெயர் பெற்றோர்","பிறர்க்கு உவமம் தான் அல்லது,","தனக்கு உவமம் பிறர் இல், என","அது நினைத்து, மதி மழுகி,","அங்கு நின்ற எற் காணூஉச்","‘சேய் நாட்டுச் செல் கிணைஞனை!","நீபுரவலை எமக்கு’ என்ன,","மலைபயந்த மணியும், கடறுபயந்த பொன்னும்,","கடல் பயந்த கதிர் முத்தமும்,","வேறுபட்ட உடையும், சேறுபட்ட தசும்பும்,","கனவிற் கண்டாங்கு, வருந்தாது நிற்ப,","நனவின் நல்கியோன், நகைசால் தோன்றல்;","நாடுஎன மொழிவோர் அவன் நாடென மொழிவோர்","வேந்தென மொழிவோர், ‘அவன் வேந்தென மொழிவோர்",". . . . . பொற்கோட்டு யானையர்","கவர் பரிக் கச்சை நன்மான்","வடி மணி வாங்கு உருள",". . . . நல்தேர்க் குழுவினர்,","கத ழிசை வன்க ணினர்,","வாளின் வாழ்நர், ஆர்வமொடு ஈண்டிக்,","கடல் ஒலி கொண்ட தானை","அடல்வெங் குருசில்! மன்னிய நெடிதே!"],"p":"","r":"In the dewy watches of the night, his hair wet, the drummer came to the palace where the king slept and sang 'may the gods guard him, may the righteous one live'; the king, whom men call the one comparison for others with none for himself, saw him and called the far-travelling drummer his patron, and gave him, as if in a dream but awake, gems from the hills, gold from the forests, pearls from the sea, fine cloth and pots of toddy; and the poet blesses the victorious lord of the sea-loud army to last long.","n":"Lines 10 and 11 state a theory of praise inside a praise poem: the king is the standard others are compared to, and nothing is his standard. The king's calling the bard 'patron to us' (நீ புரவலை எமக்கு) inverts the roles of giver and receiver. Two lines are broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 377","lb":"PN377","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"சோழன் இராசசூயம் வேட்ட பெருநற் கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பனி","பனி",1,"pira","scene","dew"],["கிணைதொடாக்","கிணை",4,"parai","scene",""],["மணியும்","மணி",16,"pira","gift_food","gem"],["பொன்னும்","பொன்",16,"pira","gift_food",""],["முத்தமும்","முத்தம்",17,"pira","gift_food","pearl"],["தசும்பும்","தசும்பு",18,"unavu","gift_food","toddy pot"],["யானையர்","யானை",23,"maa","scene",""],["நன்மான்","நல் மான்",24,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:378":{"book":"purananuru","num":376,"t":["தென் பரதவர் மிடல் சாய,","வட வடுகர் வாள் ஓட்டிய","தொடையமை கண்ணித் திருந்துவேல் தடக்கைக்,","கடுமா கடை இய விடுபரி வடிம்பின்,","நற்றார்க் கள்ளின், சோழன் கோயில்,","புதுப்பிறை யன்ன சுதைசெய் மாடத்துப்,","பனிக்கயத் தன்ன நீள்நகர் நின்று, என்","அரிக்கூடு மாக்கிணை இரிய ஒற்றி,","எஞ்சா மரபின் வஞ்சி பாட,","எமக்கென வகுத்த அல்ல, மிகப்பல,","மேம்படு சிறப்பின் அருங்கல வெறுக்கை","தாங்காது பொழிதந் தோனே; அது கண்டு,","இலம்பாடு உழந்தஎன் இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல்,","விரல்செறி மரபின செவித்தொடக் குநரும்,","செவித்தொடர் மரபின விரற்செறிக் குநரும்,","அரைக்கமை மரபின மிடற்றியாக் குநரும்,","மிடற்று அமை மரபின அரைக்கு யாக்குநரும்,","கடுந்தெறல் இராமன் உடன்புணர் சீதையை","வலித்தகை அரக்கன் வெளவிய ஞான்றை,","நிலஞ்சேர் மதர் அணி கண்ட குரங்கின்","செம்முகப் பெருங்கிளை இழைப்பொலிந் தா அங்கு,","அறாஅ அருநகை இனிதுபெற் றிகுமே,","இருங்குளைத் தலைமை எய்தி,","அரும்படர் எவ்வம் உழந்ததன் தலையே."],"p":"","r":"In the Chola's palace, with stucco terraces like the new moon and a long hall cool as a pool, the drummer sang the vanji theme and the king showered on him more precious jewels than were ever meant for such as he; his long-starved kin put finger rings on their ears and ear rings on their fingers, waist chains on their necks and neck chains on their waists, like the red-faced monkey clan that found the jewels Sita dropped when the demon carried her from Rama, and had unending laughter; after great suffering he has become head of a large clan.","n":"The Ramayana is cited for comedy: the monkeys decking themselves with Sita's jewels are the bard's family who have never owned ornaments. It is one of the few Sangam references to the Rama story, and it presupposes that the jewel episode was well known to the audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 378","lb":"PN378","poet":"ஊன்பொதி பசுங்குடையார்","pat":"சோழன் செரப்பாழி எறிந்த இளஞ்சேட் சென்னி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["கடுமா","கடு மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",5,"unavu","scene",""],["பனிக்கயத்","கயம்",7,"pira","simile","pool"],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",8,"parai","scene",""],["குரங்கின்","குரங்கு",20,"maa","simile","monkey"]],"en":[["king","சோழன்",5],["king","இராமன்",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:379":{"book":"purananuru","num":377,"t":["யானே பெறுக, அவன் தாள்நிழல் வாழ்க்கை;","அவனே பெறுக, என் நாஇசை நுவறல்;","நெல்லரி தொழுவர் கூர்வாள் மழுங்கின்,","பின்னை மறத்தோடு அரியக், கல்செத்து,","அள்ளல் யாமைக் கூன்புறத்து உரிஞ்சும்","நெல்லமல் புரவின் இலங்கை கிழவோன்","வில்லி யாதன் கிணையேம்; பெரும!","குறுந்தாள் ஏற்றைக் கொளுங்கண் அவ்விளர்!","நறுநெய் உருக்கி, நாட்சோறு ஈயா,","வல்லன், எந்தை, பசிதீர்த்தல்’ எனக்,","கொன்வரல் வாழ்க்கைநின் கிணைவன் கூறக்,","கேட்டதற் கொண்டும் வேட்கை தண்டாது.","விண்தோய் தலைய குன்றம் பிற்பட,","‘ . . . . ரவந்தனென், யானே-","தாயில் தூவாக் குழவிபோல, ஆங்கு அத்","திருவுடைத் திருமனை, ஐதுதோன்று கமழ்புகை","வருமழை மங்குலின் மறுகுடன் மறைக்கும்","குறும்படு குண்டகழ் நீள்மதில் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"Let me have life in the shade of his feet and let him have my tongue's praise: hearing your drummer say 'we are drummers of Villiyathan, lord of Ilangai of rich paddy, where reapers whose sickles blunt whet them on the curved back of a mud tortoise taking it for a stone; our lord melts fragrant ghee and gives daily rice and knows how to end hunger', my longing did not abate, and leaving the sky-touching hills behind I came like a child to its mother to that rich town whose fragrant smoke veils the streets like rain-mist.","n":"The poem is built on report: the patron's fame reaches the poet through another drummer's speech, which the poem quotes, so one bard's praise becomes another bard's road. The tortoise mistaken for a whetstone makes the land's fertility comic and exact. Line 8 is obscure and line 14 broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 379","lb":"PN379","poet":"புறத்திணை நன்னாகனார்","pat":"ஓய்மான்வில்லியாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில்"],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லரி","நெல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["யாமைக்","யாமை",5,"pira","scene","tortoise"],["ஏற்றைக்","ஏற்றை",8,"maa","gift_food","boar"],["நறுநெய்","நெய்",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நாட்சோறு","சோறு",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மங்குலின்","மங்குல்",17,"pira","simile","mist"]],"en":[["town","இலங்கை",6],["patron","வில்லி யாதன்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:380":{"book":"purananuru","num":378,"t":["தென் பவ்வத்து முத்துப் பூண்டு","வட குன்றத்துச் சாந்தம் உரீ இ.",". . . . . . . ங்கடல் தானை,","இன்னிசைய விறல் வென்றித்,","தென் னவர் வய மறவன்,","மிசைப் பெய்தநீர் கடல்பரந்து முத்தாகுந்து,","நாறிதழ்க் குளவியொடு கூதளம் குழைய,","தேறுபெ. . . . . . . . த்துந்து,","தீஞ்சுளைப் பலவின் நாஞ்சிற் பொருநன்;","துப்புஎதிர்ந் தோர்க்கே உள்ளாச் சேய்மையன்;","நட்புஎதிர்ந் தோர்க்கே அங்கை நண்மையன்;","வல்வேல் கந்தன் நல்லிசை யல்ல,",". . . த்தார்ப் பிள்ளையஞ் சிறாஅர்;","அன்னன் ஆகன் மாறே, இந்நிலம்","இலம்படு காலை ஆயினும்,","புலம்பல்போ யின்று, பூத்தஎன் கடும்பே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Nanjil of the sweet jackfruit, a strong warrior of the Pandya, who wears pearls of the southern sea and sandal of the northern hill, in whose land rain becomes pearl in the sea and fragrant wild flowers flourish, is far beyond thought to those who meet him in enmity and close as the palm to those who come in friendship; because he is such, though this land should fall into want, the loneliness of the poet's family is gone and it has blossomed.","n":"Three lines are broken (3, 8, 13), and line 12 mentions a Kandan of the strong spear whose relation to the patron is lost. The colophon fields were filled from Duraisamy Pillai's edition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","natpu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 380","lb":"PN380","poet":"கருவூர்க் கதப்பிள்ளை","pat":"நாஞ்சில் வள்ளுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["முத்துப்","முத்து",1,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",2,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",7,"maram","scene",""],["கூதளம்","கூதளம்",7,"maram","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தென் னவர்",5],["patron","நாஞ்சிற் பொருநன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:19"},"pn:381":{"book":"purananuru","num":379,"t":["ஊனும் ஊணும் முனையின், இனிதெனப்,","பாலிற் பெய்தவும், பாகிற் கொண்டவும்","அளவுபு கலந்து, மெல்லிது பருகி,","விருந்து உறுத்து, ஆற்ற இருந்தென மாகச்,","‘சென்மோ, பெரும! எம் விழவுடை நாட்டு?’ என,","யாம்தன் அறியுநமாகத்’ தான் பெரிது","அன்புடை மையின், எம்பிரிவு அஞ்சித்","துணரியது கொளாஅ வாகிப், பழம்ஊழ்த்துப்,","பயம்பகர் வறியா மயங்கரில் முதுபாழ்ப்","பெயல்பெய் தன்ன, செல்வத்து ஆங்கண்","ஈயா மன்னர் புறங்கடைத் தோன்றிச்,","சிதாஅர் வள்பின் சிதர்ப்புறத் தடாரி","ஊன்சுகிர் வலந்த தெண்கண் ஒற்றி,","விரல்விசை தவிர்க்கும் அரலையில் பாணியின்,","இலம்பாடு அகற்றல் யாவது? புலம்பொடு","தெருமரல் உயக்கமும் தீர்க்குவெம்; அதனால்,","இருநிலம் கூலம் பாறக், கோடை","வருமழை முழக்கு இசைக்கு ஓடிய பின்றைச்,","சேயை யாயினும், இவணை யாயினும்","இதற்கொண்டு அறிநை; வாழியோ, கிணைவ!","சிறுநனி, ஒருவழிப் படர்க’ என் றோனே - எந்தை,","ஒலிவெள் அருவி வேங்கட நாடன்;","உறுவரும் சிறுவரும் ஊழ்மாறு உய்க்கும்","அறத்துறை அம்பியின் மான, மறப்பின்று,","இருங்கோள் ஈராப் பூட்கைக்","கரும்பன் ஊரன் காதல் மகனே!"],"p":"","r":"After the bards had feasted long on meat and sweet milk-and-jaggery drinks and asked leave to go home, the chief of Venkatam, the son of the Karumban town man, afraid of their parting, told the kinai player that he would end his poverty and set him a sign to come back by after the summer rains, which the poet praises as a ferry that carries great and small in turn.","n":"The chief fears the bard's leaving (எம்பிரிவு அஞ்சி), the word akam uses for lovers' separation, so the host-guest bond is spoken of as a bond of love. The opening quotation marks are unbalanced, so where the chief's speech begins (line 5 or line 15) is uncertain, and the simile of rain on a barren tangle (lines 8 to 10) may describe the wasted wealth of misers or the bard's wasted waiting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","virundhu","anbu","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 381","lb":"PN381","poet":"புறத்திணை நன்னகனார்","pat":"கரும்பனூர் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன் மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஊனும்","ஊன்",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பாலிற்","பால்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பாகிற்","பாகு",2,"unavu","gift_food","jaggery syrup"],["தடாரி","தடாரி",12,"parai","scene",""],["கூலம்","கூலம்",17,"unavu","scene",""],["கிணைவ","கிணைவன்",20,"seythi","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",22,"pira","scene",""],["அம்பியின்","அம்பி",24,"pira","simile","ferry boat"]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கட",22],["patron","கரும்பன் ஊரன்",26]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:382":{"book":"purananuru","num":380,"t":["கடல் படை அடல் கொண்டி,","மண் டுற்ற மலிர் நோன்றாள்,","தண் சோழ நாட்டுப் பொருநன்,","அலங்கு உளை அணி இவுளி","நலங் கிள்ளி நசைப் பொருநரேம்;","பிறர்ப் பாடிப் பெறல் வேண்டேம்","அவற் பாடுதும், ‘அவன் தாள் வாழிய!’ என!","நெய் குய்ய ஊன் நவின்ற","பல்சோற்றான், இன் சுவைய","நல் குரவின் பசித் துன்பின் நின்","முன்நாள் விட்ட மூதறி சிறா அரும்,","யானும், ஏழ்மணி யங்கேள், அணிஉத்திக்,","கட்கேள்விக், சுவை நாவின்","நிறன் உற்ற, அரா அப் போலும்","வறன் ஒரீ இ, வழங்கு வாய்ப்ப,","விடுமதி அத்தை, கடுமான் தோன்றல்!","நினதே, முந்நீர் உடுத்த இவ் வியன் உலகு, அறிய;","எனதே, கிடைக்காழ் அன்ன தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை","கண்ணகத்து யாத்த நுண் அரிச் சிறுகோல்","எறிதொறும் நுடங்கி யாங்கு, நின் பகைஞர்","கேட்டொறும் நடுங்க, ஏத்துவென்,","வென்ற தேர், பிறர் வேத்தவை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The porunar declare they want only Nalankilli of the cool Chola land and will sing no other patron; fed and freed from hunger, the bard asks leave to go, saying the sea-girt world is the king's but his own part is to praise him in other kings' courts with his drum, so that his enemies tremble each time they hear it as the drumstick quivers each time it strikes.","n":"The bard sells a service, not only thanks: praise sung in rival courts is to frighten the king's enemies. Lines 10 to 15 (the children who left, the snake shedding its skin) are hard to construe and one edition reads line 10 differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","parisil","vendhan","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 382","lb":"PN382","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["இவுளி","இவுளி",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["நெய்","நெய்",8,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஊன்","ஊன்",8,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பல்சோற்றான்","சோறு",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",18,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","நலங் கிள்ளி",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:383":{"book":"purananuru","num":381,"t":["ஒண்பொறிச் சேவல் எடுப்ப ஏற்றெழுந்து,","தண்பனி உறைக்கும் புலரா ஞாங்கர்,","நுண்கோல் சிறுகிணை சிலம்ப ஒற்றி,","நெடுங்கடை நின்று, பகடுபல வாழ்த்தித்,","தன்புகழ் ஏத்தினெ னாக, ஊன்புலந்து,","அருங்கடி வியன்நகர்க் குறுகல் வேண்டிக்,","கூம்புவிடு மென்பிணி அவிழ்த்த ஆம்பல்,","தேம்பாய் உள்ள தம்கமழ் மடர் உளப்,","பாம்பு உரி அன்ன வடிவின, காம்பின்","கழைபடு சொலியின் இழை அணி வாரா.","ஒண்பூங் கலிங்கம் உடீ இ, நுண்பூண்","வசிந்துவாங்கு நுசுப்பின், அவ்வாங்கு உந்திக்,","கற்புடை மடந்தை தற்புறம் புல்ல,","எற் பெயர்ந்த நோக்கி . . . . .",". . . . கற்கொண்டு,","அழித்துப் பிறந்ததென னாகி, அவ்வழிப்,","பிறர், பாடுபுகழ் பாடிப் படர்பு அறி யேனே;","குறுமுலைக்கு அலமரும் பால் ஆர் வெண்மறி,","நரைமுக வூகமொடு, உகளும், சென. . .",". . . . . . கன்றுபல கெழீ இய","கான்கெழு நாடன், நெடுந்தேர் அவியன், என","ஒருவனை உடையேன் மன்னே, யானே;","அறான், எவன் பரிகோ, வெள்ளியது நிலையே?"],"p":"","r":"Rising at cockcrow in the cold dawn, the bard beat his small drum at the long gate, blessed the bullocks and praised the chief, who clothed him in cloth fine as a snake's slough; reborn as it were, he will sing no other patron, since he has one man, Aviyan of the tall chariot, and need not fear whatever the drought-star Venus portends.","n":"அழித்துப் பிறந்தனென் (line 16): the gift is felt as a second birth. The last line ties the bard's security to the patron against the omen of Venus, a motif this chapter repeats (384, 386, 388).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["parisil","eegai","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 383","lb":"PN383","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","scene","cock"],["சிறுகிணை","கிணை",3,"parai","scene",""],["பகடுபல","பகடு",4,"maa","scene","bullock"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",9,"pira","simile",""],["வெண்மறி","மறி",18,"maa","scene","kid"],["வூகமொடு","ஊகம்",19,"maa","scene","monkey"],["கன்றுபல","கன்று",20,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","அவியன்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:384":{"book":"purananuru","num":382,"t":["மென் பாலான் உடன் அணை இ,","வஞ்சிக் கோட்டு உறங்கும் நாரை","அறைக் கரும்பின் பூ அருந்தும்;","வன் பாலான் கருங்கால் வரகின்",". . .","அங்கண் குறுமுயல் வெருவ, அயல","கருங்கோட்டு இருப்பைப் பூஉறைக் குந்து;","விழவின் றாயினும், உழவர் மண்டை","இருங்கெடிற்று மிசையொடு பூங்கள் வைகுந்து;",". . . . . கிணையேம் பெரும!","நெல் என்னாம், பொன் என்னாம்,","கனற்றக் கொண்ட நறவு என்னும்,",". . . . மனை என்னா, அவை பலவும்","யான் தண்டவும், தான் தண்டான்,","நிணம் பெருத்த கொழுஞ் சோற்றிடை","மண் நாணப் புகழ் வேட்டு,","நீர் நாண நெய் வழங்கிப்,","புரந்தோன் எந்தை; யாம் எவன் தொலைவதை;","அன்னோனை உடையேம் என்ப; இனி வறட்கு","யாண்டு நிற்க வெள்ளி, மாண்ட","உண்ட நன்கலம் பெய்து நுடக்கவும்,","தின்ற நண் பல் ஊன் தோண்டவும்,","வந்த வைகல் அல்லது,","சென்ற எல்லைச் செலவு அறி யேனே!"],"p":"","r":"The kinai player describes the chief's land, where the heron sleeps on the vanji branch and the hare starts among the millet and farmers' bowls hold fish and toddy without a festival, and says that though he tried to stop him the chief kept giving paddy, gold and toddy, feeding with fat rice and ghee for fame; such a protector he had, so he knew only the day that came and never counted what was spent, whatever Venus foretold.","n":"The colophon's thurai, கையறுநிலை, makes this a lament for a dead patron; the poem itself has only the past tense புரந்தோன் and அன்னோனை உடையேம் என்ப, which allows it but never says he is dead. The opening sets the soft wet land (மென் பாலான்) against the hard dry land (வன் பாலான்), both rich.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","kaiyarunilai","kinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 384","lb":"PN384","poet":"புறத்திணை நன்னாகனார்","pat":"கரும்பனூர் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கையறுநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கையறுநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["வஞ்சிக்","வஞ்சி",2,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","scene",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",4,"maram","scene",""],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",6,"maa","scene",""],["இருப்பைப்","இருப்பை",7,"maram","scene",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["கெடிற்று","கெடிறு",9,"pira","scene","catfish"],["நெல்","நெல்",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நறவு","நறவு",12,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சோற்றிடை","சோறு",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நெய்","நெய்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஊன்","ஊன்",22,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:385":{"book":"purananuru","num":383,"t":["வெள்ளி தோன்றப், புள்ளுக்குரல் இயம்ப,","புலரி விடியல் பகடுபல வாழ்த்தித்,","தன்கடைத் தோன்றினும் இலனே; பிறன் கடை,","அகன்கண் தடாரிப் பாடுகேட்டு அருளி,","வறன்யான் நீங்கல் வேண்டி, என் அரை","நிலந்தினச் சிதைந்த சிதாஅர் களைந்து,","வெளியது உடீஇ, என் பசிகளைந் தோனே;","காவிரி அணையும் தாழ்நீர்ப் படப்பை","நெல்விளை கழனி அம்பர் கிழவோன்.","நல்அரு வந்தை, வாழியர்; புல்லிய","வேங்கட விறல்வரைப் பட்ட","ஓங்கல் வானத்து உறையினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The bard had not even come to his gate, but hearing his drum at another man's door at dawn, the lord of Ambar took pity, stripped off his earth-eaten rags, dressed him in white and ended his hunger; the poet wishes good Aruvanthai more years than the raindrops on Pulli's Venkatam.","n":"The gift came unasked: the chief heard the drum at someone else's gate and called the bard in. புல்லிய (line 10) can be read as 'of Pulli', naming the chief of Venkatam whom akam poets also name, or as an epithet; the link to Akananuru 61 takes the first reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 385","lb":"PN385","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"அம்பர் கிழான் அருவந்தை","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புள்ளுக்குரல்","புள்",1,"pul","scene",""],["பகடுபல","பகடு",2,"maa","scene","bullock"],["தடாரி","தடாரி",4,"parai","scene",""],["நெல்விளை","நெல்",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",8],["town","அம்பர்",9],["patron","அரு வந்தை",10],["hill","வேங்கட",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:386":{"book":"purananuru","num":384,"t":["நெடு நீர நிறை கயத்துப்","படு மாரித் துளி போல,","நெய் துள்ளிய வறை முகக்கவும்,","சூடு கிழித்து வாடுஊன் மிசையவும்,","ஊன் கொண்ட வெண் மண்டை","ஆன் பயத்தான் முற்று அழிப்பவும்,","வெய்து உண்ட வியர்ப்பு அல்லது,","செய் தொழிலான் வியர்ப்பு அறியாமை","ஈத்தோன் எந்தை, இசைதனது ஆக;","வயலே நெல்லின் வேலி, நீடிய கரும்பின்","பாத்திப் பன்மலர்ப் பூத்த துப்பின;","புறவே , புல்லருந்து பல்லா யத்தான்,","வில்இருந்த வெங்குறும் பின்று;","கடலே, கால்தந்த கலம் எண்ணுவோர்","கானற் புன்னைச் சினைநிலக் குந்து;","கழியே, சிறுவெள் உப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி,","பெருங்கல் நன்னாட்டு உமண்ஒலிக் குந்து;","அன்னநன் நாட்டுப் பொருநம், யாமே;","பொரா அப் பொருந ரேம்,","குணதிசை நின்று குடமுதற் செலினும்,","குட திசை நின்று குணமுதல் செலினும்,","வடதிசை நின்று தென்வயிற் செலினும்,","தென்திசை நின்று குறுகாது நீடினும்,","யாண்டும் நிற்க, வெள்ளி; யாம்","வேண்டியது உணர்ந்தோன் தாள்வா ழியவே!"],"p":"","r":"Killi Valavan gave so that his bards ate ghee-fried meat and milk food and knew the sweat of hot eating but never the sweat of work; his land holds paddy fields, herds with no bandits, ships counted under the punnai and salt traders on the backwaters, and his bards, who never fight, wish long life to the feet of the one who knew their want, whichever way Venus moves.","n":"The four lands (வயலே, புறவே, கடலே, கழியே) are listed as one king's territory, a whole landscape in a single puram catalogue. பொரா அப் பொருநரேம், porunar who do not fight, plays on பொருநர் as both bard and warrior.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 386","lb":"PN386","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளிவளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["மாரித்","மாரி",2,"pira","simile","rain"],["நெய்","நெய்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வாடுஊன்","ஊன்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஆன்","ஆன்",6,"unavu","gift_food","cow's milk"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",10,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",10,"maram","scene",""],["பல்லா","ஆ",12,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கலம்","கலம்",14,"pira","scene","ship"],["புன்னைச்","புன்னை",15,"maram","scene",""],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",16,"pira","scene",""],["உமண்","உமணர்",17,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:387":{"book":"purananuru","num":385,"t":["வள் உகிர வயல் ஆமை","வெள் அகடு கண் டன்ன,","வீங்கு விசிப் புதுப் போர்வைத்","தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை இயக்கி, “என்றும்","மாறு கொண்டோர் மதில் இடறி,","நீறு ஆடிய நறுங் கவுள,","பூம்பொறிப் பணை எருத்தின,","வேறு வேறு பரந்து இயங்கி,","வேந்துடை மிளை அயல் பரக்கும்,","ஏந்து கோட்டு இரும்பிணர்த் தடக்கைத்,","திருந்து தொழிற் பல பகடு","பகைப்புல மன்னர் பணிதிறை தந்து, நின்","நசைப்புல வாணர் நல்குரவு அகற்றி,","மிகப்பொலியர், தன் சேவடியத்தை !” என்று","யாஅன் இசைப்பின், நனிநன்று எனாப்,","பலபிற வாழ்த்த இருந்தோர் தங்கோன்!","மருவ இன்நகர் அகன் கடைத்தலைத்,","திருந்துகழல் சேவடி குறுகல் வேண்டி,","வென் றிரங்கும் விறன் முரசினோன்,","என் சிறுமையின், இழித்து நோக்கான்.","தன் பெருமையின் தகவு நோக்கிக்,","குன்று உறழ்ந்த களி றென்கோ;","கொய் யுளைய மா என்கோ?","மன்று நிறையும் நிரை என்கோ?","மனைக் களமரொடு களம் என்கோ?","ஆங்கவை கனவுஎன மருள, வல்லே, நனவின்","நல்கி யோனே, நகைசால் தோன்றல்;","ஊழி வாழி, பூழியர் பெருமகன்!","பிணர் மருப்பு யானைச் செருமிகு நோன்தாள்","செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வாழி யாதன்","ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர் உயர்குடை பணிந்து, இவன்","விடுவர் மாதோ நெடிதோ நில்லாப்","புல்லிளை வஞ்சிப் புறமதில் அலைக்கும்","கல்லென் பொருநை மணலினும், ஆங்கண்","பல்லூர் சுற்றிய கழனி","எல்லாம் விளையும் நெல்லினும் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The bard sang Chelvakkadungo Vazhiyathan as the king whose elephants break enemy walls and bring tribute that ends his supplicants' poverty; the king did not look down on the singer's smallness but gave by the measure of his own greatness elephants, horses, herds and fields so many that they seemed a dream given in waking, and the poet wishes him years more than the Porunai's sand and the paddy of its fields.","n":"The poem names its own principle of measure: the gift is sized by the giver's greatness, not by the receiver's smallness (lines 20, 21). The list of gifts asked as questions (என்கோ) recurs in 396.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 387","lb":"PN387","poet":"குண்டுகட் பாலியாதனார்","pat":"சேரமான் சிக்கற்பள்ளித் துஞ்சிய செல்வக்கடுங்கோ வாழியாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"வாழ்த்தியல்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["வாழ்த்தியல்"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஆமை","ஆமை",1,"pira","simile","tortoise"],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",4,"parai","scene",""],["பகடு","பகடு",11,"maa","scene","elephant"],["களி றென்கோ","களிறு",22,"maa","gift_food",""],["மா என்கோ","மா",23,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["நிரை","நிரை",24,"maa","gift_food","cattle herd"],["யானைச்","யானை",29,"maa","scene",""],["மணலினும்","மணல்",34,"pira","scene",""],["நெல்லினும்","நெல்",36,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","பூழியர் பெருமகன்",28],["king","செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வாழி யாதன்",30],["town","வஞ்சிப்",33],["river","பொருநை",34]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:388":{"book":"purananuru","num":386,"t":["வெள்ளி தென்புலத்து உறைய, விளைவயல்","பள்ளம், வாடிய பயன்இல் காலை,","இரும்பறைக் கிணைமகன் சென்றவன், பெரும்பெயர்","சிறுகுடி கிழான் பண்ணன் பொருந்தித்,","தன்நிலை அறியுநன் ஆக, அந்நிலை","இடுக்கண் இரியல் போக, உடைய","கொடுத்தோன் எந்தை, கொடைமேந் தோன்றல்,","நுண்ணூல் தடக்கையின் நாமருப் பாக,","வெல்லும் வாய்மொழிப் புல்லுடை விளைநிலம்","பெயர்க்கும் பண்ணற் கேட்டிரோ; அவன்","வினைப்பகடு ஏற்ற மேழிக் கிணைத்தொடா,","நாடொறும் பாடேன் ஆயின், ஆனா","மணிகிளர் முன்றில் தென்னவன் மருகன்,","பிணிமுரசு இரங்கும் பீடுகெழு தானை","அண்ணல் யானை வழுதி,","கண்மா றிலியர்என் பெருங்கிளைப் புரவே!"],"p":"","r":"When Venus stood in the south and the fields withered in a barren season, the drummer went to Pannan of Sirukudi, who saw his state and gave until his trouble fled; the bard swears that if he fails to sing Pannan every day, may the protection his kin have from Vazhuthi, the Pandya of the elephant army, fail.","n":"The closing oath stakes the protection of a Pandya king on the bard's loyalty to a village landholder, so the small patron's gift binds the bard as firmly as a king's. Lines 8 to 11 (the tongue as ploughshare, the plough and the drum) can be construed more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","mazhai","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 388","lb":"PN388","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"சிறுகுடிகிழான் பண்ணன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கிணைமகன்","கிணைமகன்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["வினைப்பகடு","பகடு",11,"maa","scene","bullock"],["மேழிக்","மேழி",11,"pira","scene","plough"],["முரசு","முரசு",14,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",15,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["town","சிறுகுடி",4],["patron","பண்ணன்",4],["king","தென்னவன் மருகன்",13],["king","வழுதி",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:389":{"book":"purananuru","num":387,"t":["‘நீர் நுங்கின் கண் வலிப்பக்","கான வேம்பின் காய் திரங்கக்,","கயங் களியும் கோடை ஆயினும்,","ஏலா வெண்பொன் போகுறு காலை,","எம்மும் உள்ளுமோ பிள்ளைஅம் பொருநன்!","என்றுஈத் தனனே, இசைசால் நெடுந்தகை;","இன்றுசென்று எய்தும் வழியனும் அல்லன்;","செலினே, காணா வழியனும் அல்லன்;","புன்தலை மடப்பிடி இனையக், கன்றுதந்து,","குன்றக நல்லூர் மன்றத்துப் பிணிக்கும்,","கல்லிழி அருவி வேங்கடங் கிழவோன்,","செல்வுழி எழாஅ நல்லேர் முதியன்!","ஆத னுங்கன் போல, நீயும்","பசித்த ஒக்கல் பழங்கண் வீட,","வீறுசால் நன்கலம் நல்குமதி, பெரும!","ஐதுஅகல் அல்குல் மகளிர்","நெய்தல் கேளன்மார், நெடுங்கடை யானே!"],"p":"","r":"Athanungan, lord of Venkatam where elephant calves are tied in the village common while their mothers grieve, gave to the young porunan asking only to be remembered when the drought dries the palmyra and the neem; the bard asks the patron he now addresses to give like Athanungan and end his family's hunger, so that the women of his house never hear the dirge at his gate.","n":"The giver asks the receiver to remember him (எம்மும் உள்ளுமோ), so the patron himself treats the bard's memory as the return on the gift. The colophon names Athanungan as patron, though the poem holds him up as a model to a second patron it does not name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ninaivu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 389","lb":"PN389","poet":"கள்ளில் ஆத்திரையனார்","pat":"ஆதனுங்கன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",1,"unavu","scene","palmyra fruit"],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",9,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["கன்றுதந்து","கன்று",9,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["அருவி","அருவி",11,"pira","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",17,"yaazh","scene","funeral tune"]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கடங்",11],["patron","முதியன்",12],["patron","ஆத னுங்கன்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:390":{"book":"purananuru","num":388,"t":["அறவை நெஞ்சத்து ஆயர், வளரும்","மறவை நெஞ்சத்து தாய்இ லாளர்,","அரும்பலர் செருந்தி நெடுங்கான் மலர்கமழ்,","விழவணி வியன்களம் அன்ன முற்றத்து,","ஆர்வலர் குறுகின் அல்லது, காவலர்","கடவிலும் குறுகாக் கடியுடை வியன்நகர்,","மலைக்கணத்து அன்ன மாடம் சிலம்ப, வென்","அரிக்குரல் தடாரி இரிய ஒற்றிப்","பாடி நின்ற பன்னாள் அன்றியும்,","சென்ற ஞான்றைச் சென்றுபடர் இரவின்","வந்ததற் கொண்டு,’ நெடுங்கடை நின்ற","புன்தலைப் பொருநன் அளியன் தான்’ எனத்,","தன்உழைக் குறுகல் வேண்டி, என்அரை","முதுநீர்ப் பாசி அன்ன உடைகளைந்து,","திருமலர் அன்ன புதுமடிக் கொளீஇ,","மகிழ்தரல் மரபின் மட்டே அன்றியும்,","அமிழ்தன மரபின் ஊன்துவை அடிசில்","வெள்ளி வெண்கலத்து ஊட்டல் அன்றி,","முன்னூர்ப் பொதியில் சேர்ந்த மென்நடை","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல் பெரும்புலம்பு அகற்ற,","அகடுநனை வேங்கை வீகண் டன்ன","பகடுதரு செந்நெல் போரொடு நல்கிக்,","‘கொண்டி பெறுக!’ என் றோனே; உண்துறை","மலைஅலர் அணியும் தலைநீர் நாடன்,","கண்டார் கொண்டுமனை திருந்தடி வாழ்த்தி,",". . . . . . . . . . . . . . .","வான்அறி யலவென் பாடுபசி போக்கல்;","அண்ணல் யானை வேந்தர்","உண்மையோ, அறியலர், காண்பறி யலரே!"],"p":"","r":"At the guarded house that only well-wishers approach, the poet beat her drum for days; on the night she arrived the chief called the poor bard in, stripped the rag like old pond moss from her waist, dressed her in new cloth, served toddy and meat rice in silver vessels, sent red paddy in stacks to her family resting at the village common and told her to take it as plunder; kings with elephants do not even know such feeding.","n":"கொண்டி, the word for war booty, is used for a gift: the bard is told to carry off the paddy as soldiers carry off plunder. The closing sets the chief above crowned kings, who cannot know such hunger-ending. The colophon gives Athiyaman; the poem does not name him, and lines 1 and 2 and the gap at line 26 are hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 390","lb":"PN390","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",3,"maram","scene",""],["தடாரி","தடாரி",8,"parai","scene",""],["பாசி","பாசி",14,"pira","simile","moss"],["ஊன்துவை","ஊன்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",21,"maram","simile",""],["பகடுதரு","பகடு",22,"maa","scene","bullock"],["செந்நெல்","நெல்",22,"unavu","gift_food",""],["யானை","யானை",28,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:391":{"book":"purananuru","num":389,"t":["தண்துளி பலபொழிந்து எழிலி இசைக்கும்","விண்டு அனைய விண்தோய் பிறங்கல்","முகடுற உயர்ந்த நெல்லின் மகிழ்வரப்","பகடுதரு பெருவளம் வாழ்த்திப் பெற்ற","திருந்தா மூரி பரந்துபடக் கெண்டி,","அரியல் ஆர்கையர் உண்டு இனிது உவக்கும்","வேங்கட வரைப்பின் வடபுலம் பசித்தென,","ஈங்குவந்து இறுத்தஎன் இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல்","தீர்கை விடுக்கும் பண்பின் முதுகுடி","நனந்தலை மூதூர் வினவலின்,","‘முன்னும் வந்தோன் மருங்கிலன், இன்னும்","அளியன் ஆகலின், பொருநன் இவன்’ என,","நின்னுணர்ந்து அறியுநர் என்உணர்ந்து கூறக்,","காண்கு வந்திசிற் பெரும, மாண்தக","இருநீர்ப் பெருங்கழி நுழைமீன் அருந்தும்","ததைந்த தூவியம் புதாஅஞ் சேக்கும்","துதைந்த புன்னைச் செழுநகர் வரைப்பின்,","நெஞ்சமர் காதல் நின்வெய் யோளொடு,","இன்துயில் பெறுகதில் நீயே; வளஞ்சால்","துளிபதன் அறிந்து பொழிய,","வேலி ஆயிரம் விளைக நின் வயலே!"],"p":"","r":"When the northern land of Venkatam, rich in paddy and toddy feasts, fell into famine, the bard brought his large family south and asked in the old town for the house that relieves want; those who knew the chief recognised him as the poor porunan who had come before, so he has come to see the chief, and wishes him sweet sleep with his wife in his house among the punnai where herons roost, and fields that yield a thousandfold.","n":"The bard's credentials are his known poverty: those who know the chief vouch for him because he came before and is still poor (lines 11 to 13). The blessing of rain at its time (துளிபதன் அறிந்து) answers the famine that drove him south.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","parisil","inmai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 391","lb":"PN391","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"பொறையாற்றுக் கிழான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["பகடுதரு","பகடு",4,"maa","scene","bullock"],["மூரி","மூரி",5,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["அரியல்","அரியல்",6,"unavu","scene",""],["நுழைமீன்","மீன்",15,"pira","scene",""],["புதாஅஞ்","புதா",16,"pul","scene","heron"],["புன்னைச்","புன்னை",17,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கட",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:392":{"book":"purananuru","num":390,"t":["மதிஏர் வெண்குடை அதியர் கோமான்","கொடும்பூண் எழினி, நெடுங்கடை நின்று, யான்","பசலை நிலவின் பனிபடு விடியல்,","பொருகளிற்று அடிவழி யன்ன, என்கை","ஒருகண் மாக்கிணை ஒற்றுபு கொடாஅ,","‘உருகெழு மன்னர் ஆர்எயில் கடந்து,","நிணம்படு குருதிப் பெரும்பாட்டு ஈரத்து,","அணங்குடை மரபின் இருங்களந் தோறும்,","வெள்வாய்க் கழுதைப் புல்இனம் பூட்டி,","வெள்ளை வரகும் கொள்ளும் வித்தும்","வைகல் உழவ! வாழிய பெரிது’ எனச்","சென்றுயான் நின்றனெ னாக, அன்றே,","ஊருண் கேணிப் பகட்டுஇலைப் பாசி","வேர்புரை சிதாஅர் நீக்கி, நேர்கரை","நுண்ணூற் கலிங்கம் உடீஇ, உண்ம், எனத்","தேட்கடுப்பு அன்ன நாட்படு தேறல்","கோண்மீன் அன்ன பொலங்கலத்து அளைஇ,","ஊண்முறை ஈத்தல் அன்றியும் , கோண்முறை","விருந்திறை நல்கி யோனே - அந்தரத்து","அரும்பெறல் அமிழ்த மன்ன","கரும்புஇவண் தந்தோன் பெரும்பிறங் கடையே."],"p":"","r":"At dawn at the gate of Ezhini of the Athiyar, the poet beat her one-eyed drum and hailed him as the daily ploughman who yokes donkeys on the bloody fields of conquered forts and sows white varagu and horse-gram; that day he took off her rags, clothed her in fine cloth, poured aged toddy into golden cups and gave food and a guest's share, the descendant of the one who brought sugarcane here like heaven's ambrosia.","n":"The conqueror's destruction is sung as farming: donkeys plough the ruined fort and poor men's grains are sown where a city stood, a curse dressed as praise of his diligence (வைகல் உழவ). The sugarcane brought from heaven is the Athiyar house's own legend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 392","lb":"PN392","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"அதியமான் நெடுமான் அஞ்சி மகன் பொகுட்டெழினி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பொருகளிற்று","களிறு",4,"maa","simile",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",5,"parai","scene",""],["கழுதைப்","கழுதை",9,"maa","scene",""],["வரகும்","வரகு",10,"maram","scene",""],["கொள்ளும்","கொள்",10,"maram","scene","horse-gram"],["பாசி","பாசி",13,"pira","simile","moss"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",16,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கரும்புஇவண்","கரும்பு",21,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","அதியர் கோமான்",1],["king","எழினி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:393":{"book":"purananuru","num":391,"t":["பதிமுதற் பழகாப் பழங்கண் வாழ்க்கைக்","குறுநெடுந் துணையொடும் கூமை வீதலிற்,","குடிமுறை பாடி, ஒய்யென வருந்தி,","அடல்நசை மறந்தஎம் குழிசி மலர்க்கும்","கடனறி யாளர் பிறநாட்டு இன்மையின்","வள்ளன் மையின்எம் வரைவோர் யார்?’ என;","உள்ளிய உள்ளமொடு உலைநசை துணையா,","உலகம் எல்லாம் ஒருபாற் பட்டென,","மலர்தார் அண்ணல்நின் நல்லிசை உள்ளி,","ஈர்ங்கை மறந்தஎன் இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல்","கூர்ந்தஎவ் வம்வீடக், கொழுநிணம் கிழிப்பக்,","கோடைப் பருத்தி வீடுநிறை பெய்த","மூடைப் பண்டம் மிடைநிறைந் தன்ன,","வெண்நிண மூரி அருள, நாளுற","ஈன்ற அரவின் நாவுருக் கடுக்கும்என்","தொன்றுபடு சிதாஅர் துவர நீக்கிப்,","போதுவிரி பகன்றைப் புதுமலர் அன்ன,","அகன்றுமடி கலிங்கம் உடீஇச், செல்வமும்","கேடின்று நல்குமதி, பெரும! மாசில்","மதிபுரை மாக்கிணை தெளிர்ப்ப ஒற்றி,","ஆடுமகள் அல்குல் ஒப்ப வாடிக்,","‘கோடை யாயினும் கோடி . . . .","காவிரி புரக்கும் நன்னாட்டுப் பொருந!","வாய்வாள் வளவன் வாழ்க! எனப்","பீடுகெழு நோன்தாள் பாடுகம் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"Wandering with his kin, their pots long empty and no one in other lands who knows the duty of feeding them, the bard came thinking of Killi Valavan's fame and asks him to end his family's hunger with fat meat heaped like summer cotton bales, to strip his rags and dress him in cloth like a new pakanrai flower and give lasting wealth, promising to beat his moon-white drum and sing the sword-true Valavan of the Kaviri land.","n":"குடிமுறை பாடி (line 3): the bard's family sings lineages by inherited order, so asking is a hereditary office, not a lapse. Line 22 has a gap and is read differently in other editions.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pasi","inmai","parisil","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 393","lb":"PN393","poet":"நல்லிறையனார்","pat":"சோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["கொழுநிணம்","நிணம்",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பருத்தி","பருத்தி",12,"maram","simile",""],["மூரி","மூரி",14,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["அரவின்","அரவு",15,"pira","simile",""],["பகன்றைப்","பகன்றை",17,"maram","simile",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",20,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",23],["king","வளவன்",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:394":{"book":"purananuru","num":392,"t":["சிலையுலாய் நிமிர்ந்த சாந்துபடு மார்பின்,","ஒலிபுனற் கழனி வெண்குடைக் கிழவோன்,","வலிதுஞ்சு தடக்கை வாய்வாள் குட்டுவன்,","வள்ளிய னாதல் வையகம் புகழினும்!","உள்ளல் ஓம்புமின், உயர்மொழிப் புலவீர்!","யானும், இருள்நிலாக் கழிந்த பகல்செய் வைகறை,","ஒருகண் மாக்கிணை தெளிர்ப்ப ஒற்றிப்,","பாடுஇமிழ் முரசின் இயல்தேர்த் தந்தை","வாடா வஞ்சி பாடினேன் ஆக,","அகமலி உவகையொடு அணுகல் வேண்டிக்,","கொன்றுசினந் தணியாப் புலவுநாறு மருப்பின்","வெஞ்சின வேழம் நல்கினன் ; அஞ்சி","யான்அது பெயர்த்தனென் ஆகத், தான்அது","சிறிதென உணர்ந்தமை நாணிப், பிறிதும்ஓர்","பெருங்களிறு நல்கி யோனே; அதற்கொண்டு,","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல் பெரும்புலம்பு உறினும்,","‘துன்னரும் பரிசில் தரும்’ என,","என்றும் செல்லேன், அவன் குன்றுகெழு நாட்டே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet warns other poets not to set their hopes on Kuttuvan, however the world praises his generosity: when he sang the king's father's war-song, Kuttuvan gave him a killer elephant; when the terrified bard sent it back, the king, ashamed it had seemed too small, gave a bigger one; so the bard will never go to his land again, however his family suffers.","n":"Praise by mock complaint: the warning to poets is a boast that the gift outran anything a bard could use. The king reads the return of his gift as a judgment that it was too small (சிறிதென உணர்ந்தமை நாணி), so the giver's shame, not the receiver's need, sets the size of the second gift. வாடா வஞ்சி (line 9) names what the bard sang, a war-march song.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 394","lb":"PN394","poet":"கோனாட்டு எறிச்சலூர் மாடலன் மதுரைக் குமரனார்","pat":"சோழிய ஏனாதி திருக்குட்டுவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",7,"parai","scene",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",8,"parai","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",12,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",15,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[["patron","குட்டுவன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:395":{"book":"purananuru","num":393,"t":["மென் புலத்து வயல் உழவர்","வன் புலத்துப் பகடு விட்டுக்","குறு முயலின் குழைச் சூட்டொடு","நெடு வாளைப் பல் உவியல்","பழஞ் சோற்றுப் புக வருந்திப்,","புதல் தளவின் பூச் சூடி,","அரில் பறையாற் புள்ளோப்பி,","அவிழ் நெல்லின் அரியலா ருந்து;","மனைக் கோழிப் பைம்பயி ரின்னே,","கானக் கோழிக் கவர் குரலொடு,","நீர்க் கோழிக் கூப்பெயர்க் குந்து;","வே யன்ன மென் தோளால்,","மயில் அன்ன மென் சாயலார்,","கிளிகடி யின்னே;","அகல் அள்ளற் புள்இரீஇ யுந்து;","ஆங்கப் , பலநல்ல புலன் அணியும்","சீர்சான்ற விழுச் சிறப்பின்,","சிறுகண் யானைப் பெறலருந் தித்தன்","செல்லா நல்லிசை உறந்தைக் குணாது,","நெடுங்கை வேண்மான் அருங்கடிப் பிடவூர்","அறப்பெயர்ச் சாத்தன் கிளையேம், பெரும!","முன்நாள் நண்பகல் சுரன்உழந்து வருந்திக்,","கதிர்நனி சென்ற கனையிருள் மாலைத்,","தன்கடைத் தோன்றி, என் உறவு இசைத்தலின்,","தீங்குரல் . . கின் அரிக்குரல் தடாரியொடு,","ஆங்கு நின்ற எற் கண்டு,","சிறிதும் நில்லான், பெரிதுங் கூறான்,","அருங்கலம் வரவே அருளினன் வேண்டி,","ஐயென உரைத்தன்றி நல்கித், தன்மனைப்","பொன்போல் மடந்தையைக் காட்டி,’இவனை","என்போல் போற்று’ என் றோனே; அதற்கொண்டு,","அவன்மறவ லேனே, பிறர்உள்ள லேனே;","அகன் ஞாலம் பெரிது வெம்பினும்,","மிக வானுள் எரி தோன்றினும்,","குள மீனோடும் தாள் புகையினும்,","பெருஞ்செய் நெல்லின் கொக்குஉகிர் நிமிரல்","பசுங்கண் கருனைச் சூட்டொடு மாந்த,","‘விளைவுஒன்றோ வெள்ளம் கொள்க!’ என,","உள்ளதும் இல்லதும் அறியாது,","ஆங்குஅமைந் தன்றால்; வாழ்க, அவன் தாளே!"],"p":"","r":"After a picture of Pidavur's rich fields, where farmers eat old rice with hare and fish and women drive off parrots, the bard says he is of Sathan's kin: arriving at dusk after a day on the hot road, he was given rare vessels at once, without delay or long speech, and the chief showed his wife and told her to care for him as for himself; since then the bard thinks of no one else, for even in drought the house keeps feeding him.","n":"The chief makes his wife the bard's keeper (என்போல் போற்று), so the gift becomes a standing place in the household. The phrase 'not stopping, not saying much' (lines 27, 28) makes speed and few words part of the gift. The drought images of lines 33 to 35 show the giving does not depend on the season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","parisil","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 395","lb":"PN395","poet":"மதுரை நக்கீரர்","pat":"சோழநாட்டு பிடவூர்கிழார் மகன் பெருஞ்சாத்தன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["உழவர்","உழவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["பகடு","பகடு",2,"maa","scene","bullock"],["முயலின்","முயல்",3,"maa","scene",""],["வாளைப்","வாளை",4,"pira","scene","fish"],["தளவின்","தளவு",6,"maram","ornament",""],["பறையாற்","பறை",7,"parai","scene",""],["புள்ளோப்பி","புள்",7,"pul","scene",""],["மனைக் கோழிப்","கோழி",9,"pul","scene",""],["வே","வேய்",12,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["மயில்","மயில்",13,"pul","simile",""],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",14,"pul","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",18,"maa","scene",""],["தடாரியொடு","தடாரி",25,"parai","scene",""],["கொக்குஉகிர்","கொக்கு",36,"pul","simile",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",36,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கருனைச்","கருனை",37,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","தித்தன்",18],["town","உறந்தை",19],["town","பிடவூர்",20],["patron","சாத்தன்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:396":{"book":"purananuru","num":394,"t":["கீழ் நீரால் மீன் வழங்குந்து;","மீநீரான், கண்ணன்ன, மலர்பூக் குந்து;","கழி சுற்றிய விளை கழனி,","அரிப் பறையாற் புள் ளோப்புந்து;","நெடுநீர் தொகூஉம் மணல் தண்கான்","மென் பறையாற் புள் இரியுந்து;","நனைக் கள்ளின் மனைக் கோசர்","தீந் தேறல் நறவு மகிழ்ந்து","தீங் குரவைக் கொளைத்தாங் குந்து;","உள்ளி லோர்க்கு வலியா குவன்,","கேளி லோர்க்குக் கேளா குவன்","கழுமிய வென்வேல் வேளே;","வளநீர் வாட்டாற்று எழினி யாதன்","கிணை யேம், பெரும!","கொழுந் தடிய சூடு என்கோ?","வளநனையின் மட்டு என்கோ?","குறு முயலின் நிணம் பெய்தந்த","நறுநெய்ய சோறு என்கோ?","திறந்து மறந்து கூட்டு முதல்","முகந்து கொள்ளும் உணவு என்கோ?","அன்னவை பலபல . . .",". . . . வருந்திய","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல் அருந்தி எஞ்சிய","அளித்து உவப்ப, ஈத்தோன் எந்தை;","எம்மோர் ஆக்கக் கங்கு உண்டே;","மாரி வானத்து மீன் நாப்பண்,","விரி கதிர வெண் திங்களின்,","விளங்கித் தோன்றுக, அவன் கலங்கா நல்லிசை!","யாமும் பிறரும் வாழ்த்த, நாளும்","நிரைசால் நன்கலன் நல்கி,","உரைசெலச் சுரக்க அவன் பாடல்சால் வளனே!"],"p":"","r":"In Vattaru, where fish move in the low water and flowers like eyes bloom on the high, birds are driven off with drums and the Kosar dance the kuravai over toddy, Ezhiniyathan is strength to the weak and kin to the kinless; his kinai players list his roasts, toddy, hare-fat rice and open stores, say there is no bound to their wealth, and wish his fame to shine like the moon among the stars.","n":"'Strength to those without strength, kin to those without kin' (lines 10, 11) gives the chief's charity a social reach beyond bards. The list of foods in question form (என்கோ) is the same device as 387. Lines 21 and 22 have a gap.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","selvam","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 396","lb":"PN396","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழார்","pat":"வாட்டாற்று எழினியாதன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["மீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene","fish"],["மலர்பூக்","மலர்",2,"maram","simile",""],["அரிப் பறையாற்","பறை",4,"parai","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",7,"unavu","scene",""],["கோசர்","கோசர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["குரவைக்","குரவை",9,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["சூடு","சூடு",15,"unavu","gift_food","roast meat"],["மட்டு","மட்டு",16,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முயலின்","முயல்",17,"maa","gift_food",""],["சோறு","சோறு",18,"unavu","gift_food",""],["திங்களின்","திங்கள்",27,"pira","scene","moon"]],"en":[["town","வாட்டாற்று",13],["patron","எழினி யாதன்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:397":{"book":"purananuru","num":395,"t":["வெள்ளியும் இருவிசும்பு ஏர்தரும்; புள்ளும்","உயர்சினைக் குடம்பைக் குரல்தோற் றினவே;","பொய்கையும் போடுகண் விழித்தன; பையச்","சுடரும் சுருங்கின்று, ஒளியே; பாடெழுந்து","இரங்குகுரல் முரசமொடு வலம்புரி ஆர்ப்ப,","இரவுப் புறங்கண்ட காலைத் தோன்றி,","எஃகுஇருள் அகற்றும் ஏமப் பாசறை,","வைகறை அரவம் கேளியர்! பலகோள்","செய்தார் மார்ப! எழுமதி துயில்!’ எனத்,","தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை தெளிர்ப்ப ஒற்றி,","நெடுங்கடைத் தோன்றி யேனே; அது நயந்து,","‘உள்ளி வந்த பரிசிலன் இவன்’ என,","நெய்யுறப் பொரித்த குய்யுடை நெடுஞ்சூடு,","மணிக்கலன் நிறைந்த மணநாறு தேறல்,","பாம்புரித் தன்ன வான்பூங் கலிங்கமொடு,","மாரி யன்ன வண்மையின் சொரிந்து,","வேனில் அன்ன என் வெப்பு நீங்க,","அருங்கலம் நல்கி யோனே; என்றும்,","செறுவிற் பூத்த சேயிதழ்த் தாமரை,","அறுதொழில் அந்தணர் அறம்புரிந்து எடுத்த","தீயொடு விளங்கும் நாடன், வாய்வாள்","வலம்படு தீவிற் பொலம்பூண் வளவன்;","எறிதிரைப் பெருங்கடல் இறுதிக்கண் செலினும்,","தெறுகதிர்க் கனலி தென்திசைத் தோன்றினும்,","என்னென்று அஞ்சலம் யாமே; வென்வெல்","அருஞ்சமம் கடக்கும் ஆற்றல், அவன்","திருந்துகழல் நோன்தாள் தண்நிழ லேமே!"],"p":"","r":"At dawn in the guarded war camp, when Venus rose and birds called and drums and conch sounded, the bard beat his drum and bade the garlanded king wake; pleased, Valavan gave him fried meat, fragrant toddy, cloth like a snake's slough and rare jewels, pouring like rain to end his summer heat, so the bard fears nothing even if the sea runs dry or the sun rises in the south, living in the cool shade of his feet.","n":"The dawn song is a wake-up call in a war camp (ஏமப் பாசறை), so the bard serves the king's day as well as his fame. The final boast sets the patron's protection against cosmic upheaval, a claim of security that the ethics books would deny any wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 397","lb":"PN397","poet":"எருக்காட்டூர்த் தாயங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"கோழன் குளமுற்றத்துத் துஞ்சிய கிள்ளி வளவன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை; கடைநிலை விடையும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை","கடைநிலை விடை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",1,"pul","scene",""],["பொய்கையும்","பொய்கை",3,"pira","scene",""],["முரசமொடு","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",5,"pira","scene","conch"],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",10,"parai","scene",""],["நெடுஞ்சூடு","சூடு",13,"unavu","gift_food","roast meat"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பாம்புரித்","பாம்பு",15,"pira","simile",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",19,"maram","scene",""],["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",20,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வளவன்",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:398":{"book":"purananuru","num":396,"t":["மதிநிலாக் கரப்ப, வெள்ளி ஏர்தர,","வகைமாண் நல்லில் . . . . .","பொறிமலர் வாரணம் பொழுது அறிந்து இயம்ப,","பொய்கைப் பூமுகை மலரப், பாணர்","கைவல் சீறியாழ் கடன் அறிந்து இயக்க,","இரவுப் புறம் பெற்ற ஏம வைகறைப்,","பரிசிலர் வரையா விரைசெய் பந்தர்","வரிசையின் இறுத்த வாய்மொழி வஞ்சன்,","நகைவர் குறுகின் அல்லது, பகைவர்க்குப்","புலியினம் மடிந்த கல்லளை போலத்,","துன்னல் போகிய பெரும்பெயர் மூதூர்,","மதியத்து அன்னஎன் அரிக்குரல் தடாரி,","இரவுரை நெடுவார் அரிப்ப வட்டித்து,","‘உள்ளி வருநர் கொள்கலம் நிறைப்போய்!","‘தள்ளா நிலையை யாகியர் எமக்கு’ என,","என்வரவு அறீஇச்,","சிறி திற்குப் பெரிது உவந்து,","விரும்பிய முகத்த னாகி, என் அரைத்","துரும்புபடு சிதாஅர் நீக்கித், தன் அரைப்","புகைவிரிந் தன்ன பொங்குதுகில் உடீஇ,","அழல்கான் றன்ன அரும்பெறல் மண்டை,","நிழல்காண் தேறல் நிறைய வாக்கி,","யான்உண அருளல் அன்றியும், தான்உண்","மண்டைய கண்ட மான்வறைக் கருனை,","கொக்குஉகிர் நிமிரல் ஒக்கல் ஆர,","வரையுறழ் மார்பின், வையகம் விளக்கும்,","விரவுமணி ஒளிர்வரும், அரவுஉறழ் ஆரமொடு,","புரையோன் மேனிப் பூந்துகில் கலிங்கம்.","உரைசெல அருளி யோனே;","பறைஇசை அருவிப் பாயல் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"At dawn, as the moon hid and Venus rose, cocks cried and bards played their lutes, the poet came to Vanjan of true word, whose old town is open to friends and shut to enemies like a cave where tigers sleep, and beat his drum asking that the king stay unfailing for them; glad at the small offering, the king took off the bard's rags, dressed him in cloth like rising smoke, filled a bowl like spat fire with clear toddy, fed his kin deer meat and rice, and gave the flowered cloth and gem necklace from his own body.","n":"'Rejoicing greatly at a small thing' (line 17) turns the usual scale: the king is grateful for the bard's coming. The cloth from the king's own body (புரையோன் மேனிப் பூந்துகில்) is the most intimate gift of the chapter. Line 2 has a gap, and பாயல் in the last line may be a place name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 398","lb":"PN398","poet":"திருத்தாமனார்","pat":"சேரமான் வஞ்சன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"கடைநிலை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கடைநிலை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",3,"pul","scene","cock"],["பூமுகை","பூ",4,"maram","scene",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்","யாழ்",5,"yaazh","scene",""],["புலியினம்","புலி",10,"maa","simile",""],["தடாரி","தடாரி",12,"parai","simile",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",22,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மான்வறைக்","மான்",24,"maa","gift_food","deer"],["கொக்குஉகிர்","கொக்கு",25,"pul","simile",""],["அரவுஉறழ்","அரவு",27,"pira","simile",""],["அருவிப்","அருவி",30,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வஞ்சன்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:399":{"book":"purananuru","num":397,"t":["அடுமகள் முகந்த அளவா வெண்ணெல்","தொடிமாண் உலக்கைப் பருஉக்குற் றரிசி","காடி வெள்ளுலைக் கொளீஇ நீழல்","ஓங்குசினை மாவின் தீங்கனி நறும்புளி,","மோட்டிவரு வராஅல் கோட்டுமீன் கொழுங்குறை,","செறுவின் வள்ளை, சிறுகொடிப் பாகல்,","பாதிரி யூழ்முகை அவிழ்விடுத் தன்ன,","மெய்களைந்து, இன்னொடு விரைஇ. . .","மூழ்ப்பப் பெய்த முழுஅவிழ்ப் புழுக்கல்,","அழிகளிற் படுநர் களியட வைகின்,","பழஞ்சோறு அயிலும் முழங்குநீர்ப் படப்பைக்","காவிரிக் கிழவன், மாயா நல்லிசைக்","கிள்ளி வளவன் உள்ளி, அவன்படர்தும்;","செல்லேன் செல்லேன், பிறர்முகம் நோக்கேன்;","நெடுங்கழைத் தூண்டில் விடுமீன் நொடுத்துக்,","கிணைமகள் அட்ட பாவற் புளிங்கூழ்","பொழுதுமறுத் துண்ணும் உண்டியேன், அழிவுகொண்டு,","ஒருசிறை இருந்தேன்; என்னே! இனியே,","‘அறவர் அறவன், மறவர் மறவன்,","மள்ளர் மள்ளன்,தொல்லோர் மருகன்,","இசையிற் கொண்டான், நசையமுது உண்க’ என,","மீப்படர்ந்து இறந்து, வன்கோல் மண்ணி,","வள்பரிந்து கிடந்தஎன் தெண்கண் மாக்கிணை","விசிப்புறுத்து அமைந்த புதுக்காழ்ப் போர்வை,","அலகின் மாலை ஆர்ப்ப வட்டித்துக்,","கடியும் உணவென்ன கடவுட்கும் தொடேன்;","‘கடுந்தேர் அள்ளற்கு அசாவா நோன்சுவல்","பகடே அத்தை யான் வேண்டிவந் தது’ என,","ஒன்றியான் பெட்டா அளவை, அன்றே","ஆன்று விட்டனன் அத்தை; விசும்பின்","மீன்பூத் தன்ன உருவப் பன்னிரை","ஊர்தியொடு நல்கி யோனே; சீர்கொள","இழுமென இழிதரும் அருவி,","வான்தோய் உயர்சிமைத் தோன்றிக் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"After a picture of the rich Kaviri gardens of Killi Valavan, where field workers eat fish curry and boiled rice, the bard says he would go to no other face; living on bitter-gourd gruel his drum-wife cooked with fish from his own line, he sat apart in ruin, until, restringing his broken drum, he sang Thonrikkon as the most righteous and most valiant and asked only for a strong bullock for his cart, and before he had finished wanting it the chief gave a herd of many colours like stars with a cart.","n":"The bard's own catch and his wife's gruel are counted as ruin (அழிவுகொண்டு), so living by one's own labour is the state the patron's gift rescues him from. How the opening picture of Valavan's land joins the gift of Thonrikkon is unclear (lines 12 to 14, a gap at line 8, line 26).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parisil","inmai","eegai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 399","lb":"PN399","poet":"ஐயூற் முடவனார்","pat":"தாமான் தோன்றிக்கோன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"பரிசில் விடை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["பரிசில் விடை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["மாவின்","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",5,"pira","scene","fish"],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",6,"maram","scene",""],["பாகல்","பாகல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",7,"maram","simile",""],["தூண்டில்","தூண்டில்",15,"seythi","scene","fishing line"],["கிணைமகள்","கிணைமகள்",16,"seythi","scene",""],["பாவற்","பாகல்",16,"unavu","scene","bitter gourd"],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",23,"parai","scene",""],["பகடே","பகடு",28,"maa","gift_food","bullock"],["மீன்பூத்","மீன்",31,"pira","simile","star"],["பன்னிரை","நிரை",31,"maa","gift_food","cattle herd"],["அருவி","அருவி",33,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",12],["king","கிள்ளி வளவன்",13],["hill","தோன்றிக்",34]],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"},"pn:400":{"book":"purananuru","num":398,"t":["மாசு விசும்பின் வெண் திங்கள்","மூ வைந்தான் முறை முற்றக்,","கடல் நடுவண் கண்டன்ன என்","இயம் இசையா, மரபு ஏத்திக்","கடைத் தோன்றிய கடைக் கங்குலான்","பலர் துஞ்சவும் தான் துஞ்சான்,","உலகு காக்கும் உயர் கொள்கை,","கேட்டோன், எந்தை என் தெண்கிணைக் குரலே;","கேட்டற் கொண்டும், வேட்கை தண்டாது:","தொன்றுபடு சிதாஅர் மருங்கு நீக்கி,","மிகப் பெருஞ் சிறப்பின் வீறுசால் நன்கலம்",". . . . . . . . . . லவான","கலிங்கம் அளித்திட்டு என்அரை நோக்கி,","நாரரி நறவின் நாள்மகிழ் தூங்குந்து;","போ தறியேன், பதிப் பழகவும்,","தன்பகை கடிதல் அன்றியும், சேர்ந்தோர்","பசிப்பகை கடிதலும் வல்லன் மாதோ;","மறவர் மலிந்ததன் . . . . .","கேள்வி மலிந்த வேள்வித் தூணத்து,","இருங்கழி இழிதரும் ஆர்கலி வங்கம்","தேறுநீர்ப் பரப்பின் யாறுசீத்து உய்த்துத்,","துறைதொறும் பிணிக்கும் நல்லூர்,","உறைவின் யாணர் , நாடுகிழ வோனே!"],"p":"","r":"In the last watch of the night at full moon, the bard sounded his drum, round like the moon seen over the sea, and sang the king's lineage; the king, who stays awake while others sleep to guard the world, heard it and, still not satisfied, took away his old rag and gave fine vessels and cloth, so the bard is drunk on fresh toddy every day and has lost count of time; he drives off not only his own enemies but the hunger of those who come to him, lord of the town where ships are tied at every ford to the sacrificial post.","n":"'Hunger-enemy' (பசிப்பகை) sets the king's charity beside his wars as a second campaign of the same kind. The ships moored to the sacrificial post join trade and Vedic ritual in one image. Lines 12 and 18 have gaps.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Purananuru 400","lb":"PN400","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"சோழன் நலங்கிள்ளி","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்","ctu":"இயன்மொழி","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["இயன்மொழி"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["திங்கள்","திங்கள்",1,"pira","simile","moon"],["தெண்கிணைக்","கிணை",8,"parai","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",20,"pira","scene","ship"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pn:20"}},"links":[{"a":"pt:11","b":"pp:1:38-66","type":"same_image","note":"Patitruppattu 11 likens the Chera, who reached the sea and felled the enemy's guarded kadamba at the root (முழுமுதல் துமிய), to Murugan who cut down Sur at the root (சூருடை முழுமுதல் தடிந்த); Thirumurugatruppadai 45-60 tells the god's deed itself: he entered the cold sea and cut Sur at the root (சூர்முதல் தடிந்த), the mango at its trunk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thirumurugatruppadai shows that cutting the root in the sea was already the god's signature deed, so the Chera poet's repeated phrase is a claim that the king's sea raid re-enacted Murugan's myth, with the enemy's guardian tree standing in for Sur's tree.","gr":4},{"a":"pt:13","b":"k:734","type":"develops","note":"Kural 734 defines a land as one free of crushing hunger, unceasing disease and destructive foes; Patitruppattu 13 ends that the land the king guards has flowered, rid of disease and hunger (நோயொடு பசி இகந்து), after showing the enemy lands he ruined.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the absences that make a land; the Chera poem shows who secures them and at whose cost, since the same king's anger is the destructive foe of his neighbours, so the definition is met at home by being broken next door.","gr":4},{"a":"pt:13","b":"pn:117","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 117 says Pari's land kept its rain even when Venus ran to the south and other evil signs appeared; Patitruppattu 13 says that in the king's guarded land Venus does not run into the burning quarter and rain stands where it is wanted.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the two show two views of the omen: the lament makes a just ruler's land immune to the bad sign, while the Chera praise has the good ruler prevent the sign itself, so royal virtue was imagined as reaching even the planets' course.","gr":5},{"a":"pt:14","b":"pn:2","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 2 likens Udhiyan Cheralathan's patience, counsel, strength, wrath and grace to the five great elements; Patitruppattu 14 tells his son, named in the decade's preface as Udhiyan Cheral's son, that he is as beyond measure as earth, water, wind and sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Father and son are both praised through the elements, which shows the element catalogue working as a Chera family formula, and the son's version moves the point from the elements' separate qualities to their shared immeasurability.","gr":3},{"a":"pt:14","b":"pn:20","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 20 says that even if one could measure the sea's depth, the earth's breadth, the wind's paths and the sky, the Chera's knowledge, compassion and kindness could not be measured; Patitruppattu 14 says the king is as immeasurable as earth, water, wind and sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One poet makes the elements measurable so that the king can exceed them, the other makes them the standard of the unmeasurable, which shows that the same four-part catalogue could rank a king either above nature or alongside it.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:15","b":"pn:23","type":"same_image","note":"Both are reports of having seen the enemy's ruined land (கண்டனென் in Purananuru 23, கண்டுவந் திசினே in Patitruppattu 15), and both mark the ruin by white velai flowers growing over it (வேளை வெண்பூ; வெண்பூ வேளை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared white velai shows it was a stock sign of a razed settlement in both Pandya and Chera praise, and Patitruppattu 15 extends the form: where Purananuru 23 ends on the waste, the Chera poet adds a second sight, the king's own guarded land, so the ruin becomes one half of a judgment on rule.","gr":4},{"a":"pt:16","b":"pt:14","type":"same_image","note":"The same line, எழுமுடி கெழீஇய திருஞெமர் அகலத்து, describes the king's chest in 14 line 11, where it is the shield of the worthy, and in 16 line 17, where it is the queen's longed-for bed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The decade reuses one formula of the crowned chest for war and for love, so the turn in 16 to the waiting queen is built from the same praise language and makes the protector of the realm the cause of her loneliness.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:19","b":"pt:16","type":"develops","note":"16 asks whether the queen's bed will escape loneliness while the king lingers in camp; 19 says his love of war leaves no grace even for his modest wife, who has him only in dreams on the rare night he sleeps.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together, the two poems show the decade returning to the queen's cost of war as a counter-voice inside praise, and 19 sharpens the question of 16 into a charge, set beside the ruin of the enemy's farmers.","gr":3},{"a":"pt:17","b":"a:347","type":"same_image","note":"Patitruppattu 17 says the king's great victory drum was made from the kadamba he cut after driving back the sea (கடம்பு அறுத்து இயற்றிய வலம்படு வியன்பணை); in Akananuru 347 the heroine compares the street's gossip to the drum of Cheralathan who drove back the sea and cut the kadamba (கடம்பு அறுத்து இயற்றிய பண்ணமை முரசின்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical wording shows an akam poet borrowing the Chera's victory drum, a puram fact, to measure how loud gossip is, so the king's war trophy had become familiar enough to serve as a household simile for noise.","gr":3},{"a":"pt:18","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 211 makes the rain the model of giving that seeks no return; Patitruppattu 18 says that even if the rain-cloud fails for many years, Cheralathan will not fail the bards' hope.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats rain as the standard for duty-bound giving, but the Chera poem ranks the king above that standard because rain can fail, so the bards' praise claims for a patron a reliability the ethics book grants only to nature.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:20","b":"pt:18","type":"same_image","note":"Both close on the failure of rain: 18 says Cheralathan will not lie even if rain fails for many years (மன்உயிர் அழிய யாண்டுபல துளக்கி ... தண்இயல் எழிலி தலையாது மாறி); 20 repeats the lines for the drought (மன்உயிர் அழிய யாண்டுபல மாறித் தண்இயல் எழிலி தலையா(து) ஆயினும்) and adds that he will not let a belly go hungry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated lines show that the decade has a fixed formula for drought, and 20 turns the general promise of 18 into a concrete guarantee against hunger, ending in a blessing on the mother who bore the giver.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:patikam:2","b":"a:127","type":"same_image","note":"The preface says the king carved his bow on the Himalaya and brought costly vessels with diamonds (அருவிலை நன்கலம் வயிரமொடு கொண்டு) to his old city and gave to others; Akananuru 127 recalls Cheralathan who drove off the sea, cut the kadamba, carved his bow on the Himalaya and heaped the foes' tribute of fine vessels with diamonds (நன்கலம் ... வயிரமொடு) at Maanthai, leaving it for the earth to eat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two record the same hoard with the same jewel list but different fates, given away in the preface and left buried in the akam poem, so an akam poet could cite a puram event precisely while turning its moral from royal giving to abandoned wealth, the wealth the lover will not linger for.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:21","b":"k:86","type":"develops","note":"Kural 86 says the one who tends the departing guest and watches for the next becomes a good guest of those in the sky; Patitruppattu 21 calls the king's kitchen-fat an ஆவுதி (oblation) beside the sacrificial one and says the two smells together please the gods who abide in the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Chera poem shows the mechanism the Kural only asserts: feeding guests reaches the gods because the kitchen smoke is itself counted as an oblation, so hospitality was understood as a second sacrifice rather than merely compared to one."},{"a":"pt:22","b":"k:578","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 578 gives the world to those able to show kannottam without spoiling the task; Patitruppattu 22 lists கழிகண்ணோட்டம், excessive kindly regard, among the obstacles to the wheel of dharma, beside anger and harsh punishment.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Chera list names as a fault exactly the excess the Kural's condition guards against, which shows that the Kural's praise of kannottam was already bounded in older court ethics where too much mercy blocked a king's justice."},{"a":"pt:22","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"Kural 567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's power; Patitruppattu 22 puts தெறல் கடுமை, severity of punishment, in one list with too much kindness and too much love as obstacles to dharma's wheel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural treats harshness on its own while the Chera poem sets it beside its opposite excesses, so the older list frames royal justice as a mean between cruelty and indulgence rather than as the avoidance of cruelty alone."},{"a":"pt:23","b":"pn:23","type":"same_image","note":"In Purananuru 23 the poet says he has come having seen Nedunchezhiyan's work in enemy land, which ends as a waste where a doe whose stag a tiger killed nibbles velai with her fawn; in Patitruppattu 23 the bards say they came and saw (கண்டனம்) the lands of the Chera's foes turned to forest where the wild cow lives happily with her bull.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two bards' reports end on a female beast in the ruined land, but Kalladanar's widowed doe carries the human loss into the forest while Gauthamanar's paired wild cow celebrates the wilderness's return, so the same reporting convention could either mourn or exult over conquest."},{"a":"pt:24","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"Kural 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, the cows' yield fails and those of the six duties forget their books; Patitruppattu 24 names the six duties (ஓதல் வேட்டல் அவைபிறர்ச் செய்தல் ஈதல் ஏற்றல்) and says the king walks by the word of those who keep them, so that the world walks by his.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the Brahmins' learning depend on the king's protection, while the Chera poem makes the king's conduct depend on the Brahmins' word, so the pair shows the relation running both ways: guarded by the king, they in turn guide him."},{"a":"pt:24","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 559 says if the king bends justice the sky withholds its rain; Patitruppattu 24 ends that even if the rain-cloud forgets its season the king's wealth will keep its yield.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural binds the land's rain to the king's justice, but the praise poem claims the just king's plenty survives even a failed monsoon, so court praise pushed royal power past the cosmic dependence the ethical maxim keeps."},{"a":"pt:26","b":"pn:25","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 25 asks whether Chezhiyan's spear stopped short of further ruin when he saw the widows beating their breasts and cutting their hair; in Patitruppattu 26 the poet himself says நோகோ யானே, I grieve, at the enemy land the Chera's warriors ravaged, whose people blame the season.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show that pity for the conquered had a place inside victory praise in two forms, as the king's hoped-for restraint in the Pandya poem and as the poet's own grief in the Chera one, and in neither as blame of the king."},{"a":"pt:27","b":"pt:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Patitruppattu 27 describes the enemy's watered land, ruined by the king's angry look, as knowing no uproar but the carters' shouting; 28 says the king's own land knows no heat but the uproar of the river's red flood.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The poet uses the same 'no uproar but a harmless one' figure for the land the king destroys and the land he guards, which shows that the two were equally blessed and that only the king's anger made the difference."},{"a":"pt:28","b":"pn:20","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 20 tells a Chera that those in his shade know no burning but the cooking fire and the sun, no bow but the rainbow and no weapon but the plough; Patitruppattu 28 says the Chera's land knows no heat but the uproar of the great river's flood, even in drought.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both Chera poems praise the guarded land by negating violence with a harmless counterpart, and the Patitruppattu adds that the protection holds in drought because the river floods when the hills are dry, so the figure served as a court convention that each poet bent to a new claim."},{"a":"pt:29","b":"pt:23","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 23 shows the king feeding the street-singing bards (வயிரிய மாக்கள் ... மறுகுசிறை பாடும்) even in a drought; 29 uses the same line-words for bards tuning lutes and singing at street corners in the enemy's settlements before the army spread.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The singing bard is the decade's sign of a living land, so 29 measures the enemy's loss by the very figure 23 uses for the king's bounty: the war silences in other lands the music the king pays for at home."},{"a":"pt:30","b":"pn:2","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 2 praises Udhiyan Cheralathan, called பெருஞ்சோற்று in his colophon name, for giving the great rice without limit while the hundred fought the five; Patitruppattu 30, colophon பெருஞ்சோற்றுநிலை, sung for a son of that line, shows the rite: the drum calls warriors to scatter the great rice, a blood-mixed ball offered to the god and eaten by crows and kites.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father's poem only names the great rice as a gift of food, while the son's shows it as a pre-battle offering that binds the warriors and frightens the enemy, so the pair shows the perunjoru as a Chera rite of war that the Purananuru's epithet assumed."},{"a":"pt:patikam:3","b":"pt:24","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 24 praises the king for walking by the word of the Brahmins who keep the six duties; the preface of the decade says he made the elders of moon-clear tradition his own and finally went to the forest with Nedumbarathayanar leading.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The preface completes the portrait of 24: the king whose rule followed the learned Brahmins ended by following one of them out of rule into the forest, so the deference the song praises was remembered as leading to renunciation."},{"a":"pt:32","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"Kural 301 says anger is truly restrained only where it could take effect; Patitruppattu 32 marvels that the king, having broken the enemy and taken their land, is not angry even in the country of those who reviled him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule names the only test of restraint that counts, and the Chera poem shows a king passing it at the one moment conquest gives his anger full power, which is why the poet calls it a wonder rather than a duty."},{"a":"pt:33","b":"k:750","type":"develops","note":"Kural 750 says that whatever excellences a fort has, it is nothing without men excellent in action; Patitruppattu 33 makes the marching army itself a fortress, swords for wall, spears for hedge and arrows for moat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the defenders the real worth of a fort, and the Chera poet takes that to its end by removing the walls altogether, so that the fort exists wherever the army camps."},{"a":"pt:35","b":"pn:62","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 62 asks what winning a war can mean when both kings die, and shows demon women dancing in the blood of the dead; Patitruppattu 35 shows demons dancing in the red field and calls that war the king's unfailing wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same picture of demons dancing on the blood-field is proof of glory when a victor lives to be praised and proof of futility when no one is left to take the field, so the image itself carries no verdict and the surviving king supplies it."},{"a":"pt:36","b":"pn:369","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 369, a kalavazhi for another Chera, maps the battle onto rain and tillage, with elephants as clouds and chariots as ploughs; Patitruppattu 36, also kalavazhi, has the elephants fall with trunks cut like palms in a cleared field.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show the kalavazhi of the Chera court treating slaughter as farm work, a single cleared-field simile in one poem and a full season of ploughing and harvest in the other, so the thurai's name from the threshing floor shapes its images.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:37","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 546 says victory comes not from the spear but from the sceptre that does not bend; Patitruppattu 37 lists the king's victory (வலம்படு வென்றியும்) and his unbending principle (கோடாக் கொள்கையும்) as two separate things he has.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes straight rule the cause of victory, while the Chera poet sets the two side by side as distinct possessions, so the praise poem lacks the causal claim the maxim adds and shows the two were first counted as separate royal goods."},{"a":"pt:38","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Kural 322 ranks sharing food (பகுத்துண்டு) first among all that the authors gathered (தொகுத்தவற்றுள்); Patitruppattu 38 praises the king for the manliness that gathered the sharing of food (பகுத்(து)ஊண் தொகுத்த ஆண்மை) and for living for others.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pair of words, sharing and gathering, ranks a virtue in the Kural's list of teachings and names a king's acquired valour in the Chera poem, so a virtue the Kural places among the vows against killing appears here as a warrior's manhood."},{"a":"pt:38","b":"pn:182","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 182 says the world endures because there are people who will not eat even nectar alone and who strive for others, not for themselves; Patitruppattu 38 says the king has no wish to enjoy sweet things alone and lives for others.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Purananuru gives an unnamed class of people on whom the world rests, and the Chera praise gives the same refusal to eat alone to one named king, which shows the court poet drawing on a general moral picture to make the patron its single instance.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:40","b":"a:199","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 199 measures the wealth a man refuses to leave his wife for by the land Narmudi Cheral regained when Nannan fell in the battle at the great ford of the vakai; Patitruppattu 40 praises the same Narmudi Cheral for cutting down Nannan's vakai at the root.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The love poem adds that the country won from Nannan was one the Chera had lost before (இழந்த நாடு), which the praise poem does not say, and it shows the king's greatest gain already serving akam poets as the very largest wealth a lover could turn down."},{"a":"pt:patikam:4","b":"ku:73","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 73 says the Kosar killed Nannan's fragrant mango tree and drove him from his land; the preface of the fourth ten says Narmudi Cheral beheaded Nannan of Peruvaayil and felled his gold-bearing vakai.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On a plain reading the two disagree on which tree was Nannan's guardian and who destroyed him, so either the name Nannan covers more than one chief or felling his guarded tree was a victory several conquerors claimed, and neither poem alone shows that the deed was told of more than one enemy.","gr":4},{"a":"pt:42","b":"k:776","type":"develops","note":"Kural 776 says the hero counts as wasted every day on which he took no noble wound; Patitruppattu 42 says the king's men wear the thumbai only beside men whose bodies already hold arrows and whose chests show the surgeon's needle-scars.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the ledger of wounds inside one man's private reckoning, while the Chera poem turns it into a rule of the company, so that scars decide who may stand in the thumbai line and the wound becomes a public credential rather than a personal measure."},{"a":"pt:43","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 211 says duty seeks no return, as the world gives nothing back to the rain; Patitruppattu 43 likens the king's giving to rain that breaks a drought and floods the river banks.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both take rain as the model of giving, but the Kural uses it to show giving without return, while the Chera poet uses drought-breaking rain to show giving measured by need and graded by receiver, so the same image yields a rule of disinterest in one and a rule of fit in the other."},{"a":"pt:44","b":"pt:11","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 11 says Nedum Cheralathan cut down the enemy's kadamba at the root and made of it his roaring war drum; Patitruppattu 44 says his son cut the Mohur king's neem at the root to make a drum and dragged it off with elephants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the father's kadamba and the son's neem show the guardian tree turned into the victor's drum as a rite of the Chera line, repeated across generations, so that each king's voice in war is literally made from a defeated enemy's sacred tree."},{"a":"pt:44","b":"a:347","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 347 measures the town's gossip by the drum of Cheralathan made from the kadamba he cut after driving back the sea; Patitruppattu 44 shows the making of such a drum from the Mohur neem.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram decade shows how a Chera trophy drum was made from a felled guardian tree, and the akam poem shows that drum so well known that a heroine can use its roar to measure the gossip about her, so a war trophy had passed into the household's common speech."},{"a":"pt:45","b":"pt:20","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 20 says Nedum Cheralathan went against foes on an island in the sea and cut their kadamba; Patitruppattu 45 says his son threw his spear and drove back the sea itself, which no rain or river can change.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the father's war against islanders in the sea and the son's spear thrown at the sea show the decade's title growing from a naval campaign into a feat against nature, with the human enemy dropped and the sea left as the only adversary worth naming.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:47","b":"pn:394","type":"tension","note":"Patitruppattu 47 says that whenever Kuttuvan slays, the suppliants never cease receiving elephants; in Purananuru 394 a bard given a killing elephant is terrified, sends it back, and is given a bigger one, so he warns other poets away.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"On one reading the war elephant is the crown of royal gifts, and on the other it is a burden a poor bard cannot use or feed, so the pair shows the gift-poem's ideal of the elephant gift and the bard's comic protest against it from inside the same tradition."},{"a":"pt:49","b":"pt:44","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 44 gives the cause of the Mohur war, the king's pledge to the distant Arukai, and the felling of the neem; Patitruppattu 49 gives the kings and Velir who massed at Mohur, the blood-flood and the ruin.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two songs divide one campaign between them, the reason and the trophy in one and the coalition and slaughter in the other, so the decade tells a single war across separate praise poems and needs both to be read as history."},{"a":"pt:50","b":"a:214","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 214 has a soldier in the rain-soaked camp where the king, eager for glory, does not sleep, asking how his wife bears the evenings alone; Patitruppattu 50 asks how the sleepless king himself passes the camp days away from women's embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem treats the king's sleeplessness as the cause of the soldier's separation, while the puram poet lends the king the soldier's own longing to praise his endurance, so the same camp night is a lover's grief in one book and a king's virtue in the other."},{"a":"pt:patikam:5","b":"pt:44","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 44 says the king cut the Mohur neem at the root and dragged it with elephants for a drum; the preface names it as the neem guarded by Pazhaiyan and says the elephant cart was tied with ropes of his widowed wives' hair.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The preface supplies the detail the song withholds, the rope of the defeated king's widows' hair, so the felling of the tree is shown to be a humiliation of the enemy's household as well as the taking of his sacred tree."},{"a":"pt:52","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 222 says taking is bad even if called a good path, and giving good even without a higher world; Patitruppattu 52 says the king's hand bends down to give to suppliants and does not know how to open upward to take (இரப்போர்க்குக் கவிதல் அல்லதை இரைஇய மலர்பு அறியா).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem turns the couplet's two verbs into two postures of one hand, palm down to give and palm up to receive, so the rule against taking becomes a bodily habit the king cannot break."},{"a":"pt:53","b":"pn:98","type":"develops","note":"In Purananuru 98 the foes who see Anji's war elephants with blunted tusks bar their fort gates; Patitruppattu 53 warns the Chera to take another road home, since his elephant, seeing any gate, will charge it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the praise of the war elephant pushed one step further: in Avvai's poem the gates close in fear, while the Chera poet makes the elephant's habit so fixed that the king's own ancestral city is in danger, so the boast works by turning the threat inward."},{"a":"pt:54","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world; Patitruppattu 54 wishes that the giving king not go to the high world (உயர்நிலை உலகத்துச் செல்லாது) but stay long on the earth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets heaven aside as irrelevant to the worth of giving, while the bard treats the giver's heaven as a loss to those who live on his gifts and wishes it postponed, so the same detachment of giving from heaven appears once as an ethic and once as the suppliants' self-interest."},{"a":"pt:55","b":"k:211","type":"develops","note":"Kural 211 asks what the world gives back to the rain, to show that obligation seeks no return; Patitruppattu 55 wishes the giving king's days to pass like white clouds that pour rain, protect the land and go up to rest on the hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both use the rain cloud for the giver, but the Kural stops at the absence of return, while the Chera poem imagines what the cloud has after giving, rest on the hill, and makes that the blessing on the king's lifespan."},{"a":"pt:56","b":"pt:52","type":"tension","note":"Patitruppattu 56 says the king is not skilled at dancing before the drums of the festival ground (ஆடல் வல்லான் அல்லன்); 52, in the same decade, shows him leading the thunangai dance by lamplight, giving his lead hand, until the queen grows angry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On a literal reading the two poems disagree about whether he dances at festivals, and holding them together shows that 56's denial is a joke the decade's hearers would see through, a mock dispraise that only works because his festival dancing was known.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:57","b":"pn:64","type":"same_image","note":"Both are virali-guide poems that open with the same words, செல்லா மோதில் சில்வளை விறலி, and send the dancing woman to a king on the battlefield; Purananuru 64 says they will see Kudumi and leave behind their watery gruel, Patitruppattu 57 says they will see the king who fears suppliants' distress more than women's sulking eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared formula shows that the virali guide poem had a fixed opening used across Pandya and Chera praise, and the difference in what follows shifts the motive from the bard's hunger in the Purananuru to the patron's own anxiety in the Patitruppattu."},{"a":"pt:57","b":"k:224","type":"develops","note":"Kural 224 says being begged from is painful until one sees the asker's glad face; Patitruppattu 57 says the king fears the distress of suppliants more than the sulking eyes of the women of his house (ஒண்நுதல் மகளிர் துனித்த கண்ணினும் இரவலர் புன்கண் அஞ்சும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the giver's discomfort in general, and the poem measures it against the strongest private disturbance of akam, a wife's sulk, so the giver's pain is ranked above domestic love."},{"a":"pt:59","b":"k:120","type":"develops","note":"Kural 120 says true trade for merchants is caring for others' goods as their own; Patitruppattu 59 tells the king that protecting the land, where goods of hill and sea are shared out and the roads stay unblocked in the way of dharma, is his duty.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural places fair trade in the merchant's conscience, while the Chera poem names the condition it assumes, open roads that the king must keep, so the trader's ethic depends on a ruler's duty the couplet does not mention."},{"a":"pt:61","b":"pn:236","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 236 is Kapilar's lament that Pari told him to stay behind and did not take him in death; Patitruppattu 61 has Kapilar, Pari gone to the land from which none returns (வாராச் சேண்புலம் படர்ந்தோன்), arrive at the Chera's camp drawn by a fame for giving without regret.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the bard's two answers to his patron's death, the vow to be with Pari in the next birth and the journey to a new patron whose giving is praised in Pari's terms, so the Chera's praise is partly an elegy carried over.","gr":3},{"a":"pt:62","b":"pt:13","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 13 sets enemy farmland ruined by the king beside his own flowering land; Patitruppattu 62 describes a rich farm of cattle, grain heaps and toddy-drinking workers as what the enemy keeps if he bows and pays tribute.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The second decade shows ruin and plenty as two places; Kapilar makes them two futures of the same enemy land, so the farm scene becomes the terms of surrender rather than a picture of the king's own country.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:63","b":"pn:3","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 3 tells the Pandya his word will not move even if the earth moves (நிலம் பெயரினும், நின்சொற் பெயரல்) and is husband of a lady of faultless chastity; Patitruppattu 63 tells the Chera that even when the earth changes its nature his spoken word does not fail.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same hyperbole for a Pandya and a Chera shows that the unmoving royal word was shared praise across dynasties, and the Purananuru poet turns it into a lever (so read the suppliant's need) where Kapilar leaves it as one of four bounded traits."},{"a":"pt:64","b":"pn:107","type":"qualifies","note":"In Purananuru 107 Kapilar says Pari is not the only one who sustains the world: there is also the rain; in Patitruppattu 64 the same poet tells the Chera that he pours greater benefit than the high cloud (மழையினும் பெரும்பயம் பொழிதி).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kapilar's measure of a giver is the rain in both poems, but where Pari was granted only the rain as his equal the living Chera is set above it, which shows how the poet's praise scale shifted with the patron and how far the dead friend's standard was raised to honour the new one."},{"a":"pt:67","b":"pn:105","type":"develops","note":"In Purananuru 105 Kapilar sends a singing woman to Pari, promising jewels for his gentleness, which is sweeter than the water falling on his hill; in Patitruppattu 67 he sends a bard to the Chera, promising pearls, and fills the road with the king's battlefield of vultures, demons and hacked bodies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet's two guide poems show that the guide form takes its content from the patron: a hill chief is reached through his kindness and his streams, a crowned king through the terror of his wars, though both end in the same gift of jewels."},{"a":"pt:68","b":"a:61","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 61 tells the heroine not to suffer looking at the tall wall where the days are marked (நாள்இழை நெடுஞ்சுவர்), and pictures Pulli's men trading white elephant tusks for toddy; Patitruppattu 68 has the king's warriors hoping to trade white tusks (வெண்கோடு) for toddy while women at home draw the days on the painted wall.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The tusk bartered for toddy and the days scored on a wall were a stock pair of the far campaign and the waiting house: the akam poet splits them between the land the hero crosses and the heroine's room, while Kapilar keeps both inside one king's war, so the cost of conquest is counted at home.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:68","b":"pt:16","type":"develops","note":"Patitruppattu 16 asks whether the queen's bed will escape its loneliness while the king lingers in camp; Patitruppattu 68 shows women in the palace marking the days on the wall while the warriors fast before the fort.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two poets of two decades both end a war poem at the women left behind, which shows the waiting house was a set part of Chera praise, and Kapilar extends it from the queen alone to the whole palace and sets the warriors' own longings beside it."},{"a":"pt:69","b":"pt:13","type":"same_image","note":"Patitruppattu 13 says that in the king's guarded land Venus does not run to the burning quarter and rain stands where wanted; Patitruppattu 69 says that under the rulers before him the land yielded, the sun's heat softened, Venus stood in its place and the rains answered need.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second decade grants the good omens to the reigning king, while Kapilar grants them to the ancestors who were like him, so the same signs serve as proof of a present reign and as the inheritance a king must live up to.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:70","b":"k:43","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 43 makes the chief duty the care of five: ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself; Patitruppattu 70 says the king, with his kin around him, fed the gods with sacrifice, pleased the sages with learning, put elders before his sons, and so paid the ancient debts (தொல்கடன்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, they show two accounts of what a man owes: the Kural spreads the duty over five equal claims including guests and himself, while the Chera poem counts it as an inherited debt to gods, sages and elders paid off by sacrifice and learning, so the king's virtue is framed as settlement rather than care."},{"a":"pt:71","b":"pn:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 5 tells a Chera, lord of a forest land where elephants spread like cattle, to keep away from those who cast off grace and love and to guard the realm as one rears a child; Patitruppattu 71 shows the king burning towns and taking cattle and tribute, then says that had he not weighed the strong and the simple and shown grace (அருளாய் ஆயின்), no one here would live.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Purananuru 5 asks for grace as the standing temper of a ruler's guard, while Patitruppattu 71 finds it only after conquest, as the sparing of the defeated who pay tribute, so the two Chera poems show grace counselled as a principle of rule and praised as a victor's restraint."},{"a":"pt:71","b":"a:135","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 135 likens the heroine's shaken heart to Kazhuvul's Kamur when fourteen Velir attacked it together; Patitruppattu 71 has Kazhuvul, chief of those who live by cattle, bow his head (கழுவுள் தலைமடங்க) when the Chera's archers drove off the herds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kazhuvul is besieged by a coalition of Velir in one poem and humbled by the Chera in the other, which shows his herders' stronghold as a repeated prey of greater powers and explains why an akam poet could use it as a byword for a heart under assault."},{"a":"pt:71","b":"pt:73","type":"develops","note":"71 line 25 has the king weigh the strong and the simple (உரவரும் மடவரும் அறிவுதெரிந்(து) எண்ணி) before he shows grace; 73 line 1 has the strong and the simple reckon the king (உரவோர் எண்ணினும் மடவோர் எண்ணினும்) and find no one to compare him to.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The decade turns the same pair round: first the king judges the wise and the foolish to decide whom to spare, then the wise and the foolish judge him and find no measure, so judgement runs both ways between the king and those he rules."},{"a":"pt:72","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"Kural 467 says to think and then resolve, and that to think after resolving is a lapse; Patitruppattu 72 says the king's foes, from the greatness of their enmity and their folly, resolve without deliberating (சூழாது துணிதல்), unlike the wise who know his nature, and meet him as the doom-fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the rule as prudence in any task, while the Chera poem applies it to war and shows its breach as ruin, setting the wise counsellors who know the king against the foes who resolve unthinking, so deliberation becomes the line between the king's servants and his victims."},{"a":"pt:73","b":"k:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 7 says the mind's anxiety is hard to remove except for those who reach the feet of the one who has no comparison; Patitruppattu 73 calls the Chera one to whom others are no comparison (நினக்குப் பிறர்உவமம் ஆகா) and ends with the poet grieving (வருந்துவல் யானே) that people will not believe his praise.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the incomparable one the end of anxiety for those who come to him, but the Chera poet shows the same attribute, given to a king, bringing anxiety to the one who praises, since what has no comparison cannot be made credible to hearers."},{"a":"pt:74","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"Kural 265 says tapas is striven for here because whatever is wanted is gained as it is wanted; Patitruppattu 74 says the king told his grey-haired elder that generosity, dignity, wealth, offspring, the divine and everything belong to those who have tapas (தவம்உடை யோர்க்கென), and let him go to the wide forest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the promise of tapas as a reason to strive, while the Chera poem shows a king applying it to another, releasing his aged counsellor from court to the forest, so tapas appears as the last stage of a life of royal service and its grant as a king's virtue."},{"a":"pt:77","b":"pt:78","type":"develops","note":"77 likens the Chera's many elephants to the cattle of the Kongar spreading over the gravel upland (கொங்கர் ஆபரந் தன்ன); 78 likens the horses and elephants he had in his own land after razing Tagadur to spreading goats and cattle (ஆபரந் தன்ன யானையோன்).","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"The decade moves the herd simile from the enemy's ground to the king's: first the elephants spread like a hostile people's cattle in an army on the march, then like home herds after victory, so one image marks war-strength turning into the settled wealth of peace."},{"a":"pt:79","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"Kural 443 says that to cherish the great and make them one's own is the rarest of rare things; Patitruppattu 79 says the king honours the great and cherishes the small (பெரியோர்ப் பேணிச் சிறியோரை அளித்தி), between lines on not sparing his life in war and sparing nothing in giving.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural singles out honouring the great as a ruler's hardest gain, while the Chera poem pairs it with care for the small and sets both beside courage and giving, so the maxim appears as one term of a balanced royal portrait rather than a rarity standing alone."},{"a":"pt:patikam:8","b":"pn:230","type":"tension","note":"The preface praises Perunjeral Irumporai for defeating Adhiyaman and razing Tagadur (தகடூர் எறிந்து), sung by Arisil Kizhar; Purananuru 230, by the same Arisil Kizhar, whose colophon names its subject as Ezhini the Adhiyaman who fell fighting at Tagadur, says Death lost more than the grieving, hungry world when it took him. On the reading that both refer to that war, one counts Tagadur's fall a glory and the other a loss to the whole world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, they show one court poet singing both sides of one war, the victor's fame and the fallen chief's elegy, so praise and lament were offices a poet filled by occasion, and the Tagadur campaign survives as triumph and as ruin in the same voice.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:81","b":"a:44","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 44 has a man bid his charioteer hurry home to his wife because the king's war is finished and the foes have paid tribute; Patitruppattu 81 has the poet ask the king himself, in the rainy season with the siege still on, to yoke his chariot for one day to see his wife, so that the sleepless besieged kings may sleep too (துஞ்சா வேந்தரும் துஞ்சுக).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the akam poem the homecoming waits until the war is over, but the court poet asks the king to go home in the middle of the war and gives the besieged enemy's rest as a reason, so the mullai motif of the rainy-season return becomes, in praise poetry, a way of asking for a pause in the fighting."},{"a":"pt:82","b":"pt:90","type":"develops","note":"82 lines 12 and 13 praise wealth that does not lessen however much singers take and an army that does not lessen however much foes kill (கொளக்கொளக் குறையா, கொலக்கொலக் குறையா); 90 line 16 makes the same quality the ground of a likeness: like the sea, because taking does not diminish him (கொளக்குறை படாமையின் முந்நர்ண அனையை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems show the poet's own method of comparison: a quality he first states as a bare formula about wealth and army becomes, in the last poem of the decade, the stated reason why the king is like the sea, so the ground of the simile is set out before the simile is made."},{"a":"pt:84","b":"pt:72","type":"qualifies","note":"72 says the king's foes resolve without deliberating (சூழாது துணிதல்) and, from the greatness of their folly, do not know his nature as the wise do; 84 says his foes knew well that he was strong (வலியை ஆதல்நற்(கு) அறிந்தனர் ஆயினும்) and still rose against him, not knowing how to bow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two Chera decades give opposite causes for the same ruin: the eighth ten blames the enemy's ignorance of the king, the ninth blames their refusal to bow although they knew him, which moves the fault from a lack of judgement to pride."},{"a":"pt:85","b":"pt:61","type":"develops","note":"In 61 Kapilar comes to the Chera after Pari's death, saying he will not beg or understate but has come for the king's fame for giving; 85 says this later Chera's gifts are more than the villages Kapilan won (கபிலன் பெற்ற ஊரினும் பலவே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The seventh ten shows Kapilar arriving at the Chera court without a patron, and the ninth uses the reward he got there as the standard a later king's giving is said to exceed, so one poet's payment became the dynasty's own yardstick for generosity."},{"a":"pt:86","b":"pn:105","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 105 says Pari's gentleness is sweeter than the water that comes down every peak of his hill (நீரினும் இனிய சாயல்); Patitruppattu 86 says Poraiyan is sweeter and cooler of gentleness than the water of the Vaani (வானி நீரினும் தீந்தண் சாயலன்), after the poet admits he had feared him for his slaughter in war.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The formula of a patron gentler than running water passes from Kapilar's hill chief to a Chera king, but the later poet sets it against the king's reputation for bloodshed, so the same praise is used to answer a suppliant's fear of a warrior king."},{"a":"pt:86","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"Kural 386 says the land of a king easy to see and not harsh of speech is praised above others; Patitruppattu 86 tells how the report of the king's killing in war made the poet fear him, until he found him the patron of singers who ends the misery of the poor and is gentle as cool river water.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats being easy to approach as a plain royal virtue, while the poem shows what it has to overcome: a king's fame for killing keeps suppliants away, so his accessibility has to be proved against his own reputation."},{"a":"pt:87","b":"pn:105","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 105 sends a bright-browed virali to Pari, saying she will get jewels (சேயிழை பெறுகுவை) and likening his gentleness to water coming down his hill; Patitruppattu 87 sends a singing woman to Poraiyan, saying she will get fine jewels (நன்கலம் பெறுகுவை) and setting his kindness against the cane carried by a flood that bears sandal to the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Perunkunrur Kizhar splits Kapilar's single guide poem into two of his own, the water likeness in 86 and the sending of the singer with the promise of jewels in 87, which shows how a short Purananuru guide poem served the Patitruppattu poets as a pattern they could take apart."},{"a":"pt:88","b":"pt:patikam:4","type":"develops","note":"The fourth preface tells Narmudi Cheral's own deed: he cut off Nannan's head and felled his gold-bearing vaakai at the root (பொன்படு வாகை முழுமுதல் தடிந்து); 88 lists the wearing down of Nannan of the bright-flowered vaakai (சுடர்வீ வாகை நன்னன் தேய்த்து) among the deeds of the ancestors (பெரியோர் மருக) of the ninth ten's king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A victory that the fourth decade gives to one named king appears in the ninth as one item in a list of ancestral deeds, beside the kadamba and the spear thrown into the sea, so the decades show single kings' wars becoming the common record of the Chera line.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:88","b":"pt:20","type":"develops","note":"20 answers 'who is your king?' with Neduncheralathan, who went against foes on an island of the sea and cut the kadamba at its root (கடம்புமுதல் தடிந்த); 88 has the kadamba's whole trunk felled and the spear hurled into the dark sea as deeds of the ancestors of the present king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the second ten the felling of the kadamba is what identifies one king to a stranger, and in the ninth it has become an inherited title that makes a later king great by descent, so the pair shows a personal feat hardening into a dynastic emblem."},{"a":"pt:89","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules by the right way; Patitruppattu 89 lists the rain in its season, the paired deer, the humming boughs, unfailing fruit and tubers, grazing herds and endless grain, then gives the cause: because your sceptre is straight (நின்கோல் செம்மையின்).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet states the link between a just sceptre and rain as a rule, while the poem spreads its consequences over a whole countryside of herds, forest creatures and grain, which shows how wide a land's well-being the belief was taken to cover."},{"a":"pt:89","b":"pn:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 35 counsels the Chola that rain comes when sought in a land of the just sceptre and warns that if rain fails or yield shrinks the world blames its guardians (காவலர்ப் பழிக்கும்); Patitruppattu 89 tells the Chera that rain pours in season because his sceptre is straight and the land worships him daily.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same belief works as praise in one poem and as warning in the other: credit for the rain in the Chera poem implies the blame for drought that the Chola poem spells out, so a king praised for good seasons was held answerable for bad ones."},{"a":"pt:90","b":"pn:2","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 2 says the Chera has forbearance, breadth of counsel, strength, fierceness and grace like the nature of the five elements, without saying which goes with which; Patitruppattu 90 likens the king to water for his kindness, to the sky for being beyond measure and to the sea for not lessening however much is taken, naming each ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ninth ten does explicitly what the Purananuru catalogue leaves implicit, joining each natural power to one royal virtue by a stated reason, so the pair shows the elemental praise of Chera kings being turned from a list into reasoned comparison."},{"a":"pt:patikam:9","b":"pt:patikam:7","type":"same_image","note":"The seventh preface says Selvakkadungo, after his sacrifice, gave Ogandur and 'confounded the purohita' (புரோசு மயக்கி); the ninth preface uses the same phrase of Ilanjeral Irumporai (புரைஅறு கேள்விப் புரோசு மயக்கி), beside mantra worship, the installing of the spirits of the crossroads and the shanti rite.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated phrase shows that the preface-writers had a fixed slot for a king's dealings with his purohita, and the ninth preface fills it with a larger ritual programme than the seventh, so the prefaces record the growing weight given to priestly rites in the picture of Chera kingship."},{"a":"pt:fragment:1","b":"a:124","type":"develops","note":"Fragment 1 says enemies cannot escape except by heaping rare jewels with elephants and speaking submissively (யானையொ(டு) அரும்கலம் ... பணிந்துவழி மொழிதல்); Akananuru 124 opens with the same formula (நன்கலம் களிற்றொடு ... வணங்கினர், வழிமொழிந்து) as the condition on which a soldier waiting in camp may go home to his wife.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The submission the praise poet demands as the enemy's only course is, in the akam poem, the event a homesick soldier waits for, so the formula of tribute served both genres and the end of the king's war was imagined from the camp as the start of a lover's return.","gr":4},{"a":"pt:fragment:1","b":"pn:17","type":"qualifies","note":"Fragment 1 states as the poet's rhetorical question that enemies have no way but to submit (பணிந்துவழி மொழிதல் அல்லது); Purananuru 17 gives the enemy kings' own words as they decide to submit to the Chera Irumporai for fear of losing their walls and forts.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two show that the Chera praise poets treated submission not only as the king's due but as a reasoned choice the enemy makes on seeing his anger, so the boast of the fragment rests on a scene of calculation that Purananuru 17 spells out.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:fragment:2","b":"k:744","type":"tension","note":"Kural 744 says a fort is that which destroys the besieging enemy's spirit (உறுபகை ஊக்கம் அழிப்ப தரண்); Fragment 2 lists a fort with tall wall, battlements, guard forest and deep moat and says that it is the defending kings who lose heart (நெஞ்சுபுகல் அழிந்து) at the Chera march.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On the plain reading the two make opposite claims about the same contest of nerve: the Kural gives the fort the power to break the attacker's spirit, while the praise poem says a perfect fort could not keep its own kings' spirit from breaking, so the war of morale the Kural assigns to walls the poet assigns to the king's army."},{"a":"pt:fragment:3","b":"ai:67","type":"develops","note":"Fragment 3 has a woman say that another, for all her excellence, is not her equal and knows who her equals are (என்னொடு புரையுநள் அல்லள்); Ainkurunuru 67, a wife's speech in marutham, says the hero's new woman, foolish, sets herself against the wife who is no match for her (தன்னொடு நிகரா என்னொடு நிகரி).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Ainkurunuru 67 supplies the frame the three-line fragment lacks: claims of who is whose equal are the weapon of the marutham quarrel between wife and new woman, which makes it likely the fragment is such a speech and that its point is standing in the household rather than beauty."},{"a":"pt:fragment:4","b":"pn:22","type":"same_image","note":"Fragment 4 has the bard come to see the king (வந்தனென் பெரும கண்டனென்) in his camp with the army camped in foreign land (வேறுபுலத்(து) இறுத்த ... தானையொடு), where elephants full of strength (மைந்துமலி) tear up their posts; Purananuru 22 ends with the poet come to see the Chera Irumporai (வந்து இனிது காண்டிசின்) with his army camping in foreign land (வேறுபுலத்து இறுக்கும் தானையோடு) and opens with strong elephants (மைந்து மலிந்த மழ களிறு) standing at their posts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared phrases show that the bard's visit to a Chera king in his foreign war camp was a set scene with its own diction, and the one change, elephants standing quiet at the post in Purananuru 22 against elephants tearing the post up in the fragment, turns the same scene from settled order to war still pending.","gr":2},{"a":"pt:fragment:4","b":"k:610","type":"develops","note":"Kural 610 says a king without sloth (மடியிலா மன்னவன்) gains the whole earth; Fragment 4 praises a king who does not stop for the rains nor slacken for the cold (பனிஎன மடியாய்) and keeps his army in foreign land.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states freedom from sloth as a ruler's general virtue, while the fragment shows what it meant in war: refusing the seasonal halt of the rains and the cold that normally ended a campaign, so the king's tirelessness was measured against the calendar of war."},{"a":"pn:1","b":"t:16","type":"develops","note":"Both name the god by his marks, the konrai, the matted hair and the woman who is part of him; Purananuru 1 lists them without a name, Tirumanthiram 16 calls him the one whose part is the bright-browed woman.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set beside Tirumular, the anthology's invocation shows that konrai, matted hair and the woman-part were already a fixed set of marks that identified the god without his name, and it adds what Tirumular's verse lacks: the god may also hide that female form within himself."},{"a":"pn:3","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"3 ends by calling the ending of suppliants' poverty the king's true strength (வன்மை); Kural 225 ranks the strength of removing others' hunger above the strength of enduring one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking of strengths is abstract, and the Pandya counsel turns it into a test of practice: the giver must read the need from the face before it is spoken, and the deadly road the suppliants crossed measures what that strength owes them."},{"a":"pn:5","b":"k:243","type":"develops","note":"5 tells the king not to side with those who cast off grace (அருள்) and love and take a hell that never leaves; Kural 243 says those whose hearts hold அருள் do not enter the dark, painful world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes grace a person's safeguard against hell; the Chera counsel turns the same link into a rule of government, where grace is shown by guarding the realm as one rears a child and hell is the lot of rulers who drop it."},{"a":"pn:6","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the weighing balance for leaning to no side: 6 wishes the king may not hold to one side, like the beam of a balance (தெரிகோல் ஞமன்ன் போல); Kural 118 makes not leaning, like a levelled scale, the ornament of the worthy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same balance serves as a king's blessing in the Purananuru and as a general virtue in the Kural, which shows that impartiality was pictured as a public act of weighing done by whoever judges, before it was a disposition of the private person."},{"a":"pn:6","b":"k:978","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 978 says greatness always bows; 6 asks the king's umbrella and head to bow before the god's shrine and the sages, his garland to wilt only in the smoke of burned enemy lands, and his anger to yield before his women's sulking.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural makes humility a constant trait of the great, the Purananuru poet gives it addresses: the king bows to god, sages and wives and to no one on the field, so royal humility was a question of before whom, not of always."},{"a":"pn:9","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"9 praises the Pandya who, before battle, tells cows, brahmins, women, the sick and the sonless to take shelter; Kural 773 calls generous help to the foe in misfortune the cutting edge of valour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural puts mercy at the end of valour, once the foe has fallen; the Purananuru shows a king putting it before the first arrow by warning those who cannot fight, so the restraint of the brave had a set procedure as well as a maxim."},{"a":"pn:10","b":"k:561","type":"agrees","note":"10 says the Chola, seeing a proven wrong, weighs it fairly and punishes fittingly (ஒப்ப நாடி அத்தக ஒறுத்தி); Kural 561 defines a king as one who inquires fittingly and punishes in proportion (தக்காங்கு நாடி ... ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two share the very words for inquiry and proportion, so the Kural's definition of the just king reads like a rule drawn out of praise such as this, and 10 adds what 561 leaves out: the sentence is softened for the one who comes and stands at the king's feet.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:10","b":"k:541","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 541 makes justice an inquiry without favour to anyone (கண்ணோடாது); 10 praises a king who lessens the penalty when the offender submits.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that the old ideal allowed mercy after the verdict but not favour within it: the Kural bars kindly regard in the inquiry, while the Purananuru king reduces the sentence for submission, not for kinship or closeness.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:12","b":"pn:11","type":"qualifies","note":"11 lists as glory the gold jewels and the silver-strung gold lotus a conquering Chera gave his singer and bard; 12 asks a Pandya whether giving bards gold lotuses and poets chariots is dharma when it comes from land taken to others' pain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Side by side, the two poems show that the anthology did not take the gift economy on trust: the gold lotus that crowns the bard in 11 becomes in 12 a question about where a king's gifts come from."},{"a":"pn:12","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"12 asks whether gifts made from others' land, taken so that it pains them, are dharma; Kural 659 says all that is taken with tears leaves with tears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Nettimaiyar leaves his question to the king, and the Kural answers it with a law of return, which shows that the poet's courteous irony carries a real moral charge against the plunder that pays for the bards' gifts.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:14","b":"k:1035","type":"qualifies","note":"14 praises the king's hands, hardened by goad, bowstring and giving, against the soft hands of his poets, which do nothing but eat; Kural 1035 honours those who eat from their own hands' work, who do not beg and who give to those who ask.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Kural the hand that works for its food is the hand that gives; Kapilar reverses the order and makes the poets' soft, idle hands the proof of the patron's care, so the court valued being fed as a sign of the giver's greatness where the Kural values self-support."},{"a":"pn:15","b":"pn:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Both praise the same Pandya, Mudukudumi: 9 for warning cows, brahmins, women and the sick to shelter before battle; 15 for ploughing enemy towns with donkeys, driving chariots over their fields and bathing his elephants in their guarded tanks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the righteous warning of 9 limited whom the king would kill, not what he would destroy: the same king who spares cows and the sick ploughs the enemy city into waste."},{"a":"pn:16","b":"pn:18","type":"qualifies","note":"16 praises a Chola for turning cool sugarcane fields into waste and feeding fire to a safe land; 18 tells a Pandya that food is land joined with water and that those who give food give life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anthology holds both the vanji boast of burned farmland and the counsel that farmland is life, which shows that the counsel of 18 was a duty owed to one's own land, while the enemy's fields remained a fit measure of glory."},{"a":"pn:17","b":"a:60","type":"same_image","note":"17 praises the Irumporai as lord of cool Thondi, with screwpine, wide fields, sandy groves and backwaters; Akananuru 60 compares a fisherman's guarded daughter's beauty to Thondi of the Porayan of the strong chariots.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Poraiyan port that the praise poet counts among the king's possessions became, in akam, the standard for a girl's well-guarded beauty, which shows love poets taking their measure of value from the Chera wealth that puram poets praised."},{"a":"pn:18","b":"k:542","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 542 says the whole world lives looking to the sky and the people to the ruler's sceptre; 18 says land that sows and looks to the sky (வித்திவான் நோக்கும் புன்புலம்) does not help its ruler however wide, and tells him to build tanks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural sets rain and the sceptre side by side as the two things people depend on, Kudapulaviyanar makes the ruler answerable for freeing the land from the sky, turning dependence on rain into a duty of public works."},{"a":"pn:18","b":"k:20","type":"develops","note":"18 opens its argument with bodies that cannot exist without water (நீர்இன்று அமையா யாக்கை); Kural 20 says the world cannot go on without water (நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகு).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two share the phrase but draw different conclusions from it: the Kural derives the world's dependence on the sky, the Purananuru the king's duty to store water, so one premise supported both a praise of rain and a policy against relying on it."},{"a":"pn:19","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Nedunchezhiyan's victory over the seven at Alankanam (ஆலங் கானத்து; எழுவர் நல்வலம்): 19 shows the field where mothers wept and Death fled ashamed, Akananuru 36 makes the victors' shout there the measure of how loud the gossip about the hero is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poet borrowed only the victors' roar from Alankanam to measure a scandal, while the puram poem on the same battle keeps the weeping of the old houses' women, so one battle served love poetry as a byword for noise and praise poetry as a field of grief.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:20","b":"k:563","type":"tension","note":"20 ends: all living beings fear (அஞ்சும்) you; Kural 563 says a ruler who acts so as to cause dread perishes quickly. On the reading that the beings fear the king, 20 praises what the Kural condemns; on the reading that they fear for him, the two agree.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ambiguity of 20's last line matters because the Kural's chapter against causing dread makes a feared king a doomed king, so Kurunkozhiyur Kizhar's praise only holds if the people's fear is anxiety for their ruler and not dread of him."},{"a":"pn:21","b":"k:743","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 743 defines a fort by height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility; 21 describes Kanapper with a moat beyond depth, a sky-high wall and a sunless guard forest, and praises the king who took it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet uses the full list of what makes a fort good only to measure the man who took it, so the Kural's criteria guarantee nothing to the defender: in the praise poem a perfect fort is the conqueror's ornament."},{"a":"pn:23","b":"pn:25","type":"develops","note":"Both by Kalladanar to Nedunchezhiyan: 23 ends on a doe and fawn whose stag the tiger has killed; 25 ends on widows beating their breasts and shearing their hair.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet states openly in 25 what 23 leaves as a bare image, which supports reading the bereaved doe of 23 as the enemy's widows and shows that a puram poet could move between the hidden and the stated forms of one grief."},{"a":"pn:23","b":"a:175","type":"same_image","note":"23 praises the victor of Alankanam; Akananuru 175 compares the lightning of the returning rains to the spear Chezhiyan raised after winning at Alankanam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroine's speech in an akam poem borrows the Pandya's Alankanam victory, which 23 celebrates as fact, to measure the flashing of the rains that mark her lover's promised return, so a war became a stock image of brilliance within the poets' own generation."},{"a":"pn:24","b":"a:266","type":"same_image","note":"24 names the Mizhalai of Evvi, the generous Vel, among the lands Chezhiyan holds; Akananuru 266 names Evvi of Needur, true of sword, and the uproar after his men's victory.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One chief is remembered in the Purananuru for open-handed giving and for lands that passed to the Pandya, and in the Akananuru as a byword for the din of his war victory, so the akam poets kept a lesser king's martial name alive as a measure of loud gossip."},{"a":"pn:24","b":"k:240","type":"agrees","note":"24 ends: those who knew say the ones who truly lived are those who did not go as the many go but stood and died with lasting fame; Kural 240 says those who live without renown do not live.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's flat verdict appears in 24 as a saying the poet attributes to the wise and sets after a blessing of royal pleasure, so the maxim was already proverbial and served to bend a feast poem toward the thought of death."},{"a":"pn:26","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"26 calls Chezhiyan அடுகளம் வேட்ட, the one who sacrificed the killing field; Akananuru 36 measures the town's gossip by the roar when Chezhiyan at Alankanam களம்வேட்ட, after killing seven kings in one day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same phrase for the battlefield sacrifice appears in a praise poem and in a heroine's quarrel with her husband, which shows the Pandya's war rite was famous enough as a phrase to serve an akam speaker as the loudest noise she could name."},{"a":"pn:27","b":"n:359","type":"same_image","note":"27 line 6 மரைஇலை போல மாய்ந்திசினோர் பலரே (many died like the lotus leaf); Naladiyar 359 ends மரையிலையின் மாய்ந்தார் பலர் (many perished like the lotus leaf).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar repeats the Purananuru's half-line almost word for word but moves it from nobly born kings who died without a poet's praise to people whose expectations came to nothing, and the earlier poem shows what the lotus-leaf death meant: dying without a name, beside the flower that is remembered."},{"a":"pn:27","b":"k:234","type":"develops","note":"27 reports the saying that those whom poets praise reach a sky-car that no charioteer drives; Kural 234 says that if one wins long fame on earth, the gods' world does not honour the புலவர்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The belief that poets' praise carries a man to heaven, which 27 gives as current hearsay, is the background the elliptical couplet assumes when it ties earthly fame to the gods' world, so the Purananuru supplies the premise the Kural leaves out.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:28","b":"k:754","type":"qualifies","note":"28 tells the king his wealth can achieve virtue, wealth and pleasure, and failure would be his own neglect; Kural 754 says wealth gained by right means without wrong yields virtue and pleasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the yield of virtue and pleasure depend on how wealth was gained, while the court poet makes it depend on whether the king takes care to use it, so the two books place the moral test of wealth at opposite ends, getting and spending."},{"a":"pn:29","b":"k:332","type":"same_image","note":"29 sets its blessing in a world where people pass in turn like players at a festival; Kural 332 likens wealth's coming and going to a crowd that gathers at a dance hall and disperses.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books reach for the festival stage to show transience, but the Purananuru applies it to the players, the people themselves, and draws the counsel to turn stored wealth into fame, while the Kural applies it to the audience, wealth itself, so the same image grounds two different lessons about what passes."},{"a":"pn:31","b":"k:39","type":"develops","note":"31 opens: as wealth and pleasure follow behind virtue, so the two other kings' umbrellas follow behind Nalankilli's; Kural 39 says only what comes through virtue is joy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ordering the Kural states as an ethical rule is used by the court poet as a ready image to rank three crowned kings, which shows the precedence of virtue over wealth and pleasure was a commonplace secure enough to carry a political claim."},{"a":"pn:34","b":"k:110","type":"agrees","note":"34 quotes dharma: there is expiation for killing cows' udders, destroying unborn children or wronging elders, but none for killing a benefit; Kural 110 says those who killed any good may escape, but not one who killed a good done to him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into a rule what the Purananuru presents as a known saying with its list of lesser crimes, and the poet turns it on himself, so the maxim was already used to bind a bard to praise his patron: gratitude here is the poet's own obligation, not only the patron's claim.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:35","b":"k:1034","type":"agrees","note":"35 says the king's umbrella is for sheltering his people and his victories are the fruit of the plough's furrow; Kural 1034 says those with the shade of grain-ears see many kings' umbrellas under their own king's umbrella.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both texts set the royal umbrella against the farmer and make conquest depend on the plough, but 35 says it to the king as counsel while the Kural says it of the farmer as praise, so the same economic claim is spoken from both ends of the realm."},{"a":"pn:35","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"35 says rain comes when sought in the land of the just sceptre, but also that the world blames kings for failed rains and harvests; Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules rightly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the link between just rule and rain as a fact, while the court poet adds that people will blame the king for drought whatever its cause, so in the Purananuru the doctrine is also a political danger the king must manage."},{"a":"pn:36","b":"n:241","type":"agrees","note":"36 tells the besieging king that to have fought a rival who sits at ease behind his walls is a matter of shame; Naladiyar 241 says the worthy, seeing their enemies humbled, feel shame and do not advance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's maxim about shame before a humbled foe is in 36 an actual plea to stop a siege, which shows the poets used this sense of honour to bring wars to an end, and that the shame was felt to fall on the victor.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:37","b":"pn:39","type":"same_image","note":"Both by Nappasalaiyar to Killivalavan open by naming him the descendant of the Chola who relieved a dove's distress (37 புள்ளுறு புன்கண் தீர்த்த; 39 the king who entered the scale for the dove).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet uses one ancestral legend of mercy in opposite ways: in 37 it heads praise of a king who spares no fine town, and in 39 it is set aside as inherited fame that cannot be his own, so the legend served both to honour the line and to ask what the living king had added."},{"a":"pn:38","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"38 says heaven lacks giving and asking, so gift-seekers prefer the king's land; Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books cut the worth of giving loose from heaven, but from opposite sides: the Kural says giving is good even without a heaven to reward it, and the Purananuru says heaven is poorer than earth because no one gives there, so giving becomes what makes this world worth more than the next."},{"a":"pn:40","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"40 asks the conqueror to be sweet of speech and easy of access (இன்சொல் எண்பதத்தை); Kural 386 says the land of a king easy to see and not harsh of speech is praised above others.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's two marks of a praised king are in 40 exactly the two requests a poet puts to a victorious king, so the couplet's criteria were the counsel bards gave in person, and the poem ties them to the king's fame among his poets.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:41","b":"n:36","type":"qualifies","note":"41 opens 'காலனும் காலம் பார்க்கும்' (even Death watches for its time) to say the king does not wait; Naladiyar 36 tells the reader not to ask whether Death comes today or another day, for it stands right behind.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both treat Death as an agent that chooses its hour, but the Purananuru makes that patience the measure a king exceeds, while the Naladiyar makes the unknown hour the reason for doing good now, so one belief about death supports a war boast in one book and a moral urgency in the other."},{"a":"pn:42","b":"k:544","type":"develops","note":"Kural 544: the world clings to the feet of the king who embraces his people and rules by the sceptre; 42: under his unbending sceptre the land knows no war cry, and all poets turn to him as rivers run to the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the reciprocity abstractly, while 42 shows what the embrace consists of (fields that feed guests, no raid-cry heard) and what the turning to the king looks like (poets flowing in like rivers), so the couplet's 'world at his feet' can be read as a picture of a watered, hospitable country."},{"a":"pn:43","b":"k:1015","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1015 calls those who feel shame for another's fault as for their own the dwelling place of shame; in 43 the prince, wronged by the poet's words, is ashamed 'as if you had erred' (நீபிழைத் தாய்போல் நனிநா ணினையே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's definition of the finest shame is acted out in 43 by a prince who blushes for the man who insulted him, and the poem adds the consequence the couplet omits: that shame shown this way disarms the offender, who then takes the fault on himself."},{"a":"pn:44","b":"k:745","type":"tension","note":"Kural 745 praises a fort that is hard to take, holds stored food and is easy for those inside to hold; 44 tells a besieged king that sitting secure behind shut gates and a long wall is shameful while his elephants starve and his people cry for milk and water. On the reading that both judge the king who holds out in a fort, they disagree.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural values the fort as a means of holding out, while Kovur Kizhar sees the same holding out from inside, through starving beasts and infants, and calls it neither aram nor maram, so the pair shows the fort's strength and its moral cost as two views of one siege.","gr":10},{"a":"pn:45","b":"k:888","type":"develops","note":"Kural 888: a house with inner enmity wears itself away like gold rubbed by a file; 45: two Cholas wearing the same aar garland fight, so whichever loses, their own house loses.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's file image says the house wears itself down, and 45 supplies the political arithmetic behind it, that between two branches of one house no outcome is a win and the only gain goes to rival dynasties, so the couplet's metaphor gets a concrete case and an outside beneficiary.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:45","b":"a:346","type":"develops","note":"45 warns two Cholas that their quarrel will give deep delight to other kings; Akananuru 346 closes on a Chola, Killi Valavan, worsted at Koodal and 'Kothai Marban's joy was great'.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"An akam poet's closing allusion records exactly the outcome Kovur Kizhar predicted in puram, a rival Chera rejoicing at a Chola defeat, which shows that akam poets drew on the same remembered politics of the three crowns (whether this Killi Valavan is 45's Chola is not certain)."},{"a":"pn:46","b":"pn:43","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems address Chola princes as descendants of the king who saved the dove (புறவு): 43 to rebuke a prince, 46 to stop the killing of children.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed together, the two poems show the dove story working as a family standard that poets could hold up to any Chola, in 43 to shame and in 46 to restrain, so the legend served as a check on royal conduct, not only as praise."},{"a":"pn:47","b":"k:586","type":"tension","note":"Kural 586 counsels the king to use spies who go in the guise of renunciants and wanderers; 47 insists that the bard's wandering life, crossing many wastes from patron to patron, harms no one. On the reading that 47 answers a suspicion of the wandering bard, the two disagree about whether such travellers should be trusted.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's advice to disguise spies as wanderers is the very reason a real wanderer could fall under suspicion, and 47 gives the wanderer's side, a life of gifts and honour that 'knows no harm to others', so the pair shows the cost the state's craft laid on the travelling poet.","gr":8},{"a":"pn:48","b":"a:60","type":"same_image","note":"48 praises Thondi of the seaside grove, the Chera's town, as fragrant with the king's garland and the neytal of its backwater; Akananuru 60 likens the heroine's bright bangles to Thondi of Poraiyan of the strong chariot.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same Chera port that a puram poet offers as his home and his king's glory becomes in an akam poem a measure of a girl's beauty, which shows that akam poets used a king's famous town as a byword for desirable wealth."},{"a":"pn:49","b":"a:346","type":"same_image","note":"49 asks how to name Kothai of the raised sword; Akananuru 346 ends on Kothai Marban rejoicing when a Chola was worsted at Koodal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Chera whom a puram poet praises as lord of three landscapes appears in akam as a rival who delights in a Chola defeat, which shows akam poets citing a living king's partisanship, not only his wealth, and lets the reader see the three-crown politics behind the praise (whether both name the same Kothai is not certain)."},{"a":"pn:50","b":"k:234","type":"develops","note":"50 says the king spared and fanned the poet as if he had heard that the higher world has a dwelling only for those with fame here; Kural 234 says that if one wins wide fame on earth the world of the gods does not honour the learned (or the gods) instead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both tie fame on earth to standing in the world beyond, and 50 shows the belief at work in a single act of royal mercy, while the Kural states it as a rule whose terms are disputed; the poem suggests that for a Sangam king fame was a practical reason for forbearance."},{"a":"pn:52","b":"pn:51","type":"develops","note":"51 and 52 are both poems to Maran Vazhuthi: 51 says the kings who resisted him are pitiable, living a day like winged termites; 52 shows what that pity means, their towns turning to jungle and their common hall abandoned.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read in sequence, 51 gives the general verdict and 52 the landscape of its execution, so the reader sees how Sangam praise of a conqueror moved from a simile of brief life to a detailed picture of a deserted village."},{"a":"pn:54","b":"k:1055","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1055: askers can stand before people because there are those on earth who do not hide what they have; 54: suppliants walk into the king's court 'as if they owned it' (உடையோர் போல), because he gives without stint.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural explains why asking is possible at all, and 54 shows the confidence such a giver creates, a beggar entering a court with raised head; the poem also adds the other face the Kural omits, the same king's land as a tiger's ground to his rivals."},{"a":"pn:55","b":"k:546","type":"agrees","note":"55: though elephants, horses, chariots and warriors are excellent, a king's victory has the righteous way at its root; Kural 546: it is not the spear but the unbending sceptre that gives victory.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru poet already ranks the four-limbed army below righteous rule before the Kural's couplet does, and he spells out the straight sceptre as impartiality toward one's own and others, so the Kural's maxim had a court-counsel form addressed to a named Pandya."},{"a":"pn:55","b":"k:541","type":"develops","note":"Kural 541 defines justice as acting after inquiry without favour to anyone; 55 asks the king not to bend the rod because a man is 'ours' and not to destroy merit because he is 'others'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural forbids favour in one direction only, while 55 names both failures, favouring one's own and injuring outsiders, so the poem shows that impartiality was understood as a double duty, protecting strangers' merit as well as resisting kin."},{"a":"pn:55","b":"pn:59","type":"same_image","note":"55 asks the king to be like the sun in fierce valour and like the moon in cool gentleness; 59 says the king is like the sun to his enemies and the moon to people like the poet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same sun-and-moon pair is counsel in 55 and description in 59, so the anthology shows a stock image of kingship moving between what a king should be and what a poet says he already is, with the second poem naming whom each face is turned to.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:56","b":"pn:58","type":"same_image","note":"56 names the palmyra-bannered god and the sapphire-dark god among the four guardians of the world; 58 likens the allied Chola and Pandya to the two great gods, the milk-white one with the palmyra banner and the blue one with the wheel, standing together.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One poem divides the two gods among four to praise one king, the other pairs them to praise two allied kings, so the reader sees that poets chose a god-pair or a god-set to match the number and relation of the kings they praised."},{"a":"pn:57","b":"pn:52","type":"qualifies","note":"52 dwells on the enemy's land turned to jungle as the measure of the king's glory; 57 permits plunder, burning and killing in the enemy's land but asks the king to spare the guardian trees.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside 52's relish of devastation, 57 shows that the same war poetry also drew a limit, and drew it at the sacred tree rather than at people, which tells us what the poets held more inviolable than the lives of the conquered."},{"a":"pn:58","b":"k:826","type":"agrees","note":"58 warns two allied kings against outsiders who come between them 'seeming good, seeming kind, seeming to follow the old ways'; Kural 826 says the words of the unreconciled, though they speak good things like friends, are soon seen through.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's general warning about feigned friendship is applied in 58 to an alliance of kings, where the danger is not the enemy's attack but his pleasant counsel, so the pair shows the couplet's subject was already a matter of statecraft.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:58","b":"pn:45","type":"develops","note":"45 tells two quarrelling Cholas that their fight will delight other kings; 58 tells a Chola and a Pandya that if they never break their union the whole earth will be theirs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems state one calculation from opposite sides, division gives the gain to outsiders and union takes it from them, so together they show Sangam poets thinking of alliances between crowns in terms of who profits.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:60","b":"a:140","type":"same_image","note":"60 likens Valavan to the strong ox that drags the salt cart's sunken wheel out of the rut on the road from the backwaters to the hill country; Akananuru 140 likens the speaker's suffering to that of the ox yoked to the father's salt cart sunk in deep mire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The salt cart in the mire is a shared image of the coastal trade road, and the two books use opposite ends of it: the puram poet praises the king as the ox that frees the wheel, the akam speaker feels like the ox straining under it, so one working scene serves for strength in one and for heartache in the other.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:65","b":"a:55","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 55's mother, sleepless after her daughter's elopement, compares her lot to the noble men who, hearing Cheralathan's bitter words when he sat north after Venni, went with him to the other world; 65 laments the same king's sitting north (வடக்கு இருந்தனன்) out of shame at a back wound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poet can use the king's death as a simile for a mother's loss only because puram poems such as 65 had made it known, and the akam poem adds what 65 does not say: that men of his court followed him into death, so the two together give the event and its aftermath."},{"a":"pn:66","b":"pn:65","type":"develops","note":"Both poems are on the death after Venni: 65 mourns the king who sat north ashamed of a back wound, and 66, addressed to the victor Karikal, says that king is better than him; both use the phrase புறப்புண் நாணி.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the honour code reversing a battle's result: the loser's chosen death, mourned in 65 as the stopping of a whole country's life, is used in 66 to rank him above the victor, said to the victor's face.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:66","b":"a:246","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 246's friend says the gossip about the hero was louder than the uproar at Venni when Karikal broke the strength of kings and eleven Velir and their drums were left on the field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poet remembers Venni for the noise of Karikal's victory over many kings, while 66 remembers it for the defeated king's honourable death, so one battle served akam as a measure of loudness and puram as a lesson in honour.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:66","b":"k:970","type":"develops","note":"Kural 970 says the world praises the radiance of those who will not live when disgrace comes; 66 says the king who sat north ashamed of his wound reached the world of great fame (மிகப் புகழ் உலகம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"66 shows what the Kural's general praise of honour could mean in practice: a dishonour as small as a wound's place on the body, a death chosen for it, and a poet ranking the dead man above the living victor in public."},{"a":"pn:76","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"The half-line எழுவர் நல்வலம் அடங்க (the strength of the seven brought down) stands in 76 line 12 and in Akananuru 36 line 20, both of Cheliyan's victory at Alankanam; Akananuru 36 names the seven kings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The identical half-line shows that the Alankanam victory had a fixed formula that passed from praise poetry into akam, where the war-cry of that day becomes the measure of how loud the town's gossip is about the hero's new bride."},{"a":"pn:76","b":"pn:192","type":"same_image","note":"76 opens: killing and being killed is not new (புதுவது அன்று), it is the way of the world; 192 says dying is not new (சாதலும் புதுவது அன்றே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same commonplace serves opposite ends: in 192 the oldness of death leads to calm toward great and small alike, while in 76 it is granted only so that one king's feat can stand out as the thing never heard of before.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:72","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"In 72 the king accepts, if he breaks his vow, that his people will find no shade under his rule and will weep and blame him as cruel; Kural 555 says the tears of those who cannot bear their suffering are what wear away a king's wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the people's tears as a force that destroys a king's wealth; 72 shows a king himself choosing those tears as the worst penalty he could stake, so the Kural's warning was already a ruler's own measure of failure.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:72","b":"pn:78","type":"develops","note":"In 72 the kings mock Neduncheliyan as a boy (இளையன் இவன்); in 78 the new warriors say 'the fighter is young' (பொருநனும் இளையன்) and are pursued to their fathers' town and killed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"78 tells, through a court poet, the sequel that 72 vows in the king's own voice, so the anthology keeps both the vow and its fulfilment, and the taunt of youth becomes the hinge of the king's fame.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:77","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"77 pictures the king as a boy just weaned, with the child's amulet still on; 72 is the same king's vow against kings who mocked him as young.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"77 shows that the enemies' taunt in 72 was literally true, which makes the boy's vow and its performance the more striking and explains why his poets kept returning to his age."},{"a":"pn:71","b":"k:507","type":"agrees","note":"Among the curses 71 accepts is to put an incapable man in office in his assembly out of fondness (அன்பின் அவையத்துத் திறன்இல் ஒருவனை நாட்டி); Kural 507 says trusting the ignorant out of fondness brings every kind of folly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names fondness as the cause of a bad appointment; 71 shows how grave such an appointment was held to be, since a king lists it among the curses on himself beside losing his wife, his friends and his birthright."},{"a":"pn:73","b":"k:891","type":"agrees","note":"73 says the fool who ignored the strength of the strong (ஆற்றல் உடையோர் ஆற்றல் போற்றாது) will hardly escape alive; Kural 891 says not slighting the strength of those able to act (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் இகழாமை) is the chief of all safeguards.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical wording shows the Kural turning a king's threat into a general safeguard: what 73 says from the side of the strong man about to strike, the Kural says to everyone who might provoke him.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:74","b":"k:1066","type":"qualifies","note":"74 asks whether a warrior line would bear a son who begs a little drink to quench his hunger; Kural 1066 says even asking water for a cow degrades the tongue more than anything.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes asking shameful even for the worthiest cause; 74 roots the same horror in a warrior code that will not let even a stillborn child go unmarked by the sword, so the shame of begging is shown as a matter of lineage as well as of general ethics."},{"a":"pn:75","b":"k:552","type":"agrees","note":"75's petty heir begs dues from his people (குடிபுரவு இரக்கும்); Kural 552 says the asking (இரவு) of a king with his sceptre is like a robber with a spear saying 'hand it over'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books call a king's demand on his people begging, and 75 adds the cause the Kural leaves out: the petty heir values the throne as wealth to take from, where the worthy one counts the same fortune as light as a dry stick.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:67","b":"pn:216","type":"develops","note":"In 67 Pisir Aandhai trusts that King Killi will reward a swan for saying it serves at his feet; in 216 Kopperuncholan tells his doubting courtiers that the poet, whom he knows only by hearing, will come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each partner stakes something on the other's regard for a name alone: the poet promises the king's gift for it, the king promises the poet's arrival, so the anthology keeps this friendship from both sides."},{"a":"pn:68","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"68 promises the bard a lord who will give so much that he forgets other men's doors (பிறன்கடை மறப்ப); Kural 1053 says even asking has a grace when one stands before those who hide nothing and know their duty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural allows asking only before the right giver, and the guide poem is the bard's practical answer: it tells him which door to stand at so that he need never stand at another, which shows ஆற்றுப்படை solving the Kural's problem of asking."},{"a":"pn:70","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"70 says the king's giving is no windfall like woodcutters finding gold; Kural 216 likens the wealth of the good-hearted man to a fruit tree ripening in the middle of the town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"70 makes explicit what the Kural's tree in the town only implies: the value of a generous man's wealth to those who ask is that it can be counted on, unlike a lucky find, so reliability is part of what giving means."},{"a":"pn:62","b":"pn:63","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems, by Kazhathalaiyar and Paranar, are on the battle in which Neduncheralathan and Peruvirarkilli both died; both list the fallen army and silent drums and both kings dead.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two poets on the same double death close differently, one with heaven's feast and a blessing on fame, the other with a question about the orphaned land, so the thokai nilai could console with glory or count the cost to the country."},{"a":"pn:80","b":"a:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 6 calls Tittan of Uranthai the great giver who brings the wealth of rain (மாவண் தித்தன்); 80 asks Tittan to witness the hero's victory 'whether he grants or not' (நல்கினும் நல்கான் ஆயினும்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The akam poet uses Tittan's fame as a giver as a byword, while 80 makes his grace toward one man uncertain, so the same chief appears as a public giver and as a withholding judge whose favour must be won.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:84","b":"k:1065","type":"same_image","note":"84 says her lord has great shoulders though he eats புற்கை (coarse gruel); Kural 1065 says that even gruel thin as water (அடுபுற்கை) is sweetest when one's own effort earned it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's gruel of the self-reliant poor already stood in the older poem for a strong man's austere fare, cited by a woman as proof of his strength, so the couplet's dignity of plain food draws on a heroic image, not only on poverty."},{"a":"pn:85","b":"pn:83","type":"develops","note":"85 describes a crowd split into two sides over the contest, one saying he wins and one saying he does not; 83 curses 'this bewildered town, split two ways and never on one side' (இருபாற் பட்ட).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with 85, the divided town of 83 is a crowd taking sides over Killi's contest, so the woman's curse wishes her own inner division onto spectators who argue about the very man she loves."},{"a":"pn:86","b":"k:69","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 69: a mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than when she bore him; in 86 a mother likens her womb to a tiger's empty den and says her son will be found on the battlefield.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's mother waits to hear others praise her son, while the Purananuru mother does not know where he is and stakes her pride on her womb and the battlefield, which shows that the worth a mother hoped for could be a warrior's death as readily as a good name.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:86","b":"pn:278","type":"develops","note":"86's mother says her son will show himself on the battlefield; in 278 an old mother searches the battlefield and rejoices more than at his birth on finding her son fallen with his body cut apart.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two run from boast to proof: the field where 86's son will 'appear' is the field 278's mother has to search among the corpses, and the joy she measures against the day of birth is the price of the first mother's confidence."},{"a":"pn:87","b":"k:771","type":"agrees","note":"87 opens களம்புகல் ஓம்புமின், தெவ்விர் (enemies, beware of entering the field); Kural 771 opens என்னைமுன் நில்லன்மின் தெவ்விர் (enemies, do not stand before my lord).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's chapter on an army's pride keeps a whole Sangam speech-type, the warning to foes addressed as தெவ்விர் with a prohibitive verb, and swaps Avvai's craftsman's image of strength for the hero-stones of the slain, so the couplet is the genre cut down to its threat."},{"a":"pn:88","b":"pn:87","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Avvai's warnings to Anji's foes under the thurai தானை மறம், each built on the imperative ஓம்புமின் (beware).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Side by side they show the formula's moving part: 87 rests the threat on one unnamed warrior wrought like a month-made wheel, 88 on the chief himself in his finery, so the same prohibition could be backed by the army or by the king."},{"a":"pn:90","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"90 asks whether deer herds can stand where a fierce tiger rages; Kural 599 says even a huge, sharp-tusked elephant fears when a tiger attacks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the tiger is the spirit that neither numbers nor size can resist, but Avvai sets it on a herd to praise a king while the Kural sets it on an elephant to teach ookkam, so one image served both royal praise and a general maxim on energy.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:91","b":"k:82","type":"develops","note":"Kural 82: even if it were the medicine of deathlessness, eating it alone while the guest waits outside is not to be wished; in 91 Anji gives Avvai the nelli fruit that keeps death away instead of eating it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's extreme case, the food of immortality not eaten alone, has a remembered instance in a chieftain who gave a poet the fruit of long life, so the maxim's hyperbole was also a story the tradition told of a real giver."},{"a":"pn:91","b":"pn:235","type":"develops","note":"91 thanks Anji for giving Avvai the nelli fruit so that she might escape death; 235, Avvai's lament for him, recalls how he shared toddy and food with her and says the spear that pierced him fell on the tongues of poets.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gift of long life in 91 turns bitter in 235: the poet made to outlive her patron is left to mourn him, and the spear that killed the giver silences the singers he fed, so the pair binds the bard's fortune to the patron's life."},{"a":"pn:91","b":"a:162","type":"same_image","note":"91 calls Anji lord of the Athiyar with கழல்தொடி தடக்கை; Akananuru 162 names a கழல்தொடி அதிகன் who never fails the hope of suppliants and owns a hill of jackfruit and vengai (probably the same Athiyar house, though the akam poem does not say which chief).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"An akam poet uses the same epithet and the same fame for never failing those who ask, which 91 shows in one deed, to set the hill where a lover recalls a hard-won meeting, so the Athiyar chief's giving was a byword love poets could lean on."},{"a":"pn:92","b":"k:66","type":"qualifies","note":"92: a child's prattle does not keep to the lute and makes no sense, yet moves fathers; Kural 66: those who call the flute and lute sweet have not heard their children's prattle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves unsaid that prattle fails every test of music, and Avvai says it outright and builds on it, so read together the father's delight is shown to be grace rather than taste, which is what lets her use it for a patron's love of an imperfect poem."},{"a":"pn:93","b":"k:776","type":"develops","note":"Kural 776: the hero counts as wasted every day on which he took no noble wound (விழுப்புண்); 93 tells how a king who died of sickness was cut with a sword on darbha grass so as to go where the battle-slain go, and ends on Anji's விழுப்புண்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows why a day without a wound could be counted lost: a death without one had to be given a wound by rite before the dead could reach the heroes' world, so the Kural's ledger rests on a belief about where the dead go."},{"a":"pn:94","b":"pn:315","type":"develops","note":"94: gentle to us like the bull elephant that lets village boys wash its tusks, harsh to foes like its musth; 315: Anji can stay unseen like the fire-stick tucked in the eaves and blaze like the fire it kindles.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Avvai gives Anji the same two-sided nature twice in different images, gentle and fierce in 94, hidden and blazing in 315, which shows that the double character, not any single image, was her settled portrait of him."},{"a":"pn:95","b":"pn:315","type":"same_image","note":"95: he gives food when he has it and eats together with the poor when he has none; 315: when he has, he can eat, and he gives to suppliants even beyond what he owes.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both poems answer the same question, what Anji does when his stores run low, and together show that his fame as a giver rests on sharing want with his dependants, not on abundance.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:95","b":"k:227","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 227: the disease called hunger does not touch one used to sharing his food; 95 praises a lord who shares when he has and goes without together with his dependants when he has not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural promises the habitual sharer freedom from hunger, while Avvai's lord shares and still has empty days, so the poem shows the virtue the Kural rewards at work in a world where sharing did not prevent want but made it common to all."},{"a":"pn:97","b":"pn:98","type":"develops","note":"97 lists Anji's swords, spears, elephants and horses worn by war and tells enemies to pay tribute; 98 lists what his enemies do on seeing his elephants, horses, spear and warriors, and ends in pity for their doomed land.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two catalogues show one army from two sides, its weapons worn out in 97 and the enemy's barred gates and bound wounds in 98, so the tribute demanded in 97 is backed by the ruin of fields that 98 foresees."},{"a":"pn:99","b":"a:352","type":"same_image","note":"99 says Anji is beyond ordinary singers and that Paranar sang his victory at Kovalur; in Akananuru 352 the heroine calls her friend's news sweeter than a bard's famous song that set Anji's renown.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam heroine's comparison assumes that songs fixing Anji's fame were well known and loved, which 99 confirms by naming a poet who sang it, so a puram repertoire stands behind the love poem's simile."},{"a":"pn:100","b":"k:775","type":"develops","note":"100 ends with the eyes that glared at foes staying red even when he looks at his small son; Kural 775 calls it defeat if the glaring eye blinks when a spear is thrown.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks the warrior's glare not to waver before a spear, and Avvai shows it cannot waver even before his own child, so together the fixed red eye is both the warrior's ideal and a cost the poem lets the reader feel."},{"a":"pn:101","b":"pn:114","type":"same_image","note":"Both Avvai on Athiyaman (101) and Kapilar on Pari (114) use the elephant's food-ball (கவளம்) as their simile: held safe between the tusks in 101, chewed and dropped in 114.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same image of a patron's court serves praise and lament: in the living patron's poem the food-ball is the gift kept safe for the bard, and in the dead patron's poem it is the dropped leavings that mark where feasts used to be."},{"a":"pn:101","b":"a:162","type":"develops","note":"101 argues at length that Athiyaman's gift never fails however long it is delayed; the akam poem 162 calls Athikan, of the same house, one who never let a suppliant's hope fail (நசைபிழைப்பு அறியா).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"What 101 has to argue as a bard's private reassurance had become, in an akam poet's line, a fixed epithet of the Athiyaman house, so the praise poem shows the experience behind the epithet.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:102","b":"pn:116","type":"same_image","note":"102 likens the prince to the spare axle salt-carters (உமணர்) tie under a loaded cart; in 116 Pari's orphaned daughters climb a heap of leaves to count the salt-carters' carts going by.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The salt caravan stands for dependence on a protector in praise and for the loss of one in lament: in 102 the patron is the carters' reserve, in 116 his daughters have nothing to do but watch the carts that no longer concern them."},{"a":"pn:103","b":"a:352","type":"develops","note":"103 is Avvai's guide poem sending a viraliyar to Anji; Akananuru 352 cites a bard's song that set down Anji's good fame (அஞ்சி நல்லிசை நிறுத்த) as a model of fine craft.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together they show the life of a praise song: Avvai directs performers to Anji, and a later akam poet can take a bard's song of Anji's fame as the standard of a well-made song, so the guide poems made the fame the akam poets borrowed."},{"a":"pn:104","b":"k:495","type":"tension","note":"Kural 495 says the crocodile wins in deep water and is killed by anything once out of it; 104 makes the young king a crocodile that kills an elephant in a foot-deep children's pool.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural uses the crocodile to teach that strength belongs to the ground, while Avvai uses it to claim a strength that needs almost no ground, so a court poet's praise could deny the very limit the ethics book counsels kings to respect."},{"a":"pn:104","b":"k:698","type":"develops","note":"Kural 698 tells those who serve a king not to slight him as young (இளையர்); 104 warns the enemy's warriors not to slight Avvai's lord as young (இளையன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The warning against scorning a young king is a rule of court conduct in the Kural and a battlefield threat in Avvai, which shows the same caution was voiced to subjects as duty and to enemies as danger."},{"a":"pn:106","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 221 calls giving only what goes to those who have nothing, all else being a measured exchange; 106 says Pari holds himself bound to give even to the foolish and the weak, as the gods accept even the erukkam flower.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines true giving by the asker having nothing to return, and Kapilar supplies the image for it, the god who accepts a worthless flower, so the giver's duty is modelled on a god's acceptance rather than on a bargain.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:107","b":"k:211","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 211 says obligation seeks no return, for the world gives nothing back to the rain; 107 says Pari is not the only one, for the rain too sustains the world.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kapilar's mock rebuke works only because the comparison of the giver with the rain was already accepted, as the Kural makes it a principle, so the joke assumes the doctrine and the doctrine has here a named man as its model."},{"a":"pn:107","b":"n:97","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 97 sets the rain and the world's doing what it should side by side as the two things the world survives by; 107 sets Pari and the rain side by side as sustainers of the world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's pairing of rain with human giving is the background against which 107's line reads as praise, and 107 shows that pairing applied to one patron centuries before it was stated as a general rule."},{"a":"pn:108","b":"k:223","type":"develops","note":"Kural 223 praises giving without uttering the misery of 'I have nothing'; 108 says Pari would not say 'I will not come' even if suppliants asked for him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural forbids the giver one refusal, the plea of having nothing; 108 imagines the case past it, where nothing is left and the giver offers himself, so the poem tests the maxim at its limit."},{"a":"pn:109","b":"pn:105","type":"develops","note":"105 sends a viraliyar to Pari with the promise of jewels; 109 tells the besieging kings that the only way to take Parambu is to come as bards with their viralis behind them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kapilar turns his own guide-poem formula into siege terms: the road that brings a performer gifts is the only road into the fort, which shows the guide poem's convention used as a political weapon."},{"a":"pn:109","b":"a:303","type":"same_image","note":"109 says Parambu needs no ploughing because its hill yields four foods by itself; Akananuru 303 describes flocks of sparrows of Pari's Parambu flying out at dawn to bring back red paddy and returning at evening.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both describe food coming into Parambu without its people farming, which suggests the self-feeding hill was a known part of Pari's legend that an akam poet could use as a ready simile for something sure to return."},{"a":"pn:110","b":"a:78","type":"same_image","note":"110 tells the three kings that Parambu is hard to take though they join forces; Akananuru 78 recalls Pari, with Kapilar beside him, driving off the tusked kings after years of fighting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poem treats as settled history what 110 states as a boast during the siege, so the pair shows the defence of Parambu passing from Kapilar's own war-speech into the shared stock of akam similes.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:111","b":"pn:112","type":"tension","note":"111 says the hill cannot be won by kings with the spear and only a singer can have it; 112 says kings with victorious drums have taken our hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side in the anthology, the boast that the hill could not be taken and the daughters' report that it was taken show how the editors let the poems record the siege's outcome against Kapilar's claim."},{"a":"pn:112","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 says even kings who led armies under the umbrella fall when their deeds turn and their enemies take their wives; 112 has a giver's daughters say the kings took their hill and they have no father.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar explains a ruler's fall by his own spent deeds, but 112 gives no cause and its subject is a famous giver, so the lament qualifies the moral account: here loss falls on the blameless and the poem does not explain it."},{"a":"pn:113","b":"pn:200","type":"develops","note":"113 ends with Kapilar leaving Parambu to find husbands for Pari's daughters; in 200 he offers them in marriage to Vichikko.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems give the beginning and a later stage of one errand: the lament's last line becomes a formal offer in 200, which shows the duty of the dead patron's poet carried out as a sequence of poems."},{"a":"pn:116","b":"pn:109","type":"develops","note":"109 predicts that the besieging kings will not take Parambu by force; 116 recalls that the daughters once counted from the hill the horses of kings who came to fight without knowing Pari's strength, and now count salt carts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read after 109, 116 shows what the confident siege-speech left out: the kings did come, and the poet who promised the fort was safe now measures its fall by what the daughters count."},{"a":"pn:117","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of a ruler who wields the sceptre rightly; 117 says Pari's land never lacked rain, even under the omens of drought, because of the straightness of his sceptre.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule is given to crowned kings, and 117 grants it to a hill chief and sets it above the stars' omens, so the poem shows the doctrine of the just sceptre extended to any lord who rules well.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:119","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"Kural 216 likens the wealth of a kindly man to a fruit tree ripening in the middle of a town; 119 likens Pari to a lone tree on a long road without shade.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural puts its tree where everyone can reach it, while 119 puts it where nothing else gives shelter, so the poem adds that a giver's worth is measured by how few others give.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:120","b":"pn:119","type":"same_image","note":"Both lists of the land's harvest break off with the same question, நந்துங் கொல்லோ (will it last?), before naming the land as the dead patron's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared refrain shows the poems were made as a set in which each catalogue of plenty is undercut by the same doubt, so the anthology's sequence on Pari works like a song with a refrain."},{"a":"pn:121","b":"k:528","type":"develops","note":"121 tells Kari to give up the common gaze (பொது நோக்கு) and know each bard's rank (வரிசை); Kural 528 says a king who looks by rank (வரிசையா) rather than in common (பொதுநோக்கான்) draws many to live around him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's two technical words for graded favour already stand in Kapilar's advice to a patron, so the Kural's rule for a king's kin was first argued by poets about how gifts should be ranked among themselves."},{"a":"pn:121","b":"pn:140","type":"qualifies","note":"121 says giving is easy and knowing due rank (வரிசை அறிதல்) is hard; 140 shows the lord of Nanjil, 'knowing rank' (வரிசை அறிதலின்), answering a request for rice with an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems show that வரிசை cut both ways: 121 wants the patron to rank the recipient, 140 praises a patron who ranked by his own greatness and so overpaid, so the measure of a gift could be the giver's standing as well as the receiver's worth.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:121","b":"a:152","type":"tension","note":"121 asks the patron to stop treating all poets alike; Akananuru 152 praises Ay for giving freely to those who come whether skilled or not (வல்லினும் வல்லார் ஆயினும் சென்றோர்க்கு). On the plain reading the two ideals of giving are incompatible.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poet borrows as an ornament exactly the indiscriminate giving that Kapilar argues against, so the ideal of the patron who gives to all was common fame, and 121 is a poet's dissent from it, not the standard view."},{"a":"pn:123","b":"a:209","type":"same_image","note":"123 praises Malayan of unfading fame (தொலையா நல்லிசை) at Mullur; Akananuru 209 names the Mullur king Kari whose fame does not pass (செல்லா நல்லிசை), who killed Ori and gave the Kolli hills to the Cheras.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam friend uses Kari's lasting fame as a fixed point to measure the heroine's beauty, which shows that the bards' praise of Kari had become a shared reference that akam poets could cite to measure something else entirely.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:126","b":"a:35","type":"same_image","note":"126 calls Malaiyaman Kari lord of a land of palmyra groves (பெண்ணையம் படப்பை நாடு); Akananuru 35 names Kari of the long chariot, lord of Kovalur, and the great river Pennai (பெண்ணையம் பேரியாறு) at his ford.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's lament in Akananuru 35 places her daughter's flight on the river of Kari's land, so a puram patron's territory supplies the akam poem's geography, and the shared பெண்ணையம் suggests that 126's phrase may name the river land rather than palms alone.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:128","b":"a:322","type":"same_image","note":"128 says Ay's Pothiyil may be approached only by a dancing woman, while proud kings can hardly come near; Akananuru 322 compares the beloved, hard to win, to Pothiyil, hard to enter (புகலரும் பொதியில் போல).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam lover's simile takes its force from the puram boast: Pothiyil's inaccessibility was a known fact of Ay's power, and the akam poet turned a chief's closed hill into a measure of a woman's reserve."},{"a":"pn:122","b":"pn:135","type":"same_image","note":"122 says nothing is Kari's own except his wife (நினது என இலை); 135 wishes Ay long life with the inheritance he does not grasp as his (எமதெனப் பற்றல் தேற்றா).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets praising two different chiefs use the same figure of the owner who owns nothing, which marks it as a convention of the patron's praise rather than a fact about either man."},{"a":"pn:127","b":"k:1005","type":"develops","note":"127 sets Ay's emptied palace against the cities of drum-owning kings who eat alone, give nothing and lose their fame; Kural 1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, though they hold crores.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's paradox that unshared wealth is poverty is shown in 127 from the other side, a bare palace that is rich in fame, so the pair gives both halves of one reckoning: the miser's full store is empty and the giver's empty hall is full."},{"a":"pn:134","b":"n:95","type":"tension","note":"134 rejects as a merchant of virtue anyone who gives because what is done in this life serves the next; Naladiyar 95 tells the giver to give with an eye to the next world and this one (மறுமையும் இம்மையும் நோக்கி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's advice is the very motive the Sangam poet holds up for scorn, which shows that giving for the next birth was a live doctrine both poets knew, and that the older praise poetry judged it a lesser reason than custom and honour."},{"a":"pn:134","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"134 says Ay does not give as a trade for the next world but because it is the path of the worthy; Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both refuse to found giving on reward hereafter, but they put different things in its place: the Kural a bare judgment that giving is good, the Purananuru the example of the worthy, so the same denial could rest either on principle or on custom."},{"a":"pn:136","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"136 argues that those who give to the bard give to others and those who give to others give to themselves; Kural 221 says only giving to the destitute is giving and all else is a measured return (குறியெதிர்ப்பை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The starving bard is exactly the Kural's destitute recipient, yet he argues that such giving does come back to the giver, so the Kural's line between true gift and loan was drawn against an older view in which even giving to the poor was defended by its return."},{"a":"pn:139","b":"n:5","type":"develops","note":"139 urges the lord of Nanjil to give now, since he does not fear death for his king and a battle may come any day; Naladiyar 5 says not to hold back a gift thinking it can be given later, since Death drags all away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's general reason for giving now, the certainty of death, appears in 139 as a bard's practical argument tied to the warrior patron's own courage, which shows how the moralist's maxim was already a working plea in the bard's trade.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:129","b":"pn:123","type":"same_image","note":"123 counts Malayan's chariots as more than the raindrops on Mullur; 129 counts Ay's elephants against the stars of a sky with no dark patch.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kapilar and Mudamosiyar measure gifts against a natural multitude, but Mudamosiyar adds a condition the real sky fails, so the second poet sharpens the formula by saying nature itself falls short of the count."},{"a":"pn:141","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"141 says Pekan's giving does not look to the next world (மறுமை நோக்கின்றோ அன்றே) but to others' want; Kural 222 says giving alone is good even if there were no higher world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's hypothetical, giving is good even without heaven, is already the bards' test of a true patron, and 141 adds the positive motive the couplet leaves unstated: the giver looks at the receiver's want, as the useless gift to the peacock proves."},{"a":"pn:141","b":"pn:145","type":"develops","note":"141 cites the cloak given to the peacock as proof that Pekan gives without calculation; 145 cites the same cloak (படாஅம் ஈத்த) to plead for his abandoned wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The peacock story served the bards both ways: as praise of pure giving in 141 and as leverage in 145, where a patron famous for pitying a bird is shamed into pitying his wife, so a patron's legend could be used against him."},{"a":"pn:142","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Both liken giving to rain; Kural 211 makes the rain a duty that asks no return, while 142 notes that rain also wastes itself on salt land and calls Pekan's giving கொடைமடம், folly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps only the dutiful side of the rain image, but the Purananuru poet saw its waste as well, so the rain figure for generosity carried an admitted cost of indiscrimination that the maxim leaves out.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:143","b":"pn:144","type":"develops","note":"143 asks who the weeping woman at the door was (யார்கொல் அளியள்) without answering; 144 tells the same kind of scene and lets the woman answer that she is no kin of Pekan's and that he goes to another woman.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read in sequence, the Pekan poems work as a cycle in which each poet adds what the last withheld: Kapilar leaves the woman unnamed, Paranar gives her a voice and names the cause, so the reproach of a patron was built up by several poets in turn.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:144","b":"a:262","type":"same_image","note":"Paranar rebukes Pekan in 144 for leaving his wife for another woman; Paranar in Akananuru 262 has a lover compare his beloved's fragrance to Pekan's rain-cloud hill (வண்மகிழ்ப் பேகன் கொண்டல் மாமலை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet uses Pekan's hill as a sweet image of union in akam while rebuking Pekan's own broken marriage in puram, so the patron's name entered akam similes as pure fame, cleansed of the scandal the puram poems record."},{"a":"pn:150","b":"pn:152","type":"same_image","note":"In 150 a strong-bowed hunter feeds the poet, gives his chain and will not tell his name, and is Nalli; in 152 a hunter who killed five beasts with one arrow is abashed when hailed as king, gives the meat of his kill, and is Ori.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two poets tell the same scene of a chieftain met as an unnamed hunter in the forest who feeds the bard from his kill, so the incognito hunter-giver was a recognised type of patron story, in which the gift is proved genuine by being given before the giver is known."},{"a":"pn:150","b":"a:238","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 238 names Nalli for rain-like giving of chariots and elephants to suppliants (மழைசுரந் தன்ன ஈகை) as a simile's backdrop; 150 tells one of his gifts in full, a chain from his chest given to a ragged poet in the forest.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The akam epithet compresses into a formula of chariots and elephants a fame that the puram poem shows was made of personal, unannounced gifts, so the akam poet's shorthand assumes hearers who knew stories like 150.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:151","b":"pn:150","type":"agrees","note":"151 praises the house of Kandeerakkon, where women give she-elephants when the lord is away; 150 shows Nalli, whom the colophons call Kandeerakkon, giving while concealing his name.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems praise this house for giving that does not wait on the lord being seen as giver, by his women in his absence or by himself unnamed, so the Kandeera fame was for giving as a household habit, not a court performance."},{"a":"pn:151","b":"a:152","type":"tension","note":"151 explains a snub: the poets shunned the hill of Nannan's kin because of a door shut against singers; Akananuru 152 praises Nannan for famous giving, flinging elephants (இசைநல் ஈகைக் களிறுவீசு). On the reading that the same line of Nannan is meant, the two reputations are incompatible.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Whether one Nannan or two, the name carried opposite reputations for giving in the two anthologies, which shows that a patron's fame among bards was contested and could be undone by one shut door."},{"a":"pn:153","b":"pn:149","type":"develops","note":"149 says Nalli's giving made the bards forget the hours of their modes; 153 says Ori's gifts made the troupe stop dancing and singing, since they were no longer hungry.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Two poets praise two patrons with the same joke, that plenty spoils the performer's craft, which shows that the bards themselves saw their art as driven by need and counted its loss as the fullest proof of a gift."},{"a":"pn:154","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"154 makes asking for a little water from someone known the natural image for seeking out the generous; Kural 1066 says even asking water for a cow is the greatest degradation of the tongue. On the plain reading the same act is natural in one and shameful in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The bard and the moralist used the same smallest request in opposite ways: for the poet it shows that seeking the generous is as natural as thirst, for the Kural it shows that no request is small enough to be clean, though 154 itself concedes that asking is hard (இரத்தலோ அரிதே).","gr":2},{"a":"pn:154","b":"pn:204","type":"same_image","note":"154 says the thirsty by the sea ask a known person for a little water; 204 says the thirsty do not drink from the great sea but find many paths to drinking water, even if muddied by cattle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sea that cannot quench thirst was a shared figure for great but useless wealth, and the two poets turn it differently: 154 uses it to explain why poets pass by kings, 204 to say a refused seeker should go elsewhere without blaming the giver."},{"a":"pn:157","b":"k:1015","type":"develops","note":"157 counts among Erai's virtues being ashamed at others' destitution (பிறர் கையறவு தான் நாணுதல்); Kural 1015 calls those who feel shame for others' disgrace as for their own the dwelling of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's widened shame, felt for what others suffer, appears first as a chief's virtue praised by a woman poet of his hill, where its object is concretely other people's want rather than their disgrace in general."},{"a":"pn:158","b":"a:209","type":"qualifies","note":"158 lists Malaiyan (who rode Kari) and Ori among the seven dead givers; Akananuru 209 says Kari of Mullur killed Ori and gave Kolli to the Cheras (ஓரிக் கொன்று சேரலர்க்கு ஈத்த).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The canon of the seven givers sets side by side two men one of whom killed the other, so the list honours giving alone and is silent about the wars among the givers, which the akam poet remembers in a simile."},{"a":"pn:158","b":"pn:152","type":"develops","note":"158 names 'Ori of the strong bow who ruled Kolli' (கொல்லி ஆண்ட வல்வில் ஓரி) in one line; 152 tells the hunt in which his single arrow passed through five beasts and the gifts he gave the bards.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epithet in the roll-call condenses an exploit that 152 tells whole, so the list of seven assumed an audience that knew the separate poems on each giver.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:159","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"159 will refuse an elephant given grudgingly and take a kunri seed given gladly; Kural 1057 says the heart rejoices on seeing those who give without scorn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the asker's joy at a gift without contempt; 159 turns it into a rule of conduct for the asker, who must refuse a grudging gift even while his family starves, so the manner of giving decided whether a bard could accept at all.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:159","b":"k:1061","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1061 says not asking even from those who give gladly is worth ten million times more; 159 treats asking a glad giver as honourable and refuses only the grudging gift.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bard's code and the Kural's draw the line of dignity in different places: for the bard the giver's gladness makes asking clean, for the Kural even the gladdest giver does not, so the Kural's chapter on the dread of asking rejects the very compromise the parisil poems lived by.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:160","b":"pn:164","type":"same_image","note":"160 and 164, by different poets to the same Kumanan, both show the poet's child sucking his mother's milkless breast and crying (வறுமுலை), with empty cooking pots.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets bring Kumanan the same picture of the child at the dry breast, so the starving household was a set piece of the parisil kadaanilai addressed to this patron, not one poet's private confession."},{"a":"pn:160","b":"n:111","type":"develops","note":"160 asks to be sent off quickly (வல்லே விடுதல் வேண்டுவல்); Naladiyar 111 says keeping a seeker waiting till hope dies is worse than ingratitude.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar faults delay as the giver's breach of truth; 160 shows what the delay costs at the other end, a mother distracting a hungry child at home, which is why the bard asks for speed before size."},{"a":"pn:161","b":"pn:162","type":"develops","note":"In 161 Perunchiththiranar asks Kumanan for a tusker to ride home on so that ungiving kings are shamed; in 162 the same poet ties an elephant to Ilaveliman's guard tree and leaves, saying givers exist elsewhere.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems show the gift elephant doing what 161 promised: the bard's reward from one patron becomes a public rebuke staked at the door of a patron who failed him, so giving among chiefs was a contest the bards could stage."},{"a":"pn:162","b":"k:1055","type":"develops","note":"162 says to Ilaveliman: see that askers exist and that givers exist; Kural 1055 says askers stand before others because there are people in the world who do not withhold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural explains asking by the existence of givers in general; 162 uses that same general truth as a weapon against one particular chief, so the principle that kept the asker's dignity also let him walk away from a mean patron."},{"a":"pn:163","b":"pn:159","type":"develops","note":"In 159 the poet pictures his wife boiling unsalted greens and his starving household as the reason for his plea to Kumanan; in 163 he tells her to give Kumanan's gift away to all, without consulting him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The household whose hunger was the bard's argument for the gift becomes, as soon as the gift arrives, a giver to its own kin and creditors, so the bard's reward was expected to circulate, not to be saved."},{"a":"pn:164","b":"pn:160","type":"same_image","note":"Both petitions to Kumanan picture the bard's child sucking a breast dry for want of food (160 lines 17 to 19; 164 lines 3 to 5).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two different poets press Kumanan with the same picture of the child at the milkless breast, which shows it was a set argument of the plea for a gift (பரிசில் கடாநிலை) rather than one poet's private report.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:165","b":"pn:160","type":"develops","note":"160 shows Kumanan at Muthiram flinging away gold vessels so the bards' troupe may not fail; 165 shows a giver who has lost his land offering his own head because a disappointed bard is worse to him than that loss.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside 160's lavish lord in his own hill, 165 tests the same habit of giving after the land is gone: what remains to give is the self, so the giver's fame survives his loss of wealth."},{"a":"pn:165","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 230 says death is the bitterest thing, yet sweet when one cannot give; in 165 the giver says a bard going away disappointed is worse than losing his country and gives his head.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking of giving above life is a figure of speech in a couplet; 165 gives it a narrative in which a dispossessed chief literally prefers death to failing a bard, so the maxim had a heroic story behind it.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:165","b":"k:233","type":"agrees","note":"165 opens: those who wished to last in an unlasting world set up their fame and died; Kural 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except towering fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the rule abstractly; 165 places it as the premise of a gift poem and adds its converse, that the ungiving rich are forgotten, so fame here is something the bard both records and withholds."},{"a":"pn:167","b":"k:776","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 776 makes the day without a noble wound a wasted day; 167 grants the king's scars are sweet to hear of but says they are harsh to see, and the enemies' whole skins sweet to see.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural counts the wound only as the warrior's own ledger of worth, 167 adds the onlooker's split response, so the wound's value lies in report and fame, not in how it looks."},{"a":"pn:169","b":"a:113","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the Kosar of many spears: in Akananuru 113 as loyal guards whose rich land stands for the heroine's lover, in 169 as young men practising spear throws at a murukku post, the simile for Pittan's strength.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The Kosar were a byword for spear-skill and fidelity known to both genres: the akam poet borrows their loyalty to measure a lover's land, the puram poet their training post to measure a chief, so their fame served as a ready image in both kinds of poem."},{"a":"pn:170","b":"pn:180","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems bring in the dark-handed smith (கருங்கைக் கொல்லன்): in 170 the anvil that takes his hammer is the chief facing his foes; in 180 the poor chief asks his town's smith to forge a spear to feed the bards.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The smith's forge stands in both poems for the link between fighting and giving: 170 makes the chief the anvil that wears out his enemies, and 180 shows the spear made there as the source of the bards' meal.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:173","b":"k:227","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 227 calls hunger a fierce disease (பசியென்னும் தீப்பிணி) that will not touch one who shares food; 173 calls Pannan the physician of the disease of hunger (பசிப்பிணி மருத்துவன்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books treat hunger as a disease, but the Kural says sharing protects the sharer while 173 makes the giver the healer of other people's hunger, so the image moves from the giver's safety to the hungry crowd's cure.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:173","b":"pn:70","type":"develops","note":"In 70 a bard steering another to Killi Valavan names on the way the village of Pannan the open-handed; in 173 a Chola king, Killi Valavan by the colophon, wishes his own years on Pannan and calls him the physician of hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"70 gives Pannan as a landmark of generosity on the road to the king; 173 has a king himself defer to that small-village chief, so Pannan's fame for giving stood in the anthology beside, not beneath, a crowned king's."},{"a":"pn:174","b":"pn:123","type":"develops","note":"123 is Kapilar's praise of Malayan, whose gifts outnumber the raindrops on Mullur; 174 refers to Mullur as the hill truthful-tongued Kapilan sang and praises a later chief of the same house.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"174 cites the earlier poem by its poet and place, which shows that a Sangam poet could rest a patron's fame on a famous song already in circulation, and that Mullur's renown was itself made by Kapilar's verse."},{"a":"pn:175","b":"a:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the formula of the Moriyar with sky-touching umbrellas whose chariot wheels cut a pass through rock (Akananuru 69 lines 10 to 12; 175 lines 6 to 8).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same Mauryan landmark serves akam as the far, dangerous road the hero crosses for wealth and puram as a figure for the fixed, unfailing care of a patron, so the formula's sense came from the poem it was set in, not from the place."},{"a":"pn:175","b":"k:107","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 107 says one remembers the friendship of those who wiped away one's sorrow for seven births; 175 says the poet will forget Athanungan only when he forgets himself, when life leaves the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural stretches gratitude across seven births, while the Purananuru poet bounds it by this one life and locates it in his body, which shows the older poem's gratitude making no claim on rebirth."},{"a":"pn:176","b":"pn:109","type":"develops","note":"109 says Parambu's springs are as many as the stars and that it cannot be starved; 176 uses those cool clear springs as what people living beside them did not value.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"109 offers the springs as proof that Pari's hill never fails, and 176 turns that very constancy into a lesson on neglect: what is always at hand, a spring or a near patron, goes unvalued.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:179","b":"pn:158","type":"develops","note":"158 lists the seven great givers who are dead and turns to Kumanan as the one left; 179 opens with the generous of the earth dead and the bard's bowl upturned, and names Nagan of Nalai as the one who will fill it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"After the seven givers died became a reusable opening: 158 applies it to a hill king and 179 to a Pandya's soldier, so a lesser patron could be raised into the line of the great givers by the same formula."},{"a":"pn:180","b":"pn:167","type":"develops","note":"167 says the king's sword-scarred body is sweet to hear of and harsh to see; 180 describes a chief whose body is all scars, who has no wealth and gives by having a spear forged.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"167 values the scars as fame; 180 shows them as the price of the bards' meal, since a chief without treasure gives only by fighting, so the wound in puram poetry is both glory and the means of giving."},{"a":"pn:171","b":"n:159","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 159 says the excellent honour a frequent visitor each time they see him; 171 says Kotran gives whether we come today, later or every day, never saying he gave before.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar states as a mark of character what 171 records of one chief's practice with bards, so the later maxim about the frequent visitor had its model in the patron who gave daily without reproach."},{"a":"pn:168","b":"n:100","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 100 says the word 'they gave' is heard through the three worlds, further than drum or thunder; 168 says bards praise Kotran across the Tamil land every day, shaming kings who do not give.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"168 shows the mechanism the Naladiyar leaves out: the fame of giving travels because the bards who received the gifts carry it, and it is aimed at the kings who did not give."},{"a":"pn:182","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the world stands because of a certain kind of person: 182 names those who strive for others, Kural 996 those of courtesy (பண்புடையார்), without whom it would sink into the earth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into a two-line rule what the Pandya prince builds as a list of concrete refusals, so the reader of the Kural can see which deeds (not eating alone, dying for honour, refusing blame-tainted gain) its abstract பண்பு was taken to mean.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:182","b":"k:322","type":"agrees","note":"182 opens its catalogue with not eating alone even the nectar of the gods; Kural 322 makes sharing one's food the chief of all virtues.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Sharing food heads the list in both books, which shows that in early Tamil ethics the shared meal, not an inner disposition, was the first test of a good person.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:182","b":"pn:194","type":"same_image","note":"182 opens உண்டால் அம்ம, இவ்வுலகம் (the world exists, indeed) and 194 says இன்னாது அம்ம, இவ் வுலகம் (the world is harsh, indeed): the same exclamation over the same world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two poets use one formula to say opposite things about this world, and each turns it to action, so the formula shows the anthology arguing with itself: the world is kept going by the selfless in 182, and is bitter yet must be met by seeking good in 194.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:183","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank learning above birth: in 183 a man of the highest order defers to a learned man of the lowest, and Kural 409 says the unlearned high-born lack the worth of the learned low-born.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's maxim was already a king's teaching in the Purananuru, where Nedunchezhiyan keeps the four orders by name and lets learning reverse them, so neither text abolishes birth rank: both use it as the measure that learning overturns.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:183","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"Kural 69 has the mother rejoice when her son is called worthy; 183 has the mother's heart turn toward the accomplished child among children of one womb.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural shows a mother's joy at one son's public praise, the Purananuru admits the harder consequence, that learning makes her favour one child over his brothers, so the pair shows that the honour of learning reorders even family love.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:184","b":"k:552","type":"develops","note":"Kural 552 calls the sceptre-holder's demand a robber's demand at spear-point; 184 likens the greedy king to an elephant loose in a field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural condemns excess levy from the subject's side as robbery, while Pisirandhaiyar argues from the king's side that grabbing wastes more than it yields and leaves the king himself unfed, so the same rule is given both a moral and a prudential ground.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:186","b":"k:542","type":"develops","note":"Kural 542 sets the world's dependence on the sky beside the people's dependence on the sceptre; 186 denies rice and water the name of life and gives it to the king alone.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural keeps rain and rule as parallel supports, but Mosikeeranar ranks the king above food and water and then turns the claim into the king's own duty to know it, so the pair shows the same dependence read once as praise and once as a burden laid on the ruler.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:187","b":"k:737","type":"tension","note":"Kural 737 defines a country by its limbs: waters, a well-placed hill, the water from it and a strong fort. 187 says no kind of ground, plain, forest, low or high, makes a land good, only the goodness of its men. On the reading that both answer what makes a land good, they disagree.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's chapter on the land lists natural and built assets, while Avvaiyar dismisses terrain altogether, so a reader sees two answers to one question, resources or people, and that the Purananuru gave the moral answer before the treatise gave the material one.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:187","b":"pn:186","type":"qualifies","note":"186 says the world lives by its king; 187 says a land is good wherever its men are good, naming no king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side in the anthology, the two maxims locate the source of a land's well-being in different hands, the ruler in 186 and the men at large in 187, so the reader sees that Sangam counsel did not rest the realm on the king alone.","gr":7},{"a":"pn:188","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn on a small child's hand in the food: Kural 64 says gruel stirred by children's small hands is sweeter than nectar; 188 lists the child's touching, biting, stirring and scattering of ghee rice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the image a measure of sweetness, while the Pandya king makes its absence a verdict that a rich man's feasting life is profitless, so one scene serves as delight in one book and as the test of a wasted life in the other.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:189","b":"n:9","type":"agrees","note":"189 says the use of wealth is giving and whoever means to enjoy it alone loses much; Naladiyar 9 says the man who neither eats, gives nor relieves his kin but guards his wealth is counted as one who has lost it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both declare kept wealth already lost, but for different reasons: Nakkeerar from the fixed measure one body can eat and wear, the Naladiyar from the miser's failure in every use, so the pair gives the conclusion one ground in the body and one in duty.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:190","b":"n:300","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the tiger that will not eat prey that has fallen on its left side and goes hungry instead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar uses the tiger's refusal as a model of one's own honour, while the Chola poet uses it to choose whom to befriend, so the image was a shared emblem of high-mindedness that each book bent to its own question.","gr":7},{"a":"pn:191","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 11 says the wise, knowing grey hair will come, renounced in childhood; 191 says grey hair has not come to the poet because of his good wife, children, servants, king and the worthy men of his town. On the reading that both speak of how to meet old age, one counsels leaving the household and the other credits it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's answer to ageing is renunciation, while Pisirandhaiyar's is a good household and good company, so the pair shows two Tamil remedies for the same fear, one by leaving the world early and one by living well in it.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:192","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 379 asks why those who accept good when it comes grieve when it does not; 192 neither rejoices that life is sweet nor calls it bitter, because a life follows its order.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both ground equanimity on an order the person does not control, but the Kural names it ஊழ் and argues, while Kaniyan gives the picture of a raft carried on a flood, so the abstract fate of the Kural is given an image of how it works.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:192","b":"k:397","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 397 says every land and town becomes one's own, and asks why anyone stays unlearned; 192 says every town is ours and everyone our kin, with no condition of learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes belonging everywhere a reward of learning, while Kaniyan makes it a consequence of seeing that good and ill are not given by others, so the pair shows the same cosmopolitan claim earned once by study and once by insight.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:194","b":"pn:192","type":"tension","note":"194 declares இன்னாது அம்ம, இவ் வுலகம், this world is harsh, and blames its maker; 192 says we do not in disgust call life bitter (இன்னாது என்றலும் இலமே). Read as judgments of the world, they are incompatible.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One anthology holds both the verdict that the world is bitter and the refusal to pass that verdict, using the same word இன்னாது, so the reader sees that Purananuru ethics offered two stances to grief, protest and equanimity, rather than one doctrine.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:194","b":"n:23","type":"tension","note":"Both set a wedding drum against a funeral drum. Naladiyar 23 draws the lesson that the worthy hold to the way of escape; 194 tells those who know the world's nature to look for what is sweet (இனிய காண்க). On the reading that இனிய means good deeds in this life, the two conclusions diverge.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same sight of joy and mourning side by side leads the Naladiyar to renunciation and the Purananuru poet to seeking good within the world, which shows the later didactic book reusing a Sangam image for a different, ascetic conclusion.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:195","b":"k:335","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 335 says good deeds must be hurried to before the death rattle stops the tongue; 195, facing old men who have done no good, asks at least that they refrain from harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's urgency assumes one can still act well, while the Purananuru poet sets a floor for those who will not, so the pair shows that early Tamil counsel allowed abstaining from harm to count as a road to the good when doing good had failed.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:196","b":"k:1051","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1051 says if those worth asking withhold, the blame is theirs; 196 says a false promise or a false refusal lessens the giver's fame.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural states as a rule that withholding shames the giver, not the asker, and Avur Moolankizhar applies it to a named king's delay, so the rule had been used as a poet's weapon before it became a maxim.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:196","b":"k:1060","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1060 says the asker must not be angry; 196, after naming the king's fault, blesses his sons and leaves.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The poet shows how the Kural's demand for an asker without anger can be met without silence: he names the fault plainly and still blesses, so rebuke and the absence of anger are shown to go together.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:197","b":"k:408","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 408 says wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than the poverty of the good; 197 says the poet thinks nothing of the riches of the unfeeling and thinks of the poverty of the wise with joy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's comparison is spoken by a poet to a Chola king's face in the Purananuru, which shows that the maxim's ranking of the wise poor over the ignorant rich was first a bard's claim on his own dignity before a patron.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:197","b":"pn:192","type":"develops","note":"192 ends பெரியோரை வியத்தலும் இலமே, we do not marvel at the great; 197 says the poet does not marvel (வியத்தலோ இலமே) at kings' umbrella wealth but at small chiefs who know how to treat poets.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kaniyan's refusal to admire the great is a philosophical stance, and Kumaranar turns it into a bard's practice before a king, choosing whom to admire by conduct, so the pair shows a cosmological equanimity becoming a social rule of honour.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:197","b":"n:206","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 206 says saltless millet gruel among one's own is sweeter than milk rice from strangers' hands; 197 prizes the small chief's greens and varagu rice given with regard over kings' wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both value a poor meal for the regard it is given with, but the Naladiyar finds that regard among kin while the Purananuru finds it in a patron who knows a poet's worth, so the pair shows the bard's patron standing where the family stands in the later book.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:198","b":"pn:199","type":"same_image","note":"198 has the poet long for the king's grace like the bird that longs for a raindrop; 199 likens suppliants to birds that return daily to a fruiting banyan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems make the suppliant a bird, but 198's lone bird waits for a drop and begs not to be forgotten, while 199's flock returns without shame to a sure tree, so the pair shows the bard's two postures, anxious waiting and confident return, and what decides between them is the patron.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:199","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 216 likens the wealth of the kindly to a fruit tree ripening in the middle of a town; 199 likens suppliants to birds that return to a fruiting banyan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural sees the fruiting tree from the owner's side, as wealth placed where all can reach it, while Perumpadhumanaar sees it from the birds' side, as a place to return to daily, so together they give both halves of the gift relation and make the asker's return a sign of the giver's worth.","gr":7},{"a":"pn:200","b":"pn:201","type":"develops","note":"In 200 Kapilar offers Pari's daughters to Vichikko as a suppliant and brahmin; in 201 he offers them to Irungovel as their father's friend, naming himself அந்தணன், புலவன்.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together, Kapilar's two offers show him grounding the same gift in different claims, his standing as suppliant and brahmin in 200 and his friendship with their father in 201, so the reader sees how a bard, not a kinsman, asserted the right to give a dead patron's daughters.","gr":7},{"a":"pn:201","b":"pn:202","type":"develops","note":"201 offers Pari's daughters to Irungovel with praise of his lineage; 202, after the refusal, recalls that his ancestors' Araiyam fell for scorning a poet and withdraws the offer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read as a pair, the two poems show the bard's praise of lineage working as a bargaining position: the same forty-nine generations that 201 flatters become in 202 a house with a known record of ruin for slighting poets."},{"a":"pn:201","b":"a:78","type":"same_image","note":"201 is spoken after Pari's fall, with his daughters taken to another chief; Akananuru 78 measures the heroine's brow by the flowers of the pool of Pari, the giver who drove off kings (வேந்தர்ஓட்டிய).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet keeps Pari as the victor whose hill still blooms, while Kapilar's poem records the aftermath in which his daughters need a protector, so akam fixes a hero at his height that puram follows past his death."},{"a":"pn:202","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"202 closes by wishing Irungovel's spear victory; Akananuru 36 lists Irungo Venman among the seven defeated in one day by Chezhiyan at Alankanam.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"If the two name the same house, the akam friend's list of fallen kings shows the defeat that Kapilar's parting blessing does not foresee, and shows akam poets drawing on puram battle-lists as common knowledge to scale a quarrel.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:203","b":"k:224","type":"qualifies","note":"203 calls it cruel (இன்னாது) for a patron to refuse a returning bard with 'you took before'; Kural 224 says being asked is itself painful (இன்னாது) to the giver until he sees the asker's glad face.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same word marks the pain of the asking moment from opposite sides, the giver's in the Kural and the refused bard's in 203, so the couplet's candour about the giver's discomfort is answered by a poet who names what that discomfort costs the asker."},{"a":"pn:204","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"204 ranks four acts: asking low, refusing lower, giving high, refusing a gift higher; Kural 222 ranks two: taking is bad even on a good path, giving good even without heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poet's ladder adds a rung the Kural lacks, the refusal of what is offered, which shows that the Kural's verdict against taking had a heroic form in which the proud receiver outranks the giver."},{"a":"pn:204","b":"k:1051","type":"tension","note":"204 says those who come in hope blame the omens and the hour, never the patron; Kural 1051 says that when the worthy withhold, the blame is theirs and not the asker's. On the plain reading the two place the blame for a refused request in incompatible places.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The bard's courtesy in 204 is a deliberate refusal to lay the blame that the Kural says belongs to the withholder, so the poem shows the decorum that kept a disappointed poet in a patron's favour against the moral rule."},{"a":"pn:204","b":"a:209","type":"same_image","note":"204 praises Ori as a giver who pours like the rain cloud without limit; Akananuru 209 names Kari of Mullur, who killed Ori of the strong bow and gave Kolli to the Cheras.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem preserves Ori as a living patron while the akam friend cites his killing as a famous deed of another chief, so one man's fame survives in the anthologies as both a patron's praise and a rival's trophy.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:205","b":"a:13","type":"same_image","note":"205, by Perunthalai Sathanar, praises the hunter lord of Kodai whose bards never leave without chariots and elephants; Akananuru 13, by the same poet, names the Kodai lord who gives to suppliants without limit (வரைநிலை இன்றி) except the captured elephant.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same poet turns his patron's giving into an akam measure of what the hero would forgo by leaving, and his akam line keeps a detail the puram poem omits, that one gift, the captured elephant, was only promised by word."},{"a":"pn:205","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"205 says the bard does not want even the three kings' gifts if given without regard (பெட்பின்றி); Kural 1057 says the heart rejoices on seeing those who give without scorn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the receiver's joy at a gift given without contempt; 205 turns it into a condition of acceptance, so the manner of giving decided whether a bard would take the gift at all, not only how he felt about it.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:206","b":"pn:207","type":"develops","note":"206 has Avvai leave Anji's gate saying that wherever bards go, there is rice; 207 has Perunchithiranar tell his heart that the world is wide and providers many for those who want honour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets leaving two patrons reach the same exit, the claim that the world is wide, which shows it was a stock defence of the bard's honour against a patron's slight, not one poet's pique."},{"a":"pn:206","b":"k:1060","type":"qualifies","note":"206 shows the bard leaving in pride when kept waiting at the gate; Kural 1060 says the asker should not be angry when refused, since his own want shows wealth is hard to come by.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Avvai is not refused a gift but denied recognition at the gate, so the pair separates two cases the Kural's counsel of patience joins: want may excuse a refusal, but the bards held that a slight to their rank was not to be borne."},{"a":"pn:207","b":"k:786","type":"develops","note":"207 rejects a gift that does not make the heart glad and leaves the face fallen (அகம்நக வாரா முகன்அழி); Kural 786 says friendship that makes the face smile is not friendship, only that which makes the heart smile.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's pair of face and heart (முகநக, அகநக) already stands in a bard's test of a gift, so the measure the Kural applies to friendship was first a poet's measure of patronage."},{"a":"pn:208","b":"pn:134","type":"same_image","note":"208 says the poet is not a trading bard (வாணிகப் பரிசிலன்) for a gift given unseen; 134 says Ay is not a merchant of virtue (அறவிலை வணிகன்) who gives for a return in the next life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The figure of the trader is used against both sides of the gift: a giver who expects return and a receiver who takes without recognition are both merchants, so the ideal gift was one where neither party was trading."},{"a":"pn:209","b":"pn:207","type":"same_image","note":"209 likens the bard to birds that leave a fruit tree gone bare; 207 says only the faint-hearted pine for a hard fruit that will never ripen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poets cast the patron as a fruit tree, but 209 blames the tree for being bare while 207 blames the bard for waiting on unripe fruit, so the same image could carry a reproach to the patron or a resolve to leave him."},{"a":"pn:210","b":"pn:211","type":"develops","note":"Both poems are Perunkunrur Kizhar's to Kudakko Cheral Irumporai; 210 quotes the wife who thinks him dead, 211 ends at the house where the rat has died and the son has forgotten the breast.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems bring the bard's household to the king's court in two ways, the wife's speech in 210 and the bare house in 211, which shows the poor home used as evidence in a bard's case against a patron who kept him waiting."},{"a":"pn:211","b":"k:1067","type":"develops","note":"211 tells of a patron who showed the gift as if in hand and then stood by a lie; Kural 1067 begs all who beg not to ask of those who conceal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's advice not to beg from concealers is given from outside; 211 shows from inside what the bard does when the advice comes too late, shaming by praise (நாணக் கூறி) as his only remedy."},{"a":"pn:212","b":"pn:215","type":"same_image","note":"212, by Pisirandhaiyar, praises Kopperuncholan through the toddy, tortoise and eel of his land; 215, by the king, praises Pisirandhaiyar through the varagu, curd and tamarind gruel of his.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Each friend praises the other through the food of the other's home, the Chola's rich marutham fare and the Pandya-country poet's plain mullai fare, so the friendship poems are built as a matched pair across two kingdoms."},{"a":"pn:213","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"213 counsels the king that a war with those who rose against him (traditionally his sons) is lost whichever side wins; Kural 889 says inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed, holds ruin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural asserts that enmity within brings ruin; 213 gives the reason in a single case, since in a war inside the house the king loses his heirs if he wins and his honour if he loses."},{"a":"pn:214","b":"pn:134","type":"tension","note":"214 urges good deeds on the reckoning that they may win the world without deeds, release from birth, or fame; 134 praises Ay for not being a merchant of virtue who holds that what is done here serves the next life. On the plain reading 214 recommends the very motive 134 scorns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same anthology holds a king reasoning openly about the returns of merit and a poet scorning such reasoning in a patron, so the Sangam poets did not share one view of whether a deed's next-world return was a fit motive."},{"a":"pn:214","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"214 reasons that even if there is no joy beyond, good deeds may end rebirth or leave fame; Kural 222 says giving is good even if there is no higher world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both test good conduct on the supposition that there is no heaven, but the Kural rests there while the king reaches for a further reward, release or fame, so the pair shows two ways of answering doubt about the next world.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:215","b":"n:238","type":"develops","note":"215 says Pisiron may stay away in prosperity but will not stay away in trouble; Naladiyar 238 is an oath to rush to a friend in distress and put one's life in his hands.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's oath is a figure of speech, but in the tradition this chapter records the friend did come to share the king's death, so the puram poems supply a case in which the oath was taken literally."},{"a":"pn:216","b":"k:785","type":"develops","note":"216 says the friend, known by hearing alone and never met for many years, will come; Kural 785 says neither meeting nor long acquaintance is needed, feeling alone gives the claim (கிழமை) of friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule and the king's wager share the technical word கிழமை, so the couplet's claim that friendship needs no meeting has a famous royal case behind it, one staked on a seat kept for a man never seen.","gr":7},{"a":"pn:217","b":"pn:216","type":"develops","note":"216 has the king tell doubters that his unseen friend will come; 217 marvels that the king said 'he will come' and the friend came without fail.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The second poem is the fulfilment of the first, and it moves the wonder from the friend's faith to the king's foresight, so the pair shows the lament praising the dead man for having judged a friend rightly."},{"a":"pn:218","b":"n:236","type":"develops","note":"218 says the worthy join the worthy as gems from distant places meet in one jewel; Naladiyar 236 says the water-lily and the kuvalai grow in one pool, yet the small do not equal the great.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Naladiyar shows that nearness does not make likeness and the Sangam poem that distance does not prevent it, so together they make nature, not place, the ground of fellowship.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:219","b":"pn:218","type":"develops","note":"218 says the worthy fall to the side of the worthy; 219 tells the fasting king that many have already sat with him in his resolve.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The maxim of 218 becomes in 219 a crowd on the river island, which shows that the lament poems treat the number of those who joined the king's death as the measure of his worth.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:220","b":"pn:212","type":"develops","note":"212 shows Kopperuncholan's land of festivals and his daily gift of laughter; 220 shows the assembly of the old town empty after Killi has gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems bracket the Chola's life in the chapter, the festive court of 212 and the empty hall of 220, so the sequence of the anthology itself tells the story of the king's death."},{"a":"pn:221","b":"k:771","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 771 boasts that many who stood against the speaker's lord now stand as stones (கல்நின் றவர்); 221 ends with the generous patron himself become a hero-stone (நடுகல் ஆயினன் புரவலன்), mourned by poets.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Kural the hero-stone is the fate a warrior threatens on his enemies, while in 221 it is the end of the patron the poets loved, so the same stone that marks a foe's defeat also marks the giver's fame, and the boast of 771 is one side of a monument that kaiyarunilai shows from the other."},{"a":"pn:222","b":"a:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 55 recalls Cheralathan sitting facing north (வான்வடக் கிருந்தென) after his wound at Venni and the worthies who went with him to the other world; in 222 the poet reproaches his king for telling him to come only after his son was born.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The akam simile shows that companions dying with a king who sat facing north was a known and honoured act, which explains why being told to wait for a son's birth is felt in 222 as a lack of love: the friend was barred from an honour the custom gave him."},{"a":"pn:223","b":"k:801","type":"develops","note":"Kural 801 defines old friendship as a bond that never curtails the liberties of intimacy (கிழமை); 223 says the king, even as a hero-stone, gives room to friends of old intimacy (கிழமைத் தொன்னட் புடையார்) who come to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's definition is carried past death in 223: the claim of intimacy that a friend may exercise unasked includes the right to follow the king into death, and the stone honours it by making room, so pazhaimai is tested at its extreme.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:224","b":"a:55","type":"same_image","note":"224 mourns the king of the colophon, Karikalan, listing his battles, feeding of bards and sacrifice; Akananuru 55 recalls Karikal Valavan's victory at Venni, after which the defeated Cheral sat facing north and worthies died with him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Karikalan is remembered in the akam simile through the mourning his victory caused in the defeated court, and in 224 through his own women's stripped ornaments, so the same king stands in the corpus at both ends of the grief that war produces."},{"a":"pn:225","b":"pn:237","type":"same_image","note":"Line 7 of 225 and line 13 of 237 are the same line, கள்ளி போகிய களரியம் பறந்தலை, naming the cremation ground where Nalankilli's strength now lies and where the young hero perished.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets mourning different patrons reach for the identical line for the burning ground, which shows that kaiyarunilai drew on a shared stock of formulas, and that what made each poem particular was what it set on that ground: an army's might in 225, a bard's lost hope in 237.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:226","b":"n:20","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 20 pictures merciless Death roaming for its victims and seizing even the unborn while the mother wails; 226 says Death could not have taken Valavan by rage or force and must have begged him like a bard.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the Naladiyar's account no one escapes a Death that takes by force, while 226 claims that this king was beyond Death's force and fell only to his own generosity, so the bardic lament denies the very powerlessness before Death that the moralist uses to urge virtue."},{"a":"pn:228","b":"pn:231","type":"qualifies","note":"228 asks how a potter could make an urn big enough for Valavan, with the earth for his wheel and a mountain for clay; 231 says that whether Anji's pyre is low or reaches the sky, his fame will not perish.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"228 measures the king's greatness by the size of the funeral vessel it would need, while 231 declares the size of the pyre irrelevant to fame, so the two poems show the funeral rite being used both as the measure of greatness and as the thing greatness escapes."},{"a":"pn:230","b":"pn:227","type":"same_image","note":"227 tells Death it has cooked and eaten its seed (வித்துஅட்டு உண்டனை) in taking Valavan; 230 likens Death to a failing farmer who eats his seed (வித்து உண்டாஅங்கு) in taking Ezhini.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets on different kings make the same argument through the seed image, that a slain warrior was the source of Death's future harvest of enemies, which shows that taunting Death with its own loss was a recognised move in kaiyarunilai and not one poet's conceit.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:231","b":"k:233","type":"develops","note":"Kural 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except unmatched renown; 231 says Anji's fame will not perish whether his pyre shrinks or rises to the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Avvai's poem gives the Kural's general claim its occasion: fame is undying precisely at the pyre, where everything else of the man is being burned, so the maxim's 'nothing else stands' is shown in the act of the body's ending.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:232","b":"pn:235","type":"develops","note":"In 235 Avvai remembers Anji sharing his toddy with her; in 232 she pictures toddy poured from a small cup on his hero-stone and asks whether he who refused a whole country will accept it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The toddy he once shared with his poet returns in 232 as a funeral offering to his stone, so the rite reverses their old roles (now she gives and he must receive), which is why Avvai cannot believe he will accept it."},{"a":"pn:233","b":"a:115","type":"same_image","note":"233 refuses the dawn report of spear wounds on the chest of Evvi, leader of the bards' families; Akananuru 115 uses the bards who, in the battle where Evvi fell (எவ்வி வீழ்ந்த செருவில் பாணர்), broke off the curved necks of their lutes, as a simile for konrai pods on a desert path.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The akam poet assumes his hearers know that bards mourned Evvi by breaking their lutes, so the death the puram poet cannot bring himself to believe became, in the akam tradition, a fixed public memory usable as a passing image of curved pods.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:234","b":"k:229","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 229 says eating alone to fill one's own store is more bitter than begging; 234 grieves that Evvi, who always ate with many at a gate open to the world, is now fed alone a small rice ball on grass by his wife.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural condemns solitary eating as the miser's choice, and 234 shows death forcing that very condition on the most open-handed host, so the bitterness the Kural assigns to the hoarder becomes, in the lament, the injury done to the giver by his funeral rite."},{"a":"pn:235","b":"k:1007","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1007 likens the wealth of one who gives nothing to a beautiful woman growing old alone; 235 ends by likening the many lives that die without giving to pakanrai flowers that wither unworn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books picture what is not given as beauty that no one enjoys, but Avvai moves the image from the miser's wealth to the miser's life and sets it against a giver who has just died, so the waste is measured by the loss of one who did give."},{"a":"pn:236","b":"pn:222","type":"develops","note":"Kapilar says Pari told him 'stay behind' (ஒழிக) and would not let him come; Pothiyar says the king left him here (என்இவண் ஒழித்த) telling him to come only after his son's birth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Two poets of different patrons voice the same grievance, that the patron forbade his friend to follow him in death, and Kapilar resolves it differently, by asking fate for the next birth, so the pair shows that kaiyarunilai had a recognised place for the friend refused the right to die with his lord.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:237","b":"k:772","type":"develops","note":"Kural 772 says carrying a spear that missed an elephant is sweeter than an arrow that hit a hare; 237 says a tiger that misses the elephant it stalked will not stalk a rat, and the poet resolves to seek great gifts.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Kural's warrior maxim about aiming high is used in 237 by a bard to turn from mourning to his next patron, so the ethic of the great target governed the bard's choice of patron as well as the soldier's choice of foe."},{"a":"pn:239","b":"k:922","type":"tension","note":"Kural 922 says not to drink toddy, and that only those who do not care to be counted by the worthy should; 239 lists draining jars of sweet toddy among the deeds of a man who 'did all that should be done' and so earned fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On a plain reading the two disagree about whether drinking belongs to a praiseworthy life: the Kural excludes the drinker from the esteem of the worthy, while the Purananuru lament counts the toddy jar beside justice and giving in a life complete enough to make the manner of burial indifferent."},{"a":"pn:240","b":"a:152","type":"same_image","note":"240 calls Aay Andiran never stinting singers of horses, elephants and chariots; Akananuru 152 names 'Aay's forest' after the Aay who gives elephants to all who come, skilled or not (வல்லினும் வல்லார் ஆயினும்).","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The akam poet uses Aay's open-handedness to name a place, while the puram poem records what followed his death, his poets scattering to other lands, so the patron's giving outlived him as a landmark in love poetry even as the household it fed dissolved.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:241","b":"pn:240","type":"develops","note":"Two kaiyarunilai poems on the death of Ay Andiran: 240 stays at the pyre and the scattering bards, 241 looks to the drum of the heavenly hall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the pair shows that a lament for a patron could face downward to the hungry dependents or upward to his welcome among the gods, and that the heavenly version is offered as consolation for the earthly one."},{"a":"pn:241","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"241 presents Andiran's giving of chariots as what announces him in heaven; the Kural says giving is good even if there were no higher world.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural's hypothetical answers a real habit of praise: poets like this one tied giving to a welcome in the sky, and the couplet deliberately cuts that tie so that giving no longer needs the reward."},{"a":"pn:243","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"Both picture old age on a staff, but the Naladiyar calls youth faulty and the wise renounce it in infancy, while 243 calls its untaught youth precious and grieves for it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same image of the old man on a staff serves the Jain moralist as a warning against enjoying youth and the Sangam poet as proof that youth was the best of life, so the later book is arguing against a value the older poetry held."},{"a":"pn:245","b":"t:157","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 157 calls the mourners who burn the body and bathe unjust for returning to life; 245 is a mourner who burned his wife and is appalled that he still lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's poem complicates the Tirumanthiram's charge: survival after the pyre is shown not as forgetting but as grief's own failure to kill, which the survivor feels as shame."},{"a":"pn:246","b":"pn:248","type":"tension","note":"The queen of 246 refuses the widow's life of greens and alli; the widow of 248 lives it and pities the lilies that now feed her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Placed side by side, the two poems show that the widow's diet was a known, codified life that one woman could choose to bear and another could scorn as worse than the pyre.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:247","b":"pn:246","type":"develops","note":"Both are filed as ஆனந்தப் பையுள: 246 gives the queen's refusal in her own voice, 247 shows a widow at the burning ground from outside, wavering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The resolve that 246 voices without a tremor is seen in 247 from the outside as hesitation and fear for one's young life, so the same thurai holds both the heroic and the frightened face of the same choice."},{"a":"pn:250","b":"pn:248","type":"same_image","note":"Both name alli as the widow's food (அல்லி உணவின், அல்லிப் படுஉம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated alli food shows a fixed sign of widowhood shared across poets, which 250 sets against the house's former feasting and 248 against the girl's lily-leaf garment."},{"a":"pn:252","b":"k:274","type":"tension","note":"The Kural likens the false ascetic to a hunter hiding in a bush to snare birds; 252 says this ascetic really was a hunter, snaring women with words, before he became one.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both use the hunter and the snared bird for the ascetic, but in opposite order: for the Kural the hunter hides inside the ascetic, for the Sangam poet the ascetic has replaced the hunter, so the same image can accuse or praise a renunciant."},{"a":"pn:252","b":"pn:251","type":"develops","note":"Two poems by the same poet on the ascetic's life: 251 sets the seducer against the ascetic at the fire, 252 names his past as hunting with a net of words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the poet working one contrast twice, with the second poem turning the past from the women's pining to the man's own art of capture, which makes the renunciation a giving up of skill as well as pleasure."},{"a":"pn:254","b":"pn:253","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the bare, pale, bangle-less hand raised among the kin (வளையில் வறுங்கை ஓச்சிக் கிளையுள்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical line in poems by different poets shows that the widow's raised empty arm was a fixed formula of the முதுபாலை thurai, used in 253 for the wife's own shame and in 254 for the news she must carry to the mother."},{"a":"pn:254","b":"k:69","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's mother rejoices more at hearing her son called worthy than at his birth; 254's mother praises her son daily, and the speaker dreads her hearing of his death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem shows the other side of the Kural's maxim: the mother whose joy rests on hearing her son praised is the one whom the word of his death will strike hardest."},{"a":"pn:256","b":"pn:255","type":"develops","note":"Both are spoken by a wife alone with her husband's body in the wilds: 255 tries to move him to the hill's shade, 256 asks the potter for an urn wide enough for two.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken in order, the two widows move from refusing the death (walk a little, she tells the corpse) to arranging to share it, which shows the range of response that the முதுபாலை thurai held."},{"a":"pn:258","b":"k:924","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says shame turns her back on drinkers; in 258 the toddy jar is reserved for the raider as an honour before his deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that drinking was an honour of the warrior's rite (உண்டாட்டு) in the older poetry, so the Kural's chapter against liquor is rejecting a heroic custom, not only a private vice."},{"a":"pn:260","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says a death that is really existence is hard to achieve save for the skilled; 260 shows the hero's body left shaking with arrows while his name passes onto the stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"260 gives the Kural's abstract paradox a concrete form: the 'death that lives' was a practice with a body left on the bank, a stone set in a narrow path, and a name crowned with peacock feathers."},{"a":"pn:260","b":"pn:259","type":"develops","note":"259 warns the karanthai warrior against the ambush as he chases the herd; 260 tells of a karanthai warrior who recovered the herd and died of the arrows.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the warning of 259 and the hero-stone of 260 give the two ends of the herd-recovery: the danger seen in advance and the death it brings, which is why the recovery's praise ends at a stone."},{"a":"pn:246","b":"a:25","type":"parallel_domain","note":"246's speaker is the queen of Bhoothapandiyan; the colophon of Akananuru 25 names Bhoothapandiyan as its poet, whose hero vows 'let my life suffer' at parting.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Through the colophons, the akam poet who wrote a lover's promise not to stay away is also the husband whose wife refused to outlive him, so the anthologies let a convention of inseparable lovers stand beside one recorded marriage."},{"a":"pn:261","b":"pn:250","type":"same_image","note":"Both address the dead lord's house with புல்என் றனையால் (you have become desolate) and set a widow beside it: 261 likens the house to a widow with shorn head, 250 shows the widow shorn and eating lily seeds inside it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One formula serves two poets, and the widow sits once in a simile and once in the house, which shows that the empty hall and the widow's state were one picture of a lord's death, interchangeable as tenor and vehicle."},{"a":"pn:261","b":"pn:280","type":"same_image","note":"261 likens the house to a கழிகலம் மகடூஉ with shorn head; in 280 the wife foresees becoming one of the கழிகல மகளிர் with shaven head.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In 261 the shorn widow is only an image for a ruined house; in 280 the wife speaks it of herself as her own future, so the pair shows the same stock figure moving from ornament to fate depending on who speaks.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:263","b":"pn:335","type":"develops","note":"335 says the people know no god worshipped with poured paddy except the stone of the man who fell killing an elephant; 263 tells a passing bard not to go by such a stone without worship.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"335 states the creed of the hero-stone as a general fact of a small warrior land, and 263 shows it working as an obligation laid on travellers, so together they show the cult reaching beyond the dead man's village to the road."},{"a":"pn:264","b":"a:269","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe the making of a hero-stone: 264's name carved, red flower garland bound with maral fibre and peacock feather; Akananuru 269's names washed, turmeric, and a karanthai garland tied with fibre on the stone by the road.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet borrows the puram rite almost item by item to fill the heroine's fear of the road, which shows that the hero-stone was a fixed, widely known ritual whose details an akam audience would recognise as danger without being told.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:264","b":"pn:232","type":"develops","note":"In 232 Avvaiyar asks whether the patron who would not accept a whole country will now accept toddy poured at his feather-crowned stone; in 264 the bards may still be coming to a patron who is now a stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poems turn on the reversal the stone makes in the gift relation: in 232 the giver becomes a receiver of offerings, and in 264 the receivers arrive to find no giver, so together they show that a patron's death was grieved as the end of a circuit of giving."},{"a":"pn:265","b":"pn:221","type":"same_image","note":"221 ends நடுகல் ஆயினன் புரவலன் (the patron has become a hero-stone); 265 says கல்ஆ யினையே (you have become a stone) and adds that the bards' wealth went with him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The formula 'he became a stone' is used of a crowned king in 221 and of a local cattle-defender in 265, which shows that the hero-stone honour, and the bards' claim on the dead, were not reserved for kings."},{"a":"pn:265","b":"k:771","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 771 threatens enemies that many who stood before the lord now stand as stones; in 265 the stone is the hero's own, and the poet grieves that patronage and victory went with him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's boast treats the hero-stone as the enemy's fate and a mark of the lord's might, while 265 shows the same stone from the side of those left behind, so the pair shows that the honour Valluvar's warrior wields as a threat was, for the bards, a loss of livelihood."},{"a":"pn:266","b":"n:287","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 287 says want takes away good nature and sharpness of mind; 266 names its poverty as அறிவுகெட நின்ற, which has ruined his understanding, and as making him hide from guests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's maxim that poverty destroys the mind is given here as a poet's own testimony with a concrete symptom, hiding from the guest one longed for, which shows that the loss of hospitality was felt as the loss of reason itself."},{"a":"pn:266","b":"n:286","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 286 says the man who cannot give to those who come hungry had better leave his village; 266 asks the king to end such poverty quickly, as a plea in the assembly is answered.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Naladiyar makes the poor host's shame his own to solve by leaving, while the Purananuru poet places the remedy on the king as a matter of justice, so the pair shows two answers to the same shame: private withdrawal and a public claim on the ruler."},{"a":"pn:269","b":"pn:262","type":"same_image","note":"Both are உண்டாட்டு feasts after a cattle raid: 262 orders toddy drawn and a ram killed for the returning lord, 269 shows the warrior drinking and answering the drummer's request with the sword's deed.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"262 is the feast prepared and 269 the feast in progress, and in both the drink is bound to the deed that earned it, which shows that the victory feast was a rite of reward rather than mere celebration."},{"a":"pn:271","b":"pn:272","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the nochchi on women's waists and on the defender's head: 271 ends with the garland bloodied and carried off by a kite, 272 with the tree's honour of crowning the man who would not yield.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pairing of girdle and battle garland yields lament in one poet and praise in the next, which shows that the thurai செருவிடை வீழ்தல் could be sung through the flower alone, with the warrior's death left wholly implicit."},{"a":"pn:274","b":"k:774","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 774's hero sends his spear away with an elephant and laughs as he pulls one from his own body; 274's warrior hurls his spear at the oncoming elephant and, weaponless, takes the enemy's attack upon his body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's couplet compresses a set scene that the Purananuru gives at length with dress and enemy horse, which shows that Valluvar's chapter on martial pride was built on stock battle episodes the audience already knew from heroic song.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:277","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"Kural 69: the mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than when she bore him (ஈன்ற பொழுதின் பெரிதுவக்கும்); 277: the old mother's joy at hearing her son died killing an elephant is greater than on the day she bore him (ஈன்ற ஞான்றினும் பெரிதே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the heroic measure of a mother's joy but moves its object from a son's death in battle to his being called worthy, so the pair shows Valluvar civilising a formula of the battlefield into one of learning and character."},{"a":"pn:278","b":"k:69","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 69's mother rejoices on hearing her son praised; 278's mother hears a report of his flight, refuses it, searches the field, and rejoices more than at his birth only on seeing his body.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural makes a mother's greatest joy depend on what she hears said of her son, 278 makes hearsay the thing to be refuted by sight of the wounds, which shows that the heroic mother trusted only the body's evidence of honour."},{"a":"pn:277","b":"pn:278","type":"same_image","note":"Both are உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி poems about an old mother whose son died in battle, and both end on joy greater than on the day she bore him (ஈன்ற ஞான்றினும் பெரிது).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One closing formula carries two different plots, joy at good news in 277 and joy after a false report of flight is disproved in 278, which shows that the thurai fixed the ending and left poets free to vary the path to it."},{"a":"pn:279","b":"pn:312","type":"develops","note":"312 lists the duties: to bear and rear the son is the mother's, to make him a warrior the father's, to kill elephants the son's; in 279 the father is already dead and the mother herself arms and dresses her only son for battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"312's tidy division of duties breaks down in 279, where the mother takes over the father's task of sending the son out because the men are dead, which shows the ideal of the warrior mother under the pressure of successive losses.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:279","b":"pn:86","type":"same_image","note":"In 86 a mother says her womb is like a den a tiger has left and her son will be found on the battlefield; in 279 a mother with one son sends him to the battle after husband and kinsman have fallen.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"86 gives the mother's own proud voice and 279 an onlooker's awe at the same type, which shows that the warrior mother was praised both from within and from outside, and that the onlooker felt the cost (கெடுக சிந்தை) the mother does not speak."},{"a":"pn:280","b":"pn:250","type":"same_image","note":"280's widows eat the seeds of the small white water lily (ஆம்பல் அல்லி உண்ணும்); 250's widow is the wife 'of lily-seed food' (அல்லி உணவின் மனைவி) with hair cut and bangles removed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated detail of lily-seed food in two poets shows that the widow's diet was a fixed public mark of her state, so that in 280 the wife can foretell her whole future by naming what she will eat."},{"a":"pn:280","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"In 246 the queen rejects the widow's life of lily-food and gravel bed and chooses the pyre; in 280 the wife foresees that life and says it will be harder than even the present grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"246 treats widowhood as a choice to be refused by fire, while 280 accepts it as the expected course and judges it the heavier burden, so the pair shows that the widow's austerities were seen as harder than death by both speakers, though only a queen could choose against them.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:281","b":"pn:296","type":"same_image","note":"281 lists the rites for guarding a wounded man's life (neem in the house, mustard, kaanchi sung, incense); 296 shows the same rites (வேம்புசினை ஒடிப்பவும், காஞ்சி பாடவும், ஐயவி புகைப்பவும்) in every house while the hero's chariot is late.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two show the rite was done not only over a wound already seen but in anticipation, by every house in the village, so 296's anxiety is the same guarding as 281 started before the news has come."},{"a":"pn:282","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"282: the hero's body is gone and only his split shield remains, but he is in the mouths of poets; Kural 235: a death that is really existence comes only to the skilled.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's paradox of a death that is life gets in 282 its working mechanism: the poets' tongues are where the vanished hero now exists, so the Kural's 'skilled' death depended on someone to sing it."},{"a":"pn:283","b":"a:262","type":"same_image","note":"283 line 6 names the assembly hall of the Kosar (கோசர் அவைக்களத்தானும்); Akananuru 262 names the town-elder Kosar (ஊர்முது கோசர்) who blinded Anni Mignili's father.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both poets know the Kosar as a body that sits in judgment, an institution in the puram fragment and the source of a borrowed story in the akam poem, so the Kosar's council was famous enough for an akam speaker to cite it as a known wrong.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:283","b":"a:44","type":"same_image","note":"283 names the Azhumbil man who will not submit (அழும்பிலன் அடங்கான்); Akananuru 44 uses Azhumbil (அழும்பில் அன்ன அறாஅ யாணர்) as the measure of unfailing plenty, in a Chola king's praise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet uses Azhumbil only as a by-word for wealth, while the puram fragment remembers it as the seat of a man who resisted, so the same place carried prosperity in love poetry and defiance in war poetry."},{"a":"pn:284","b":"k:774","type":"same_image","note":"284: the warrior who killed an elephant laughs at the back of the man who fled; Kural 774: having sent his spear off with an elephant, he laughs as he pulls one from his own body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place a warrior's laugh just after an elephant kill, but 284 turns it on the coward and the Kural turns it on the hero's own wound, which shows the Kural moving the scorn of fear from the enemy to the self."},{"a":"pn:285","b":"pn:297","type":"qualifies","note":"297 says cool fields (தண்ணடை) are the due of speared warriors; 285 shows a lord bowing his head when a warrior he had given only a barren small village (கரம்பைச் சீறூர்) dies, the fertile fields left out.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"297 states the warrior's claim to good land; 285 shows what happened when a lord fell short of it, the shame made public at the death, so the grant was a debt the community watched."},{"a":"pn:286","b":"pn:291","type":"same_image","note":"286 says her son was not laid on the bier and covered in pure white cloth (தூவெள் அறுவை போர்ப்பித்திலதே); 291 has the dead hero lying under pure white cloth (தூவெள் அறுவை மாயோன்) on the field.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"291 shows the white cloth as the covering given to a fallen warrior, which fixes the sense of 286's last line: the mother speaks of the shroud and bier of the dead, not of illness or bed, so her son's death is in question either way.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:287","b":"k:777","type":"qualifies","note":"287 says the warriors who do not run, if they die, enjoy the weddings of flawless women in the high world; Kural 777 names only world-circling fame as what those who do not want life seek.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names fame alone and its commentator adds heaven; 287 already makes heaven the reward and dismisses land, so the commentator's addition had a Sangam warrant that the Kural itself left out."},{"a":"pn:288","b":"pn:291","type":"same_image","note":"288 ends with the kites crowding the dead warrior's chest so that no one can embrace him; in 291 the mourner tells the children to keep the birds' clamour off the dead man and drives away the jackal herself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the birds at the body as the thing the living had to fight, so 291's guarding is the answer to 288's loss: the kin's last duty was to take back the body from the birds.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:289","b":"k:517","type":"develops","note":"289: like a ploughman choosing the best bull, the king picks the man among the old fighting houses and gives him his own gold bowl; Kural 517: having reasoned that this man will do this by this means, leave it to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's choice of the right man for a task appears in 289 as a public honour, the king's own bowl handed over before battle, so the selection the Kural treats as reasoning was also a gift that bound the chosen man to go first."},{"a":"pn:289","b":"pn:293","type":"develops","note":"289 ends with the drum crying 'today is the taking of flowers' (பூக்கோள் இன்று); 293, whose colophon thurai is பூக்கோட் காஞ்சி, follows the war-drum's call to the flower-selling woman who must go into other houses.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"289 hears the flower-taking from the camp as an honour; 293 hears the same call from the village and sees the flower-seller's trade and the wives' loss, so the one ritual is shown from both ends."},{"a":"pn:290","b":"k:764","type":"develops","note":"290: this man's grandfather died beside the king's grandfather, and he too will take the spears meant for the king; Kural 764: an army is one with courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's 'courage that has come down' is, in Avvaiyar, a three-generation bond between one fighting family and one royal family, so inherited courage meant inherited loyalty to a particular house."},{"a":"pn:290","b":"pn:298","type":"qualifies","note":"290 asks the king to give this warrior toddy before he drinks himself; 298's warrior complains the king gave his men the dregs and drank the clear, and now does not send him first.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Together they show the order of drinking as the measure of the order of dying: 290 asks for the first cup on the strength of the family's dead, 298 reads a poor cup as the loss of the vanguard.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:292","b":"pn:298","type":"develops","note":"292 defends a warrior who stopped the king's toddy being mixed and will not wait his turn; 298 has a warrior angry that the king does not tell him 'you go first'.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both turn a quarrel over drink into a claim to the front line, which shows the feast's order was read as a promise about the battle, and a warrior who seized one was claiming the other.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:294","b":"k:763","type":"same_image","note":"294: no one approaches the warrior standing alone, as no one approaches the gem the serpent spits (அரவுஉமிழ் மணியின் குறுகார்); Kural 763: a host of rats roaring like the sea perish when the cobra breathes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's cobra verse never mentions an army and its commentator reads it as one hero against a crowd; 294 uses the serpent exactly so, for a lone fighter in a confused battle whom the crowd will not approach, so the allegorical reading follows an image of the war poems."},{"a":"pn:295","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"295: seeing her son broken in the battle press, his mother's dry breasts flow; Kural 69: a mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than when she bore him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's mother hears praise and her joy exceeds the birth; Avvaiyar's mother sees the body and her breasts give milk as at the birth, so the Kural's comparison with childbirth has a harsher Sangam form in which the worth was proved by dying."},{"a":"pn:295","b":"pn:278","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the thurai உவகைக் கலுழ்ச்சி; in 278 a mother who swore to cut off the breast that fed him if he fled finds him dead facing the enemy; in 295 the mother's withered breasts flow on seeing his glory.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems make the mother's breast the measure of the son's conduct, cut off for flight in 278 and filled again for his death in 295, so the thurai of joyful weeping has a fixed bodily sign."},{"a":"pn:297","b":"pn:287","type":"qualifies","note":"297 says it is right that cool fields (தண்ணடை) be given to speared warriors; 287 asks what getting cool fields is (தண்ணடை பெறுதல் யாவது?) to men who, if they die, enjoy the high world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One poem claims the land grant as the warrior's due and the other waves it aside for heaven, so the war poems held two accounts of the hero's wage, and which one a poet used depended on whether he spoke for the living or the willing dead."},{"a":"pn:299","b":"k:769","type":"qualifies","note":"299: the poor chief's gram-fed horse cleaves the front while the rich kings' ghee-fed horses hang back; Kural 769: an army wins if it is free of shrinking, lasting grievance and poverty (வறுமை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural counts poverty among the things that lose a war, while 299 makes the poor chief the winner and the rich the ones who shrink, so the Kural's rule is about an army's supply and the poem's point is that wealth does not supply courage."},{"a":"pn:301","b":"k:772","type":"develops","note":"301's hero will lift his spear only against the king's mounted elephant (வேந்தூர் யானைக்கு அல்லது); Kural 772 prefers a spear that missed an elephant to an arrow that hit a hare.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the warrior's preference for a worthy target as a maxim of honour, and 301 shows the same preference working as a tactic, since the enemy camp is warned that a man who ignores lesser blows is saving himself for their king."},{"a":"pn:302","b":"pn:303","type":"same_image","note":"Both kuthirai maram poems name the young rider with one formula: நோக்கினர்ச் செகுக்கும் காளை (302) and எள்ளுநர்ச் செகுக்கும் காளை (303).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epithet for the young horseman was a fixed frame with a free slot for his victims, those who look at him or those who mock him, so two poets of the same thurai shared a stock line and varied only whom it threatened.","gr":5},{"a":"pn:303","b":"k:774","type":"same_image","note":"303's speaker killed an elephant yesterday and now waits for a young rider's spear in his chest; Kural 774's warrior sends his spear off with an elephant and laughs as he pulls a spear from his own body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both pair the elephant killed with the spear received, so the couplet's laughing warrior is the same type as 303's boaster, and the poem supplies the couplet's missing sequence: the elephant was yesterday, the wound is today's test."},{"a":"pn:304","b":"k:664","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 664 says speaking is easy and doing as one said is hard; in 304 the king's camp trembles because the hero's word is never two (இரண்டா காது அவன் கூறியது).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural treats word and deed as a gap to be closed by effort, but 304 shows a man whose record has closed it so firmly that his spoken vow alone frightens an army, so keeping one's word becomes a weapon and not only a virtue."},{"a":"pn:305","b":"k:685","type":"develops","note":"Kural 685 says the envoy speaks briefly and brings good; 305's brahmin says only a few words (சொல்லிய சொல்லோ சிலவே) and the siege ladders and elephants' bells are put away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's rule for the envoy, brevity that achieves its end, has a historical scene behind it in which the good brought was a war called off, and the poem credits that to the words' fewness rather than to the speaker's power."},{"a":"pn:306","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81 says all household life is for receiving guests; 306's woman prays at the hero-stone first that she may have guests (விருந்து எதிர் பெறுகதில்) and then for her husband's war.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's claim that household life exists for guests was a prayer before it was a maxim: in 306 a warrior's wife asks the hero-stone for guests before she asks for her husband's victory, so hospitality ranked first even in a house that lived by war."},{"a":"pn:306","b":"pn:314","type":"same_image","note":"Both set a small village (சீறூர்) beside hero-stones (நடுகல்): in 306 a woman worships the stone, in 314 the village of thin nelli stands by a field where stones rise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the hero-stone as part of the warrior village's daily setting, worshipped by the women and standing beside the living hero who is the village's stay, so the dead and the living defenders were kept in one view."},{"a":"pn:307","b":"k:780","type":"develops","note":"Kural 780 says a death that fills the patron's eyes with tears is worth begging for; in 307 the enemy king himself, seeing the fallen hero, says nothing is finer than to stay on this field.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures a good death by the tears of one's own lord, while 307 goes further and makes the enemy king the witness who envies the death, so the honour of a field death did not depend on whose side mourned it.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:308","b":"pn:301","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the king's mounted elephant as the warrior's chosen target with the same phrase: வேந்தூர் யானை (301 line 15) and வேந்துஊர் யானை (308 line 5).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's own elephant was the standard mark of a warrior's ambition, so 301's threat and 308's report are the before and after of one deed: the spear that 301 says is reserved for it is the spear 308 says now stands in its face."},{"a":"pn:309","b":"k:771","type":"develops","note":"Both are spoken by a follower of 'my lord' (என்னை): 309 says enemies fear at the report that he is in the camp, Kural 771 warns enemies not to stand before him, since many who did now stand as stones.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's chapter on martial pride keeps the Purananuru's speaking voice, the retainer boasting of என்னை, and 309 shows what the couplet compresses: the lord's power is the fear his name spreads before any blow, which is why the stones of 771 are the result rather than the boast."},{"a":"pn:310","b":"pn:279","type":"develops","note":"In 279 a mother whose kin fell oils her only son's tuft (குடுமி) and sends him to battle; in 310 the son of a man who fell the other day lies on the shield with his tuft like a horse's mane after killing an elephant.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Together they form a sequence the anthology never states as one story: the boy sent out with oiled tuft after his father's death comes back to lie on the shield, so 310 shows the cost and the pride that 279's resolve was preparing."},{"a":"pn:312","b":"k:67","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 67 says the father's duty is to make his son first in the assembly; 312 says the father's duty is to make him a worthy man (சான்றோன்) but the son's own duty is to kill elephants and return.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both assign the father the son's standing among men, but 312 places that standing inside a chain of duties ending in war, so the 'worthy man' of a warrior house was finished by the battlefield, not by the assembly the Kural has in mind."},{"a":"pn:312","b":"k:69","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 69 says the mother rejoices more to hear her son called சான்றோன் than at his birth; 312's mother says bearing him is her duty and making him சான்றோன் is the father's.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same word marks the same two stages, birth and being called worthy, and 312 shows why the Kural's mother only hears of it: in this division of duties the worthiness is made by others, so her joy is at a work she began but did not finish."},{"a":"pn:313","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"Kural 218 says those who know what is due do not fail in obligation even when without means; 313's hero has nothing in hand yet is bound (கடவ) to give elephants and chariots to suppliants.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's abstract duty, giving even without means, is shown in 313 with its practical answer: a warrior without wealth gives what he will win, so the obligation was made possible by the economy of plunder the Kural does not mention."},{"a":"pn:314","b":"k:1027","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1027 likens the one who carries the family's burden to the stout-hearted in battle; 314 shows one man as lamp of his house and dam of the routed army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural uses the battle hero as a simile for the family's mainstay, while 314 shows that in the warrior village they were literally the same man, so the couplet's comparison grew from a social fact that it turned into a figure.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:315","b":"k:490","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 490 says to fold up like the heron when it is time and strike like its beak when the moment comes; 315 says Anji can stay unseen like the fire-drill in the eaves and blaze like its fire when he appears.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both reduce right timing to one object with two states, but Avvai's image puts the force inside the waiting thing (the fire is already in the stick), which adds to the couplet that restraint is stored strength rather than mere patience.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:316","b":"k:926","type":"tension","note":"Kural 926 says sleepers are like the dead and toddy-drinkers eat poison; 316 praises as 'our lord' a man asleep in a drunken drowse who pawns his sword for guests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two judge the same figure oppositely, since the drunken sleeper whom the Kural calls as good as dead is in 316 the generous lord of the bards, which shows that the Kural's ban on drink rejects a heroic ideal the Sangam village celebrated.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:316","b":"pn:317","type":"same_image","note":"Both show a drinking warrior at rest in his front yard (சீயா முன்றில் in 316, முன்றில் கிடந்த பெருங்களியாளற்கு in 317) after giving everything away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two consecutive poems share a portrait, the reveller who has given away his sword or his bedding, which suggests the vallaan mullai thurai had a stock figure in whom poverty, drink and open-handedness were marks of the same character."},{"a":"pn:319","b":"k:89","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 89 calls neglect of guests poverty in the midst of wealth; in 319 the wife, with an empty jar and only roasted hare, takes in the bard and promises a golden lotus on her husband's return.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet defines the failure, poverty inside wealth, and 319 shows its converse, generosity inside poverty, where what is lacking is paid on credit against the husband's next war, so hospitality was measured by readiness to give, not by stores."},{"a":"pn:319","b":"pn:320","type":"same_image","note":"Both invite a bard to stay in a warrior's small house and eat game (முயல் in 319, வள்ளூரம் in 320), with ஈங்குஇருந் தீமோ in 319 and தங்கினை சென்மோ, பாண in 320.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together they give the vallaan mullai poem's fixed shape, a description of the poor or wild household, an offer of roasted game, and a closing pledge of the lord's giving, so the bard's welcome was a set form that each poet filled with his own scene."},{"a":"pn:320","b":"pn:329","type":"same_image","note":"320 lines 17 and 18 and 329 lines 8 and 9 are the same: அருகாது ஈயும் வண்மை / உரைசால் நெடுந்தகை ஓம்பும் ஊரே.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems end on an identical couplet after different openings, a hunter's yard and a village by the hero-stones, which shows that the closing praise of the town's guardian was a detachable formula the poets set after their own scenes."},{"a":"pn:327","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than begging, even water for a cow; 327 praises a chief who begs millet face to face from small farmers to feed bards and his kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's limit case (begging even for a good cause is degrading) is exactly what the Purananuru holds up as heroic, so the pair shows that the shame of asking was a later ethical rule, while the older warrior ethic counted begging for others as part of generosity.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:331","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 219 defines the generous man's poverty as being unable to do what he should; 331 says its chief, though very poor, can make what is not there and still give.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Valluvar treats poverty as the one thing that disables the generous man, while the Purananuru poet shows a poor chief whose skill in contriving, rationing and scattering defeats that disablement, so the pair shows generosity measured in the Kural by means and in the poem by craft.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:333","b":"k:85","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 85 asks whether the field of a man who feeds guests and eats the leftovers even needs sowing; in 333 the wife has fed guests until she must pound the seed millet itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rhetorical promise that the hospitable house needs no seed is played out literally in the poem, where hospitality has eaten the seed, so the pair shows the real cost that the maxim's confidence passes over.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:324","b":"k:780","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 780 calls a death that makes one's patron weep worth begging for; 324 praises a companion who will die when his king dies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural places the good death before the lord's, earning his tears, while the poem binds the companion's death to follow the king's, so together they show two orders of loyalty in Tamil war ethics: dying for the lord and dying with him."},{"a":"pn:332","b":"pn:95","type":"develops","note":"In 95 Avvaiyar contrasts one king's spears, feathered, garlanded and oiled in the palace, with Adiyaman's, their tips broken from use and lying in the smith's shed; 332 gives a single warrior's spear both lives, idle in the hut and garlanded in procession, and then the elephant charge.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Avvaiyar splits the honoured spear and the used spear between two kings to shame one, while 332 joins them in one spear, so the pair shows that the garlanded spear was not itself a mark of idleness; only the spear that never went to battle was."},{"a":"pn:337","b":"pn:176","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the cold spring of Pari's Parambu: in 176 its clear water is neglected by those who live by it; in 337 Kapilar likens the confined girl to it as hard to see.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One famous spring serves two opposite points, a good thing slighted because it is near and a good thing prized because it is out of reach, which shows that Pari's hill had become a shared figure of the precious whose sense each poet could turn."},{"a":"pn:337","b":"a:60","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 60's friend says the mother will keep the girl under closer guard than the Chola treasure and asks the hero not to crush her bangled girl; 337 says the girl is confined to the house (மனைச்செறிந் தனளே) and asks who will crush (ஞெமுக்குவோரே) her young breasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same confinement of the maturing girl and the same verb for the lover's embrace appear in both, but in akam the guard is a mother keeping off one lover while in puram it is brothers with bloody spears keeping off kings, which shows the puram poet enlarging an akam household scene into war."},{"a":"pn:336","b":"a:60","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 60 calls the mother who will guard the girl அறன்இல் யாய்; 336 calls the mother who reared the girl's beauty அறன்இலன் and பண்புஇல் தாய்.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both poems blame the mother with the same word, but the akam friend blames her for guarding the girl too closely and the puram poet for rearing a beauty that draws war, which shows that the girl's beauty, a private matter in akam, becomes a public danger in the kaanchi poems.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:338","b":"a:90","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 90's friend says that even the Kosars' town would not be accepted as the price of the girl; 338 says the father will not give his daughter even if the three crowned kings bring gifts, unless they bow.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The akam friend's boast that no price is enough for the girl is the same claim the puram father makes against kings, so the pair shows a shared convention of the priceless daughter that in akam keeps a lover waiting and in puram starts a war."},{"a":"pn:334","b":"pn:326","type":"same_image","note":"In both, the wife feeds bards and guests while the husband gives or holds the gold ornament taken from a war elephant's face.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets build the old warrior house (மூதின் முல்லை) from the same pair of acts, the wife's feeding and the husband's elephant gold, which shows that the thurai required the wife's hospitality as an equal half of the house's heroism."},{"a":"pn:335","b":"pn:329","type":"develops","note":"329 shows the village hero-stone given daily offerings, bathed and lit with ghee; 335 says the stone of the warrior who fell killing an elephant is the only god of its land.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"329 shows the rite and 335 states the creed behind it, so the pair shows that in these small warrior villages the hero-stone was not one cult among others but the whole of public worship."},{"a":"pn:348","b":"pn:347","type":"same_image","note":"Both close on the town's trees straining at the root because kings have tethered war elephants to them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kapilar and Paranar both show the coming war through the trees the kings' elephants are tied to, so this was a stock way of picturing harm to the town without blaming the girl."},{"a":"pn:359","b":"pn:356","type":"develops","note":"356 describes the burning ground as the survivor of all; 359 opens with the same ground and turns it into counsel to give.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The cremation-ground description that stands alone in 356 is the first half of an argument in 359, which shows it was a stock opening for perunkaanchi counsel to kings."},{"a":"pn:343","b":"a:149","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe Musiri's trade: gold arriving by ship and pepper going out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same port appears from both sides of the genre line, and in both the speaker refuses Musiri-scale wealth for a woman: the father will not sell her, the hero will not leave her for it."},{"a":"pn:348","b":"a:227","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Thazhumban and his town Unur.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem praises Thazhumban for truthful speech and the akam poem for never turning comers away, so his fame reached akam poets as that of a host and landmark, and both use his rich Unur as a byword for plenty."},{"a":"pn:351","b":"a:208","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Eyinan; 351 likens the girl's beauty to his vaakai, while Akananuru 208 recalls his death at Paazhi. Uncertain: வாகை may be a victory or a place.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poet uses Eyinan's vaakai as a measure of beauty while the akam poet uses his fall in battle as a measure of shame avenged, so one chieftain's name served both as a token of victory and of a hero's death."},{"a":"pn:356","b":"k:336","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls it the world's 'greatness' that one here yesterday is gone today; 356 gives that greatness a place, the burning ground that has seen everyone's back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's bitter abstraction becomes a physical place in the Purananuru, and the ground that outlives all men is the 'greatness' the couplet names without showing.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:357","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Both use crossing water as the image of what one needs at death; the Kural's swimmer needs the Lord's feet, 357's needs a raft that wealth is not.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The crossing image the Kural gives a god already stood in the Purananuru without one, carried by what a man keeps in place of wealth, so the Kural's theology was laid on an older ethical figure.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:358","b":"k:347","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on one verb of holding or leaving repeated across the lines; the Kural says griefs cling to clingers, 358 that Fortune leaves those who cling and stays with those who let go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they give both halves of one argument the Kural states only negatively: holding brings grief, and letting go is what keeps Fortune, so renunciation is offered even to kings as a gain."},{"a":"pn:359","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says nothing but renown stands undying; 359 says blame stands too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Adding that blame lasts as well turns the Kural's consolation into a warning, so giving is urged not to earn a name but to escape the other lasting name."},{"a":"pn:359","b":"n:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say give now because death is certain; the Naladiyar warns that wealth is useless after the funeral drum, 359 promises a fame that outshines the burning ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same deathbed counsel serves two motives: the Naladiyar argues from the uselessness of hoarded wealth, the Purananuru from the name left behind, so the later book drops the reward the earlier one offered.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:360","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says wealth does not stay, so do at once what stays; 360 says even great rulers' wealth does not stay, so fill the hands of gift-seekers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves 'what stays' unnamed, and the Purananuru names it as filling the hands of those who seek gifts, which shows the bard's claim behind the maxim."},{"a":"pn:361","b":"n:5","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 5 says those who gave first escape the waste land through which Death drags its captives; 361 tells Death to its face that a king who has given to brahmins, bards and suppliants does not fear its coming.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's promise that givers escape Death is a future reward, while the Purananuru poet shows the same claim as a present state, a king already fearless, so the Jain maxim restates as doctrine what the court poets had staged as a king's achieved confidence."},{"a":"pn:363","b":"n:21","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 21: even umbrella-kings on elephants were carried off and none is left; 363: kings who ruled the whole earth alone are more than the sand and went to the burning ground, so act now and renounce.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's general observation about dead kings appears in the Purananuru as an argument put to one living king, with the outcaste's saltless rice as its sting, which shows the impermanence of kings was a counsel form before it became a moral commonplace."},{"a":"pn:364","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"364 answers the nearness of the burning ground with feasting: roast ram, toddy, gifts, joy now. Naladiyar 16 says joy that does not last, like the lamb nibbling its garland at the place of slaughter, is not found among the wise. On the plain reading, the feast 364 urges is exactly the lamb's pleasure the Naladiyar condemns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poems draw their conclusion from the same nearness of death, so the disagreement shows that impermanence by itself did not settle how to live: the Sangam kaanchi poet drew enjoyment and giving from it, the Jain poet withdrawal."},{"a":"pn:365","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"In 365 the Earth, personified as a woman, weeps at being claimed by king after king like a bought woman; in Kural 1040 the good woman called Land laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The personified Earth judges men in both, but the Purananuru's Earth grieves at being owned by a succession of kings while the Kural's mocks those who do not work her, so the same figure speaks for the land against its rulers in one and against its idle tenants in the other."},{"a":"pn:366","b":"pn:363","type":"tension","note":"Both close on the same line, மடங்கல் உண்மை மாயமோ அன்றே; 363 draws from it that the king should renounce the whole sea-bounded world (முழுதுடன் துறந்தே), 366 that he should work by day, drink with women and give food and bulls. The tension holds if துறந்தே in 363 means giving up rule, not only giving away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A single refrain on death's reality served kaanchi poets as a premise for opposite conclusions, renunciation in one and feasting and giving in the other, so the formula was shared stock and the counsel was the poet's own choice."},{"a":"pn:367","b":"n:120","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 120: nothing goes with a person but the deeds they did; 367: the lands do not go with their owners, only the good done in living is a raft when one sinks, so give to brahmins and suppliants and drink the toddy women bring.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Avvaiyar and the Naladiyar hold the same doctrine that only deeds accompany a person, but Avvaiyar counts the court's feasting alongside giving as the way to use the allotted days, so the doctrine did not by itself require the ascetic conclusion the Jain anthology draws."},{"a":"pn:369","b":"pn:368","type":"develops","note":"368 calls the Chera 'ploughman whose plough is the sword' (வாளேர் உழவ) in a single epithet; 369 works the whole figure out: clouds, lightning, thunder, wind, rain, plough, seed, crop, sheaves, threshing and guarded heap.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the epithet in 368 turns out to be the compressed form of the full rain-to-harvest allegory in 369, and in both the bard's request stands where the labourer's share of a harvest would, so the figure carries the bard's claim to payment."},{"a":"pn:370","b":"pn:378","type":"develops","note":"Both by Oonpothi Pasungudaiyar for Ilanchetchenni: 370 opens on the poet's large kin (இரும்பே ரொக்கற்கு) starving on fibre and bark and asks for elephants on the battlefield; 378 shows his kin (இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல்), long in want, decking themselves wrongly with the jewels the king poured out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems are the before and after of one bard's family under one patron, and the comedy of 378's misplaced jewels depends on the hunger 370 describes, so the pair records the bard's rise from want to a family that does not know how to wear wealth.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:371","b":"k:872","type":"develops","note":"371 recalls the king's fame as 'ploughing with the bow' (வில்லேர் உழவின்) and sings his battle as threshing; Kural 872 says one may take on the enmity of bow-ploughmen (வில்லேர் உழவர்) but not of word-ploughmen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's bow-ploughman is a set epithet from the களவழி poems, where the battle is literally worked through as tillage and threshing, so the couplet takes the bards' highest praise of a warrior king and ranks it below the work of words."},{"a":"pn:372","b":"pn:26","type":"develops","note":"Both by Mangudi Kizhar for Nedunchezhiyan: in 26 the king cooks a battlefield sacrifice with crowned heads as the hearth and blood as the water; in 372 the same field-sacrifice, with enemy heads as hearth stones and a demon stirring, is recited by the drummer who then asks for the king's necklace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet used the battlefield sacrifice once as direct royal praise and once as the set-piece before a bard's request, which shows the gruesome image had become a repertoire piece a performer could reuse to earn a gift."},{"a":"pn:374","b":"pn:129","type":"develops","note":"Both by Mudamosiyar for Ay Andiran: 129 says the elephants Ay gave to suppliants outnumber the stars; 374 asks the sun whether it has Ay's generosity or shines in the sky for nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet twice measures Ay against the sky and twice finds the sky short, first in number and then in giving, so the reversal of the usual comparison of king to sun was his deliberate manner of praise, not a single conceit."},{"a":"pn:376","b":"k:1061","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1061: not begging, even from those who give gladly without hiding, is worth ten million times more; 376: after one night's gift the drummer has not thought of begging since, and his drum has not sounded at other givers' gates.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural reaches not-begging by self-respect, while the Purananuru bard reaches it because one patron gave so fully that no further asking was needed, so the poem shows the ideal attained from the giver's side rather than the asker's."},{"a":"pn:378","b":"a:70","type":"same_image","note":"378 likens the bard's kin decked in misplaced jewels to the monkeys who wore the jewels Sita dropped when Ravana took her; Akananuru 70 likens the hushed town to the banyan at Kodi that Rama silenced for his secret counsel.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two Sangam allusions to the Rama story both use a marginal episode, dropped jewels and a silenced tree, as a simile for a crowd, so the story was known in enough detail that poets could cite its by-ways without telling it.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:379","b":"pn:376","type":"develops","note":"Both by Purathinai Nannaganar for an Oymaan chief: in 376 a drummer, given one night's gift, never begs again; in 379 the poet sets out because he heard another drummer quote the patron's habit of daily rice and ghee.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the satisfied drummer of 376 is the kind of witness whose report sets the poet of 379 on the road, so the gift was also the patron's advertisement and bards recruited one another to his gate.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:380","b":"pn:140","type":"same_image","note":"380 calls the patron தீஞ்சுளைப் பலவின் நாஞ்சிற் பொருநன் and says his giving made the poet's family bloom though the land be in want; 140 calls him தடவுநிலைப் பலவின் நாஞ்சில் பொருநன், who answered a request for a little rice with an elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The jackfruit epithet for the Nanjil lord recurs in two poets, and 140's anecdote of overgiving supplies the deed behind 380's general claim that his family no longer feels want, so the chief's fame rested on a story bards shared.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:381","b":"pn:384","type":"develops","note":"Same poet, same patron (Karumbanur kizhan): 381 records the chief's own speech promising to end the bard's poverty; 384, under the thurai கையறுநிலை, says the bard knew only the day that came while the chief kept giving.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the pair runs from promise to fulfilment and, if the colophon of 384 is right, to loss: the sign the chief set for the bard's return in 381 becomes in 384 a life lived without counting days, which is exactly what is exposed when such a protector dies.","gr":2},{"a":"pn:382","b":"pn:400","type":"develops","note":"Both by Kovur Kizhar for Nalankilli: 382 promises to sing the king in rival courts so his enemies tremble; 400 says he drives off both his enemies and the hunger of those who come to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows what the bard's paid praise carries: 382 offers fame as a weapon against the king's enemies, and 400 supplies its content, a king whose war and whose feeding are one campaign, so the gift buys propaganda for exactly the two powers it praises."},{"a":"pn:390","b":"pn:392","type":"develops","note":"Avvaiyar at the gate of Athiyaman (390) and of his son Ezhini (392): the same stripping of rags, new cloth, toddy and food.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Father and son receive the same bard with the same ritual of rags exchanged for cloth, which shows the reception as an inherited house custom of the Athiyar rather than one chief's whim, while the son's poem adds conquest as the source of what is given.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:388","b":"pn:173","type":"develops","note":"388 is the drummer's own account of Pannan of Sirukudi feeding him in famine; 173, ascribed to Killi Valavan, calls Pannan the physician of hunger and sends bards to his house.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A crowned Chola king and a starving drummer both name a village landholder as the remedy for hunger, and 388 binds the bard's loyalty to him by an oath on a Pandya's protection, so Pannan's fame for feeding crossed kingdoms and outranked kings in the bards' own reckoning."},{"a":"pn:391","b":"pn:385","type":"develops","note":"Both by Kalladanar and both use Venkatam: 385 wishes a patron years more than the raindrops on Venkatam; 391 says the northern Venkatam land fell to famine and drove the bard south.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same poet uses Venkatam's rain once as the measure of endless plenty and once as the place where the rain failed, so the hill stands in bardic poetry for the uncertainty of rain itself, which is why the bard's life follows the patron and not the land."},{"a":"pn:388","b":"a:54","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 54 calls Pannan of Sirukudi one who does not live for himself but belongs to others, and sets the hero's homecoming by the gooseberries of his garden; 388 tells how Pannan fed a drummer in famine.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet can use Pannan's name as a byword for living for others without explaining it, which shows that the puram reputation 388 records was common knowledge by the time an akam homecoming could borrow it as a landmark.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:385","b":"a:61","type":"same_image","note":"385 blesses Aruvanthai with years more than the raindrops on புல்லிய வேங்கட; Akananuru 61 names Pulli, the chief of the cattle raiders, as lord of the festive Venkatam where toddy is bartered for paddy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If புல்லிய in 385 names Pulli, a bard of the south uses another chief's hill as the measure of his own patron's blessing, and akam remembers the same hill as a frontier reward the hero would not stay for, so Venkatam served both genres as the edge of the known Tamil world."},{"a":"pn:389","b":"a:83","type":"same_image","note":"389 describes Venkatam where a captured elephant calf is tied in the common while its mother grieves; Akananuru 83 shows Pulli's young men separating a calf from its wailing mother and tying it at the toddy houses of the Venkatam town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The calf-taking scene was a fixed picture of Venkatam shared by both genres, and the pairing shows the akam hero's heart racing past a scene that the puram poet offers as plain proof of the lord's wealth, the same image put to opposite uses of distance and of plenty.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:395","b":"a:122","type":"same_image","note":"395 places Pidavur east of Uranthai of Tittan of the small-eyed elephants; Akananuru 122 compares the many obstacles to secret love to the guarded walls of Tittan's Uranthai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Tittan's Uranthai is a fixed point on both maps: puram uses it to locate a small chief's village and akam uses its fortifications as a measure of how hard a meeting is, so a king's fame became the grid by which smaller places and private feelings were placed."},{"a":"pn:396","b":"a:216","type":"same_image","note":"396 praises Ezhiniyathan of Vattaru, whose land has the toddy-drinking Kosar; Akananuru 216 names Athan Ezhini, lord of Selli, among whose people the young Kosar make garlands, and compares the parathai's pain to the elephant his spear pierced.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"If the two Ezhinis are one chief (both are named with the Kosar), akam remembers him by a war wound inflicted on an elephant, used as a courtesan's image of suffering, while puram remembers him as feeder of the kinless, showing how a patron's two reputations were divided between the genres.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:386","b":"k:1065","type":"tension","note":"386 praises the king because his bards knew only the sweat of eating hot food and never the sweat of work; Kural 1065 says nothing is sweeter than even thin gruel earned by one's own effort.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two rank the same pair in opposite order: the bard's highest praise is to be spared labour by a giver, while the Kural makes self-earned food the sweetest, so the Kural's ethic of not asking is written against the very economy of patronage the Purananuru celebrates."},{"a":"pn:399","b":"k:1065","type":"tension","note":"399: the bard lived on bitter-gourd gruel his wife cooked with fish he caught himself and called it ruin (அழிவுகொண்டு) until the chief's gift came; Kural 1065: thin gruel from one's own effort is the sweetest food.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem gives the exact case the Kural imagines, gruel from one's own catch, and judges it the opposite way, as ruin to be ended by a patron, so the couplet is seen to argue against the bard's way of life and not merely in the abstract."},{"a":"pn:393","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"393: the bard's family sings lineages by inherited order (குடிமுறை பாடி) and asks the king for food and cloth; Kural 1062 curses the maker of the world if anyone must live by begging.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"What the Kural treats as an outrage against the world's order, a life that depends on asking, the bard presents as a hereditary office with its own proper order of song, so the two disagree on whether living by the gift is a shame or a calling.","gr":3},{"a":"pn:389","b":"k:1059","type":"develops","note":"389: as he gives, Athanungan asks the bard whether he will remember him in the drought; Kural 1059: what renown would givers have without people who ask?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states abstractly that the giver's fame needs the asker; the poem shows a giver who knows it, asking to be remembered as he gives, so the bard's memory is the very return the giver seeks."},{"a":"pn:395","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"395: the chief shows his wife to the bard and tells her to care for him as for himself; Kural 81: the whole of keeping house and guarding goods is for tending guests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says the household exists for the guest; the poem shows the moment that claim is enacted, when the master hands the guest over to the mistress of the house, so the guest is taken into the household rather than merely fed by it.","gr":4},{"a":"pn:394","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"394: Kuttuvan gives a killer elephant, and when the frightened bard returns it gives a bigger one, ashamed the first seemed small; Kural 221: true giving is giving to those who have nothing.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines a gift by the receiver's want, but Kuttuvan's elephants are sized to the giver's honour and are useless to a hungry bard, so the poem shows a royal giving that the Kural's definition would hardly count as ஈகை at all.","gr":6},{"a":"pn:387","b":"k:87","type":"qualifies","note":"387: the king did not look at the bard's smallness but gave by the measure of his own greatness; Kural 87: the fruit of hospitality has no set measure, being as great as the guest's worth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures hospitality by the guest's worth, while the poem praises a king precisely for ignoring the guest's low worth and measuring by his own, so royal giving runs on a scale opposite to the household's."},{"a":"pn:400","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"400: besides driving off his own enemies, the king can drive off the hunger-enemy of those who come to him; Kural 225: the strength to bear hunger ranks behind the strength to remove others' hunger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks hunger-removal above ascetic endurance as a kind of strength; the poem makes it a second warfare of the king, so the couplet's ranking appears to draw on a heroic vocabulary in which feeding was counted as victory."},{"a":"pn:397","b":"n:1","type":"tension","note":"397: under the king's protection the bard will fear nothing even if the sea ends or the sun rises in the south; Naladiyar 1: those who once ate six-flavoured food may go begging for gruel, so wealth cannot be counted as lasting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bard claims a security from his patron that survives cosmic upheaval, while the Naladiyar denies that any wealth or provision lasts, so the two make incompatible claims about whether a gift can make a life safe."}]});
